  <doc-data|<doc-title|Four-dimensional Yang--Mills
  theory<next-line><vspace*|6pt> <with|font-size|1.19|A constructive
  approach>>|<doc-author|<author-data|<author-name|Working exposition from
  the supplied manuscripts>>>|<doc-date|Draft of 19 November 2026>>

  <chapter*|Main theorem>

  The construction starts from Wilson lattice gauge theory on periodic
  four-dimensional lattices. Its continuum observables are local
  gauge-invariant fields. Their Euclidean correlations determine the
  relativistic theory described below.

  <no-indent><with|font-series|bold|Main theorem (continuum Yang\UMills
  fields).> Let <math|G> be a compact matrix group with simple nonabelian
  real Lie algebra <math|<gLie>>. Fix a faithful unitary representation and
  its normalized trace inner product, a positive penalty parameter for the
  block transformation, and a sufficiently large inverse coupling at the
  coarsest scale. There is a choice of lattice spacings tending to zero,
  admissible physical periods tending to infinity, bare Wilson couplings, and
  normalizations of local observables with the following properties.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(i)><with|font-shape|italic|Continuum limit.> At each fixed
    admissible period the correlations converge along the full sequence of
    lattice spacings. From any increasing sequence of these periods one can
    extract a single subsequence along which the correlations of all orders
    converge to smooth functions at pairwise distinct points in
    <math|<RR><rsup|4>>. The same subsequence works for all derivatives. The
    resulting local fields form nested finite-dimensional spaces, obtained by
    imposing successive bounds on the scaling dimension of the polynomial
    labels (curvature counts as two, each derivative as one, and dimensions
    add in products). They contain the identity and admit commuting spacetime
    derivatives. At each bound the field space and its gauge-invariant free
    Maxwell counterpart have the same dimension and carry isomorphic
    representations of <math|O<around|(|4|)>>.

    <item*|(ii)><with|font-shape|italic|Relativistic theory.> The limiting
    correlations are averages, with respect to a probability measure of full
    support on a compact metrizable space of phases, of Euclidean covariant,
    reflection-positive correlations. Every phase has the same field spaces
    and reconstructs a local, Poincaré-covariant theory on four-dimensional
    Minkowski space. Its Wightman distributions are tempered, its
    energy\Umomentum spectrum lies in the closed forward light cone, and its
    invariant vacuum is unique up to scalar multiples. The weak commutant of
    the fields consists of scalars.

    <item*|(iii)><with|font-shape|italic|Stress tensor and local products.>
    The phases share a real, symmetric, conserved stress tensor whose local
    charges generate translations and rotations through the Ward identities.
    Products of nearby fields admit an operator product expansion to each
    fixed finite order, with coefficient and remainder bounds uniform in the
    phase. A fixed Taylor subtraction also extends the separated Euclidean
    correlations across coincident points as distributions of locally finite
    order.
  </enumerate>

  Vacuum uniqueness here is a statement within each phase. Uniqueness of the
  infinite-volume limit and a positive mass gap remain open in this
  construction.

  <section*|Where the theorem is proved>

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch19:conditional>
  (p.<nbsp><pageref|ch19:conditional>) assembles the conclusions for one and
  the same family of Wilson measures. The main steps of its proof can be read
  separately as follows.

  <paragraph|The two limits in (i).> Theorems<nbsp><reference|ch10:finite-history>
  and<nbsp><reference|ch10:convergence> tune the bare coupling and prove
  convergence of the renormalization group trajectory at each fixed scale.
  With the bounds on observable insertions from
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>,
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch12:uv-theorem> proves the full cutoff limit at
  fixed volume. Section<nbsp><reference|ch13:extraction> then extracts one
  subsequence of volumes for all separated correlations and their
  derivatives. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch13:rank> identifies the
  dimensions of the field spaces. Section<nbsp><reference|ch13:calculus>
  constructs their inclusions and derivatives, and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch14:rotations> identifies their
  <math|O<around|(|4|)>> representations.

  <paragraph|Phases and relativistic fields in (ii).>
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch15:characters> gives the integral decomposition
  into phases. Sections<nbsp><reference|ch15:phase-symmetry>
  and<nbsp><reference|ch15:fields> establish symmetry, reflection positivity
  and equality of the field spaces in every phase.
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch16:boundary-section> constructs the tempered
  boundary values. Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch16:fields> reconstructs the
  local fields and proves the spectrum condition.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch16:vacuum> proves vacuum uniqueness in every
  phase, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch16:irreducible> determines the
  weak commutant.

  <paragraph|The stress tensor and local products in (iii).>
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch14:normalized-stress> fixes the normalization
  of the conserved tensor by its Euclidean Ward identities.
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch16:charges-section> continues them to the
  relativistic charge law<nbsp><eqref|ch16:charge-law>.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch18:ope-theorem> gives the finite-order expansion
  and its remainder estimate. The distinct assertion about extension across
  the collision diagonals is Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch18:extension-theorem>.

  <paragraph|A further conclusion in one selected phase.> Let
  <math|\<Theta\>> be the trace of the Euclidean stress tensor minus its
  expectation in the phase selected in Section<nbsp><reference|ch15:selection>,
  and let <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>> be its two-point function at
  separation <math|r\<gtr\>0> in that phase. The additional estimates of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch17:trace> give

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|<RR>><gLie>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>.
  </equation*>

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch17:all-radii> proves the limit for arbitrary
  radii tending to zero. Section<nbsp><reference|ch17:exclusion> specifies
  the finite class of mass-shell correlation functions excluded by this
  asymptotic.

  <chapter*|About this draft>

  This book is being written from three supplied accounts of a proposed
  construction of four-dimensional Yang\UMills theory, together with the
  detailed outline accompanying Scott's account. The aim is a readable
  mathematical exposition with definitions at their first use, consistent
  notation, and precise separation of standard arguments from estimates
  specific to the construction.

  The present draft contains the nineteen planned chapters, including the
  construction, reconstruction, quantitative trace law and finite-order
  operator products. The accompanying blueprint gives bounded source packets
  and a common notation registry; the audits record the detailed hypothesis
  matching and corrections made while writing. This is a working exposition,
  not an independent certification of the supplied manuscripts or a
  substitute for further mathematical review.

  The intended construction first removes the lattice spacing at each fixed
  physical volume and then extracts an infinite-volume subsequence. Its
  limiting correlations describe gauge-invariant local observables.
  Reconstruction, the stress tensor and operator products are developed after
  the continuum field spaces. No positive mass gap is asserted. Nor does the
  plan assert uniqueness of the infinite-volume limit or independence of all
  choices in the blocking procedure.

  The reader is assumed to know basic probability, analysis, compact Lie
  groups and the general aims of constructive quantum field theory. Standard
  results are cited where used; their application is explained without
  reproducing textbook proofs. Source-specific claims whose hypotheses have
  not yet been matched are not treated as proved merely because the source
  labels them theorems.

  <chapter*|Reading guide>

  The construction has two limiting operations. First the lattice spacing is
  removed while the physical volume is held fixed. Then the volume increases
  along a subsequence. The first operation requires a stability argument, not
  just compactness. The second uses compactness only after bounds uniform in
  volume have been established. Keeping these operations separate also fixes
  the meaning of the local observables throughout the book.

  A lattice expression with the correct classical limit is not yet a
  normalized quantum field. Integration can mix it with other expressions of
  the same or lower degree, including the constant. We therefore begin with
  finite-dimensional spaces of polynomial labels, construct their local
  lattice representatives, and later determine their normalization by
  transporting all coefficients together. Fields are finally defined by
  equality of all separated correlation responses. This prevents a formal
  equation of motion from being identified prematurely with a vanishing
  interacting field.

  The main analytic work is the block step. Its quadratic part determines a
  Gaussian fluctuation field and a stationary background. The nonlinear
  integral is separated into regions of small curvature, where Taylor and
  Gaussian estimates apply, and complementary regions, where a positive
  action cost is retained. Local terms are reorganized as polymer activities:
  functions associated with connected supports, controlled by weighted norms.
  The support weights must accommodate the full dependence of an activity,
  including the cutoffs and local gauge choices used to define it. This
  distinction matters when several insertions interact.

  Once the block estimates are available, one scale schedule must satisfy all
  fixed finite requirements. It is chosen slowly enough that each stage's
  constants can be controlled before the next set of requirements is
  introduced. A common trajectory then determines the couplings, activities
  and observable normalizations for the entire Wilson family. Observable
  insertions are tracked by commuting auxiliary variables whose squares are
  zero. Coefficients involving several variables retain the terms generated
  when insertions merge under blocking.

  The continuum part uses this information in a different order from a formal
  perturbative calculation. Finite families of free-field correlation
  functionals separate the polynomial labels and control the dimension of the
  interacting field spaces. Coordinate reflection positivity constructs
  Hilbert spaces and bounded slab operators before continuous rotations are
  known. A conserved tensor, local flux estimates and positivity then
  establish rotational Ward identities and fix the stress normalization. Only
  then are phases separated and relativistic fields reconstructed.

  <\padded-center>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|2|2|1|-1|cell-valign|top>|<cwith|2|2|1|-1|cell-vmode|exact>|<cwith|2|2|1|-1|cell-height|16pt>|<cwith|3|3|1|-1|cell-valign|top>|<cwith|3|3|1|-1|cell-vmode|exact>|<cwith|3|3|1|-1|cell-height|16pt>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-valign|top>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-vmode|exact>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-height|16pt>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-valign|top>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-vmode|exact>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-height|16pt>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-valign|top>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-vmode|exact>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-height|16pt>|<cwith|7|7|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Chapters>|<cell|Role
    in the argument>>|<row|<cell|1--3>|<cell|Lattice conventions, local
    labels and exact microscopic equations; free Maxwell correlations that
    separate polynomial labels.>>|<row|<cell|4--9>|<cell|Exact blocking,
    Gaussian kernels, large-field estimates, local matching, polymer
    expansions and the nonlinear step.>>|<row|<cell|10--12>|<cell|One common
    trajectory, observable insertions and the full ultraviolet limit at fixed
    physical volume.>>|<row|<cell|13--16>|<cell|Infinite-volume correlations,
    field quotients, Ward identities, phases and relativistic
    reconstruction.>>|<row|<cell|17>|<cell|The quantitative trace coefficient
    and its all-radius consequence.>>|<row|<cell|18--19>|<cell|Finite
    operator products, specified contact extensions, and assembly with the
    exact scope of the conclusions.>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The final two analytic refinements have different purposes. The trace law
  needs a rate beyond convergence to the traceless free tensor; an error that
  tends to zero may still be too large after multiplication by the running
  coupling. An operator product expansion needs a signed replacement estimate
  for a whole cluster, uniform under repeated copies. Neither follows from
  the existence of separated correlations alone. A conservative finite-order
  extension across collision diagonals, by contrast, follows from polynomial
  separated bounds and a specified Taylor subtraction.

  The final assembly chapter collects the proved dependencies and
  distinguishes the conclusions from further questions not addressed here.
  The accompanying chapter audits provide source locations and review
  records; they are working documents rather than prerequisites for reading
  the mathematical exposition.

