  <chapter|Lattice gauge theory and the continuum
  problem><label|ch01:lattice>

  The starting point is a probability measure on finitely many group-valued
  variables. Its definition requires neither gauge fixing nor a continuum
  functional integral. The construction problem is to choose its coupling and
  its local observables as the lattice is refined, and to control their
  correlations uniformly enough to remove the cutoff.

  Two limits have different roles. At a fixed physical volume, the lattice
  spacing will tend to zero along a prescribed sequence. Only afterwards will
  the volume tend to infinity, along a subsequence. Keeping these operations
  separate is essential: compactness can produce a subsequence, but cannot by
  itself give convergence along every ultraviolet cutoff.

  <section|The gauge group and its normalization><label|ch01:gauge>

  Fix a closed subgroup <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>> in a
  specified faithful unitary representation. Its real Lie algebra
  <math|<gLie>> consists of skew-Hermitian matrices, and we assume that
  <math|<gLie>> is simple and nonabelian. The group itself need not be
  connected. We use the invariant inner product

  <\equation>
    <around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>><rsub|<gLie>>=-<frac|1|n<rsub|G>><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|X*Y|)>,<space|2em>X,Y\<in\><gLie>.<label|ch01:metric>
  </equation>

  The choice of representation is part of the normalization of the model, not
  an inessential choice of matrix coordinates.

  Write <math|d<rsub|<gLie>>=dim<rsub|<RR>><gLie>> and let
  <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>> be an orthonormal basis for
  <eqref|ch01:metric>. The adjoint Casimir is the positive number

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|<gLie>>=-<frac|1|d<rsub|<gLie>>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|d<rsub|<gLie>>><tr><rsub|<gLie>><around*|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)><rsup|2>|)>.<label|ch01:casimir>
  </equation>

  Here <math|<math-up|ad><around|(|X|)>*Y=<around|[|X,Y|]>> and
  <math|<tr><rsub|<gLie>>> is the real operator trace. Invariance of the
  inner product makes <math|<math-up|ad><around|(|X|)>> skew-adjoint.
  Simplicity implies the familiar identity
  <math|-<tr><rsub|<gLie>><around|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|X|)><math-up|ad><around|(|Y|)>|)>=C<rsub|<gLie>>*<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>><rsub|<gLie>>>;
  nonabelianity gives <math|C<rsub|<gLie>>\<gtr\>0>. We shall use the
  constants

  <\equation>
    b<rsub|0>=<frac|11*C<rsub|<gLie>>|48*\<pi\><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>\<kappa\><rsub|G>=2*b<rsub|0>=<frac|11*C<rsub|<gLie>>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>.<label|ch01:slope>
  </equation>

  Their role in the construction will be to describe the leading logarithmic
  growth of the inverse coupling. Defining these constants does not yet
  establish that growth for the nonperturbative lattice model.

  For <math|G=S*U<around|(|n|)>> in its defining representation, our metric
  is <math|-<math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|X*Y|)>/n>, and

  <\equation>
    d<rsub|<gLie>>=n<rsup|2>-1,<space|2em>C<rsub|<gLie>>=2*n<rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<kappa\><rsub|G>=<frac|11*n<rsup|2>|12*\<pi\><rsup|2>>.<label|ch01:su-n>
  </equation>

  The conventional Wilson parameter is <math|\<beta\>=2*n/g<rsup|2>>. Thus
  <math|\<beta\>\<sim\>\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log <around|(|1/a|)>> corresponds to
  <math|g<rsup|-2>\<sim\><around|(|11*n/24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>*log
  <around|(|1/a|)>>. This is the normalization of the one-loop coefficient of
  Gross\UWilczek and Politzer<nbsp><cite|GrossWilczek1973|Politzer1973>. The
  exact control of the error in that law is a later construction problem.

  <section|Links, holonomies and the Wilson
  measure><label|ch01:wilson-section>

  For an integer <math|s\<geqslant\>1>, set

  <\equation*>
    \<Lambda\><rsub|s>=<around|(|<ZZ>/s<ZZ>|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em>E<rsub|s>=\<Lambda\><rsub|s>\<times\><around|{|1,2,3,4|}>.
  </equation*>

  The element <math|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>> of <math|E<rsub|s>> represents the
  oriented link from <math|y> to <math|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>>. A configuration is
  <math|U\<in\>G<rsup|E<rsub|s>>>. The variable on the reversed link is, by
  definition, <math|U*<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)><rsup|-1>>. A path holonomy is the
  ordered product of its link variables, with this inverse convention on
  backward steps. We use row transport: multiplying a row vector at the
  initial point by that ordered product transports it to the final point.

  The elementary positively oriented plaquette in the
  <math|<around|(|\<mu\>,\<nu\>|)>>-plane, with <math|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>>,
  has holonomy

  <\equation>
    U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=U*<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>*U*<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>,\<nu\>|)>*U*<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<nu\>>,\<mu\>|)><rsup|-1>*U*<around|(|y,\<nu\>|)><rsup|-1>.<label|ch01:plaquette>
  </equation>

  Define the plaquette energy and the normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance
  from the identity by

  <\equation>
    w<around|(|u|)>=1-<frac|1|n<rsub|G>><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><space|0.17em>u,<space|2em>\<chi\><around|(|u|)>=<frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|u-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>|<sqrt|n<rsub|G>>>,<space|2em>\<chi\><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>=2*w<around|(|u|)>.<label|ch01:chord>
  </equation>

  Both functions are invariant under conjugation and inversion; they vanish
  at the identity. The last identity follows directly from
  <math|u<rsup|\<ast\>>*u=1>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Wilson measure><label|ch01:wilson>Let <math|d*h> be probability
    Haar measure on <math|G>. The Wilson energy and measure on
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>> are

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>>w<around|(|U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|ch01:wilson-energy>>>|<row|<cell|d*\<mu\><rsub|s,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|e\<in\>E<rsub|s>>d*h<around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>,>|<cell|Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>>|<cell|=<big|int>e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|e\<in\>E<rsub|s>>d*h<around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch01:wilson-law>>>>>
    </align>
  </definition>

  Every unoriented coordinate square is counted once, through
  <math|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>>. There is no additional factor of <math|1/2> in
  <eqref|ch01:wilson-energy>. The configuration space is compact and the
  integrand defining <math|Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>> is continuous and strictly
  positive, so <math|0\<less\>Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>\<less\>\<infty\>> for every
  real <math|\<beta\>>. The reflection-positivity arguments below require
  <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>.

  A site gauge transformation <math|g:\<Lambda\><rsub|s>\<to\>G> acts by

  <\equation>
    U<rsup|g>*<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>=g<around|(|y|)>*U*<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>*g*<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>.<label|ch01:gauge-action>
  </equation>

  If <math|\<gamma\>> is a path from <math|y> to <math|z>, its holonomy
  transforms as <math|U<rsub|\<gamma\>><rsup|g>=g<around|(|y|)>*U<rsub|\<gamma\>>*g<around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>>.
  Thus a closed holonomy transforms by conjugation. Together with left and
  right invariance of Haar measure, this proves gauge invariance of the
  energy and of the law. These are the basic properties of Wilson's lattice
  formulation<nbsp><cite|Wilson1974>; no gauge fixing is involved in defining
  the probability measure.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Locality><label|ch01:locality>A function of <math|U> is local on
    <math|X\<subset\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>> if it depends only on links whose
    two endpoints belong to <math|X>. It is gauge invariant if its value is
    unchanged by <eqref|ch01:gauge-action> for every site gauge
    transformation. A finite stencil is a finite subset of
    <math|<ZZ><rsup|4>>, placed in the torus by translation followed by
    reduction modulo <math|s>.
  </definition>

  Locality and gauge invariance are different requirements. A single link
  matrix entry is local but usually not gauge invariant. A traced holonomy
  around a fixed lattice loop satisfies both.

  Gauge transformations also explain why small fields must be defined through
  plaquettes rather than individual links. The pure-gauge configuration
  <math|U*<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>=g<around|(|y|)>*g*<around|(|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
  can have links far from the identity while every plaquette holonomy is the
  identity. Later, small-field regions will be specified by bounds on the
  gauge-invariant quantity <math|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>>
  from <eqref|ch01:chord>.

  The connection with classical curvature is most cleanly stated using exact
  parallel transport. For a smooth <math|<gLie>>-valued connection <math|A>,
  let the transport on a segment from <math|x> to <math|x+a*e<rsub|\<mu\>>>
  solve

  <\equation*>
    u<rprime|'><around|(|t|)>=u<around|(|t|)>*a*A<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|x+t*a*e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>,<space|2em>u<around|(|0|)>=1,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  The plaquette product then has the expansion

  <\equation>
    U<rsub|x;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=1+a<rsup|2>*F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|(|x|)>+O<around|(|a<rsup|3>|)>,<space|1em>F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<nu\>>-\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>+<around|[|A<rsub|\<mu\>>,A<rsub|\<nu\>>|]>.<label|ch01:classical-curvature>
  </equation>

  Consequently <math|w<around|(|U<rsub|x;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>=<frac|1|2>*a<rsup|4>\|F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|(|x|)>\|<rsub|<gLie>><rsup|2>+O<around|(|a<rsup|5>|)>>.
  This familiar classical expansion motivates the action. It is not a
  regularity assertion about typical lattice configurations, nor about an
  eventual continuum random connection.

  <section|Physical scales and lattice symmetries><label|ch01:scales-section>

  We keep distances in lattice units distinct from physical distances. For
  <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>>, let <math|r<rsub|s><around|(|y|)>> be its
  unique representative in <math|<around|(|-s/2,s/2|]><rsup|4>\<cap\><ZZ><rsup|4>>.
  The periodic distance in lattice units is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><rsub|s><around|(|y,z|)>=min<rsub|k\<in\>s<ZZ><rsup|4>><around|\||<wide|y|~>-<wide|z|~>+k|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>,
  </equation*>

  where any lifts <math|<wide|y|~>,<wide|z|~>> may be used. This distance is
  translation invariant and invariant under signed coordinate permutations.
  At a centered-cell boundary the chosen representative itself need not
  transform as an ordinary vector; periodic statements are unaffected.

  Let <math|H<rsub|4>> denote the finite group of signed permutation
  matrices. An affine lattice isometry <math|y\<mapsto\>h*y+z>,
  <math|h\<in\>H<rsub|4>>, acts on configurations by pulling back oriented
  paths, using inverse links when orientation is reversed. Plaquette traces
  and product Haar measure are preserved, so the Wilson law is invariant
  under these isometries. Full Euclidean rotation invariance is not a lattice
  symmetry. Its recovery in the continuum is a separate part of the argument.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Scale geometry><label|ch01:scales>Choose odd integers
    <math|L<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3> and set

    <\equation>
      D<rsub|0>=1,<space|2em>D<rsub|m+1>=L<rsub|m>*D<rsub|m>,<space|2em>a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|m>=log
      D<rsub|m>.<label|ch01:scale-sequence>
    </equation>

    Choose strictly increasing physical periods <math|R<rsub|p>>, each an
    integer multiple of four. At scale <math|m> and volume index <math|p>,
    the lattice has <math|s<rsub|m,p>=R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> sites per side and
    physical spacing <math|a<rsub|m>>.
  </definition>

  Its physical side is exactly <math|a<rsub|m>*s<rsub|m,p>=R<rsub|p>>.
  Increasing <math|m> means refining the lattice; integrating out one scale
  goes from <math|m+1> to <math|m>. The index <math|N> will denote the finest
  scale in a finite renormalization trajectory. At cutoff <math|N>, a lattice
  site has physical placement

  <\equation>
    <wide|y|^>=a<rsub|N>*r<rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>><around|(|y|)>\<in\><around|(|-R<rsub|p>/2,R<rsub|p>/2|]><rsup|4>.<label|ch01:placement>
  </equation>

  Further growth and divisibility requirements on these sequences will be
  imposed when the estimates that require them have been established.

  Odd block ratios give centered blocks without an asymmetric choice of
  boundary sites. At a step of ratio <math|L>, every fine site can be written
  uniquely as <math|L*y+z>, with a coarse site <math|y> and
  <math|z\<in\><around|{|-<around|(|L-1|)>/2,\<ldots\>,<around|(|L-1|)>/2|}><rsup|4>>.
  The resulting parent map commutes with signed coordinate permutations. The
  corresponding coarse point and its fine representative have the same
  physical position.

  If an observable uses a fixed stencil <math|\<Sigma\>\<subset\><ZZ><rsup|4>>,
  its physical support has diameter at most
  <math|a<rsub|N><math-up|diam><around|(|\<Sigma\>|)>>. Such an observable
  becomes local at a point as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. This is different from
  a loop of fixed physical size, whose number of links grows with <math|N>.
  The latter is not included merely by proving estimates for every fixed
  stencil.

  <section|Reflection positivity><label|ch01:reflection>

  Reflection positivity is the link between Euclidean correlations and a
  positive Hilbert-space inner product. On the lattice it also gives
  chessboard estimates, which convert a bound on many reflected copies of a
  local observable into a bound on a correlation of separated observables. We
  use these classical results as inputs<nbsp><cite|OsterwalderSeiler1978|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978|Seiler1982>.

  Here are the support and orientation conventions for the finite-volume
  positivity statement. Fix an even side <math|s=2*h> and one coordinate,
  temporarily called <math|t>. A site reflection acts by <math|t\<mapsto\>-t>
  modulo <math|2*h>; a link reflection acts by <math|t\<mapsto\>-1-t>. Their
  positive sectors are respectively <math|1\<leqslant\>t\<less\>h> and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<less\>h>. In the first case the two fixed time slices
  are excluded. In either case observables in the positive algebra depend
  only on links whose two endpoints are in the indicated sector.

  If the reflected coordinate is <math|\<mu\>> and the reflection of sites is
  <math|\<vartheta\>>, the reflected configuration is

  <\equation>
    <around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)><rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|y|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|U<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*y|)>,>|<cell|\<nu\>\<ne\>\<mu\>,>>|<row|<cell|U<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*y-e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>,>|<cell|\<nu\>=\<mu\>.>>>>><label|ch01:reflection-links>
  </equation>

  The inverse in the second line is essential. For a complex observable set
  <math|<around|(|\<theta\>*F|)><around|(|U|)>=<wide|F*<around|(|\<Theta\>*U|)>|\<bar\>>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite-volume reflection positivity><label|ch01:lattice-rp>Let
    <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>. For either of the two reflection cuts above
    and every bounded Borel gauge-invariant observable <math|F> in the
    positive algebra,

    <\equation>
      <big|int><around|(|\<theta\>*F|)><around|(|U|)>*F<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|2*h,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\>0.<label|ch01:rp>
    </equation>

    The same conclusion holds after translating the cut or choosing another
    coordinate direction.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    This is the finite-torus form of lattice gauge reflection positivity
    <cite|OsterwalderSeiler1978|Seiler1982>. Polarization gives a positive
    semidefinite pairing between finite linear combinations of positive-side
    observables. The support restriction is part of the statement: it is not
    an assertion about arbitrary dependence on links crossing the reflection
    planes. Positivity of the Boltzmann density, by itself, would not imply
    <eqref|ch01:rp>.
  </proof>

  For clarity, one version of the chessboard construction can be described
  without observables on seams. Write <math|s=2*q*\<ell\>> and cut each
  coordinate into <math|2*q> intervals of length <math|\<ell\>>. Let the base
  cell consist of sites with all coordinates strictly between <math|0> and
  <math|\<ell\>>. An observable local on this cell reads no seam links.
  Reflecting the cell repeatedly through the planes at multiples of
  <math|\<ell\>> gives its copies in all <math|M=<around|(|2*q|)><rsup|4>>
  cells. Denote the resulting real pullback of a function <math|F> to cell
  <math|C> by <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|C>*F>; the orientation of links is always
  transformed as in <eqref|ch01:reflection-links>. For a bounded
  gauge-invariant <math|F> on the base cell, put

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|cb>,<with|math-font|cal*|T>>=<around*|[|<big|int><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|T>>\<cal-R\><rsub|C><around|\||F|\|><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|s,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>|]><rsup|1/M>,<label|ch01:chessboard-root>
  </equation>

  where <math|<with|math-font|cal*|T>> is this specified tiling. The quantity
  in brackets is nonnegative. This notation records the tiling as well as the
  observable; it is not a volume-independent norm by definition.

  The chessboard estimate bounds, for one base-cell observable
  <math|F<rsub|C>> per cell,

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||<big|int><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|T>>\<cal-R\><rsub|C>*F<rsub|C>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|s,\<beta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|T>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|C>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|cb>,<with|math-font|cal*|T>>.<label|ch01:chessboard>
  </equation>

  Apply the abstract chessboard theorem <cite-detail|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978|Theorem<nbsp>4.1>
  to the nonnegative observables <math|<around|\||F<rsub|C>|\|>>, one
  coordinate at a time. The slots are the <math|2*q> strips in that
  coordinate: translation by <math|\<ell\>> gives cyclicity, and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch01:lattice-rp> gives its reflection
  Cauchy\USchwarz hypothesis across the strip seams. The involution reflects
  the local strip coordinates, including link orientation. Repeating in the
  other three coordinates gives the reflected copies in
  <eqref|ch01:chessboard-root>. No power-of-two restriction on <math|2*q> is
  needed. Empty cells are filled with the constant one. More flexible
  placements used later require explicit control of support, seams and
  divisibility. They are not consequences of changing the tiling symbol in
  <eqref|ch01:chessboard-root> without checking those conditions.

  <section|Correlations and the order of the limits><label|ch01:limits>

  Recall that <math|a<rsub|N>> is the lattice spacing and <math|R<rsub|p>>
  the physical side of the torus, as in Definition<nbsp><reference|ch01:scales>.
  For now let <math|\<cal-T\>> be an arbitrary set of local-observable
  labels. A family of insertions assigns to <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\>> and
  <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>> a continuous
  gauge-invariant function <math|I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v,y;U|)>>. For each
  fixed <math|v>, require locality on a translate of a finite stencil
  <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|v>> independent of <math|N> and <math|p>. The next
  chapter constructs such local representatives; their necessary
  cutoff-dependent normalization is a later issue.

  Choose bare couplings <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> and abbreviate
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>=\<mu\><rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>>,
  the Wilson probability from <eqref|ch01:wilson-law>. For a list
  <math|v=<around|(|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n>|)>>, define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsup|<math-up|lat>><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>>|<cell|=<big|int><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>;U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p><around|(|U|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|C<rsup|<math-up|lat>><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;f|)>>|<cell|=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>><rsup|n>>f*<around|(|<wide|y|^><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,<wide|y|^><rsub|n>|)>*M<rsup|<math-up|lat>><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>.<eq-number><label|ch01:lattice-smear>>>>>
  </align>

  The factor <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>> is the physical volume element for
  <math|n> insertions. No limit is built into these definitions.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Separated test functions><label|ch01:separated-test>Set

    <\equation*>
      <Conf><rsub|n>=<around|{|<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\><around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:x<rsub|i>\<ne\>x<rsub|j><text|
      for >i\<ne\>j|}>.
    </equation*>

    A separated test function is an element of
    <math|C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf><rsub|n>|)>>, extended by
    zero to <math|<around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>. It is admissible at
    volume <math|p> if its support is contained in
    <math|<around|(|<around|(|-R<rsub|p>/2,R<rsub|p>/2|)><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>.
  </definition>

  Such test functions can depend jointly on all positions; they need not
  factor into one-point test functions. Compactness of their support gives a
  positive distance from the collision diagonals. We write
  <math|<math-up|sep><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|x|)>=min<rsub|i\<ne\>j><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
  with value <math|+\<infty\>> for <math|n\<leqslant\>1>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Ordered convergence><label|ch01:ordered-limit>A lattice family
    converges to kernels <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>> along an
    increasing volume-index map <math|P> if, for every <math|n>, every list
    <math|v> and every separated test function <math|f>, the following hold.
    At each admissible fixed volume <math|p> there is a limit

    <\equation>
      lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>> C<rsup|<math-up|lat>><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;f|)>=C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>,<label|ch01:uv-limit>
    </equation>

    and the retained volumes satisfy

    <\equation>
      lim<rsub|k\<to\>\<infty\>> C<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>=<big|int><rsub|<Conf><rsub|n>>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.<label|ch01:volume-limit>
    </equation>

    The test function is eventually admissible along <math|P>.
  </definition>

  The first limit uses every cutoff <math|N> in the chosen spacing sequence.
  The second may require a subsequence of volumes. The desired construction
  has the stronger quantifier order

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(i)>choose one lattice family;

    <item*|(ii)>prescribe any increasing sequence of volume indices;

    <item*|(iii)>extract one refinement that serves every label list and
    separated test function.
  </enumerate>

  In symbols, for every strictly increasing map
  <math|p<rsub|<math-up|seq>>:<NN>\<to\><NN>> there should be a strictly
  increasing <math|q<rsub|<math-up|seq>>> such that
  <math|P=p<rsub|<math-up|seq>>\<circ\>q<rsub|<math-up|seq>>> works in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ch01:ordered-limit>. The lattice family,
  including its normalizations, is chosen before
  <math|p<rsub|<math-up|seq>>>. Separate subsequences for different
  observables would not suffice.

  The bare inverse coupling is to satisfy

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|log <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\><rsub|G>*<space|1em><around|(|N\<to\>\<infty\>|)>,<space|2em><text|uniformly
    in >p,
  </equation*>

  with <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>> as in <eqref|ch01:slope>. This is one part of
  the target, not a hypothesis that supplies the required continuum bounds. A
  fixed parameter <math|b\<gtr\>0> specifies the soft constraint in the block
  transformation; it is distinct from <math|\<beta\>> and from the
  coefficient <math|b<rsub|0>> in <eqref|ch01:slope>.

  The eventual limit is a family of correlations of gauge-invariant local
  fields. It is not presented as a probability measure on continuum
  connections. Its decomposition into phases, reconstruction as relativistic
  fields, and stress tensor require additional arguments. In particular,
  reflection positivity alone does not establish the full reconstruction
  theorem. Likewise, a unique vacuum in each reconstructed phase does not
  imply a positive mass gap. Neither a mass gap, uniqueness of the
  infinite-volume limit, nor independence of the block parameter <math|b> is
  included in the conclusions described here.

  Finally, <eqref|ch01:volume-limit> concerns separated positions. Extending
  correlations as distributions across coincident points is a later
  renormalization problem. Such an extension is not automatically a limit of
  the unsubtracted lattice correlations at collisions. This distinction will
  remain important when products of local fields are considered.

  <section|How the construction is organized><label|ch01:strategy>

  The finite Wilson measure is easy to define; its behavior as the lattice
  spacing vanishes is the problem. The number of integration variables grows,
  and local observables must be rescaled and mixed before their correlations
  can have nonzero finite limits. Weak-coupling perturbation theory predicts
  some of these changes, but it does not bound the integral over all gauge
  configurations. The construction therefore keeps the finite integral exact
  while organizing its estimates by scale.

  An exact block transformation integrates out information between two
  successive lattice spacings and leaves a density on the coarser links. The
  transformed density generally has many interactions, even when the original
  action is a sum of plaquette terms. The useful question is whether these
  new interactions admit a stable description: a prescribed local action, a
  small remainder on configurations with small plaquette curvature, and
  strongly suppressed contributions where that condition fails. Here and
  throughout the book, small curvature means small plaquette holonomy; it
  does not mean that every link is close to the identity in an arbitrary
  gauge.

  These two parts of configuration space require different estimates. On the
  small-curvature part, a stationary background and its Gaussian fluctuations
  organize the local integral. Taylor coefficients of low order modify the
  prescribed action. The other terms must gain enough powers of the block
  ratio or the inverse coupling to remain small after summing over their
  positions. On the complement, positive action supplies an exponential
  penalty. Connected sets of defects are summed with their full combinatorial
  multiplicity. This is the role of the polymer expansion: it controls the
  sum once the local integral and energy estimates have been proved. It does
  not supply those estimates by itself.

  The leading change in the coefficient of curvature squared is logarithmic
  in the block ratio. Matching this change determines the constant
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>> in <eqref|ch01:slope>. One must then tune a whole
  finite-cutoff trajectory so that its coarse endpoint has a prescribed
  coupling. The same choice must work with all the estimates that will be
  required for local observables. Choosing a different trajectory each time
  an observable is introduced would not construct a single quantum field
  theory.

  Local observables bring an additional finite-dimensional problem. A
  monomial with <math|r> curvature factors and <math|k> derivatives has
  <em|scaling dimension> <math|2*r+k>. Gauge-invariant polynomials in
  curvature and its derivatives can mix under blocking. A transfer matrix
  records this mixing at each fixed scaling dimension. Bounds for this matrix
  and for the remainder of each inserted density are needed in addition to
  bounds for the density without insertions. Several insertions also require
  estimates uniform away from their collision diagonals. Bounds for lattice
  differences are essential: bounded correlations alone would not give smooth
  continuum kernels.

  The free Maxwell theory enters as a local comparison at short distances.
  Its role is to distinguish field labels, identify possible equation
  relations, and normalize certain symmetry identities. Such a comparison
  does not assert that all correlations of the interacting theory are
  Gaussian. It must be established for the same finite-cutoff family whose
  continuum limit is being taken.

  After these estimates are available, two separate arguments remove the
  cutoffs. At a fixed physical volume, stability of the renormalization
  trajectory gives a Cauchy property as the ultraviolet cutoff increases.
  Uniform estimates in the physical volume then permit one common
  infinite-volume subsequence, as specified in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ch01:ordered-limit>. Neither argument can
  replace the other.

  Finally, lattice symmetries and positivity must be promoted to the
  continuum statements needed for reconstruction. Coordinate reflection
  positivity gives an initial Hilbert-space structure. Bounds on translation
  operators, continuous Euclidean symmetries, and control of the correlations
  must be established with compatible normalizations. Decomposition into
  phases and relativistic reconstruction follow only after these steps. The
  trace identity and the expansion of products at short separation require
  further estimates of their own. This order explains why the book keeps the
  exact finite model, the local analytic estimates, the choice of a common
  family, and the continuum consequences distinct.

  <chapter|Local gauge-invariant observables><label|ch02:observables>

  A continuum observable needs more than a lattice expression with the right
  classical limit. Integration over fluctuations can mix it with other
  observables, including the constant. We therefore separate two tasks. This
  chapter constructs finite-dimensional spaces of classical polynomial labels
  and local lattice representatives of those labels. Later, the
  renormalization group will supply the cutoff-dependent linear maps that
  normalize the representatives at a fixed physical scale.

  The classical connections used below are test configurations. Their jets
  provide a precise way to say which local polynomial a lattice observable
  represents. We do not assume that a continuum random gauge field is a
  smooth connection, or even that such a random connection has been
  constructed. Throughout, <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>> and the
  metric on its Lie algebra <math|<gLie>> are those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch01:gauge>.

  <section|Radial jets and polynomial labels><label|ch02:jets-section>

  For a smooth connection near the origin, radial gauge is the condition
  <math|x<rsup|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>=0>. In this gauge the
  connection is recovered from curvature by the usual radial identity

  <\equation>
    A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>t*x<rsup|\<nu\>>*F<rsub|\<nu\>*\<mu\>>*<around|(|t*x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t.<label|ch02:radial-identity>
  </equation>

  Repeated coordinate indices are summed from <math|1> to <math|4>. The
  identity explains why a homogeneous potential of polynomial degree
  <math|k+1> carries the same scaling dimension as a <math|k>th derivative of
  curvature, namely <math|k+2>. We use this observation to define labels
  algebraically.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Radial jets><label|ch02:radial-jets>A formal radial jet is a
    sequence <math|J=<around|(|A<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>>, where
    <math|A<rsub|k>=<around|(|A<rsub|k,\<mu\>>|)><rsub|\<mu\>=1><rsup|4>> is
    a <math|<gLie>>-valued one-form with homogeneous polynomial coefficients
    of degree <math|k+1>, satisfying

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>=1><rsup|4>x<rsup|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|k,\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    Write <math|\<cal-J\>> for the space of these sequences and
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> for the finite-dimensional real
    space obtained by retaining only <math|k+2\<leqslant\>d>. It is the zero
    space for <math|d\<less\>2>.
  </definition>

  Only finitely many jet coordinates enter any polynomial below. A coordinate
  of <math|A<rsub|k>> has classical scaling dimension <math|k+2> and
  polynomial degree one. The scaling dimension of a monomial is the sum of
  those of its factors. For a homogeneous polynomial in jet coordinates, we
  also call its scaling dimension its <em|weight>. The ordinary polynomial
  degree counts factors of jet coordinates; to distinguish it from scaling
  dimension we call it the <em|factor count>. Constant gauge transformations
  act on every coefficient by <math|<Ad><around|(|G|)>>. Orthogonal changes
  of Euclidean coordinates act by the corresponding one-form transformation.
  Both actions preserve scaling dimension and factor count.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Polynomial labels><label|ch02:labels>For <math|d\<in\><NN>>, let

    <\equation>
      V<rsub|d>=<CC><around|[|\<cal-J\>|]><rsup|<Ad><around|(|G|)>><rsub|<text|scaling
      dimension>\<leqslant\>d><label|ch02:label-space>
    </equation>

    be the space of gauge-invariant complex polynomials of scaling dimension
    at most <math|d>. Choose nested real bases
    <math|<around|(|b<rsub|d,i>|)>> whose elements have definite scaling
    dimension <math|w<rsub|i>> and factor count <math|f<rsub|i>>, with the
    constant polynomial as one basis vector. Conjugation means conjugating
    coefficients in these real bases. Define the factor-count operator by

    <\equation>
      N<rsub|<math-up|fac>>*b<rsub|d,i>=f<rsub|i>*b<rsub|d,i>,<space|2em>t<rsup|N<rsub|<math-up|fac>>>*b<rsub|d,i>=t<rsup|f<rsub|i>>*b<rsub|d,i>*<space|1em><around|(|t\<gtr\>0|)>.<label|ch02:factor-scaling>
    </equation>
  </definition>

  The spaces are finite-dimensional: only <math|A<rsub|k>> with
  <math|k+2\<leqslant\>d> can occur, and every nonconstant coordinate has
  scaling dimension at least two. The invariant subspace decomposes according
  to scaling dimension and factor count, so the stated bases can be chosen
  once and for all. The constant has bidegree <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>; the
  zero-factor subspace is precisely <math|<CC>\<b-1\>>.

  The curvature at the origin is <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|0,\<nu\>>-\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|0,\<mu\>>>:
  the commutator term vanishes there in radial gauge. Its coordinates have
  scaling dimension two and factor count one. An important invariant
  polynomial is

  <\equation>
    c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>,\<nu\>><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>,F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<gLie>>,<label|ch02:curvature-square>
  </equation>

  with bidegree <math|<around|(|4,2|)>>. The inner product is extended
  complex bilinearly when used in a complex polynomial, not sesquilinearly.

  There are three distinct integers in this notation. A basis element has
  scaling dimension <math|w<rsub|i>> and factor count <math|f<rsub|i>>; it
  also belongs to a chosen space <math|V<rsub|d>> with
  <math|w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d>. We shall retain that last choice as part of
  the observable label, writing <math|v=<around|(|d,c|)>> with
  <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>. The same polynomial <math|c> can occur at
  different tags <math|d>. Its eventual normalization depends on the tag, so
  the resulting insertions must not be identified without a comparison
  theorem. None of these integers is being asserted to be the exact scaling
  dimension of the interacting quantum field.

  <section|Test holonomies and Taylor normalization><label|ch02:taylor-section>

  A label is a polynomial of a jet. A lattice observable is a function of
  link matrices. To compare them, insert the parallel transports of a
  truncated radial connection into the lattice observable.

  For <math|J\<in\>\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>, write
  <math|A<rsup|\<leqslant\>d><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k+2\<leqslant\>d>A<rsub|k,\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>>.
  For a positive link <math|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>> of <math|<ZZ><rsup|4>> and
  a real parameter <math|h>, define

  <\equation>
    U*<around|(|h,J|)><around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>=u<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>u<rprime|'><around|(|t|)>=u<around|(|t|)>*h*A<rsup|\<leqslant\>d><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|h*<around|(|y+t*e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|)>,<space|1em>u<around|(|0|)>=1.<label|ch02:test-holonomy>
  </equation>

  The solution remains in <math|G>. Standard dependence of solutions of
  analytic ordinary differential equations on parameters gives analytic
  dependence on the finitely many jet coordinates near zero; see
  <cite|Teschl2012>. Reversed links have inverse transport, in the convention
  of Section<nbsp><reference|ch01:wilson-section>.

  Define the jet dilation

  <\equation>
    <math-up|sc><rsub|h><space|0.17em>J=<around|(|h<rsup|k+2>*A<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>.<label|ch02:jet-dilation>
  </equation>

  Homogeneity in <eqref|ch02:test-holonomy> gives
  <math|U*<around|(|h,J|)>=U*<around|(|1,<math-up|sc><rsub|h><space|0.17em>J|)>>.
  In particular, a homogeneous label of scaling dimension <math|w> satisfies
  <math|c<around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h><space|0.17em>J|)>=h<rsup|w>*c<around|(|J|)>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Taylor map and local section><label|ch02:taylor-map>For a
    finite-link function <math|O> analytic near the identity configuration,
    <math|<math-up|Tay><rsub|d><space|0.17em>O> is the part of scaling
    dimension at most <math|d> in the Taylor expansion at <math|J=0> of
    <math|O<around|(|U*<around|(|1,J|)>|)>>. A local polynomial section is a
    complex-linear map

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>:V<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\><around|{|<text|polynomial
      functions of finitely many link entries and their conjugates>|}>
    </equation*>

    with <math|<math-up|Tay><rsub|d><space|0.17em>P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|c|]>=c>.
  </definition>

  The term section simply means a right inverse of the Taylor map. The
  polynomial condition is global in the link matrices; it is stronger than
  analyticity near the identity. For a gauge-invariant observable the Taylor
  polynomial is <math|<Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant, since constant gauge
  transformations conjugate the test holonomies.

  Equivalently, the section identity requires, for every <math|J> and
  <math|0\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>d>,

  <\equation>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsup|q>>*P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|c|]>*<around|(|U*<around|(|h,J|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h=0>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsup|q>>*c<around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h><space|0.17em>J|)>|\|><rsub|h=0>.<label|ch02:taylor-identity>
  </equation>

  It fixes every homogeneous component of scaling dimension at most <math|d>.
  It also does not prescribe the value of the lattice observable on a typical
  rough configuration.

  <section|Finite-stencil representatives><label|ch02:sections>

  The construction uses lattice curvature transported to a common point.
  Extend the metric <eqref|ch01:metric> to the ambient real matrix space as
  <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|X<rsup|\<ast\>>*Y|)>/n<rsub|G>>,
  and let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|<gLie>>> be orthogonal projection onto
  <math|<gLie>>. For a plaquette based at <math|z>, set

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>=\<Pi\><rsub|<gLie>>*<frac|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|-1>|2>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|\<gamma\>> is a lattice path from the origin to <math|z>, its
  transported curvature is

  <\equation>
    \<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>><around|(|U|)>=<Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>*F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>.<label|ch02:transported-curvature>
  </equation>

  Under a site gauge transformation it is conjugated by the gauge element at
  the origin. It is a real polynomial in the entries and conjugate entries of
  the links it reads, because inverse unitary matrices are conjugate
  transposes.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Local polynomial representatives><label|ch02:local-sections>For
    each <math|d> there is a finite centered stencil
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>\<subset\><ZZ><rsup|4>>, invariant under the
    signed permutation group <math|H<rsub|4>>, and a local polynomial section
    <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> with the following properties. Its
    values are gauge invariant and local on <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>>. It
    preserves the unit and complex conjugation, and satisfies

    <\equation>
      P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|c|]>*<around|(|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>=P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>*<around|[|h\<cdot\>c|]><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>h\<in\>H<rsub|4>.<label|ch02:section-covariance>
    </equation>

    Here <math|h<rsup|\<ast\>>> pulls back lattice links and
    <math|<around|(|h\<cdot\>c|)><around|(|J|)>=c*<around|(|h<rsup|-1>\<cdot\>J|)>>
    is the induced action on labels. Moreover, each basis column
    <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]>> is a
    polynomial in finitely many transported curvatures, and every monomial in
    that polynomial has at least <math|f<rsub|i>> factors.
  </theorem>

  The last assertion is what makes the section useful for estimates. An
  arbitrary right inverse of the Taylor map need not preserve this lower
  bound on factor count.

  <\proof>
    We give the finite-dimensional construction, invoking the usual inverse
    function theorem rather than reproving it.

    First consider the maps <math|J\<mapsto\>\<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,J|)>|)>>.
    Their linearizations at the zero jet separate
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>. Indeed, differentiating transport
    at the identity gives the line integral of the connection along the link.
    The derivative of a plaquette curvature is therefore its circulation. The
    derivative of the conjugating transport in
    <eqref|ch02:transported-curvature> contributes nothing, since the
    curvature itself vanishes at the identity.

    To verify separation, suppose all these linearized plaquette measurements
    vanish on <math|A=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|d-2>A<rsub|k>>. For each
    coordinate plane the circulation around its unit square based at <math|z>
    is the polynomial

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|(|z|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<nu\>>-\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>*<around|(|z+t*e<rsub|\<mu\>>+u*e<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t*<space|0.17em>d*u.
    </equation*>

    It vanishes at every integer <math|z>, and hence is the zero polynomial.
    If <math|k> is the largest degree index with <math|A<rsub|k>\<ne\>0>, its
    homogeneous part of degree <math|k> is
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|k,\<nu\>>-\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|k,\<mu\>>>.
    Thus <math|d*A<rsub|k>=0>. The radial identity and Euler's identity give

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>x<rsup|\<mu\>>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|k,\<nu\>>-\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*A<rsub|k,\<mu\>>|)>=<around|(|k+2|)>*A<rsub|k,\<nu\>>.
    </equation*>

    It follows that <math|A<rsub|k>=0>, a contradiction. Descending through
    the degree indices proves separation. Since
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> is finite-dimensional, a finite
    collection of measurements already has injective joint derivative.

    Fix such a collection and write

    <\equation*>
      <with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|J|)>=<around*|(|\<sigma\><rsub|1>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,J|)>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,J|)>|)>|)>\<in\><gLie><rsup|M>,<space|2em>D=D*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)>.
    </equation*>

    The map is analytic, <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)>=0>, and
    <math|D> is injective. Both the jet space and <math|<gLie><rsup|M>> have
    invariant real inner products. Hence the inverse of <math|D> on its
    range, composed with orthogonal projection onto that range, is an
    equivariant linear map <math|C> with <math|C*D=1>. The inverse function
    theorem applied to <math|C*<with|math-font|cal*|S>> gives an analytic
    local inverse <math|g>; this is the standard finite-dimensional theorem,
    for example <cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem<nbsp>9.24> together with its
    analytic form. Set <math|f=g*C>. Then <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0> and
    <math|f*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|J|)>=J> near zero. Equivariance
    follows from uniqueness of the inverse as a germ.

    Let <math|T<rsub|d>*f> be the ordinary Taylor polynomial of <math|f>
    through degree <math|d>. It has no constant term and is equivariant. For
    each real basis polynomial <math|b<rsub|d,i>> define
    <math|Q<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=b<rsub|d,i>*<around|(|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|z|)>|)>>.
    Then <math|Q<rsub|i>> is invariant, is real on real arguments, and has no
    monomial of degree below <math|f<rsub|i>>. The unit column is one.
    Consequently

    <\equation*>
      \<Psi\><rsub|c><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|1><around|(|U|)>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M><around|(|U|)>|)><space|1em><text|for
      >c=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*b<rsub|d,i>
    </equation*>

    is a gauge-invariant polynomial cylinder function. Since
    <math|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|J|)>|)>=J+O<around|(|<around|\||J|\|><rsup|d+1>|)>>,
    its Taylor map is <math|c>. Each jet coordinate has scaling dimension at
    least two, so every monomial of polynomial degree greater than <math|d>
    also has scaling dimension greater than <math|d>. No truncation of
    <math|Q<rsub|i>> after substitution is needed.

    Finally average over the finite lattice symmetry group:

    <\equation>
      P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|c|]><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||H<rsub|4>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|h\<in\>H<rsub|4>>\<Psi\><rsub|h<rsup|-1>\<cdot\>c>*<around|(|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>.<label|ch02:section-average>
    </equation>

    Uniqueness of the parallel-transport equation gives
    <math|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U*<around|(|h<rsub|0>,J|)>=U*<around|(|h<rsub|0>,h<rsup|-1>\<cdot\>J|)>>
    for a scale parameter <math|h<rsub|0>>. Thus every summand still has
    Taylor map <math|c>. Reindexing the finite sum proves
    <eqref|ch02:section-covariance>. Reality and the unit are preserved. A
    signed image of a transported curvature is another such curvature,
    possibly with a minus sign from orientation reversal. The group action
    preserves factor count, so the lower bound on monomial degree survives
    averaging. Enclosing all paths and plaquettes in a centered box produces
    the finite stencil <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>>. For <math|d\<less\>2> there
    are only constants and the construction reduces to the constant section.
  </proof>

  All choices in this theorem are made on the covering lattice
  <math|<ZZ><rsup|4>>, before selecting a torus period. For a site
  <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>>, write
  <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|c|]><around|(|y;U|)>> for
  evaluation after translating the stencil to <math|y> and reducing its links
  modulo <math|s>. The construction remains well-defined if different
  covering links become the same torus link. Gauge invariance, covariance and
  the polynomial identities survive that identification.

  The abstract jet space admits an <math|O<around|(|4|)>> action, but the
  covariance in <eqref|ch02:section-covariance> concerns only
  <math|H<rsub|4>>. Arbitrary rotations do not act on the lattice links. This
  distinction prevents the classical label symmetry from being mistaken for
  an already proved symmetry of the continuum correlations.

  <section|Small-field bounds for the representatives><label|ch02:bounds>

  Recall that <math|V<rsub|d>> is the polynomial label space of scaling
  dimension at most <math|d> from Definition<nbsp><reference|ch02:labels>;
  factor count counts curvature-jet factors instead of their scaling
  dimensions.

  We now use the factor-count property of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch02:local-sections>. Recall from
  <eqref|ch01:chord> that <math|\<chi\><around|(|u|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|u-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>/<sqrt|n<rsub|G>>>
  measures the size of a plaquette. On a fixed local stencil, a bound
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>\<leqslant\>t> gives a factor
  <math|t> for each transported curvature in
  <eqref|ch02:transported-curvature>. The same factor is retained under
  suitably scaled derivatives.

  For a collection <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|e>|)><rsub|e\<in\>E<rsub|s>>> of
  Lie algebra elements, define the left derivative

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|A><rsup|L>*F<around|(|U|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*\<varepsilon\>>*F<around*|(|<around|(|exp
    <around|(|\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|e>|)>*U<rsub|e>|)><rsub|e\<in\>E<rsub|s>>|)>|\|><rsub|\<varepsilon\>=0>.
  </equation*>

  An ordered list of these derivatives is used; no commutation of Lie
  derivatives is assumed. Fix a nonnegative spatial exponent <math|p<rsub|0>>
  and a site <math|y>. A permitted direction at threshold <math|t> satisfies

  <\equation>
    <around|\||A<rsub|e>|\|><rsub|<gLie>>\<leqslant\>t*<around*|(|1+<math-up|dist><rsub|s><around|(|e<rsup|->,y|)>|)><rsup|p<rsub|0>>,<label|ch02:direction>
  </equation>

  where <math|e<rsup|->> is the initial site of the positive link. This is a
  local definition for the estimate; the later polymer norms will specify
  which orders and spatial exponents they retain.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform section bounds><label|ch02:section-bounds>For every
    <math|d,q,p<rsub|0>> there are finite constants
    <math|C<rsub|d,q,p<rsub|0>>> and <math|C<rsub|d>>, independent of
    <math|s>, <math|y> and <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>, with the following
    properties. If every plaquette used by the translated section has
    distance from the identity at most <math|t>, then for every list of
    permitted directions of length <math|k\<leqslant\>q>,

    <\equation>
      <around*|\||D<rsub|A<rsub|1>><rsup|L>*\<cdots\>*D<rsub|A<rsub|k>><rsup|L>*P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|y;U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,q,p<rsub|0>>*t<rsup|f<rsub|i>>.<label|ch02:section-small-bound>
    </equation>

    For arbitrary configurations,

    <\equation>
      sup<rsub|i,y,U><around|\||P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|y;U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>.<label|ch02:section-global-bound>
    </equation>

    The same constants apply when the stencil wraps around the torus.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Projection is contractive and <math|U<rsup|-1>=U<rsup|\<ast\>>>, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsub|<gLie>>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>|2*<sqrt|n<rsub|G>>>\<leqslant\>\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Transport by a unitary matrix preserves the metric. An undifferentiated
    curvature measurement is therefore bounded by <math|t> on the stated
    domain.

    On the fixed stencil the right side of <eqref|ch02:direction> is bounded
    by a constant times <math|t>, uniformly in the period: periodic distance
    is no larger than distance to a chosen covering-space representative.
    Differentiating a fixed path product <math|k> times gives a finite sum of
    products with exactly <math|k> direction matrices and otherwise unitary
    factors. Its derivatives are bounded by a constant times
    <math|t<rsup|k>>. The same conclusion holds for each transported
    curvature. For a positive order <math|k>, this is in particular bounded
    by a constant times <math|t>, since <math|t\<leqslant\>1>.

    Every monomial representing the column <math|b<rsub|d,i>> has at least
    <math|f<rsub|i>> curvature factors. Apply the ordered product rule: each
    factor, whether or not differentiated, contributes a factor bounded by a
    constant times <math|t>. Summing the finitely many resulting terms proves
    <eqref|ch02:section-small-bound>. Matrix entries of a unitary matrix have
    modulus at most one, so the polynomial cylinder formula gives
    <eqref|ch02:section-global-bound>. If covering links coincide on the
    torus, one first differentiates the covering expression and assigns the
    same direction to identified links. The number of terms and their bounds
    do not increase with the period.
  </proof>

  The scaling operator <math|t<rsup|N<rsub|<math-up|fac>>>> from
  <eqref|ch02:factor-scaling> is tailored to this estimate. By linearity,
  permitted derivatives of <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>*<around|[|t<rsup|-N<rsub|<math-up|fac>>>*c|]>>
  are bounded by <math|C<rsub|d,q,p<rsub|0>>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>>
  on the small-field domain. The powers <math|t<rsup|f<rsub|i>>> cancel
  column by column. Outside that domain the global bound instead gives
  <math|C<rsub|d>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>*t<rsup|-f<rsub|i>>>.
  The missing small-field gain must later be supplied by the probability cost
  of a large-field region; it cannot be obtained by extrapolating
  <eqref|ch02:section-small-bound>.

  <section|Local representatives of the equations of
  motion><label|ch02:equation-section>

  The ordinary section <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> reproduces Taylor
  coefficients, but need not reproduce equations of motion. We construct a
  second section for this purpose. Everything in this section concerns the
  finite-volume Wilson measure; no renormalization estimate is used.

  A radial polynomial potential <math|A<rsub|j>>, homogeneous of degree
  <math|j+1>, is a <em|Maxwell solution potential> if

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|d*A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=0<space|1em><text|for
    every >\<nu\>.
  </equation*>

  Its curvature is automatically closed. Let
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>\<subset\>V<rsub|d>> be the polynomials vanishing on
  all such solution jets through degree <math|d>. This is the degree-<math|d>
  part of the equation ideal; multiplication is understood only when the
  resulting scaling dimension remains at most <math|d>. The condition
  preserves both scaling dimension and factor count. It is the kernel of
  restriction to solution jets, rather than a statement about the interacting
  measure.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|An equation-adapted local section><label|ch02:equation-adapted>There
    are a real, symmetry-equivariant linear section <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>
    from <math|V<rsub|d>> to local continuous gauge-invariant functions of
    links, a real equivariant automorphism <math|R<rsub|d>> of
    <math|V<rsub|d>>, and finite constants <math|r<rsub|d>,M<rsub|d>>, with
    the following properties:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*1=1>, <math|R<rsub|d>*1=1>, and
      <math|<math-up|Tay><rsub|d><space|0.17em>\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*p=p>. The
      section has a fixed finite stencil, independent of the torus.

      <item>If <math|p> has factor count <math|s> and scaling dimension
      <math|w>, then <math|R<rsub|d>*p-p> is a sum of terms whose pair
      <math|(<text|factor count>,<text|scaling dimension>)> is strictly
      larger than <math|<around|(|s,w|)>> in lexicographic order. Every term
      of <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*p> is a product of at least <math|s>
      transported curvature coordinates.

      <item>For every <math|p\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, every torus of side at
      least <math|M<rsub|d>>, every anchor <math|o>, every real
      <math|\<beta\>\<ne\>0>, and every bounded measurable <math|S> depending
      only on links with both endpoints farther than <math|r<rsub|d>> from
      <math|o>,

      <\equation>
        <big|int>\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*<around|(|R<rsub|d>*p|)><around|(|o,U|)>*S<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=0.<label|ch02:microscopic-null>
      </equation>

      No gauge invariance or regularity of <math|S> is assumed.
    </enumerate>

    The small-field derivative bounds for a fixed section proved above also
    apply to <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>, with its own finite constants.
  </theorem>

  The regrading <math|R<rsub|d>> is essential: the exact interacting relation
  is <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*<around|(|R<rsub|d>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>|)>>, not
  an assertion that <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>|)>>
  has zero Wilson correlations. The proof below supplies the finite-stencil
  construction, whereas the compact-group integration by parts underlying it
  is the usual lattice equation-of-motion identity for Wilson's measure
  <cite|Wilson1974|OsterwalderSeiler1978>.

  <subsection|Curvature tests avoiding a prescribed link>

  A curvature coordinate means a real colour component of
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>>> in
  <eqref|ch02:transported-curvature>. Call it admissible for an oriented link
  <math|e> if neither its plaquette nor its transporting path uses the
  underlying unoriented link <math|e>. Such a coordinate is independent of
  <math|U<rsub|e>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|ch02:avoiding-tests>For each <math|e>, the linearizations at the
    zero jet of admissible curvature coordinates span the dual of the finite
    radial jet space <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    It suffices to show that their common annihilator is zero. Removing one
    link does not disconnect <math|<ZZ><rsup|4>>, so every plaquette not
    containing <math|e> admits a transporting path avoiding <math|e>. At the
    zero jet transport has no effect on linearized curvature. Consequently a
    jet in the annihilator has zero sampled linear curl on all plaquettes
    except the finitely many containing <math|e>.

    For each orientation, this sampled curl is a polynomial in the
    plaquette's integer base point. A polynomial vanishing at all but
    finitely many integer points vanishes identically: fix all but one
    coordinate, use the one-variable assertion, and repeat. If
    <math|A<rsub|j>> is the highest nonzero homogeneous layer, the
    highest-degree part of its sampled curl is <math|d*A<rsub|j>>; lower
    layers cannot contribute to that part. Thus <math|d*A<rsub|j>=0>. Radial
    gauge and Euler's identity give

    <\equation*>
      0=\<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsup|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|j,\<mu\>>|)>=A<rsub|j,i>+x<rsup|\<mu\>>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*A<rsub|j,\<mu\>>=<around|(|j+2|)>*A<rsub|j,i>,
    </equation*>

    where closedness was used in the last equality. This contradicts the
    choice of <math|j>. Descending through the layers proves the assertion.
    Finite-dimensional duality now selects a finite spanning collection of
    admissible coordinates.
  </proof>

  The equality of the leading sampled curl with <math|d*A<rsub|j>> follows
  directly by integrating the polynomial connection around a unit square:
  Stokes' formula averages <math|d*A<rsub|j>> over that square, which changes
  only lower polynomial degrees. This also explains why no assumption about
  an infinite-dimensional configuration space enters the lemma.

  <subsection|Construction of the equation-adapted section>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch02:equation-adapted>>
    We first represent the generators of the equation ideal by Wilson forces.
    We then integrate by parts in a single link and assemble the local
    section.

    <vspace*|0.5fn><no-indent><em|Step 1. Representation by Wilson forces.>

    Write <math|S<rsub|W>> for the Wilson action without its factor
    <math|\<beta\>>. For a positive link <math|e> and an orthonormal colour
    basis <math|<around|(|E<rsub|a>|)>>, define its force <math|F<rsub|e>> by

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|e,a>*S<rsub|W>=<around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a>,F<rsub|e>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|e,a>*f<around|(|U|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*t>*f*<around|(|U<rsub|e>\<leftarrow\>e<rsup|t*E<rsub|a>>*U<rsub|e>|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>.
    </equation*>

    Differentiating the plaquettes incident on <math|e> expresses
    <math|F<rsub|e>> as a signed sum of plaquette-curvature coordinates based
    at the initial vertex of <math|e>. The precise signs follow the chosen
    plaquette orientations. Transport the force to the anchor by a path
    avoiding <math|e>, and take a colour component there. Its linearization
    is the corresponding discrete divergence of sampled linear curls.

    Fix a layer <math|j>. These linear force functionals, restricted to jets
    through layer <math|j>, span every functional which acts only on
    <math|A<rsub|j>> and annihilates the Maxwell solution potentials in that
    layer. Indeed, if all force functionals vanish, their values at integer
    base points form identically zero polynomials. Their top-degree terms are

    <\equation*>
      -<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|d*A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>.
    </equation*>

    This follows by taking the first difference of the sampled curl in its
    transverse coordinate. Thus the top layer is a Maxwell solution
    potential. Every functional of the indicated type annihilates the common
    force kernel and is therefore a finite linear combination of these
    forces. Notice that this argument identifies the top layer only; it does
    not identify the entire kernel of the discrete force system with the
    continuum solution jet space.

    Here is the corresponding description of the polynomial ideal. Choose a
    linear projection of each jet layer onto its solution subspace and let
    <math|\<cal-E\>> be their direct sum. In the full, not necessarily
    invariant, polynomial algebra the kernel of
    <math|p\<mapsto\>p*<around|(|\<cal-E\>*J|)>> is generated by linear
    functionals vanishing on those solution subspaces. To check this without
    an ideal-generation theorem, use

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|s>u<rsub|\<nu\>>-<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|s><around|(|\<cal-E\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|s><around|(|u<rsub|i>-\<cal-E\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<less\>i>\<cal-E\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u<rsub|\<nu\>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>\<gtr\>i>u<rsub|\<nu\>>.
    </equation*>

    Choose homogeneous linear coordinates, so both gradings are preserved.
    Every homogeneous element of <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> is therefore a sum
    of products <math|\<lambda\>*z<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*z<rsub|s-1>>, where
    <math|\<lambda\>> is a one-layer equation functional. Individual summands
    need not be gauge invariant.

    Represent <math|\<lambda\>> by the finite force combination just
    constructed on its truncated jet space. For each force summand involving
    <math|e>, represent each <math|z<rsub|i>> by a linear combination of
    curvature coordinates avoiding <math|e>, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch02:avoiding-tests> on the <em|full> jet space
    through degree <math|d>. Multiply these representatives by that force
    summand. The resulting function <math|f> has at least <math|s> curvature
    factors. Its Taylor polynomial is

    <\equation>
      <math-up|Tay><rsub|d><space|0.17em>f=\<lambda\>*z<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*z<rsub|s-1>+<around|{|<text|terms
      strictly higher in ><around|(|s,w|)>|}>.<label|ch02:equation-triangle>
    </equation>

    There are two distinct errors. The nonlinear part of a curvature or force
    coordinate increases factor count. The linear force representation was
    fixed only through the layer of <math|\<lambda\>>; its remaining linear
    terms increase the scaling dimension while preserving the factor count.
    This is why the order in <eqref|ch02:equation-triangle> puts factor count
    first.

    <vspace*|0.5fn><no-indent><em|Step 2. Integration by parts and
    construction of the section.>

    Every force term above has the form <math|<around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a>,F<rsub|e>|\<rangle\>>*H<rsub|a>>,
    where <math|H<rsub|a>> includes the transporting matrix, the other
    curvature factors, and an exterior spectator. All these factors avoid
    <math|e>. Freeze the other links. Haar invariance on the compact group
    gives

    <\equation*>
      0=<big|int><rsub|G>\<partial\><rsub|e,a><around|{|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|W>>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*U<rsub|e>=-\<beta\>*<big|int><rsub|G><around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|a>,F<rsub|e>|\<rangle\>>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*S<rsub|W>>*<space|0.17em>d*U<rsub|e>.
    </equation*>

    Multiplication by <math|H<rsub|a>> is legitimate because it is constant
    in <math|U<rsub|e>>. Fubini then proves the desired zero expectation,
    even for a merely bounded measurable exterior spectator. This proof never
    differentiates that spectator.

    All lists used in constructing a basis are finite. Enlarge one radius to
    contain their plaquettes, paths, and differentiated links. Enlarge one
    period threshold so that distinct relevant cover links cannot become
    identified with a differentiated link after periodization. These two
    choices work for every anchor by translation. They are independent of
    <math|\<beta\>> and of the exterior spectator.

    Average the constructed functions over the gauge element at the anchor.
    Every transported curvature transforms there by the adjoint action, so
    this is a finite-dimensional average of colour tensors. It preserves the
    number of curvature factors and <eqref|ch02:equation-triangle>. It also
    preserves the exact zero identity: gauge invariance of the Wilson law
    transfers the gauge transformation to the bounded exterior spectator
    without changing its support. The result is fully gauge invariant.

    Choose a basis of each homogeneous part of <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>,
    perform this construction there, and extend to a basis of
    <math|V<rsub|d>> by using <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> on a
    complement. The resulting linear map <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> preserves
    the unit. Its Taylor map is

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|d>=<math-up|Tay><rsub|d><space|0.17em>\<cal-F\><rsub|d>=I+T<rsub|d>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|T<rsub|d>> strictly increases the finite lexicographic
    grading. Thus <math|T<rsub|d>> is nilpotent and
    <math|R<rsub|d><rsup|-1>=I-T<rsub|d>+T<rsub|d><rsup|2>-\<cdots\>> is a
    finite sum. Average <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|d>> simultaneously on domain and
    range over signed coordinate permutations and over conjugation. These
    operations preserve the ideal, both gradings, and the unit; hence the
    same triangular conclusion remains valid. Finally put

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-V\><rsub|d>=\<cal-F\><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    This gives the Taylor identity, all symmetries, and
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d>*p=\<cal-F\><rsub|d>*p> for
    <math|p\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>. Both <math|R<rsub|d>> and its inverse
    preserve the lower bound on factor count. The fixed finite products
    therefore obey exactly the small-field derivative argument used for
    <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>, with new constants for the new
    stencil.
  </render-proof>

  The identity <eqref|ch02:microscopic-null> is exact at each cutoff. It
  gives no bound on a renormalized coefficient change. To use it for
  continuum null fields one must compare this section with the section used
  along the actual renormalization history, with one invertible comparison
  map for all labels and all spectators.

  <section|Examples and the remaining normalization
  problem><label|ch02:normalization>

  The unit illustrates the simplest section:
  <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|\<b-1\>|]>=1> for every
  <math|d>. Its eventual physical normalization still depends on the
  normalization index. There will be a reference scale for each <math|d>, and
  a choice of coefficient making the renormalized insertion exactly one. This
  does not follow by putting a universal factor <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>> in
  front of the raw constant section.

  For the curvature-square polynomial <math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> in
  <eqref|ch02:curvature-square>, a useful classical representative is the
  clover average

  <\equation>
    P<rsup|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|y;U|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\>\<ni\>y>w<around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>,<label|ch02:clover>
  </equation>

  where the sum has four corner plaquettes in each of the six coordinate
  planes, hence <math|24> terms. Orientation does not matter because
  <math|w<around|(|u<rsup|-1>|)>=w<around|(|u|)>>. For a smooth test
  connection the expansion <eqref|ch01:classical-curvature> yields

  <\equation*>
    P<rsup|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|y;U*<around|(|h,J|)>|)>=h<rsup|4>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>><around|(|J|)>+O<around|(|h<rsup|5>|)>
  </equation*>

  at the origin, and thus <math|<math-up|Tay><rsub|4><space|0.17em>P<rsup|<math-up|cl>>=c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>.
  The factors are consistent: each plaquette contributes
  <math|<frac|1|2>*h<rsup|4><around|\||F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|\|><rsub|<gLie>><rsup|2>>,
  four copies occur per plane, and the prefactor is <math|1/4>. This example
  fixes the normalization, but the general construction is free to use a
  different section satisfying the same Taylor identity.

  The clover formula does not solve the quantum normalization problem. Under
  blocking, the curvature square can mix with the constant. A general
  renormalized version therefore involves both a multiplicative coefficient
  and an additive constant, with coefficients depending on the cutoff and
  volume. More generally, a label in <math|V<rsub|d>> can mix with other
  labels in the same finite-dimensional space. The correct normalization is
  consequently an invertible linear map on <math|V<rsub|d>>, whose existence
  and estimates must be proved along the chosen renormalization trajectory.

  There is also a distinction between polynomial relations and vanishing
  fields. Some polynomial combinations represent free Maxwell equations of
  motion. The interacting lattice equations arise instead from Haar
  integration by parts, and include nonlinear corrections. To identify the
  null space of continuum observables requires both those identities and a
  rank estimate. Neither the section theorem nor its classical Taylor
  normalization identifies the interacting null space with the unmodified
  free equation ideal.

  The output of this chapter is therefore concrete but limited: a common
  finite-stencil representative for every polynomial label, with symmetry and
  threshold estimates uniform in the torus period. These are exactly the
  local data needed to begin the renormalization of observables. The uniform
  control of their correlations and their continuum normalization remain the
  task of the subsequent construction.

  <assign|chapter-nr|2>

  <chapter|The free Maxwell comparison theory><label|ch03:maxwell>

  The reference theory for short distances consists of independent Maxwell
  fields, one for each direction in the Lie algebra. Its role is more precise
  than a formal approximation to the action. It supplies finite-dimensional
  spaces of local fields, correlation functionals that distinguish those
  fields, and a normalization for the stress tensor. The interacting
  construction must eventually reproduce these data in an appropriate
  short-distance comparison. No interacting limit is assumed in this chapter.

  We fix the positive inner product on the real Lie algebra <math|<gLie>> and
  write <math|d<rsub|<gLie>>=dim<rsub|<RR>><gLie>>. Choose an orthonormal
  basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|d<rsub|<gLie>>>>.
  Greek letters <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>,\<rho\>,\<sigma\>> from the middle of the
  alphabet denote coordinate indices in <math|<around|{|1,2,3,4|}>>;
  <math|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>> denote colour indices. Time is the fourth
  coordinate. The superscript <math|<math-up|Max>> always denotes the free
  reference theory. We first define its correlations at distinct points; this
  requires no multiplication of distributions at the same point.

  <section|Curvature covariance><label|ch03:covariance>

  Let

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:green>\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>x\<ne\>0.
  </equation>

  Its locally integrable extension is the fundamental solution of
  <math|-\<Delta\>> in four dimensions. A convenient way to specify the
  curvature covariance is to start from independent Gaussian vector-potential
  components with covariance <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>*\<Gamma\>*<around|(|x-y|)>>,
  and take a curl in each argument. The vector potential in this description
  is a device for computing the covariance; it is not one of the
  gauge-invariant local observables used in the construction.

  Write <math|F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=-F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<nu\>*\<mu\>>>
  for the curvature components. Their two-point function is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:curvature>S<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|2>*<around|(|F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>,F<rsup|\<beta\>><rsub|\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>;x,y|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*C<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>*<around|(|x-y|)>,
  </equation>

  where, with <math|h<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*\<Gamma\>>,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>>|<cell|=-\<delta\><rsub|\<nu\>*\<sigma\>>*h<rsub|\<mu\>*\<rho\>>+\<delta\><rsub|\<nu\>*\<rho\>>*h<rsub|\<mu\>*\<sigma\>>+\<delta\><rsub|\<mu\>*\<sigma\>>*h<rsub|\<nu\>*\<rho\>>-\<delta\><rsub|\<mu\>*\<rho\>>*h<rsub|\<nu\>*\<sigma\>>,<eq-number><label|ch03:kernel>>>|<row|<cell|h<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<frac|8*z<rsub|\<mu\>>*z<rsub|\<nu\>>-2*\<delta\><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|6>>.<eq-number><label|ch03:hessian>>>>>
  </align>

  The minus signs in the first and last terms arise because a derivative in
  <math|y> is minus a derivative in <math|z=x-y>. This convention matters
  both for reflection positivity and for the stress normalization.

  The covariance is translation invariant and transforms as a two-form in
  each pair of indices under <math|O<around|(|4|)>>. At nonzero separation it
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:maxwell-equations>\<partial\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*C<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>+\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*C<rsub|\<nu\>*\<lambda\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>+\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>*\<mu\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*C<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>=0.
  </equation>

  The first identity is the Bianchi identity; the second is the source-free
  Maxwell equation. They hold off the origin. A distributional derivative can
  have a contact term at the origin, so these identities must not be used to
  infer a choice of contact extension.

  For a multi-index <math|a=<around|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|4>|)>>, put
  <math|<around|\||a|\|>=a<rsub|1>+\<cdots\>+a<rsub|4>> and
  <math|\<partial\><rsup|a>=\<partial\><rsub|1><rsup|a<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|4><rsup|a<rsub|4>>>.
  Derivatives of the fields have covariance

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:jet-covariance>S<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|2>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsup|a>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>,\<partial\><rsup|c>*F<rsup|\<beta\>><rsub|\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>;x,y|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*<around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||c|\|>>*\<partial\><rsup|a+c>*C<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>,\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>*<around|(|x-y|)>.
  </equation>

  In particular, for each fixed derivative order, this kernel is smooth away
  from zero and homogeneous of degree <math|-4-<around|\||a|\|>-<around|\||c|\|>>.
  Thus its size on <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<geqslant\>r>,
  <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>, has a polynomial bound in
  <math|r<rsup|-1>>. This elementary estimate is the free counterpart of the
  much harder uniform bounds required from the lattice construction.

  <section|Local polynomials and Wick correlations><label|ch03:wick>

  A local polynomial is a polynomial in finitely many curvature derivatives
  at one point. Its scaling dimension is computed by assigning degree
  <math|2> to <math|F> and degree <math|1> to each derivative. For example,
  <math|F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>*F<rsup|\<beta\>><rsub|\<rho\>*\<sigma\>>>
  has degree <math|4>, whereas <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>>
  has degree <math|3>. Scaling dimension is an algebraic grading of the
  reference model; it is not an assertion about anomalous dimensions of the
  interacting fields.

  The polynomial attached to one insertion is Wick ordered. We use the usual
  Gaussian pairing rule, whose operator formulation is due to
  Wick<nbsp><cite|Wick1950>. Here it can be taken as the definition of the
  separated correlation functions. Expand each polynomial into monomials,
  regard each factor as a distinguishable occurrence, and sum over pairings
  of all occurrences subject to the condition that paired occurrences belong
  to different insertions. For each pair use <eqref|ch03:jet-covariance>. The
  empty pairing has value one. The resulting finite sum is denoted by

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>,<space|2em>x<rsub|i>\<ne\>x<rsub|j>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>j|)>.
  </equation*>

  There is no covariance at zero separation in this definition.

  For instance, if <math|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|2>,Y<rsub|1>,Y<rsub|2>> are linear
  curvature derivatives,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>(:X<rsub|1>*X<rsub|2>:,:Y<rsub|1>*Y<rsub|2>:;x,y)>|<cell|=C*<around|(|X<rsub|1>,Y<rsub|1>;x-y|)>*C*<around|(|X<rsub|2>,Y<rsub|2>;x-y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+C*<around|(|X<rsub|1>,Y<rsub|2>;x-y|)>*C*<around|(|X<rsub|2>,Y<rsub|1>;x-y|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|C*<around|(|X,Y;z|)>> denotes the bilinear covariance obtained
  from <eqref|ch03:jet-covariance>. By contrast, in a word containing several
  insertions in the same time half-space, contractions between those
  <em|different> insertions are included. Wick ordering removes
  self-contractions at each insertion, not all contractions inside a word.

  The pairing rule gives complex multilinearity, symmetry under simultaneous
  permutation of labels and positions, reality, and the unit identity

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|n+1><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|1,P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Differentiating an insertion differentiates its incident covariances. The
  product rule therefore agrees with ordinary differentiation of the
  correlation function in that insertion position.

  If the labels are homogeneous of degrees
  <math|d<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,d<rsub|n>>, then

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:homogeneity>S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;\<lambda\>*x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<lambda\>*x<rsub|n>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>>*S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  For general labels of bounded degree, decompose into finitely many
  homogeneous pieces. Equations <eqref|ch03:jet-covariance> and
  <eqref|ch03:homogeneity> give bounds on all derivatives on separated
  configuration spaces. In particular, smearing against a smooth compactly
  supported function whose support avoids collisions is well defined. This
  statement is weaker than specifying the correlations on test functions
  whose supports meet collision diagonals.

  <section|The on-shell polynomial space><label|ch03:shell>

  The Maxwell identities impose linear relations on the jet variables. They
  should be imposed before discussing strict positivity or the number of
  independent fields. At each integer <math|j\<geqslant\>0>, let
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j>> be the real vector space of homogeneous
  degree-<math|j> polynomial two-forms on <math|<RR><rsup|4>>, with values in
  <math|<gLie>>, satisfying

  <\equation*>
    d*F=0,<space|2em>\<delta\>*F=0.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<delta\>*F=0> means <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=0>
  for every <math|\<nu\>>; the harmless choice of sign for the codifferential
  does not affect its kernel. These are finite-dimensional spaces. They
  encode the possible homogeneous Taylor coefficients of a source-free
  Maxwell curvature. A Taylor coefficient of order <math|j> is normalized as
  <math|D<rsup|j>*F<around|(|0|)>/j>!.

  The phrase <em|on shell> will mean restriction to these solution jets. It
  does not refer to restricting a point of Euclidean spacetime. A linear
  coordinate on <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j>> has scaling dimension <math|j+2>. As
  in Definition<nbsp><reference|ch02:labels>, we also call `weight' the
  scaling dimension of a homogeneous jet polynomial. Consequently polynomials
  of weight at most <math|d> use only the spaces <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j>>
  with <math|j\<leqslant\>d-2>. Constants are retained at every degree. The
  colour action is the adjoint action of <math|G>; invariant polynomials are
  the gauge-invariant local observables of the free reference theory.

  To compare this definition with the radial-potential labels
  <math|V<rsub|d>>, associate to a closed homogeneous curvature
  <math|F<rsup|<around|(|j|)>>> the radial potential

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:radial>A<rsup|<around|(|j+1|)>><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>t*x<rsup|\<nu\>>*F<rsup|<around|(|j|)>><rsub|\<nu\>*\<mu\>>*<around|(|t*x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t=<frac|1|j+2>*<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>x<rsup|\<nu\>>*F<rsup|<around|(|j|)>><rsub|\<nu\>*\<mu\>><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  There is a sum over <math|\<nu\>> in the integral as well. This is the
  usual radial homotopy formula: <math|x<rsup|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>=0> and
  <math|d*A=F> when <math|d*F=0>. The connection here is the <em|linear>
  Maxwell potential; no nonabelian commutator is inserted into this formula.
  Restricting a label in <math|V<rsub|d>> to these radial potentials gives a
  map

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|d>:V<rsub|d>\<longrightarrow\><around|{|<text|complex
    polynomials on Maxwell solution jets of weight at most >d|}>.
  </equation*>

  It preserves the unit, conjugation and the geometric actions. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:free-space>\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>=<math-up|im><space|0.17em>\<omega\><rsub|d>.
  </equation>

  Equivalently, this is <math|V<rsub|d>/ker \<omega\><rsub|d>> as a vector
  space. The image formulation is useful: it does not assume a separate
  surjectivity theorem onto a larger invariant-polynomial space.

  The covariance identities <eqref|ch03:maxwell-equations> make the Wick
  correlations well defined on these on-shell polynomial images. Products of
  a Maxwell equation with another jet polynomial vanish in every separated
  Wick correlation. Conversely, proving that <em|all> correlation-null
  polynomials vanish on shell requires a nondegeneracy argument; it does not
  follow from writing down the equations.

  The free spaces form a filtration. The inclusion
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>\<to\>\<cal-M\><rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
  for <math|d\<leqslant\>e> retains the same polynomial, while
  differentiation raises weight by one. For a simple nonabelian Lie algebra
  there is no nonzero invariant linear form on <math|<gLie>>. Therefore every
  gauge-invariant field of degree less than four in this polynomial image is
  a constant. This low-degree statement follows from the weights: a
  nonconstant monomial below degree four would have just one curvature factor
  or one of its first derivatives.

  <section|Reflection and the meaning of nondegeneracy><label|ch03:reflection>

  Let <math|\<vartheta\>*<around|(|\<b-x\>,t|)>=<around|(|\<b-x\>,-t|)>>.
  Reflection acts on a two-form by pullback, so a component with one time
  index changes sign and a purely spatial component does not. On a local
  polynomial, <math|\<theta\>> combines this action with complex conjugation.
  On a word it also reverses the order and reflects every insertion point.
  This is the antilinear reflection used in the Osterwalder\USchrader form,
  not merely conjugation of scalar coefficients.

  For words <math|w<rsub|i>> supported at distinct points in the positive
  half-space, the free field has reflection positivity:

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:rp><big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  The same inequality holds for smooth joint test functions supported on
  separated positive-time configurations. This is the standard Gaussian
  reflection-positive construction underlying Osterwalder\USchrader
  reconstruction<nbsp><cite|OsterwalderSchrader1973|OsterwalderSchrader1975>,
  applied to the curvature covariance rather than to an unconstrained
  vector-potential observable. Its hypotheses can be checked at the
  one-particle level: spatial Fourier transformation of <eqref|ch03:green>
  gives

  <\equation*>
    <frac|e<rsup|-<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|>*<around|(|t+s|)>>|2<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|>>*<space|2em><around|(|t,s\<gtr\>0|)>,
  </equation*>

  and taking the two curls with the reflected two-form indices gives the
  positive transverse-polarization Gram form. Derivative jets act on the same
  factors. The Gaussian Fock construction then gives <eqref|ch03:rp>;
  contractions within each positive-time word are included in its Fock
  vector. On compact separated supports the kernels are smooth, so the
  corresponding form on joint test functions follows by integration. We use
  this standard argument by citation rather than repeating the Fock-space
  construction.

  A simple sign check is informative. At separation <math|z=r*e<rsub|4>>, the
  six unordered curvature components have diagonal covariance. Three purely
  spatial components have value <math|1/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|4>|)>>;
  the three components <math|F<rsub|i*4>> have value
  <math|-1/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|4>|)>>. Reflection changes the
  sign of <math|F<rsub|i*4>> in the first slot. The reflected one-jet form is
  therefore positive in all six directions. Without the tensorial reflection,
  the same displayed covariance would give the wrong positivity statement.

  Positive semidefiniteness of <eqref|ch03:rp> alone does not identify its
  null space. For later rank arguments one needs the stronger statement that
  a nonzero on-shell polynomial has a strictly positive reflected two-point
  pairing at two suitable points. Derivatives of different orders may mix in
  that pairing. Checking one homogeneous order at a time does not establish
  the statement for their sum.

  <section|Strict positivity of finite jet families><label|ch03:strict>

  We now identify the null relations in the free polynomial theory. Recall
  from Section<nbsp><reference|ch03:shell> that <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j>> is
  the space of homogeneous degree-<math|j> Maxwell curvature solutions. Write
  a member as

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|a*b><around|(|v|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|j>>P<rsub|a*b;i<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*i<rsub|j>>*v<rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*v<rsub|i<rsub|j>>.
  </equation*>

  The derivative-index coefficients are symmetric. On this coefficient space
  use the Frobenius inner product, summing both orders of the antisymmetric
  field indices, all ordered derivative indices and colours. In particular,
  no factor <math|1/2> is inserted for the field indices. The linear jet
  probe represented by <math|P> is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:probe>\<ell\><rsub|P><around|(|F|)>=<frac|1|j!>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,i<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|j>,\<alpha\>>P<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b;i<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*i<rsub|j>>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|j>>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|1/j>! matches our Taylor convention. Every linear
  functional on the order-<math|j> solution jets has this form.

  Put <math|I<around|(|z|)>=z/<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>> and
  <math|R<rsub|z>=1-2*z*z<rsup|<math-ss|T>>/<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>. For a
  two-form, <math|R<rsub|z><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>> means applying
  <math|R<rsub|z>> to both form indices. The full reflection on a polynomial
  solution is <math|<around|(|R<rsub|z>\<cdot\>P|)><around|(|v|)>=R<rsub|z><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P*<around|(|R<rsub|z>*v|)>>:
  the derivative indices are reflected as well. For real probes define

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|z>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|P>,\<ell\><rsub|R<rsub|z>\<cdot\>Q>;z,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Extend this bilinearly across different derivative orders. Covariance and
  exchange of the two insertions show that <math|B<rsub|z>> is symmetric.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Finite-jet nondegeneracy><label|ch03:jet-positive> For every
    <math|z\<ne\>0> and integer <math|m\<geqslant\>1>, the form
    <math|B<rsub|z>> is positive definite on
    <math|<big|oplus><rsub|j\<less\>m>\<cal-S\><rsub|j>>.
  </proposition>

  The scalar differentiation identity needed in the proof is the harmonic
  case of Hobson's differentiation formula<nbsp><cite|Hobson1892>. In our
  normalization, if <math|P\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|j>>, it says, componentwise,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:hobson><frac|1|j!>*P<around|(|\<partial\>|)>*\<Gamma\><around|(|y|)>=<frac|<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j-2>*P<around|(|y|)>,<space|2em>y\<ne\>0.
  </equation>

  Here <math|P<around|(|\<partial\>|)>> means the ordered coefficient
  contraction without the factor <math|1/j>!. Maxwell solutions are harmonic,
  as follows by applying a divergence to the Bianchi identity. Thus the
  harmonic hypothesis of the scalar formula holds in each component. We use
  that classical differentiation result; the following finite-dimensional
  argument explains its application to the mixed jet orders needed here.

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<cal-R\><around|(|P|)><around|(|y|)>> be the curvature
    response obtained by contracting <math|\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|j>*C<around|(|y|)>/j>!
    with the coefficients of <math|P>. Taking the two curls in
    <eqref|ch03:hobson> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:kelvin>\<cal-R\><around|(|P|)><around|(|y|)>=c<rsub|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-4>*R<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P<around|(|I<around|(|y|)>|)>,<space|2em>c<rsub|j>=<frac|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>|\<pi\><rsup|2>>.
    </equation>

    For clarity, the identities determining the coefficient are as follows.
    Set <math|V<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>y<rsub|a>*P<rsub|a*i><around|(|y|)>>
    and <math|b<rsub|j>=<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>.
    The divergence of the differentiated Green potential is
    <math|D<rsub|i>=-2*<around|(|j+1|)>*b<rsub|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j-4>*V<rsub|i>>.
    The Maxwell equations and homogeneity give
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|i>*V<rsub|m>-\<partial\><rsub|m>*V<rsub|i>=<around|(|j+2|)>*P<rsub|i*m>>.
    The response is <math|2*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|m>*D<rsub|i>-\<partial\><rsub|i>*D<rsub|m>|)>>,
    so its coefficient is <math|4*<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*b<rsub|j>=c<rsub|j>>
    and its two-form factor is

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|i*m><around|(|y|)>-2<around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>*V<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>-y<rsub|m>*V<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|)>=<around|(|R<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P<around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|i*m>.
    </equation*>

    This verifies <eqref|ch03:kelvin>, including its sign, off the origin.

    Fix the separation <math|z>. For <math|P\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|j>> define a
    finite tower <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*P> by

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:triangular><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*P|)><rsub|k>=<frac|c<rsub|j>|c<rsub|k>><math-up|Taylor><rsub|k><around|[|P|(><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-I<around|(|z|)>)],<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>j.
    </equation>

    The operator <math|<math-up|Taylor><rsub|k>> extracts the homogeneous
    term of degree <math|k> at zero. Translation preserves the Maxwell
    equations, and each homogeneous term separately satisfies them. Thus all
    components in <eqref|ch03:triangular> lie in their declared solution
    spaces. The degree-<math|j> component is <math|P> itself.

    By the finite Taylor formula, the response of this tower is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*P|)><around|(|y|)>=c<rsub|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-4>*R<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*P*<around|(|I<around|(|y|)>-I<around|(|z|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|D*I<around|(|z|)>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|z>>, its
    Taylor coefficients at <math|y=z> vanish through order <math|j-1>, while
    its order-<math|j> coefficient is <math|c<rsub|j><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2*j-4>*<around|(|R<rsub|z>\<cdot\>P|)>>.
    For <math|Q\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|k>>, differentiation in the second
    insertion gives

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|z>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*P,Q|)>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k><around*|\<langle\>|R<rsub|z>\<cdot\>Q,<math-up|Taylor><rsub|k><around|[|\<cal-R\>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*P|)>|]><around|(|z|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>.
    </equation*>

    It follows that this expression vanishes if <math|k\<less\>j>, and at
    <math|k=j> equals <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j>*c<rsub|j><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2*j-4><around|\<langle\>|P,Q|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>>.
    Transforming the second argument introduces only lower orders in addition
    to its unchanged top order. Symmetry therefore gives, on the full tower,

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:diagonal-gram>B<rsub|z>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*P,\<Sigma\><rsub|z>*Q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*2<rsup|j>|\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*j+4>>*<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|j>,Q<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|F>.
    </equation>

    Every coefficient is positive. Finally <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|z>> is
    invertible: it is the identity plus a strictly degree-lowering map, hence
    its inverse is a finite geometric sum. This proves positivity on the
    whole tower, including mixtures of derivative orders.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Polynomial positivity and finite separating
    families><label|ch03:detectors>Every nonzero element of
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> has strictly positive
    reflected two-point pairing at a fixed separated pair. For any basis
    <math|<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|s>|)>> of this space and fixed
    distinct points <math|x,y>, the linear map

    <\equation*>
      P\<longmapsto\><around*|(|S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P,P<rsub|j>;x,y|)>|)><rsub|j=1><rsup|s>
    </equation*>

    is injective. Consequently a label in <math|V<rsub|d>> vanishes in every
    separated correlation if and only if its on-shell polynomial
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|d>> vanishes.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    This is the standard symmetric-Fock-space consequence of the one-particle
    Gram form and Wick's rule<nbsp><cite|Wick1950>. Explicitly, choose
    coordinates <math|X<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|M>> diagonalizing the
    positive form on a tower containing all variables of the polynomial, with
    positive squared lengths <math|b<rsub|i>>. The reflected pairing of Wick
    monomials is

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|a>,X<rsup|c>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<math-up|ref>>=\<b-1\><rsub|a=c>*<space|0.17em>a!<big|prod><rsub|i>b<rsub|i><rsup|a<rsub|i>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the squared norm of a polynomial is the sum of the squared absolute
    values of its coefficients with these positive weights; the constant
    coefficient has weight one. Restricting to the gauge-invariant image
    preserves positivity. If all basis-column pairings vanish, the pairing
    with the reflected conjugate of the polynomial vanishes too, forcing that
    polynomial to be zero. This also identifies the correlation-null labels.
    On any fixed finite-dimensional degree space, the map of correlation
    pairings has a positive minimum norm on the unit sphere. Small
    perturbations of its matrix remain injective; applying this fact to
    interacting correlations still requires a quantitative comparison
    estimate.
  </proof>

  <section|Quadratic fields and normalization checks><label|ch03:quadratic>

  We return to the free curvature covariance <eqref|ch03:curvature>; each of
  the <math|d<rsub|<gLie>>> colours has that same covariance and different
  colours have zero cross-covariance. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>=1><rsup|d<rsub|<gLie>>><big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>=1><rsup|4>:F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<rho\>>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<nu\>*\<rho\>>:,>|<cell|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>Q<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>,<eq-number><label|ch03:quadratics>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|4>Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>,>|<cell|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>>|<cell|=-Q<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>+<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>*Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>.<eq-number><label|ch03:stress>>>>>
  </align>

  The sign of <math|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>> is chosen to match the shell-flux
  convention in the supplied construction. A complete stress-normalization
  argument must check that convention against the translation Ward identity;
  conservation alone does not determine the sign.

  The tensor in <eqref|ch03:stress> is symmetric and traceless. Its
  divergence vanishes as an on-shell Wick polynomial. These are the ordinary
  algebraic Maxwell stress identities, obtained from the Bianchi and
  source-free equations; the same identities hold in separated correlations.
  The scalar <math|\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> is nevertheless nonzero. It
  is the curvature-square operator, not the trace of the free stress tensor.

  With the present normalization, Wick's rule gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:scalar-pair>S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>,\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>;x,y|)>=<frac|3*d<rsub|<gLie>>|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|8>>,<space|2em>x\<ne\>y.
  </equation>

  For checking constants, at separation <math|r*e<rsub|4>> one has
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>,\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>>:<around|(|F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)><rsup|2>>:.
  The two cross-pairings of each square, the six unordered components, and
  the prefactor <math|1/4> give <math|<tfrac|1|4>\<cdot\>2\<cdot\>6*d<rsub|<gLie>>=3*d<rsub|<gLie>>>.
  Orthogonal covariance makes the answer independent of the direction of
  <math|x-y>. The calculation uses only separated Wick contractions.

  This normalization will be needed when comparing a renormalized scalar
  field with the free curvature square. It does not itself identify the
  interacting trace, its coefficient, or its logarithmic asymptotic. Those
  conclusions require the interacting stress Ward identity and quantitative
  estimates on moving comparison fields.

  <section|The degree-four conserved tensor><label|ch03:classification>

  The finite-dimensional classification needed for the stress tensor is small
  enough to state explicitly. In this section <em|centered> means that the
  constant Wick coefficient is zero. By the Wick prescription it is also the
  one-point expectation. We consider real symmetric tensor fields
  <math|J<rsub|a*b>> of degree at most four, invariant under the colour
  action and transforming as a rank-two tensor under the signed coordinate
  permutations <math|H<rsub|4>>. Conservation is an identity in the on-shell
  polynomial space, or equivalently in every separated free correlation by
  Corollary<nbsp><reference|ch03:detectors>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Conserved tensor line><label|ch03:stress-line> The space of such
    centered tensors has dimension three. Its conserved subspace is the line
    <math|<RR>T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>>, with <math|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>> as in
    <eqref|ch03:stress>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Simplicity of <math|<gLie>> excludes invariant linear colour forms and
    makes an invariant symmetric bilinear colour form a multiple of the fixed
    metric. Through degree four a centered invariant polynomial therefore
    contains two undifferentiated curvature factors. Indeed, a derivative on
    one of two factors would raise the degree to at least five, while a
    single differentiated factor still has no invariant colour contraction.

    Recall the quadratic fields <math|Q<rsub|a*b>> and
    <math|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>> from <eqref|ch03:quadratics>. A basis of the
    tensor candidates is

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><rsub|a*b>>|<cell|=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*Q<rsub|a*a>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|a*b>>|<cell|=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>-2*Q<rsub|a*a>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|a*b>>|<cell|=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|)>*Q<rsub|a*b>.>>>>
    </align*>

    There is no sum over <math|a> on the right sides of the first two
    formulas. To see that these span, consider sign reversal of each
    coordinate. A diagonal component can contain only squares of curvature
    components. Permutations fixing its distinguished axis divide them into
    two classes: those containing that axis, and those containing neither
    instance of it. An off-diagonal component with indices <math|a,b> must be
    odd in axes <math|a,b> and even in the others. Its possible contractions
    are <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*c>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|b*c>>:
    with <math|c\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>; permutations identify their
    coefficients. This gives exactly the three candidates displayed above.

    For an explicit rank check, let <math|Q<rsub|i*j,i*j>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:<around|(|F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|i*j>|)><rsup|2>>:.
    Evaluate the first component of the divergence of each
    <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|l|)>>> at <math|e<rsub|1>>, against
    <math|Q<rsub|12,12><around|(|0|)>> and
    <math|Q<rsub|23,23><around|(|0|)>>. The two rows of these evaluations,
    after division by <math|d<rsub|<gLie>>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>>,
    are

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:divergence-matrix><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-256>|<cell|0>|<cell|128>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|-512>|<cell|-128>>>>>.
    </equation>

    These numbers come directly from the curvature covariance
    <eqref|ch03:kernel> and the two cross-pairings in a quadratic Wick
    correlation. For example, <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|Q<rsub|11>,Q<rsub|12,12>;t*e<rsub|1>,0|)>=2*d<rsub|<gLie>>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4>*t<rsup|8>|)>>;
    its derivative at <math|t=1> gives the first entry after the stated
    division. The first two columns are independent. Also
    <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><rsub|12>=Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|12>=0>,
    whereas the correlation of <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|12><around|(|e<rsub|1>|)>>
    with <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|13>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|23>:<around|(|0|)>>
    equals <math|-16*d<rsub|<gLie>>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>>.
    Thus the candidate tensors are themselves independent, and the kernel of
    divergence has dimension at most one.

    The Maxwell stress is conserved and

    <\equation*>
      -T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>=<tfrac|1|2>Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>-<tfrac|1|4>Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>+Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>.
    </equation*>

    It is nonzero by the preceding off-diagonal correlation. Hence it spans
    that kernel.
  </proof>

  This result fixes only a line of conserved tensors. Multiplying the stress
  by a scalar preserves all the properties used in the proposition. We next
  fix its normalization by a local Ward flux and then classify linear
  responses on fields of arbitrary bounded degree.

  <section|Local stress fluxes><label|ch03:ward>

  The sign in <eqref|ch03:stress> is fixed by a local identity, not by
  conservation. Let <math|x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>> be distinct points
  and choose a real <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)>>
  equal to one near <math|x<rsub|0>>, with support avoiding
  <math|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>>. For a vector field <math|v> define
  the flux

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:flux>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>,v>*<around|(|P<rsub|0>;P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*v<rsub|b><around|(|y|)>*S<rsub|n+2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|a*b>,P<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;y,x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
  </equation>

  The integrand is supported on <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*\<chi\>>,
  a compact set separated from all the insertions. It is therefore an
  ordinary integral of a smooth function; no extension of a product through
  <math|x<rsub|0>> is involved.

  For a constant translation <math|v=e<rsub|b>>, let
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|v>=\<partial\><rsub|b>> act on local polynomial
  labels. For <math|v<around|(|y|)>=K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>,
  <math|K<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=-K>, let <math|\<delta\><rsub|v>=\<cal-L\><rsub|K>>
  be the infinitesimal action induced on jets by the pullback of two-forms
  under <math|y\<mapsto\>x<rsub|0>+e<rsup|t*K>*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>.
  Equivalently, on a smooth closed two-form <math|f> its variation is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:lie><around|(|\<cal-L\><rsub|v>*f|)><rsub|i*j>=v<rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*f<rsub|i*j>+<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i>*v<rsub|b>|)>*f<rsub|b*j>+<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|j>*v<rsub|b>|)>*f<rsub|i*b>=<around|(|d*\<iota\><rsub|v>*f|)><rsub|i*j>,
  </equation>

  with sums over <math|b>. On polynomial labels the action is the induced
  Leibniz derivation, including the derivative indices of each jet. For the
  dilation <math|v<around|(|y|)>=y-x<rsub|0>>, this action at the center is
  the weight operator

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:weight><with|math-font|cal*|E>=<big|sum><rsub|j,\<lambda\>><around|(|j+2|)>*X<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*X<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>>,
  </equation>

  where <math|X<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>> are any linear coordinates on
  order-<math|j> solution jets, including colour. The sum is finite on each
  polynomial.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Normalized free Ward identities><label|ch03:free-ward> For
    translations, centered rotations and centered dilation,

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:ward-identity>\<cal-F\><rsub|\<chi\>,v>*<around|(|P<rsub|0>;P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>|)>=S<rsub|n+1><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|v>*P<rsub|0>,P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>.
    </equation>

    This holds for arbitrary finite Wick polynomials and arbitrary finite
    spectator lists. The common coefficient is <math|+1> for the stress
    <eqref|ch03:stress> and the outward-flux convention <eqref|ch03:flux>.
  </proposition>

  The Ward mechanism is the standard free-field action of the stress current.
  We include the normalization and the treatment of two contractions at the
  enclosed insertion, since neither is supplied by a formal integration by
  parts through a singularity. Wick's theorem is used throughout in its usual
  form<nbsp><cite|Wick1950>.

  <\proof>
    Polarize our stress between closed two-forms <math|f,g>:

    <\equation*>
      t<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|f,g|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|c><around|(|f<rsub|a*c>*g<rsub|b*c>+g<rsub|a*c>*f<rsub|b*c>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d>f<rsub|c*d>*g<rsub|c*d>,
    </equation*>

    with colour pairings understood. The Bianchi identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|sum><rsub|c><around*|(|<around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>f|)><rsub|c>*g<rsub|b*c>+f<rsub|b*c><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>g|)><rsub|c>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The tensor is symmetric and traceless. Thus contracting with a constant,
    skew-linear or scalar-linear <math|v> produces no additional divergence
    term from <math|D*v>.

    Suppose <math|f> is a smooth Maxwell wave near
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>, and <math|g> is the
    covariance pulse from <math|F<rsub|i*j><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Regularize its Green function by convolution with a smooth compactly
    supported unit-mass approximate identity
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>>, so <math|-\<Delta\>*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>=\<rho\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>>.
    The resulting closed pulse has divergence

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>g<rsub|\<epsilon\>>|)><rsub|c>=\<delta\><rsub|c*j>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-x<rsub|0>|)>-\<delta\><rsub|c*i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*\<rho\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\>-x<rsub|0>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Integrate the displayed divergence identity against <math|\<chi\>*v>. An
    integration by parts against <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>> shows that
    the flux converges to <math|\<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*j>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>-\<partial\><rsub|j>*<around|(|v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*i>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=<around|(|\<cal-L\><rsub|v>*f|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Here <math|\<chi\>=1> near the center. On
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*\<chi\>>, the regularized
    covariance <math|g<rsub|\<epsilon\>>> and its derivatives of each fixed
    order converge uniformly to <math|g> and the corresponding derivatives as
    <math|\<epsilon\>\<to\>0>. Differentiating in the pulse center, while
    holding <math|v,\<chi\>> fixed, gives the same identity for every jet
    probe. For dilation, <eqref|ch03:lie> becomes
    <math|<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*f+2*f>; its
    order-<math|j> jet at <math|x<rsub|0>> is <math|<around|(|j+2|)>> times
    the original jet. This gives <eqref|ch03:weight>.

    In a Wick correlation, extract the two factors at the stress insertion.
    The remaining sum has three types. If both partners are outside
    <math|x<rsub|0>>, the two waves are smooth throughout the cutoff support
    and conservation gives zero flux. If exactly one partner is at
    <math|x<rsub|0>>, the preceding calculation differentiates that
    occurrence. Summing its possible partners reconstructs Wick's rule, and
    summing occurrences gives the Leibniz derivation
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|v>>.

    If both partners are at <math|x<rsub|0>>, their jet orders <math|j,l>
    give pulse homogeneities <math|-4-j> and <math|-4-l>. Conservation away
    from <math|x<rsub|0>> makes the flux independent of the choice of
    <math|\<chi\>>. Rescaling a cutoff by <math|t> about <math|x<rsub|0>>
    multiplies this same flux by <math|t<rsup|e-5-j-l>>, where <math|e=0> for
    translation and <math|e=1> for rotation or dilation. Taking <math|t=2>
    forces the flux to vanish. The flux integral involves the two pulses only
    away from their common singularity at <math|x<rsub|0>>. The polarization
    counts each ordered pair twice, canceling its factor <math|1/2>.
    Combining the three types proves <eqref|ch03:ward-identity>.
  </proof>

  <section|Separating fields by insertions of bounded scaling
  dimension><label|ch03:bounded-detector>

  The polynomial labels and their scaling dimensions are those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch02:jets-section>; throughout this section they
  are evaluated in the free curvature field defined in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch03:covariance>.

  Corollary<nbsp><reference|ch03:detectors> detects a field of degree
  <math|d> by another field of degree <math|d>. A different statement is
  needed when a charge can raise degree: its response must be tested by
  spectators whose degree remains fixed. Their <em|number> may be arbitrary.

  We first make explicit the polynomial space used here. The free image
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> in <eqref|ch03:free-space> is
  the full space of invariant polynomials of weight at most <math|d> on the
  solution jets. Indeed, the radial map identifies each order of solution
  jets with a linear subspace of the corresponding radial-potential space.
  Choose an invariant inner product and its orthogonal projection onto this
  subspace, separately in each weight. Composing a polynomial with these
  projections extends it to the radial jets without changing its weight or
  invariance. This is the usual compact-group averaging argument; no equality
  with a larger space of unrestricted formal jets is being assumed.

  Let <math|\<frak-P\>> be the polynomial algebra on all solution jets and
  <math|\<frak-P\><rsup|G>> its invariant subalgebra. For
  <math|D\<geqslant\>0>, define

  <\equation>
    <label|ch03:ND>\<cal-N\><rsub|D>=<around|{|P\<in\>\<frak-P\>:S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,P<rsub|1><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>|)>=0|}>,
  </equation>

  where the equality is required for every separated word with
  <math|P<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsub|D><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>. There is no
  restriction on the degree of <math|P> or the length <math|n>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|A separating family of insertions of bounded scaling
    dimension><label|ch03:uniform-detector>There is a finite
    <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>> such that <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|D<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<cap\>\<frak-P\><rsup|G>=<around|{|0|}>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First restrict all jet orders to at most <math|j>. Hilbert's
    finite-generation argument<nbsp><cite|Hilbert1890>, using compact-group
    averaging, gives finitely many invariant generators homogeneous in
    ordinary polynomial degree. Let <math|b<rsub|j>> bound their weights. To
    apply that standard theorem, the jet space is finite-dimensional, the
    polynomial ring is Noetherian, and averaging preserves its invariant
    ideal and homogeneous pieces. Thus an invariant polynomial of ordinary
    degree <math|k> is a sum of products of generators of total ordinary
    degree <math|k>, each of weight at most <math|b<rsub|j>>.

    If a polynomial in <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|b<rsub|j>>> is nonzero, take its
    lowest nonzero ordinary-degree part <math|P<rsub|k>>. Express its
    reflected conjugate as such a sum of products, and put the factors at
    distinct spectator points approaching a common point different from
    <math|x<rsub|0>>. There are precisely <math|k> spectator legs.
    Higher-degree parts of <math|P> cannot pair with them, and for
    <math|P<rsub|k>> every spectator leg pairs with a leg at
    <math|x<rsub|0>>. No singular spectator\Uspectator contraction survives.
    The limit is the strictly positive two-point pairing of <math|P<rsub|k>>
    with its reflected conjugate, contradicting <eqref|ch03:ND>. This proves
    detection at bounded jet order.

    To obtain a uniform bound, we use a collision that lowers the highest jet
    order. Write <math|X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>> for the
    colour-<math|\<alpha\>> order-<math|j> coordinate associated with an
    uncoloured probe <math|p>. Fix a polynomial <math|P> with highest jet
    order <math|j> and a quadratic spectator
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:X<rsub|k,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>*X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>>:,
    where <math|l\<less\>k>. Contracting just its order-<math|k> leg with the
    top-order variables of <math|P> leaves the polynomial

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:replacement>\<cal-R\><rsub|p,q,j><around|(|z|)>*P=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>,\<lambda\>>X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>*C<rsub|k,j>*<around|(|p,e<rsub|\<lambda\>>;z|)><frac|\<partial\>*P|\<partial\>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|e<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>>.
    </equation>

    The covariance <math|C<rsub|k,j>> is in one colour. We claim that

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:replacement-null>P\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|D>,<space|1em>k+l+4\<leqslant\>D<space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>\<cal-R\><rsub|p,q,j><around|(|z|)>*P\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|D>*<space|1em><around|(|z\<ne\>0|)>.
    </equation>

    Here is the necessary separation of singularities. A differentiated
    curvature covariance of orders <math|k,j> is harmonic, homogeneous of
    degree <math|-r>, <math|r=4+k+j>, and becomes smooth after multiplication
    by <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-1|)>>>. A nonzero such
    harmonic function cannot become smooth after multiplication by
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-2|)>>>. Indeed, for a harmonic
    homogeneous <math|H> of degree <math|-r>,

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\>*<around|(|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*s>*H|)>=4*s*<around|(|s+1-r|)><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*s-2>*H.
    </equation*>

    Starting with a smooth extension at <math|s=r-2> and iterating would
    extend <math|H> smoothly to zero, contradicting its negative homogeneity
    unless it vanishes. A product of two harmonic poles of orders
    <math|r<rsub|1>,r<rsub|2>>, on the other hand, is cleared by
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>-2|)>>>.

    Insert the quadratic spectator at <math|x<rsub|0>+t*z> and keep all other
    spectators fixed. Its two contractions with <math|P> give finitely many
    homogeneous product poles. Subtract them before multiplying by
    <math|t<rsup|r>>. The limit of the remaining expression is the
    correlation with <eqref|ch03:replacement>: the lower single-contraction
    orders vanish, and contractions with remote spectators are smooth. The
    original full correlation is zero by <eqref|ch03:ND>. Successively
    removing the highest powers of <math|t<rsup|-1>> shows that the surviving
    harmonic pole is minus a product-pole coefficient of the same degree, or
    is zero. The extra radial cancellation just proved forces it to be zero
    in either case. This proves <eqref|ch03:replacement-null>; in particular
    we have not assumed existence of the unmodified rescaled collision limit.

    Put <math|g=d<rsub|<gLie>>> and choose
    <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>max <around|(|b<rsub|g>,2*g+5|)>>. Use
    <math|k=g+1> and <math|l=0,\<ldots\>,g-1>. For each <math|l> choose a
    nonzero probe <math|q<rsub|l>> and a solution jet on which it has value
    one. Such jets exist in every order: for example, the curl of
    <math|e<rsub|3><math-up|Re><around|(|x<rsub|1>+i*x<rsub|2>|)><rsup|l+1>>
    is a nonzero homogeneous Maxwell solution of degree <math|l>. If the
    highest order <math|j> of <math|P> exceeds <math|g>, each polynomial
    <eqref|ch03:replacement> has smaller weight than <math|P>. Strong
    induction on weight and <eqref|ch03:replacement-null> therefore make it
    zero.

    The matrix of polynomial entries <math|<around|(|X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>|)><rsub|l,\<alpha\>>>
    has nonzero determinant: assigning the chosen order-<math|l> jet to
    colour <math|l+1> and zero to the other colours makes it the identity.
    Multiplication by its adjugate, in the polynomial integral domain, shows
    that every colour-resolved top-order contraction derivative in
    <eqref|ch03:replacement> vanishes. As <math|p,z> vary, the functionals
    <math|C<rsub|k,j>*<around|(|p,\<cdot\>;z|)>> span the dual of the
    order-<math|j> solution space. To verify this last assertion, a vector
    annihilated by all of them has a response wave whose order-<math|k> jets
    vanish everywhere off zero. It is then a polynomial of degree below
    <math|k> there; its negative homogeneity makes it zero.
    Formula<nbsp><eqref|ch03:kelvin> makes the original vector zero. Hence
    all top-order partial derivatives of <math|P> vanish. Descending removes
    every jet order above <math|g>, and the fixed-order argument with
    <math|b<rsub|g>> concludes the induction.
  </proof>

  <section|Finite correlation criteria for infinitesimal
  symmetries><label|ch03:finite-ward>

  We now give the two finite-dimensional consequences used later to normalize
  translations and remove an internal rotation defect. Throughout this
  section the Lie algebra is simple and nonabelian. Recall that
  <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> consists of invariant
  polynomials of weight at most <math|d> on the solution jets
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j>>; its elements are local Wick fields, not
  distributions defined on collision sets. For maps <math|A> on this space, a
  <em|Ward sum> means

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|A>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A*P<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|n>;x|)>.
  </equation*>

  All points in this definition are distinct. The word length is arbitrary.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Characterization of infinitesimal
    translations><label|ch03:vector-tests> Let <math|d\<geqslant\>max
    <around|(|4,D<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>. Consider real-preserving complex-linear
    maps <math|A<rsub|b>:\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>\<to\>\<cal-M\><rsub|d+1><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
    which kill the unit, send <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
    into <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|e+1><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> for
    <math|e\<leqslant\>d>, and transform as a vector under signed coordinate
    permutations. Their Ward sums vanish identically if and only if
    <math|A<rsub|b>=c*\<partial\><rsub|b>> for a common real scalar <math|c>.
    There are finitely many evaluations of configuration derivatives of these
    Ward sums, at collinear configurations with line axis different from
    <math|b>, whose common kernel is precisely this translation line.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Translation invariance proves one direction. We prove the converse,
    starting at weight four. Let <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sc>>>
    be the scalar-valued solution jets, so
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|j>=<gLie>\<otimes\>\<cal-S\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sc>>>,
    and write <math|U<rsub|j>=<around|(|\<cal-S\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sc>>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
    for the uncoloured order-<math|j> probes and
    <math|Q*<around|(|p,q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>*X<rsub|k,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>>
    for their colour-contracted quadratic. The invariant metric on a simple
    Lie algebra is unique up to scalar. Hence
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> is spanned by the unit and
    <math|Q*<around|(|p,q|)>> with <math|p,q\<in\>U<rsub|0>>; weight five
    adds exactly the mixed quadratics with
    <math|p\<in\>U<rsub|0>,q\<in\>U<rsub|1>>. Inversion of all coordinates
    acts by <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|e>> at weight <math|e>. The filtration
    and vector parity therefore imply that
    <math|A<rsub|b>*Q*<around|(|p,p|)>> is a mixed quadratic. It has a unique
    representation by a linear map <math|D<rsub|p>:\<cal-S\><rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|sc>>\<to\>U<rsub|1>>:

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|b>*Q*<around|(|p,p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>Q*<around|(|e<rsup|\<lambda\>>,D<rsub|p><around|(|e<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Uniqueness follows by assigning independent zero- and first-order
    solution jets in one colour. Here and until the coefficient comparison,
    <math|b> is fixed.

    Denote the one-colour covariance of probes by
    <math|C*<around|(|p,q;z|)>>. The three-quadratic Wick rule is

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|S<rsub|3><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|Q*<around|(|a,v|)>,Q*<around|(|p,p|)>,Q*<around|(|q,q|)>;x,y,z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>=4*d<rsub|<gLie>>*<around*|[|C*<around|(|a,p;x-y|)>*C*<around|(|v,q;x-z|)>+C*<around|(|v,p;x-y|)>*C*<around|(|a,q;x-z|)>|]>*C*<around|(|p,q;y-z|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    In the Ward sum for three quadratics at <math|t*u,0,z>, multiply by
    <math|t<rsup|5>> and let <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. The third response has
    no fifth-order pole. The one-jet covariance is odd, so the other two
    terms have opposite signs. The zero-order covariance is invertible,
    allowing the probe at <math|z> to be chosen so its contraction against
    any <math|q> is <math|q<around|(|X|)>>, for any prescribed
    <math|X\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|sc>>>. Consequently

    <\equation>
      <label|ch03:panel>C*<around|(|D<rsub|p><around|(|X|)>,q;u|)>*q<around|(|X|)>=C*<around|(|D<rsub|q><around|(|X|)>,p;u|)>*p<around|(|X|)>.
    </equation>

    If <math|p<around|(|X|)>=0>, varying <math|q> with
    <math|q<around|(|X|)>\<ne\>0> shows that
    <math|D<rsub|p><around|(|X|)>=0>. The separation used here follows from
    <eqref|ch03:kelvin>: a probe pairing to zero with all zero-order probes
    at every displacement has zero response wave, hence is zero. Thus
    <math|D<rsub|p><around|(|X|)>=p<around|(|X|)>*f<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>>
    for some <math|f<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>\<in\>U<rsub|1>>. Cancellation in
    <eqref|ch03:panel> gives

    <\equation*>
      C*<around|(|f<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>,q;u|)>=C*<around|(|f<rsub|b><around|(|q|)>,p;u|)>.
    </equation*>

    This identity and separation prove additivity and real homogeneity of
    <math|f<rsub|b>>; the original signed-coordinate covariance passes to
    <math|f<rsub|b>>.

    For completeness the remaining coefficient determination is very small.
    Write <math|c<rsub|i*j>> for the probe <math|F<rsub|i*j>> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*c<rsub|i*j>> for its first derivative. Parity
    allows only <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*c<rsub|12>,\<partial\><rsub|3>*c<rsub|23>,\<partial\><rsub|4>*c<rsub|24>>
    in <math|f<rsub|1><around|(|c<rsub|12>|)>>. The swap of axes <math|3,4>
    and the divergence equation reduce their span to
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*c<rsub|12>>. Similarly the candidates for
    <math|f<rsub|1><around|(|c<rsub|23>|)>> are
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*c<rsub|23>,\<partial\><rsub|2>*c<rsub|13>,\<partial\><rsub|3>*c<rsub|12>>;
    the swap of axes <math|2,3> and the Bianchi equation reduce them to
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*c<rsub|23>>. The two coefficients agree by
    reciprocity: differentiation of the covariance gives
    <math|C*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|1>*c<rsub|12>,c<rsub|23>;e<rsub|1>+e<rsub|3>|)>=-1/<around|(|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>\<ne\>0>.
    Signed permutations then give <math|f<rsub|b>=c<rsub|0>*\<partial\><rsub|b>>
    on every probe. Since <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*Q*<around|(|p,p|)>=2*Q*<around|(|p,\<partial\><rsub|b>*p|)>>,
    we have <math|A<rsub|b>=c*\<partial\><rsub|b>> on weight four, with
    <math|c=c<rsub|0>/2>.

    Set <math|B<rsub|b>=A<rsub|b>-c*\<partial\><rsub|b>>. It kills the stress
    and still has zero Ward sums. Write its Ward sum for a word with an
    additional stress insertion. Apply the flux formula <eqref|ch03:flux> to
    this insertion with the dilation vector field
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>=y-x<rsub|0>>. The term acting on the stress is
    zero. The free Ward identity then gives the Ward sum with
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|E>*B<rsub|b>*P> in the distinguished slot and
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|E>*P> in the other terms. Subtract the
    ordinary Ward sum for the word starting with
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|E>*P>. It follows that
    <math|<around|[|<with|math-font|cal*|E>,B<rsub|b>|]>*P\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|d>>.
    Proposition <reference|ch03:uniform-detector> makes this commutator zero,
    even though its weight can be <math|d+1>. Thus <math|B<rsub|b>> preserves
    each weight eigenspace. But vector covariance makes it anticommute with
    coordinate inversion, which is the scalar <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|e>>
    on that eigenspace. Therefore <math|B<rsub|b>=0>.

    Finally, if every configuration derivative of a Ward sum vanishes at all
    strict collinear configurations along one axis other than <math|b>, real
    analyticity implies that it vanishes everywhere. One can connect any
    separated configuration to such a collinear configuration without
    collisions: order its projections on that axis, break ties by tiny
    ordered perturbations, and interpolate to the resulting line points. The
    projected differences keep a strict sign until the separated endpoint.
    Analytic continuation along the segment proves the assertion. The space
    of permitted maps is finite-dimensional over <math|<RR>>. An intersection
    of kernels of linear functionals on it is already the intersection of a
    finite subfamily (take a basis of their span in the dual). Real and
    imaginary parts of the evaluations give the required finite family.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|A vanishing criterion for an internal rotation
    defect><label|ch03:internal-tests> For <math|d\<geqslant\>4>, let
    <math|\<Delta\>:<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<times\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<to\><math-up|End><rsub|<CC>><around|(|\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>|)>>
    be real-bilinear and alternating. Assume its values preserve reality and
    the weight filtration and kill the unit, and that

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\>*<around|(|s*K*s<rsup|-1>,s*L*s<rsup|-1>|)>=\<rho\><around|(|s|)>*\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*\<rho\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1>
    </equation*>

    for orientation-preserving signed coordinate permutations <math|s>. If
    every value of <math|\<Delta\>> has zero Ward sums, then
    <math|\<Delta\>=0>. It suffices to check that finitely many of these Ward
    sums vanish.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|K,L> and put <math|U=\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>>. At weight
    four write <math|B<rsub|x>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P,Q;x,0|)>>.
    Recall <math|R<rsub|x>=I-2*x*x<rsup|<math-ss|T>>/<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>,
    the reflection normal to <math|x>. The zero-order covariance is
    <math|\<pi\><rsup|-2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-4><big|wedge><rsup|2>R<rsub|x>>
    in each colour. Wick's rule consequently gives, for
    <math|<around|\||x|\|>=<around|\||y|\|>>,

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|x>*<around|(|P,Q|)>=B<rsub|y>*<around|(|\<rho\>*<around|(|R<rsub|y>*R<rsub|x>|)>*P,Q|)><space|2em><around|(|P,Q\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|Max>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The two-point Ward sum says that <math|U> is skew for every
    <math|B<rsub|x>>. These forms are nondegenerate by strict reflected
    positivity. Comparing skewness for <math|x> and <math|y> therefore shows
    that <math|U> commutes with <math|\<rho\>*<around|(|R<rsub|x>*R<rsub|y>|)>>
    on this space. In particular it commutes with each coordinate
    quarter-turn <math|q<rsub|a*b>>, by choosing <math|x> parallel to
    <math|e<rsub|a>> and <math|y> parallel to <math|e<rsub|a>+e<rsub|b>>.

    An alternating bilinear map on <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>
    invariant under simultaneous conjugation by these quarter-turns is zero,
    even when its values lie in an arbitrary real vector space. To see this
    on the basis <math|E<rsub|a*b>=e<rsub|a>*e<rsub|b><rsup|<math-ss|T>>-e<rsub|b>*e<rsub|a><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>,
    a half-turn in the plane spanned by the third and fourth axes fixes one
    member of a pair sharing an axis and negates the other. For a disjoint
    pair, the even permutation exchanging their two ordered axis pairs
    exchanges the arguments, so alternation gives zero. Equal pairs vanish by
    alternation. Bilinearity proves the claim. Covariance and the commutation
    just proved apply this observation to <math|\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*P>
    for every weight-four <math|P>. In particular <math|U> kills the stress.

    For each <math|H\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>, repeat
    the preceding commutator argument using the rotation vector field
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>=H*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>> in the flux formula
    <eqref|ch03:flux>. This gives <math|<around|[|\<cal-L\><rsub|H>,U|]>*P\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|d>>.
    This time the commutator itself lies in
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>, so a single reflected
    two-point pairing shows that it vanishes. The uniform bound
    <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>> is unnecessary. Exponentiation in the
    finite-dimensional space shows that <math|U> commutes with every
    quarter-turn on the whole space. Covariance and the same alternating-map
    argument give <math|\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*P=0> for every <math|P>.
    The finiteness assertion again follows by taking a basis of the span of
    the Ward functionals in the dual of the finite-dimensional space of
    allowed <math|\<Delta\>>.
  </proof>

  Each criterion involves finitely many evaluations on a specified space of
  possible responses. Their application to the interacting construction
  requires proving that its responses obey the stated filtration, covariance,
  and comparison hypotheses. No such interacting input is part of the
  free-field results proved here.

  <assign|chapter-nr|3>

  <chapter|Exact blocking and effective densities><label|ch04:blocking>

  A renormalization step is first an exact integration, and only then an
  estimate. We define that integration on every fine configuration, including
  large fields. Its output is generally not a Wilson density. We therefore
  also introduce coordinates for the effective density: a prescribed leading
  action, a scalar normalization, local corrections on small-field regions,
  and a sum over polymers covering the large-field sites.

  The gauge group, Haar measure and plaquette energy <math|w> are as in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch01:lattice>. Recall that
  <math|a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>> is the physical spacing,
  <math|R<rsub|p>> is the physical period, and the odd ratio <math|L<rsub|m>>
  relates levels <math|m+1> and <math|m>. In this chapter we examine a single
  step <math|m+1\<to\>m>. Write <math|L=L<rsub|m>>, fix a positive integer
  smoothing order <math|j>, and set <math|R=<around|(|j+3|)>*L>. Fine links
  <math|U> live on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m+1>>> and coarse
  links <math|V> on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>>. Patches are
  first described on the covering lattice and then placed periodically. The
  constants and path coefficients introduced here are inputs; their
  simultaneous choice along a trajectory will require the later estimates.

  <section|Local gauges and a symmetric frame choice><label|ch04:frames>

  To average fine transports, their endpoints must first be expressed in
  common gauge frames. A deterministic axial gauge is convenient for bounds
  but singles out coordinate directions. We combine a symmetric minimizing
  choice near the flat field with a finite randomized choice elsewhere. The
  use of gauge-covariant averages in a block transformation belongs to
  Baªaban's renormalization-group method<nbsp><cite|Balaban1987RG>; the
  precise branch rules below are part of the present construction.

  Let <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>=<around|{|-R,\<ldots\>,R|}><rsup|4>>. For a
  permutation <math|o> of the four coordinate directions, denote by
  <math|T<rsub|U>*<around|(|x\<to\>y;o|)>> the transport along the path which
  changes coordinates in order <math|o>. For a root
  <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>, put

  <\equation>
    g<rsub|x><rsup|z,o>=T<rsub|U>*<around|(|z\<to\>x;o|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|x><rsup|z,o>=<around|(|g<rsub|0><rsup|z,o>|)><rsup|-1>*g<rsub|x><rsup|z,o>.<label|ch04:axial-frame>
  </equation>

  The normalized frame satisfies <math|h<rsub|0><rsup|z,o>=1>. The
  unnormalized frame gives link coordinates
  <math|U<rsub|<around|(|x,\<mu\>|)>><rsup|z,o>=g<rsub|x><rsup|z,o>*U*<around|(|x,\<mu\>|)><around|(|g<rsub|x+e<rsub|\<mu\>>><rsup|z,o>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  Their distances from the identity are unchanged if the normalized frame is
  used instead, since that change conjugates every transformed link by the
  same matrix.

  A symmetric choice is possible if all relevant loop transports are small.
  For <math|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>> and orders <math|o,o<rsub|0>>, define
  the loop based at the patch center by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|x*y;o><rsup|o<rsub|0>>=g<rsub|x><rsup|0,o<rsub|0>>*T<rsub|U>*<around|(|x\<to\>y;o|)><around|(|g<rsub|y><rsup|0,o<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Its normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance from the identity is gauge
  invariant. Fix a small constant <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>\<gtr\>0>.
  When all these distances from the identity are strictly below
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>>, use the normalized minimizer, with
  <math|h<rsub|0>=1>, of

  <\equation>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|U><around|(|h|)>=<frac|1|24<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>|\|><rsup|2>>*<big|sum><rsub|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>><big|sum><rsub|o\<in\>S<rsub|4>>w*<around*|(|h<rsub|x>*T<rsub|U>*<around|(|x\<to\>y;o|)>*h<rsub|y><rsup|-1>|)>.<label|ch04:frame-energy>
  </equation>

  The test includes every order <math|o<rsub|0>>, so it does not privilege a
  preliminary axial gauge.

  If the test fails, use axial frames. Fix
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16> and call a pair
  <math|<around|(|z,o|)>> eligible when

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|e<text| internal to >\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|e><rsup|z,o>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*<around|(|2*R+1|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\><text|
    internal to >\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>.<label|ch04:axial-eligible>
  </equation>

  Choose uniformly among eligible pairs. On the minimizing branch give all
  pairs the same uniform mass, but let each pair specify the same minimizing
  frame. The redundant labels allow both branches to be described by one
  finite sum.

  The following local assertion ensures that these rules are well-defined.
  Its uniformity in the size of the patch is essential.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform frame choice><label|ch04:frame-lemma>There is a
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on the matrix
    group, for which the minimizer of <eqref|ch04:frame-energy> with
    <math|h<rsub|0>=1> is unique whenever the loop test holds. For every
    configuration and every fixed coordinate order, at least one root
    satisfies <eqref|ch04:axial-eligible> with
    <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=16>. These constants do not depend on
    <math|R>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We first record the geometric input. The nearest-point projection
    <math|\<pi\>> onto the compact smooth submanifold <math|G> is uniquely
    defined and smooth on a uniform tubular
    neighborhood<nbsp><cite-detail|Foote1984|p.<nbsp>153, Lemma>. Its
    derivative at <math|1> is the orthogonal projection
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|<gLie>>> onto the Lie algebra. Hence on a sufficiently
    small convex ball about <math|1>,

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><around|(|A|)>-\<pi\><around|(|B|)>-\<Pi\><rsub|<gLie>>*<around|(|A-B|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4><around|\<\|\|\>|A-B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>.<label|ch04:projection-bound>
    </equation>

    This is the mean-value estimate for <math|D*\<pi\>-\<Pi\><rsub|<gLie>>>,
    not a new projection theorem.

    Make one origin-based axial gauge change. On the tested branch all its
    route matrices <math|W<rsub|x*y;o>> obey
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|x*y;o>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d>,
    where <math|d> can be chosen arbitrarily small by reducing
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>>. Reversing a route gives its inverse
    and permutes the coordinate orders. A minimizer exists by compactness.
    Common left multiplication leaves the energy invariant, so minimization
    with the normalization <math|h<rsub|0>=1> also gives an unconstrained
    minimizer after allowing that common multiplier. Comparison with the
    constant frame, and the squared triangle inequality, give

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|av><rsub|x,y><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|x>-h<rsub|y>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4*d<rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Here and below <math|<math-up|av>> denotes the uniform average over the
    indicated finite set. Multiply all <math|h<rsub|x>> on the left by one
    group element so that <math|1> is nearest to their centroid
    <math|<wide|h|\<bar\>>=<math-up|av><rsub|x><space|0.17em>h<rsub|x>>. Then
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>2*d>.
    Coordinatewise minimization shows that <math|h<rsub|x>> is nearest to

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>=<math-up|av><rsub|y,o><space|0.17em>h<rsub|y>*W<rsub|x*y;o><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Including the diagonal term <math|y=x> does not invalidate this
    assertion: it adds a term whose maximum is attained at the old coordinate
    <math|h<rsub|x>>. Since <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>-<wide|h|\<bar\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d>,
    all feedback matrices lie within <math|3*d> of <math|1>. For small enough
    <math|d> they lie in the projection ball, and
    <math|h<rsub|x>=\<pi\><around|(|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>|)>>.

    Center two minimizers <math|h,k> in this way and set
    <math|u=max<rsub|x><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|x>-k<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>>.
    Their feedback differences satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|k|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>u,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|k|)>-<around|(|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-<wide|k|\<bar\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d*u.
    </equation*>

    The two centroids are normal to <math|G> at <math|1>, so
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|<gLie>>*<around|(|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-<wide|k|\<bar\>>|)>=0>.
    At an index attaining the maximum, <eqref|ch04:projection-bound> gives
    <math|u\<leqslant\><around|(|d+1/4|)>*u>. Taking <math|d\<leqslant\>1/4>
    forces <math|u=0>. Thus minimizers differ only by a common left
    multiplier, removed by <math|h<rsub|0>=1>. Only averages occur in the
    contraction, which explains why its constants do not grow with the patch.

    For the axial alternatives, apply the usual plaquette telescoping
    argument to a rectangular slice with <math|r> varying coordinates. Choose
    a layer perpendicular to the last coordinate whose internal plaquette sum
    is no greater than the average over layers. Use the axial construction on
    that layer, then extend it along the last coordinate, setting those links
    to one. Every remaining link differs from its transported link on the
    chosen layer by a product of intervening plaquettes. The triangle
    inequality for the normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance and conjugation
    invariance yield, writing <math|n=2*R+1>,

    <\equation*>
      \<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|link>>*<around|(|X,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>\<leqslant\>n*<around|{|\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|link>>*<around|(|X<rsub|0>,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>+\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Induction with the bound <math|4*r*n*\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>>
    closes because <math|n*\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X<rsub|0>,U|)>\<leqslant\>\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>>.
    The construction follows the prescribed coordinate order; the selected
    layers specify a root. At <math|r=4> the bound is
    <eqref|ch04:axial-eligible> with constant <math|16>.
  </proof>

  Only smoothness of the nearest-point projection was used here. Analytic
  stationary-field estimates will need more; they are not consequences of
  this lemma alone.

  <section|Gauge-covariant block averaging and the exact
  kernel><label|ch04:kernel-section>

  Translate the preceding frame construction to every patch
  <math|L*y+\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>> centered at a coarse site <math|y>. Write
  <math|h<rsub|y,z>> for its normalized frame at offset <math|z>. For a
  coarse edge <math|e=<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>> define

  <\equation>
    Y<rsub|e,z>=h<rsub|y,z><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|L-1>U*<around|(|L*y+z+r*e<rsub|\<mu\>>,\<mu\>|)>|)>*h<rsub|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>,z><rsup|-1>.<label|ch04:connector>
  </equation>

  Both ends are expressed in the frames of the coarse endpoints. In
  particular, under a fine gauge transformation <math|g>, this connector
  becomes <math|g*<around|(|L*y|)>*Y<rsub|e,z>*g*<around|(|L*y+L*e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>>.

  Let <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>> be the <math|j>-fold convolution of the
  uniform probability on <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|L-1|)>/2>>. It is
  nonnegative, has sum one, is invariant under <math|H<rsub|4>>, and is
  supported in <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|j*<around|(|L-1|)>/2>\<subset\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>.
  Put <math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>><rsub|e>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*Y<rsub|e,z>>.
  The gauge-covariant block average, obtained by projecting the weighted
  connector average to the group, minimizes

  <\equation>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|e><around|(|u|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*w*<around|(|Y<rsub|e,z>*u<rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>u\<in\>G.<label|ch04:predictor-cost>
  </equation>

  Since all matrices <math|Y<rsub|e,z>> and <math|u> are unitary,

  <\equation*>
    2*n<rsub|G>*\<cal-P\><rsub|e><around|(|u|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>\<\|\|\>Y<rsub|e,z>-<wide|Y|\<bar\>><rsub|e>\<\|\|\><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|Y|\<bar\>><rsub|e>-u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the minimizers are precisely the nearest group points to
  <math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>><rsub|e>>. If the minimum is less than
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>, its distance to <math|G> is
  less than <math|<sqrt|2*n<rsub|G>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>>.
  Choose the cutoff so that this lies within the tubular neighborhood used in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:frame-lemma>. The block average is then unique,
  independently of the number of connectors or the weights.

  On this branch set <math|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>> to that minimizer
  and assign uniform mass to redundant block average labels
  <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>. If the test fails, let label <math|z>
  select <math|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>=Y<rsub|e,z>> with probability
  <math|w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>>. Equality with either cutoff belongs to
  the alternative branch; the strict inequalities defining the minimizing
  branches are part of the convention.

  Collect all frame and block average choices in a finite label
  <math|\<alpha\>> and denote their joint probability by
  <math|\<lambda\>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\>|)>>. Frame choices are made first,
  followed by conditionally independent block average choices. Thus

  <\equation>
    \<lambda\>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\>|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>\<lambda\>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\>|)>=1.<label|ch04:tag-law>
  </equation>

  The frame lemma supplies an eligible choice whenever it is needed.

  Fix the block penalty <math|b\<gtr\>0>. For <math|\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0>,
  let

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|u|)>=<frac|e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*w<around|(|u|)>>|<big|int><rsub|G>e<rsup|-\<gamma\>*w<around|(|v|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*h<around|(|v|)>>.
  </equation*>

  This is a probability density on <math|G> with respect to Haar measure.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Exact block map><label|ch04:block-map>For
    <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>, define the kernel and its action on fine
    densities by

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|k<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|(|U,V|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>\<lambda\>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|e<text|
      coarse>>q<rsub|b*\<beta\>>*<around*|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\>|)><rsup|-1>|)>,<eq-number><label|ch04:kernel>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<cal-B\><rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>>*f|)><around|(|V|)>>|<cell|=<big|int>k<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|(|U,V|)>*f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U,<eq-number><label|ch04:block-operator>>>>>
    </align>

    where <math|d*U> and <math|d*V> denote product probability Haar measure
    on their respective link spaces.
  </definition>

  For fixed <math|\<beta\>> this is a bounded Borel kernel. The tests are
  Borel, unique minimizers depend continuously on their data within the
  uniqueness domain, and all other choices involve finitely many continuous
  path products and Borel eligibility tests. At a branch boundary continuity
  is not asserted.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Normalization and covariance><label|ch04:kernel-properties>The
    kernel is nonnegative and normalized in the coarse variable:

    <\equation>
      <big|int>k<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>>*<around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=1.<label|ch04:kernel-normalization>
    </equation>

    It is covariant under fine gauge transformations and the induced coarse
    gauge transformations <math|y\<mapsto\>g*<around|(|L*y|)>>. It is also
    covariant under signed coordinate permutations and corresponding
    fine/coarse translations. For every integrable fine density <math|f>,

    <\equation>
      <big|int>\<cal-B\><rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>>*f*<space|0.17em>d*V=<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*U.<label|ch04:exact-integral>
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For normalization, integrate each coarse-link factor using right
    invariance of Haar measure, then sum <eqref|ch04:tag-law>. Fubini gives
    <eqref|ch04:exact-integral>, with absolute integrability following from
    the same computation applied to <math|<around|\||f|\|>>. These are
    elementary applications of Haar invariance, rather than estimates on the
    renormalization group. For covariance, gauge transformations conjugate
    rooted loop tests and transform the endpoints of <eqref|ch04:connector>;
    signed lattice isometries permute roots, orders and offsets and invert
    reversed links. The objective functions and eligibility tests are
    unchanged. Uniqueness on the minimizing branches, and reindexing of the
    finite alternatives, then give the stated covariance of the kernel.
  </proof>

  Probabilistically, if <math|U> has density <math|f/<big|int>f> with
  <math|f\<geqslant\>0>, one first draws <math|\<alpha\>>, and then draws
  independent <math|R<rsub|e>> of density <math|q<rsub|b*\<beta\>>> and sets
  <math|V<rsub|e>=R<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>>. The resulting
  coarse density is <math|\<cal-B\><rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>>*f/<big|int>f>.
  This description includes all branches. Exactness means preservation of
  this integral and law; it does not mean that the coarse density remains of
  Wilson form.

  <section|A first-power bound for the actual block
  average><label|ch04:predictor-geometry>

  The later tree comparison needs the block average on both of its branches,
  including configurations far from the identity. Let
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>> be the sum of fine plaquette
  distances from the identity in the lifted macro-box of radius
  <math|<around|(|j+7|)>*L> centered at <math|L*y>. Write
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|q>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>*\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>>,
  counting periodic lifts with their multiplicities. For a coarse plaquette
  <math|p> based near <math|y>, we shall prove

  <\equation>
    \<chi\><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>\<partial\>*p>\<chi\>*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>+C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|q>
    <big|sum><rsub|y>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j>.<label|ch04:macro-comparison>
  </equation>

  The constants are independent of the block ratio and period. The
  first-power estimate is essential: a square-root consequence of a
  squared-energy comparison would not give the factor needed here.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|First-power block average comparison><label|ch04:predictor-comparison>Fix
    the smoothing order <math|j> and frame eligibility constants. For the
    block average of <eqref|ch04:predictor-cost>, every choice of
    positive-mass frame and block average tags satisfies
    <eqref|ch04:macro-comparison>, with <math|C<rsub|j>,K<rsub|j>>
    independent of <math|L> and the period, whenever the coarse period is at
    least <math|100*<around|(|j+8|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We first record the estimate for a minimizing frame on a finite set of
    <math|N> points. Write <math|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>> for its path
    transports, with reverse paths paired among the labels <math|\<alpha\>>,
    and put <math|b<rsub|i>=<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>\<chi\><around|(|W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>>|)>>
    and <math|b=<math-up|av><rsub|i><space|0.17em>b<rsub|i>>. If <math|h>
    minimizes the averaged squared-distance energy, then some <math|c\<in\>G>
    satisfies

    <\equation>
      <math-up|av><rsub|i><space|0.17em>\<chi\>*<around|(|h<rsub|i>*c<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*b.<label|ch04:frame-first-power>
    </equation>

    Here the constant is independent of <math|N> and the number of path
    labels. To verify this, comparison with the constant frame and
    <math|\<chi\><rsup|2>/2\<leqslant\>\<chi\>> give
    <math|<math-up|av><rsub|i,j><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|i>-h<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*b>.
    Let <math|m=<math-up|av><rsub|j><space|0.17em>h<rsub|j>> and
    <math|m<rsub|i>=<math-up|av><rsub|j,\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>h<rsub|j>*W<rsub|i*j*\<alpha\>><rsup|-1>>.
    Varying one frame coordinate shows that <math|h<rsub|i>> is a nearest
    group point to <math|m<rsub|i>>, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|m<rsub|i>-m|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|n<rsub|G>>*b<rsub|i>>.
    The diagonal self-term causes no problem: freezing its second endpoint
    adds a nonnegative squared distance from the identity which is zero at
    the minimizer.

    When <math|b> is sufficiently small, choose a nearest group point
    <math|c> to <math|m>. The mean-square bound puts <math|m> in a fixed
    tubular neighborhood of <math|c>. The smooth nearest-point projection of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:frame-lemma> is uniformly Lipschitz there. For
    <math|b<rsub|i>> smaller than a fixed constant it therefore gives
    <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|h<rsub|i>*c<rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*b<rsub|i>>.
    For the other indices, <math|\<chi\>\<leqslant\>2\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*b<rsub|i>>.
    Averaging proves <eqref|ch04:frame-first-power>. If <math|b> is not
    small, the same conclusion follows from <math|\<chi\>\<leqslant\>2> after
    enlarging <math|C<rsub|G>>.

    For a coordinate-path gauge on a box of side <math|q> and
    <math|N=q<rsup|4>> points, path counting gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|x,y>\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|x\<to\>y>|)>\<leqslant\>4*N*q*<big|sum><rsub|e<text|
      in box>>\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, in each coordinate segment the mixed coordinates of its endpoints
    are uniformly distributed; summing the coordinate line first bounds its
    mean by <math|q/N> times the bond sum. Summing the at most four segments
    gives the formula. Apply <eqref|ch04:frame-first-power> after any
    comparison gauge <math|H>. For a minimizing frame <math|f>, this gives a
    constant rotation <math|c> such that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\>*<around|(|c<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|i>*H<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*q*E*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>>,
    where <math|E> is the sum of bond distances from the identity. For an
    eligible axial fallback frame, apply the same path count to the pure
    gauge <math|f*H<rsup|-1>>, whose bond sum is at most
    <math|E*<around|(|f\<cdot\>U|)>+E*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>>. The
    eligibility rule bounds the first term by <math|K<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*q>
    times the sum of plaquette distances from the identity. Thus both frame
    branches obey the same bound with this additional term.

    Choose an axial gauge <math|H> on the macro-box of radius
    <math|<around|(|j+7|)>*L>. The axial estimate in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:frame-lemma> gives
    <math|E*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>.
    On each translated frame patch, whose side is
    <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>>, the preceding bound gives rotations
    <math|c<rsub|z>> with

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>\<chi\>*<around|(|c<rsub|z><rsup|-1>*f<rsub|z,i>*H<rsub|L*z+i><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    The convolution weights obey <math|w<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|av>>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-4>>:
    condition on all but the first of the <math|j> uniform points. For a
    fixed sum there is at most one possible first point. The two endpoint
    errors in an averaged connector consequently cost at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>. For the
    middle straight path, each of its <math|L> translations is injective, so
    its unweighted sum is at most <math|L*E*<around|(|H\<cdot\>U|)>>. Its
    weighted sum has the same bound <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>.

    For any <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\>G> the selected group-valued block
    average satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\>*<around|(|Q*u<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*max
      <around|{|1,\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>><rsup|-1>|}>*<big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<chi\>*<around|(|Y<rsub|i>*u<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    On the minimizing branch this follows by comparison of the nearest group
    point to the matrix centroid with <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>>. On the fallback
    branch, the minimum cost is at least <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>,
    whereas the cost at <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>> is at most the displayed
    weighted first moment, and <math|\<chi\>\<leqslant\>2>. This holds for
    every selected fallback connector, however small its probability. Use
    <math|u<rsub|\<ast\>>=c<rsub|z>*c<rsub|z+e<rsub|\<mu\>>><rsup|-1>>. The
    four rotations cancel around a coarse plaquette, proving its block
    average curvature bound. Replacing its four block average links by
    <math|V> adds precisely their four mismatch distances from the identity.
    Finally, a fixed fine plaquette belongs to only
    <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|1|)>> macro-boxes centered at points of
    <math|L<ZZ><rsup|4>>; count its periodic lifts with multiplicity. This
    proves the second bound in <eqref|ch04:macro-comparison> as well.
  </proof>

  <section|Small fields and density coordinates><label|ch04:density-coordinates>

  The cutoff used to distinguish small from large fields must remain fixed
  while the actual coupling is varied for tuning. To express that
  distinction, let <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>1> and use the
  reference values

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>|<cell|=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>,>|<cell|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>>|<cell|=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|t<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>*M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>,>|<cell|B<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>.<eq-number><label|ch04:thresholds>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|\<tau\><rsub|m>=log D<rsub|m>> is the logarithmic scale from
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ch01:scales>. We use these formulas only where
  <math|0\<less\>t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>. The actual coupling ranges in
  <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>=<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|]>>.
  The reference value and threshold are not recomputed when that coupling
  changes. Their eventual compatibility with the scale schedule is a later
  choice.

  At the present scale write <math|\<Lambda\>=\<Lambda\><rsub|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>>,
  and let <math|<math-up|Bad><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> be the sites incident to
  at least one plaquette with <math|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>\<gtr\>t<rsub|m>>.
  A set <math|X> is small-field if <math|X\<cap\><math-up|Bad><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>>;
  write its indicator as <math|h<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>>. Thus every plaquette
  meeting <math|X> must have small distance from the identity. This
  definition is gauge invariant and involves a closed condition on
  small-field sites.

  We keep two kinds of local corrections.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Regular and large-field activities><label|ch04:activities>A
    regular activity consists of a support <math|X<rsub|i>>, an anchor
    <math|o<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>>, and a real Borel gauge-invariant
    function <math|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> local on <math|X<rsub|i>>.
    It vanishes at the flat configuration and has the required number of
    ordered left derivatives on small-field configurations.

    A large-field activity has an exclusion support <math|Y<rsub|i>>, a
    nonempty prescribed set <math|I<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bad>>\<subset\>Y<rsub|i>>,
    and an additional Borel gauge-invariant eligibility test
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>>. Its real Borel value
    <math|K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|lf>>> depends on links internal to
    <math|Y<rsub|i>> and on the indicators of large-field sites in
    <math|Y<rsub|i>>, and vanishes outside

    <\equation>
      <around|{|U:<math-up|Bad><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>\<cap\>Y<rsub|i>=I<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bad>>|}>\<cap\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>.<label|ch04:eligibility>
    </equation>

    The extra test reads the same links and large-field indicators.
  </definition>

  Neither activity is assumed nonnegative. Activities may be indexed by a
  countable set; their summability will be part of the norm conditions.

  There are two different meanings of support in this definition. The set
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> determines exclusion and coverage: selected
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> will be disjoint. But deciding whether a site belongs to
  <math|<math-up|Bad><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> reads plaquettes meeting that
  site, whose links can extend beyond <math|Y<rsub|i>>. The <em|full
  dependence region> includes those links as well as the internal links and
  any links read by other tests. Two disjoint exclusion supports can have
  overlapping full dependence regions. A connected expansion based on
  dependence must use the latter, whereas the exact-cover sum below uses the
  former. Disjoint exclusion supports do not imply probabilistic
  independence.

  <section|The prescribed leading action><label|ch04:leading-action>

  The running coupling is a coordinate relative to a prescribed leading
  action. Its construction will come from Gaussian integration and local
  matching; here we specify the form of that coordinate without claiming the
  estimates needed to select it.

  At scale <math|m>, fix constants <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>,c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>,R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>\<gtr\>0>
  with <math|16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|clip><rsub|t><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*r<rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|r\<leqslant\>t,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*r,>|<cell|r\<gtr\>t,>>>>><eq-number><label|ch04:clip>>>|<row|<cell|\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>><rsub|m,x><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=\<b-1\>*<around*|{|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>*e<rsup|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>/R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>><text|
    for every >y,\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>|}>.<eq-number><label|ch04:relaxed-test>>>>>
  </align>

  The distance is periodic and measured in lattice units. Although written as
  a test on every plaquette, <eqref|ch04:relaxed-test> reads only a finite
  neighborhood: <math|\<chi\>\<leqslant\>2> makes all tests with
  <math|t<rsub|m>*e<rsup|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>/R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>>\<geqslant\>2>
  automatic. Any claim of locality must include the remaining tested
  plaquettes in its dependence region.

  For each orientation <math|\<frak-p\>=<around|(|\<mu\>,\<nu\>|)>>, fix a
  summable linear combination of transported curvatures,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|\<frak-p\>>*<around|(|U,x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*<around|(|U,x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>> is a plaquette curvature
  transported to <math|x>, as in <eqref|ch02:transported-curvature>. Also
  specify local real invariant multilinear expressions
  <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|q|)>>*<around|(|U,x|)>>, <math|q=2,3,4>, in
  such curvatures, each multiplied by the small-field indicator of its
  declared footprint. The footprint contains the paths, plaquettes and
  anchor; its event indicator may read additional adjacent plaquettes. Assume
  the coefficient sums converge absolutely at the fixed finite volume. Set

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|P<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>>|<cell|=\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|x,\<frak-p\>><around*|[|<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|m>><around|(|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|x;\<frak-p\>>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>><rsub|m,x><around|(|U|)>*<around|\||\<cal-L\><rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|\<frak-p\>>*<around|(|U,x|)>|\|><rsup|2>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|x,i><around|(|A<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|3|)>>+A<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>|)>*<around|(|U,x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|x,i>A<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>*<around|(|U,x|)>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|P<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|<around|\||H<rsub|4>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|h\<in\>H<rsub|4>>P<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<beta\>,h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>.<eq-number><label|ch04:path-action>>>>>
  </align>

  The coefficients are held fixed as <math|\<beta\>> varies. Thus this action
  is affine in <math|\<beta\>>. Its ultimate normalization requires the
  quadratic coefficient in the <math|\<beta\>>-dependent part to be the
  curvature square, and no such coefficient in the separate quadratic
  correction. Those are matching conditions, not consequences of the
  displayed form.

  The function called clip in <eqref|ch04:clip> is not a smooth cutoff. At
  <math|r=t> it drops from <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*t<rsup|2>/2> to
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<rsup|2>> immediately to the right. Effective
  density coordinates are therefore Borel functions, not globally
  differentiable functions of the links. Derivative estimates refer to the
  regular expressions on small-field domains with the event indicators held
  fixed. In particular, a derivative with respect to the actual coupling does
  not differentiate the threshold or its indicator.

  <section|The exact-cover representation and its
  norms><label|ch04:density-norms>

  The activity labels and their exclusion supports are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ch04:activities>. Their full dependence regions
  also contain the links needed for every test; the two sorts of support must
  remain distinct in the sums below.

  Recall that <math|Y<rsub|i>> is an exclusion support and
  <math|I<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bad>>> its prescribed set of large-field
  sites, as in Definition<nbsp><reference|ch04:activities>. Let
  <math|\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> be the finite subsets
  <math|\<frak-a\>> of large-field activity indices such that the
  <math|Y<rsub|i>>, <math|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>, are pairwise disjoint and
  <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>I<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bad>>=<math-up|Bad><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>.
  These are exact covers of the large-field sites. They do not select a
  preferred partition into connected components.

  A density coordinate tuple <math|\<rho\>> consists of <math|\<beta\>>, a
  real scalar <math|v<rsub|<math-up|sc>>> per site, the regular activities
  and the large-field activities, with the fixed action
  <eqref|ch04:path-action>. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|V<rsub|\<rho\>><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=P<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|U|)>-v<rsub|<math-up|sc>><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\>|)><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|-V<rsub|\<rho\>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-a\>\<in\>\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|lf>><around|(|U|)>.<eq-number><label|ch04:effective-density>>>>>
  </align>

  The empty product is one. Because each <math|I<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bad>>>
  is nonempty, if there are no large-field sites the only exact cover is
  empty and the sum is one. Otherwise a single support may cover two regions,
  or two disjoint supports may cover them separately; both alternatives
  belong in the sum. A tuple satisfying the definitions alone need not give a
  positive density. Positivity follows when a later construction proves that
  its density equals an exact block integral of a positive input.

  Fix an integer derivative order <math|q\<geqslant\>2>, a nonnegative
  spatial exponent <math|\<varpi\>>, a support-weight exponent
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, and the threshold <math|t<rsub|m>>. For a regular
  activity anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>>, use the seminorm

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>=sup<rsub|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>q>
    sup<rsub|U,A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|k>><around|\||D<rsub|A<rsub|1>><rsup|L>*\<cdots\>*D<rsub|A<rsub|k>><rsup|L>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|U|)>|\|>,<label|ch04:local-seminorm>
  </equation>

  where <math|U> is small-field on <math|X<rsub|i>> and
  <math|<around|\||<around|(|A<rsub|r>|)><rsub|e>|\|><rsub|<gLie>>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsup|->,o<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsup|\<varpi\>>>.
  The left derivatives are those of Section<nbsp><reference|ch02:bounds>.
  Required regularity includes existence and uniform bounds at the boundary
  of the small-field domain, interpreted through the regular local
  expression; no derivative of <math|h<rsub|X<rsub|i>>> is included.

  For a set <math|X\<subset\>\<Lambda\>>, let
  <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>> be the minimum number of
  radius-<math|B<rsub|m>> periodic balls whose union is connected in the
  nearest-neighbor graph and covers <math|X>. Set
  <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>. The
  covering union may contain additional sites. Its exponential weight
  penalizes extended supports without requiring a support itself to be
  connected. Choose majorants <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>
  and <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|U><around|\||K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|lf>><around|(|U|)>|\|>>,
  and define

  <\equation>
    <\eqsplit>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-E\><rsub|m><around|(|\<rho\>|)>>|<cell|=sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\>>
      <big|sum><rsub|i:o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-K\><rsub|m><around|(|\<rho\>|)>>|<cell|=sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\>>
      <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i>.>>>>
    </eqsplit>

    <label|ch04:weighted-sums>
  </equation>

  The first is an anchored sum: each regular activity is counted at one site.
  The second counts a large-field activity at every site of its exclusion
  support. Replacing an anchored sum by a containing-site sum requires a
  bound for the number of possible anchors; it is not a harmless notational
  change. Likewise, replacing <math|Y<rsub|i>> by its full dependence region
  changes the weight and requires a geometric estimate.

  Finite weighted sums ensure that <eqref|ch04:effective-density> is
  well-defined at fixed volume. Indeed

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-E\><rsub|m>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|lf>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-K\><rsub|m>,
  </equation*>

  and the absolute sum over all finite selections is at most
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+k<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>exp
  <around|(|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-K\><rsub|m>|)>>. Restricting to
  disjoint exact covers only lowers this bound. The estimate is a
  finite-volume convergence statement, not a uniform bound on the entire
  partition function.

  <section|Conditions for a one-step induction><label|ch04:admissibility>

  The preceding coordinates describe possible densities; their smallness
  conditions will describe the inputs of a one-step theorem. There are
  additional normalization conditions. On a nonwrapping support, choose
  centered coordinate lifts <math|\<xi\><rsub|o<rsub|i>><around|(|y|)>>. For
  every pair of affine link fields of the form

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>>=u<rsub|\<mu\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>\<xi\><rsub|o<rsub|i>><around|(|y|)><rsup|\<nu\>>*v<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>,<space|2em>u<rsub|\<mu\>>,v<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>\<in\><gLie>,
  </equation*>

  require the Hessian at zero of <math|A\<mapsto\>E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|exp
  A|)>> to vanish on that pair. Together with
  <math|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|1|)>=0> and the vanishing
  linear term, this excludes the low-order local contributions assigned to
  the leading action. The first derivative vanishes for a gauge-invariant
  activity by invariance under constant gauge transformations: each linear
  coefficient would otherwise be an invariant linear functional on the simple
  Lie algebra. The meaning of the affine Hessian condition is fixed in the
  chosen local coordinates; no coordinate lift is imposed on a wrapping
  support by this definition.

  One may prescribe the intended size bounds

  <\equation>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around*|(|-<around|(|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|1/5>|)>,<label|ch04:activity-bounds>
  </equation>

  with the reference quantities of <eqref|ch04:thresholds>. In an induction,
  <math|q,\<varpi\>,\<sigma\>> may depend on a regularity-stage index
  <math|J<rsub|m>>; that index is different from the scale <math|m>. For now
  they remain specified input parameters. Neither
  <eqref|ch04:activity-bounds> nor the density identity proves that the block
  map preserves the class.

  Parameter continuity is also stated at fixed coordinates. A curve
  <math|u\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsub|u>> has fixed supports, anchors, prescribed
  large-field sets, extra tests and event indicators. Its coupling and scalar
  are continuous. At each <math|u<rsub|0>>, differences of regular
  derivatives and large-field values have majorants
  <math|e<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>,k<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>> satisfying

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|1em><around|(|u\<to\>u<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Both sums here are over all indices, at a fixed finite volume. This
  continuity condition is strong enough to control the density and its
  integral; it differentiates no moving large-field boundary.

  The analytic task remaining is now precise. Starting with a tuple at the
  fine level, one must construct a coarse tuple whose density is exactly
  <math|\<cal-B\><rsup|<around|(|m|)>><rsub|\<beta\>><math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\>|)>>,
  whose coupling has the claimed adjustment, and whose activities obey the
  coarse conditions. A class defined as the outputs reachable from a Wilson
  starting density can only be introduced after that construction. It must
  not be confused with the much larger set of coordinate tuples satisfying
  the displayed norm bounds.

  <assign|chapter-nr|4>

  <chapter|Gaussian kernels and stationary backgrounds><label|ch05:gaussian>

  The quadratic approximation serves two purposes. It identifies the
  covariance of the fluctuations eliminated by a block transformation, and it
  supplies the inverse needed to construct a stationary background for the
  nonlinear action. These uses require different estimates. Exact matrix
  identities hold on each finite lattice; uniform locality requires bounds
  which survive changes of the lattice size and of the blocking history.

  We first fix the normalization of the exact identities. We then isolate the
  additional hypotheses for uniform analytic continuation and for a
  stationary background. The general block transformation algebra is
  standard; see Baªaban\UFeldman\UKnörrer\UTrubowitz
  <cite|BalabanFeldmanKnoerrerTrubowitz2016>. The construction of local
  propagators and backgrounds for lattice gauge theory originates in
  Baªaban's work <cite|Balaban1984Propagators|Balaban1985Variational>. Those
  references explain the method. Their results cannot be applied to a
  different averaging map without checking its hypotheses.

  <section|Lattice differential forms and zero modes><label|ch05:forms>

  In this chapter distances and Fourier variables are in lattice units. Let
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>=<around|(|<ZZ>/s<ZZ>|)><rsup|4>>. A lattice
  <math|r>-form assigns an element of <math|<gLie>> to each site and each
  increasing <math|r>-tuple of coordinate directions. The scalar product is
  the sum over sites and increasing tuples, using the Lie algebra metric of
  <eqref|ch01:metric>. In particular there is no volume normalization in this
  scalar product. Write <math|\<Omega\><rsup|r>*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>;<gLie>|)>>
  for this finite-dimensional space. The forward difference is
  <math|\<nabla\><rsub|\<mu\>>*u<around|(|x|)>=u*<around|(|x+e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>-u<around|(|x|)>>,
  and the exterior derivative is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d<rsub|r>*u|)><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*\<mu\><rsub|r>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|r><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i>*\<nabla\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|i>>*u<rsub|\<mu\><rsub|0>*\<cdots\><wide|\<mu\><rsub|i>|^>\<cdots\>*\<mu\><rsub|r>>.
  </equation*>

  Commutation of the differences gives <math|d<rsub|r+1>*d<rsub|r>=0>. An
  adjoint is always taken for the stated counting scalar products.

  A linearized gauge transformation of a one-form <math|a> is
  <math|a\<mapsto\>a+d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>>. If <math|H> is a positive
  self-adjoint operator on two-forms, the quadratic action and its kinetic
  operator are

  <\equation>
    S<rsub|0><around|(|a|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,H*d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>K=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*d<rsub|1>.<label|ch05:kinetic>
  </equation>

  Thus <math|K> vanishes on gradients. It also vanishes on constant
  one-forms. On a periodic lattice the latter are harmonic modes and are not
  gradients of periodic scalar functions. Removing gauge directions alone
  therefore does not make <math|K> invertible.

  For <math|p\<in\><around|(|2*\<pi\>/s|)><ZZ><rsup|4>/<around|(|2*\<pi\><ZZ>|)><rsup|4>>
  our Fourier convention is

  <\equation>
    <wide|u|^><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>>e<rsup|-i*p\<cdot\>x>*u<around|(|x|)>,<space|1em>u<around|(|x|)>=s<rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|p>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*<wide|u|^><around|(|p|)>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=s<rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|p><around|\||<wide|u|^><around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>.<label|ch05:fourier>
  </equation>

  Set <math|q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|\<mu\>>>-1> and
  <math|\<lambda\><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>>.
  On real momenta this is <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>>.
  The symbol of <math|d<rsub|r>> is exterior multiplication by <math|q>. In
  particular, on one-forms,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||q\<wedge\>a|\|><rsup|2>+<around|\||<wide|q|\<bar\>><math-plus|\<lrcorner\>>a|\|><rsup|2>=\<lambda\><around|(|p|)><around|\||a|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Here contraction denotes the adjoint of exterior multiplication. For
  <math|p\<ne\>0> this gives the orthogonal decomposition into a gradient and
  its orthogonal complement. At <math|p=0> the whole one-form space is
  harmonic.

  Whenever a Gaussian probability measure is used below, its precision is
  strictly positive on the specified coordinate space. One may work on a
  fixed slice with the harmonic modes removed, or add a positive regulator. A
  massless covariance at zero momentum is not defined by writing
  <math|1/\<lambda\><around|(|0|)>>. Conversely, an identity between
  quadratic forms can remain valid on a space where no normalized massless
  Gaussian exists.

  <section|The exact Gaussian block transformation><label|ch05:schur>

  Let <math|E<rsub|f>,E<rsub|c>> be real finite-dimensional inner-product
  spaces, <math|Q:E<rsub|f>\<to\>E<rsub|c>> a linear block map, and
  <math|b\<gtr\>0> the block penalty. Suppose initially that
  <math|K<rsub|f>\<gtr\>0> on <math|E<rsub|f>>. Define

  <\equation>
    A=K<rsub|f>+b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*Q,<space|2em>W=A<rsup|-1>*b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>K<rsub|c>=b*I-b<rsup|2>*Q*A<rsup|-1>*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>.<label|ch05:schur-data>
  </equation>

  These formulas describe the normalized Gaussian problem. When the physical
  quadratic exponent is <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|A<rsub|f>,K<rsub|f>*A<rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>+b*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q*A<rsub|f>-V|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|)>/2>,
  the variables here are <math|a=<sqrt|\<beta\>>*A<rsub|f>> and
  <math|v=<sqrt|\<beta\>>*V>. In unscaled link logarithms the fluctuation
  covariance is <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|K<rsub|f>+b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*Q|)><rsup|-1>>.
  Thus the block parameter remains <math|b> after normalization, although the
  original block kernel has penalty <math|b*\<beta\>>.

  The map <math|W> sends a coarse field to the stationary fine field. The
  fluctuation covariance is <math|A<rsup|-1>>. The Schur-complement formulas
  give

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|K<rsub|c>>|<cell|=<around|(|b<rsup|-1>*I+Q*K<rsub|f><rsup|-1>*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|-1>,<eq-number><label|ch05:coarse-cov>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|a,K<rsub|f>*a|\<rangle\>>+b*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v,K<rsub|c>*v|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|a-W*v,A*<around|(|a-W*v|)>|\<rangle\>>.<eq-number><label|ch05:completion>>>>>
  </align>

  These are the usual finite-dimensional Gaussian identities, or the real
  quadratic specialization of the block transformation identities in
  <cite|BalabanFeldmanKnoerrerTrubowitz2016>; no separate proof is needed.
  The accompanying normalization is

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|E<rsub|f>>e<rsup|-<around|\<langle\>|a,K<rsub|f>*a|\<rangle\>>/2-b*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>*<space|0.17em>d*a=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|dim
    E<rsub|f>/2><around|(|det A|)><rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|-<around|\<langle\>|v,K<rsub|c>*v|\<rangle\>>/2>.
  </equation*>

  The Lebesgue measure here comes from the specified inner product. In a
  normalized block kernel there is also the factor
  <math|<around|(|b/2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|dim E<rsub|c>/2>>.

  There is an equivalent probabilistic statement. If <math|a> and
  <math|\<xi\>> are independent centered Gaussians with covariances
  <math|K<rsub|f><rsup|-1>> and <math|I>, then
  <math|v=Q*a+b<rsup|-1/2>*\<xi\>> has covariance
  <math|b<rsup|-1>*I+Q*K<rsub|f><rsup|-1>*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>>. Conditional on
  <math|v>, the fine field has mean <math|W*v> and covariance
  <math|A<rsup|-1>>.

  The identities <eqref|ch05:schur-data> and <eqref|ch05:completion> also
  hold for <math|K<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>0>, provided <math|A> is invertible.
  Their derivation uses no inverse of <math|K<rsub|f>>. In this case
  <eqref|ch05:coarse-cov> and the joint probability interpretation are
  unavailable unless the remaining null directions have been treated. In the
  gauge problem, every use of this extension must specify the slice or
  quotient on which <math|A> is invertible.

  The useful consequences, with the same domains and adjoints, are

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|f>*W=Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|c>,<space|2em>Q*W=I-b<rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>,<space|2em>W<rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|f>*W+b*<around|(|Q*W-I|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|Q*W-I|)>=K<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  The last identity says that the stationary fine action together with its
  block penalty is exactly the coarse quadratic action.

  <section|Averaging and the Fourier alias formula><label|ch05:alias>

  To check the scaling factor in the covariance recursion, take
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L*s>> as fine lattice and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>>
  as coarse lattice, with odd <math|L\<geqslant\>3>. Let
  <math|w<around|(|z|)>> be a finitely supported real averaging kernel of
  total mass one, periodized on the fine lattice, and use

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|M*a|)><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<around|(|z|)>*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|L-1>a<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|L*y+z+r*e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The inner sum is a sum along a link of length <math|L>, not its average.
  Define the symbols

  <\equation*>
    <wide|w|^><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<around|(|z|)>*e<rsup|i*\<theta\>\<cdot\>z>,<space|2em>r<rsub|L><around|(|t|)>=L<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|L-1>e<rsup|i*r*t>.
  </equation*>

  The sign in <math|<wide|w|^>> is chosen for this formula for <math|M>. The
  aliases of a coarse momentum <math|p> are
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|z>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/L>, with one
  representative for each <math|z\<in\><around|(|<ZZ>/L<ZZ>|)><rsup|4>>.

  Suppose an auxiliary, translation-invariant Gaussian one-form has diagonal
  covariance symbol <math|<math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>|)>>
  in the direction index. Color indices are suppressed and are unchanged by
  this calculation. The symbol of the covariance of
  <math|M*a+b<rsup|-1/2>*\<xi\>> is

  <\equation>
    c<rprime|'><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=b<rsup|-1>+L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|z<math-up|mod><space|0.17em>L><wide|w|^><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*<wide|w|^>*<around|(|-\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*r<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>*r<rsub|L>*<around|(|-\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>*c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>.<label|ch05:alias-recursion>
  </equation>

  This formula is exact for a positive regulated covariance. It can also be
  used for nonzero modes of a massless symbol with the zero mode handled
  separately.

  For clarity, the normalization check is short. Substitution of
  <eqref|ch05:fourier> into <math|M*a> gives
  <math|<wide|M*a|^><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|-4>*<big|sum><rsub|z>L*<wide|w|^><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*r<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>*<wide|a|^><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>>.
  Fine Fourier covariances carry the volume <math|<around|(|L*s|)><rsup|4>>,
  whereas coarse ones carry <math|s<rsup|4>>. Consequently the covariance
  factor is <math|L<rsup|-8>*L<rsup|2>*L<rsup|4>=L<rsup|-2>>. This is the
  factor used in <eqref|ch05:alias-recursion>.

  The particular smoothing in the supplied construction uses

  <\equation*>
    <wide|w|^><rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|4><around*|(|L<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||r|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|L-1|)>/2>e<rsup|i*r*\<theta\><rsub|\<nu\>>>|)>,<space|2em><wide|w|^><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<wide|w|^><rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|j> is the integer smoothing order. Centering makes
  <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|L>> even on real and complex momenta. The recurrence
  then contains <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsup|2*j>>.
  This choice fixes an auxiliary covariance. Identification with the
  quadratic form of a particular gauge-equivariant block map additionally
  requires checking that its linearization differs from <math|M> only by an
  appropriate gradient, and that the chosen treatment of zero modes agrees.

  <section|Gauge reduction and a regular curvature
  symbol><label|ch05:curvature>

  At a real nonzero momentum suppose <math|c<rsub|\<mu\>>\<gtr\>0> and put
  <math|w<rsub|\<mu\>>=c<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>>. Minimizing over a scalar
  gauge parameter gives

  <\equation>
    min<rsub|\<phi\>> <big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>w<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|\||a<rsub|\<mu\>>+q<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<phi\>|\|><rsup|2>=<frac|<with|math-display|true|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>>w<rsub|\<mu\>>*w<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|\||q<rsub|\<mu\>>*a<rsub|\<nu\>>-q<rsub|\<nu\>>*a<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|><rsup|2>>|<with|math-display|true|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>w<rsub|\<mu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|><rsup|2>>>.<label|ch05:quotient>
  </equation>

  It is the weighted orthogonal projection formula. Thus a diagonal two-form
  metric representing the quotient form is

  <\equation>
    <around|(|H<rsub|0>|)><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>=<around*|[|c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*c<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><frac|q<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*q<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>|c<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>>|]><rsup|-1>.<label|ch05:H0>
  </equation>

  Formula <eqref|ch05:H0> is initially an identity away from zero. Its
  regular extension at zero is a separate issue.

  Here is the cancellation used for that extension. Let <math|M> now denote
  the total dilation over several steps; it is a number, distinct from the
  averaging operator of the previous section. Put
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>=M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\>*<around|(|p/M|)>>.
  Suppose the accumulated covariance has the representation

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=<frac|A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>+G<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>,<space|1em>A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=F<around|(|p|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|-p<rsub|\<mu\>>/M|)>,<space|1em>B<rsub|\<mu\>>=A<rsub|\<mu\>>+\<lambda\><rsub|M>*G<rsub|\<mu\>>.
  </equation*>

  The remainder <math|G<rsub|\<mu\>>> collects the nonprincipal aliases and
  the accumulated block noise. Assume that
  <math|F,G<rsub|\<mu\>>,A<rsub|\<mu\>>,B<rsub|\<mu\>>> are holomorphic on a
  common neighborhood and that <math|F,A<rsub|\<mu\>>,B<rsub|\<mu\>>> do not
  vanish there. The elementary identity <math|q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>/M|)><rsup|-1>=M*q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|p/M|)>>,
  interpreted after multiplication to remove possible zeros, gives

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*F=M<rsup|2>*q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|p/M|)>*q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|-p/M|)>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently, if

  <\equation>
    h=F<rsup|-1>-<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>><frac|q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>*G<rsub|\<mu\>>|A<rsub|\<mu\>>*B<rsub|\<mu\>>>,<space|2em>\<lambda\><rsub|M>*h=<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>><frac|q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|-p|)>|B<rsub|\<mu\>>>,<label|ch05:pole-cancel>
  </equation>

  then <eqref|ch05:H0> becomes

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|H<rsub|0>|)><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=<around|(|h*B<rsub|\<mu\>>*B<rsub|\<nu\>>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The second equality in <eqref|ch05:pole-cancel> follows by subtracting
  <math|1/B<rsub|\<mu\>>> from <math|1/A<rsub|\<mu\>>>; it is not an
  additional assumption. If <math|h> is also nonvanishing, the last formula
  proves holomorphic extension through the apparent massless pole. When
  <math|F<around|(|0|)>=A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|0|)>=1>, it gives
  <math|H<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=I>.

  This calculation proves pole cancellation, not a uniform zero-free strip.
  For that stronger conclusion the nonprincipal aliases, accumulated noise,
  and reciprocal factors must be bounded uniformly over every admitted
  blocking history. These are precisely the estimates which cannot be
  replaced by finite-dimensional Gaussian algebra.

  One can modify the metric away from exact two-forms without changing the
  action. For <math|\<zeta\>\<geqslant\>0> define, at real momentum,

  <\equation>
    H<rsup|-1>=H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>+\<zeta\>*<around|(|d<rsub|2>*H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|d<rsub|2>*H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|)>.<label|ch05:metric-modification>
  </equation>

  If <math|H<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, this defines a positive <math|H>, and
  <math|H<rsup|-1>*H<rsub|0>*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>>, because
  <math|d<rsub|2>*d<rsub|1>=0>. Therefore
  <math|H*d<rsub|1>=H<rsub|0>*d<rsub|1>>. Positivity on all two-forms and
  agreement on exact two-forms are different assertions; this modification is
  useful because later estimates need both.

  <section|Uniform estimates over finite blocking
  histories><label|ch05:histories>

  We now verify the uniform reciprocal bounds required in
  <eqref|ch05:pole-cancel> for the actual averaging recurrence. A finite
  history consists of odd ratios <math|L<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,L<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>3>
  and smoothing orders <math|j<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,j<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>4>,
  with <math|j<rsub|h-1>-j<rsub|h>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>>. Set
  <math|M<rsub|h>=L<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*L<rsub|h>>, <math|M<rsub|0>=1>. The
  order used by the <math|h>th block map is its incoming order
  <math|j<rsub|h-1>>. Denote the centered box symbol
  <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|L>> of Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:alias> by
  <math|w<rsub|L>> in the following formulas.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform curvature metric><label|ch05:history-prop>Fix the final
    smoothing order <math|j=j<rsub|n>\<geqslant\>4> and the penalty
    <math|b\<gtr\>0>. Start with the nonzero-mode covariance
    <math|c<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=1/\<lambda\><around|(|p|)>> and
    iterate <eqref|ch05:alias-recursion> along any such finite history. The
    resulting curvature metric <math|H<rsub|0>> has a holomorphic, periodic
    extension to a common strip about real momenta, with uniformly bounded
    entries and inverse entries. On real momenta <math|H<rsub|0>> has uniform
    positive upper and lower bounds and <math|H<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=I>. The
    constants depend on <math|j,b>, but not on the history length, ratios or
    torus period.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The box composition identity <math|w<rsub|A>*<around|(|B*\<theta\>|)>*w<rsub|B><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=w<rsub|A*B><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>
    holds for odd <math|A,B>, by decomposing a centered interval of length
    <math|A*B> into centered intervals of length <math|B>. Thus the
    accumulated filter is

    <\equation>
      f<around|(|\<theta\>|)>=w<rsub|M<rsub|n>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>*<big|prod><rsub|<around|{|h:j<rsub|h-1>=j<rsub|h>+1|}>>w<rsub|M<rsub|h>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>.<label|ch05:history-filter>
    </equation>

    The directional filter is <math|f<around|(|\<theta\>|)>*r<rsub|M<rsub|n>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>:
    the link factors compose by <math|r<rsub|A>*<around|(|B*t|)>*r<rsub|B><around|(|t|)>=r<rsub|A*B><around|(|t|)>>.

    Here and below momentum estimates are on an enlarged cube
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>\<less\>\<pi\>+\<eta\>>,
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>>,
    for a sufficiently small fixed <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>. Write
    <math|M=M<rsub|n>> and choose centered alias representatives
    <math|z<rsub|\<mu\>>\<in\><around|[|-<around|(|M-1|)>/2,<around|(|M-1|)>/2|]>>.
    The finite geometric sum gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||r<rsub|M>*<around|(|<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>+2*\<pi\>*z<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>/M|)>|\|>,<space|1em><around*|\||M<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||r|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|M-1|)>/2>e<rsup|i*r*<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>+2*\<pi\>*z<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>/M>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|C|1+<around|\||z<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed the numerator of the geometric quotient is bounded, and
    <math|M*<around|\||e<rsup|i*<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>+2*\<pi\>*z<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>/M>-1|\|>>
    is bounded below by <math|c*<around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>|)>>
    for <math|z<rsub|\<mu\>>\<ne\>0>; the principal case is bounded directly
    by the finite sum. At the principal real alias the centered factor has a
    positive lower bound.

    The additional factors in <eqref|ch05:history-filter> have a uniform
    product bound. A factor of side <math|A\<leqslant\>M> has modulus at most
    <math|exp <around|(|C*\<eta\>*A/M|)>> on the cube evaluated at <math|p/M>
    or any other alias. The sides occurring in the product increase at least
    geometrically, so their sum divided by <math|M> is bounded. On the real
    principal alias the same factors have product bounded away from zero. To
    see this without multiplying a fixed lower bound infinitely often, use
    <math|<around|\||log w<rsub|A>*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*A/M> on
    that real cube, with the value at a removable zero interpreted
    continuously. The bound follows from the geometric quotient near zero and
    compactness for <math|A/M> bounded below. Again <math|<big|sum>A/M> is
    bounded. Consequently, with <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|z|)>=<big|prod><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||f*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>+2*\<pi\>*z<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>/M|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<Gamma\><around|(|z|)><rsup|-j>,<space|2em><around|\||f*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0
    </equation*>

    in the first estimate on a complex cube and in the second on its real
    slice.

    There is also an exact description of the accumulated noise. The
    independent noise introduced at step <math|h> is propagated only through
    the suffix <math|h+1,\<ldots\>,n>. If that suffix has total ratio
    <math|D=M<rsub|n>/M<rsub|h>>, its contribution, before multiplication by
    <math|b<rsup|-1>>, is

    <\equation*>
      D<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|z<math-up|mod><space|0.17em>D>f<rsub|<math-up|suffix>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*f<rsub|<math-up|suffix>>*<around|(|-\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*r<rsub|D><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>*r<rsub|D>*<around|(|-\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    This includes <math|1> when <math|h=n> and <math|D=1>. To verify the
    formula, apply <eqref|ch05:alias-recursion> to one constant covariance
    and split each residue modulo a product of ratios into its successive
    residues. The filter composition identities above identify the summands
    and the factors multiply to <math|D<rsup|-2>>. Summing over the noises
    gives precisely the noise part of the iterated recurrence. Each suffix
    contribution is bounded by <math|C<rsub|j>*D<rsup|-2>> because

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z>\<Gamma\><around|(|z|)><rsup|-2*j>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><ZZ>><around|(|1+<around|\||k|\|>|)><rsup|-2>|)><rsup|4>\<less\>81.
    </equation*>

    As <math|D\<geqslant\>3<rsup|n-h>>, the total noise is at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>0>3<rsup|-2*r>>, uniformly in
    <math|n>.

    Separate the principal alias of the propagated initial covariance from
    all other aliases. The former gives <math|A<rsub|\<mu\>>/\<lambda\><rsub|M>>
    with the notation of <eqref|ch05:pole-cancel>; the latter are

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z\<ne\>0><frac|f<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*f*<around|(|-\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*r<rsub|M><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|-\<theta\><rsub|z,\<mu\>>|)>|M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    For a nonprincipal real alias the denominator is bounded below by
    <math|c*<around|(|1+max<rsub|\<mu\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>|)><rsup|2>>.
    Its change on a complex strip of width <math|\<eta\>> is at most
    <math|C*\<eta\>*<around|(|1+max<rsub|\<mu\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>|)>+C*\<eta\><rsup|2>>.
    Decreasing <math|\<eta\>> gives the same lower bound in modulus. The
    preceding summability therefore bounds this sum holomorphically. Together
    with the suffix noise estimate it proves a uniform holomorphic bound for
    <math|G<rsub|\<mu\>>>, which is nonnegative on the real slice.

    Recall that <math|F=f*<around|(|p/M|)>*f*<around|(|-p/M|)>>,
    <math|A<rsub|\<mu\>>=F*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|p<rsub|\<mu\>>/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|-p<rsub|\<mu\>>/M|)>>
    and <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>=A<rsub|\<mu\>>+\<lambda\><rsub|M>*G<rsub|\<mu\>>>.
    The principal filter bounds give positive real lower bounds for
    <math|F,A<rsub|\<mu\>>>, and hence for <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>>. All three
    have uniform complex upper bounds. Cauchy estimates on a slightly larger
    cube therefore give uniformly bounded reciprocals on a smaller one. The
    identity in <eqref|ch05:pole-cancel> now supplies the real lower bound
    for <math|h>: since <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|B>>,

    <\equation*>
      h<around|(|p|)>=<frac|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>/B<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>\<geqslant\>c/C<rsub|B>*<space|1em><around|(|p\<ne\>0|)>.
    </equation*>

    We used uniform comparison of both <math|\<lambda\><around|(|p|)>> and
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>> with
    <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>> on the real cube. At zero <math|h=1>. Its
    defining formula gives a holomorphic upper bound using the reciprocals
    already obtained, and another Cauchy estimate gives a uniformly bounded
    reciprocal for <math|h>. Thus <math|<around|(|H<rsub|0>|)><rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=<around|(|h*B<rsub|\<mu\>>*B<rsub|\<nu\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
    has all the asserted bounds. The order of this argument is important: the
    lower bound for <math|h> uses <math|B<rsub|\<mu\>>>, not an assumed
    inverse bound for <math|h>. Periodicity holds first on real overlaps by
    permutation of the aliases in the original covariance recurrence.
    Analytic continuation then joins the cubes to a strip. The same Fourier
    symbol works on every finite-period grid.
  </proof>

  This proves uniform bounds for arbitrary finite histories. Convergence for
  a prescribed sequence of growing histories is stronger and is not a
  consequence of this proposition alone.

  <section|Exact transports and the curvature defect><label|ch05:transports>

  Recall that <math|H<rsub|0>> is the two-form metric in <eqref|ch05:H0>;
  <math|H> denotes its positive completion from
  <eqref|ch05:metric-modification>, agreeing with <math|H<rsub|0>> on exact
  two-forms. Fine and coarse subscripts below specify the scale of each
  metric.

  We describe the algebra at one real nonzero coarse momentum. This also
  specifies which transport must be estimated. The use of aliases and
  block-periodic Fourier coordinates is standard; see
  <cite|BalabanFeldmanKnoerrerTrubowitzBloch2016>. Write
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|l>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L>,
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|r>=L*d<rsub|r><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l>|)>> for the
  rescaled fine exterior derivative, and <math|d<rsub|r>=d<rsub|r><around|(|p|)>>
  for the coarse one. Let

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|l>=L<rsup|-2><math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|f,\<mu\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l>|)>|)>,<space|1em>D=<math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|c,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|)>,<space|1em>x=d<rsub|0>,<space|1em>x<rsub|l>=\<delta\><rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  For this paragraph <math|H,H<rsub|l>> denote the unmodified curvature
  metrics. Set

  <\equation*>
    R=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*d<rsub|1>,<space|1em>R<rsub|l>=\<delta\><rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>,<space|1em>\<rho\>=x<rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>*x,<space|1em>\<pi\>=\<rho\><rsup|-1>*x<rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The gauge quotient identity gives

  <\equation*>
    R=D<rsup|-1>-\<rho\><rsup|-1>*D<rsup|-1>*x*x<rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>,<space|2em>D*R=I-x*\<pi\>.
  </equation*>

  The fine formulas have subscript <math|l>. Define

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|l>=w<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l>|)><rsup|j>,<space|2em>F<rsub|r,l>=m<rsub|l><big|wedge><rsup|r><math-up|diag><around|(|r<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l,\<mu\>>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The link identity and the covariance recurrence say, respectively,

  <\equation>
    F<rsub|r+1,l>*\<delta\><rsub|r>=d<rsub|r>*F<rsub|r,l>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|l>F<rsub|1,l>*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>=D-b<rsup|-1>*I.<label|ch05:transport-input>
  </equation>

  Introduce the positive scalar <math|\<alpha\>=<big|sum><rsub|l><around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2><tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>>,
  and put <math|e<rsub|l>=<wide|m|\<bar\>><rsub|l><tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>/\<alpha\>>,
  <math|y<rsub|l>=<around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>*\<rho\>/\<rho\><rsub|l>>,
  and <math|C=H*d<rsub|1>*D*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>. The rescaled one-form
  and two-form transports are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R+e<rsub|l>*x<rsub|l>*\<pi\>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-T\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>+y<rsub|l>*F<rsub|2,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|I-C|)>.<eq-number><label|ch05:transport-formula>>>>>
  </align>

  Their defining identities are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|l>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*R,>|<cell|\<delta\><rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-T\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>,>|<cell|\<cal-T\><rsub|l>*H*d<rsub|1>>|<cell|=H<rsub|l>*\<delta\><rsub|1>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|l>F<rsub|1,l>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l>>|<cell|=I-b<rsup|-1>*R.>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|ch05:transport-identities>>>>>
  </align>

  These follow by substituting the quotient formula for <math|R> and using
  <eqref|ch05:transport-input>; in the last equality the gradient
  contribution is <math|x*\<pi\>*<big|sum><rsub|l>m<rsub|l>*e<rsub|l>=x*\<pi\>>.
  In particular the term <math|I-C> cannot simply be omitted: it is
  annihilated by <math|d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>> and annihilates
  <math|H*d<rsub|1>>, but controls the transport on the complementary
  two-forms.

  The physical kernels from a coarse site to a fine site <math|L*y+r> are
  obtained from the alias sums

  <\equation*>
    L<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|l>e<rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|l>\<cdot\>r>*\<cal-A\><rsub|l><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>L<rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|l>e<rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|l>\<cdot\>r>*\<cal-T\><rsub|l><around|(|p|)>
  </equation*>

  by coarse Fourier inversion. Denote the resulting operators by <math|W> and
  <math|T>. Restoring counting adjoints gives

  <\equation>
    T<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1,f>=d<rsub|1,c>*Q,<space|2em>T*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>=H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*W,<space|2em>Q*W=I-b<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>.<label|ch05:physical-transports>
  </equation>

  Here <math|Q> is the straight averaging operator in this construction. The
  same statements for a block average containing a gradient correction
  require its corresponding choice of the gradient part of <math|W>.

  The apparent poles in <eqref|ch05:transport-formula> can be removed using
  the principal covariance split. We record the crucial cancellation to avoid
  concealing a uniformity assumption. Write
  <math|s=\<lambda\><rsub|M<rsub|c>><around|(|p|)>>,
  <math|D=s<rsup|-1><math-up|diag><around|(|B<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>,
  <math|D<rsub|0>=s<rsup|-1><math-up|diag><around|(|B<rsub|0,\<mu\>>|)>>, and

  <\equation*>
    D=F<rsub|1,0>*D<rsub|0>*F<rsub|1,0><rsup|\<ast\>>+U,<space|2em>U=<math-up|diag><around|(|u<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The tail <math|U> consists of nonprincipal aliases and the current noise;
  it is uniformly holomorphic by the estimates just proved. Let
  <math|z<rsub|\<mu\>>=u<rsub|\<mu\>>/B<rsub|\<mu\>>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <frac|<around|\||m<rsub|0>*r<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|0,\<mu\>>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*B<rsub|0,\<mu\>>|B<rsub|\<mu\>>>=1-s*z<rsub|\<mu\>>.
  </equation*>

  With <math|J=F<rsub|2,0><rsup|-1>>, define the bounded analytic matrix

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-B\>=H<rsub|0>*<around*|[|<math-up|diag><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>><around|(|z<rsub|\<mu\>>+z<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>-s<big|wedge><rsup|2><math-up|diag><around|(|z<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|H<rsub|0>> means the fine metric at alias zero, not a new
  modification of the metric. Entrywise multiplication gives
  <math|s*\<cal-B\>=H<rsub|0>-y<rsub|0>*F<rsub|2,0><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*F<rsub|2,0>>,
  and hence

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|0>=y<rsub|0>*F<rsub|2,0><rsup|\<ast\>>+<around*|(|\<cal-B\>*J*d<rsub|1><math-up|diag><around|(|B<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>-H<rsub|0>*J*d<rsub|1>*U|)>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  All factors are regular at zero. For <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>>, the
  corresponding principal denominator is <math|s*\<alpha\>=<around|\||m<rsub|0>|\|><rsup|2>*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>B<rsub|0,\<mu\>>+s*\<alpha\><rsub|\<ne\>0>>,
  which is bounded below on the real cube. Its reciprocal is therefore
  uniformly holomorphic. Subtracting the leading diagonal term in
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>> leaves the regular factor

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|u<rsub|\<mu\>>/B<rsub|\<mu\>>-\<alpha\><rsub|\<ne\>0>/<around|(|s*\<alpha\>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For nonprincipal aliases the denominators have the lower bounds used in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:history-prop>. These formulas give bounded
  principal columns and bounds <math|C*\<Gamma\><around|(|l|)><rsup|-j>> for
  both nonprincipal columns on a smaller cube. Adjoints in these analytic
  formulas mean transpose at opposite momentum, with <math|l> replaced by
  <math|-l>.

  Each fine difference in the alias sum contributes
  <math|e<rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|l,\<mu\>>>-1>, bounded by
  <math|C*L<rsup|-1>*<around|(|1+max<rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||l<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>|)>>.
  The summability estimate for <math|\<Gamma\>> therefore gives, for
  <math|r\<leqslant\>j-2>, exponentially weighted coarse Fourier coefficient
  bounds of order <math|L<rsup|-1-r>> for <math|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W>
  and <math|L<rsup|-2-r>> for <math|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*T>. Fourier
  contour displacement is the usual conversion of strip analyticity to
  exponential coefficient decay. It is performed after summing the aliases,
  whose permutation under <math|p\<mapsto\>p+2*\<pi\>*n> gives periodicity.
  When a fine difference crosses a block boundary, its coarse argument is
  shifted by one block; this is included by the phase
  <math|e<rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|l>\<cdot\>r>> and must not be dropped. At
  zero momentum the principal columns are <math|I> and the other columns
  vanish, so the identities extend there without choosing a massless Gaussian
  normalization.

  <subsection|A positive factorization of the defect>

  The next argument uses exterior-complex exactness together with the bounds
  just obtained. Exactness for a regular sequence is standard Koszul algebra
  <cite-detail|StacksKoszul|Lemma 15.31.2>. The quantitative version needed
  here preserves exponential coefficient bounds: if an exponentially summable
  matrix row <math|A> on positive-degree forms satisfies
  <math|A*d<rsub|r>=0>, it can be written <math|A=V*d<rsub|r+1>> on a smaller
  strip, with a fixed norm cost. One obtains this version by successively
  dividing <math|A<around|(|p|)>-A<around|(|p|)>\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>> by
  <math|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1> and contracting the <math|a>th exterior index.
  The elementary quotient

  <\equation*>
    <frac|e<rsup|i*n*p<rsub|a>>-1|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>=<big|sum><rsub|h=0><rsup|n-1>e<rsup|i*h*p<rsub|a>>*<space|1em><around|(|n\<gtr\>0|)>
  </equation*>

  and the corresponding negative sum for <math|n\<less\>0> show that a
  division costs at most <math|C/\<eta\>> when width <math|\<eta\>> is
  reduced to <math|\<eta\>/2>. There are only four coordinates. This is the
  usual coordinate proof of Koszul exactness, with the coefficient norm
  retained. Applying it on both sides shows that a symmetric two-form row
  <math|E> with <math|E*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*E=0> has
  <math|E=d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|0>*d<rsub|2>>, with <math|C<rsub|0>>
  symmetric and exponentially summable. For completeness, first choose a left
  lift <math|d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*W<rsub|0>=E>. Since
  <math|d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*W<rsub|0>*d<rsub|1>=0>, exactness gives
  <math|W<rsub|0>*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|3><rsup|\<ast\>>*V<rsub|0>>. The top
  differential <math|d<rsub|3><rsup|\<ast\>>> has trivial kernel on analytic
  four-form coefficients, so <math|V<rsub|0>*d<rsub|0>=0>. A right lift gives
  <math|V<rsub|0>=X<rsub|0>*d<rsub|1>>. Thus
  <math|W<rsub|1>=W<rsub|0>-d<rsub|3><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|0>> satisfies both
  <math|W<rsub|1>*d<rsub|1>=0> and <math|d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*W<rsub|1>=E>.
  Lift once more to write <math|W<rsub|1>=C<rsub|0>*d<rsub|2>>, and replace
  <math|C<rsub|0>> by <math|<around|(|C<rsub|0>+C<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2>.
  All these lifts act on the infinite lattice coefficients before
  periodization; finite-period harmonic modes do not supply the analytic
  exactness used here.

  Put <math|B<rsub|c>=H<rsub|c><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|B<rsub|f>=H<rsub|f><rsup|-1>> for the unmodified metrics, and

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|0>=B<rsub|c>-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|f>*T-b<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Equation <eqref|ch05:physical-transports> gives
  <math|Z<rsub|0>*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>=0>. Applying the two-sided lift to
  <math|H<rsub|c>*Z<rsub|0>*H<rsub|c>> and the one-sided lift to
  <math|d<rsub|2,f>*B<rsub|f>*T*H<rsub|c>> produces

  <\equation>
    Z<rsub|0>=<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|0>*<around|(|d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>|)>,<space|2em>S*d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>=d<rsub|2,f>*B<rsub|f>*T.<label|ch05:defect-lifts>
  </equation>

  The estimates are <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|0><rprime|'>>
  and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsup|\<ast\>>*S|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*L<rsup|-2>>.
  Indeed a fixed fine-residue entry of <math|T> or <math|B<rsub|f>*T> has
  weighted coefficient bound <math|C*L<rsup|-2>> and one fine difference has
  bound <math|C*L<rsup|-3>>. A Gram product sums over <math|L<rsup|4>>
  residues. Its bounds are thus <math|L<rsup|4>*<around|(|C*L<rsup|-2>|)><rsup|2>=O<around|(|1|)>>
  and <math|L<rsup|4>*<around|(|C*L<rsup|-3>|)><rsup|2>=O<around|(|L<rsup|-2>|)>>,
  respectively. The lift acts on coarse exterior indices separately for every
  fine residue, so it introduces no further residue sum.

  Modify the inverse metrics as in <eqref|ch05:metric-modification>, with
  coefficients <math|\<zeta\><rsub|c>,\<zeta\><rsub|f>>. Write the modified
  inverse metrics as <math|<wide|B|~><rsub|c>,<wide|B|~><rsub|f>>, and
  <math|<wide|H|~><rsub|c>=<wide|B|~><rsub|c><rsup|-1>>. Set

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|c>=<around|(|I+\<zeta\><rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c>*B<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>R<rsub|0>=C<rsub|0>+\<zeta\><rsub|c>*I-\<zeta\><rsub|f>*S<rsup|\<ast\>>*S.
  </equation*>

  Expansion of <eqref|ch05:defect-lifts> gives the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <wide|H|~><rsub|c>*<around|(|<wide|B|~><rsub|c>-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*<wide|B|~><rsub|f>*T-b<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*<wide|H|~><rsub|c>=d<rsub|2,c><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|0>*D<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2,c>.<label|ch05:positive-defect>
  </equation>

  If <math|\<zeta\><rsub|c>\<geqslant\>C<rsub|0><rprime|'>+2> and
  <math|L<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|f>*C<rsub|1>>, then
  <math|R<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>I>. Since <math|D<rsub|c><rsup|-1>> has a
  uniform upper bound for fixed <math|\<zeta\><rsub|c>>, the three-form
  metric <math|D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|0>*D<rsub|c>> is uniformly
  strictly positive. Its inverse and positive square root have exponential
  coefficient bounds on a smaller strip by the usual analytic functional
  calculus. This proves positivity on the whole three-form space.

  For each adjacent pair of stages choose the coarse modification large
  enough for its finitely many incoming possibilities. Fix these coefficients
  before choosing the block ratios. Then the condition
  <math|L<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|f>*C<rsub|1>> is a finite lower
  bound on each permitted ratio. No constant uniform over all smoothing
  orders is asserted: the estimates are uniform over histories ending at a
  fixed order.

  <section|Convergence at a fixed scale><label|ch05:horizon-convergence>

  The curvature metrics and transport operators in this section are those of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|ch05:curvature>
  and<nbsp><reference|ch05:transports>; their histories now vary while the
  observation scale stays fixed.

  The preceding uniform bounds also give convergence for the particular
  nested histories used in the construction. The nesting is essential: fix
  the ratios, smoothing orders and modification coefficients at every scale,
  and increase only the terminal cutoff. At a fixed scale <math|m>, the
  history with terminal scale <math|N+1> extends the history with terminal
  scale <math|N> at its fine end. An arbitrary sequence of bounded histories
  need not converge.

  We first record how to identify limits on the infinite lattice. Write a
  translation-invariant row as <math|K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>>, where
  <math|z\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>> and <math|a,b> range over fixed finite
  coordinate sets. For an adjacent-scale row, include the finitely many fine
  residues in these coordinate sets. Suppose

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>><around|\||K<rsub|N,a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<space|2em><around|(|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0|)>
  </equation*>

  uniformly in <math|N>. Every subsequence has a further subsequence
  converging in each coefficient, by diagonal extraction. Its limit has the
  same bound, by finite partial sums. For every
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<less\>\<sigma\>>, coefficient convergence
  implies

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><rsub|z>e<rsup|\<tau\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>*<around|\||K<rsub|N,a*b><around|(|z|)>-K<rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|ch05:horizon-weighted>
  </equation>

  Indeed each summand is bounded by <math|2*C*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<sigma\>-\<tau\>|)><around|\||z|\|><rsub|1>>>,
  a summable sequence. If the symbols have a specified limit on a dense set
  of real momenta, any two cluster symbols agree there, hence everywhere by
  continuity. Fourier inversion then identifies every coefficient.
  Alternatively, agreement of cluster matrices on an unbounded sequence of
  torus sides suffices: for fixed <math|z>, the periodized entry is
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|h>K*<around|(|z+n*h|)>>, and its terms with
  <math|h\<ne\>0> tend to zero as <math|n\<to\>\<infty\>>. Either criterion
  proves uniqueness of the cluster limit and therefore convergence of the
  full sequence. This argument identifies the lattice-cover coefficients, not
  merely a matrix on one fixed torus.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Convergence of the selected Gaussian
    rows><label|ch05:horizon-limit>Fix a scale and the nested schedule just
    described. The unmodified curvature metrics, their inverses, the adjacent
    one- and two-form transports, and the modified metrics of
    <eqref|ch05:metric-modification> converge in the sense of
    <eqref|ch05:horizon-weighted>, at every width strictly smaller than a
    common positive width available at that scale. The same conclusion holds
    for the specified coordinate exterior lifts, their amended three-form
    metric in <eqref|ch05:positive-defect>, and their positive square roots.
    It also holds for a positive square root of <math|<wide|H|~>-c*I>
    whenever the fixed direct coefficient <math|c> is chosen strictly below
    the uniform lower bound of <math|<wide|H|~>>. All limits are for the
    actual chosen rows on the cover and their periodizations. No choice of a
    subsequence, matrix eigenbasis or square-root signs is made.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|d=N-m> be the history depth and <math|M<rsub|d,m>> its total
    dilation. Let <math|u<rsub|d,m,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>> be the squared
    modulus of its accumulated directional filter, evaluated at
    <math|p/M<rsub|d,m>>. Thus <math|u<rsub|0,m,\<mu\>>=1>; the filter is the
    product of the centered-box and link factors used in
    <eqref|ch05:history-filter>. Write <math|g<rsub|m,\<mu\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>
    for the single-step directional filter at scale <math|m>, including the
    link average <math|r<rsub|L<rsub|m>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>.
    Composition gives

    <\equation*>
      u<rsub|d+1,m,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=<around|\||g<rsub|m,\<mu\>>*<around|(|p/L<rsub|m>|)>|\|><rsup|2>*u<rsub|d,m+1,\<mu\>>*<around|(|p/L<rsub|m>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Every factor is an average of unit complex numbers on real momenta,
    possibly multiplied by a unit phase. Its modulus is at most one.
    Induction, with the schedule fixed at each scale, gives
    <math|0\<leqslant\>u<rsub|d+1,m,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|d,m,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Thus the directional filter squares converge at every real <math|p>,
    including any fixed reciprocal translate.

    The accumulated noise is also convergent. Extending the history adds one
    independent noise contribution propagated through all the previously
    fixed coarser steps. The old contributions are unchanged, and every new
    contribution is nonnegative at real momenta. The accumulated noise
    therefore increases with depth. The suffix estimate in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:history-prop> bounds it uniformly, and
    the noise at the last blocking step contributes one. Its limit is finite
    and at least one. This argument concerns the actual nested noise list; it
    is not a monotonicity assertion for arbitrary histories of the same
    length.

    For the initial covariance contribution, extend the finite nonprincipal
    alias sum to all <math|z\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>> by
    zero outside the centered representatives modulo <math|M=M<rsub|d,m>>.
    Its summand at an eventually included fixed <math|z> is

    <\equation*>
      <frac|u<rsub|d,m,\<mu\>>*<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>|M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\>*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/M|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The denominator tends to <math|<around|\||p+2*\<pi\>*z|\|><rsup|2>>,
    which is positive for nonzero <math|z> and <math|p> in the base cube. The
    alias estimates above dominate these summands by
    <math|C*<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|>|)><rsup|-2>>,
    independently of depth. Dominated convergence proves convergence of the
    sum. At <math|p\<ne\>0> the principal summand converges by the same
    argument without an alias sum. Consequently each directional covariance
    <math|c<rsub|N,m,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>> has a finite limit at real
    <math|p\<ne\>0>, bounded below by <math|b<rsup|-1>>.

    The curvature metric is the rational expression <eqref|ch05:H0> in these
    covariances and the exterior derivatives. Its scalar quotient denominator
    is <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>><around|\||q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>|\|><rsup|2>/c<rsub|N,m,\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>>;
    its limit is positive away from zero. Thus its symbol converges there.
    The uniform analytic bounds already proved and the cluster-limit
    criterion identify one limit on the cover, including at zero. Inverses
    have the same conclusion. This avoids assigning a finite massless
    covariance to the zero mode.

    For adjacent transports the ratio <math|L=L<rsub|m>> is fixed as <math|N>
    increases. At <math|p\<ne\>0>, every one of its finitely many aliases
    <math|\<theta\><rsub|l>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L> is a nonzero real
    momentum. All covariance factors in <eqref|ch05:transport-formula>
    therefore converge. The denominators <math|\<rho\>,\<rho\><rsub|l>> have
    positive limits, and so does

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\>=<big|sum><rsub|l><around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2><tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>:
    </equation*>

    the principal centered-box factor is nonzero on the real base cube and
    <math|D<rsub|l>> has a positive diagonal limit. Hence the finite alias
    formulas and their Fourier reconstruction converge. The previously proved
    uniform exponential bounds identify unique cover limits for <math|W,T>.
    At exceptional zero momenta uniqueness follows from continuity of cluster
    symbols, rather than division by a vanishing expression.

    Products preserve this convergence by the weighted convolution
    inequality, with the fixed finite coordinate multiplicity included. For
    uniformly bounded inverse rows, cluster limits satisfy the inverse
    identity on each torus; uniqueness of the finite-dimensional inverse and
    recovery from unbounded periods identify their cover limit. The
    coordinate exterior lifts used in <eqref|ch05:defect-lifts> are also
    continuous after a width decrease. Each elementary operation is
    coordinate evaluation or a divided difference

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<wide|K|^><rsub|N><around|(|p|)>-<wide|K|^><rsub|N><around|(|p\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>|)>|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>.
    </equation*>

    Its numerator converges, its denominator is nonzero on a dense set, and
    its smaller-width bound was proved in the exterior construction. The
    cluster criterion consequently applies through the finitely many
    coordinate operations. This requires the fixed coordinate lift of that
    construction, not a fresh arbitrary solution of the exterior identity.

    It follows that <math|C<rsub|0>,S,D<rsub|c>> and
    <math|D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|0>*D<rsub|c>> in
    <eqref|ch05:positive-defect> converge, as do the modified metrics and
    inverses. Finally consider one of the stated positive square roots. Its
    uniform analytic bound gives cluster rows. On each finite torus their
    matrices are positive semidefinite and have the same square, namely the
    limit of the underlying positive matrix. The positive semidefinite square
    root is unique by the finite-dimensional spectral theorem. Thus the
    cluster matrices agree on every torus, and period recovery identifies the
    cover row. This proves convergence for the chosen roots themselves, not
    merely for their squares.
  </proof>

  In particular all canonical path-row coefficients obtained from these rows
  converge, with a strictly positive gap between the available width and the
  width used to sum paths. Finite products, fixed exterior operations and the
  fixed positive factorizations preserve this conclusion. The actual block
  average correction introduced below is a fixed finite-range row at the
  selected scale, so its application to the convergent straight transport
  also preserves convergence. These are statements about the quadratic data.
  They do not imply cutoff continuity of the nonlinear activity arrays or of
  marked integral maps; those require their own stability arguments.

  <section|Uniform inverse bounds: the hypotheses that
  matter><label|ch05:inverse>

  A spectral gap in Euclidean norm is not, by itself, a bound for an inverse
  in supremum norm independent of volume. One sufficient framework is an
  exponentially local matrix with a uniform positive gap. To state its
  quantitative content, assign each coordinate <math|i> a lattice site
  <math|a<rsub|i>>, with uniformly bounded coordinate multiplicity per site,
  and define

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>=max <around*|{|sup<rsub|i>
    <big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsup|\<eta\>*d*<around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>><around|\||T<rsub|i*j>|\|>,sup<rsub|j>
    <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<eta\>*d*<around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>|)>><around|\||T<rsub|i*j>|\|>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|d> is periodic lattice distance. Suppose
  <math|H=H<rsup|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>m*I> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>C>
  uniformly in the volume. For some <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<less\>\<eta\><rsub|0>>,
  depending only on <math|m,C,\<eta\><rsub|0>> and the coordinate
  multiplicity, <math|H<rsup|-1>> has uniformly bounded exponentially
  weighted row and column sums, after reducing the exponent once more if
  necessary. This is the usual weighted resolvent estimate behind
  exponentially local lattice propagators; compare
  <cite|Balaban1984Propagators>. It follows by conjugating with
  <math|e<rsup|\<eta\>*d*<around|(|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i<rsub|0>>|)>>> and
  applying the resolvent identity to the resulting small perturbation. In
  particular <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>>
  is bounded independently of volume.

  For analytic momentum-dependent matrices, an analogous perturbation
  argument must use holomorphic coordinates. Suppose <math|K<around|(|p|)>>
  is holomorphic in a strip of width <math|\<sigma\>> and, in fixed
  coordinates, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|K<around|(|p|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>A>
  there, while at real <math|x> its least singular value is at least
  <math|a\<gtr\>0>. Cauchy estimates and the inverse perturbation lemma give
  a smaller strip, depending only on <math|a,A,\<sigma\>>, on which
  <math|K<rsup|-1>> is holomorphic and uniformly bounded. This statement is
  dimension independent.

  In weighted Fourier spaces the weights often involve
  <math|<around|\||q<around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p|)>|\|>> and are not
  holomorphic. The preceding argument is justified if, at every real anchor
  <math|x>, the matrices defining the weighted norms have holomorphic
  replacements in a fixed neighborhood of <math|x>, uniformly equivalent to
  the original norms, and coinciding with them on the vertical slice
  <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p=x>. One applies Cauchy's estimate in
  these replacement coordinates. A bound in nonholomorphic coordinates alone
  does not justify differentiating the weighted matrix. This distinction is
  essential when the weight degenerates with a scale parameter.

  <section|The actual block average and the forest
  coordinates><label|ch05:actual-coordinates>

  We now identify the nonlinear coordinates behind the Gaussian calculation.
  Fix <math|j,L>, a coarse period <math|s\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|j+8|)>>,
  and in each centered block <math|L*y+\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|L-1|)>/2>> a
  spanning tree rooted at <math|L*y>. Trees contain only internal block
  edges. Denote by <math|\<iota\><rsub|F>> extension from free-edge
  coordinates to all fine edges, with value zero on tree edges. For coarse
  links <math|V>, their replica is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|<math-up|parent><around|(|e<rsub|->|)>=<math-up|parent><around|(|e<rsub|+>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|V<rsub|y,\<mu\>>,>|<cell|<math-up|parent><around|(|e<rsub|->|)>=y,<math-up|parent><around|(|e<rsub|+>|)>=y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The centered-block convention and odd <math|L> make these the only
  possibilities for a positive fine edge in direction <math|\<mu\>>. In
  particular the replica is one on every tree edge. The actual forest chart
  is

  <\equation>
    U*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)><rsub|e>=exp <around|(|<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|F>*\<psi\>|)><rsub|e>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>exp
    v|)><rsub|e>.<label|ch05:forest-chart>
  </equation>

  It is a chart on the tree slice, not a choice of independent variables on
  tree edges. Its derivative at the identity is exactly

  <\equation>
    D*U*<around|(|0,0|)><around|[|h,z|]>=<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>h+\<iota\><rsub|F>*z,<space|2em>D*U*<around|(|0,0|)><around|[|0,z|]>=\<iota\><rsub|F>*z.<label|ch05:forest-derivative>
  </equation>

  All color coordinates are orthonormal for the trace metric. Thus
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><rsub|F>*z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>
  in counting norms. This identifies the vertical coordinates used by the
  Hessian with the arrays in the tree inequality below; no volume-dependent
  coordinate conversion occurs.

  <subsection|A common analytic branch for all local labels>

  The minimizing frame and block average of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking> are real analytic near the identity.
  For the frame this follows directly from its chart equation: its quadratic
  objective at zero is <math|<tfrac|1|2><math-up|av><rsub|r,r<rprime|'>><around|\||h<rsub|r>-h<rsub|r<rprime|'>>|\|><rsup|2>>,
  positive definite after <math|h<rsub|0>=0> is imposed. For the block
  average, its Hessian in the group variable is the identity. Complexify
  these finite-dimensional real analytic chart equations and apply the
  analytic implicit-function theorem <cite-detail|LeblSCV|Theorem 1.3.8>. The
  resulting critical points are the unique minimizers already identified in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:frame-lemma>, after reducing the neighborhood if
  necessary.

  At fixed <math|j,L> every such equation reads a translate of one fixed
  finite patch. Choose a radius in the supremum norm of all its input
  logarithms on which these analytic extensions and their derivatives are
  bounded. There are finitely many directions and local tree shapes at fixed
  <math|L>; take their common radius and bounds. Periodic pullback to a patch
  has supremum norm at most one, so these bounds are independent of <math|s>.
  Decrease this radius until every loop test and minimum-cost test is
  strictly on its minimizing branch. Every local label then selects the same
  frame and block average as a function of the full fine field on the whole
  common ball. Redundant uniform branch masses are constant there. This is
  equality of the local action functions before stationarity is imposed, not
  merely equality along a stationary graph. It concerns the uncut action;
  shrinking smooth cutoff plateaux are treated separately.

  Let <math|a> be an infinitesimal fine field. Write
  <math|I<rsub|a>*<around|(|r\<to\>r<rprime|'>;o|)>> for its signed sum along
  a coordinate path in the frame patch at <math|L*y>. The linearized
  minimizing-frame equation gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=-<math-up|av><rsub|r<rprime|'>,o><space|0.17em>I<rsub|a>*<around|(|L*y+r\<to\>L*y+r<rprime|'>;o|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|H<rsub|y,r><around|(|a|)>>|<cell|=\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|r|)>-\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|0|)>,>|<cell|R*a<around|(|y|)>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*H<rsub|y,r><around|(|a|)>.<eq-number><label|ch05:frame-linearization>>>>>
  </align>

  Indeed its equation is <math|H<rsub|r>-<math-up|av><rsub|r<rprime|'>><space|0.17em>H<rsub|r<rprime|'>>=-<math-up|av><rsub|r<rprime|'>,o><space|0.17em>I<rsub|a>*<around|(|r\<to\>r<rprime|'>;o|)>>,
  and <math|H<rsub|0>=0> fixes the additive constant. Differentiating the
  actual connector and then its block average therefore yields

  <\equation>
    \<cal-Q\>*a=M*a+d<rsub|0,c>*R*a,<space|2em>\<cal-Q\>*d<rsub|0,f>*\<phi\>=d<rsub|0,c><around|(|\<phi\>\|<rsub|L*\<Lambda\><rsub|s>>|)>.<label|ch05:actual-Q>
  </equation>

  Here <math|M> is exactly the straight length-<math|L> average with the
  convolution weights used in <eqref|ch05:alias-recursion>. The second
  identity follows also by differentiating gauge covariance at block roots.
  Each route has length <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>>; hence <math|R> has
  finite coarse range and absolute row sum at most <math|C<rsub|j>*L>. These
  statements include the root normalization. Dropping it would change
  <math|\<cal-Q\>>.

  <subsection|The tree comparison and the actual Hessian>

  For a fine edge <math|e>, join its endpoints to their block roots along the
  chosen trees. In the tree slice the resulting walk has transport
  <math|U<rsub|e>>. It lies in one or two adjacent blocks and has length at
  most <math|2*L<rsup|4>-1>. In the macro axial gauge of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch04:predictor-geometry>, its distance from the
  identity is bounded by this length times the sum of bond distances from the
  identity in the macro-box. The first-power frame estimate controls the two
  root-to-frame discrepancies; the block average estimate controls the coarse
  root-to-root transport. Thus

  <\equation>
    \<chi\><around*|(|U<rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,L>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>+\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|e<text|
    crosses blocks>|}>>*\<chi\>*<around|(|V<rsub|y,\<mu\>>*Q<rsub|y,\<mu\>><around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>.<label|ch05:nonlinear-tree>
  </equation>

  For an internal edge the walk closes at its root and only the first term is
  needed. For a crossing edge, insert the block average between the two root
  frames and use the triangle inequality for the normalized Hilbert\USchmidt
  distance. The endpoint errors are bounded by the sum in
  <eqref|ch04:frame-first-power>, so this step requires no pointwise frame
  estimate not already proved. The constant is allowed to depend on <math|L>;
  the stronger pre-<math|L> factor in <eqref|ch04:macro-comparison> is not
  required for this use.

  Apply <eqref|ch05:nonlinear-tree> to <math|U=exp
  <around|(|\<epsilon\>*a|)>> with <math|a> zero on tree edges, and
  <math|V=1>. Divide by <math|<around|\||\<epsilon\>|\|>> and let
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<to\>0>. The first-order variation of the distance from
  the identity is the color norm, so

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||a<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,L>*<big|sum><rsub|p<text| in
    the macro-patch at >y><around|\||<around|(|d<rsub|1,f>*a|)><rsub|p>*<around|\||+\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|e<text|
    crosses blocks>|}>>|\|>*<around|(|\<cal-Q\>*a|)><rsub|y,\<mu\>>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Square, use the finite macro-patch cardinality, and sum over edges. A fine
  plaquette belongs to a bounded number of macro-patches, and each coarse
  edge occurs for at most a fixed number of fine edges at fixed <math|L>.
  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|j,L>\<\|\|\>d<rsub|1,f>*a\<\|\|\><rsub|2><rsup|2>+C<rsub|j,L>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,<space|1em>a=\<iota\><rsub|F>*z.<label|ch05:tree-comparison>
  </equation>

  Both constants are uniform in the period and in the allowed trees. The use
  of periodic lifts preserves the bounded multiplicity.

  Let <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|f>> be the uncut fine action, without the common
  inverse coupling or inherited loop action, and set

  <\equation>
    f*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>=\<cal-A\><rsub|f>*<around|(|U*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>|)>+b*<big|sum><rsub|e<text|
    coarse>>w*<around*|(|<around|(|exp v|)><rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e>*<around|(|U*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>|)><rsup|-1>|)>.<label|ch05:actual-action>
  </equation>

  Assume its actual quadratic Taylor coefficient is
  <math|K<rsub|f>=d<rsub|1,f><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>> with
  <math|H<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*I\<gtr\>0>. This coefficient is
  read from the same path rows in <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|f>>: each quadratic
  path square contributes the square of its linearized curvature row, and
  atoms of curvature degree at least three contribute nothing at second
  order. Identifying their sum with a chosen metric <math|H<rsub|f>> is an
  exact coefficient condition on the incoming path action.

  The first derivatives of both terms of <eqref|ch05:actual-action> vanish at
  the identity. Therefore the second derivative of the forest chart does not
  enter its Hessian. By <eqref|ch05:forest-derivative>, for
  <math|a=<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>h+\<iota\><rsub|F>*z> and
  <math|a<rprime|'>=<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>h<rprime|'>+\<iota\><rsub|F>*z<rprime|'>>,
  it is precisely

  <\equation>
    D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|h,z|)>,<around|(|h<rprime|'>,z<rprime|'>|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1,f>*a,H<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*a<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>+b*<around|\<langle\>|h-\<cal-Q\>*a,h<rprime|'>-\<cal-Q\>*a<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>.<label|ch05:actual-hessian>
  </equation>

  No derivative of the nonlinear block average beyond its actual first
  derivative is missing here: its residual vanishes at zero. In particular,

  <\equation>
    D<rsub|\<psi\>><rsup|2>*f*<around|(|0,0|)>\<geqslant\><frac|min
    <around|(|\<varepsilon\>,b|)>|1+B<rsub|j,L>+C<rsub|j,L>>*I.<label|ch05:actual-gap>
  </equation>

  This proves the vertical gap for the actual forest chart, rather than
  assuming it from the flat Fourier calculation.

  <subsection|A smooth representative of the stationary
  tangent><label|ch05:actual-tangent>

  Let <math|W<rsub|s>> denote the straight-average transport already
  constructed in Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:transports>. Its identities in
  physical coordinates are

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|f>*W<rsub|s>=M<rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|c>,<space|2em>M*W<rsub|s>=I-b<rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  These hold for the same incoming metric and penalty as
  <eqref|ch05:actual-hessian>. Since <math|K<rsub|c>*d<rsub|0,c>=0>,
  replacing <math|M> by <math|\<cal-Q\>=M+d<rsub|0,c>*R> does not change the
  right side of the first identity. The second identity does require a
  gradient correction.

  Choose a smooth compactly supported cardinal partition of unity
  <math|\<varphi\>> on <math|<RR><rsup|4>>:
  <math|\<varphi\><around|(|n|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|n=0>> for
  <math|n\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>> and <math|<big|sum><rsub|n\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>>\<varphi\>*<around|(|x-n|)>=1>.
  For example, in one dimension divide a smooth bump supported in
  <math|<around|(|-1,1|)>> and positive there by the sum of its integer
  translates, and take the fourfold product. Define a periodic extension of
  coarse site fields to fine sites by

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|E*h|)><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>><big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>>\<varphi\>*<around|(|x/L-y-s*k|)>*h<around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  It equals <math|h<around|(|y|)>> at <math|x=L*y>, is supported within one
  coarse block of its input, and has fine differences bounded by
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r>*L<rsup|-r>>.
  Now put

  <\equation>
    W<rsub|a>=W<rsub|s>-d<rsub|0,f>*E*R*W<rsub|s>.<label|ch05:corrected-transport>
  </equation>

  By <eqref|ch05:actual-Q>,

  <\equation>
    \<cal-Q\>*W<rsub|a>=M*W<rsub|s>=I-b<rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>,<space|2em>K<rsub|f>*W<rsub|a>=\<cal-Q\><rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rsub|c>,<space|2em>d<rsub|1,f>*W<rsub|a>=d<rsub|1,f>*W<rsub|s>.<label|ch05:actual-transport-identities>
  </equation>

  Thus <math|W<rsub|a>> minimizes the actual quadratic energy and has the
  same reduced quadratic form <math|K<rsub|c>>. This holds also on constant
  modes: <math|E> extends constants to constants, gradients have zero curl,
  and no inverse of a zero-momentum Laplacian has been introduced.

  The correction preserves the needed smooth interpolation bounds. The
  finite-range row <math|R> has size <math|C<rsub|j>*L> and the already
  proved kernel of <math|W<rsub|s>> has size <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-1>> with
  exponential coarse decay. Their composition <math|R*W<rsub|s>> therefore
  has an <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|1|)>> exponentially decaying coarse kernel.
  Applying <math|r+1> fine differences to <math|E> gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|a>*<around|(|L*y+x,y<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*L<rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*d*<around|(|y,y<rprime|'>|)>>,<space|1em>r\<leqslant\>j-2,<label|ch05:actual-bridge>
  </equation>

  with the same permitted orders as for <math|W<rsub|s>>. The extension adds
  only bounded coarse range, so it preserves exponential summability. This
  argument uses finite-difference bounds of a cardinal extension, not an
  assumption that a discontinuous block-constant extension is smooth.

  Finally adjust <math|W<rsub|a>*v> by the unique gradient
  <math|d<rsub|0,f>*\<phi\>> whose site potential is zero at every block root
  and makes all tree edges vanish. It is obtained successively along each
  tree. Such a gradient leaves <math|\<cal-Q\>*W<rsub|a>*v> unchanged by
  <eqref|ch05:actual-Q>, and leaves its curl unchanged. Write the resulting
  representative as <math|W<rsub|F>*v>. Coercivity <eqref|ch05:actual-gap>
  makes it the unique minimizer in the forest slice. Once a stationary graph
  <math|\<psi\>=\<rho\><around|(|v|)>> has been constructed, differentiating
  its equation and using <eqref|ch05:actual-hessian> proves

  <\equation>
    <math-up|rep><space|0.17em>v+\<iota\><rsub|F>*D*\<rho\><around|(|0|)>*v=W<rsub|F>*v.<label|ch05:stationary-tangent>
  </equation>

  The smooth representative <math|W<rsub|a>> and the forest representative
  <math|W<rsub|F>> are distinct. Estimate<nbsp><eqref|ch05:actual-bridge> is
  asserted for <math|W<rsub|a>>, not for tree coordinates. Gauge-invariant
  quadratic insertions see the same curl in either representative.

  <section|A stationary background on a uniform
  neighborhood><label|ch05:stationary>

  We first state the quantitative implicit-function estimate needed for the
  nonlinear action. Let <math|x> be the coarse coordinates and <math|y> the
  free fine coordinates after gauge fixing. Both carry the supremum norm.
  Write <math|f*<around|(|x,y|)>> for the action including the block penalty,
  and <math|F*<around|(|x,y|)>=D<rsub|y>*f*<around|(|x,y|)>>. All norms below
  are operator norms induced by these supremum norms.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform stationary graph><label|ch05:stationary-prop>Suppose
    <math|F> is holomorphic on a product neighborhood
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|0>>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|0>>,
    with <math|F*<around|(|0,0|)>=0>. Let
    <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>=D<rsub|y>*F*<around|(|0,0|)>> be invertible and
    assume, uniformly in the number of coordinates,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>J,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|y>*F*<around|(|x,y|)>-H<rsub|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*max
      <around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|x>*F*<around|(|x,y|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|0\<less\>r<rsub|y>\<less\>r<rsub|0>> and
    <math|0\<less\>r<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|y>> so that
    <math|J*C*r<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>1/4> and
    <math|J*B*r<rsub|x>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|y>/4>. Then for
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|x>>
    there is a unique solution <math|y=\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> of
    <math|F*<around|(|x,y|)>=0> in <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|y>>.
    It is holomorphic, satisfies <math|\<rho\><around|(|0|)>=0> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|y>/2>,
    and obeys

    <\equation*>
      D*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>=-<around|[|D<rsub|y>*F*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>|]><rsup|-1>*D<rsub|x>*F*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|4|3>J*B.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    This is the quantitative contraction form of the implicit-function
    theorem: apply it to <math|y-H<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>*F*<around|(|x,y|)>>
    on the closed <math|r<rsub|y>>-ball. The two displayed smallness
    conditions give contraction constant <math|1/4> and displacement of the
    origin at most <math|r<rsub|y>/4>. We use the standard theorem rather
    than repeat its proof; holomorphic regularity is the analytic
    implicit-function theorem <cite-detail|LeblSCV|Theorem 1.3.8>. The real
    smooth version is also a direct application of the implicit-function
    theorem <cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem 9.28>. For a merely
    <math|C<rsup|N>> real action the conclusion is <math|C<rsup|N-1>> for
    <math|\<rho\>>, not holomorphy.
  </proof>

  In applications the operator bounds in this proposition should be deduced
  from local derivative estimates before any dimension dependent conversion
  of norms. For example, suppose each coarse or fine coordinate has an anchor
  <math|a<rsub|i>>, the total number of coordinates anchored at any site is
  bounded uniformly in the volume, and, for
  <math|2\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>N>, every rooted derivative satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|q>>*f<around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*exp
    <around*|(|-\<eta\>*<big|sum><rsub|h=1><rsup|q>d*<around|(|a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>|)><space|1em><text|for
    every >t.
  </equation*>

  The lattice shell sum <math|S<rsub|\<eta\>>=sup<rsub|v>
  <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|-\<eta\>*d*<around|(|v,a<rsub|i>|)>>> is bounded
  uniformly in the torus size. Summing the second derivative gives
  <math|B\<leqslant\>C*S<rsub|\<eta\>>>; summing the third gives the Hessian
  Lipschitz bound <math|C*S<rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|2>>. A real Hessian gap
  together with exponential locality supplies the required <math|J> by the
  previous section. These implications yield a uniform real neighborhood. A
  uniform complex neighborhood requires the corresponding complex estimates,
  which do not follow from a fixed finite number of real derivatives.

  At fixed derivative order, higher derivatives of the background and of its
  Hessian can then be controlled by repeated implicit differentiation. If
  only polynomial dependence on an auxiliary volume is needed, this
  conversion can be postponed until after the uniform neighborhood and the
  first derivative have been obtained. It must not be used to choose the
  neighborhood itself. For a positive real Hessian <math|H<around|(|x|)>>,
  the determinant contribution is differentiated by the standard identity
  <math|D*log det H<around|[|h|]>=<tr><around|(|H<rsup|-1>*D*H<around|[|h|]>|)>>.

  <subsection|Supplying the local derivative estimates>

  We retain the distinction from Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:actual-tangent>:
  <math|W<rsub|a>> is the smooth representative of the actual stationary
  tangent, whereas <math|W<rsub|F>> is its representative in the forest
  coordinates. They have the same curvature, but the fine-difference bounds
  apply to <math|W<rsub|a>>.

  The derivative hypotheses have a direct origin in the exponentially
  weighted path expansion. We spell it out because a bound for the action on
  each finite torus would not supply a uniform neighborhood.

  Suppose the uncut classical action is a sum of local analytic terms indexed
  by rooted finite path collections. Let <math|\<ell\>> be the total path
  length plus one. Require that each collection have connected support
  containing its root, and that

  <\equation>
    sup<rsub|z> <big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>:<math-up|root><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>=z><around|\||c<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<sigma\>*\<ell\><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>>\<leqslant\>P.<label|ch05:path-budget>
  </equation>

  A bounded number of paths per term is sufficient; products of their matrix
  entries, normalized traces and fixed invariant contractions are allowed.
  The constants <math|\<sigma\>,P> and this number are independent of the
  torus. Choose local coordinate maps with uniformly bounded analytic
  extensions and fixed support radius at the chosen block ratio.

  A complex coordinate of modulus at most <math|r> changes each matrix
  factor's norm by at most <math|e<rsup|C*r>>. Therefore a term of total
  length <math|\<ell\>> has complex modulus at most
  <math|C*e<rsup|C*r*\<ell\>>>. Choose <math|r> so that
  <math|C*r\<less\>\<sigma\>/4>. Differentiating <math|q> times selects at
  most <math|C<rsub|q>*<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)><rsup|q>> positions; the same
  estimates hold on the smaller complex ball. If the differentiated
  coordinates have prescribed anchors <math|a<rsub|i<rsub|1>>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|i<rsub|q>>>,
  connectedness implies

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|h=1><rsup|q>d*<around|(|a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|R>*q*<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)><space|1em><text|for
    every >t.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|C<rsub|R>> allows for the finite support radius of the
  coordinate maps, fixed at this block ratio. A fraction of the exponential
  weight in <eqref|ch05:path-budget> controls this distance, and a further
  fraction absorbs the polynomial <math|<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)><rsup|q>>. The
  remaining exponential weight bounds the sum over possible roots of a term
  visiting a fixed anchor: the number of roots within distance
  <math|C<rsub|R>*<around|(|1+\<ell\>|)>> grows only polynomially in
  <math|\<ell\>>. It follows, for every fixed <math|N>, that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<partial\><rsub|i<rsub|q>>*f<around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|N>*exp
    <around*|(|-\<eta\><rsub|N>*<big|sum><rsub|h>d*<around|(|a<rsub|i<rsub|t>>,a<rsub|i<rsub|h>>|)>|)>,<space|2em>2\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>N,
  </equation*>

  on a common complex ball. Block penalties and local coordinate terms add
  finite-range contributions satisfying the same estimate. Smooth cutoffs are
  not being analytically continued: the action here is the uncut action whose
  germ at zero defines the background.

  For the actual forest chart <eqref|ch05:forest-chart>, the deterministic
  tree comparison and the Hessian identification have already been proved in
  <eqref|ch05:tree-comparison>\U<eqref|ch05:actual-gap>. The chosen block
  average is analytic on a common fixed-patch ball, and all its redundant
  local labels agree there as functions of the full field. The derivative
  bounds above give exponential locality of that Hessian.
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:inverse> now supplies a supremum-norm bound
  for its inverse independent of volume; a sufficiently small complex
  perturbation preserves this bound. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:stationary-prop>
  therefore applies on a uniform neighborhood.

  This argument proves the uniform stationary construction for uncut path
  actions satisfying <eqref|ch05:path-budget>, the actual incoming quadratic
  coefficient condition in <eqref|ch05:actual-hessian>. The actual block
  average and forest coordinates supply the local analytic-coordinate
  hypotheses and the deterministic comparison <eqref|ch05:tree-comparison>.
  It does not require a later matching theorem. If two local descriptions
  agree as functions on the same coordinate neighborhood, uniqueness makes
  their stationary graphs agree there. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch05:stationary-tangent>
  identifies the tangent with the forest representative of the actual
  corrected transport <math|W<rsub|a>>. The stationary chain rule and
  <eqref|ch05:actual-transport-identities> give the coarse quadratic form as
  the Hessian of the reduced action. Equality of actions only on a previously
  selected graph would not suffice for this argument.

  The constants are uniform in the period after the block ratio, smoothing
  order and path bound have been fixed. Uniformity in an arbitrary block
  ratio is a separate weighted-norm estimate; the Euclidean tree gap above is
  allowed to depend on <math|L>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform actual stationary chart><label|ch05:actual-stationary>Fix
    the group, <math|j,L,b>, an exponential path bound
    <eqref|ch05:path-budget>, and the actual incoming quadratic metric with
    <math|H<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*I\<gtr\>0>. For the uncut
    action <eqref|ch05:actual-action> there are radii
    <math|r<rsub|V>,r<rsub|\<psi\>>\<gtr\>0> and constants
    <math|c,J,C<rsub|\<rho\>>\<gtr\>0>, independent of the allowed period and
    block trees, such that its unique stationary graph <math|\<rho\>>
    satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|\<psi\>>/2,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<rho\><around|(|v|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<rho\>>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>r<rsub|V>|)>.
    </equation*>

    On the corresponding smaller real product chart the vertical Hessian is
    at least <math|c*I> and its inverse has supremum operator norm at most
    <math|J>. All local tag descriptions define the same action and
    stationary graph there. For every prescribed finite order <math|N>,
    ordinary derivatives of <math|f> through order <math|N>, of
    <math|\<rho\>> through order <math|N-1>, and of the centered log
    determinant of its vertical Hessian through order <math|N-2> are bounded
    by fixed polynomials in the total coordinate number <math|n>. The radii
    and first-derivative bound do not acquire a factor depending on <math|n>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    To check the last assertions, the third rooted derivative bound gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<around|(|w|)>-H<around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2\<to\>2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    by row and column sums. Reduce the common ball until this is at most half
    the gap in <eqref|ch05:actual-gap>. The weighted inverse argument then
    applies uniformly throughout that real ball.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:stationary-prop> gives the graph and its
    volume-independent Lipschitz constant. Repeated implicit differentiation
    isolates <math|H*D<rsup|k>*\<rho\>> at order <math|k>; every other term
    involves lower derivatives of <math|\<rho\>> and derivatives of <math|f>
    of order at most <math|k+1>. Converting bounded coordinate entries to
    ordinary multilinear norms costs only a fixed power of <math|n> at fixed
    order. Induction therefore proves the asserted polynomial bounds. The
    identities <math|D*H<rsup|-1>=-H<rsup|-1>*<around|(|D*H|)>*H<rsup|-1>>
    and <math|D*log det H=<tr><around|(|H<rsup|-1>*D*H|)>> give the
    determinant assertion, with one additional factor <math|n> for the trace.
    These are standard implicit and Gaussian determinant differentiation
    rules; no polynomial-in-volume bound is used to select the radii. For an
    application requiring <math|k+4> derivatives, take the path/coordinate
    derivative order at least <math|k+6> so that the stated determinant
    margin is retained.
  </proof>

  <section|Scope of the completed Gaussian construction><label|ch05:pending>

  For each fixed smoothing order, the finite-history construction now
  provides uniformly analytic positive curvature metrics, the specified one-
  and two-form transports, and a positive three-form factor for the amended
  curvature defect. Its parameter choices precede the choice of the scale
  sequence. The nonlinear stationary graph is supplied for each incoming path
  action with the explicit local bound and actual quadratic coefficient
  condition just stated; its tree comparison and block average branch
  compatibility are now proved for the chosen block kernel.

  For the nested selected schedule, Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:horizon-limit>
  now also proves fixed-scale convergence of the actual quadratic rows at
  every smaller exponential width. For the actual nonlinear step, the chosen
  path representation and block average must be checked against
  <eqref|ch05:path-budget> and the actual quadratic coefficient condition
  <eqref|ch05:actual-hessian>. The common minimizing branch and equality of
  its local descriptions have been checked above. These are precise interface
  conditions, not consequences of Gaussian algebra. These conditions will be
  imposed on the admissible path action and retained by the nonlinear step; a
  Gaussian identity alone would not justify dropping them.

  <assign|chapter-nr|5>

  <chapter|Large fields and local Gaussian
  integration><label|ch06:large-fields>

  A block transformation integrates every configuration. The usefulness of
  small-field coordinates therefore depends on a bound for their complement.
  This chapter separates two mechanisms. Selected large plaquette fields and
  large block average discrepancies retain a deterministic positive energy.
  Failures of smooth local cutoffs in the remaining coordinates are
  controlled by simultaneous Gaussian tail estimates. These mechanisms use
  different estimates and must be combined under the same integral.

  We work at one adjacent pair of scales. The algebraic decompositions below
  are exact. The energy and differentiated integral estimates use uniform
  bounds for the chosen path rows and actual sector matrices. We construct
  these bounds for the same local integral below; the flat Gaussian
  identities alone would not supply them.

  <section|Selected tests and the local energy>

  Fix a stage, a block ratio <math|L>, the penalty <math|b\<gtr\>0>, and the
  fine and coarse path data of <eqref|ch04:path-action>. Throughout this
  chapter distances are coarse lattice distances, except that path cutoffs
  use true fine length. Write

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|f>=t<rsub|m+1>,<space|2em>t<rsub|c>=t<rsub|m>,<space|2em>B=B<rsub|m>,<space|2em>R<rsub|\<ast\>>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>,<space|2em>t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-49/100>.
  </equation*>

  The thresholds <math|t<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>*M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>>
  and <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> were defined in
  <eqref|ch04:thresholds>. At fixed <math|L>, as
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>,

  <\equation>
    <frac|t<rsub|f>|t<rsub|c>>\<longrightarrow\>1,<space|2em>B<rsup|n>*<frac|t<rsub|f>|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>B<rsup|n>*<frac|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>><rsup|2>|t<rsub|f>>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><around|(|n<text|
    fixed>|)>.<label|ch06:scales>
  </equation>

  The coarse torus period is at least <math|100*<around|(|B+1|)>>. All
  constants below are independent of that period.

  For a configuration pair <math|<around|(|U,V|)>> and the block average
  <math|Q<around|(|U|)>> of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>, put

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|p><rsup|f>=\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>,<space|2em>r<rsub|p><rsup|c>=\<chi\><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>,<space|2em>n<rsub|e>=\<chi\>*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<chi\>> is the normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance from the
  identity. A selection <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>>
  consists of fine plaquettes, coarse mismatch edges, and coarse sites. Its
  size is the sum of the three cardinalities. Its location contains the
  projected corners of its plaquettes, the endpoints of its edges, and its
  sites. Write <math|A<rsup|<around|[|r|]>>> for the coarse
  <math|r>-neighborhood of that location. Fix <math|W\<gtr\>0>. A selection
  is consistent if, on all tests meeting <math|A<rsup|<around|[|5*B|]>>>,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|r<rsub|p><rsup|f>\<geqslant\>t<rsub|f>/4*<space|1em><around|(|p\<in\>A<rsub|f>|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|r<rsub|p><rsup|f>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|f>/2*<space|1em><around|(|p\<nin\>A<rsub|f>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|n<rsub|e>\<geqslant\>W*t<rsub|f>*<space|1em><around|(|e\<in\>A<rsub|n>|)>,>|<cell|>|<cell|n<rsub|e>\<leqslant\>2*W*t<rsub|f>*<space|1em><around|(|e\<nin\>A<rsub|n>|)>,>>>>
  </align*>

  and <math|A<rsub|c>> is exactly the set of coarse sites incident to
  plaquettes with <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<gtr\>t<rsub|c>> in that
  neighborhood. Every chosen frame tag must have positive mass. The overlaps
  in the first two threshold pairs permit a partition with smooth cutoffs;
  consistency does not prescribe a unique selection.

  For the exact integral use a global allocation before forming components.
  Choose a smooth function <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|f>> equal to zero below
  <math|t<rsub|f>/4> and to one above <math|t<rsub|f>/2>, with values in
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, and an analogous <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|n>> for
  the thresholds <math|W*t<rsub|f>,2*W*t<rsub|f>>. Give a fine selection the
  weight

  <\equation*>
    <big|prod><rsub|p\<in\>A<rsub|f>>\<vartheta\><rsub|f><around|(|r<rsub|p><rsup|f>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|p\<nin\>A<rsub|f>><around|(|1-\<vartheta\><rsub|f><around|(|r<rsub|p><rsup|f>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and use the analogous product for mismatch edges. Summing over all
  selections gives one at every configuration. Take <math|A<rsub|c>> to be
  the actual coarse large-field set. Each nonzero global allocation obeys the
  consistency inequalities on every test, not merely within <math|5*B>. The
  weaker local definition above suffices for the core energy estimate; later
  component factors retain the global allocation's restrictions on their
  larger dependence regions. This refinement adds local eligibility tests
  without discarding any part of the original integral.

  An allocation sector fixes the selection, its assignment to connected
  components of its neighborhoods, a rooted spanning tree in every block, and
  all frame and block average tags. Tree links are set to the identity. These
  choices fix the coordinates and supports before differentiation. The set
  <math|D\<subset\>A<rsub|f>\<cup\>A<rsub|n>> consists of tests exceeding
  <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. Let <math|H> be the output anchors at distance
  at most <math|4*B/5> from <math|D>. Local counting gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||H|\|>\<leqslant\>K*B<rsup|4><around|\||D|\|>,<space|2em><around|\||A<rsup|<around|[|2*B|]>>|\|>\<leqslant\>K*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>.<label|ch06:counts>
  </equation>

  The constants may depend on the fixed stage and ratio.

  <subsection|An exact completion of squares>

  Let <math|F<around|(|g|)>=P<rsub|<gLie>>*<around|(|g-g<rsup|-1>|)>/2>, with
  the orthogonal projection taken in the fixed trace metric. Direct and path
  curvature rows are grouped as

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,f>>>*F<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>,L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|)>,<space|1em>X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,c>>>*F<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>,L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The <math|L<rsub|\<ast\>,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>> are the path sums of
  <eqref|ch04:path-action>, truncated at true fine length
  <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Let <math|\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>
  denote the relaxed small-field test of <eqref|ch04:relaxed-test>. Define
  capped coarse rows and local kinetic energies by

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<around*|(|\<alpha\><rsub|p>*<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,c>>>*F<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>,\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>><around|(|V|)>*L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|)>,>|<cell|\<alpha\><rsub|p>>|<cell|=min
    <around|(|1,t<rsub|c>/r<rsub|p><rsup|c>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|K<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|f>><around|(|r<rsub|p><rsup|f>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>><around|(|U|)>*<around|\||L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|\|><rsup|2>,>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|c>><around|(|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>><around|(|V|)>*<around|\||L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|\|><rsup|2>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
  </align*>

  Set <math|\<alpha\><rsub|p>=1> when <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>=0>. The clip is
  the Borel quadratic/linear function in <eqref|ch04:clip>, with its
  respective scale coefficients. In particular
  <math|<around|\||v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|f>>.

  For clarity the square-completion input can be stated without choosing a
  particular row factorization. Suppose the flat fine, penalty, and
  complement maps <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|n>,N> satisfy

  <\equation>
    M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=I,<space|2em>M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>=X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>>.<label|ch06:flat-identities>
  </equation>

  The superscript denotes linearized curvature and
  <math|Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>=<sqrt|b>*<around|(|v-Q*a|)>>, with the linear
  block average appropriate to the chosen fine/coarse pair. For the selected
  quadratic data these identities follow from the transport and
  positive-defect identities of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>. Here
  is their row realization, including the exact alignment premise. Let
  <math|C<rsub|\<nu\>>> map curvature to the direct and path rows actually
  used at scale <math|\<nu\>\<in\><around|{|f,c|}>>, and require
  <math|C<rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|\<nu\>>=<math-ss|H><rsub|\<nu\>>>,
  where <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|\<nu\>>> is the chosen modified curvature
  metric. This is the quadratic coefficient condition of
  <eqref|ch05:actual-hessian>; it is not a conclusion for an arbitrary path
  action. Put <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|\<nu\>>=<math-ss|H><rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|P<rsub|c>=C<rsub|c><math-ss|B><rsub|c>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
  The positive direct row makes <math|C<rsub|c>> injective, so
  <math|P<rsub|c>> is an orthogonal projection. Equation
  <eqref|ch05:positive-defect> supplies an exponentially local row <math|K>
  with

  <\equation*>
    K<rsup|\<ast\>>*K=<math-ss|B><rsub|c>-T<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|B><rsub|f>T-b<rsup|-1>*d<rsub|1,c>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Choose

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|f>=C<rsub|f><math-ss|B><rsub|f>T*C<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|n>=b<rsup|-1/2>*d<rsub|1,c><rsup|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>N=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I-P<rsub|c>>>|<row|<cell|K*C<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>>>>>.
  </equation*>

  Direct multiplication gives the first identity in
  <eqref|ch06:flat-identities>. For the second, use
  <math|X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>>=C<rsub|f>*d<rsub|1,f>*a>,
  <math|X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>>=C<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>*v>, and
  <math|T<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1,f>=d<rsub|1,c>*\<cal-Q\>>. The last identity
  is <eqref|ch05:physical-transports> with the actual block average of
  <eqref|ch05:actual-Q>: its extra coarse gradient has zero curl. The metric
  modification does not change these identities on curls. Thus both row
  identities hold for the same aligned action rows and actual block average.
  The selected path construction imposes this alignment: in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch07:classical> it chooses
  <math|C<rsub|c>=<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>*I,\<rho\>|)><rsup|T>>
  with <math|\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<rho\>=<math-ss|H><rsub|c>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*I>,
  and realizes these very rows by transported plaquettes. Cubic and quartic
  matching terms do not change their Hessian, while the separate loop
  correction is not part of the classical metric. The update in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch09:step-theorem> uses precisely this
  construction. At Wilson initialization the direct and additional rows have
  coefficients <math|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>> and
  <math|<sqrt|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>>, so their Gram matrix is <math|I>;
  the terminal fine metric is unmodified (<math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>=0>). The
  first coarse modification and every later one obey
  <eqref|ch05:positive-defect>. Thus alignment is an invariant of the
  selected construction, although it remains a necessary premise when
  applying the estimate to other path actions. Transport coefficients along
  their fixed coordinate routes using the replicated coarse field, and
  discard routes of fine length greater than <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Denote
  the resulting maps by <math|\<Phi\>,\<Phi\><rsub|n>,\<cal-N\>> and put
  <math|Z<around|(|e|)>=<sqrt|b>*F*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>.
  Their adjoints below are the actual transported adjoints.

  The rows <math|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>> and <math|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>> are
  the transported linear comparison terms. Throughout this chapter, a
  comparison term means one of these specified rows, not an independently
  chosen approximation.

  Define six anchored quantities:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|f>>|<cell|=K<rsub|f>+<tfrac|1|2>\|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>\|<rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>,X<rsub|f>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|n>>|<cell|=<tfrac|b|2>n<rsub|e><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>\|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>\|<rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>,Z|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|c>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c>,X<rsub|c>|\<rangle\>>-<tfrac|1|2>\|v<rsub|c>\|<rsup|2>-K<rsub|c>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|N>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|\||\<cal-N\>*v<rsub|c>|\|><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|L>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c>,\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|D>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c>,<around|(|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>|)>*v<rsub|c>|\<rangle\>>.<eq-number><label|ch06:six-terms>>>>>
  </align>

  Every expression is evaluated at its corresponding plaquette, edge, or
  complement-row anchor. On summing over the whole torus, the cross terms and
  squares cancel exactly:

  <\equation>
    <big|sum><around|(|E<rsub|f>+E<rsub|n>+E<rsub|c>+E<rsub|N>+E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>=<big|sum>K<rsub|f>+<frac|b|2>*<big|sum><rsub|e>n<rsub|e><rsup|2>-<big|sum>K<rsub|c>.<label|ch06:global-energy>
  </equation>

  This equality is algebraic and does not assume flatness. The local energy
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>> is instead the sum of the six terms at anchors in
  <math|A<rsup|<around|[|2*B|]>>>. Cancellation across its boundary is
  unavailable. The two signed defects <math|E<rsub|L>,E<rsub|D>> must
  therefore be estimated locally.

  <subsection|Direct bounds and the signed comparison>

  Assume the transported maps have absolute row and column sums bounded by
  <math|C*L<rsup|-2>> and <math|C*L<rsup|2>> for <math|\<Phi\>>,
  respectively, and by <math|C> for <math|\<Phi\><rsub|n>,\<cal-N\>>. Bounds
  involving <math|L<rsup|-2>> below require <math|C> fixed before choosing
  <math|L>. Put

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|f>=<big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>A<rsub|f>:r<rsub|p><rsup|f>\<gtr\>t<rsub|f>>r<rsub|p><rsup|f>,<space|2em>R<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  The direct fine term, when <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|f>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|f>>,
  is at least <math|<tfrac|1|2><around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,f>>>*r<rsub|p><rsup|f>-<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>*<around|(|p,0|)>|\|>|)><rsup|2>>.
  Since <math|<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*L<rsup|-2>*t<rsub|f>>,
  a selected plaquette in this range contributes at least
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,f>>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>/128> for large <math|L>.
  Above <math|t<rsub|f>> the linear clip retains a multiple of
  <math|t<rsub|f>*r<rsub|p><rsup|f>>.

  There is one additional loss: a path row whose relaxed test fails is no
  longer part of a square. Such a row based at <math|p> has a witness
  <math|q> with

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|q><rsup|f>\<gtr\>t<rsub|f>*exp
    <around|(|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,q|)>/R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a fixed witness the number of possible bases is at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
  <around|(|r<rsub|q><rsup|f>/t<rsub|f>|)>|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsub|q><rsup|f>/t<rsub|f>>.
  All witnesses lie within <math|B/50> for sufficiently large reference
  coupling. They belong to the selected set by consistency. Split path
  endpoints into those with distance from the identity at most
  <math|t<rsub|f>> and the rest. The row bound handles the former, and the
  column bound handles the latter, giving

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|<text|omitted rows >p><around|\||L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|f>.
  </equation*>

  Multiplication by <math|<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*L<rsup|-2>*t<rsub|f>>
  absorbs this loss into the linear clip after increasing <math|L>.
  Consequently

  <\equation>
    <big|sum>E<rsub|f>\<geqslant\><frac|c<rsub|<math-up|lin,f>>|2>*t<rsub|f>*R<rsub|f>+<frac|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,f>>|128>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||<around|{|p\<in\>A<rsub|f>:r<rsub|p><rsup|f>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|f>|}>|\|>.<label|ch06:fine-bound>
  </equation>

  The mismatch square similarly gives, for sufficiently large <math|W> after
  <math|L>,

  <\equation>
    <big|sum>E<rsub|n>\<geqslant\><frac|b|8>*<big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e><rsup|2>.<label|ch06:mismatch-bound>
  </equation>

  The coarse path contribution cancels exactly in <math|E<rsub|c>>. The
  direct part is <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,c>>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|p>-\<alpha\><rsub|p><rsup|2>/2|)>\|F<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\|<rsup|2>-<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|c>><around|(|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>|)>>.
  The squared normal displacement from the tangent space is
  <math|O<around|(|\<chi\><around|(|g|)><rsup|4>|)>>, so
  <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<chi\><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>-C*\<chi\><around|(|g|)><rsup|4>>
  near the identity. Choose fixed <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> so that also
  <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>3*\<chi\><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>/4>
  below <math|\<delta\>>. The clip coefficients satisfy
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,c>>\<gtr\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin,c>>>, and hence

  <\equation>
    E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-C*t<rsub|c><rsup|4>,>|<cell|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|c>,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir,c>>*t<rsub|c>*r<rsub|p><rsup|c>/4,>|<cell|t<rsub|c>\<less\>r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>,>>|<row|<cell|-c<rsub|<math-up|lin,c>>*t<rsub|c>*r<rsub|p><rsup|c>,>|<cell|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<gtr\>\<delta\>.>>>>><label|ch06:coarse-bound>
  </equation>

  The remaining ingredient is genuinely about the chosen nonabelian paths and
  block average. Its quantitative form is recorded explicitly.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Local signed comparison from compatible
    rows><label|ch06:signed-input>Assume <eqref|ch06:flat-identities>, the
    stated transported row and column bounds, and a common positive
    exponential bound for the uncut path kernels, with their coefficients
    aligned with the curvature rows used in those identities. Then, on every
    consistent sector for the block average of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>, there are <math|C,K> fixed before
    <math|L,W> and <math|Z=Z<rsub|j,L,W>> such that

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|H><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin,c>>*t<rsub|c>*<big|sum><rsub|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>r<rsub|p><rsup|c>-C*t<rsub|f>*<around|(|L<rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>-Z*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*B<rsup|4><around|\||D|\|>,<eq-number><label|ch06:affected>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|A<rsup|<around|[|2*B|]>>\<setminus\>H><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>-<frac|a|4>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>.<eq-number><label|ch06:unaffected>>>>>
    </align>

    Every coarse plaquette with <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<gtr\>\<delta\>> lies
    in <math|H> and their number is at most <math|K<around|\||D|\|>>. Here

    <\equation*>
      a=min <around|{|c<rsub|<math-up|lin,f>>/8,c<rsub|<math-up|dir,f>>/128,b/32,c<rsub|<math-up|dir,c>>/64|}>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    In a lifted patch of radius <math|<around|(|j+7|)>*L>, let
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|q>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>*r<rsub|q><rsup|f>>
    be the sum of fine plaquette distances from the identity with the actual
    route multiplicities. The required comparison is

    <\equation>
      r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>\<partial\>*p>n<rsub|e>+C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|q>
      <big|sum><rsub|y>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j>.<label|ch06:macro-comparison>
    </equation>

    This estimate does hold for the precise branches of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>, as the following argument shows.
    It is a first-power bound; comparison of squared frame energies alone
    would give an inadequate square-root bound.

    The first-power block average estimate is
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:predictor-comparison>; its comparison uses the
    same macro-frame and rooted paths as here.

    The same bound localizes coarse plaquettes with
    <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>. If every fine or mismatch test
    in their fixed macro-patch is at most <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>,
    <eqref|ch06:macro-comparison> bounds <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>> by
    <math|C<rsub|j,L>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>\<less\>\<delta\>> after increasing
    the coupling threshold. Thus each such plaquette has a test in <math|D>
    in that patch. The fixed patch fits inside the <math|B>-neighborhood used
    to define <math|H>, and bounded macro-patch multiplicity gives the
    asserted count <math|K<around|\||D|\|>>. Separating the small-distance
    contribution from the selected excess gives
    <math|r<rsub|p><rsup|c>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,L,W>*t<rsub|f>+\<Delta\><rsub|p>>
    with <math|<big|sum><rsub|p>\<Delta\><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|L<rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>>.
    The constant multiplying the excess must remain independent of
    <math|L,W>. Applying the transported row and column bounds then gives
    <eqref|ch06:affected>; the positive displayed term is obtained by
    absorbing its upper bound into the enlarged loss constants. It is not a
    new positivity assertion for <math|E<rsub|L>>.

    Outside <math|H>, all fine plaquette distances from the identity and
    block average mismatches in the surrounding fixed macro-patches are at
    most <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. The unselected tests obey this too,
    since <math|2*W*t<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> eventually.
    The verified macro-comparison bounds coarse plaquette distances from the
    identity there by <math|C<rsub|j,L,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. Choose a
    coarse axial gauge on a box of radius <math|B/2>. Its sum of bond
    distances from the identity is bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|j,L,W>*B<rsup|5>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. To compare a fine
    link with the replicated coarse link, join its two endpoints to their
    block roots along the actual tree. All tree transports are the identity.
    The resulting walk lies in two neighboring blocks. In a macro axial gauge
    its distance from the identity is bounded by the route length times the
    sum of bond distances from the identity in the macro-box. Comparing a
    crossing walk with the block average adds the two endpoint frame errors
    already bounded in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:predictor-comparison>, and
    then the mismatch <math|n<rsub|e>>. Consequently, with a constant
    depending on the fixed tree family and <math|L>,

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\><around*|(|U<rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,L>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>+n<rsub|e<rsub|c>>
    </equation*>

    for crossing edges; omit the mismatch for internal edges. Any tree in a
    fixed block has uniformly bounded path length (at most
    <math|L<rsup|4>-1>), which suffices here because this constant is chosen
    after <math|L>. This is not a pre-<math|L> excess estimate. The parent
    lift of the coarse gauge preserves these distances between corresponding
    links. It therefore places both fine and coarse logarithms in a common
    chart, throughout radius <math|2*B/5>, with bound
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>=C<rsub|j,L,W>*B<rsup|10>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>.

    The derivative of the actual rooted block average also matches the flat
    identity. Near the identity all selected frame and block average branches
    are minimizing; the redundant tags do not change their values. The
    finite-patch minimizing frame is smooth there by the same nondegenerate
    implicit equation used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch04:frame-lemma>. Write
    <math|h<rsub|y,z><around|(|exp <around|(|\<epsilon\>*a|)>|)>=1+\<epsilon\>*H<rsub|y,z><around|(|a|)>+O<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|<wide|H|\<bar\>><rsub|y>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*H<rsub|y,z>>.
    Differentiating <eqref|ch04:connector>, and then its nearest-point block
    average, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<cal-Q\>*a|)><rsub|y,\<mu\>>=<around|(|\<cal-A\>*a|)><rsub|y,\<mu\>>+<wide|H|\<bar\>><rsub|y><around|(|a|)>-<wide|H|\<bar\>><rsub|y+e<rsub|\<mu\>>><around|(|a|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<cal-A\>> averages the straight length-<math|L> link sums
    with the same weights. Thus <math|\<cal-Q\>-\<cal-A\>> is a coarse
    gradient. Its curl vanishes exactly. This verifies the block average
    identification needed in <eqref|ch06:flat-identities>. Their aligned row
    realization was derived above from <eqref|ch05:physical-transports> and
    <eqref|ch05:positive-defect>; the incoming path action must still satisfy
    the exact quadratic coefficient condition in <eqref|ch05:actual-hessian>.
    Second-order Taylor bounds are uniform in the period because each block
    average reads a fixed finite patch at fixed <math|j,L>.

    Set <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>=<around|(|1+R<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>+e<rsup|-\<tau\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
    where <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0> is a common path decay rate. Subtracting the
    flat identities <eqref|ch06:flat-identities> before estimating terms
    gives the required bounds

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||<around|(|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>|)>*v<rsub|c>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|f>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Indeed a transport of length <math|s> differs from its flat value by at
    most <math|C*s*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>; the omitted tails have
    size <math|C*e<rsup|-\<tau\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. The count
    <eqref|ch06:counts> and the limits <eqref|ch06:scales> yield
    <eqref|ch06:unaffected>. This completes the verification of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:signed-input>. It uses the aligned flat
    identities for the chosen path family, not merely a decay estimate for a
    flat Fourier kernel.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Energy bound under the local comparisons><label|ch06:energy>Under
    the hypotheses of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:signed-input>, choose
    <math|L> sufficiently large, then <math|W> sufficiently large, then the
    reference coupling sufficiently large. Uniformly in the period and
    consistent sector,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|A>*<around|(|U,V|)>\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>,<space|2em>\<kappa\>=a/2\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    The threshold may depend on <math|j,L,W>; <math|a> is fixed by the direct
    and linear clip coefficients and <math|b>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The bounds <eqref|ch06:fine-bound> and <eqref|ch06:mismatch-bound> leave
    <math|a*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>> per selected fine or mismatch test,
    <math|a*t<rsub|f>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> per test in <math|D>, and enough
    linear excess to absorb <math|C*t<rsub|f>*<around|(|L<rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>>.
    The choices are in that order: increase <math|L> for the fine excess, and
    <math|W> for the mismatch excess.

    Assign each coarse large-field site to one incident plaquette with
    distance from the identity above <math|t<rsub|c>>. A plaquette has at
    most four such sites. Since <math|t<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*t<rsub|c>>
    eventually, the middle range of <eqref|ch06:coarse-bound> contributes
    <math|4*a*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>> per plaquette and therefore
    <math|a*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>> per assigned site. The exceptional plaquettes
    with distance from the identity above <math|\<delta\>> number at most
    <math|K<around|\||D|\|>>; their negative energies cancel the positive
    term in <eqref|ch06:affected>. Their missing site contributions cost at
    most <math|4*a*K*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||D|\|>>. Also
    <math|E<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0>. The total remaining losses are bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|4*a*K+Z*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*<around|\||D*<around|\||+C*t<rsub|c><rsup|4>*K*B<rsup|4>|\|>*A*<around|\||+<frac|a|4>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>|\|>*A|\|>.
    </equation*>

    Increase the coupling threshold until
    <math|<around|(|4*a*K+Z*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a*t<rsub|f>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>
    and <math|C*t<rsub|c><rsup|4>*K*B<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>a*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>/4>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch06:scales> justifies both choices. The first loss
    is absorbed by the positive energy contribution retained for <math|D>,
    leaving at least <math|a*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>/2>.
  </proof>

  <section|The actual sector Gaussian><label|ch06:sector>

  Fix the sector and the frozen free links <math|Y>. Tree links are not
  integration variables. Active coordinates are logarithms of the remaining
  free links outside the selected <math|10*B>-neighborhood. Linearize the
  retained square terms at the actual replicated coarse field, including
  their actual block average and fixed cutoffs. Write their affine stack as

  <\equation>
    Q<rsub|Y><around|(|u|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\||<math-ss|D>u+b<rsub|Y>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em><math-ss|A>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|D>,<space|2em>f<rsub|Y>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>>b<rsub|Y>.<label|ch06:actual-matrix>
  </equation>

  If hypercubic orientations are averaged, the stack uses the square roots of
  their weights. A row is retained when its fixed stencil meets an active
  edge. This rule differs from retaining rows whose anchors are a specified
  distance from the selected set. A comparison between the corresponding
  matrices requires a proof. Identity coordinates may be adjoined for
  inactive variables, with their trivial integrals normalized to one.

  The stencil contains the block average patch and every endpoint of a
  retained path. Its coarse radius is at most
  <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>+5+r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>\<leqslant\>B/50> for large
  coupling. Thus a retained stencil lies in the small-field region when its
  active edge is outside the selected <math|10*B>-neighborhood. This
  geometric observation permits local estimates at the actual field; it does
  not by itself prove a lower bound on <math|<math-ss|A>>.

  <subsection|Transferring a local tree estimate to the actual rows>

  The gap needed below can be obtained without identifying actual and flat
  matrices. The geometric comparison just proved implies a local inequality
  on the specified block-tree slice:

  <\equation>
    <around|\||a<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*<big|sum><rsub|p\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|e>><around|\||<around|(|d<rsub|f>*a|)><rsub|p>|\|>+<around|\||\<cal-Q\>*a<around|(|e<rsub|c>|)>|\|>.<label|ch06:tree-control>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|e>> is a fixed macro-patch of fine plaquettes,
  <math|e<rsub|c>> is the coarse edge in the direction of <math|e>, and
  <math|\<cal-Q\>> is the derivative of the actual block average at the
  identity. The inequality is for arrays vanishing on the chosen tree,
  extended by zero on frozen edges. For internal edges the last term may be
  omitted. Its nonlinear origin is worth specifying: join the endpoints of
  <math|e> to their block roots along the same tree, compare the resulting
  transport with the block average in a common macro-frame, and bound the
  discrepancy by the macro-patch plaquette sum. Differentiating this
  comparison at the identity gives <eqref|ch06:tree-control>. The route
  constants and endpoint bounds are those proved above for the same trees and
  tags. No coordinate change to an unrelated tree is made.

  The tree comparison <eqref|ch05:tree-comparison> applies to these
  coordinates: the replica is varied by left multiplication on free bonds,
  tree coordinates are zero, and the block average is the actual rooted block
  average of <eqref|ch05:actual-Q>. Thus the selected direct and block
  average rows are exactly its derivatives, multiplied by their fixed
  positive square weights. Each such derivative differs, in one common gauge
  on its macro-patch, from its flat derivative by at most
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> times the sum of input norms on its fixed stencil.
  This follows from continuity of the finite-patch derivative on the
  minimizing branch once clearance and small coarse plaquettes put that
  entire patch in a small common gauge. Insert the comparison in
  <eqref|ch06:tree-control>, square, and sum over active edges. Fixed stencil
  multiplicities give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*<big|sum><rsub|p><around|\||D<rsub|p>*a*<around|\||<rsup|2>+C<rsub|L>*<big|sum><rsub|e>|\|>*D<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>*a|\|><rsup|2>+C<rsub|L>*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  All norms here are counting norms, without division by volume. The row
  selector introduces no error: an omitted row has a stencil disjoint from
  the active coordinates, hence annihilates their zero extension. For
  <math|C<rsub|L>*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1/2> and positive lower
  direct and penalty coefficients, this proves
  <math|<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|D>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|L>*I>. The
  other rows add nonnegative squares. The gauge may depend on the edge at
  which the local inequality is applied, but it is fixed during
  differentiation; invariance of the color norm permits the subsequent sum.

  This provides the precise route from a local tree comparison to the actual
  matrix gap. It leaves no need to compare whole determinants of actual and
  flat matrices. The identification here is local and preserves the original
  forest; row selection only removes terms annihilating the zero extension.
  Their constants may depend on the fixed ratio <math|L>, unlike the excess
  constant in <eqref|ch06:macro-comparison>, which is needed before <math|L>
  is chosen.

  <subsection|Local inverse columns>

  For an edge coordinate <math|i>, let <math|E<rsub|i>> be its two projected
  endpoints. Two useful symmetric windows are

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|sec>>>|<cell|=<around|{|i<rprime|'>:max<rsub|x\<in\>E<rsub|i>,y\<in\>E<rsub|i<rprime|'>>><math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>B/20|}>,>>|<row|<cell|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>|<cell|=<around|{|i<rprime|'>:min<rsub|x\<in\>E<rsub|i>,y\<in\>E<rsub|i<rprime|'>>><math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>B|}>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Restrict them to the active coordinate set. Both contain a ball of radius
  <math|B/60> about <math|i> for large <math|B>. Use one prescription
  throughout a given integral and define

  <\equation>
    P<rsub|i<rprime|'>*i>=\<b-1\><rsub|i<rprime|'>\<in\>\<Omega\><rsub|i>><around|(|<math-ss|A><around|[|\<Omega\><rsub|i>,\<Omega\><rsub|i>|]><rsup|-1>|)><rsub|i<rprime|'>*i>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|P+P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>m<rsub|Y>=-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*f<rsub|Y>.<label|ch06:windows-def>
  </equation>

  The matrix <math|\<Gamma\>> is finite range. Symmetry alone does not make
  it a covariance; positivity follows from the following comparison.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Window estimates from actual matrix
    bounds><label|ch06:windows>Assume <math|c*I\<leqslant\><math-ss|A>\<leqslant\>C*I>
    and an exponentially weighted Schur bound <math|sup<rsub|i>
    <big|sum><rsub|i<rprime|'>>e<rsup|\<tau\>*d*<around|(|i,i<rprime|'>|)>><around|\||<math-ss|A><rsub|i*i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C>,
    with the analogous column bound. Assume bounded coordinate multiplicity
    per coarse site. Then, for some <math|\<eta\>,c<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>,
    uniformly in the active set, period, and principal compression,

    <\equation>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'>*B>.<label|ch06:window-error>
    </equation>

    Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>> is the
    maximum of the exponentially weighted row and column sums. For
    sufficiently large <math|B>, <math|c<rsub|1>*I\<leqslant\>\<Gamma\>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|1>*I>.
    Fixed-order derivatives obey corresponding bounds if the actual matrix
    has them, with all windows and selected coordinate sets held fixed.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The weighted conjugation argument of Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:inverse>
    gives exponential inverse bounds for <math|<math-ss|A>> and all its
    principal compressions. The constants are uniform because a principal
    compression preserves the gap and the weighted bound. The column
    <math|P*e<rsub|i>> solves <math|<math-ss|A>P*e<rsub|i>=e<rsub|i>> inside
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i>>. Outside that window its residual crosses a
    distance at least <math|B/60> from <math|i>. The convolution of two
    exponentially decaying kernels therefore gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|A>P-I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'>*B>>
    after decreasing the decay rate. Multiplication by
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>> proves the first estimate in
    <eqref|ch06:window-error>; adjunction proves the second. Operator norm is
    bounded by Schur norm, so comparison with
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>C<rsup|-1>*I> proves positivity.

    For derivatives, use <math|D<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>=-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1><around|(|D<math-ss|A>|)><math-ss|A><rsup|-1>>
    and its iterates, also on the compressions. Every term in a
    differentiated residual still crosses the window boundary. Polynomial
    spatial weights are moved along a kernel using
    <math|1+d*<around|(|o,i<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+d*<around|(|o,i|)>|)>*<around|(|1+d*<around|(|i,i<rprime|'>|)>|)>>
    and absorbed by a smaller exponential rate. These operations leave the
    factor <math|e<rsup|-c<rprime|'>*B>> at any fixed order.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The source and derivatives on a fixed sector>

  The source in <eqref|ch06:actual-matrix> uses a bounded extension of the
  frozen logarithms. This detail is essential. Let <math|v<rsub|Y>> be the
  patched, smoothly clipped frozen displacement, extended by zero off the
  frozen coordinates, with <math|<around|\||v<rsub|Y>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2*d>
  and <math|d\<leqslant\>r*t<rsub|f>>, for fixed <math|r>. If
  <math|b<around|(|V|)>> is the row value at the replicated field and
  <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|<math-up|all>><around|(|V|)>> is its derivative on
  all free coordinates, including the frozen ones, the source is

  <\equation>
    b<rsub|Y>=b<around|(|V|)>+<math-ss|D><rsub|<math-up|all>><around|(|V|)>*v<rsub|Y>,<space|2em>f<rsub|Y>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>>b<rsub|Y>.<label|ch06:clipped-source>
  </equation>

  The row weights are included in both terms. The original integrand is
  preserved by putting the difference from this affine square into the local
  correction factors. No estimate below replaces an arbitrary frozen Haar
  logarithm by a small logarithm.

  Here is why the source is uniformly small. A retained row has an active
  coordinate outside the <math|10*B> neighborhood of the selected set. Its
  whole stencil has radius at most <math|B/50>; hence its anchor, endpoints,
  and fixed macro-patches have more than <math|9*B> clearance for large
  <math|B>. All coarse tests used by the row are therefore small. In the
  common local gauge, the four-link curvature formula, the absolute
  path-coefficient bounds, and the smooth block average branch give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||b<around|(|V|)>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|b>*t<rsub|f>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|D><rsub|<math-up|all>><around|(|V|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a>.
  </equation*>

  The comparison term is extended without its large-field indicator when the
  field is varied. Thus <eqref|ch06:clipped-source> implies
  <math|<around|\||b<rsub|Y>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|C<rsub|b>+2*r*C<rsub|a>|)>*t<rsub|f>>,
  uniformly over the frozen Haar variables. The column bound for
  <math|<math-ss|D>> now gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\||f<rsub|Y>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around|\||m<rsub|Y>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|f>,<space|2em><around|\||<math-ss|A>m<rsub|Y>+f<rsub|Y>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-c*B>.<label|ch06:actual-forcing>
  </equation>

  The second inequality uses <math|<math-ss|A>m<rsub|Y>+f<rsub|Y>=<around|(|I-<math-ss|A>P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*f<rsub|Y>>
  and the window estimate. In particular <math|g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|A>m<rsub|Y>+f<rsub|Y>|)>>
  remains exponentially small for <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>> on the
  coupling band: <math|g<rsup|-1>*t<rsub|f>=M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> up
  to fixed constants grows slower than <math|e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*B>>.

  For clarity, the corresponding derivative assertion has a different scale.
  For any fixed allowed order <math|k>, use dimensionless variation
  parameters whose coarse link increments and clipped frozen displacements
  have derivatives bounded by <math|C<rsub|k>*t<rsub|f>>. Keep the selector,
  forest, and windows fixed. Differentiating a path word costs a fixed power
  of its length. The exponential path bound absorbs that power after
  decreasing its decay rate. A derivative with anchor <math|o> can occur only
  on a word whose dependence region contains <math|o>; its distance to the
  row is bounded by the word length plus the fixed block average radius.
  Consequently the same argument bounds the derivatives of <math|<math-ss|D>>
  in exponentially weighted row and column norms, uniformly in volume.
  Products give the bounds for <math|<math-ss|A>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|D>>.
  All fixed positive-order derivatives of
  <math|b<rsub|Y>,f<rsub|Y>,m<rsub|Y>> are <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|f>|)>> in
  these normalized directions, and the residual derivatives retain the factor
  <math|e<rsup|-c<rsub|k>*B>>. Unscaled derivatives instead carry the
  corresponding powers of <math|t<rsub|f><rsup|-1>>.

  These derivatives exist on a common neighborhood, not just on the set
  satisfying the large-field indicator inequalities. Indeed the row formulas
  with the event indicator omitted extend smoothly there. If their first
  derivative norm is at most <math|C>, a ball of radius
  <math|c<rsub|L>/<around|[|2*<around|(|1+C|)>|]>> preserves the actual gap
  <math|c<rsub|L>/2>. Every principal compression remains invertible on that
  ball. Inverse differentiation and the proof of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:windows> therefore apply with fixed
  selectors even if nearby fields change their large-field indicators. The
  radius need not be uniform in arbitrary unnormalized coordinates.

  <subsection|The exact integral and its normalization>

  Let <math|n> be the real dimension of the active coordinate space. For any
  <math|m\<in\><RR><rsup|n>>, positive covariance <math|\<Gamma\>>, and
  integrable factor <math|F>, the ordinary Gaussian change of variables
  <math|u=m+g*z> gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsub|<RR><rsup|n>>e<rsup|-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|Y><around|(|u|)>>*F<around|(|u|)>*<space|0.17em>d*u>|<cell|=\<cal-C\><rsub|Y>*<big|int>F*<around|(|m+g*z|)>*exp
    <around|{|\<eta\>\<cdot\>z+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|-1>-<math-ss|A>|)>*z|}>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|z|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-C\><rsub|Y>>|<cell|=<around|(|2*\<pi\>*g<rsup|2>|)><rsup|n/2><around|(|det
    \<Gamma\>|)><rsup|1/2>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|Y><around|(|m|)>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<eta\>>|<cell|=-g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|A>m+f<rsub|Y>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch06:gaussian-identity>>>>>
  </align>

  This is the usual finite-dimensional completion of squares with an
  arbitrary reference covariance. We use it as an identity, not an
  approximation. In particular <math|m=m<rsub|Y>> and
  <eqref|ch06:windows-def> do not remove the linear or quadratic residual in
  the exponent.

  In a sector integral <math|F> includes the chart indicators, the Haar
  density of every active link, the branch masses, incoming activities, and
  all remaining action terms. It is extended by zero off its original chart.
  The frozen variables are subsequently integrated with probability Haar
  measure <math|d*Y>. When <math|\<Gamma\>> depends on <math|Y>, the
  resulting reference law is

  <\equation*>
    d*Y*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\><around|(|Y|)>><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is a conditional Gaussian law, generally not a product of an independent
  Haar field and a fixed Gaussian. The positive scalar
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|Y>> must be retained with the Haar Jacobians and
  relative determinants. A change of tree coordinates preserves this combined
  integral, not its determinant factor in isolation. Zero-dimensional active
  sectors have determinant one and no Gaussian variables.

  <section|Differentiation on a common dependence region>

  The cutoffs and large-field indicators in the sector definition are fixed
  Borel tests. Their indicators are not differentiated. Even on a fixed
  sector an incoming large-field activity can be merely measurable.
  Differentiating it as a function of its fine links would be unjustified. A
  local change of variables instead keeps those physical fine links fixed.

  Write an active physical link as <math|U<rsub|e>=exp
  <around|(|u<rsub|e>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>>.
  At a base field <math|V<rsub|0>>, set <math|u=m<rsub|Y><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>+g*z>.
  Within a protected logarithmic chart define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>>|<cell|=log
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|exp <around|(|u<rsub|e>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V<rsub|0>|)><rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|z<rsub|V>>|<cell|=g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|u<rsub|V>-m<rsub|Y><around|(|V|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch06:protected-change>>>>>
  </align>

  Then <math|exp <around|(|u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>=U<rsub|e>>
  exactly. The following extension makes this a legitimate change on the
  whole Gaussian space.

  Choose a fixed logarithm radius <math|d<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> and a smooth
  radial function <math|\<chi\>> equal to one on
  <math|<around|\||u|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>> and zero on
  <math|<around|\||u|\|>\<geqslant\>2*d<rsub|\<ast\>>>. For a group element
  <math|M> sufficiently near the identity put

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|M><around|(|u|)>=u+\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>,<space|2em>\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>=\<chi\><around|(|u|)>*<around|{|log
    <around|(|exp <around|(|u|)>*M|)>-u|}>.
  </equation*>

  The logarithm is used only on the support of <math|\<chi\>>. At <math|M=1>,
  <math|\<psi\>=0> and <math|D<rsub|u>*\<psi\>=0>. Compactness of that
  support permits one common neighborhood of <math|1> on which
  <math|<around|\||\<psi\>|\|>\<less\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>/2> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|u>*\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/2>. For every
  target <math|v>, the map <math|u\<mapsto\>v-\<psi\>*<around|(|M,u|)>> is a
  contraction of the complete Euclidean space, so <math|F<rsub|M>> is
  bijective. Its derivative is invertible, and the inverse function theorem
  gives a smooth inverse. Its determinant is positive by continuity from
  <math|M=1>. All fixed parameter derivatives of <math|\<psi\>> and of this
  determinant are bounded uniformly in <math|u>, since they vanish outside a
  fixed compact set. These are the usual finite-dimensional contraction and
  inverse-function arguments.

  Apply this map separately on each active bond with
  <math|M<rsub|e><around|(|V|)>=<around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V<rsub|0>|)><rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>>,
  and set

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|V>*z=g<rsup|-1>*<around|{|F<rsub|M<around|(|V|)>>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>+g*z|)>-m<rsub|Y><around|(|V|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The without the event indicator extension of <math|m<rsub|Y>> described
  above is used off the valid sector. The map is globally smooth and
  invertible, and its Jacobian is the product of the positive bond Jacobians;
  the factors <math|g> cancel. If a protected factor is supported where
  <math|<around|\||u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>8*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|f>>
  and <math|8*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>, then
  <math|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>\<less\>d<rsub|\<ast\>>> by the displacement
  bound. There <math|F<rsub|M>> equals the logarithmic map and holds the
  physical link fixed. Nested cutoffs, with the smaller support inside
  <math|<around|\||u<rsub|V>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|f>>,
  leave a strict margin. Thus a protected measurable factor is constant on a
  neighborhood of the base parameter whenever it contributes; it does not
  have to be differentiated. Cutoff remainders must likewise vanish on a
  neighborhood, rather than only at the base point.

  Use one coordinate change on the union <math|I> of all coordinates read by
  the product under consideration. If <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I>> is its
  marginal covariance, the density ratio contributed by that change is

  <\equation>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V,z|)>=<around|\||det
    D<rsub|z>*z<rsub|V>|\|><space|0.17em><frac|<around|(|det
    \<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|-z<rsub|V><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|V>/2>|<around|(|det
    \<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|-z<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>*z/2>>.<label|ch06:density-ratio>
  </equation>

  The marginal is the principal covariance block <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I>>,
  whose inverse is generally not a principal block of
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|-1>>. Applying separate changes to overlapping factors
  would not reproduce this common ratio.

  The familiar derivative formulas

  <\equation*>
    D*log det \<Gamma\><rsub|I>=<tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*D*\<Gamma\><rsub|I>|)>,<space|2em>D*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>=-\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<around|(|D*\<Gamma\><rsub|I>|)>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  show that the logarithmic derivative of the Gaussian part is

  <\equation*>
    -<tfrac|1|2><tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<dot\>><rsub|I>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<dot\>><rsub|I>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*z-z<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|z|\<dot\>>.
  </equation*>

  Higher fixed derivatives are polynomials in the coordinates, with
  coefficients controlled by the window estimates and the coordinate change.
  Ordinary Gaussian moment formulas, in particular Isserlis'
  formula<nbsp><cite|Isserlis1918>, then give polynomial bounds in
  <math|<around|\||I|\|>> for fixed derivative order. The trace costs a
  factor proportional to <math|<around|\||I|\|>>; it is not uniformly bounded
  independently of the dependence region. For local factors with <math|s>
  anchors one typically has <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|4>*s>.

  A factor's dependence region includes every cutoff test, block average
  path, window matrix entry and frozen coordinate needed to evaluate it. This
  region is different from the exclusion support <math|Y<rsub|i>> in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ch04:activities>. Disjoint exclusion supports
  imply no independence. Even disjoint coordinate sets give conditional
  Gaussian independence only when their cross covariance vanishes.
  Integration over shared frozen variables can destroy that independence
  after conditioning is removed.

  <subsection|Differentiation under the common Gaussian law>

  There is a useful distinction between the bounds used to justify
  differentiation and those used to estimate the resulting derivatives. The
  first may depend on the fixed finite volume. Since <math|\<psi\>> has
  compact support and the frozen logarithms are clipped, <math|T<rsub|V>*z-z>
  and its fixed parameter derivatives are bounded uniformly in <math|z> and
  in the frozen variables on one neighborhood. If
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\>*I>,
  shrink that neighborhood so <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\>*I/2>.
  The Gaussian density ratio and its first <math|k> derivatives are then
  bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>><around|(|z|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-\<lambda\><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>/8>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|2*k>,
  </equation*>

  with a finite constant <math|C>. Multiplication by the reference Gaussian
  density makes this integrable. The Jacobian has bounded derivatives. For
  the local correction factors, the endpoint cutoffs ensure bounded arguments
  whenever the factor is nonzero. Hence their derivatives are also bounded on
  that neighborhood. Dominated differentiation follows. For a quadratic
  correction this last assertion requires both endpoints of every nonzero
  matrix term to carry the protecting cutoff; it is not a consequence of a
  cutoff at only one endpoint.

  At the base point <math|T<rsub|V<rsub|0>>*z=z> and the ratio is one. Its
  differentiated logarithm is a polynomial of degree at most two in <math|z>
  and the derivatives of <math|T<rsub|V>*z>, and repeated exponential
  differentiation gives a fixed-degree polynomial bound. Matrix products are
  estimated in row and column norms; a trace costs <math|<around|\||I|\|>>.
  Therefore, if <math|s> counts the factor anchors and
  <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|4>*s>, there are fixed integers
  <math|r<rsub|k>,m<rsub|k>> such that

  <\equation>
    <around|\||D<rsup|j>*\<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V<rsub|0>,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|k><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|m<rsub|k>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||I|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|r<rsub|k>>,<space|1em>j\<leqslant\>k,<label|ch06:base-scores>
  </equation>

  where <math|A<rsub|k>> is subpower, meaning
  <math|A<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*x<rsup|\<epsilon\>>>
  eventually for each <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. In this statement the
  normalized variation derivatives of <math|g<rsup|-1>*m<rsub|Y>> and of
  <math|g<rsup|-1>*\<psi\>> must have subpower bounds; these follow from
  <eqref|ch06:actual-forcing> and <math|t<rsub|f>/g\<asymp\>M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>>.
  The relevant marginal inverses have uniform operator bounds; when row
  bounds are used, a polynomial factor in <math|<around|\||I|\|>> suffices
  and is already allowed in <eqref|ch06:base-scores>. No assertion about
  independence of the coordinates is involved.

  <section|Simultaneous tail estimates>

  A cutoff remainder is bounded but need not be pointwise small. Its
  smallness comes from a large-coordinate event. An event label specifies one
  particular cutoff and its anchor; repeated appearances of that event label
  must be counted once. Suppose each remainder with event label <math|w>
  vanishes unless

  <\equation>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|w>=<around|{|\<exists\>i:d*<around|(|i,w|)>\<leqslant\>2*B,<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>T|}>,<space|2em>T=\<xi\>*t<rsub|f>/<around|(|2*g|)>,<label|ch06:tail-event>
  </equation>

  for fixed <math|\<xi\>\<gtr\>0>. Verifying this implication uses the actual
  mean, block average response, and event indicators. A covariance estimate
  by itself cannot prove it.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Joint tails with bounded local
    multiplicity><label|ch06:joint-tails>Let <math|z> be centered Gaussian
    with covariance <math|C\<leqslant\>M*I>. Suppose an event label has at
    most <math|N> possible witness coordinates, and each coordinate can
    witness at most <math|Q> distinct event labels. For <math|r> distinct
    event labels, if <math|T\<geqslant\>M/2+H*Q> with <math|H\<gtr\>0>, then

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-P\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|w=1><rsup|r>\<cal-E\><rsub|w>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*N*e<rsup|-H>|)><rsup|r>.
    </equation*>

    No independence between the events is assumed.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    This is the exponential Markov bound for a Gaussian, with the local
    multiplicities kept explicit. For any distinct coordinate set <math|J>
    and signs <math|\<sigma\><rsub|i>>, the variance of
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>\<sigma\><rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>> is at most
    <math|M<around|\||J|\|>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-P\>*<around|{|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>T*<around|(|i\<in\>J|)>|}>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>*exp
      <around|{|<around|(|M/2-T|)><around|\||J|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Assign one witness to each event label. There are at most
    <math|N<rsup|r>> assignments. For the distinct coordinates in an
    assignment, <math|<around|\||J|\|>\<geqslant\>r/Q> and
    <math|<around|\||J|\|>\<leqslant\>r>. The displayed estimate is at most
    <math|2<rsup|r>*e<rsup|-H*r>>. Summing assignments proves the assertion.
  </proof>

  For the four-dimensional lattice, bounded multiplicity of coordinates and
  event labels per coarse site gives <math|N,Q\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|d>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>
  for a fixed finite power <math|d>. On the actual coupling band,
  <math|t<rsub|f>/g\<asymp\>M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. It
  grows faster than every power of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>>.
  Thus, for any fixed <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, one can arrange
  <math|H\<geqslant\>2*\<tau\>*log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>+log
  <around|(|2*N|)>> and obtain

  <\equation>
    \<bbb-P\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|w=1><rsup|r>\<cal-E\><rsub|w>|)>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f><rsup|-2*\<tau\>*r>.<label|ch06:power-tail>
  </equation>

  The same statement holds uniformly under frozen conditioning if the
  covariance bound and local vanishing assertion are uniform in that
  conditioning.

  <subsection|The deterministic estimate behind a cutoff remainder>

  To distinguish the analytic issue from the probability estimate, write
  <math|B<rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>> for a fine curvature or block average row
  minus its comparison term, and <math|R<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>> for its
  transported affine approximation on the same local patch. On the
  small-coordinate event, <math|v=m<rsub|Y>+g*z> has size
  <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|f>|)>>. Taylor expansion on the common coarse
  gauge, with its polynomial-in-<math|B> gauge bound, gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||B<rsub|p>*<around|(|U,V|)>-R<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>=o<around|(|t<rsub|f>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The first term contains the product-logarithm and transport errors; the
  second is the omitted path tail. Here the fixed cutoff
  <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>> is a positive fixed multiple of <math|B>, so its
  exponential dominates all powers of the coupling. The row bound also gives
  <math|<around|\||R<rsub|p><around|(|v|)>-R<rsub|p><around|(|m<rsub|Y>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<xi\>*t<rsub|f>>
  if all noise coordinates in its dependence region obey
  <math|<around|\||g*z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<xi\>*t<rsub|f>>.

  It remains to bound the affine mean row, not just the mean:

  <\equation>
    <around|\||R<rsub|p><around|(|m<rsub|Y>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>,<label|ch06:mean-target-needed>
  </equation>

  or the corresponding bound after subtracting the prescribed nonzero linear
  target. If that target is within the strict plateau margin, choose
  <math|\<xi\>> so <math|C*\<xi\>> is less than one third of the margin and
  increase the coupling threshold to absorb the two errors. The curvature,
  block average, and likelihood tests are then on their plateaux. The same
  strict inequalities persist on a parameter neighborhood and supply the
  zero-germ assertion used in <eqref|ch06:pointwise-jets>.

  There is a concrete local transfer identity for this estimate. Let <math|v>
  be one candidate field on a common clean ball of radius <math|8*B>, zero on
  the tree, with <math|<around|\||v|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>>.
  Suppose every affine row in that ball satisfies
  <math|<around|\||<math-ss|D>v+b<rsub|Y>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>, where
  <math|\<epsilon\>=C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
  Put <math|E=<math-ss|A>P-I>. Then, with inactive identity coordinates
  included,

  <\equation*>
    m<rsub|Y>-v=E<rsup|\<ast\>>*v-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|<math-ss|A>v+f<rsub|Y>|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|A>v+f<rsub|Y>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<math-ss|D>v+b<rsub|Y>|)>+\<Pi\><rsub|<math-up|inactive>>*v.
  </equation*>

  These are exact identities. A target more than <math|30*B> from the
  selected set has all its windows in the clean ball. The inactive term
  vanishes there, since all non-tree coordinates are active and <math|v> is
  zero on the tree. Hence

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||m<rsub|Y><around|(|i|)>-v<around|(|i|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<epsilon\>+C*e<rsup|-c*B><around|\||v|\|><rsub|\<infty\>><space|1em><text|within
    distance >B<text| of the target>.
  </equation*>

  The row bound transfers the same estimate to the target affine row.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Cancellation for the actual affine
    mean><label|ch06:mean-cancellation>Use the same adjacent quadratic pair
    in <eqref|ch06:flat-identities>, its stationary tree response, and the
    comparison term defined by transporting and truncating those full maps.
    Then <eqref|ch06:mean-target-needed> holds uniformly at targets farther
    than <math|30*B> from the selected set. The constants may depend on the
    fixed step.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|a=\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*h> be the stationary fine response to a
    coarse logarithmic field <math|h>, and set
    <math|w=X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>><around|(|h|)>> and
    <math|p=<around|(|X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>><around|(|a|)>,Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>*<around|(|a,h|)>|)>>.
    Write <math|T*w=<around|(|M<rsub|f>*w,M<rsub|n>*w|)>>. Quadratic Gaussian
    matching gives <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsub|2><rsup|2>=<around|\||w|\|><rsub|2><rsup|2>>,
    with the physical counting norms. The two identities
    <eqref|ch06:flat-identities> give <math|T<rsup|\<ast\>>*p=w> and
    <math|T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=I>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|p-T*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>-2\<\|\|\>w\<\|\|\><rsub|2><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|T*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=-<around|\<\|\|\>|N*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Both sides have opposite signs, so <math|p=T*w> and <math|N*w=0>. In
    particular every linear row minus its comparison term vanishes. This
    argument also applies to each signed orientation with its transported
    forest; it never assumes that a rooted response commutes with the
    symmetry.

    Choose one coarse gauge on the clean <math|8*B> ball and let <math|h> be
    its logarithmic field, extended by zero outside. The local tree gauge
    construction bounds <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>>.
    The tree-straightened response has a bounded row norm at fixed <math|L>:
    the exponentially decaying stationary kernel is summed absolutely, and
    its straightening correction is a sum along finitely many tree edges per
    block. Thus the relative candidate <math|v=\<cal-U\><rsub|F>*h-<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>h>
    is zero on the tree and has <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>>.

    Compare an actual row in the inner ball with its full linear row.
    Taylor's theorem for the four-link curvature and fixed block average
    branch bounds the nonlinear base-row remainder by
    <math|C<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|2>>. For a path
    term, differentiating its products adds powers of the length, summable
    against the exponential path bound. Parallel transports change by at most
    their length times <math|C<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>;
    multiplying by a row input costs a further
    <math|O<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>. The
    same estimates apply to the comparison term, since the comparison term
    uses exactly the transported full maps appearing in <math|T>. Its
    clipping is inactive on this clean stencil. Omitted paths contribute at
    most <math|C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    after decreasing <math|\<sigma\>>.

    Finally the actual fluctuation derivative is evaluated at the replica,
    whereas the transported flat derivative is evaluated at zero before
    transport. Their difference has row norm at most
    <math|C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>>, by the same second-derivative and transport
    estimates. Applied to <math|v>, it costs
    <math|C*B<rsup|2*a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>>. No equality of these two matrices
    is used. The frozen source is absent on the clean inner ball: all its
    non-tree coordinates are active. The exact linear cancellation therefore
    gives <math|<around|\||<math-ss|D>v+b<rsub|Y>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a<rprime|'>>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a<rprime|'>>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    simultaneously on every required row, using the one field <math|h>. The
    local transfer identity above proves <eqref|ch06:mean-target-needed>.
  </proof>

  This conclusion uses the aligned path realization of the full maps in
  <eqref|ch06:flat-identities>. A different comparison term with the same
  numerical norm bounds would not satisfy it. It establishes the remote event
  indicator implication as follows. The direct part of the fine comparison
  term is at most <math|C*L<rsup|-2>*t<rsub|f>>, and the block average
  comparison term at most <math|C*t<rsub|f>>. Choose <math|L> and the block
  average plateau width <math|W> so these bounds lie strictly inside their
  respective plateau thresholds, as in the energy estimate. The preceding
  proposition, the nonlinear Taylor error, and the noise estimate then leave
  the fine plaquette distance from the identity below <math|t<rsub|f>/4> and
  the block average discrepancy below <math|W*t<rsub|f>>. All corresponding
  likelihood factors are on their plateaux. The bond increment is bounded by
  <math|C*t<rsub|f>> on this event, so a sufficiently broad fixed chart
  cutoff is also one. A cutoff-failure indicator assigned within the
  compulsory component region is zero by definition; every other such
  indicator has these small-field neighborhoods. Replacing <math|\<xi\>> by
  <math|\<xi\>/2> gives strict margins on a neighborhood and hence the
  zero-germ support condition. This proves the implication
  <eqref|ch06:tail-event> for these actual event indicators, with the fixed
  comparison term and plateau choices just stated.

  <subsection|The bounded-jet route for local
  activities><label|ch06:bounded-jet-route>

  The actual local expansion offers a direct way to combine differentiation
  and rare events. It is useful even when no aggregate quadratic majorant has
  been established. Let <math|F*<around|(|V,z|)>> be a product of local
  factors after the single protected change above. Suppose, at the base
  point, its derivatives through order <math|k> satisfy

  <\equation>
    <around|\||D<rsup|j>*F*<around|(|V<rsub|0>,z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|m<rsub|k>>*\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\>><around|(|z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>k,<space|1em>x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>.<label|ch06:pointwise-jets>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-E\>> is the intersection of the events for <math|r>
  distinct cutoff remainders. This support assertion concerns all
  derivatives: each remainder must vanish identically on a parameter
  neighborhood outside its event. Assume the local endpoint protections
  needed for dominated differentiation in the preceding subsection.

  Then the common Gaussian integral, including its density ratio, obeys

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||D<rsup|j>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>>*<around|[|\<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V,z|)>*F*<around|(|V,z|)>|]><rsub|V=V<rsub|0>>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|A|~><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|<wide|m|~><rsub|k>>*x<rsup|-\<tau\>*r>*<big|prod><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>k,<label|ch06:actual-jet-integral>
  </equation>

  provided <eqref|ch06:power-tail> holds with exponent <math|2*\<tau\>*r>.
  The constants are uniform in the frozen variables, which can subsequently
  be integrated against their Haar probability measure.

  Here is the moment argument in full. If every Gaussian coordinate has
  variance at most <math|M>, the elementary exponential estimate gives
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|q>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|M*q<rsup|2>/2>>
  for each positive integer <math|q>. Writing <math|N=<around|\||I|\|>> and
  <math|F<rsub|I>=1+N+<big|sum><rsub|I><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>>, one has
  <math|F<rsub|I>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+2*N|)>*<around|(|1+max<rsub|I><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>|)>>
  and hence

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\>*F<rsub|I><rsup|q>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+2*N|)><rsup|q>*<around|(|1+2*N*e<rsup|M*q<rsup|2>/2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This uses no independence. Apply Leibniz' rule, <eqref|ch06:base-scores>,
  and <eqref|ch06:pointwise-jets>. Cauchy\USchwarz gives
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\>>*F<rsub|I><rsup|q>|]>\<leqslant\>\<bbb-P\><around|(|\<cal-E\>|)><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*F<rsub|I><rsup|2*q>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
  Since <math|N\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|4>*s>, all remaining costs are polynomial
  in <math|s> and subpower in <math|x>, proving
  <eqref|ch06:actual-jet-integral>.

  For its application, <eqref|ch06:pointwise-jets> must be verified for the
  actual factors, with their complete dependence regions. Ordinary
  exponentiated correction terms satisfy it once their logarithmic
  derivatives have small amplitude: the partition formula for derivatives of
  <math|e<rsup|h>-1> gives a fixed multiple of that amplitude when
  <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Large-field components use their
  smooth local extension only when the entire required coarse neighborhood is
  small-field. Their physical indicator is not differentiated. The residual
  Gaussian density is first decomposed into the actual protected linear and
  quadratic correction factors; it is not copied once for every activity.
  These are specific conditions on the exact expansion, not automatic
  consequences of the Gaussian gap.

  <subsection|One majorant for the product>

  Here <math|z> has the conditional law <math|d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\><around|(|Y|)>>>,
  with frozen links <math|Y> integrated in probability Haar measure. The
  normalization <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|Y>> and the residual exponential are
  both those of <eqref|ch06:gaussian-identity>.

  The residual Gaussian density in <eqref|ch06:gaussian-identity>, the
  Jacobian ratios, and differentiated factors produce unbounded functions of
  <math|z>. Bounds on their separate integrals cannot simply be multiplied.
  The correct estimate is under a single common Gaussian law.

  For a decomposition that retains an unbounded exponential instead, the
  following alternative criterion is available. Suppose a finite product of
  local factors has, after up to <math|k> allowed derivatives, a majorant of
  the form

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|k><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|d>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|k><around|(|z|)>*exp
    <around|{|\<eta\>\<cdot\>z+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsup|\<ast\>>*K*z|}>*<big|prod><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|w>M<rsub|w>*\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\><rsub|w>>,<label|ch06:majorant-form>
  </equation>

  where <math|s> counts anchors, <math|P<rsub|k>> is a fixed-degree
  polynomial in absolute coordinates with controlled moments,
  <math|K\<geqslant\>0>, and the product over <math|w> uses distinct event
  labels. The numbers <math|a<rsub|i>> bound ordinary local factors;
  <math|M<rsub|w>> bound cutoff or branch likelihood amplitudes. Their number
  must include all allowed local tags. Assume

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8,<space|2em><tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|)>+<around|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*\<eta\>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<lambda\><rsub|i>.<label|ch06:quadratic-control>
  </equation>

  These are bounds on the sum of all local corrections. Smallness of each
  summand separately does not suffice when the number of factors grows.
  Exponential kernel bounds and bounded overlap of their full dependence
  regions are one way to obtain them. For the linear term, a useful estimate
  uses weights <math|w<rsub|i*a>\<gtr\>0> with <math|sup<rsub|a>
  <big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i*a>\<leqslant\>C>; then
  <math|<around|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>\<eta\><rsub|i><around|(|a|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||\<eta\><rsub|i><around|(|a|)>|\|><rsup|2>/w<rsub|i*a>>.
  It controls the cross terms in the common square.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Common local integral bound><label|ch06:common-majorant>Assume
    <eqref|ch06:majorant-form>\U<eqref|ch06:quadratic-control>, with uniform
    polynomial moment bounds for <math|P<rsub|k>> under the corresponding
    tilted Gaussians. Assume the local vanishing implication
    <eqref|ch06:tail-event> for the same coordinates and event indicators.
    Then the absolute differentiated integral is at most

    <\equation*>
      C<rprime|'><rsub|k><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>|)>*<around|(|1+s|)><rsup|d<rprime|'>>*e<rsup|C<rprime|'>*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<lambda\><rsub|i>>*<big|prod><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|w<text|
      distinct>><around|(|M<rsub|w>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f><rsup|-\<tau\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The statement holds after integration of the frozen Haar variables when
    all inputs are uniform in those variables. It requires no independence
    between the local factors or the tail events.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The standard Gaussian quadratic-exponential formula, with
    <math|<wide|K|~>=\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>>, is

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<Gamma\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\>\<cdot\>z+z<rsup|\<ast\>>*K*z/2>=det
      <around|(|I-<wide|K|~>|)><rsup|-1/2>*exp
      <around*|{|<tfrac|1|2>\<eta\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|I-<wide|K|~>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*\<eta\>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Use it also with doubled exponent to control the square of the majorant.
    The strict norm inequality in <eqref|ch06:quadratic-control> bounds the
    tilted covariance and mean; Gaussian polynomial moments remain
    controlled. Cauchy\USchwarz and <eqref|ch06:power-tail> give the asserted
    bound.
  </proof>

  Finally, Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:energy> contributes
  <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>>.
  Throughout the actual band, <math|\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>\<asymp\><around|(|M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|2>>.
  Any verified per-selected-test logarithmic losses of size
  <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|d>*<around|(|1+log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>|)><rsup|d>|)>+\<beta\>*O<around|(|B<rsup|d>*t<rsub|f><rsup|3>|)>>
  are <math|o<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>|)>>. They can be included
  while retaining <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>>
  for any fixed <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'>\<less\>\<kappa\>> once the
  coupling threshold is increased. This last comparison concerns losses
  actually assigned per selected test. It cannot absorb an unexplained
  volume-dependent normalization.

  <section|Weighted bounds for the primitive local
  factors><label|ch06:primitive-bounds>

  We now retain the individual correction labels, including repeated paths,
  instead of estimating only their sum as an operator. Fix a derivative order
  <math|k>, a support exponent <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0>, and a halo
  multiplier <math|h> large enough to contain every window and protected
  variation used above. For a support <math|S>, write
  <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>> for its connected covering number by
  <math|B>-balls. A family of nonnegative amplitudes
  <math|a<rsub|\<lambda\>>> has containing-site bound <math|\<epsilon\>> if

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>:x\<in\>S<rsub|\<lambda\>>>e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>>*a<rsub|\<lambda\>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>.
  </equation*>

  These are full dependence regions, not only exclusion supports. The
  statement concerns the positive derivative orders available in the incoming
  path bounds. Additional fixed polynomial costs in <math|B> or
  <math|M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> are subpower in
  <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>>.

  <subsection|Gaussian correction series>

  Put <math|D=<math-ss|A>\<Gamma\>-I>. The window estimates give
  exponentially small weighted entry and fixed derivative envelopes for
  <math|D>. For the interpolating precision
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|s>=I+s*<around|(|<math-ss|A>-I|)>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1>, the same argument applies uniformly:
  its gap is at least <math|min <around|(|1,c<rsub|L>|)>> and its weighted
  bounds are uniform. Let <math|P<rsub|s>> be its local inverse columns and
  <math|E<rsub|s>=<math-ss|A><rsub|s>P<rsub|s>-I>. The exact inverse and
  trace expansions are

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|A><rsub|s><rsup|-1>=P<rsub|s>*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-E<rsub|s>|)><rsup|n>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><rsup|-1>-<math-ss|A>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-D|)><rsup|n+1><math-ss|A>.
  </equation*>

  All series converge in weighted matrix norm. Integrating
  <math|<tr><around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|A>-I|)><math-ss|A><rsub|s><rsup|-1>|)>>
  gives <math|log det <math-ss|A>>. The <math|n=0> term is its local diagonal
  normalization; the remaining words are <math|<around|(|<math-ss|A>-I|)>*P<rsub|s>*<around|(|-E<rsub|s>|)><rsup|n+1>>.
  The second determinant series comes from <math|log det <around|(|I+D|)>=log
  det <math-ss|A>+log det \<Gamma\>>. This determinant identity is legitimate
  even if <math|D> is not symmetric: <math|I+D=<math-ss|A>\<Gamma\>> is
  similar to a positive matrix, and the convergent real logarithm series
  agrees with its positive determinant.

  Each word is indexed by its ordered sequence of bonds, with color indices
  summed inside the matrix entries. For each fixed derivative order <math|k>,
  the corresponding derivative of the amplitude is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|k><around|(|B|)>*e<rsup|-c*B>*<around|{|C<rsub|k><around|(|B|)>*e<rsup|-c*B>|}><rsup|n>,
  </equation*>

  with possibly a smaller positive <math|c>. The endpoint cutoffs in a
  quadratic word bound each Gaussian endpoint and its normalized derivatives
  by a subpower function. A linear residual has one such endpoint. Inactive
  endpoints give a zero germ because both precision and covariance have
  identity inactive blocks. Thus all these estimates hold for the protected
  factors on the entire sample space.

  For completeness, the sum over word supports is also small. Consecutive
  bonds have distance at most <math|3*B+2>. There are at most
  <math|K=C<rsub|L>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>> possible next bonds. A word of
  length <math|n+u>, for fixed <math|u>, has support cover at most
  <math|<around|(|n+u+1|)>*s<rsub|h>>, where <math|s<rsub|h>> depends only on
  the halo multiplier. If that support contains a prescribed site, its
  starting bond has at most <math|C<rsub|L>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|n+u+1|)><rsup|4>>
  possible locations. Fixed derivatives cost an additional polynomial in
  <math|n+u>. Absorb these polynomials by
  <math|C<rsub|k>*2<rsup|<around|(|k+4|)>*n>>. The containing-site sum is
  consequently bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|k><around|(|B|)>*e<rsup|-c*B>*<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|{|C<rsub|k><around|(|B|)>*e<rsup|-c*B>*2<rsup|k+4>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*s<rsub|h>>*K|}><rsup|n>.
  </equation*>

  Its ratio is at most <math|1/2> eventually. The sum is smaller than
  <math|x<rsup|-A>> for every prescribed <math|A\<gtr\>0>. This proves
  smallness of the individual determinant and inverse-correction labels with
  their full supports, including repeated bonds, rather than merely a small
  operator norm.

  <subsection|Short local and inherited terms>

  The remaining primitive estimates can be displayed together. Each bound
  below is a containing-site bound with full fixed halos and fixed-order
  normalized derivatives, on the coarse small-field neighborhoods needed for
  those derivatives:

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|family>>|<cell|<text|bound>>>|<row|<cell|<text|remote
    nonlinear action remainders>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|3>>>|<row|<cell|<text|remote
    extracted mean squares>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|4>>>|<row|<cell|<text|local
    determinant differences>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*t<rsub|f>>>|<row|<cell|<text|local
    Haar logarithms>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|linear
    Gaussian residual and correction words>>|<cell|x<rsup|-A><space|1em><text|for
    every fixed >A>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited regular
    activities>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*\<delta\><rsub|f>>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited
    quadratic loop terms>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*\<Lambda\><rsub|f>>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited
    cubic and quartic terms>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*\<beta\>*<around|(|t<rsub|f><rsup|3>+t<rsub|f><rsup|4>|)>>>|<row|<cell|<text|omitted
    path tails>>|<cell|C<rsub|k>*B<rsup|a<rsub|k>>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*e<rsup|-c*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<delta\><rsub|f>> is the incoming regular-activity bound and
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|f>> the quadratic loop coefficient bound. For example
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|-1/2>> and
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|f>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|1/8>> make every displayed
  family small by a fixed negative power of <math|x>. The factors from
  differentiation and counting can be absorbed by reducing the decay exponent
  if necessary.

  The first two lines use the affine square with the exact actual derivative.
  Taylor's remainder is quadratic in the displacement; multiplying by the
  base affine row, of size <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|f>|)>> on the protected
  chart, gives <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|f><rsup|3>|)>>. The square of the
  remainder is smaller. The extracted mean square is
  <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|4>|)>> by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:mean-cancellation>. For derivatives the
  directions are the normalized small-field directions used above; applying
  the same finite Taylor formula with parameter derivatives gives the same
  powers, with their fixed path-bound costs. No derivative of a large-field
  indicator is taken.

  For the third line, compare actual and flat matrices in the common small
  gauge on the local window. Their difference is
  <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>|)>>. The resolvent identity for each
  principal compression transfers this to the diagonal integral defining the
  local logarithmic normalization. The window size and trace introduce only
  powers of <math|B>. The fourth line follows from <math|log
  J<around|(|u|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||u|\|><rsup|2>|)>> for the Haar
  density in exponential coordinates and from the protected chart bound.
  Every finite-halo site count is polynomial in <math|B>.

  For inherited terms there is a support issue in addition to these pointwise
  powers. If an incoming support <math|X> has fine cover number
  <math|d=c<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>, with
  <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B>, then its projected <math|h*B> halo
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|h>+<frac|10*P<rsub|h>|L>*d,<space|2em>x<rsub|0>\<in\>S\<Longrightarrow\>d*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,\<pi\>*o<rsub|X>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|h+20|)>*B*<around|(|1+d|)>,
  </equation*>

  for a fixed <math|P<rsub|h>>. To prove the first bound, traverse a spanning
  tree of the fine covering centers, sample that walk every <math|L*B> steps,
  and cover each projected halo by at most <math|P<rsub|h>> coarse balls. The
  walk has length at most <math|10*B*d>, giving the displayed coefficient.
  The same walk proves the anchor-distance bound. Thus an incoming anchor
  weight <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\>*d>> absorbs the coarse support weight and all
  polynomial multiplicities provided

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\>*<space|0.17em>10*P<rsub|h>/L+4*\<epsilon\>\<less\>\<sigma\>
  </equation*>

  for some fixed <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Counting possible anchors costs
  <math|C<rsub|L>*B<rsup|4>*<around|(|1+d|)><rsup|4>>; derivative factors
  cost another fixed power of <math|1+d>, absorbed by
  <math|e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*d>>. Summation of the remaining geometric series
  proves the inherited site bounds. For path labels the analogous cost is
  their true length, including winding and repetitions; the unused
  exponential path factor absorbs its support and its omitted tail. This
  explains why a positive incoming additional support decay, not merely an
  unweighted coefficient sum, is required.

  <subsection|Components and assembly under one law>

  All constituent factors keep their original full dependence regions,
  including cutoff and path tests. Their exclusion supports, defined in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|ch04:activities>, determine exact coverage, not
  independence; the parameter <math|\<sigma\>> in the weighted support sums
  is the exponential locality weight of <eqref|ch04:weighted-sums>.

  The relevant energy is the remainder after Gaussian extraction, not the
  core energy alone. We specify the allocation to prevent a positive affine
  square from being lost in this estimate. Let <math|O> be a selected
  component with <math|n> tests. Its assigned action rows have anchors within
  <math|200*B> of <math|O>; their core consists of the rows within
  <math|2*B>. The remaining rows form an annulus <math|Q>. Define the
  ownership components by repeatedly merging selected sets whose closed
  <math|500*B> neighborhoods intersect. Distinct owners then have disjoint
  <math|500*B> neighborhoods (separation greater than <math|1000*B>
  suffices), so their <math|200*B> action and <math|304*B> Haar assignments
  cannot overlap. The enlarged finite-range adjacency changes only the fixed
  polynomial counting constants. This geometric disjointness does not assert
  probabilistic independence.

  For this component estimate impose the allocation's weak test conditions
  through distance <math|300*B>: every unselected fine plaquette has distance
  from the identity at most <math|t<rsub|f>/2>, every unselected block
  average discrepancy is at most <math|2*W*t<rsub|f>>, the stored coarse
  large-field indicators equal the actual coarse large-field indicators, and
  no coarse large-field site occurs away from the selected coarse sites.
  Retained frame tags have positive likelihood. These are conditions on the
  actual factor; they are stronger than the initial <math|5*B> consistency
  definition. For an annular anchor at distance greater than <math|2*B> from
  the selected set, its required <math|B> neighborhood and the smaller
  comparison patches are consequently free of selected tests. All such reads
  fit inside the retained <math|300*B> region. Smooth chart cutoff factors
  are retained throughout <math|304*B>. Local determinant terms are assigned
  through <math|200*B>, whereas Haar terms are assigned through <math|304*B>;
  these two sets are not identified.

  Let <math|H> be the actual fine and block average rows extracted from the
  annulus by the stencil selection. The selected stencil has radius at most
  <math|B/50>. A retained row meets an active coordinate outside the selected
  <math|10*B> neighborhood, so its entire stencil lies outside the
  <math|<around|(|10-2/50|)>*B> neighborhood and hence outside the <math|2*B>
  core. We first work in one signed orientation, before its probability
  weight is applied. Denote its row derivative and source by
  <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|\<sigma\>>,b<rsub|Y,\<sigma\>>>, including physical
  direct/penalty coefficients but excluding the square root of the
  orientation weight. The global matrix in <eqref|ch06:actual-matrix> stacks
  <math|<sqrt|\<omega\><rsub|\<sigma\>>><math-ss|D><rsub|\<sigma\>>>.
  Suppress <math|\<sigma\>> temporarily and set

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|H><around|(|<wide|u|~>|)>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>H><around|\||<around|(|<math-ss|D><rsub|\<sigma\>><wide|u|~><rsub|<math-up|active>>+b<rsub|Y,\<sigma\>>|)><rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The orientation probability is not included in this half-square; it will
  appear once in the average below. Physical row coefficients and the frozen
  part of the signal are included once: equivalently apply the all-coordinate
  derivative to the full signal and add its replica value. The signal
  <math|<wide|u|~>> is the actual active increment, the clipped frozen
  logarithm on frozen free bonds, and zero on tree bonds. Where the retained
  cutoff factors are nonzero it reconstructs the original fine field on every
  selected stencil. Indeed the clean annular fine plaquettes and block
  average mismatches, together with the common-tree comparison, put each
  frozen mismatch within its logarithm cutoff's inner plateau. On active
  bonds the cutoff support gives <math|<around|\||<wide|u|~>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*w*t<rsub|f>>;
  take the fixed width <math|w> larger than the frozen comparison constant.
  Both tree field and replica are identity on tree bonds. Thus this same
  signal, uniformly bounded by <math|2*w*t<rsub|f>>, may be used in the
  affine approximation; an unrelated logarithm or an unrestricted Gaussian
  sample cannot be substituted.

  Write <math|W<around|(|Q|)>> for the sum of the six local energy terms
  <eqref|ch06:six-terms> on the annulus, with the same fine clip threshold as
  the core. Then

  <\equation>
    W<around|(|Q|)>-X<rsub|H><around|(|<wide|u|~>|)>\<geqslant\>-\<epsilon\><around|(|x|)>*n,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|3>+C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*e<rsup|-c*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>=o<around|(|t<rsub|f><rsup|2>|)>.<label|ch06:annulus-extraction>
  </equation>

  To verify the inequality, work in one clean local gauge about each annular
  row. The actual nonlinear row minus its comparison term has norm
  <math|M\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>> and differs from its actual
  affine approximation by <math|E\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-c*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
  Taylor expansion gives this for the actual replica derivative directly;
  comparing first to transported flat rows adds an error of the same order.
  Hence the difference of the two half-squares is at most
  <math|E*<around|(|2*M+E|)>/2>. The fine and block average energies equal
  their nonlinear residual squares plus the nonnegative difference between
  the squared group distance and the squared curvature representative. If a
  row is not selected, no affine square is subtracted, so only its
  nonnegative residual remains. The coarse counterterm costs
  <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|f><rsup|4>|)>>. The null-row linear term vanishes
  by the cancellation in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:mean-cancellation>;
  its nonlinear square is <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|4>|)>>.
  The two signed mismatch terms are a comparison term of size
  <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|f>|)>> paired with the quadratic Taylor/transport
  errors, hence <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|3>|)>+O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*e<rsup|-c*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>.
  There are at most <math|C*B<rsup|4>*n> such rows, proving
  <eqref|ch06:annulus-extraction>. The clean annular fields are below the
  fine threshold, so the fine clip is its quadratic branch. The retained path
  tests also equal one: within their nontrivial range the patch is clean, and
  beyond that range their permitted bound exceeds the uniform bound on the
  distance from the identity in the group. This checks the actual row formula
  used in the argument.

  For precision, let <math|\<sigma\>> range over the signed lattice
  orientations with their probability weights
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<sigma\>>>, transforming the field, forest, tags,
  selection, and rows together. Every term inside the following average is
  the unweighted single-orientation quantity just defined. Thus each
  orientation probability is counted exactly once. The energy assigned to
  this component after Gaussian extraction is exactly

  <\equation>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|O><rsup|<math-up|rem>>=<math-up|Av><rsub|\<sigma\>><around|{|\<cal-E\><rsub|\<sigma\>*O>+W<around|(|Q<rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>-X<rsub|H<rsub|\<sigma\>>><around|(|<wide|u|~><rsub|\<sigma\>>|)>+C<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|m<rsub|Y>|)>|}>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<sigma\>><around|(|m<rsub|Y>|)>\<geqslant\>0.<label|ch06:component-extracted-energy>
  </equation>

  This is a finite row identity: every near action row is assigned once; a
  selected fine or block average row subtracts its actual affine square; its
  extracted mean square is retained with positive sign. Unselected rows
  subtract zero. The mean-square sum is <math|C<rsub|\<sigma\>>>. The near
  action rows partition into the core and annulus, so
  <eqref|ch06:component-extracted-energy> and
  <eqref|ch06:annulus-extraction>, followed by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:energy>, give

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|O><rsup|<math-up|rem>>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<kappa\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>-\<epsilon\><around|(|x|)>|)>*n\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2>\<kappa\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*n
  </equation*>

  at large coupling. This is the needed bound on the extracted energy.
  Dropping <math|X<rsub|H>> before proving the annular estimate would be
  invalid.

  The remaining local normalization, Haar, tag, and frozen-coordinate costs
  are bounded by <math|C*B<rsup|a>*<around|(|1+log
  x|)><rsup|a>*n=o<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>|)>*n>. The component
  amplitude consequently satisfies

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|<math-up|comp>>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|-\<kappa\><rprime|'>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>*n|}>
  </equation*>

  for a fixed positive <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'>> smaller than the retained
  energy coefficient. Fixed derivatives of its smooth extension on a fully
  small-field coarse neighborhood add polynomial costs per component and are
  absorbed by a further decrease of <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'>>. The physical
  Borel indicator is held fixed. This assertion is where the retained cutoff
  factors are nonzero, under the precise core/annulus allocation just
  specified; where a compulsory cutoff factor vanishes the full component
  factor is zero. Connected selections with prescribed root have at most
  <math|<around|{|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|a>|}><rsup|n>> possibilities: list
  them by a rooted spanning tree, using their finite-range adjacency. This
  entropy and <math|e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|C*\<alpha\>*n>>
  are absorbed by the same remaining positive energy bound. A component's
  physical Borel indicator is held fixed; the derivative assertion concerns
  its smooth extension on the stated small-field coarse neighborhood.

  Finally replace each small logarithm <math|f<rsub|\<lambda\>>> by
  <math|e<rsup|f<rsub|\<lambda\>>>-1>. For
  <math|<around|\||f<rsub|\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, the exponential
  partition formula preserves its amplitude up to a constant depending on
  <math|k>. The previous bounds furnish the pointwise product jets
  <eqref|ch06:pointwise-jets>; derivative costs polynomial in the total
  support size are absorbed by a small further decrease of the support
  exponent. Warnings carry the joint-event factors proved above.
  Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch06:actual-jet-integral> then gives a product
  majorant under the one actual conditional Gaussian, followed by the frozen
  Haar probability integral. This is the local integration estimate required
  before the connected expansion. Incoming large-field activities retain
  their given amplitudes and participate in this same product bound; they are
  not claimed to become pointwise small by Gaussian integration.

  <subsection|Comparison with the linear local
  integral><label|ch06:linear-comparison>

  The estimates also give the small parameter needed to compare a local
  nonlinear integral with its linear Gaussian counterpart. Fix the same
  forest, coordinate set, windows, and clean coarse field throughout the
  comparison. Write <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|<math-up|lin>>> for the window
  covariance formed from the corresponding flat rows, and <math|v> for the
  tree-straightened linear relative response on the common clean
  neighborhood. Taylor expansion of the actual rows and the principal
  resolvent identity give, on those windows,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\>-\<Gamma\><rsub|<math-up|lin>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>,<space|2em><around|\||m<rsub|Y>-v|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>*e<rsup|-c*B>.
  </equation*>

  The second estimate is the local mean transfer proved above; the first is
  valid in weighted row norms, or with a harmless local polynomial factor in
  an unweighted matrix norm. Both have fixed-order versions under the same
  available path derivative bounds. In particular the mean shift in Gaussian
  units costs

  <\equation*>
    g<rsup|-1>*<around|\||m<rsub|Y>-v|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|a>*g<around|(|M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|2>+C*B<rsup|a>*M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>*e<rsup|-c*B>.
  </equation*>

  Thus it is of size <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>, rather than merely
  bounded.

  The physical coordinate comparison uses the same tree chart. Its nonlinear
  map and its first-order Taylor map differ by

  <\equation*>
    C*B<rsup|a>*<around|(|t<rsub|f>+g<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*B<rsup|a>*<around|(|t<rsub|f><rsup|2>+g<rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)>
  </equation*>

  on their protected common chart. Dividing by the fine small-field radius
  gives <math|C*B<rsup|a>*<around|(|t<rsub|f>+<around|(|g<rsup|2>/t<rsub|f>|)><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.
  All its fixed Gaussian moments are <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>> on a
  region with polynomial-in-<math|B> dimension. This is a Taylor estimate
  with polynomial moment control, not a uniform bound on unbounded samples;
  the chart-complement events are controlled separately.

  For an interpolation of the positive covariances and of the centered means,
  differentiation of its common Gaussian density ratio therefore has local
  polynomial moments bounded by <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>, with the
  appropriate polynomial factor in the dependence-region size. Indeed the
  logarithmic derivative is the trace, quadratic, and linear score displayed
  after <eqref|ch06:density-ratio>; each coefficient contains one of the
  covariance or normalized mean differences just estimated. Integrating this
  derivative compares the two laws. For an inherited activity with only
  <math|k> available derivatives, this comparison must be implemented by
  holding its physical fine links fixed with the protected change of
  variables. Differentiate the common density, Jacobian, smooth cutoff
  factors, and at most the available <math|k> activity derivatives. Taylor
  expansion of <math|D<rsup|k>*E<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|nonlinear>>|)>-D<rsup|k>*E<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|linear>>|)>>
  would require an unavailable <math|<around|(|k+1|)>>st derivative and is
  not justified here. The physical-map estimate above supplies bounds on the
  compensating map and its density scores; it is not permission to lose an
  extra derivative of the incoming activity. This export concerns a local
  region whose entire window neighborhoods are clean. It does not compare a
  seeded sector to an unseeded law, does not replace the tree response by a
  different gauge response, and does not identify separately chosen event
  indicators. Such replacements need their own tail or support decomposition.

  <section|What must be checked for a concrete step>

  The local integral estimate now follows from the actual gap, clipped
  source, cancellation of the aligned comparison terms, strict margins in the
  event inequalities, and the primitive bounds above. Its hypotheses are the
  adjacent pair's compatible full quadratic identities, actual rooted block
  average, exponential path bounds through the requested derivative order,
  and the incoming activity bounds with a strictly larger support exponent
  than the one used in the conclusion. These are incoming data, not estimates
  that can be created by selecting a scale schedule. All matrices, comparison
  terms, forests, tags, and frozen sources in the argument belong to that
  same pair.

  The chapter controls local amplitudes and their integrals. Reconstruction
  of the exact block density additionally requires assigning every nonlinear
  row, determinant contribution, Haar factor, and event indicator exactly
  once. In particular the two near sets for local determinant terms and Haar
  terms need not coincide, and the flat determinant scalar is extracted only
  once for the whole forest. Those identities, and the subsequent connected
  regrouping, are distinct from the analytic bounds proved here.

  <assign|chapter-nr|6>

  <chapter|Local matching and the one-loop coefficient><label|ch07:matching>

  A renormalization step has two distinct normalization tasks. Its classical
  stationary action must retain the prescribed quadratic form and agree with
  a gauge-invariant local action through degree four. Its Gaussian
  determinant changes the coefficient of that quadratic form. The first task
  is a local Taylor construction; the second requires a comparison of
  determinants uniform in the block ratio. This chapter separates the two, so
  that a perturbative coefficient does not stand in for an estimate on the
  actual block integral.

  Fix one step <math|m+1\<to\>m>, an odd block ratio <math|L=L<rsub|m>>, and
  the Lie algebra metric of Chapter<nbsp>1. Momentum is measured in lattice
  units at the indicated level. The adjoint Casimir <math|C<rsub|G>> is
  defined by <math|<tr><rsub|<gLie>><around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>X<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>Y|)>=-C<rsub|G>*<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>>>;
  set

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\><rsub|G>=<frac|11*C<rsub|G>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  The group is compact with simple nonabelian Lie algebra. Constants may
  depend on fixed stage bounds, but constants described as uniform in
  <math|L> are chosen before <math|L>. All local Taylor expansions below
  concern the analytic action near the flat field, where small-field
  indicators are constant. They do not differentiate a cutoff boundary.

  <section|Common Taylor kernels and Ward identities><label|ch07:kernels>

  Write <math|C<rsub|n><around|(|E|)>=D<rsup|n>*E<around|(|0|)>/n>! for the
  normalized symmetric Taylor coefficient of an action <math|E>. A
  translation-invariant coefficient is specified by its values on plane
  waves. After division by the number of lattice sites, its symbol is a
  tensor <math|f*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|n-1>|)>>, with
  <math|p<rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|i\<gtr\>0>p<rsub|i>>. The tensor has one
  direction and one colour index for each field. The symbol is periodic with
  period <math|2*\<pi\>> in each independent momentum. A common symbol means
  one function chosen on the infinite lattice whose restriction to each
  finite momentum grid describes the corresponding torus coefficient. This
  requirement is stronger than a separate smooth extension of each finite
  grid.

  For <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, write <math|<around|\||f|\|><rsub|\<sigma\>>\<leqslant\>A>
  when every component is holomorphic and bounded by <math|A> on the strip
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|i><rsup|a>|\|>\<less\>\<sigma\>>.
  There are only finitely many direction and colour components at fixed
  <math|n>. Reducing the strip gives an exponentially weighted sum of Fourier
  coefficients, with a constant depending on <math|n,\<sigma\>,G>. Thus
  common strip bounds imply locality uniformly in the period. An
  order-<math|r> zero means that all momentum Taylor coefficients of total
  degree below <math|r> vanish.

  Let <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1> and
  <math|d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>*v=q<around|(|p|)>\<wedge\>v> be the lattice
  curl. Suppose an analytic gauge-invariant difference <math|E> has vanishing
  Taylor coefficients below degree <math|n>. Its infinitesimal gauge identity
  at that degree contains only the linear gauge variation
  <math|d<rsub|0>*\<omega\>>: the nonlinear part multiplies lower
  coefficients, which are zero. Consequently its first nonzero symbol
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:ward>\<delta\><rsub|i>*f=0*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>i\<less\>n|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|i>=\<iota\><rsub|q<around|(|p<rsub|i>|)>>.
  </equation>

  Contraction is algebraic, without complex conjugation; it acts only on the
  direction index of the indicated leg. Reality means
  <math|<wide|f*<around|(|-p|)>|\<bar\>>=f<around|(|p|)>> on real momenta.
  Constant gauge transformations make its colour tensor adjoint invariant. A
  Ward identity verified on all finite momentum grids passes to the common
  continuous symbol by density. This last step requires a common kernel.

  <section|Division by the lattice curl><label|ch07:division>

  The algebraic Poincaré lemma explains why gauge-invariant leading
  coefficients are built from curvature; see
  <cite-detail|BarnichBrandtHenneaux2000|Section<nbsp>4>. Its local continuum
  form does not give the periodic strip bounds needed here. We give the
  quantitative adaptation, including the constraint on the sum of momenta.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Quantitative curvature representation><label|ch07:curl> Let
    <math|f> have <math|n> one-form legs, satisfy <eqref|ch07:ward>, and obey
    <math|<around|\||f|\|><rsub|\<sigma\>>\<leqslant\>A>. For
    <math|n\<geqslant\>3> there are <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0,K\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    depending only on <math|n,\<sigma\>>, and a periodic holomorphic tensor
    <math|g> with two-form legs such that

    <\equation*>
      f=\<delta\><rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|n-1>*g,<space|2em><around|\||g|\|><rsub|\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>K*A.
    </equation*>

    For <math|n=2> the same assertion holds if <math|f> has order <math|r+2>,
    <math|r\<geqslant\>0>; then <math|g> can be chosen with order <math|r>.
    Reality and invariant colour coefficients can be imposed with a fixed
    additional norm factor.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The elementary division is

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|a>*f<around|(|p|)>=<frac|f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|p\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>|)>|e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>-1>.
    </equation*>

    It is holomorphic at the apparent zeros. On a Fourier monomial the
    quotient is a geometric sum with <math|<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>> terms
    and no frequency larger than <math|<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>>. A smaller
    strip absorbs this polynomial factor into the exponential Fourier decay.
    Hence <math|D<rsub|a>> is bounded from a fixed strip to a smaller one and
    loses at most one Taylor order.

    For a cycle <math|\<delta\>*f=0> in one exterior factor, write
    <math|f=e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>u+v>, with <math|u,v> containing no
    <math|e<rsub|a>>. Then <math|\<delta\><rprime|'>*v=-q<rsub|a>*u>, and
    direct substitution gives

    <\equation*>
      f=\<delta\>*<around|(|e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>D<rsub|a>*v|)>+v\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>.
    </equation*>

    Repeat over the four axes. At positive exterior degree the last remainder
    has no possible component and is zero. At degree zero the last remainder
    is <math|f<around|(|0|)>>, so that case is solvable precisely when
    <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0>. The construction preserves auxiliary
    constraints independent of the momentum being divided. It raises the
    exterior degree by one, loses at most one Taylor order, and has a bounded
    smaller-strip norm.

    Only the dependent momentum <math|p<rsub|0>> needs extra care. The
    following induction supplies it. With <math|t> independent momenta and
    exterior degrees <math|<around|(|d,1,\<ldots\>,1|)>>, a cycle in every
    factor has a zeroth-factor primitive preserving the other cycles whenever
    <math|d+2*t\<gtr\>4>. For <math|t=0> its degree exceeds four and it is
    zero. For <math|t\<gtr\>0>, replace <math|p<rsub|1>> by <math|p<rsub|0>>
    as independent variable and apply the preceding division. This invertible
    integral shear only reduces the strip by a fixed factor. It gives
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*X=f>, preserving the cycles in factors
    <math|2,\<ldots\>,t>. Apply another zeroth-factor division to
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|1>*X>, obtaining
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y=\<delta\><rsub|1>*X>. At <math|p<rsub|1>=0> the
    tensor <math|Y<rsub|0>> is a cycle with zeroth degree <math|d+2> and
    <math|t-1> independent momenta. Induction gives
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Z=Y<rsub|0>>. Extend <math|Z> independently of
    <math|p<rsub|1>> and set <math|Y<rprime|'>=Y-\<delta\><rsub|0>*Z>. It
    vanishes at <math|p<rsub|1>=0>, so scalar division in factor one gives
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|1>*D=Y<rprime|'>>. The corrected primitive
    <math|X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*D> has contraction <math|f> in factor zero and
    zero in factor one. All other cycles were retained. The invariant
    quantity <math|d+2*t> makes this a terminating induction.

    Initially <math|d=1,t=n-1>, so the induction applies for
    <math|n\<geqslant\>3>. After the zeroth-factor lift, divide the
    independent legs successively. For <math|n=2>, start instead with an
    order-<math|r+2> tensor. The first lift has order <math|r+1>; contraction
    in factor one restores one order, and the next lift <math|Y> has order
    <math|r+1>. Thus <math|Y<around|(|0|)>=0> without induction. Its scalar
    lift has order <math|r>, the corrected primitive has order <math|r+1>,
    and the last division leaves order <math|r>. All operations are bounded
    on a common smaller strip and their number is fixed.

    Finally replace <math|g> by <math|<around|(|g<around|(|p|)>+<wide|g*<around|(|-p|)>|\<bar\>>|)>/2>
    and project each colour coefficient onto the invariant real tensors.
    Contraction acts on direction indices and commutes with this
    finite-dimensional projection. These operations preserve the
    reconstruction and all retained orders, with a fixed norm factor.
  </proof>

  Choose coordinate paths from the origin to the Fourier displacements
  <math|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|n-1>>. A curvature insertion transported
  along such a path is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:slot>F<rsub|\<gamma\>,a*b>*<around|(|U,x|)>=<Ad><around|(|U<rsub|x+\<gamma\>>|)><math-up|proj><rsub|<gLie>><frac|U<rsub|x+z;a*b>-U<rsub|x+z;a*b><rsup|-1>|2>,<space|2em>\<gamma\>:0\<longrightarrow\>z.
  </equation>

  All factors transform at the common anchor <math|x>, so an invariant
  multilinear colour contraction is gauge invariant. Its length is the sum of
  the path lengths plus four per plaquette. The linearization of each factor
  is the corresponding curl. The Fourier coefficients of <math|g> therefore
  produce an exact leading Taylor coefficient <math|f>. Symmetrizing the legs
  implements the convention <math|C<rsub|n>=D<rsup|n>/n>!; it introduces no
  extra factorial.

  If <math|a> denotes such a multilinear term and <math|<around|\||a|\|>> the
  sum of absolute colour coefficients, the result is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:path-norm><big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||a|\|>*e<rsup|w<math-up|length><around|(|a|)>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|w>*A*<space|2em><around|(|0\<less\>w\<less\>\<eta\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Averaging over signed coordinate symmetries preserves matching and this
  bound. Absolute convergence permits periodization on every torus. For the
  two-leg case with <math|r=2>, the curvature coefficients
  <math|c<around|(|z|)>> obey

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:moments><big|sum><rsub|z>c<around|(|z|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|z>z<rsub|a>*c<around|(|z|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>a\<leqslant\>4|)>.
  </equation>

  These are the value and first derivatives of its symbol at zero. They hold
  separately for each orientation and colour pair.

  <section|The marginal quadratic coefficient><label|ch07:quadratic>

  A quadratic Taylor symbol includes the factor <math|1/2>!. For its
  transverse entry, define

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:alpha>\<alpha\><around|(|f|)>=2<math-up|Re><around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*f<rsub|22><rsup|u*u>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>,
  </equation>

  where <math|u> is any unit colour basis vector. The kinetic symbol
  <math|b<rsub|H>> of <math|J> has quadratic homogeneous term

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|b<rsub|H>|)><rsub|a*b><rsup|u*v,<around|[|2|]>><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>-p<rsub|a>*p<rsub|b>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|u*v>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|\<alpha\><around|(|b<rsub|H>|)>=1>. The factor two in
  <eqref|ch07:alpha> is therefore fixed by the Taylor convention, not by a
  choice of coupling scheme.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Removal of the kinetic coefficient><label|ch07:unique> An analytic
    quadratic symbol from an invariant real action, with exchange symmetry
    and signed-coordinate symmetry, has <math|f-\<alpha\><around|(|f|)>*b<rsub|H>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|4>|)>>.
    Its coefficient <math|\<alpha\>> is independent of the transverse axis
    pair and the chosen unit colour.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The Ward identity first kills the constant term. Its linear term is also
    zero: inversion of all coordinates acts positively on the two one-form
    indices and negatively on a linear momentum polynomial. Edge translation
    phases have constant term one; their higher terms multiply the already
    vanishing constant coefficient.

    At quadratic order the colour-symmetric diagonal forms are scalar
    multiples of the invariant metric. A self-adjoint operator commuting with
    the adjoint action has invariant eigenspaces, which are ideals;
    simplicity leaves only one eigenvalue. Coordinate reflections allow only
    <math|p<rsub|c><rsup|2>> in a diagonal direction entry and
    <math|p<rsub|a>*p<rsub|b>> in an <math|a,b> off-diagonal entry.
    Permutation symmetry identifies their coefficients. The transverse Ward
    equations then force the tensor to be a scalar multiple of
    <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I-p*p<rsup|<math-ss|T>>>. One transverse
    test determines that scalar. Subtracting the actual kinetic symbol
    preserves all symmetries, not merely their leading parts. Its residual
    cubic coefficient vanishes by inversion, since all lower coefficients now
    vanish. Thus the residual has order four.
  </proof>

  Apply the two-leg curvature representation to this residual. The resulting
  quadratic curvature terms have the zero moments <eqref|ch07:moments>. They
  cannot change the coupling: their curvature symbol is
  <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>> and their link symbol is
  <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|4>|)>>. The coefficient of curvature
  square is carried entirely by <math|\<beta\>>. This statement refers to
  Taylor coefficients at the flat field; it does not identify a nonlinear
  effective action with its quadratic truncation.

  <section|Haar measure and gauge completion><label|ch07:completion>

  The Gaussian contribution at a stationary fine configuration
  <math|U<rsub|v>> is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:thermal>T<around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det Q<around|(|v|)>-log J<rsub|<math-up|Haar>><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|Q> is the fluctuation Hessian without its factor
  <math|\<beta\>>, <math|J<rsub|<math-up|Haar>>> is the density of Haar
  measure in the chosen chart, and <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1>> is the
  inherited quadratic loop action. Field-independent constants may be
  subtracted throughout. This does not alter any Taylor coefficient of
  positive degree.

  The following finite-dimensional identity accounts for the ghost factor.
  Let <math|<math-ss|J>> be the joint Hessian in left tangent coordinates,
  <math|\<cal-R\>> the infinitesimal gauge insertion, and <math|\<cal-I\>> a
  slice embedding. Assume

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|J>\<cal-R\>=0,<space|1em><around|\||det
    <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>|\|>=1,<space|1em>Q=<around|(|D*s|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>*<space|0.17em>D*s,<space|1em>J<rsub|<math-up|Haar>>=<around|\||det
    D*s|\|>.
  </equation*>

  These are identities at the stationary configuration. Given a gauge
  condition <math|F> with invertible <math|G=F*\<cal-R\>>, put
  <math|C=<math-ss|J>+F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F>. Block triangular factorization in
  the basis <math|<around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>> gives

  <\equation*>
    det C=<around|(|det G|)><rsup|2>*det <around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>,<space|2em><tfrac|1|2>log
    det Q-log J<rsub|<math-up|Haar>>=<tfrac|1|2>log det C-log <around|\||det
    G|\|>.
  </equation*>

  The fluctuation form is positive on the slice. With a positive regulated
  ghost operator the absolute value can be dropped. This is the
  finite-dimensional Faddeev\UPopov determinant identity; the moving Haar
  Jacobian is part of it. Omitting that Jacobian would change the quantity
  being compared. Background-field perturbation theory uses exactly this
  vector-minus-ghost combination<nbsp><cite|Abbott1982>.

  For the lattice comparison choose <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>> and

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>m<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>,<space|2em>\<Delta\><rsub|U>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|X*\<cal-R\>=I> is the derivative of the gauge frame, take
  <math|F=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+m<rsub|\<mu\>>*X>; then
  <math|G=\<Delta\><rsub|U>+m<rsub|\<mu\>>>. The auxiliary fine operator is
  <math|A<rsub|\<mu\>>=A+d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>+M<rsub|\<mu\>>>,
  where <math|A> is the fine kinetic Hessian and <math|M<rsub|\<mu\>>>
  applies <math|m<rsub|\<mu\>>> to each one-form component. Its determinant
  response is <math|\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<frac|1|2>*log det A<rsub|\<mu\>>-log
  det G>. The same ghost appears in both expressions and cancels in their
  difference.

  <section|The auxiliary lattice determinant><label|ch07:one-loop>

  Perturbative matching for the Wilson lattice action is treated in
  Lüscher\UWeisz<nbsp><cite|LuscherWeisz1995>; our comparison additionally
  controls the selected non-Wilson path action and its block average. The
  continuum coefficient is the classical calculation of Gross\UWilczek and
  Politzer<nbsp><cite|GrossWilczek1973|Politzer1973>. We use the
  background-field formulation<nbsp><cite|Abbott1982>; the following
  normalization check and integrable error criterion are the parts needed for
  the lattice application. Recall that <math|\<alpha\>> includes the factor
  two converting a normalized quadratic Taylor coefficient into the
  coefficient of <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*v,d<rsub|1>*v|\<rangle\>>>.

  For external momentum <math|t*e<rsub|1>>, polarization <math|e<rsub|2>>,
  and unit colour <math|u>, the leading ghost vertex is
  <math|-2*i*k<rsub|2><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>u>. The vector vertex has
  four copies of this orbital term and the spin term
  <math|2*i*t*<around|(|E<rsub|21>-E<rsub|12>|)><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>u>.
  The orbital\Uspin trace is zero. With vector and ghost bubble weights
  <math|-1/4> and <math|+1/2>, respectively, and
  <math|<tr><around|(|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>u|)><rsup|2>=-C<rsub|G>>, the
  coefficient extracted by <eqref|ch07:alpha> has homogeneous integrand

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:pole>h<around|(|k|)>=4*C<rsub|G>*<around*|(|<frac|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|6>>-<frac|4*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>*k<rsub|2><rsup|2>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|8>>-<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|4>>|)>.
  </equation>

  This is the standard background-field bubble calculation, with its colour
  normalization expressed in our metric. It is not a claim that the full
  lattice integrand equals <math|h>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The lattice integrand remainder><label|ch07:integrand-input> Let
    the incoming analytic path action have a fixed positive exponential
    coefficient bound, a uniformly positive curvature metric, and kinetic
    normalization <math|H<around|(|0|)>=I>. Form its actual left-coordinate
    Hessian, complete it by <math|d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>>, and use
    the covariant regulator <math|m<rsub|\<mu\>>> defined above. The
    auxiliary quadratic coefficient has an integrand <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>>
    satisfying

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<alpha\><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>I<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>*<space|0.17em>d*k,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||I<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>A*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||I<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>-h<around|(|k|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>D<around|(|<around|\||k*<around|\||<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|\|>*k|\|><rsup|-6>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The constants depend on the fixed path bounds, metric floor and group,
    but not on <math|\<mu\>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>> or the torus period. The
    finite-torus Hessian is the corresponding momentum mesh average, with an
    exponentially small per-site error relative to the integral at each fixed
    <math|\<mu\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We specify the derivatives used in the comparison. In one vector or ghost
    channel let <math|a<around|(|k|)>> be the zero-background denominator,
    <math|b*<around|(|p;k|)>> its first background insertion, and
    <math|c*<around|(|p,p<rprime|'>;k|)>> its second insertion. These are
    actual derivatives of the same operator; the internal momentum of
    <math|b*<around|(|p;k|)>> changes from <math|k> to <math|k+p>. Their
    contribution to the logarithmic Hessian is

    <\equation>
      <label|ch07:response-integrand><tr><around|{|a<around|(|k|)><rsup|-1>*c*<around|(|-p,p;k|)>-a<around|(|k|)><rsup|-1>*b*<around|(|-p;k+p|)>*a*<around|(|k+p|)><rsup|-1>*b*<around|(|p;k|)>|}>.
    </equation>

    Take half the vector expression minus the ghost expression and apply the
    normalized external coefficient functional. This defines
    <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>>. Finite Fourier inversion gives the identical
    formula with the internal integral replaced by the mesh mean. Thus the
    integrand is associated with the actual fine action, rather than with a
    separately chosen continuum model.

    We first check its local Taylor data. A transported plaquette curvature
    has, under the formal scaling <math|A<around|(|x|)>=\<epsilon\>*a*<around|(|\<epsilon\>*x|)>>,
    leading term <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|2>*<around|(|d*a+<around|[|a,a|]>|)>>
    in the corresponding orientation. Its remaining terms have weight at
    least three; a Taylor derivative or a factor of <math|A> counts as one
    weight. This follows by expanding the four edge exponentials and their
    transports. At weight four the row squares and direct term therefore have
    colour-direction coefficient <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*I+<wide|\<rho\>|^><around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<wide|\<rho\>|^><around|(|0|)>=I>.
    Cubic and quartic curvature terms have weights at least six and eight.
    The leading local density is consequently the Wilson curvature-square
    density. All differences from it have weight at least five. Exponential
    path summability justifies these expansions term by term and bounds their
    Taylor remainders: each derivative costs a polynomial in path length,
    absorbed by a smaller width.

    The Hessian in left increments is obtained from <math|S<around|(|log
    <around|(|e<rsup|x<rsub|+>>*e<rsup|x<rsub|->>|)>|)>> by two
    <math|x<rsub|+>> derivatives. The Baker\UCampbell\UHausdorff correction
    starts with two fields and hence raises the weight relative to its linear
    term. Accordingly, after two internal and <math|n> background
    derivatives, the difference from the Wilson response has momentum order
    at least <math|3-n>, for <math|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>2>. This is a
    statement about the combined momenta of all legs. It gives uniform
    compact derivative bounds as well as the indicated orders.

    In particular the completed vector denominator is

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|k|)>=d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<around|(|k|)>*d<rsub|1><around|(|k|)>+d<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>*d<rsub|0><around|(|k|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*I+O<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|3>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The ghost denominator is <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>*I>, with
    <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>|\|><rsup|2>=<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+O<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|4>|)>>.
    For a mark of direction <math|a> and colour <math|u>, expanding the
    Wilson cubic vertex and adding the gauge-fixing vertex gives the linear
    vector insertion

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*<around|(|p;k|)><rsub|i*j>=<around*|[|-i*<around|(|2*k<rsub|a>+p<rsub|a>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|i*j>+2*i*p<rsub|j>*\<delta\><rsub|i*a>-2*i*p<rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|j*a>|]><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>u.
    </equation*>

    The ghost linear insertion is <math|-i*<around|(|2*k<rsub|a>+p<rsub|a>|)><math-up|ad><space|0.17em>u>.
    These expressions can also be read from the continuum quadratic operator
    in background gauge. The actual lattice remainder in each is
    <math|O<around|(|<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|2>|)>>,
    by the weight comparison just proved. The second insertion and its first
    two external momentum derivatives are uniformly bounded on the real
    momentum cube. No cancellation of a seagull term has been assumed.

    For completeness we give the bounds that propagate this information
    through the regulator and the inverses. At a fixed internal momentum
    <math|k> put <math|T=<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>>, and for a matrix path
    <math|M<around|(|t|)>> write

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|M|\<\|\|\>><rsub|T,2>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>2>
      T<rsup|j><around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsup|<around|(|j|)>><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>>.
    </equation*>

    The paths have displaced momenta <math|k+t*e<rsub|1>>, with velocities of
    bounded size. The product rule bounds products by a fixed multiple of the
    product of these norms. The two inverse derivative identities give
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|T,2>\<leqslant\>C/T<rsup|2>>
    whenever <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M|\<\|\|\>><rsub|T,2>\<leqslant\>C*T<rsup|2>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C/T<rsup|2>>.

    The real denominator floor holds globally on the centered cube. Indeed
    <math|\<omega\><around|(|k|)>\<geqslant\>c<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>>, the
    exterior identity controls the sum of transverse and longitudinal parts,
    and

    <\equation*>
      x+\<mu\><rsup|2><around*|(|<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|)><rsup|9>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|x\<geqslant\>0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Thus both channel denominators are bounded below by <math|c*T<rsup|2>*I>.
    The massive ghost operator <math|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I> has
    denominator, first-insertion and second-insertion bounds of respective
    sizes <math|T<rsup|2>,T,1> in the scaled derivative norms. Inverting
    gives sizes <math|T<rsup|-2>,T<rsup|-3>,T<rsup|-4>>: a first insertion is
    <math|-R*b*R>, and a second is the sum of <math|-R*c*R> and the two
    ordered <math|R*b*R*b*R> terms. Multiplication by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>,
    taking nine products, and multiplying once more by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>
    gives regulator sizes

    <\equation>
      <label|ch07:regulator-budgets>\<mu\><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsup|2>/T,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsup|2>/T<rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    This uses <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>/T<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1>. Product
    differentiation through order two preserves the same sizes in
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|T,2>>; the number nine
    is fixed. The four constant direction selectors in the vector regulator
    do not change these powers.

    The first term of <eqref|ch07:response-integrand>, differentiated twice
    in <math|t>, is bounded by <math|C*T<rsup|-2>+C*\<mu\><rsup|2>*T<rsup|-6>>.
    Its unshifted inverse is independent of <math|t>; the local second
    insertion has a unit-scale bound, while its regulator part uses
    <eqref|ch07:regulator-budgets>. The second term has two inverses of size
    <math|T<rsup|-2>> and two insertions of size <math|T>, with two
    derivatives costing <math|T<rsup|-2>>. Its bound is <math|C*T<rsup|-4>>.
    Since <math|T> stays bounded above on the cube, these estimates give the
    first asserted radial bound.

    For the comparison use <math|T=<around|\||k|\|>> and restrict to
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>T\<leqslant\>1>. Let
    <math|\<delta\>=T+\<mu\><rsup|2>/T<rsup|2>>. The actual denominator minus
    the free quadratic denominator has scaled norm at most
    <math|C*\<delta\>*T<rsup|2>>. The resolvent identity therefore bounds the
    inverse difference by <math|C*\<delta\>*T<rsup|-2>>. Likewise the first
    insertion minus its displayed linear part is bounded by
    <math|C*\<delta\>*T>: the local remainder is
    <math|O<around|(|T<rsup|2>|)>> and the regulator contribution is
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>/T|)>>. Subtract the free bubble by
    telescoping its four factors. Each term contains one difference, so its
    second external derivative is bounded by
    <math|C*\<delta\>*T<rsup|-4>=C*<around|(|T<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*T<rsup|-6>|)>>.
    The seagull estimate above is bounded by the same expression on this
    annulus. The free vector-minus-ghost bubble is exactly <eqref|ch07:pole>,
    proving the second radial inequality.

    At fixed <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> the denominators are invertible on a
    smaller complex strip by their positive real floor and analyticity. All
    three response layers come from the same infinite-lattice path series.
    The integrand thus has a common holomorphic extension for that fixed
    <math|\<mu\>>. For a periodic analytic function, the difference between
    its mesh mean and its integral is the sum of nonzero Fourier coefficients
    whose indices are multiples of the mesh side. Exponential coefficient
    decay bounds this error by <math|C<rsub|\<mu\>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|\<mu\>>*n>>.
    The constants in this last recording estimate may depend on
    <math|\<mu\>>; the radial constants proved above do not. This proves the
    final assertion and justifies the coefficient integral.
  </proof>

  <section|Uniform comparison of determinants><label|ch07:comparison>

  Two estimates must be distinguished. An absolute determinant Taylor bound
  may grow like the number <math|L<rsup|4>> of fine sites per block. The
  <em|difference> between the blocked and auxiliary determinant must have a
  bound independent of <math|L> in order to identify the logarithmic
  coefficient. The relevant mechanism is finite rank together with a
  trace-class regulator.

  Here is the structure behind the trace-norm requirement. Writing <math|H>
  for the block average derivative, <math|B> for its penalty Hessian,
  <math|Z> for the coarse-frame projection, and <math|K=m<rsub|\<mu\>>*X>,
  cancellation of the fine kinetic Hessians leaves

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Theta\>=>|<cell|H<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*H-Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z+P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>*K+K<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+K<rsup|\<ast\>>*K<eq-number><label|ch07:theta>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|12mm>-P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|\<mu\>>*P<rsub|f>.>>>>
  </align>

  The first two terms factor through spaces of dimensions at most <math|4*dim
  <gLie>> and <math|dim <gLie>> per coarse momentum. Thus
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|V*W|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>r<around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>>>
  with <math|r> independent of <math|L>. Derivatives preserve the
  intermediate spaces.

  For the regulator, at zero background the alias eigenvalues of
  <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>> are bounded by <math|C*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|-2>>
  in a smaller complex strip. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>><around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|-6>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  Given bounded first two insertions of <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>>, distribute at
  most two derivatives among the three factors in
  <math|<around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>. Every term retains one
  undifferentiated trace-class cube; the other factors have operator bounds.
  Hence the first two insertions of <math|m<rsub|\<mu\>>> have trace norm
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>. This explains the ninth power and
  avoids an erroneous factor <math|L<rsup|4>>. To apply this trace estimate,
  we establish the actual weight conversions, stationary insertions and
  scaled inverse bounds below.

  <subsection|The stationary branch and the block average contact
  term><label|ch07:contact>

  We verify the contact assertion for the block average actually used in the
  block integral. Let <math|\<zeta\><around|(|x|)>> be its stationary
  representative in centered coordinates, with
  <math|\<zeta\><around|(|0|)>=0> and tangent
  <math|<around|(|W*x,r<rsub|x>|)>>, where <math|r<rsub|x>> is its coarse
  scalar component after recentering. For each scalar determinant function
  <math|f*<around|(|x,z|)>>, constant colour rotations act orthogonally on
  its matrices and leave <math|f> invariant. Every invariant linear
  functional on the simple Lie algebra vanishes, since it annihilates
  brackets and <math|<around|[|<gLie>,<gLie>|]>=<gLie>>. Applying this to
  each coordinate gives <math|D*f<around|(|0|)>=0>. Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:tangent-chain>D<rsup|2>*<around|[|f*<around|(|x,\<zeta\><around|(|x|)>|)>|]><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]>=D<rsup|2>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|u,W*u,r<rsub|u>|)>,<around|(|v,W*v,r<rsub|v>|)>|]>.
  </equation>

  Second derivatives of the stationary branch have disappeared for this
  specific reason. Matrix Hessians themselves need not have vanishing first
  derivative; the statement is about their invariant scalar determinants.

  Use rooted coordinates for the following exact calculation. Let
  <math|w<rsub|x><around|(|v|)>> be the logarithmic change in the block
  average under an internal left increment <math|v>, with
  <math|w<rsub|x><around|(|0|)>=0>. Let <math|r<around|(|x|)>> be the
  derivative of the block penalty with respect to that block average
  increment, and set

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|x><around|(|a|)><rsub|i*j>=<around|\<langle\>|a,D<rsub|v><rsup|2>*w<rsub|x><around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|i>,e<rsub|j>|]>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>Z<around|(|x|)>=S<rsub|x><around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>A<around|(|x|)>=M<rsub|<math-up|core>><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The Hessian chain rule gives <math|M<rsub|<math-up|act>>=A+Z>, with
  <math|r<around|(|0|)>=Z<around|(|0|)>=0>. The rooted-to-centered internal
  change is triangular with determinant one, so it does not change the
  determinant. Define <math|Y<around|(|x|)>> by
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|a,Y<around|(|x|)>|\<rangle\>>=<tr><around|(|A<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|x><around|(|a|)>|)>>.
  The block average is covariant, and the internal matrices transform by the
  same congruence. Cyclicity of trace therefore makes <math|Y> a covariant
  coarse vector. Constant colour rotations imply <math|Y<around|(|0|)>=0>.

  The linear block identity <math|Q*W=I-b<rsup|-1>*K<rsub|c>> fixes the
  derivative of the penalty gradient: <math|D*r<around|(|0|)>=-K<rsub|c>=:J>.
  Expanding <math|log det <around|(|A+Z|)>-log det A> through degree two now
  gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsup|2>*<around|(|log det
    M<rsub|<math-up|act>>-log det M<rsub|<math-up|core>>|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|u,v|]>=>|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|J*u,D*Y<around|(|0|)>*v|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|J*v,D*Y<around|(|0|)>*u|\<rangle\>><eq-number><label|ch07:contact-exact>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|-<tr><around|(|A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|0>*<around|(|J*u|)>*A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>*S<rsub|0>*<around|(|J*v|)>|)>.>>>>
  </align>

  In particular the derivative of <math|A<rsup|-1>> is included in
  <math|D*Y>; <math|Y<around|(|0|)>=0> removes the term with
  <math|D<rsup|2>*r>.

  There is an exact identity <math|D*Y<around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|0,c>=0>. Gauge
  the trivial configuration by a coarse gauge transformation, then restore
  the chosen tree gauge. Gauge invariance preserves stationarity; uniqueness
  of the local stationary solution identifies the restored configuration with
  the selected branch on this curve. Covariance and <math|Y<around|(|0|)>=0>
  show that <math|Y> vanishes there. Differentiation proves the identity.
  This uses the actual block average, rather than an independently chosen
  linear average.

  At fixed <math|L>, the finite-stencil block average and the analytic
  inverse of <math|A<around|(|0|)>> give common holomorphic kernels for every
  term in <eqref|ch07:contact-exact>. Normalized finite-torus traces are
  momentum mesh averages. Their limit is the integral of these same kernels;
  exponential Fourier decay gives uniform convergence also along varying
  external mesh momenta. Write <math|M<around|(|p|)>> for the mixed kernel of
  <math|D*Y>. Its Ward identity along <math|p=t*e<rsub|\<nu\>>> reads
  <math|q<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>*M<rsub|j*\<nu\>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>=0>
  in each colour. Hence every column of <math|M<around|(|0|)>> is zero and
  <math|M<around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>|)>>. Since
  <math|J<around|(|p|)>=-K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>,
  the first two terms of <eqref|ch07:contact-exact> have order at least
  three, and the last has order at least four. Therefore the contact
  correction has zero <math|\<alpha\>>. Only qualitative analyticity at fixed
  <math|L> was used here. Its constants need not be uniform in <math|L>,
  because this is an exact vanishing statement.

  <subsection|Bounds for the actual centered matrices><label|ch07:weighted>

  The actual stationary tangent is <math|W<rsub|a>> from
  <eqref|ch05:corrected-transport>; its forest representative is
  <math|W<rsub|F>> from <eqref|ch05:stationary-tangent>. As above,
  <math|\<beta\>> is the actual inverse coupling, and <math|b> is the soft
  constraint coefficient in the blocking kernel.

  We establish the operator estimates for the selected path and block
  average. The inputs are their positive normalized metric, fixed exponential
  path bounds, and the stationary identification and smooth representative of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:transports>; all refer to the same adjacent
  pair. The coarse scalar part of the smooth representative may be nonzero
  after recentering. Its derivatives below are merely required to be bounded,
  not to vanish.

  Recall that <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>> is the inverse block ratio. Use the
  unitary block Fourier transform <math|P<around|(|p|)>> on fine residues,
  and set

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|<around|\||q*<around|(|<math-up|Re><around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|l>h<rsub|l><around|(|p|)><rsup|2><around|\||<around|(|P<around|(|p|)><rsup|-1>*u|)><rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The joint space also has a coarse scalar component with its ordinary
  Euclidean norm. Opposite momenta are paired in the dual norm. On a fixed
  small coarse strip, <math|P> and <math|P<rsup|-1>> have bounded operator
  norms, <math|h<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, and
  <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>5*h<rsub|l>>. Thus
  a unit internal direction has normalized residue <math|L<rsup|2>> norm
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>> and its first fine difference has normalized
  <math|L<rsup|2>> norm <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>. External smooth
  bridge legs have the same powers in supremum norm, by
  <eqref|ch05:actual-bridge>. Extending to the fixed padded block average
  patch only changes constants: its number of residue blocks is fixed at the
  given smoothing order, and its Bloch phases are uniformly bounded.

  <paragraph|Fine kinetic insertions.> The zero-background kinetic form is a
  curl on each internal leg. Its first background insertion is a sum of
  products with one finite difference among three legs; its second insertion
  needs none among four legs. These representations have exponentially
  summable coefficients with uniform bounds. For the first insertion,
  subtract its constant momentum coefficient, which is zero by the
  weight-four Taylor calculation in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:integrand-input>,
  and use coordinate division. The zero-background representation is exactly
  <math|d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*H*d<rsub|1>>; the second insertion already
  has the required form. The factor count is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|n+2|)>+<text|number of differences>=4*<space|2em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>n\<leqslant\>2|)>.
  </equation*>

  Hölder with two internal <math|L<rsup|2>> factors and the remaining
  supremum factors costs <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|4>|)>> in normalized
  counting measure. Summing the fine anchor costs <math|L<rsup|4>>, canceling
  it. A translation by <math|z> introduces a Bloch factor of modulus at most
  <math|C*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|>/L>>. The exponential decay of the
  coefficients makes the resulting sum over <math|z> uniformly bounded. Thus
  the fine kinetic Hessian and its first two background insertions have
  bounded operators between the stated weighted spaces, uniformly in
  <math|L>. This is an estimate for the actual left-coordinate response,
  whose split-field definition was used above.

  <paragraph|The centered frame and penalty.> A small supremum bound alone is
  not enough for two internal directions. Use normalized probability measure
  on the padded block and the three norms
  <math|L<rsup|\<infty\>>,L<rsup|2>,L<rsup|1>>. External directions use the
  first, one internal direction the second, and two internal directions the
  third. Products are controlled by <math|L<rsup|2>*L<rsup|2>\<subset\>L<rsup|1>>,
  and averages contract these norms. A path of <math|O<around|(|L|)>> links,
  each of whose positive derivatives is <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>> in its
  assigned norm, has bounded derivatives of every fixed order. Indeed
  adjoining one more link changes the next derivative by
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>> times a polynomial in already bounded lower
  derivatives; summing <math|O<around|(|L|)>> such changes costs
  <math|O<around|(|1|)>>. For coordinate connector paths, a fixed-position
  read is a coordinatewise splice of its endpoints. On the endpoint pair
  space this is a measure-preserving coordinate permutation, followed by a
  bounded-measure restriction and translation. The argument therefore applies
  to the actual averaged connectors.

  Here is why centering gives uniform frame derivatives. Let <math|F<rsub|x>>
  be the minimizing frame after recentering, <math|E<rsub|x*y>> its averaged
  transport, <math|\<nu\>> normalized counting measure on its block, and
  <math|\<omega\>> the smoothing probability. If <math|\<cal-P\>> is the
  nearest-group map and <math|\<Pi\>=D*\<cal-P\><around|(|1|)>>, define

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|y>\<nu\><around|(|y|)>*F<rsub|y>,<space|2em>B<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|y>\<nu\><around|(|y|)>*F<rsub|y>*<around|(|E<rsub|x*y>-1|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|T|)>=\<cal-P\><around|(|T|)>-\<Pi\>*T,<space|2em>S<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*B<rsub|x>+R*<around|(|J<rsub|0>+B<rsub|x>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The minimizing equation <math|F<rsub|x>=\<cal-P\>*<around|(|J<rsub|0>+B<rsub|x>|)>>
  and centering <math|<big|sum><rsub|x>\<omega\><around|(|x|)>*\<Pi\>*F<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*1>
  imply exactly

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|x>=\<Pi\>*1-<big|sum><rsub|y>\<omega\><around|(|y|)>*\<Pi\>*S<rsub|y>+S<rsub|x>.
  </equation*>

  Now <math|E<rsub|x*y><around|(|0|)>-1=0> and <math|D*R<around|(|1|)>=0>. At
  derivative order <math|n+1> the right side depends on frame derivatives
  only through order <math|n>. Induction using the preceding product bounds
  gives uniform derivatives through order four, also with two internal
  directions. The smoothing probability satisfies
  <math|\<omega\>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<nu\>>: its reduction modulo <math|L>
  is uniform, so every atom has mass at most <math|L<rsup|-4>>, and its
  support has <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L<rsup|4>|)>> sites. This controls the
  change of averaging measure. The argument estimates the selected minimizer;
  its existence and local uniqueness come from
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>.

  Connector averaging and the fixed smooth penalty are compositions of these
  operations. Their mixed derivatives with up to two external and two
  internal directions are uniformly bounded. The centered frame logarithmic
  derivative <math|X> has normalized <math|L<rsup|2>> output bounded by a
  constant, so its unnormalized output costs at most <math|C*L<rsup|2>>.
  Rooted and centered descriptions differ by a triangular coordinate change
  of determinant one; the same averaged-path bound controls its external
  coarse coordinates. Consequently <math|F=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+m<rsub|\<mu\>>*X>
  has a uniform weighted operator bound, since
  <math|m<rsub|\<mu\>>=O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>> cancels
  <math|L<rsup|2>>. Frame covariance gives <math|X*\<cal-R\>=I> in the same
  coordinates, and hence <math|F*\<cal-R\>=\<Delta\><rsub|U>+m<rsub|\<mu\>>>.
  Gauge signs are fixed here by taking the fine component of <math|\<cal-R\>>
  to be <math|d<rsub|U>>.

  <paragraph|Uniform inverse bounds.> At zero background the auxiliary fine
  form has the weighted floor <math|2<rsup|-10>*min
  <around|(|1,\<varepsilon\>|)>>, where <math|H<rsub|f>\<geqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*I>.
  This follows from the exterior identity and the scalar regulator floor
  proved in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:integrand-input>. The
  block-completed form needs a quotient estimate. In centered coordinates it
  is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-G\><around|(|u,r|)>=<around|\<langle\>|u,K<rsub|f>*u|\<rangle\>>+b*<around|\||M*u-d<rsub|0,c>*r|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|M> is the straight averaged connector. Indeed the rooted linear
  block average is <math|Q=M+d<rsub|0,c>*R> by <eqref|ch05:actual-Q>; setting
  <math|r=r<rsub|<math-up|root>>-R*u> converts
  <math|Q*u-d<rsub|0,c>*r<rsub|<math-up|root>>> to the displayed expression.
  Under a fine gauge variation, the new scalar coordinate changes by the
  smoothed scalar mean, since <math|R*d<rsub|0,f>> is root evaluation minus
  that mean. This is the same centering used in the frame calculation, and
  the triangular coordinate change has determinant one. Subtract a gauge
  vector to make every nonprincipal alias of <math|u> transverse and
  eliminate <math|r> using the principal scalar alias. The latter scalar mean
  is <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> times a product of centered box sums, bounded away
  from zero after division by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> on the principal real
  cube. Write the resulting fine aliases as <math|u<rsub|l>> and set
  <math|A<rsub|h>=<big|sum><rsub|l\<ne\>0>h<rsub|l><rsup|2><around|\||u<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2>>.
  The fine metric floor gives control of <math|A<rsub|h>>, since
  <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c*\<mu\>> for
  <math|l\<ne\>0>. The principal coefficient of <math|M> has least singular
  value <math|c<rsub|j>*\<mu\>>: its connector sum is of order <math|L> and
  the unitary block Fourier coefficient is <math|L<rsup|-2>>. For its upper
  bound, each fixed-position averaged read has coefficients
  <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L<rsup|-4>|)>> on
  <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L<rsup|4>|)>> sites, hence Euclidean row norm
  <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L<rsup|-2>|)>>. Summing its <math|L> connector
  positions and converting a weighted unit vector with
  <math|h<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>L<rsup|-1>> gives a fixed bound. Thus the full
  <math|M> has bounded weighted norm. Consequently

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsup|2><around|\||u<rsub|0>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*<around|(|<around|\||M*u|\|><rsup|2>+A<rsub|h>|)>,<space|2em>inf<rsub|a><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|u,r|)>-\<cal-R\>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<cal-G\><around|(|u,r|)>.
  </equation*>

  These estimates include zero coarse momentum; the scalar principal alias
  removes the coarse scalar coordinate there as well.

  Moreover <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\||\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>*a|\|>>,
  where <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<Delta\>+m<rsub|\<mu\>>>. For the fine
  component this is <math|h<rsub|l><around|\||q<rsub|l>|\|>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|l><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>>;
  the coarse averaging component has norm <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>
  and obeys the same bound. Since <math|F*\<cal-R\>=\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
  and <math|F> is bounded, the quotient estimate implies a positive weighted
  floor for <math|\<cal-G\>+F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F>. All constants are fixed
  before <math|L>.

  The weights are not holomorphic. To continue this floor, at each real
  anchor freeze <math|h<rsub|l>> while retaining the holomorphic block
  Fourier matrix. On a fixed-radius coarse ball these frozen weights are
  uniformly comparable to the moving weights, because
  <math|h<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>> and <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>-q<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\>*<around|\||p-x|\|>>.
  Cauchy's inequality in the frozen coordinates bounds the change in operator
  norm, and a fixed smaller strip preserves half the real floor. This proves
  uniform holomorphic inverse bounds for both matrices. Differentiating
  <math|M<rsup|-1>*M=I> then bounds the first two inverse insertions.

  <paragraph|Trace norms and recording.> The kinetic response cancels
  identically, including its two derivatives, in <eqref|ch07:theta>. The
  finite-rank terms retain their fixed-dimensional coarse intermediate
  spaces. For the regulator, the trace-class cube argument preceding this
  subsection applies. In the terms containing <math|m<rsub|\<mu\>>*X>, its
  factor <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> compensates for the <math|L<rsup|2>> frame
  derivative. In the separate term <math|P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|\<mu\>>*P<rsub|f>>,
  it compensates for two weighted-to-Euclidean conversions, each of size
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|-1>|)>>. These costs occur in different terms;
  the same factor is not used to compensate for both. Positive external
  derivatives of <math|d<rsub|U>> have a fine-link factor
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>>; the single weighted conversion costs at most
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|-1>|)>>. Their first two insertion norms are
  therefore bounded in both placements needed for
  <math|d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>> and
  <math|d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>>. The remaining products use
  exactly these compatible input/output norms. Thus <math|\<Theta\>> and its
  two insertions have uniform trace norm. This proves all operator and trace
  bounds used in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:comparison-prop> for this
  same action and frame.

  Finally the common Fourier representations follow from the infinite path
  coefficients and finite block average stencils. All derivatives are taken
  before restricting to a torus grid. Products and inverse derivatives
  preserve this identification, with intermediate momenta shifted by the
  inserted external momentum. The Fourier mesh estimate at the end of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:integrand-input> gives the finite-volume
  exponential error. The preceding contact proof identifies the actual
  stationary thermal coefficient. Hence the comparison concerns the same
  density, block average and stationary branch throughout.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Common comparison estimates><label|ch07:comparison-input> On a
    fixed positive strip of coarse momenta, the stationary background
    insertions and all intermediate shifted momenta obey the following
    bounds, uniformly in <math|L> and in the period. After removing the block
    average contact term, the blocked completed matrix <math|C<rsub|0>> and
    <math|<wide|A|~><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-up|diag><around|(|A<rsub|\<mu\>>,I|)>>
    have bounded inverses and bounded first and second normalized background
    insertions in compatible weighted Hilbert norms. Their difference
    <math|\<Theta\>> and its first two insertions have bounded trace norm in
    these same norms. The common quadratic symbols represent the finite-torus
    coefficients with an error tending exponentially to zero with the coarse
    period. The stationary tangent represents the actual block average and
    obeys the exact coarse\Ufine matching identities.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The preceding weighted inverse, insertion and trace estimates prove this
    proposition for the actual normalized path and block average.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Block comparison><label|ch07:comparison-prop> For the same
    normalized path and block average, the curvature-square coefficients of
    the blocked response and the auxiliary fine determinant satisfy
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>-\<alpha\><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C>,
    uniformly in <math|L,N,p>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For a positive matrix continued holomorphically near zero background,

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|v>*D<rsub|w>*log det A=<tr><around|(|A<rsup|-1>*A<rsub|v*w>-A<rsup|-1>*A<rsub|v>*A<rsup|-1>*A<rsub|w>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Subtract this formula for <math|C<rsub|0>> and
    <math|<wide|A|~><rsub|\<mu\>>>, and use
    <math|C<rsub|0><rsup|-1>-<wide|A|~><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>=-C<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>*<wide|A|~><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>>.
    Telescoping produces terms containing a trace-class difference or one of
    its derivatives, with all other factors bounded. The trace ideal
    inequality gives a uniform bound for the difference symbol on a common
    strip. The ghosts cancel. The contact correction has zero
    <math|\<alpha\>> by the preceding order argument.

    Folding the fine symbol into coarse momentum also preserves
    <math|\<alpha\>>. The auxiliary determinant is gauge invariant, so its
    quadratic symbol annihilates longitudinal legs. For a nonprincipal alias
    the equation <math|K<rsub|f><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>*N<rsub|l>=A<rsub|l>*K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>>
    and the positive transverse fine inverse give
    <math|N<rsub|l><rsup|\<perp\>>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>,
    since <math|K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.
    Thus nonprincipal aliases contribute order four even when their
    longitudinal amplitudes do not vanish. The zero alias has factors
    <math|L<rsup|4>> from cell volume, <math|L<rsup|-2>> from the two
    interpolated link fields, and <math|L<rsup|-2>> from extraction at
    momentum <math|p/L>. Their product is one. The inherited loop has order
    four and contributes no <math|\<alpha\>>. Finally Cauchy's inequality on
    a circle inside the common strip bounds the second Taylor coefficient of
    the difference by a fixed multiple of its supremum. This proves the
    assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The one-loop drift>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One-loop drift><label|ch07:slope> For an adjacent pair with the
    normalized path, metric floor and actual stationary identification used
    in Section<nbsp><reference|ch07:weighted>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L|\|>\<leqslant\>C
    </equation*>

    with a constant depending only on the fixed stage inputs.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    On the unit three-sphere the angular means are
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*\<theta\><rsub|2><rsup|2>=1/4> and
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|2><rsup|2>=1/24>.
    Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||k|\|>\<less\>1>h<around|(|k|)>*<space|0.17em>d*k=<frac|8*\<pi\><rsup|2>*C<rsub|G>|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>>*<around*|(|<frac|1|4>-<frac|1|6>-1|)>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>=-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log \<mu\><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    The two error integrals on this annulus are uniformly bounded:
    <math|<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|1>r<rsup|3>*r<rsup|-3>*d*r\<leqslant\>1>
    and <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|1>r<rsup|3>*r<rsup|-6>*d*r\<leqslant\>1/2>.
    The ball <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>> has volume
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|4>|)>> and integrand
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|-4>|)>>; the region outside the unit ball is
    also uniformly integrable. Thus <math|\<alpha\><around|(|\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>=-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    \<mu\><rsup|-1>+O<around|(|1|)>>. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:comparison-prop>
    and <math|\<mu\>=L<rsup|-1>> finish the proof.
  </proof>

  The sign is for downward blocking: the coefficient <math|\<beta\>> of the
  kinetic action decreases by <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L> at one loop.
  This proposition does not assert the exact nonlinear recursion for
  <math|\<beta\>>. Connected interactions contribute to that recursion and
  are handled by the one-step integral identity.

  <section|Classical matching through fourth order><label|ch07:classical>

  <subsection|Uniform classical stationary
  coefficients><label|ch07:classical-uniform>

  To construct the cubic and quartic matching terms, we first need classical
  coefficient bounds fixed before <math|L>. The fixed-<math|L> stationary
  chart of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian> provides existence and
  differentiation, but its tree-coordinate inverse alone does not provide
  these stronger bounds. We obtain them from the weighted quotient just
  constructed.

  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|(|v,z|)>> be the joint classical
  action, with coarse field <math|v> and internal fine-plus-frame coordinates
  <math|z>. It contains the fine kinetic action and the actual block penalty,
  without <math|\<beta\>> or the inherited loop. At zero its first derivative
  vanishes. Write <math|<with|math-font|cal*|H>=D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)>>
  and let <math|\<cal-R\>> denote its infinitesimal internal gauge action.
  Its completed inverse is

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>=<around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|H>+F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F|)><rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  with the uniform weighted bound just proved. For a coarse direction
  <math|u> let <math|T*u=<around|(|u,W<rsub|a>*u,r<rsub|u>|)>> be the full
  stationary tangent in the smooth centered representative. In particular

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:tangent-orthogonal>D<rsup|2>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)><around|[|T*u,<around|(|0,z|)>|]>=0<space|1em><text|for
    every internal >z.
  </equation>

  This is the differentiated stationarity equation, with the actual block
  average identification of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>.
  Stationarity in all joint internal directions follows from stationarity on
  the forest slice and gauge invariance: the forest slice together with the
  joint gauge directions spans the internal tangent space. Thus coarse-frame
  directions are included in <eqref|ch07:tangent-orthogonal>. Define the
  internal covector

  <\equation*>
    B*<around|(|u,v|)><around|[|z|]>=D<rsup|3>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)><around|[|T*u,T*v,<around|(|0,z|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  Both the external cubic and quartic vertices and <math|B> have uniformly
  bounded common Fourier kernels. Here is the power count in their actual
  weighted norms. A fine-action cubic vertex has at least one finite
  difference among its three legs; a quartic vertex needs none. Their
  exponentially summable coefficient bounds follow from the same
  normalized-path expansion and coordinate division used for the kinetic
  insertions above. An undifferentiated external leg costs
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>> in supremum norm, and its differentiated leg
  costs <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>. An internal unit weighted leg
  costs these same powers in normalized <math|L<rsup|2>> norm. Thus three
  external legs with one difference, four external legs, and two external
  legs with one internal leg and one difference each cost
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|4>|)>> in normalized residue summation. In the
  last case <math|L<rsup|2>\<subset\>L<rsup|1>> on probability measure is
  sufficient. The sum over <math|L<rsup|4>> fine anchors cancels this factor.
  Finitely many coordinate-change terms have the same or higher weight.
  Penalty vertices are bounded by the centered-frame mixed-derivative
  induction above, which allows up to four derivatives and includes the
  bounded coarse scalar legs <math|r<rsub|u>>. The bounds for <math|B> are
  dual weighted norms, not Euclidean coordinate bounds with a
  dimension-dependent conversion.

  The covector <math|B> annihilates gauge directions. To check this, write
  the internal gauge identity as <math|D<rsub|z>*<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|(|v,z|)>*<around|[|\<cal-R\>*<around|(|v,z|)>*a|]>=0>
  and differentiate twice along <math|T*u,T*v>. The term containing
  <math|D*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)>> is zero. The two terms with
  a derivative of <math|\<cal-R\>> vanish by <eqref|ch07:tangent-orthogonal>,
  since those derivatives are internal vectors. The remaining term is

  <\equation*>
    B*<around|(|u,v|)>*<around|[|\<cal-R\><around|(|0|)>*a|]>=0.
  </equation*>

  Therefore <math|w=<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>*B*<around|(|u,v|)>> is a
  representative of the quotient solution
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|H>*w=B*<around|(|u,v|)>>. Indeed, pairing
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C>*w=B*<around|(|u,v|)>> with <math|\<cal-R\>*a>
  gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|F*\<cal-R\>*a,F*w|\<rangle\>>=0>. The
  regulated gauge matrix <math|F*\<cal-R\>> is invertible, so <math|F*w=0>
  and <math|<with|math-font|cal*|H>*w=B*<around|(|u,v|)>>. Any other solution
  differs by a gauge vector, against which <math|B> vanishes. This justifies
  using the bounded completed inverse in the exchange term; it does not
  replace a degenerate Hessian by an arbitrary inverse.

  If <math|E<around|(|v|)>> is the stationary value, implicit differentiation
  now gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsup|3>*E<around|(|0|)><around|[|u<rsub|1>,u<rsub|2>,u<rsub|3>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|3>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)><around|[|T*u<rsub|1>,T*u<rsub|2>,T*u<rsub|3>|]>,<eq-number><label|ch07:uniform-stationary-jets>>>|<row|<cell|D<rsup|4>*E<around|(|0|)><around|[|u<rsub|1>,u<rsub|2>,u<rsub|3>,u<rsub|4>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|4>*<with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|0|)><around|[|T*u<rsub|1>,T*u<rsub|2>,T*u<rsub|3>,T*u<rsub|4>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>-<big|sum><rsub|<around|{|i*j|}>*<around|{|k*l|}>>B*<around|(|u<rsub|i>,u<rsub|j>|)>*<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>*B*<around|(|u<rsub|k>,u<rsub|l>|)>,>>>>
  </align>

  where the sum is over the three partitions into two unordered pairs. These
  formulas follow by differentiating stationarity; the higher branch
  derivatives multiply its first derivative or
  <eqref|ch07:tangent-orthogonal>. On the diagonal the last sum is
  <math|3*B*<around|(|u,u|)>*<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>*B*<around|(|u,u|)>>,
  agreeing with the earlier formula. The gauge-annihilation argument makes
  the formulas independent of the representative chosen to differentiate the
  stationary branch.

  For Fourier waves, each exchange inverse has intermediate coarse momentum
  equal to a sum of two external momenta. Restrict each external strip by a
  fixed factor so that all these sums remain in the inverse strip. The vertex
  and inverse bounds then give a common strip and a uniform bound for both
  symbols in <eqref|ch07:uniform-stationary-jets>, before choosing <math|L>.
  Their finite-volume identities are exact: every vertex and inverse is the
  restriction of the same common Fourier matrix, and momentum conservation
  leaves the coarse volume factor exactly once. Dividing by <math|3>! and
  <math|4>! gives normalized common Taylor symbols with the required bounds.
  No stationary neighborhood of size uniform in <math|L> has been inferred
  from this argument; only its fixed-order coefficients need that uniformity
  here.

  <subsection|Construction of the matching curvature terms>

  Let <math|E<around|(|v|)>> be the classical stationary action of
  Chapter<nbsp>5, with the factor <math|\<beta\>> removed. Its Hessian is
  prescribed by the exact free block relation. Write the corresponding
  plaquette metric as <math|H=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*I+\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<rho\>>,
  with <math|H<around|(|0|)>=I> and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<gtr\>0>. The
  direct Wilson term together with the squares of the transported rows of
  <math|\<rho\>> gives an analytic gauge-invariant action
  <math|J<around|(|v|)>> with this Hessian. Indeed the transported rows
  linearize to <math|\<rho\>*d<rsub|1>*v>, so

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|2>*J<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>=<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*v,H*d<rsub|1>*v|\<rangle\>>=D<rsup|2>*E<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>.
  </equation*>

  The construction uses the coarse metric matched to the incoming Gaussian
  form, not an arbitrary positive coarse metric.

  The preceding stationary coefficient estimates and the fixed row bounds
  supply the hypotheses of the following construction.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Classical matching><label|ch07:classical-match>Suppose <math|E>
    and <math|J> are invariant analytic germs on every torus, have zero value
    and first derivative and identical Hessians. Suppose their normalized
    coefficients of orders three and four have common strip bounds, and the
    row coefficients of <math|J> have an exponential bound. There are
    invariant cubic and quartic curvature terms <math|S<rsub|3>,S<rsub|4>>,
    chosen once for all periods, such that

    <\equation*>
      E-J-S<rsub|3>-S<rsub|4>=O<around|(|v<rsup|5>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Their weighted sums are bounded by a fixed multiple of the input Taylor
    bounds on a smaller positive width.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The leading coefficient of <math|E-J> is cubic and satisfies the
    homogeneous Ward identities. Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch07:curl> and the
    transported construction produce <math|S<rsub|3>> with that coefficient.
    The fourth coefficient of <math|E-J-S<rsub|3>> is again transverse, since
    its lower coefficients vanish. Apply the lemma again to obtain
    <math|S<rsub|4>>. It is essential to subtract the fourth coefficient of
    <math|S<rsub|3>>: a nonlinear transported cubic curvature term has
    nonzero higher Taylor coefficients. The exponential path bound controls
    each fixed Taylor derivative on a smaller complex link ball; factors of
    path length are absorbed by a width loss. Thus the fourth coefficient of
    <math|S<rsub|3>> has the required common bound. This proves both the
    matching and the bounds. Polarization converts the equality on rays to
    equality of all derivatives through order four.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Preserving the classical coefficient bound>

  These estimates can also be arranged to preserve a fixed classical
  coefficient bound, rather than producing an unspecified function of the
  incoming bound. Keep the Gaussian metric bounds fixed, and denote the bound
  on the remaining incoming path coefficients by <math|A>. Compare the fine
  action with the Wilson action, evaluated on the <em|same> tangents <math|T>
  and with the <em|same> completed inverse
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>>. The weight-four normalization
  proved above implies that their cubic vertex difference has at least two
  finite differences, and their quartic vertex difference has at least one.
  Coordinate division of their common symbols gives these factors with
  exponentially summable coefficients bounded by a polynomial
  <math|P<around|(|A|)>>. A slightly stronger incoming exponential width
  absorbs the polynomial path-length factors.

  When all legs are external, the extra difference gives
  <math|L<rsup|4>*\<mu\><rsup|5>=\<mu\>>. For the difference of the covectors
  <math|B>, move differences on the single internal leg to the two external
  legs by momentum conservation. In a coordinate this uses

  <\equation*>
    q*<around|(|-p<rsub|1>-p<rsub|2>|)>=-e<rsup|-i*<around|(|p<rsub|1>+p<rsub|2>|)>>*<around|{|q<around|(|p<rsub|1>|)>+e<rsup|i*p<rsub|1>>*q<around|(|p<rsub|2>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  It is a discrete integration by parts, with bounded translation factors; it
  leaves at most two differences on any external leg. The actual smooth
  tangent bounds through order two therefore give the same gain. The internal
  leg is estimated only in normalized <math|L<rsup|2>>, without asking for a
  second weighted derivative. Thus the vertex and dual-vertex remainders are
  bounded by <math|P<around|(|A|)>/L>.

  The Wilson vertices, block penalty, and their exchange pairing have bounds
  depending only on the fixed Gaussian metric and block average data. The
  inverse in the exchange term depends only on the quadratic action, so
  higher incoming coefficients do not change it. Expanding the two factors
  <math|B=B<rsub|<math-up|W>>+\<delta\>*B> in
  <eqref|ch07:uniform-stationary-jets> shows that the normalized classical
  symbols, and then their curvature families, satisfy a bound of the form

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:classical-budget>A<rsub|<math-up|out>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|stage>>+<frac|P<rsub|<math-up|stage>><around|(|A|)>|L>.
  </equation>

  The squared remainder costs <math|P<around|(|A|)><rsup|2>/L<rsup|2>> and is
  absorbed into the displayed polynomial. Subtracting the prescribed coarse
  quadratic row and the quartic contribution of the constructed cubic family
  preserves this form: those operations have fixed coarse-scale bounds, and
  the latter operation is linear in that cubic family. The output strip is
  chosen from the Gaussian coarse strip. It need not shrink with each step:
  an incoming coefficient displaced by <math|z> costs at most
  <math|e<rsup|c*\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|>/L>> on that output strip, which is
  absorbed by the fixed incoming width after choosing <math|L> large enough.
  Finally choose an outgoing classical bound larger than
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|stage>>> and then <math|L> so that the second term in
  <eqref|ch07:classical-budget> is smaller than the difference between these
  two bounds. For a finite collection of stage data these choices are
  simultaneous.

  Consequently the cubic and quartic curvature families are constructed for
  this same stationary action with bounds fixed at the stage before <math|L>.
  Their input Hessian is the matched coarse metric. This completes the
  classical matching construction without invoking a later scale schedule or
  a nonlinear local-integral estimate.

  <section|Inherited loops: four differences and alias suppression>

  Taylor degree here counts field factors. It is distinct from scaling
  dimension, which also counts derivatives, as in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch02:jets-section>; a momentum difference therefore
  improves scaling dimension without adding a field
  factor.<label|ch07:inherited-proof>

  Let <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> denote the incoming
  weighted loop sum. We prove the estimate

  <\equation>
    <label|ch07:inherited><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><text| pulled
    back>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|w<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\>C*L<rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>.
  </equation>

  from the transports of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>. Group
  incoming quadratic curvature terms by their relative displacement <math|z>.
  Their coefficient matrix <math|r<around|(|z|)>> has exponential sum bounded
  by <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> and zero zeroth and
  first moments. Thus the fine link symbol

  <\equation*>
    S<around|(|p|)>=d<rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*<wide|r|^><around|(|p|)>*d<rsub|1><around|(|p|)>
  </equation*>

  has order four, satisfies <math|S*d<rsub|0>=d<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*S=0>,
  and has strip bound <math|C*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
  Symmetrization and colour indices are understood; they cost fixed
  constants.

  An analytic periodic function with an order-four zero is a sum of products
  of four coordinate differences <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>> times
  analytic periodic functions on a smaller strip, with linearly bounded
  coefficient norms. To see this quantitatively, write
  <math|f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|0|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*D<rsub|a>*f*<around|(|p<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,p<rsub|a>,0,\<ldots\>,0|)>>
  using the divisions of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch07:curl>, and repeat on the
  quotients, which retain one fewer vanishing order. There are finitely many
  terms. For a quadratic symbol, move two differences to the opposite leg by
  <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=-e<rsup|i*p<rsub|a>>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>>.
  The phases merely translate a coefficient by finitely many lattice sites.
  Each term now has two differences on each of its two field arguments.

  The straight stationary transport <math|W<rsub|s>> has common exponentially
  local kernels with <math|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|s>=O<around|(|L<rsup|-1-r>|)>>
  for <math|r\<leqslant\>2>, by Section<nbsp><reference|ch05:transports>. By
  <eqref|ch05:corrected-transport>, the tangent of the actual block average
  differs from this representative by a fine gradient; the gauge-invariant
  quadratic symbol <math|S> annihilates that difference on both sides.
  Consequently it is legitimate to use <math|W<rsub|s>> in this calculation,
  even if the tree-gauge representative itself has poor difference bounds.
  Each differentiated leg costs <math|C*L<rsup|-3>>. Summing the fine anchor
  in one coarse block costs <math|L<rsup|4>>, so the pulled-back quadratic
  symbol has bound

  <\equation*>
    C*L<rsup|4>*L<rsup|-3>*L<rsup|-3>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=C*L<rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>.
  </equation*>

  Displacing an argument by a fine displacement <math|z> costs at most
  <math|C*e<rsup|\<sigma\><around|\||z|\|>/L>> on a coarse strip of width
  <math|\<sigma\>>. Choose <math|L> after the fine coefficient width <math|w>
  so that <math|\<sigma\>/L\<less\>w>; absolute summability controls these
  displacements. Holomorphy and the common finite-torus representation follow
  from normal convergence. A final fixed strip reduction converts this to the
  weighted coefficient bound required in <eqref|ch07:inherited>.

  The order-four zero on the coarse lattice requires another argument. For
  aliases <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L>, let
  <math|N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>> be the fine-wave amplitude of the transport.
  The coarse symbol is

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|p|)>=L<rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|l>N<rsub|-l>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|<math-ss|T>>*S<around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>*N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|l\<ne\>0>, use <math|K<rsub|f><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>*N<rsub|l>=A<rsub|l>*K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>>,
  the exact stationary transport equation. Project <math|N<rsub|l>>
  orthogonally off the gradient <math|q<around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>>. The positive
  fine curvature metric implies that <math|K<rsub|f><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>+d<rsub|0><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)>*d<rsub|0><around|(|k<rsub|l>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  is invertible near <math|p=0> for this fixed <math|l,L>. As
  <math|K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>, the
  transverse amplitude is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>. The
  Ward identities of <math|S> remove the longitudinal amplitudes, so each
  nonprincipal alias has order four. The principal alias has order four
  because <math|S*<around|(|p/L|)>> does. This argument requires no lower
  inverse bound uniform in <math|L> for an individual nonprincipal alias.

  Finally the nonlinear stationary pullback has the same Hessian as this
  linear pullback: the inherited action has zero first derivative, so its
  chain rule has no term involving the second derivative of the branch. This
  proves the weighted gain and retained moment cancellations for the actual
  inherited action, once the actual and straight stationary tangents have
  been identified up to gauge as above.

  <section|Output coefficients and the remaining analytic
  width><label|ch07:output>

  Suppose the stationary determinant symbol has bound <math|\<Phi\>> at a
  common width, the inherited loop symbol has bound <math|R> and order four,
  and the kinetic symbol has bound <math|B<rsub|H>>. Subtract the extracted
  coefficient <math|\<alpha\>*b<rsub|H>>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch07:unique>
  and the two-leg division produce a quadratic curvature family with bound

  <\equation*>
    K*<around|(|\<Phi\>+<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*B<rsub|H>+R|)>
  </equation*>

  and the zero moments <eqref|ch07:moments>. Together with the prescribed
  rows and the cubic/quartic families of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:classical-match>,
  this defines one common output path action. If the determinant common
  symbol approximates its finite period values with error
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<epsilon\>>, the same coefficient error is
  retained by linear subtraction. For real plane waves with component
  amplitudes at most one, the Hessian error is at most
  <math|2*<around|(|4*dim <gLie>|)><rsup|2><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<epsilon\>>:
  expansion in component waves costs the square of their number, and
  <math|D<rsup|2>=2*C<rsub|2>>.

  All sums should be bounded first at a width
  <math|w<rprime|'>\<gtr\>w\<gtr\>0>. Each fixed differentiation of a
  transported term costs a polynomial in its path length; the factor
  <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|w<rprime|'>-w|)><math-up|length>>> absorbs it. This
  gives the fixed derivative orders needed for local expansion. Parameter
  derivatives require the corresponding derivative bounds on the common input
  kernels. Linear curvature division preserves such bounds because its
  operations and widths are chosen independently of the particular kernel. No
  parameter regularity follows merely from matching the kernels pointwise.

  For a quantitative descent, use the inherited estimate
  <eqref|ch07:inherited>. If the new loop bound is <math|A*L<rsup|4>+B*log
  L+C<rsub|0>>, choose <math|L> so that <math|C*L<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
  then the coupling entry threshold so that

  <\equation*>
    A*L<rsup|4>+B*log L+C<rsub|0>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>2*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>.
  </equation*>

  Their sum fits the outgoing bound. This is the order of choices: widths and
  fixed amplitudes, block ratio, then coupling threshold.

  The comparison bounds and lattice integrand estimates have now been derived
  from the same normalized analytic path, positive metric and actual
  stationary block average. The inherited estimate follows from its
  identified tangent and the common transport bounds. These results are
  one-step statements: their application to an entire selected history
  requires that its incoming paths obey the fixed stage bounds and its
  stationary charts satisfy the identifications of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>. They do not replace the nonlinear
  local-integral bounds or prove the existence of an infinite scale schedule.

  <assign|chapter-nr|7>

  <chapter|Polymer expansions and connected correlations><label|ch08:chapter>

  An effective density is useful only if it remains local after integration.
  Its logarithm should therefore be expressed as a sum of terms supported on
  connected sets, with a bound that does not grow with the surrounding
  volume. The cluster expansion provides this passage from local activities
  to connected terms. Its convergence is a standard result of statistical
  mechanics. The work specific to the gauge theory is to put the activities
  produced by the block integration within its hypotheses.

  This chapter states the abstract results in the form needed for that
  application. It does not prove another cluster-expansion theorem. We use
  Kotecký\UPreiss convergence<cite|KoteckyPreiss1986>, in the explicit rooted
  form of Ueltschi<cite-detail|Ueltschi2004|Theorem<nbsp>1>, and explain how
  support weights, activity derivatives and large-field constraints enter.
  The distinction between a general cluster estimate and a bound on the
  actual gauge-theory activities will remain explicit throughout.

  <section|Activities, supports and incompatibility><label|ch08:activities>

  Let <math|\<Lambda\>> be a finite set of lattice sites. A <em|polymer
  label> <math|\<gamma\>> belongs to a finite or countable set
  <math|\<cal-P\>>, has a nonempty support
  <math|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>\<subset\>\<Lambda\>>, and carries a complex number
  <math|z<rsub|\<gamma\>>>, called its <em|activity>. Different labels may
  have the same support. The labels can retain information about an
  integration sector, derivative or local Taylor coefficient; they are not
  identified solely by their supports.

  A symmetric, reflexive relation <math|\<gamma\>\<nsim\>\<eta\>> specifies
  <em|incompatibility>. Reflexivity means that a compatible collection
  contains a label at most once. We assume

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:overlap>\<gamma\>\<nsim\>\<eta\><space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>X<rsub|\<gamma\>>\<cap\>X<rsub|\<eta\>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>.
  </equation>

  For the hard-core gas the implication is an equivalence. Some estimates
  below need only the displayed implication; the formula for deleting all
  polymers meeting a set will be stated for the hard-core case.

  The polymer partition function is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:gas>Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A\<subset\>\<cal-P\><text|
    finite>>>|<row|<cell|A<text| pairwise
    compatible>>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|\<gamma\>\<in\>A>z<rsub|\<gamma\>>.
  </equation>

  The empty collection contributes one. This sum is a polynomial for finitely
  many labels. For countably many labels it is well defined whenever
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  since its absolute sum is at most <math|exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>|)>>.
  The cluster criterion below implies this summability in finite volume.
  Complex or signed activities are allowed; they need not be probability
  weights.

  We keep track of spatial size with a nonnegative function
  <math|c<around|(|X|)>> on nonempty subsets of <math|\<Lambda\>>. The needed
  properties are

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:cover-properties><around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\>h*<space|0.17em>c<around|(|X|)>,<space|2em>c*<around|(|X\<cup\>Y|)>\<leqslant\>c<around|(|X|)>+c<around|(|Y|)><space|1em><text|if
    >X\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>,
  </equation>

  where <math|h\<gtr\>0> is fixed. One choice is the number of sites, with
  <math|h=1>. A useful multiscale choice is the minimum number of
  radius-<math|B> lattice balls whose union is connected and contains
  <math|X>. In that case <math|h=<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>> is an upper
  bound on the size of a ball, including on a torus. The symbol <math|c> will
  always denote this support-size function in this chapter, not a field
  coefficient.

  For <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>0> define the site norm

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:site-norm><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<alpha\>>=sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<Lambda\>>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>:x\<in\>X<rsub|\<gamma\>>><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>>.
  </equation>

  It counts every polymer at each site in its support. A norm which counts
  only the chosen anchor of a polymer is different. Passing from an anchored
  bound to <eqref|ch08:site-norm> requires a support-size estimate or a loss
  in the exponential weight. This distinction matters when checking a cluster
  criterion for an effective density.

  <section|The logarithm and its convergence criterion><label|ch08:criterion>

  For a tuple <math|\<b-gamma\>=<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<gamma\><rsub|n>|)>>,
  form the graph on the <em|occurrences> <math|1,\<ldots\>,n>, joining
  distinct vertices whenever their labels are incompatible. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:ursell>\<varphi\><rsup|T><around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<text|
    connected spanning graph>>>|<row|<cell|H<text| uses incompatibility
    edges>>>>>>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||E<around|(|H|)>|\|>>.
  </equation>

  For <math|n=1> its value is one; for a disconnected incompatibility graph
  it is zero. Repeated labels are allowed in this tuple, even though the gas
  <eqref|ch08:gas> contains each label at most once. They are necessary in
  its logarithm: for a gas with one label, <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>>=1+z>
  and <math|log Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>>=z-z<rsup|2>/2+\<cdots\>>. The <math|n>!
  below compensates for ordering the occurrences; it is not part of the
  definition <eqref|ch08:ursell>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Standard polymer convergence criterion><label|ch08:kp>Suppose
    <math|A<rsub|\<gamma\>>\<geqslant\>0> and

    <\equation>
      <label|ch08:kp-condition><big|sum><rsub|\<eta\>:\<eta\>\<nsim\>\<gamma\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<eta\>>|\|>*e<rsup|A<rsub|\<eta\>>>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|\<gamma\>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>*e<rsup|A<rsub|\<gamma\>>>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation>

    Then the cluster series

    <\equation>
      <label|ch08:log>\<cal-L\><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|1|n!>*<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<gamma\><rsub|n>>\<varphi\><rsup|T><around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>z<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    converges absolutely and <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>><around|(|z|)>=e<rsup|\<cal-L\><around|(|z|)>>>.
    For a fixed label <math|\<gamma\><rsub|1>>, its absolute rooted series
    satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|ch08:root><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|1|<around|(|n-1|)>!>*<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,\<gamma\><rsub|n>><around|\||\<varphi\><rsup|T><around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|i=2><rsup|n><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|i>>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|A<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|1>>>.
    </equation>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This is the hard-core specialization of the cited convergence theorem:
    the pair interaction is <math|-1> on incompatible pairs and zero
    otherwise. There is no need to assume
    <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>>\<gtr\>0> before taking the logarithm. The
    series defines the branch continued from zero activity. For real
    activities it is real, and the exponential identity then gives
    <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>>\<gtr\>0>. For complex activities the
    conclusion is nonvanishing.
  </proof>

  The connected graph sum must be taken before its absolute value. Bounding
  the sum of the absolute values of all connected graphs would discard the
  cancellation on which convergence depends. The standard tree-graph
  inequality is one way to establish <eqref|ch08:root>; we use the result
  rather than repeat its combinatorial proof.

  <section|A spatially local bound with a geometric tail><label|ch08:tail>

  The site norm from <eqref|ch08:site-norm> gives a particularly transparent
  criterion. Suppose that, for some <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:reserve><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<alpha\>+\<delta\>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|0>,<space|2em>0\<less\>q\<less\>1,<space|2em>h*\<gamma\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>q*\<delta\>.
  </equation>

  The parameter <math|\<delta\>> is the additional exponential support decay;
  it will be spent to sum polymers that meet one another. The parameter
  <math|q> measures smallness and controls the number of occurrences in a
  cluster.

  Write <math|X<around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i>X<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|i>>>.
  A direct weighted application of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch08:kp> yields,
  for every site <math|x> and integer <math|k\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:weighted-tail><big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>k><frac|1|n!>*<big|sum><rsub|\<b-gamma\>:x\<in\>X<around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>><around|\||\<varphi\><rsup|T><around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)><around|\||<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>|\|>*z<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|i>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<around|(|X<around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|0>*q<rsup|k-1>.
  </equation>

  This form is slightly stronger than the bound with an additional
  <math|<around|(|1-q|)><rsup|-1>> obtained by separately summing estimates
  at each occurrence number. Either form is sufficient for the applications.

  Here is the hypothesis matching for this standard adaptation. Apply the
  rooted theorem to the inflated activities
  <math|<wide|z|~><rsub|\<gamma\>>=q<rsup|-1><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<alpha\>*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>>>
  and take <math|A<rsub|\<gamma\>>=\<delta\>*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>>.
  By <eqref|ch08:overlap> and <eqref|ch08:cover-properties>, the left side of
  <eqref|ch08:kp-condition> is bounded by
  <math|q<rsup|-1><around|\||X<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>*\<gamma\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>>.
  For a nonzero connected coefficient the union weight is at most the product
  of the support weights. Designating one occurrence whose support contains
  <math|x> changes <math|1/n>! to <math|1/<around|(|n-1|)>>! and gives an
  upper bound; the rooted sum is at most <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>/q>.
  Restoring the factor <math|q<rsup|n>> on terms with <math|n\<geqslant\>k>
  gives <eqref|ch08:weighted-tail>. Nonempty supports and finite
  <math|\<Lambda\>> verify the total summability required by the theorem.

  More generally, let <math|\<cal-Q\>\<subset\>\<cal-P\>> be a distinguished
  set of labels with

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>\<in\>\<cal-Q\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|\|>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<delta\>|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|\<cal-Q\>>.
  </equation*>

  Replacing the condition <math|x\<in\>X<around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>> by \Pat
  least one occurrence belongs to <math|\<cal-Q\>>\Q in
  <eqref|ch08:weighted-tail> gives <math|b<rsub|\<cal-Q\>>*q<rsup|k-1>> on
  the right. This is useful when a cluster has to meet a prescribed
  insertion. An insertion selected in this way is a distinguished occurrence
  in the combinatorial expansion, not yet a renormalized quantum field.

  The support norm is independent of <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>.
  Summing the <math|k=1> estimate over <math|x> bounds
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-L\><around|(|z|)>|\|>> by
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<gamma\><rsub|0>>. Thus extensive
  free-energy bounds coexist with volume-independent estimates on connected
  terms meeting a fixed site. It is the latter estimates that preserve
  locality in a renormalization step.

  <section|Deleting polymers and controlling local
  changes><label|ch08:deletion>

  Recall that <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>>> is the gas partition function
  <eqref|ch08:gas>, and <math|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>> is the full support of label
  <math|\<gamma\>>. For a set <math|Y\<subset\>\<Lambda\>>, delete all labels
  whose supports meet <math|Y>: put <math|z<rsup|Y><rsub|\<gamma\>>=z<rsub|\<gamma\>>>
  if <math|X<rsub|\<gamma\>>\<cap\>Y=\<varnothing\>> and zero otherwise.
  Under <eqref|ch08:reserve>, both cluster series converge and

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:deletion-identity><frac|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>><around|(|z<rsup|Y>|)>|Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>><around|(|z|)>>=e<rsup|-\<cal-L\><rsub|Y><around|(|z|)>>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\><rsub|Y><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|1|n!>*<big|sum><rsub|X<around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>\<cap\>Y\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>\<varphi\><rsup|T><around|(|\<b-gamma\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>z<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|i>>.
  </equation>

  This is simply subtraction of two absolutely convergent logarithms. Its
  local bound is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:deletion-bound><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|Y><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\><rsub|0>,
  </equation>

  and clusters with at least <math|k> occurrences contribute at most
  <math|<around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\><rsub|0>*q<rsup|k-1>>. Counting a cluster
  more than once when it meets several sites of <math|Y> only increases the
  upper bound.

  The same mechanism controls changes of activities in a bounded region. If
  two families of activities agree away from <math|Y>, their logarithmic
  cluster terms cancel unless a cluster contains a changed activity. A
  quantitative Lipschitz estimate additionally needs a bound on the
  difference of those activities, or a uniformly admissible interpolation
  between them. Agreement of supports alone gives locality of the difference,
  not its smallness.

  <section|Parameters, derivatives and connected
  insertions><label|ch08:parameters>

  Suppose <math|z<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|u|)>> is holomorphic in a complex
  parameter <math|u> on a disc and that <eqref|ch08:reserve> holds uniformly
  on each of its compact subdiscs. The cluster series then converges normally
  there. Consequently its sum is holomorphic and can be differentiated term
  by term. If the bound for a local cluster sum is <math|M> on
  <math|<around|\||u-u<rsub|0>|\|>\<less\>\<rho\>>, Cauchy's estimate gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:cauchy><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|u><rsup|r>*\<cal-L\><rsub|Y><around|(|u<rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r!\<rho\><rsup|-r>*M.
  </equation>

  There is no separate combinatorial proof for each derivative order. Several
  parameters are treated on a polydisc, with the product of the corresponding
  Cauchy factors. Only derivatives of fixed finite order are asserted unless
  additional uniform estimates are supplied.

  One can introduce formal sources for bounded quantities <math|F<rsub|j>> by
  the finite-volume generating function

  <\equation*>
    Z<around|(|\<b-t\>|)>=Z<around|(|0|)>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\>*exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|r>t<rsub|j>*F<rsub|j>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The mixed derivative of <math|log Z> at zero is the connected correlation
  (or cumulant) of the <math|F<rsub|j>>. For example,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|1>>*\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|2>>*log
    Z<around|(|\<b-t\>|)>|\|><rsub|\<b-t\>=0>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|F<rsub|1>*F<rsub|2>|)>-\<bbb-E\><around|(|F<rsub|1>|)>*\<bbb-E\><around|(|F<rsub|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The cluster representation expresses such derivatives through clusters
  carrying the distinguished source occurrences; see
  <cite-detail|Ueltschi2004|Ÿ3>. To use this representation in the gauge
  theory, the source-deformed activities must satisfy a uniform criterion on
  an actual complex neighbourhood of zero. Boundedness of the undeformed gas
  does not imply that estimate.

  There is a second way to insert observables: differentiate the unnormalized
  density and expand the result directly. This is preferable if some activity
  can vanish, because division by that activity is then meaningless. Bounds
  are assigned to the inserted activity itself. The usual mnemonic
  \Pdifferentiate the logarithm of each activity\Q is not a valid definition
  in that situation.

  <section|Large-field constraints and exact reassembly><label|ch08:forced>

  An effective gauge density includes regions where a small-field expansion
  is not available. Fix a configuration of the coarse variables for the
  moment, and let <math|D\<subset\>\<Lambda\>> be the set of sites declared
  to be in the large-field region. In addition to the regular activities
  <math|z<rsub|\<gamma\>>>, introduce large-field activities
  <math|K<rsub|\<eta\>>> with supports <math|Y<rsub|\<eta\>>> and nonempty
  sets <math|I<rsub|\<eta\>>\<subset\>D\<cap\>Y<rsub|\<eta\>>>. The set
  <math|I<rsub|\<eta\>>> records the large-field sites assigned to that
  activity. Let <math|\<frak-C\><around|(|D|)>> consist of the allowed finite
  families <math|B> of such labels with disjoint supports and

  <\equation*>
    <big|cup><rsub|\<eta\>\<in\>B>I<rsub|\<eta\>>=D.
  </equation*>

  The exact rules for eligibility are part of the definition of an effective
  density; they may exclude some otherwise possible covers. When <math|D> is
  empty, the empty family is permitted.

  For the simple overlap incompatibility rule, the constrained partition sum
  has the form

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:constrained>Z<rsub|D>=<big|sum><rsub|B\<in\>\<frak-C\><around|(|D|)>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|\<eta\>\<in\>B>K<rsub|\<eta\>>|)>*Z<rsub|<math-up|pol>><around|(|z<rsup|Y<around|(|B|)>>|)>,<space|2em>Y<around|(|B|)>=<big|cup><rsub|\<eta\>\<in\>B>Y<rsub|\<eta\>>.
  </equation>

  Applying <eqref|ch08:deletion-identity> gives the exact factorization

  <\equation>
    <label|ch08:reassembly>Z<rsub|D>=e<rsup|\<cal-L\><around|(|z|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|B\<in\>\<frak-C\><around|(|D|)>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|\<eta\>\<in\>B>K<rsub|\<eta\>>|)>*e<rsup|-\<cal-L\><rsub|Y<around|(|B|)>><around|(|z|)>>.
  </equation>

  For finite sums this is algebra; the same identity holds for absolutely
  convergent sums. It is the basic reason that removing regular polymers near
  a large-field region can be absorbed into modified activities.

  The expression <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|Y<around|(|B|)>>> need not split as a
  sum over individual members of <math|B>. A connected regular cluster can
  meet two large-field supports. Such a cluster couples them, and the
  modified activity belongs on their union together with the cluster support.
  Grouping by connected components is the appropriate reorganization. An
  arbitrary product of independently corrected large-field activities would
  omit these terms.

  Formula <eqref|ch08:reassembly> does not by itself bound the sum over
  <math|B>. That estimate requires suppression of large-field activities by
  the action, bounds on the number and geometry of eligible supports, and
  enough additional exponential support decay to absorb the regular clusters
  attached to them. These are analytic inputs to the nonlinear
  renormalization step. The abstract theorem bounds the regular cluster sum
  once those inputs have been established; it does not establish them.

  Two support notions must be distinguished in this application. The set used
  in an exclusion or covering rule need not contain every link read by an
  activity. For example, a large-field condition at a boundary site can
  inspect a plaquette extending outside its assigned covering set. Disjoint
  covering sets therefore need not imply disjoint dependence on links. The
  abstract gas above needs the overlap implication <eqref|ch08:overlap> for
  its chosen exclusion supports; it does not assume statistical independence.
  When a local integration or factorization argument also requires full
  dependence supports, those enlarged sets and their geometric estimates must
  be specified separately. One must not silently replace the covering sets by
  enlarged sets in an exact covering identity.

  <section|What an application must supply><label|ch08:application>

  The reusable conclusions of this chapter are the connected expansion
  <eqref|ch08:log>, the local tail <eqref|ch08:weighted-tail>, parameter
  differentiation under a uniform complex-domain bound, and exact removal and
  reassembly formulas. They are statements about a specified family of
  activities. To apply them after a block integration one must give:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>an exact expansion of the integrated density, including the scalar
    factor and every large-field sector;

    <item>supports containing every link and cutoff condition on which an
    activity depends, and a definite incompatibility relation;

    <item>a site-norm bound with a strictly positive support-weight reserve,
    satisfying <eqref|ch08:reserve> uniformly in the period;

    <item>corresponding estimates for the source-deformed or inserted
    activities whenever a connected observable is required;

    <item>an absolutely summable bound for the constrained large-field
    families and the clusters attached to them.
  </enumerate>

  These requirements explain why the cluster expansion is not the main
  obstruction in the construction. The general summation mechanism is already
  available in the literature. What must be proved for the Wilson model is
  that exact blocking, stationary integration, the large-field decomposition
  and the chosen observable normalization produce activities obeying those
  requirements on the same trajectory.

  <assign|chapter-nr|8>

  <chapter|The nonlinear renormalization step><label|ch09:renormalization>

  A block transformation acts on the density, not on its chosen list of local
  terms. Our task is to express its exact output in the coordinates of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>, while improving the bounds enough
  to repeat the operation. The organization by local interactions and
  large-field regions follows the constructive renormalization method of
  Baªaban <cite|Balaban1988Expansions>. We use the cluster theorem of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch08:chapter>; the work here is the identification,
  normalization and estimation of its concrete activities.

  Fix one adjacent pair of scales, with fine scale <math|m+1> and coarse
  scale <math|m>. Write <math|L=L<rsub|m>>, <math|B=B<rsub|m>>,
  <math|t=t<rsub|m>>, and <math|\<frak-b\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>. The
  actual incoming inverse coupling is <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>\<in\><around|[|\<frak-b\>/2,2*\<frak-b\>|]>>.
  All event indicators use the fixed reference thresholds of
  <eqref|ch04:thresholds>. They do not move when <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>
  varies. Write the prescribed coarse path action as

  <\equation*>
    P<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>=\<beta\>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>+\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<cal-K\>> includes its cubic and quartic terms and
  <math|\<cal-L\>> is the quadratic loop correction. This identity defines
  <math|\<cal-K\>,\<cal-L\>> from <eqref|ch04:path-action>. Neither is the
  unknown effective action.

  For this induction the derivative orders and spatial exponents are stronger
  than the minimum allowed in the density definition: take
  <math|q<rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>q<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>6> and
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>\<varpi\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>2>, with
  the prescribed derivative reserves for the local integral estimates. In
  particular the normalized affine directions used below belong to the
  activity seminorm. At the finest scale choose
  <math|0\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<leqslant\>1>.

  The estimates below distinguish proved algebraic implications from the
  analytic inputs of Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>
  and<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. In particular a product estimate under
  one Gaussian law, a local low-order matching estimate, and a one-loop drift
  estimate are different inputs.

  <section|The exact local integral and its first
  expansion><label|ch09:integral>

  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>>=\<cal-B\><rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\><rprime|'>><math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\><rprime|'>|)>>,
  with the exact kernel <eqref|ch04:block-operator>. In each sector one first
  performs the local coordinate change on its active links and leaves the
  other links in probability Haar measure.
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> gives the exact Gaussian
  identity, including the difference between the chosen finite-range
  covariance and the inverse precision.

  Here is the scalar bookkeeping in a form useful for checking reassembly.
  Suppose there are <math|N<rsub|<math-up|free>>> free tree-gauge bonds, of
  which <math|n<rsub|<math-up|act>>> use Lie algebra coordinates, and let
  <math|D> be the dimension after dummy Gaussian coordinates have been added.
  Set <math|g=<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|-1/2>>, <math|q=dim
  <gLie>>, and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|H>>> equal to the density of probability
  Haar measure in the chosen exponential coordinates at zero. With
  <math|a=\<mu\>+g*z>, the measure multiplier before the Haar logarithm is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-<around|(|D-n<rsub|<math-up|act>>*q|)>/2>*g<rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|act>>*q>*c<rsub|<math-up|H>><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|act>>>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-D/2>*\<frak-m\><rsup|N<rsub|<math-up|free>>>*e<rsup|-<around|(|N<rsub|<math-up|free>>-n<rsub|<math-up|act>>|)>*log
    \<frak-m\>>,<space|1em>\<frak-m\>=c<rsub|<math-up|H>>*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*g<rsup|2>|)><rsup|q/2>.
  </equation*>

  The dummy Gaussian integral equals one after the inactive Haar variables
  have been frozen; Fubini then permits their integration. The flat reference
  determinant uses the same forest as the sector. It is extracted once, not
  separately for every eventual connected component. The local precision
  correction, Haar logarithm, missing-coordinate factor and stationary
  residual remain among the local exponential factors. Keeping them is what
  recovers the original Haar integral.

  Expand exponentials as <math|e<rsup|V>=1+<around|(|e<rsup|V>-1|)>> and
  retain all sector allocation indicators. A complete dependence region
  contains every Haar coordinate, tag variable and Gaussian covariance halo
  read by its factor. If two such supports are disjoint, the Haar and tag
  coordinates are independent; the Gaussian cross covariance is zero because
  the chosen covariance has finite range. Their integrals therefore factor.
  Connected components of intersecting dependence regions define the first
  polymer activities.

  This factorization concerns dependence regions. It does not assert that
  large-field activities with disjoint exclusion supports <math|Y<rsub|i>>
  have disjoint full dependence regions. The large-field indicators in their
  eligibility criteria can read outside <math|Y<rsub|i>>. Those conditions
  remain in every exact-cover expression.

  The required simultaneous estimate for a connected product is

  <\equation>
    <big|int>G<rsub|M>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A<rsub|M>|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>M>u<rsub|a>.<label|ch09:joint-input>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<nu\>> is the one sector Gaussian/Haar law, <math|G<rsub|M>>
  majorizes the product and its prescribed ordered derivatives,
  <math|s<rsub|M>> counts the full dependence region in radius-<math|B>
  blocks, and <math|A<rsub|M>> is its selected large-field condition set. The
  function <math|P<rsub|j>> has subpower growth: <math|log
  P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>/log \<frak-b\>\<to\>0> at fixed stage and
  ratio. Distinct cutoff-failure labels each contribute their own factor to
  the product. The bounded-jet estimate <eqref|ch06:actual-jet-integral>,
  applied to the primitive factors constructed in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>, proves this joint bound on
  their common law. The alternative quadratic-tilt criterion in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:common-majorant> is not required here.
  Separate bounds on <math|<big|int><around|\||F<rsub|a>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>>
  cannot replace this estimate.

  The elementary power counting is as follows. The entries include the
  prescribed finite number of derivatives.

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|factor
    after deterministic subtraction>>|<cell|<text|majorant>>>|<row|<cell|<text|stationary
    cubic remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'>*g<rsup|3>>>|<row|<cell|<text|centered
    determinant variation>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>>>|<row|<cell|<text|Haar
    remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)><rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited
    quadratic-loop variation>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>*g>>|<row|<cell|<text|covariance
    and stationary residual>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|c<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'><rsub|j>*B>>>|<row|<cell|<text|incoming
    regular activity>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|cutoff
    failure at a distinct label>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>>>>
  </equation*>

  For example <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>*g<rsup|3>=g>, and the stationary mean
  is <math|O<around|(|g*M<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)>>, so a cubic
  Taylor term is bounded by <math|g> times a polynomial in
  <math|M<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> and Gaussian coordinates. The latter
  is estimated inside the common integral. The other rows follow from the
  determinant inverse identity, the second-order vanishing of the Haar
  correction, and the window errors of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>.
  The loop coefficient bound must grow subpolynomially at fixed ratio.

  After increasing the inverse coupling threshold these rows are bounded by
  <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>, since a subpower factor can be bounded by
  <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|1/16>>. Read-count polynomials are absorbed by
  additional exponential support decay. The first component expansion and
  then Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch08:kp> give an absolutely convergent
  logarithm of the ordinary gas without large-field sites. Components
  attached to a prescribed large-field site set remain in the exact-cover
  sum. The deletion identity <eqref|ch08:deletion-identity> accounts for
  ordinary polymers excluded by a selected large-field region. Repeated
  polymer labels in the logarithm are counted at every occurrence, with the
  factor <math|1/n>! of <eqref|ch08:log>.

  All rearrangements can first be made for finite selections. Under
  <eqref|ch09:joint-input> and the strict cluster criterion, the absolute
  envelopes allow passage to the countable sums. Thus this procedure gives an
  identity for <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>>>, rather
  than an asymptotic expansion for its logarithm.

  <section|Improving the regular remainder><label|ch09:improvement>

  The first bound <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>> is too large for the target
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>>.
  There are three different sources of improvement.

  <subsection|Terms containing several elementary factors>

  Give every elementary ordinary factor a degree one, and give a
  first-expansion polymer the sum of its factors' degrees. A cluster degree
  is the sum over its polymer occurrences. Multiply each elementary majorant
  by <math|h=\<frak-b\><rsup|3/8>> before performing either expansion. The
  resulting local smallness parameter is <math|h*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/16>>.
  The weighted rooted bound of Section<nbsp><reference|ch08:tail>, applied
  with these inflated activities, bounds the whole sum by
  <math|P<rsub|j>*h*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>. Restoring the original weights
  for terms of total degree at least three gives

  <\equation>
    P<rsub|j>*h<rsup|-3>*<around|(|h*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>|)>=P<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-19/16>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>.<label|ch09:degree-gain>
  </equation>

  The gap <math|19/16-23/20=3/80> absorbs the remaining subpower factor. This
  counts elementary factors, not just vertices in the second cluster
  expansion; a single polymer may already have degree three. The same
  calculation applies after placing a fixed number of derivatives.

  An incoming regular activity accompanied by a further factor is bounded by
  the rooted estimate with that incoming activity distinguished:
  <math|P<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>.
  Long path terms have an extra factor <math|e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*B>>. Both fit
  the right side of <eqref|ch09:degree-gain> after increasing the threshold.

  <subsection|The linear inherited regular term>

  The component linear in an incoming regular activity must use its
  affine-Hessian cancellation. In local radial gauge, the lifted potential
  splits into parts with inverse-ratio sizes
  <math|L<rsup|-1>,L<rsup|-2>,L<rsup|-3>>: a constant part, its affine
  variation, and its remainder. The constant part is removed by a local gauge
  transformation. Curvature therefore begins at weight two. Gauge and color
  invariance remove linear curvature terms, and the incoming affine-Hessian
  condition removes the quadratic curvature term of weight four. Every
  surviving selection in the ordered Taylor expansion has weight at least
  five.

  Here is the support estimate needed to sum that local calculation. Let
  <math|d> be the incoming covering number. For <math|d<rsup|3>\<less\>L> the
  inspected support admits a nonwrapping radial gauge and the per-label gain
  is <math|L<rsup|-5>>. For the other supports use the original bound. Both
  cases are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|L><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-5>*<around|(|1+d|)><rsup|15>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|L><around|(|d|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsup|-5>,>|<cell|d<rsup|3>\<less\>L,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|d<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The full chain rule adds a fixed polynomial in <math|d>. Choose the scale
  ratio and incoming support exponent so that, after support compression, the
  outgoing weight uses at most half of the incoming exponential decay. The
  remaining decay absorbs the polynomial in <math|d>. There are
  <math|L<rsup|4>> fine anchors above each coarse anchor. Consequently the
  inherited principal norm is at most <math|V<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-1>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,
  with <math|V<rsub|j>> fixed before <math|L>.

  For the comparison with the actual integral use the same forest and
  physical local coordinates on both sides. The estimates of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch06:linear-comparison> give a covariance
  difference <math|O<around|(|P<around|(|B|)>*t<rsub|f>|)>> and a mean
  difference, in Gaussian units, of order
  <math|P<around|(|B|)>*g<around|(|M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|2>+P<around|(|B|)>*M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>*e<rsup|-c*B>>,
  where <math|t<rsub|f>=t<rsub|m+1>> and <math|g=<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>|)><rsup|-1/2>>.
  Each is <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>. To see where the gain
  occurs, for a covariance interpolation <math|C<rsub|s>> with fixed positive
  lower bound, the logarithmic density derivative is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2>*<around*|{|z<rsup|\<ast\>>*C<rsub|s><rsup|-1>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*C<rsub|s><rsup|-1>*z-<tr><around|(|C<rsub|s><rsup|-1>*<wide|C|\<dot\>><rsub|s>|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  A varying mean adds its linear Gaussian score. Every coefficient contains a
  covariance or normalized-mean difference. Its fixed moments therefore
  contain the small factor just stated, times a polynomial in the local
  dimension.

  Hold the physical links in the incoming activity fixed by the protected
  coordinate change of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>. The
  nonlinear coordinate correction is then differentiated through its Jacobian
  and density, where the same gain is available. This avoids requiring one
  extra derivative of the incoming activity when adjacent levels have equal
  derivative orders. Telescope all remaining factor products, marking one
  changed factor. The nonquadratic local weights contribute a gain of order
  <math|g> with subpower losses; a second incoming activity contributes
  <math|O<around|(|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|)><rsup|2>|)>>
  instead. Common Gaussian moments and the unused support exponent sum the
  resulting polynomial costs. Together with the principal estimate this
  proves

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|\<sigma\>+3><around|(|<text|inherited
    term>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|V<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-1>+\<omega\><rsub|j,L><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|j,L><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>.
  </equation*>

  For this comparison the local windows and their dependence halos must be
  clean. A window meeting a selected large-field component is assigned to
  that component; a failed remote cutoff has the joint Gaussian-tail bound of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>. Large remaining supports gain
  <math|e<rsup|-c*B>> from the unused incoming support weight. Thus no
  comparison between unrelated event indicators or a seeded and an unseeded
  law has been made. The actual interpolation estimates are essential; the
  scaling-dimension count alone would not prove the comparison remainder.

  <subsection|Low-order terms and localization>

  One- and two-factor terms must be combined before estimating them. The
  degree-four classical matching and quadratic determinant matching must
  apply to the same integral and stationary branch. Rather than use a formal
  cumulant remainder, we retain a finite stationary region and estimate its
  complement. The resulting centered auxiliary bound, with
  <math|h=\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>, is

  <\equation>
    P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around*|{|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1>*h+\<frak-b\>*h<rsup|5>+h<rsup|3>|}>+\<frak-b\><rsup|-K>,<label|ch09:aux-input>
  </equation>

  where <math|K> can be any fixed positive number. Its largest power is
  <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-19/16+o<around|(|1|)>>>, which is smaller than
  <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>>. The argument below specifies the actual
  sector and stationary estimates needed to obtain this bound.

  <subsection|The stationary integral with its cutoff
  retained><label|ch09:stationary-remainder>

  We now give the local analytic implication behind the auxiliary bound. It
  is an application of Laplace's method, for which we refer to
  <cite-detail|Wong2001|Chapter II>, with three features that must be checked
  here: the integration dimension grows with the auxiliary period, the
  stationary Hessian depends on the coarse field, and the integration cutoff
  cannot be discarded before estimating its complement. All actions and
  matrices in the following argument belong to the same pure small-field
  block integral: incoming activities are omitted, the prescribed path and
  block kernel are unchanged, and the fine-field indicator
  <math|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|f><text|for
  every fine >p|}>>> is retained, where <math|t<rsub|f>=t<rsub|m+1>>. Thus
  its integrand is zero on the fine large-field complement. The other fine
  sectors have already been assigned to the large-field expansion.

  Put <math|z=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>,
  <math|g=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsup|-1/2>> and <math|h=z<rsup|-7/16>>. The
  letter <math|z> is a scalar inverse coupling parameter in this subsection;
  the Gaussian integration variable is <math|\<xi\>>. Auxiliary periods and
  coordinate counts are bounded by fixed powers of <math|1+log z>. Write
  <math|M=M<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> for the cutoff amplitude in
  <eqref|ch04:thresholds>, and take <math|D=d<rsub|0>*M> with a fixed small
  <math|d<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>. We shall use the following two distinct facts:

  <\equation*>
    M=z<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em><around|(|1+log
    z|)><rsup|k>=o<around|(|M<rsup|\<epsilon\>>|)><space|1em><text|for every
    fixed >k,\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Both follow directly by taking logarithms of <math|M=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log z|)><rsup|2>|)>>.

  Let <math|x> be the coarse logarithmic coordinates, with their supremum
  norm, and <math|y\<in\><RR><rsup|n>> the free fine coordinates after tree
  gauge fixing. The actual classical action including the block penalty is
  <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>>; write <math|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> for its
  stationary branch and <math|A<around|(|x|)>=D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>>
  for its positive Hessian. The extra exponent
  <math|\<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>> includes the fine loop term and minus the
  logarithm of the product Haar-chart Jacobian. Suppose the branch, action,
  extra exponent and relative logarithmic determinant have the fixed
  derivative orders required below on a common neighborhood, with bounds
  <math|z<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. The Hessian gap is uniform in the auxiliary
  volume at the fixed block ratio. These are the stationary and path
  estimates of Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>\U<reference|ch07:matching>;
  no different flat covariance is substituted here.

  Set <math|s<around|(|x|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log det A<around|(|x|)>> and define
  the smooth divided difference

  <\equation*>
    V*<around|(|g,x,\<xi\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|(|1-t|)>*D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+t*g*\<xi\>|)>*<around|[|\<xi\>,\<xi\>|]>*<space|0.17em>d*t.
  </equation*>

  Stationarity gives <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+g*\<xi\>|)>-\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>=g<rsup|2>*V*<around|(|g,x,\<xi\>|)>>,
  including the limiting value <math|V*<around|(|0,x,\<xi\>|)>=<tfrac|1|2>\<xi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*A<around|(|x|)>*\<xi\>>.
  With <math|\<gamma\><rsub|0>=N*<around|(|0,A<around|(|0|)><rsup|-1>|)>>,
  put

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a*<around|(|g,x,\<xi\>|)>>|<cell|=s<around|(|x|)>-s<around|(|0|)>+V*<around|(|0,0,\<xi\>|)>-V*<around|(|g,x,\<xi\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>-\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+g*\<xi\>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|R*<around|(|g,x|)>>|<cell|=<big|int><rsub|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>D>e<rsup|a*<around|(|g,x,\<xi\>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|0><around|(|\<xi\>|)>,<space|2em>L*<around|(|g,x|)>=log
    R*<around|(|g,x|)>.<eq-number><label|ch09:truncated-integral>>>>>
  </align>

  The determinant factor is essential: at <math|g=0> the exact identity is

  <\equation*>
    R*<around|(|0,x|)>=N*<around|(|0,A<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>|)>*<around|{|<space|0.17em><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<space|0.17em>|}>,
  </equation*>

  which is a truncated mass, not identically one. Changing <math|\<xi\>> to
  <math|-\<xi\>> also gives the exact parity
  <math|R*<around|(|-g,x|)>=R*<around|(|g,x|)>>.

  Here are the uniform bounds needed for this elementary application of
  Laplace's method. Differentiating the divided difference on
  <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>D> costs a fixed polynomial in
  <math|D> and the stationary derivative bounds. It is therefore
  <math|z<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> at every fixed order. The exponent vanishes
  at <math|<around|(|g,x|)>=<around|(|0,0|)>>, so on
  <math|<around|\||g|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>h>
  it is at most one in absolute value for large <math|z>. If the reference
  truncated mass is at least <math|1/2>, then
  <math|e<rsup|-1>/2\<leqslant\>R\<leqslant\>e>, and the same subpower
  derivative bounds hold for <math|L>. To obtain derivatives through order
  <math|r> and the two derivatives in <math|g> used for parity, it suffices
  to retain derivatives of <math|\<phi\>,\<rho\>> through order <math|r+4>
  and of the other terms through order <math|r+2>. These are finite-stage
  requirements.

  If <math|A<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>c*I> and <math|n> is at most a
  fixed polynomial in <math|log z>, a coordinate Gaussian tail bound gives

  <\equation*>
    1-R*<around|(|0,x|)>\<leqslant\>2*n*exp
    <around|{|-D<rsup|2>/<around|(|2*c*n|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  This is smaller than every fixed inverse power of <math|z>. The same
  conclusion for its fixed derivatives follows either by the Gaussian score
  formula or by finite-order interpolation on a ball of radius
  <math|z<rsup|-1>> inside the parameter domain. We recall the needed
  interpolation fact: a superpolynomially small value and polynomially
  bounded derivatives through order <math|r+1> imply superpolynomially small
  derivatives through order <math|r> on a smaller ball. It follows
  successively from Taylor's estimate <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*F|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*sup
  <around|\||F|\|>/u+u*sup <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*F|\<\|\|\>>> on a
  radius-<math|u> ball, applied to successive derivatives with shrinking
  radii. No coordinate-dimension factor is needed when operator norms are
  used. Parity and Taylor's theorem in <math|g> now yield, for every fixed
  <math|K\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|x><rsup|r>*L*<around|(|g,x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r><around|(|z|)>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsup|-1>+z<rsup|-K>,<space|2em>C<rsub|r><around|(|z|)>=z<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>.<label|ch09:even-gain>
  </equation>

  This includes <math|r=0>; the cutoff error at <math|g=0> has been retained
  throughout the argument.

  <subsection|Identifying the retained region in the actual
  integral><label|ch09:stationary-exterior>

  Write <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>> for the actual tree-gauge
  configuration map. Consider the region <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>=\<cal-D\><rsub|x>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>+g*<wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|0,D|)>|)>>.
  Let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Z><around|(|x|)>> be the pure block
  integral, and set

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><around|(|x|)>=\<beta\><rprime|'>*\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>+\<psi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det A<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|N> is the number of free fine links and <math|c<rsub|H>> the Haar
  density constant, its scalar normalization is
  <math|\<Omega\>=\<pi\><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>*<around|(|<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>*g|)><rsup|n>*c<rsub|H><rsup|N>>;
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>> is the block-kernel normalizing
  prefactor. When <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>> lies in the chart, all its fine
  plaquettes pass the cutoff, and all live tag actions coincide with
  <math|\<phi\>>, the change of variables gives exactly

  <\equation>
    <big|int><rsub|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>><text|(pure
    integrand)>=\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|x|)>>*R*<around|(|g,x|)>.<label|ch09:retained-identity>
  </equation>

  Tag probabilities sum to one, so there is no additional tag-count factor.
  The hypotheses about this region must be established before using the
  identity.

  Here is a verification using the actual geometry and Hessian gap. The
  actual tree comparison recognizes a fine configuration as lying in a fixed
  safe chart whenever its fine plaquettes and gauge-covariant block average
  mismatches are below a fixed <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. On that chart assume
  <math|D<rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<phi\>\<geqslant\>\<gamma\>*I/2>. Stationarity
  gives

  <\equation*>
    \<phi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>-\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<geqslant\>\<gamma\>*<around|\||y-\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|2>/4.
  </equation*>

  Thus a chart point outside <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|x>> costs at least
  <math|\<Delta\>=\<gamma\>*D<rsup|2>/4> in the exponent. A point outside the
  chart with all fine plaquettes small must have a mismatch exceeding
  <math|\<delta\>>, and contributes a fixed positive multiple of
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>*\<delta\><rsup|2>>. The possibly negative cubic,
  quartic and loop terms are bounded by the path coefficient bounds. If
  <math|W> bounds the auxiliary coordinate and site counts, their total cost
  is at most

  <\equation*>
    W*B<rsub|<math-up|loop>>,<space|2em>B<rsub|<math-up|loop>>=\<beta\><rprime|'>*C*<around|(|t<rsub|f><rsup|3>+t<rsub|f><rsup|4>|)>+\<Lambda\>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\>\<leqslant\>2*M.
  </equation*>

  All such terms and the logarithmic Haar/determinant normalization cost are
  <math|o<around|(|M<rsup|2>|)>>. In particular, the normalization loss
  <math|-N*log <around|[|c<rsub|H>*<around|(|<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>*g|)><rsup|dim
  <gLie>>|]>> is only a polynomial in <math|log z>. The bound on
  <math|\<phi\>*<around|(|x,\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>> on
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>h> also leaves the fixed
  large-mismatch penalty available, because that value tends to zero and
  <math|\<Delta\>/\<beta\><rprime|'>\<to\>0>. Consequently the exterior
  integrand, in stationary units, is bounded by
  <math|e<rsup|-\<Delta\>+o<around|(|M<rsup|2>|)>>>.

  It remains to verify the fine cutoff on the retained ball. For the folded
  auxiliary configurations used here, this can be done directly from the
  curvature transport, without a lower bound from a sector expansion. A full
  coordinate cycle of a folded field traverses an original path and then its
  inverse. Its holonomy is therefore exactly one at every transverse base
  point. Equivalently, choose a coordinate axial gauge on the original cube
  and pull that gauge back under the folding map. Plaquette filling bounds
  give logarithmic coordinates with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>P<around|(|B|)>*t<rsub|c>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1,c>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|c>+P<around|(|B|)>*t<rsub|c><rsup|2>,<space|2em>t<rsub|c>=t<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  The second estimate follows by expanding each plaquette product in these
  small link coordinates. Crucially, its leading constant is independent of
  <math|B>; the polynomial loss occurs only in the quadratic error. This
  argument would fail for an arbitrary small-curvature periodic field with
  nontrivial winding holonomies.

  Let <math|\<cal-U\><around|(|x|)>=\<cal-D\><rsub|x><around|(|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>>
  be the physical stationary fine field. Its tangent is the forest-coordinate
  lift <math|W<rsub|F>> of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>. Its curl
  agrees with that of the smooth actual lift <math|W<rsub|a>>. The curvature
  identity and the fine-row bound of the two-form transport give

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|1,f>*W<rsub|a>=H<rsub|f><rsup|-1>*T*H<rsub|c>*d<rsub|1,c>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|f><rsup|-1>*T*H<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  The operator norm here sums each fine-output row over coarse inputs; the
  opposite column sum has a different scaling. The quadratic remainder of the
  stationary map and the plaquette product is bounded using their common
  second derivatives. Thus

  <\equation>
    sup<rsub|p> \<chi\><around|(|\<cal-U\><around|(|x|)><rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*t<rsub|c>+P<rsub|j,L><around|(|B|)>*t<rsub|c><rsup|2>.<label|ch09:stationary-cutoff-margin>
  </equation>

  Choose <math|L> so that <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*t<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|f>/8>,
  using the uniform bound <math|t<rsub|c>/t<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2>. Then
  increase the inverse coupling threshold so that the second term is at most
  <math|t<rsub|f>/8>. These choices are in the required order: the constant
  in the first term is fixed before <math|L>, while the polynomial in the
  second term may depend on <math|L>.

  Plaquette functions have a fixed Lipschitz constant <math|L<rsub|0>> in the
  physical fine-link supremum coordinates. Local chart maps have fixed-stage
  derivative bounds. Consequently variation of <math|x> by <math|z<rsup|-1>>
  and of the stationary fine coordinates by <math|g*\<xi\>>,
  <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>D>, changes every plaquette distance
  from the identity by at most <math|C<rsub|j,L>*z<rsup|-1>+L<rsub|0>*g*D>,
  after incorporating the fixed chart norm conversion into <math|L<rsub|0>>.
  Choose <math|d<rsub|0>> sufficiently small, so that
  <math|L<rsub|0>*g*D\<leqslant\>t<rsub|f>/8>, and then raise the threshold
  for the microscopic term. Every point of each retained region is now fine
  small-field, with a strict margin. The common chart and tag-coincidence
  radius contain these regions because <math|h+g*D\<to\>0>. We have therefore
  obtained, on a full microscopic neighborhood of each tested small-field
  coarse configuration,

  <\equation>
    0\<leqslant\><with|math-font|cal*|Z><around|(|y|)>-\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|y|)>>*R*<around|(|g,y|)>\<leqslant\>\<Omega\>*e<rsup|-\<Phi\><around|(|y|)>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|0>*M<rsup|2>>,<space|2em>c<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0.<label|ch09:exterior-bound>
  </equation>

  This deduction uses the actual stationary tangent, its second derivatives,
  the compatible path bounds and the geometric chart-recognition estimate.
  The direct cutoff proof applies to the folded auxiliary fields and their
  microscopic neighborhoods, which are precisely the configurations used for
  localization.

  <subsection|Centering the full logarithm><label|ch09:centered-log>

  Put <math|E=log <with|math-font|cal*|Z>-log
  \<Omega\>+\<Phi\>-L*<around|(|g,\<cdot\>|)>>. Since
  <math|R\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-1>/2>, <eqref|ch09:exterior-bound> gives
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>2*e*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-c<rsub|0>*M<rsup|2>>>.
  To pass to derivatives, write the positive pure integrand at a fixed fine
  field and tag as <math|a<around|(|\<omega\>|)>*e<rsup|p*<around|(|x,\<omega\>|)>>>,
  with <math|a\<geqslant\>0> independent of <math|x>. Only the coarse-link
  kernel is differentiated. Its exponent has fixed-order derivatives bounded
  by <math|C<rsub|r>*\<beta\><rprime|'>*n<rsub|b>>, where <math|n<rsub|b>> is
  the number of coarse links. The exponential chain rule and integration give
  relative bounds

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*<with|math-font|cal*|Z><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|r>*<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>*n<rsub|b>|)>*<with|math-font|cal*|Z><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The logarithm chain rule thus gives polynomial bounds without dividing an
  absolute error by a possibly tiny partition function. The other terms in
  <math|E> have polynomial derivative bounds from the stationary estimates.
  Interpolation on the microscopic neighborhoods in
  <eqref|ch09:exterior-bound> proves that every fixed jet of <math|E> is
  smaller than every inverse power of <math|z>.

  Let <math|a<rsub|c>> be the prescribed coarse classical action and
  <math|d<rsub|c>> its quantum contribution. Define

  <\equation*>
    C=a<rsub|c>-\<phi\>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,\<rho\>|)>,<space|2em>W=d<rsub|c>-\<psi\>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>,\<rho\>|)>-<tfrac|1|2>log
    det A.
  </equation*>

  The corrected logarithm has the exact decomposition

  <\equation>
    log <with|math-font|cal*|Z>+\<beta\><rprime|'>*a<rsub|c>+d<rsub|c>=log
    \<Omega\>+L+E+\<beta\><rprime|'>*C+W.<label|ch09:pure-decomposition>
  </equation>

  Classical matching gives <math|D<rsup|r>*C<around|(|0|)>=0> for
  <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>4>. The quadratic quantum matching bounds
  <math|D<rsup|2>*W<around|(|0|)>> by an exponentially small finite-period
  error, with polynomial coordinate norm costs. Its higher fixed derivatives
  are <math|z<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. The full corrected logarithm has zero
  first derivative at zero by constant gauge invariance and simplicity of
  <math|<gLie>>. Thus <math|D*W<around|(|0|)>=-D*L<around|(|0|)>-D*E<around|(|0|)>>;
  no separate parity claim about each determinant contribution is needed.

  For a function on the radius-<math|h> ball write its scaled derivative
  bound through order <math|r> as <math|max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>r>
  h<rsup|k>*sup <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|k>*f|\<\|\|\>>>. Taylor's theorem,
  <eqref|ch09:even-gain>, and the two matching cancellations show that the
  centered expression in <eqref|ch09:pure-decomposition> has this bound at
  most

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|r><around|(|z|)>*<around|{|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsup|-1>*h+\<beta\><rprime|'>*h<rsup|5>+h<rsup|3>|}>+z<rsup|-K>,<space|2em>C<rsub|r><around|(|z|)>=z<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>,<label|ch09:pure-small-bound>
  </equation>

  where <math|K> is arbitrary and the exponentially small quadratic
  finite-period error has been included in the last term. For
  <math|h=z<rsup|-7/16>> the three powers are respectively
  <math|z<rsup|-23/16>>, <math|z<rsup|-19/16>> and <math|z<rsup|-21/16>>.
  Their largest exponent is <math|-19/16\<less\>-23/20>. Actual small-field
  logarithmic coordinates and the reflected normalized directions are bounded
  by a polynomial in <math|B> times <math|t<rsub|m>>, which is
  <math|o<around|(|h|)>>. They therefore obey the same estimate. This proves
  the auxiliary remainder needed for localization from the explicit
  stationary, matching and actual-sector inputs above. It does not require a
  formal expansion of an untruncated exponential of a cubic Gaussian
  polynomial.

  <subsection|Recovering a local density>

  The passage from auxiliary sums to local activities has an exact
  finite-dimensional explanation. Truncate the selected low-order formula,
  retaining a weighted tail <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>. Give an
  ordinary selected term <math|f<rsub|a><rsup|0>> the shares
  <math|p<rsub|x*a>=\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|a>><around|(|x|)>/<around|\||X<rsub|a>|\|>>,
  and a path term the share <math|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|a>|}>><around|(|x|)>>.
  These shares sum to one. Set

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|a<math-up|selected>>p<rsub|x*a>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>,<space|2em>M<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|a<math-up|selected>>p<rsub|x*a>*h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>.
  </equation*>

  Choose a common dependence radius <math|R> about every site receiving a
  nonzero share. Set <math|w=4*R+100*<around|(|1+B|)>>,
  <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>=w+5>, and use auxiliary tori
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> of sides <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>4>. The relevant reflection cubes must
  embed in the original torus. For a cyclic coordinate <math|q> of period
  <math|2*v>, define <math|\<pi\><rsub|v><around|(|q|)>=min
  <around|(|<wide|q|\<bar\>>,2*v-<wide|q|\<bar\>>|)>>, where
  <math|0\<leqslant\><wide|q|\<bar\>>\<less\>2*v>. Apply this map
  coordinatewise. Write <math|U<rsub|i,z>> for the pullback of the original
  field under <math|q\<mapsto\>z+\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|q|)>> on
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>. Reversed links are inverted, so small plaquette
  distances from the identity stay small. Let
  <math|F<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> denote the same local formula on
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> with its flat value subtracted, and set

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>|<cell|=<frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>|2<rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>4!>,>|<cell|G<rsub|x>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>F<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|i|)>><around|(|U<rsub|i,x>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|L<rsub|x>>|<cell|=M<rsub|x>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>h<rsub|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>>*<around|(|x-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|y|)>|)>>*F<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|i|)>><around|(|U<rsub|i,x-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|y|)>>|)>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|ch09:localization>>>>>
  </align>

  In the second line the original cube is based at
  <math|x-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|y|)>>, so its site <math|y> corresponds
  to the original site <math|x>. This dependence on <math|y> is essential.

  When <math|N<rsub|2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>> is small-field, all
  these event indicators are one. Classify sites of <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>
  by their distances, through <math|R>, from the reflection faces. With the
  recentering just specified, the local expression in each class is
  independent of <math|i>. The multiplicity of each class is polynomial in
  <math|i> of degree at most four; only the all-interior class has degree
  four, with leading coefficient <math|2<rsup|4>>. The fourth-difference
  weights therefore give <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y>F<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|i|)>><around|(|U<rsub|i,x-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|y|)>>|)>=F<rsub|x><around|(|U|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>,
  and <math|L<rsub|x>=0>. This is not a pointwise assertion that
  <math|G<rsub|x>=F<rsub|x>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>.

  Sum <eqref|ch09:localization> over <math|x> and change variables
  <math|x-\<pi\><rsub|w+i><around|(|y|)>\<mapsto\>x> in each finite auxiliary
  sum. Translation covariance makes <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>=F<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>>,
  giving exactly

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|x>M<rsub|x>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*F<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>+<big|sum><rsub|x>h<rsub|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>>*G<rsub|x>+<big|sum><rsub|x>L<rsub|x>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|G<rsub|x>> is the small-field local activity and
  <math|L<rsub|x>> is a logarithmic correction supported where its enlarged
  neighborhood contains a large-field site.

  Auxiliary volumes and reflected derivative norms grow only polynomially in
  <math|B>. Thus <eqref|ch09:aux-input> implies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|)>,<space|2em>sup
    <around|\||L<rsub|x>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>.
  </equation*>

  The first fits an arbitrarily small fraction of
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>, since
  <math|23/20\<gtr\>11/10>. The second tends to zero and is incorporated into
  large-field activities by the exact operation below.

  The selected low-order terms contain no incoming regular or large-field
  activity. Their coefficients depend only on the prescribed path, actual
  inverse coupling and fixed labels. Their weighted continuity on the compact
  inverse coupling band therefore permits one truncation for all inputs with
  those data. Terms involving incoming activities have already been estimated
  separately. More generally compactness along a single activity curve would
  give a truncation only along that curve. All choices here are retained
  under restriction of the parameter interval.

  <section|A precise preparatory output><label|ch09:preparatory>

  The preceding estimates, when their concrete local inputs hold, give local
  functions <math|f<rsub|i>>, preliminary large-field activities
  <math|k<rsub|i>>, and a scalar <math|\<tau\>> satisfying

  <\equation>
    <with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<tau\>-\<beta\><rprime|'>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\><around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-F\><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>.<label|ch09:early>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-F\><rsup|0>> is the exact-cover sum of the
  <math|k<rsub|i>>, and <math|\<cal-Q\>> is the quadratic part, restricted by
  its event indicators, of the prescribed path action. The coefficient
  <math|\<alpha\>> is the matched determinant coefficient, not an adjustable
  trial inverse coupling. The bounds needed below are

  <\equation>
    sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)>*c<around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>,<space|1em>a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+1|)>*c<around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-c<rsub|j><around|(|M<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|2>>,<label|ch09:early-bounds>
  </equation>

  where <math|c<around|(|X|)>=<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>
  and <math|H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,
  <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>. The positive stage constant
  <math|c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>> is fixed from the subsequent
  subtraction and path-replacement operator norms before choosing the ratio.

  To obtain these bounds choose <math|L> first so that
  <math|V<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-1>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> fits
  the desired fraction of <math|H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>>. This is a condition
  on the fixed stage constants: the ratio
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>
  is bounded on the permitted neighboring reference-inverse-coupling bands.
  Then choose the inverse coupling threshold for the inherited comparison,
  <eqref|ch09:degree-gain>, <eqref|ch09:aux-input>, and the small logarithmic
  corrections. Choose the truncation accuracy after its polynomial
  localization costs have been specified. Allocate distinct positive
  fractions of the exponent in <eqref|ch09:joint-input> to sector
  multiplicities, local normalization losses, and the final large-field
  bound. Polynomial support costs use support-weight reserves. An unexplained
  extensive normalization cannot be absorbed into a single selected
  large-field condition.

  Equation <eqref|ch09:early> and its bounds are the concrete interface for
  the rest of the chapter. The remaining operations and estimates will now be
  proved directly from it.

  <section|Local quadratic subtraction><label|ch09:subtraction>

  For <math|a\<less\>b> define the clover curvature at <math|o> by

  <\equation*>
    F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U,o|)>=<tfrac|1|4><big|sum><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<eta\>=\<pm\>1>\<varepsilon\>*\<eta\><space|0.17em><math-up|proj><rsub|<gLie>><frac|U<rsub|o;\<varepsilon\>*a,\<eta\>*b>-U<rsub|o;\<varepsilon\>*a,\<eta\>*b><rsup|-1>|2>.
  </equation*>

  The loop follows the four signed edges and starts at <math|o>. This field
  transforms by <math|<Ad>> at <math|o> and reads a radius-two neighborhood.
  For an affine potential <math|A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|y|)>=u<rsub|\<mu\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>y<rsup|\<nu\>>*v<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>>
  its derivative at the identity is <math|v<rsub|b*a>-v<rsub|a*b>>.

  For a preliminary <math|f> on <math|X> anchored at <math|o>, put
  <math|Z=X\<cup\>N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>>. On a nonwrapping <math|Z>, let
  <math|A<rsup|q,T><rsub|c><around|(|y|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|c=b|}>>*\<xi\><rsub|o><around|(|y|)><rsup|a>*T>
  for <math|q=<around|(|a,b|)>>, and fix a unit
  <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\><gLie>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>=D<rsup|2>[f(exp\<cdot\><around|)||]><around|(|0|)><around|[|A<rsup|q,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>,A<rsup|r,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>|]>,<space|1em>Q<rsub|f><around|(|U|)>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|q,r>q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)><around|\<langle\>|F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>,F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|r>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|q<rsub|f>=0> if <math|Z> wraps. Gauge invariance makes the first
  derivative at the identity vanish and the Hessian annihilate local
  gradients. The zero-curl part of an affine potential is a discrete gradient
  of a quadratic site function. It can be extended periodically using a
  cutoff outside the dependence region. Therefore the Hessian on affine
  fields depends only on their constant curls. Constant color invariance and
  simplicity of <math|<gLie>> make its color bilinear form a multiple of the
  invariant metric. Thus <math|Q<rsub|f>> has the same affine Hessian as
  <math|f>. This is the local Ward-identity argument of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch07:kernels> in finite coordinates.

  Set

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|f>=f-f<around|(|1|)>-Q<rsub|f>,<space|2em>\<zeta\><rsub|f>=<around|(|1-h<rsub|Z>|)>*<around|{|f<around|(|1|)>+h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|f>-h<rsub|X>*f|}>.
  </equation*>

  An exact two-case identity is

  <\equation>
    h<rsub|X>*f=f<around|(|1|)>+h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|f>-\<zeta\><rsub|f>+h<rsub|Z>*R<rsub|f>.<label|ch09:subtract-identity>
  </equation>

  On a small-field <math|Z> all event indicators are one; otherwise
  <math|h<rsub|Z>=0> and the definition of <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> proves the
  equality. The remainder vanishes at the flat field and has zero affine
  Hessian on nonwrapping supports. The function <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>>
  vanishes on small-field <math|Z>. Its large-field indicator dependence must
  still be recorded in its full dependence region; no derivative of an event
  indicator is taken.

  The evaluations in normalized affine directions and polarization give
  <math|t<rsup|2>*<around|\||q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>3*a<rsub|f>>.
  Every allowed derivative of <math|F<rsup|<math-up|cl>>> is
  <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|t|)>> on its small-field domain, so

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|f>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>+sup
    <around|\||\<zeta\><rsub|f>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*a<rsub|f>.<label|ch09:subtraction-bound>
  </equation>

  In the supremum term, <math|f> and <math|Q<rsub|f>> occur only under their
  own small-field conditions. Enlarging <math|X> to <math|Z> costs a bounded
  number of covering balls.

  The conversion of anchored bounds to containing-site bounds is explicit. If
  <math|x\<in\>Z> and <math|c<around|(|Z|)>=n>, its anchor lies within
  distance <math|C*B*n> of <math|x>. There are at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>> choices. An
  additional exponent in the support weight absorbs this polynomial.
  Consequently the discrepancy series has containing-site norm at exponent
  <math|\<sigma\>+1> bounded by <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>>.

  By translation and hypercubic covariance,

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=o>q<rsub|f<rsub|i>>*<around|(|q,r|)>=\<Delta\>*\<delta\><rsub|q*r>.
  </equation*>

  An axis reflection kills off-diagonal orientation pairs; permutations
  identify the diagonal pairs. Absolute convergence follows from the
  derivative bound, and

  <\equation>
    t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>.<label|ch09:delta-bound>
  </equation>

  Thus, with <math|\<cal-C\><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||F<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\|><rsup|2>>,
  the sum of the extracted quadratic functions is
  <math|\<Delta\>*\<cal-C\><around|(|U|)>>.

  <section|Replacing the local quadratic term by the path
  action><label|ch09:path-reset>

  The path action is the tested and clipped density of
  <eqref|ch04:path-action>. Its path and cutoff data remain fixed during this
  replacement, as do the frame choices defining the actual probability kernel
  <eqref|ch04:block-operator>.

  Expand the quadratic part into plaquette channels and ordered pairs of
  entries in each row square. For a channel <math|d> let
  <math|a<rsub|d>*P<rsub|d>> be its quadratic expression without its event
  indicator, <math|a<rsub|d>*Q<rsub|d>> its constant-curvature clover
  substitute, and <math|L<rsub|d>> its actual clipped or tested expression.
  Its support <math|Y<rsub|d>> contains both paths and every nonautomatic
  relaxed small-field condition. On the small-field set of <math|Y<rsub|d>>,
  <math|L<rsub|d>=a<rsub|d>*P<rsub|d>>. Define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|d>=\<Delta\>*a<rsub|d>*<around|(|Q<rsub|d>-P<rsub|d>|)>,<space|2em>J<rsub|d>=<around|(|1-h<rsub|Y<rsub|d>>|)>*<around|{|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*L<rsub|d>-\<Delta\>*a<rsub|d>*h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|d>|}>>*Q<rsub|d>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Then

  <\equation>
    <around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*L<rsub|d>+h<rsub|Y<rsub|d>>*F<rsub|d>=\<alpha\>*h<rsub|Y<rsub|d>>*a<rsub|d>*P<rsub|d>+\<Delta\>*a<rsub|d>*h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|d>|}>>*Q<rsub|d>+J<rsub|d>.<label|ch09:channel>
  </equation>

  The equality follows on each of the two health domains. Every
  <math|F<rsub|d>> has zero affine Hessian and every <math|J<rsub|d>>
  vanishes on its small-field support.

  The selected free metric satisfies <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*I+S<around|(|0|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<around|(|0|)>=I>,
  where <math|S<around|(|0|)>> is the row coefficient matrix at zero
  momentum. Therefore summing the clover substitutes gives exactly
  <math|\<cal-C\>>, while the raw channels restricted by their event
  indicators sum to <math|\<cal-Q\>>. Add the regular terms
  <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*A<rsub|d>> for the cubic and
  quartic atoms. After the common signed-symmetry average, let <math|F> be
  the resulting sum of regular terms restricted by their event indicators and
  <math|J> the sum of the <math|J<rsub|d>>. Equation <eqref|ch09:channel>
  becomes

  <\equation>
    <around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\>+F=\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\>+\<Delta\>*\<cal-C\>+J.<label|ch09:path-identity>
  </equation>

  Combining <eqref|ch09:early>, <eqref|ch09:subtract-identity> and
  <eqref|ch09:path-identity> gives, at every field,

  <\equation>
    <with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>>=e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<tau\>-<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>-<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>+\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\>-\<cal-L\>-E>*\<cal-F\><rsup|0>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i>>+J>,<space|2em>E=<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|Z<rsub|i>>*R<rsub|f<rsub|i>>+F.<label|ch09:before-fusion>
  </equation>

  No division by the exact-cover gas was used, so this equality also holds
  where that gas vanishes or is signed.

  Weighted path sums and additional exponential decay in path length give
  constants <math|C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>,L<rsub|0>,L<rsub|1>,L<rsub|h>> such
  that

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-E\><rsub|m><around|(|E|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|reset>>*H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>+C<rsub|j>*<around|{|H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*L<rsub|0>+<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>+H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>|)>*L<rsub|1>|}>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<zeta\>+J|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<sigma\>+1,<math-up|site>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>+C<rsub|j>*<around|{|t\|\<alpha\>\|+H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>/t+H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>|}>*L<rsub|h>.<eq-number><label|ch09:reset-cost>>>>>
  </align>

  Here the site norm sums supremum majorants of logarithmic terms over
  supports containing a site. The low regular channels contain two
  curvatures, the high channels at least three; each derivative costs a
  polynomial in path length, controlled by the exponential path norm. The
  actual clipped channel can have size <math|O<around|(|t|)>> on a
  large-field configuration, which explains the term
  <math|t<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> in the second line.

  Choose <math|c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>> in <eqref|ch09:early-bounds>
  small enough to absorb the terms linear in <math|H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>> in
  the first line. At fixed ratio <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> is bounded,
  and

  <\equation*>
    t<rsup|3>/\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-2/5><around|(|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|3>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  Thus the first line is at most <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>.
  The second line tends to zero even after multiplication by
  <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>>: in particular
  <math|H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>/t=O<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-3/5>/M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)>>.
  This is the smallness needed for the exact regrouping.

  <section|Absorbing logarithmic corrections into large-field
  activities><label|ch09:fusion>

  Write the logarithmic corrections in <eqref|ch09:before-fusion> as
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|a>> on nonempty exclusion supports <math|Z<rsub|a>>,
  with <math|\<ell\><rsub|a>=0> when <math|Z<rsub|a>> is small-field. Their
  extra large-field indicator dependence regions are retained. For each
  finite connected selection <math|P> of preliminary large-field labels and
  logarithmic labels, require a nonempty large-field part and pairwise
  disjoint preliminary exclusion supports. Connectivity refers to
  intersections of these exclusion supports and the <math|Z<rsub|a>>. Set
  <math|Y<rsub|P>> equal to their union and <math|I<rsub|P>> equal to the
  union of the preliminary prescribed large-field site sets. Define

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Y<rsub|P>\<cap\><math-up|Bad><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|P>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P<rsub|<math-up|lf>>>k<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>P<rsub|log>><around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>>-1|)>,
  </equation*>

  with the conjunction of all inherited additional conditions.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Exact regrouping><label|ch09:fusion-prop>Suppose
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>sup <around|\||\<ell\><rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    The exact-cover sum of the <math|K<rsub|P>> equals
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsup|0>*e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsub|a>>>. If
    nonnegative majorants satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||k<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*v<rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|a>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+1|)>*c<around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>*v<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|1em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1,
    </equation*>

    where the unified index also includes the preliminary large-field
    supports, then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|m><around|(|K|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Expand <math|e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsub|a>>=<big|prod><rsub|a><around|[|1+<around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|)>|]>>.
    In a nonzero term each selected logarithmic label contains a large-field
    site. Exact coverage assigns that site to a preliminary prescribed
    large-field site set, so its connected component contains a large-field
    label. Taking components gives disjoint union supports and the displayed
    new prescribed-site equality. Conversely flattening a compatible family
    of new labels recovers the old exact cover and the logarithmic selection.
    No constituent label is repeated because its support is nonempty. All
    additional conditions are retained. This is a bijection of finite terms
    and does not require independence of their large-field indicator reads.

    For the bound retain <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>> from one large-field label
    per component, and bound the others by <math|v<rsub|i>>. Subadditivity of
    <math|c> on connected unions and the rooted tree bound underlying
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch08:kp> sum the connected selections through a
    fixed site. One additional unit of exponential support decay bounds the
    number of attachments, since a support has at most
    <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*c<around|(|Z|)>> sites. Their sum is
    bounded by <math|\<gamma\>>, hence by <math|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1>. The
    resulting absolute envelope justifies the countable regrouping.
  </proof>

  Take <math|\<kappa\>> to be half of the exponent retained in the second
  bound of <eqref|ch09:early-bounds>. The remaining half makes the
  preliminary large-field majorants small in the attachment norm. For the
  logarithmic terms use <math|<around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|sup
  <around|\||\<ell\>|\|>>*sup <around|\||\<ell\>|\|>> and
  <eqref|ch09:reset-cost>. Then <math|\<gamma\>\<to\>0> and a strict margin
  <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2> is available.
  Finally

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|-\<kappa\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|(|-<around|(|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|1/5>|)>=\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|lf>>
  </equation*>

  for a sufficiently large inverse coupling threshold, provided the
  neighboring reference scales have the prescribed comparison. The square
  <math|<around|(|M<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|2>> in
  <math|\<kappa\>> dominates the exponent <math|1/5> and all polynomial
  logarithmic costs.

  <section|The normalization of the field-independent term and
  continuity><label|ch09:continuity>

  The output inverse coupling and scalar are now fixed:

  <\equation>
    \<beta\>=\<beta\><rprime|'>+\<alpha\>+\<Delta\>,<space|2em>v<rsub|<math-up|sc>>=\<tau\>-<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>.<label|ch09:reset>
  </equation>

  Equation <eqref|ch09:before-fusion> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch09:fusion-prop> prove
  <math|<math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\>|)>=<with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>>>
  pointwise. Flat normalization also gives
  <math|v<rsub|<math-up|sc>>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log
  <with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>><around|(|1|)>>. The exact block
  integral of a positive input is positive; signed individual activities
  cause no contradiction.

  Projection already provides the useful estimate

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*H<rsub|<math-up|pre>>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<theta\>*c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10><around|(|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  If the matched coefficient has <math|\<alpha\>=-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
  L+O<rsub|j><around|(|1|)>> as in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch07:slope>,
  then

  <\equation>
    \<beta\>=\<beta\><rprime|'>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    L+e,<space|2em><around|\||e|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|j>+1.<label|ch09:drift>
  </equation>

  No estimate of <math|\<Delta\>> was used to define its local projection or
  prove the exact algebra.

  There is a second, intrinsic way to identify the reset. On an even-sided
  torus choose direction-one links alternately equal to <math|u> and <math|1>
  on even and odd second-coordinate slices, with
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|u|)>=r*t> for sufficiently small fixed
  <math|r\<gtr\>0>. Every nontrivial plaquette has this distance from the
  identity. The direct positive plaquette term dominates the cubic and
  quartic terms when <math|r> is small, so this small-field configuration
  <math|U<rsub|\<ast\>>> satisfies <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*t<rsup|2>>.
  Hold the constructed activities fixed and vary only a trial inverse
  coupling correction <math|d>. The scalar discrepancy is

  <\equation*>
    f<around|(|d|)>=<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>+\<alpha\>+d|)>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>+\<cal-L\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>+E<around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>-log
    <frac|<with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>><around|(|1|)>|<with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>=<around|(|d-\<Delta\>|)>*\<cal-K\><around|(|U<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It has the unique zero <math|d=\<Delta\>>. Two evaluations identify the two
  scalars only because the full-field identity has already been established.
  Matching two fields alone would not prove that identity.

  For a compact continuous input curve choose fixed local supports, cutoff
  conditions, truncations and reflected tori. Projection, subtraction and
  channel replacement are bounded linear operations at these fixed labels.
  Products are continuous by telescoping with one difference factor. For
  changes of inverse coupling hold physical field coordinates fixed; for a
  centered Gaussian of precision <math|\<beta\>*A>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|d*\<nu\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|2>>|d*\<nu\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|1>>><around|(|a|)>=<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|2>/\<beta\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|D/2>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|2>-\<beta\><rsub|1>|)>*<around|\<langle\>|a,A*a|\<rangle\>>/2>.
  </equation*>

  Its derivative is the same density ratio times
  <math|D/<around|(|2*\<beta\><rsub|2>|)>-<around|\<langle\>|a,A*a|\<rangle\>>/2>.
  On a small relative interval it is dominated by a polynomial times a
  Gaussian with a smaller positive precision. Mean and matrix changes give
  analogous Gaussian scores. Apply the common-law estimate to these scores;
  do not differentiate a merely measurable incoming large-field activity.

  Infinite regrouping requires more than coefficientwise continuity. Given a
  sequence of parameters approaching <math|u<rsub|0>>, pass to a subsequence
  whose weighted input discrepancies have sum at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>.
  Their sum added to the majorant at <math|u<rsub|0>> is a common summable
  envelope. The strict attachment margin allows this addition for small
  <math|\<varepsilon\>>. The rooted estimate then gives one summable output
  envelope; dominated convergence proves weighted continuity on this
  subsequence. Every potentially nonconvergent sequence would have such a
  convergent further subsequence, a contradiction.

  At fixed volume, the exact-cover sums obey the elementary telescoping
  estimate

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-F\><rsub|u<rsub|0>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>M<rsub|i>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i,u>-K<rsub|i,u<rsub|0>>|\|>,<space|1em>M<rsub|i>=max
    <around|(|sup <around|\||K<rsub|i,u>|\|>,sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i,u<rsub|0>>|\|>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Thus the densities are continuous uniformly in <math|U>. The positive
  block-kernel normalizers are continuous and bounded away from zero on
  compact inverse coupling intervals; dominated convergence in probability
  Haar measure proves continuity of <math|<with|math-font|cal*|Z><rsub|<math-up|blk>,u>>.
  The scalar formula and projection show continuity of
  <math|v<rsub|<math-up|sc>>> and <math|\<beta\>>. Restriction to a closed
  subinterval retains the construction and all its labels.

  <section|Wilson initialization><label|ch09:wilson>

  The finest-scale representation can be constructed directly from the Wilson
  action <cite|Wilson1974>, without a limiting argument. Write
  <math|W<around|(|u|)>=1-<math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|u|)>/n<rsub|G>>
  for the Wilson plaquette energy and <math|F<around|(|u|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<gLie>><around|(|u-u<rsup|-1>|)>/2>.
  The map <math|X\<mapsto\>F<around|(|e<rsup|X>|)>> has derivative <math|I>
  at zero and a local inverse <math|\<psi\>>. The map and its inverse are
  odd, and <math|W<around|(|u<rsup|-1>|)>=W<around|(|u|)>>, so
  <math|W<around|(|e<rsup|\<psi\><around|(|Y|)>>|)>> is even. Let
  <math|T<rsub|4>> be the degree-four Taylor term of the Wilson plaquette
  energy evaluated at <math|e<rsup|\<psi\><around|(|Y|)>>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    R<around|(|u|)>=W<around|(|u|)>-<tfrac|1|2>\|F<around|(|u|)>\|<rsup|2>-T<rsub|4><around|(|F<around|(|u|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  vanishes through degree four at the identity. Choose the finest row
  <math|<sqrt|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>>*F>, no cubic or loop term, and quartic
  atom <math|<around|(|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*T<rsub|4>> with its
  prescribed event indicator.

  For a plaquette <math|p> write <math|h<rsub|p>> for the health event
  indicator on its vertices, <math|\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>>> for
  its row cutoff condition, and <math|k<rsub|p>=<around|(|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)><around|\||F<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>|\|><rsup|2>/2>.
  Define the residual energy

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-W\><rsub|p>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>,>|<cell|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<gtr\>t,>>|<row|<cell|h<rsub|p>*k<rsub|p>+<around|(|1-h<rsub|p>|)>*<around|(|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*W<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>-\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*k<rsub|p>,>|<cell|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>t.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<around|\||F<around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<chi\><around|(|u|)>>
  and <math|W<around|(|u|)>=\<chi\><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>/2>, this is
  nonnegative; when <math|\<chi\><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<gtr\>t> it is at
  least <math|t<rsup|2>/4>. The relaxed small-field condition implies that
  the plaquette itself is small, and the condition can also fail because of a
  nearby bad plaquette. Direct substitution gives

  <\equation*>
    W<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>=L<rsub|p>+<around|(|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*h<rsub|p>*T<rsub|4><around|(|F<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>|)>+<around|(|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*h<rsub|p>*R<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>+\<cal-W\><rsub|p>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|L<rsub|p>> is the actual clipped quadratic channel. If the
  relaxed small-field condition inspects a larger neighborhood, its failure
  is included in <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|p>> by the same formula; the identity
  remains valid because on a small-field plaquette the residual is
  <math|k<rsub|p>-\<chi\><rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*k<rsub|p>>.

  Use <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|1-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*R<around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>>
  as a regular activity. Its normalized derivatives are bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|j>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>>. For orders at most five, the missing
  powers after differentiation are supplied by Taylor's formula; for higher
  fixed orders, the direction factors supply at least <math|t<rsup|5>>. Hence

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\>*t<rsup|5>/\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=O<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N><rsup|-2/5><around|(|M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|5>|)>\<to\>0.
  </equation*>

  Assign the nonnegative residual energies to connected components of
  large-field sites whose radius-<math|2*B> neighborhoods overlap. A
  component site set <math|I> has exclusion support
  <math|Y<rsub|I>=N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>> and activity

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|I>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Y<rsub|I>\<cap\><math-up|Bad><rsub|N>=I|}>>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*d<rsub|I>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|d<rsub|I>> is the sum of assigned residual energies with the
  signed average. Each large-field site meets a large-field plaquette, and a
  plaquette has at most four vertices, so
  <math|d<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>c*<space|0.17em>t<rsup|2><around|\||I|\|>> for a
  fixed <math|c\<gtr\>0>. Every residual term is assigned exactly once; its
  relaxed small-field condition has a witnessing large-field site within
  distance <math|B>. The only nonzero exact-cover selection is the actual set
  of these components, so the density identity is exact.

  A connected prescribed set of <math|r> large-field sites is bounded in
  number by a rooted spanning-walk count. The containing-site weighted sum is
  at most

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>1><around*|[|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|8>*e<rsup|5<rsup|4>*\<sigma\>-c*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>>|]><rsup|r>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><around|(|M<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|2>/2>,
  this fits <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|lf>>> for a sufficiently
  large threshold. The activity labels and eligibility criteria are
  independent of <math|\<beta\>>. The resulting full Wilson curve is
  continuous, and its scalar normalization is zero.

  <section|The one-step conclusion and its input data><label|ch09:step>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|One renormalization step><label|ch09:step-theorem>Assume the
    derivative orders and spatial exponents stated at the start of the
    chapter. Fix an adjacent quadratic pair with the positive metrics, exact
    transport identities and actual gauge-covariant block average of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>. Let the incoming path have the
    normalized quadratic coefficient and exponential coefficient and
    derivative bounds of Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking> and
    <reference|ch07:matching>. The incoming activities obey the small-field
    cancellations, large-field eligibility rules and weighted bounds of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>, with the support reserve used
    above. Assume also that the represented input density is strictly
    positive; this holds at the Wilson start and is preserved by the exact
    block kernel. Smallness of signed activities alone is not a substitute
    for this condition. Use the same forest, path realization, clipped frozen
    source and cutoff choices throughout the local integral construction of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>. Construct the outgoing path
    by the classical and quantum matching in
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. Choose the stage constants, ratio
    floors, minimum auxiliary periods and inverse coupling thresholds in the
    order specified above. Require also that the actual coarse period exceeds
    the largest local read diameter and twice the largest auxiliary
    reflection half-period used in the construction. This includes the
    minimum period <math|100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>> in
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>; enlarging the stage-dependent
    period floor ensures that every localization cube and its dependence
    neighborhood embeds in the actual torus.

    Then every admissible continuous input curve has a continuous output
    representation with fixed labels, satisfying the regular and large-field
    bounds <eqref|ch04:activity-bounds> and the pointwise identity

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|u>|)>=\<cal-B\><rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>><math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its inverse coupling is <eqref|ch09:reset> and obeys <eqref|ch09:drift>,
    uniformly in volume, cutoff and curve parameter. The construction retains
    restriction compatibility and all exact-cover cutoff conditions. Output
    inverse coupling-band membership additionally requires the numerical band
    margin in the choice of scales.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The proof consists of the convergent local expansion, the three remainder
    estimates, the exact identities <eqref|ch09:subtract-identity> and
    <eqref|ch09:path-identity>, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch09:fusion-prop>.
    Positivity and normalization refer to the actual block integral.
  </proof>

  This is a theorem for one compatible adjacent pair. Its premises include
  the actual quadratic coefficient and fixed path coefficient bounds; they
  cannot be replaced by unrelated bounds of the same numerical size. The
  common Gaussian estimate is supplied by the complete factor construction in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>, and
  Sections<nbsp><reference|ch09:stationary-remainder>\U<reference|ch09:centered-log>
  give the auxiliary estimate for the same pure integral. The classical bound
  contraction and inherited-loop contraction of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching> allow the path bounds to be retained
  with the stated order of choices.

  What remains for a whole trajectory is to make those choices
  simultaneously, tune the Wilson inverse coupling to the prescribed coarse
  endpoint, and prove stability as the ultraviolet cutoff changes. These are
  additional assertions, not consequences of applying an arbitrary finite
  step repeatedly. In particular, no higher parameter analyticity is inferred
  from the continuity proved here.

  <assign|chapter-nr|9>

  <chapter|A common trajectory and fixed-scale
  stability><label|ch10:trajectory>

  The one-step transformation must be used with a single sequence of scales
  and a consistent choice of coefficient arrays. There are two separate
  problems. First one must tune each finite Wilson theory to the same coarse
  inverse coupling. Then one must compare theories with different ultraviolet
  cutoffs. Continuity of a finite step solves the first problem, but does not
  by itself solve the second.

  We first construct the numerical schedule and the tuned finite histories
  from Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch09:step-theorem>. We then describe the
  additional quantitative comparison needed for convergence of these
  histories. Its abstract consequences are proved here. The distinction
  between that comparison and ordinary continuity remains explicit; in
  particular we do not assert analytic dependence on the tuned endpoint. The
  elementary contraction arguments are applications of Banach's
  principle<nbsp><cite|Banach1922>; we give the estimates specific to the
  oppositely directed inverse coupling and activity recursions.

  <section|What is fixed before choosing the scales><label|ch10:choices>

  A stage <math|j\<geqslant\>1> specifies finitely many derivative orders,
  support exponents, path widths and coefficient bounds. These are numerical
  bounds for the representations of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>,
  not a new collection of physical theories. Stages are nested: the
  conditions of stage <math|j+1> include those already required at stage
  <math|j>. At a fixed stage there are only finitely many permitted adjacent
  pairs. Choose constants for all these pairs simultaneously.

  The order of choices is important. Fix the quadratic metric bounds, path
  widths and classical bounds first. Choose the odd block ratio large enough
  for the classical contraction <eqref|ch07:classical-budget>, inherited-loop
  contraction <eqref|ch07:inherited>, and the support reserves in the local
  integral. Only afterwards choose inverse coupling thresholds and minimum
  auxiliary periods. Enlarging an inverse coupling threshold does not change
  the block kernel or the coefficient construction. The Wilson initialization
  can be made smaller than any prescribed fixed fraction of its activity
  bounds by this enlargement, as follows from the estimates in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch09:wilson>.

  Let <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|drift>>> be a stage bound for the error
  in the downward inverse coupling step:

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:step-drift><around*|\||\<beta\><rsub|m+1>-\<beta\><rsub|m>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|drift>><rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>> is the stage used for this step and
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>=11*C<rsub|<gLie>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>\<gtr\>0>.
  The normalization of the field-independent term is included in
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|drift>>>.
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching> fixes this coefficient with the same
  normalization of the Lie algebra metric as the block integral. Its error
  constant is fixed before the block ratio. Choose nondecreasing odd integers
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>3> above every stage
  floor, and also require

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:drift-floor><frac|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|drift>>|log
    <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|{|\<kappa\><rsub|G>/4,j<rsup|-2>|}>.
  </equation>

  Any further fixed logarithmic size bound <math|K<rsub|j>> can similarly be
  required to satisfy <math|K<rsub|j>/log
  <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>j<rsup|-2>>. Costs depending on
  the chosen <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>> are covered by later
  thresholds for the starting scales, not by redefining the constants used in
  <eqref|ch10:drift-floor>.

  Future applications may prescribe countably many finite numerical requests,
  provided their analytic estimates have already been established for a fixed
  stage. At stage <math|j> take the maximum of the first <math|j> such
  requests. This diagonal choice absorbs finitely many constants; it does not
  establish an estimate merely because it has been listed. In particular the
  comparison hypotheses below must be proved for the same coefficient and
  activity construction before they can be used.

  One particular later request can already be made explicit. Fix the
  equation-adapted sections of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch02:equation-adapted>
  once and for all. For <math|d\<leqslant\>j>, let <math|a<rsub|d*j>> be
  their derivative constants at the orders and spatial weights of stage
  <math|j>, and let <math|q<rsub|d>> bound their union stencil with
  <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>. These constants depend only on fixed
  cover paths, finite polynomials and the stage orders;
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch02:section-bounds> makes them independent of
  the block ratio, period and cutoff. Fix the stage column bound
  <math|C<rsub|j>>, support exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>>, and finite
  symmetry norm <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d>> before choosing the ratio. With

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|d*j>=2+e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|j>>*<around|(|a<rsub|d*j>+C<rsub|j>+2*q<rsub|d>|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|d>=<around|(|2*\<cal-O\><rsub|d><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  include the additional floor

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:equation-floor><with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j><rsup|1/8>\<geqslant\>max<rsub|d\<leqslant\>j>
    2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j>|)>*Q<rsub|d*j>*\<delta\><rsub|d><rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  For the bounded coarse polynomial trials also include
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j><rsup|1/8>\<geqslant\>8*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j>|)>*Q<rsup|<math-up|coarse>><rsub|d*j>>
  for <math|d\<leqslant\>j>, where <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|coarse>><rsub|d*j>>
  is their ordinary chart bound, fixed before the ratio. On that chart the
  relaxed small-field smooth cutoff is one. Its complementary global value is
  controlled by the same later square-root large-field bound condition
  described below. Here <math|C<rsub|j>> is the prescribed bound, not a norm
  recomputed after choosing <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>>. No
  terminal radius enters this floor: the raw stencil is fixed; the terminal
  requirement that its cover be at most <math|81> is imposed later by
  increasing the inverse coupling threshold and hence the available
  localization radius. The remaining terminal value norms contain inverse
  powers of the small-field threshold. The delays require their product with
  the square root of the terminal large-field bound to be at most one, as
  well as the fixed-stencil support and occupancy conditions. They are
  absorbed by choosing sufficiently large inverse-coupling thresholds, not
  included in <math|Q<rsub|d*j>>. Those delays also impose
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>> and
  all terminal reserve inequalities for <math|d\<leqslant\>J<around|(|N|)>>.
  Since only finitely many degrees are tested at a stage, this is a
  noncircular addition to the schedule. Its use is the near-identity
  comparison in Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:equation-calibration>.

  <section|A slowly increasing stage schedule><label|ch10:schedule>

  We give an explicit construction, including the initial scales. Let
  <math|b<rsub|j>,c<rsub|j>> be nonnegative integers specifying respectively
  the minimum starting scale and a requested length for a stage plateau. If
  <math|\<jmath\><rsub|j>> is the largest smoothing order used in stage
  <math|j> or a permitted adjacent pair, include <math|b<rsub|j>*log
  3\<geqslant\>2*log <around|(|\<jmath\><rsub|j>+7|)>> for <math|j\<gtr\>1>.
  This ensures that the full dependence radius of a probability-kernel
  pullback shrinks in physical units, including its frame-choice variables.
  Starting with <math|A<rsub|1>=0>, define

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|j>=c<rsub|j>+2*A<rsub|j>+1,<space|2em>A<rsub|j+1>=A<rsub|j>+b<rsub|j+1>+2*k<rsub|j>+1,<space|2em>J<around|(|m|)>=j*<space|1em><around|(|A<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>A<rsub|j+1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|J> is nondecreasing, tends to infinity, and increases by at most
  one in a single step. If <math|J*<around|(|m+1|)>=j\<gtr\>1>, then
  <math|m\<geqslant\>b<rsub|j>>. Moreover the interval
  <math|<around|[|k<rsub|j>-<around|\<lfloor\>|k<rsub|j>/2|\<rfloor\>>,k<rsub|j>+<around|\<lfloor\>|k<rsub|j>/2|\<rfloor\>>|]>>
  lies in the <math|j>th plateau: its lower endpoint is at least
  <math|A<rsub|j>>, and its upper endpoint is less than <math|A<rsub|j+1>>.

  Set

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:scales>L<rsub|m>=<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|0>=0,<space|1em>\<tau\><rsub|m+1>=\<tau\><rsub|m>+log
    L<rsub|m>,<space|2em>a<rsub|m>=e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|m>>.
  </equation>

  Thus <math|a<rsub|m>> is the lattice spacing, and <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>
  is an integer. Monotonicity gives

  <\equation*>
    m*log 3\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>m*log
    <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|J<around|(|m|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Choose a coarse target <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>0> and reference
  inverse couplings

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:reference><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>.
  </equation>

  The first-stage threshold is satisfied by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>.
  Every subsequent stage threshold <math|d<rsub|j>> is satisfied by
  increasing its starting scale until <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j>>.
  We may simultaneously require

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:late-entry>log <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|m>/j,<space|2em>V<rsub|j><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<tau\><rsub|m>>,<space|1em>J*<around|(|m+1|)>=j\<gtr\>1,
  </equation>

  Also require, for the original support radius and its per-site
  normalization cost, <math|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>>
  and <math|<around|(|L<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|site>>/\<tau\><rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>1/j>
  at stage <math|j>. Here <math|q<rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|site>>> is bounded
  by <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)>> using
  the Haar-ball normalization argument. At a fixed stage the numerator is a
  fixed polynomial in <math|log <around|(|2+\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>>, so this is a
  finite choice of a sufficiently large starting scale. It will keep
  replication costs subpower at a nearby sparse stopping scale. For the later
  grouping of finitely many local sources, also require
  <math|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|m+1>+1|)>*h<rsub|m>/\<tau\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/j>,
  where <math|h<rsub|m>=16*<around|[|1+L<rsub|m>*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>+1|)><rsup|2>|]><rsup|4>>
  and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m+1>> is the prescribed support exponent. This is
  again a fixed polynomial in <math|log <around|(|2+\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>> at
  each stage. It ensures that moving a finite number of source anchors has
  only a subpower cost. In <eqref|ch10:late-entry>, <math|V<rsub|j>> is any
  prescribed fixed polynomial bound for the required lattice side. The same
  argument works for a subexponential <math|V<rsub|j>>. Choose
  <math|b<rsub|j>> so these inequalities hold whenever
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|j>*log 3>. They then hold at every
  step of stage <math|j>, not just at its first occurrence.

  For comparison estimates requiring a window
  <math|w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>=o<around|(|s|)>> around one level of stage
  <math|j>, increase <math|c<rsub|j>> so that
  <math|w<rsub|j><around|(|s|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<lfloor\>|k/2|\<rfloor\>>>
  whenever <math|k\<geqslant\>c<rsub|j>> and <math|k*log
  3\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>k*log <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>>. At
  <math|k=k<rsub|j>> the whole requested window stays on the same plateau.
  This is useful when a fixed finite calculation must be isolated from
  changes in derivative order or support exponent.

  Only finitely many steps belong to the first plateau. Choose an integer
  <math|M> large enough that the lattice side <math|R<rsub|p>/a<rsub|m>>
  meets the required lower bound at every step of this plateau for all
  <math|p\<geqslant\>0>, where

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:periods>R<rsub|p>=4*<around|(|M+p+1|)>,<space|2em>p\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  On later plateaux <eqref|ch10:late-entry> ensures the required lattice
  side. These periods also contain arbitrarily late multiples of every
  positive integer: for a given integer <math|c>, take
  <math|p=c*<around|(|M+z+1|)>-<around|(|M+1|)>> with <math|z> arbitrarily
  large. Finally

  <\equation*>
    <frac|log L<rsub|m>|\<tau\><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  This follows from <eqref|ch10:late-entry> outside the first plateau. No
  limit involving <math|\<tau\><rsub|0><rsup|-1>> is intended.

  <section|Tuning a finite history to the coarse
  endpoint><label|ch10:shooting>

  The admissible inverse coupling band at level <math|m> is
  <math|<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|]>>.
  Define inner barriers

  <\equation*>
    \<ell\><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|1|2>\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>u<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2*\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Both lie in the band. If a step starts at <math|\<ell\><rsub|m+1>>, its
  output is at most <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L<rsub|m>/4>
  by <eqref|ch10:step-drift>\U<eqref|ch10:drift-floor>. Starting at
  <math|u<rsub|m+1>> gives an output at least
  <math|u<rsub|m>+3*\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L<rsub|m>/4>.

  We use only the intermediate value theorem. If a continuous function
  <math|f> on <math|<around|[|x,y|]>> satisfies
  <math|f<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>d\<less\>u\<leqslant\>f<around|(|y|)>>,
  take its first point <math|w> with value <math|u> and its last point
  <math|z\<leqslant\>w> with value <math|d>. Then
  <math|f<around|(|<around|[|z,w|]>|)>\<subset\><around|[|d,u|]>>. An
  excursion below <math|d> would force a later crossing of <math|d>; an
  excursion above <math|u> an earlier crossing of <math|u>. When <math|d=u>,
  choose a single point of that level set. In particular no monotonicity of
  the output inverse coupling is required.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Tuned finite histories><label|ch10:finite-history> Fix the common
    numerical choices above and the compatible finite quadratic and path
    constructions of Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian> and
    <reference|ch07:matching>. For every cutoff <math|N> and period
    <math|R<rsub|p>> there is a finite history starting at a Wilson density
    and ending with <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,0>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. It
    has the admissible activity bounds and exact block identities at every
    level <math|0\<leqslant\>m\<less\>N>. Its inverse couplings stay between
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> and <math|u<rsub|m>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    At level <math|N> use the Wilson curve with bare inverse coupling in
    <math|<around|[|\<ell\><rsub|N>,u<rsub|N>|]>>. Apply the continuous step
    of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch09:step-theorem>. The barrier inequalities
    and the interval argument give a compact subinterval on which the output
    stays between <math|\<ell\><rsub|N-1>> and <math|u<rsub|N-1>> and attains
    these endpoints. Restrict the entire previously constructed history to
    that interval. Restriction compatibility preserves the labels, activity
    bounds and exact identities. Repeat downwards. At level zero the barriers
    coincide at <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. For <math|N=0> use the Wilson
    family directly. Every input to an applied step lies in its band; no
    continuation through a forbidden inverse coupling interval has been used.
  </proof>

  Choose one such history for each <math|N,p>, retaining its path arrays,
  forests, local integral choices and output representations. The choice need
  not be unique. The paths fixed before the period are kept identical when
  the period changes. The exact identity is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>|)>=\<cal-B\><rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>><math-up|dens><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m+1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is an identity of the actual densities, including the additive scalar
  normalization. That scalar is not asserted to converge.

  The same construction reaches any endpoint
  <math|<around|\||b-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>h>, where
  <math|h=min <around|{|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/2,\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
  L<rsub|0>/4|}>>. Descend to level one and use the last barrier crossing to
  reach <math|b>. This proves existence in a common endpoint interval, but
  neither uniqueness of the shooting parameter nor continuity of its
  selection.

  <section|Uniform logarithmic running><label|ch10:running>

  Telescoping from the fixed endpoint gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:running-error><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>C<rsup|<math-up|drift>><rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>,<space|2em>N\<geqslant\>m.
  </equation>

  For each <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|r> so that
  <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>/2> for
  <math|i\<geqslant\>r>. The right side is at most

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>r>C<rsup|<math-up|drift>><rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>+<tfrac|\<epsilon\>|2><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>-\<tau\><rsub|r>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The finite initial sum is eventually at most
  <math|\<epsilon\>*\<tau\><rsub|m>/2>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:running-limit>\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>+o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>
  </equation>

  uniformly in <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and <math|p>. In particular the bare
  Wilson inverse coupling obeys <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,N>/log
  <around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>|)>\<to\>\<kappa\><rsub|G>> uniformly in
  <math|p>. This conclusion does not use convergence of histories at a fixed
  scale. A bounded stage error is not summable by itself; the small ratio in
  <eqref|ch10:drift-floor> is essential.

  By using a smaller fixed constant than <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>/4> in that
  floor, the ratios <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> can
  all be kept in an arbitrarily prescribed fixed neighborhood of one. We
  henceforth choose it small enough that

  <\equation*>
    17/20\<leqslant\><sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>23/20.
  </equation*>

  For example an error at most <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L<rsub|m>/10> at
  each step implies a relative error at most <math|1/10> after summation.
  This also covers the initial indices, including <math|m=0>.

  <section|The coefficient comparison before the activity
  comparison><label|ch10:path-stability>

  A representation by transported curvature terms is not unique. For
  comparisons we fix the linear curl-division and path-placement operations
  in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching> once on the common coefficient
  dictionaries. An alternative is the orthogonal representative modulo the
  kernel of all periodic Taylor coefficients; then the orthogonal complement
  must be taken inside the admissible subspace, so that its invariance and
  moment constraints are preserved. Equality of the action on one torus is
  insufficient to identify its coefficient array.

  At a fixed stage enlarge each incoming classical or loop coefficient bound
  by a factor <math|64>, one family at a time, and choose the ratio floor for
  that enlarged bound. The bounds in <eqref|ch07:classical-budget> and
  <eqref|ch07:inherited> then retain ordinary output bounds. This enlargement
  does not concern the positive quadratic metric, which is kept fixed. The
  fixed-order stationary calculation is valid after reducing its local
  analytic neighborhood if necessary.

  With quadratic data fixed, the outgoing cubic coefficient is affine in the
  incoming cubic coefficient. With the cubic coefficient also fixed, the same
  holds for the quartic coefficient. Finally the quadratic loop coefficient
  is affine in the incoming loop. The stationary formula
  <eqref|ch07:uniform-stationary-jets> proves the first two assertions: its
  exchange term is fixed in the quartic comparison. The inherited Hessian and
  determinant formula prove the third. Fixed linear coefficient functionals
  preserve these identities.

  Normalize each coefficient norm by its stage bound. If an affine map
  <math|T> sends the admissible ball of radius <math|64> into the output ball
  of radius one, then for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>>,<around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>,
  writing <math|\<delta\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|H-K|\<\|\|\>>\<gtr\>0>, the
  admissible extrapolation <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>=K+32*<around|(|H-K|)>/\<delta\>>
  has norm at most <math|33> and gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:affine-contraction><around|\<\|\|\>|T<around|(|H|)>-T<around|(|K|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>/16.
  </equation>

  The extrapolation preserves all linear symmetry and moment conditions. This
  is an affine estimate, not an assertion that the full nonlinear RG
  transformation is affine.

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:horizon-limit> gives convergence of the
  actual quadratic rows and free transports at every fixed level, with a
  strictly stronger exponential width than the required width. Choose the
  direct row coefficient strictly below its uniform positive metric gap, as
  required there. Their fixed-window inverses converge by the resolvent
  identity and the common inverse bound. In each successive coefficient
  comparison the change in these preceding data contributes an error
  <math|e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>\<to\>0> at fixed <math|i>. The current
  coefficient discrepancy therefore satisfies

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>\<delta\><rsub|i+1>+e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|k\<gtr\>m> and <math|N,N<rprime|'>\<gtr\>k>, iteration gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:path-truncation>\<delta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1/4|)><rsup|k-m>+<big|sum><rsub|i=m><rsup|k-1><around|(|1/4|)><rsup|i-m>*e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>.
  </equation>

  First send <math|N,N<rprime|'>\<to\>\<infty\>> at fixed <math|k>, then
  <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. This proves the Cauchy property in succession for
  cubic, quartic and loop coefficients, for the actual nested quadratic
  history and the fixed common coefficient functionals.

  There is a useful precise version of the width reserve. If coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|N><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>> on a common dictionary converge
  coordinatewise and <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>><around|\||c<rsub|N><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>|\|>*e<rsup|w<rprime|'>*\<ell\><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>>\<leqslant\>C>
  with <math|w<rprime|'>\<gtr\>w>, then their tails at width <math|w> and
  length greater than <math|r> are bounded by
  <math|C*e<rsup|-<around|(|w<rprime|'>-w|)>*r>>. If only finitely many
  dictionary entries have bounded length, finite-coordinate convergence and
  this tail bound prove convergence at width <math|w>. For a Hilbert encoding
  instead, one must verify summability of the encoding weights on that
  dictionary; a bound on individual coefficients alone does not bound the
  number of paths of a given length.

  <section|The stronger local comparison required for cutoff
  limits><label|ch10:comparison-input>

  Fix a physical period <math|R<rsub|p>>. At level <math|m> let
  <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>> be the completion of the actual activity arrays
  in their normalized weighted derivative and value norm. Zero seminorm
  arrays are identified. Equality of their evaluated partition functions is
  not this equivalence relation. Dictionaries, event indicators, supports and
  prescribed-site rules are fixed at this level independently of <math|N>. We
  pad absent labels by zero.

  To state the comparison, set

  <\equation*>
    \<chi\><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/32>,<space|2em>\<frak-b\><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|31/32>,<space|2em>\<lambda\><rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|m>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>,<space|2em>b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>*<around|(|\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<lambda\>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The symbol <math|b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> in this section is the
  inverse coupling coordinate map; it is unrelated to the block penalty
  <math|b>. On the narrow band <math|17/20\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>23/20>
  we have

  <\equation>
    <label|ch10:inverse-coordinate>\<frak-b\><rsub|m>*<around|\||\<lambda\>-\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>-b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'>|)>|\|>.
  </equation>

  The extra factor in the right side is <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|\<lambda\>+\<lambda\><rprime|'>|)>\<geqslant\>17/10>.

  Write the actual step maps at cutoff <math|N> as

  <\equation*>
    x<rsub|m>=F<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>,x<rsub|m+1>|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|m>=Q<rsub|m,N>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m+1>,x<rsub|m+1>|)>,<space|1em>\<beta\><rsub|m>-\<beta\><rsub|m+1>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>=q<rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|x<rsub|m>> is the activity array and <math|q<rsub|m>> is the
  residual scalar inverse coupling correction. It is not the vacuum
  normalization. The path coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> is the one
  already used in the exact step.

  The following strengthens the continuous finite-step statement of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch09:step-theorem>. Its proof uses the additional
  numerical reserves already allowed in the scale choices.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Quantitative comparison on the common
    data><label|ch10:stability-assumption>The actual maps are defined for
    every prescribed narrow-band inverse coupling sequence and every allowed
    activity array of norm at most <math|1/8>, including zero terminal
    arrays. They preserve that ball, satisfy
    <math|<around|\||Q<rsub|m,N>|\|>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m>/2>, and, on the
    same cutoff, obey

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|m,N><around|(|z|)>-F<rsub|m,N><around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|16><around|\<\|\|\>|z-z<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>,<eq-number><label|ch10:lipschitz>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|m,N><around|(|z|)>-Q<rsub|m,N><around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*s<rsub|m>*<around|\<\|\|\>|z-z<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>,>>>>
    </align>

    where <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z-z<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>=max
    <around|{|<around|\||\<lambda\>-\<lambda\><rprime|'>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x-x<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>|}>>.
    The strengthened Wilson terminal arrays have norm at most <math|1/8>. The
    numerical choices satisfy <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsub|m>/128>.
    The paths and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> converge at each fixed scale
    with the strict width reserve above. Finally, for two sequences of
    admissible inputs at different cutoffs, common limits of their scalar
    coordinates and Cauchy activity arrays with difference tending to zero
    imply output differences tending to zero, in both the activity norm and
    the scalar correction. This last assertion applies also to every
    zero-terminal-array history with prescribed inverse couplings.
  </proposition>

  The last condition compares changing maps <math|F<rsub|m,N>>, not just
  changing inputs to one fixed map. Uniform contractions by themselves do not
  imply it. For example <math|F<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|N>/10>
  has Lipschitz constant zero and no cutoff limit. Nor does smallness of the
  output imply the small Lipschitz constant in <eqref|ch10:lipschitz>.

  <\render-proof|Proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch10:stability-assumption>>
    We derive the contraction from finite parameter evaluations, bound those
    evaluations using the local expansion, sum the errors over scales, and
    finally prove continuity as the cutoff is removed.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|<em|Step 1.>><em|Contraction from finite parameter evaluations.>
      One sufficient quantitative estimate uses only real parameter lines.
      For a line <math|z+t*v> of admissible inputs,
      <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, suppose every finite choice of
      weights <math|a<rsub|i>> and points <math|t<rsub|i>> satisfies

      <\align>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*F*<around|(|z+t<rsub|i>*v|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>d*sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*<around|(|t<rsub|i>/r|)><rsup|n>|\|>,<eq-number><label|ch10:finite-tests>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*Q*<around|(|z+t<rsub|i>*v|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>f<rsub|m>*sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*<around|(|t<rsub|i>/<around|(|2*r|)>|)><rsup|n>|\|>,<space|2em>r\<geqslant\>2.>>>>
      </align>

      These are estimates of the actual arrays in their derivative norm, not
      just of the value of a partition function. They imply an absolutely
      convergent one-variable expansion. Indeed the map
      <math|<around|(|<around|(|t/r|)><rsup|n>|)><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0>\<mapsto\>F*<around|(|z+t*v|)>>
      extends linearly and boundedly from its span in <math|c<rsub|0>> to its
      closure. That closure contains every coordinate vector: subtract the
      first <math|n> coordinates, divide by <math|<around|(|t/r|)><rsup|n>>,
      and let <math|t*\<downarrow\>*0>. Applying the bounded map to the
      geometric expansion gives coefficients of norm at most <math|d>.
      Consequently

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<\|\|\>|F*<around|(|z+t*v|)>-F<around|(|z|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>d<around|\||t|\|>/<around|(|r-<around|\||t|\|>|)>.
      </equation*>

      The identical argument applies to <math|Q>. This short bounded-operator
      argument records the precise information in the parameter evaluations;
      it is not an inference of analyticity from continuity.

      For two inputs at distance <math|0\<less\>h\<leqslant\>1/4>, use the
      line with direction <math|<around|(|z<rprime|'>-z|)>/<around|(|4*h|)>>
      and evaluate at <math|t=4*h>. Activity norm along this line is at most
      <math|1/8+1/4\<less\>1>. An inverse coupling reserve from the narrow
      band to <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>47/40>
      makes the whole line admissible when
      <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/128>. The resulting bounds are at
      most <math|4*d*h> and <math|4*f<rsub|m>*h>. If <math|h\<gtr\>1/4>, use
      the separate output bounds; if the distance is zero, use the same
      estimate with a vanishing seminorm direction and let the parameter tend
      to zero. Thus <math|d\<leqslant\>1/256> and
      <math|s<rsub|m>=2*f<rsub|m>> imply <eqref|ch10:lipschitz> with room to
      spare. The finite-evaluation inequalities require the analytic
      estimates on the local expansion established in the next step.

      <item*|<em|Step 2.>><em|Parameter estimates from the local expansion.>
      <with|the-label|2|<label|ch10:parameter-supplier>>

      Here is the parameter estimate for the actual local factors. It uses
      the protected change of variables and real Gaussian majorants of
      Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>, retaining their fixed
      derivative order and support reserves. The incoming array is affine
      along the parameter line. The path, event indicators, held fine reads
      and coefficient projection are fixed. In particular the finite
      low-order collection in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch09:renormalization>
      is chosen on the compact inverse coupling band independently of
      incoming activity arrays. The estimates below therefore compare the
      same output construction on different lines. For the estimates one uses
      the explicit local expansion on the larger affine activity domain. Its
      polynomial products and convergent logarithmic series define the array
      and local scalar outputs there even when an extrapolated array has not
      been identified with a positive density. Positivity is required for
      actual histories, not for this algebraic extension of their local
      comparison formulas.

      Put <math|y=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>, <math|R=y<rsup|1/256>> and
      <math|\<delta\>=y<rsup|-1/128>>. The inverse coupling is a square of an
      affine function of the line parameter. The relative change on
      <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>6*R> is
      <math|O<around|(|R*y<rsup|-1/32>|)>=O<around|(|y<rsup|-7/256>|)>=o<around|(|\<delta\>|)>>.
      Choose a slightly smaller reference inverse coupling
      <math|\<beta\><rsub|0>> so that

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\><around|(|t|)>=\<beta\><rsub|0>*\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>,<space|1em>\<ell\><around|(|t|)>=a+c*t,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\>a-6*R<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>a+6*R<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>1+\<delta\>.
      </equation*>

      All these real inverse couplings remain in the reserved band.
      Compensation keeps each fine field read fixed: the Gaussian coordinate
      is rescaled by <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)>> while its fluctuation
      coefficient is divided by <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)>>. The
      cutoff-failure indicators and measurable incoming values are
      consequently not differentiated in this parameter.

      For a parameter series use the norm
      <math|<around|\||f|\|><rsub|R>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|\||f<rsub|n>|\|>*R<rsup|n>>.
      Its product estimate is the ordinary Cauchy-product inequality. A real
      local energy factor, including its coordinate prefactor, has the form

      <\equation*>
        J*<around|(|t,U|)>=A<around|(|U|)>*\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|n>*exp
        <around|{|<around|(|1-\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>|)>*\<Gamma\><around|(|U|)>|}>.
      </equation*>

      The energy <math|\<Gamma\>> can have either sign. It cannot be replaced
      by its absolute value before estimating the exponential. For
      <math|q=\<Gamma\><around|(|U|)>> the exponential series norm is at most
      <math|e<rsup|H<around|(|q|)>>>, where

      <\equation*>
        H<around|(|q|)>=<around|(|1-a<rsup|2>|)>*q+2*a*R*<around|\||c*q*<around|\||+R<rsup|2>*c<rsup|2>|\|>*q|\|>.
      </equation*>

      Choose the sign of <math|\<xi\>> so that <math|q*c*\<xi\>\<leqslant\>0>
      and take <math|3\<leqslant\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>4>. Since
      <math|a\<geqslant\>1+6*R<around|\||c|\|>>, direct expansion gives
      <math|H<around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1-\<ell\>*<around|(|R*\<xi\>|)><rsup|2>|)>*q>.
      After <math|j> coarse-field derivatives, factoring out
      <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|n>*e<rsup|<around|(|1-\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>|)>*q>>
      leaves a polynomial of degree at most <math|2*j>. Interpolate that
      polynomial at <math|2*j+1> distinct such real nodes. Their scaled
      Lagrange polynomials have a fixed coefficient norm. Thus real jet
      bounds <math|C*d<rsup|i>> at those nodes imply

      <\equation>
        <label|ch10:real-node-bound><around|\||D<rsup|i>*J|(>\<cdot\>,U)\|<rsub|R>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>*C*d<rsup|i>,<space|2em>i\<leqslant\>j.
      </equation>

      The node sign is chosen only for a pointwise bound and is never
      differentiated. The same argument applied to the Gaussian density ratio
      in <math|D> local real coordinates yields

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|D>*e<rsup|<around|(|1-\<ell\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>|)>*Q<around|(|z|)>>|\|><rsub|R>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|D>,<space|2em>Q<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>0.
      </equation*>

      Only coordinates read by the local term enter <math|D>.

      Small logarithmic factors require a different estimate. Their parameter
      dependence is polynomial of degree at most two; real values and
      derivatives of their exponential minus one have bound
      <math|v*d<rsup|i>> at <math|-1,0,1>. For <math|v\<leqslant\>1/2>,
      taking the real logarithm at these three nodes preserves a bound
      <math|C<rsub|j>*v*d<rsup|i>>. Polynomial interpolation costs
      <math|C<rsub|j>*R<rsup|2>>, and exponentiating in the coefficient
      algebra gives a bound <math|C<rsub|j>*R<rsup|2>*v*d<rsup|i>> when
      <math|C<rsub|j>*R<rsup|2>*v\<leqslant\>1>. Affine activities
      constrained to cover prescribed large-field sites have the same
      polynomial interpolation bound without taking a logarithm. These steps
      require no analyticity of the incoming activity as a function of the
      gauge field beyond the already imposed finite derivative order.

      For the primitive bound <math|v=y<rsup|-7/16+o<around|(|1|)>>>, this
      radius cost changes its exponent to
      <math|-7/16+2/256=-55/128\<less\>-2/5>. The local factor estimate
      <eqref|ch10:real-node-bound> retains its negative large-field energy:
      its additional <math|<around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n>> is absorbed into
      the fixed local factor cost. A local read set in support <math|S> has
      at most <math|C<rsub|L>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*C<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>>
      coordinates. Since <math|B> is polynomial in <math|log y>, its Gaussian
      prefactor satisfies, for any fixed <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> and
      sufficiently large <math|y>,

      <\equation*>
        <around|(|1+\<delta\>|)><rsup|C<rsub|L>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*C<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<eta\>*C<rsub|B><around|(|S|)>>.
      </equation*>

      This is absorbed by a support-exponent reserve, independently of the
      volume. The joint cutoff-failure probability and Gaussian score moments
      are those already used for the common majorant; Cauchy\USchwarz retains
      their cutoff-failure gain without assuming independent cutoff failures.

      We can therefore run the connected expansions of
      Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch08:chapter> in this coefficient norm. A
      derivative of order at most <math|j> of a product with <math|k> factors
      costs at most <math|k<rsup|j>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|j*k>>, absorbed by
      inflating each vertex bound by <math|2<rsup|j>>. Polynomial support
      moments are absorbed by another fixed support reserve. The first
      connected expansion has bound <math|y<rsup|-2/5+o<around|(|1|)>>>; the
      logarithmic expansion and spatial derivative weights consequently have
      bound <math|y<rsup|-3/8>> at radius <math|R/2>, after increasing the
      threshold for the starting scale. The low-order projection uses
      finitely many auxiliary volumes of side <math|O<around|(|B|)>> and
      supports coverable by a fixed number of <math|B>-balls. Its polynomial
      factors have exponent zero. Hence the actual raw projected regular
      family has finite-evaluation bound

      <\equation>
        <label|ch10:broad-test>\<cal-N\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*H<around|(|t<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/4>*sup<rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>*<around|(|2*t<rsub|i>/R|)><rsup|n>|\|>.
      </equation>

      Here <math|\<cal-N\>> is the full unnormalized regular derivative
      bound. The margin between <math|-3/8> and <math|-1/4> absorbs the
      projection factors. A cover number merely growing subpolynomially would
      not suffice inside an exponential weight; its uniform bound is used
      here.

      For completeness we explain the sharpening which makes this useful. At
      each real point, the stronger one-step remainder estimate gives

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-N\><around|(|H<around|(|t|)>|)>\<leqslant\>3*\<varepsilon\>*C<rsub|<math-up|st>>*x<rsup|-11/10>+O<around|(|x<rsup|-23/20+o<around|(|1|)>>|)>,<space|2em>x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>,
      </equation*>

      where the fixed response bound <math|\<varepsilon\>> can be made as
      small as desired before choosing the inverse coupling threshold. This
      is the small remainder estimate of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch09:renormalization>
      applied to this same collection. Let a finite parameter evaluation have
      moment norm <math|M<rsub|q>> at a fixed radius <math|q>. Interpolation
      at <math|k+1> fixed real nodes separates its moments through degree
      <math|k> from the remainder. The interpolating evaluation functional
      has total variation at most <math|U<rsub|k,q>*M<rsub|q>>; the remaining
      evaluation functional has moment norm at radius <math|v\<geqslant\>q>
      at most <math|<around|(|1+U<rsub|k,q>|)>*<around|(|q/v|)><rsup|k+1>*M<rsub|q>>.
      Applying the pointwise bound to the first part and
      <eqref|ch10:broad-test> to the second yields

      <\equation*>
        M<rsub|q>*<around|{|U<rsub|k,q>*sup<rsub|t>
        \<cal-N\><around|(|H<around|(|t|)>|)>+<around|(|1+U<rsub|k,q>|)>*<around|(|2*q/R|)><rsup|k+1>*y<rsup|-1/4>|}>.
      </equation*>

      Take <math|k=300>. The last power is <math|y<rsup|-365/256>>, strictly
      smaller than <math|y<rsup|-23/20>>. First choose <math|\<varepsilon\>>
      to absorb the fixed interpolation constant, then increase the inverse
      coupling threshold. The result is an arbitrarily small multiple of
      <math|x<rsup|-11/10>*M<rsub|q>>. The scalar projection costs the
      squared inverse field threshold; thus its bound has the form

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|m>=C*\<varepsilon\>*x<rsup|-11/10>/t<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em>t<around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|<around|(|log
        log x|)><rsup|2>>.
      </equation*>

      The reset is linear on finite parameter evaluations, and its fixed path
      contribution costs <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<around|(|x|)><rsup|3>|)>=o<around|(|x<rsup|-11/10>|)>>.

      Arrays constrained to cover prescribed large-field sites retain one
      compulsory large-field factor throughout fusion. Work with their
      coefficientwise majorants, not separately chosen value bounds. A raw
      hard bound <math|a<rsub|h>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-c*P<around|(|y|)><rsup|2>>>,
      where <math|P<around|(|y|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|log log y|)><rsup|2>>>, is
      negligible compared with any fixed fraction <math|\<phi\>> of
      <math|e<rsup|-P<around|(|x|)><rsup|1/5>>>. The soft correction has
      series bound <math|a<rsub|s>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/8>>. Put
      <math|p=y<rsup|1/16>> and <math|\<zeta\>=-log
      <around|(|\<phi\>*p|)>\<geqslant\>0>. Then
      <math|I=e<rsup|\<zeta\>>*a<rsub|h>+2*a<rsub|s>\<leqslant\><around|(|8*p|)><rsup|-1>+2*a<rsub|s>>
      eventually, the support ball count times <math|I> tends to zero, and

      <\equation*>
        e<rsup|-\<zeta\>>*<around|(|e<rsup|I>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>*<around|(|1/4+4*p*a<rsub|s>|)>\<leqslant\>\<phi\>.
      </equation*>

      This is exactly the connected fusion estimate with the hard factor
      retained once. Radius reduction by a fixed factor converts the
      finite-evaluation coefficient bounds into one summable majorant at
      every support. Eligibility indicators are parameter independent and
      preserve it. The result proves both inequalities
      <eqref|ch10:finite-tests>, with arbitrarily small fixed <math|d>, for
      the protected local construction. The inverse coupling bound above
      gives <math|s<rsub|m>=2*f<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*x<rsup|-1/10>>
      needed for cumulative control.

      This argument uses the real-node bounds for the same protected local
      sectors on the reserved inverse coupling band. Those bounds follow by
      applying the real estimates of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>
      with their constants fixed on that band; the rescaling keeps all fine
      reads and event indicators fixed. It adds a parameter estimate to that
      construction, not a differentiation of measurable cutoff indicators.

      <item*|<em|Step 3.>><em|Summation over scales.>

      The cumulative scalar bound is compatible with the schedule. Suppose at
      a fixed stage its bound has the proved form
      <math|s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*x<rsup|-1/10>>, where
      <math|x> is the reference inverse coupling. For the stage increment
      <math|a=\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>
      and <math|x\<geqslant\>a>,

      <\equation*>
        <around|(|x+a|)><rsup|31/32>-x<rsup|31/32>\<geqslant\><tfrac|31|32>2<rsup|-1/32>*a*x<rsup|-1/32>.
      </equation*>

      Since <math|x<rsup|-1/10>=o<around|(|x<rsup|-1/32>|)>>, a threshold for
      the starting scale ensures <math|s<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|(|x+a|)><rsup|31/32>-x<rsup|31/32>|)>/128>.
      Summation gives the required bound by telescoping, including all
      initial scales once <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> exceeds the first
      threshold. The decay estimate for <math|s<around|(|x|)>> is required
      before this numerical argument; an arbitrary stage constant would not
      suffice.

      <item*|<em|Step 4.>><em|Continuity for a common input.>
      <with|the-label|4|<label|ch10:cutoff-supplier>>

      Use now the quadratic and path coefficient limits with the strict width
      reserve established in Section<nbsp><reference|ch10:path-stability>. We
      verify the cutoff-comparison part of
      Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch10:stability-assumption> on the actual
      allowed inputs. All assertions in this paragraph are at one fixed scale
      and period; constants need not be uniform in the scale.

      For a finite collection of elementary labels and bounded path lengths,
      the fine-coordinate dimension is finite. Write each integral in the
      same physical coordinates at the two cutoffs. Convergence of the path
      coefficients with a width reserve gives convergence of its finitely
      many required derivatives, uniformly on the smaller local chart. The
      inverse identity <math|H<rsub|N><rsup|-1>-H<rsup|-1>=H<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<around|(|H-H<rsub|N>|)>*H<rsup|-1>>,
      the common positive Hessian bound and stationary uniqueness give
      convergence of means, precisions and stationary jets. Differentiated
      Gaussian densities are their ordinary polynomial scores. On a
      sufficiently late tail their precisions have a common smaller positive
      lower bound, so a fixed Gaussian exponential times a polynomial
      dominates them. The protected change of variables leaves measurable
      fine reads fixed. Thus the same input activity discrepancy is bounded
      in its prescribed norm; no derivative of a measurable indicator is
      introduced.

      Use the physical fine coordinates also for the event indicators. At a
      fixed level their threshold is determined by the fixed reference
      inverse coupling, and the cutoff-failure catalogue, support dictionary
      and clipping rule are independent of the terminal index. Thus the
      actual event indicator is the same measurable function in both
      integrals. Although its expression in a centered Gaussian variable
      would move with the mean or precision, no such moving expression is
      compared here. The local change of density includes its Jacobian and
      leaves that physical event indicator fixed. The coarse small-field
      event indicators are likewise identical. This avoids any uniform
      assertion about the measure of a moving threshold surface.

      To obtain the suprema in the output derivative bound, choose the
      protected Gaussian and chart estimates through one order beyond the
      outgoing order. This is a numerical derivative reserve for the
      integration kernels, and does not require one additional derivative of
      an incoming activity. Indeed in each differentiated integral the
      protected coordinate change holds its entire fine activity read fixed.
      An additional coarse increment differentiates the smooth density, chart
      and Jacobian scores, while the old measurable factors remain unchanged.
      The bounds of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> apply at every
      fixed requested score order; increase that order before selecting the
      stage constants. Direct path terms and the finite local projection are
      smooth and satisfy the same extra order by their exponential
      coefficient bounds. Thus this reserve is available also on a plateau
      with equal incoming and outgoing activity derivative orders. On each
      common small-field chart it gives equicontinuity of the finite
      collection of output derivatives. A finite net on the compact coarse
      configuration domain and dominated convergence then give uniform
      convergence there. The estimates extend to its closure from the
      protected neighborhood; multiplication by the same small-field event
      indicator preserves the resulting supremum bound.

      Next remove the finite-label restriction. Long paths have uniformly
      small tails by the strict exponential width reserve. Large connected
      supports and high cluster multiplicities have uniformly small tails by
      the support and attachment reserves of the polymer estimates. Remaining
      multiplicities inherited from the input arrays are treated by the
      Cauchy tail argument at the end of this chapter: a fixed late array has
      a summable value tail, and the activity-norm discrepancy bounds the
      tail of every later array. In a protected derivative the input factors
      are kept at fixed fine reads, so these are value tails; the
      differentiations fall on the controlled Gaussian and chart factors. If
      an input derivative is explicitly present, its difference is already
      controlled by the corresponding component of the input norm. First
      truncate with these uniform errors, then compare the finite integrals.
      This proves convergence in the output norm.

      The projected scalar is a finite linear functional of the same
      converging local derivatives and therefore also converges. Local
      subtraction preserves this conclusion by linearity; fusion preserves it
      by its convergent product expansion with a strict attachment margin.
      The finite auxiliary projection uses the same fields and periods at
      both cutoffs. Thus this comparison concerns the actual returned arrays
      and <math|Q<rsub|m,N>>, not some other representation of their
      densities.

      The same proof applies when the terminal activity is zero and the
      inverse couplings below it are prescribed in advance. At the initial
      auxiliary level the density is the positive bare path exponential, and
      all activity bounds are zero. After each step its activity array has
      the small bound proved above. Replacing the returned inverse coupling
      by the next prescribed inverse coupling multiplies the represented
      density by a strictly positive path exponential; its activity gas is
      unchanged. Therefore positivity is retained, and the next step is an
      allowed input to Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch09:step-theorem>. All
      stationary, support and cutoff conditions depend on the common path and
      reference band, which have not changed. In particular this induction
      supplies precisely the histories with prescribed inverse couplings used
      in the convergence proof; it does not assume that every abstract
      element of a completed norm ball represents a positive density.

      The quadratic limit of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:horizon-limit>,
      the affine coefficient comparison and the preceding local-integral
      argument give the common-input continuity required here, including all
      zero-array histories with prescribed inverse couplings. Together with
      the finite-evaluation estimates and cumulative bound, this proves
      Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch10:stability-assumption>.
    </enumerate>
  </render-proof>

  <section|Convergence of the coupled histories><label|ch10:fixed-scale>

  Recall the schedule from Section<nbsp><reference|ch10:schedule>:
  <math|J<around|(|m|)>> is the stage at scale <math|m>,
  <math|L<rsub|m>=<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|J*<around|(|m+1|)>>> is the
  block ratio, and <math|\<tau\><rsub|m>=log a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>. The
  reference inverse coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>>
  is distinct from the tuned actual inverse coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|m>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Fixed-scale convergence><label|ch10:convergence>Use the common
    construction above with its strengthened comparison reserves and fixed
    coefficient functionals. If the coarse endpoints converge to <math|b>,
    then for every fixed <math|m,p> their inverse couplings and activity
    arrays converge along the entire cutoff sequence. Their scalar
    corrections <math|q<rsub|m>> converge as well. The limits depend on
    <math|b> and the fixed common construction, and not on the choices made
    in shooting. No convergence of additive vacuum normalizations is
    asserted.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First prescribe two inverse coupling sequences having the same limit at
    each fixed level. At a level <math|k\<gtr\>m> replace the activity array
    by zero and run the same maps downwards using the prescribed inverse
    coupling sequence, without imposing the scalar recursion on this
    auxiliary history. The hypothesis on its domain is needed here. Repeated
    use of <eqref|ch10:lipschitz> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|ch10:activity-truncation><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>-x<rsub|m><rsup|<around|[|k|]>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|1/16|)><rsup|k-m>.
    </equation>

    For fixed <math|k>, the truncated arrays converge by finite downward
    induction and the common-input cutoff comparison. This statement is
    Cauchy convergence: apply that comparison to arbitrary pairs of
    subsequences. Then choose <math|k> so the two errors in
    <eqref|ch10:activity-truncation> are small. Completeness gives a common
    activity limit for both prescribed inverse coupling sequences, and common
    scalar correction limits follow from the same local comparison.

    At each fixed scale the inverse coupling coordinate lies in a compact
    real interval. A diagonal subsequence therefore makes all inverse
    coupling coordinates converge; the preceding paragraph supplies activity
    limits without compactness of their norm balls. Compare two such limiting
    histories. The finite scalar recursion, summed from their common bottom
    endpoint, gives

    <\equation>
      <label|ch10:prefix>b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|m>|)>-b<rsub|m><around|(|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|)>=-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><around|(|q<rsub|i>-q<rprime|'><rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation>

    The limits of the common path corrections <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>>
    cancel. There is no infinite sum or ultraviolet boundary term.

    The estimates <eqref|ch10:lipschitz> pass to the two limits. To justify
    this without extending a map arbitrarily to the completed space, at each
    finite cutoff run the second limiting inverse coupling sequence with a
    zero terminal array. The first paragraph identifies its limit with the
    second history. Compare it with the first history at the same cutoff and
    pass to the limit. Put <math|d<rsub|m>=max
    <around|{|<around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>-x<rprime|'><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>|}>>.
    The activity discrepancy is at most <math|1/4>. By
    <eqref|ch10:inverse-coordinate>, <eqref|ch10:prefix>, and the individual
    scalar bounds, its inverse coupling discrepancy is at most
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>s<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/128>.
    Hence <math|D=sup<rsub|m> d<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/4>. The sharper scalar
    comparison now gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|m>-\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*\<frak-b\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>s<rsub|i>*D\<leqslant\>D/32,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m>-x<rprime|'><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D/16.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|D\<leqslant\>D/16> and <math|D=0>. Every subsequence has a
    further subsequence with these same limits. A subsequence staying a
    positive distance away at one fixed level would contradict this fact, so
    the whole sequence converges. Finally <math|b<rsub|m>> is continuous,
    giving the asserted convergence of the physical inverse couplings.
  </proof>

  <section|Identifying the analytic comparison on the selected
  family><label|ch10:identification>

  For clarity we record what the additional input requires of the local
  integral, so it can be checked independently of the preceding abstract
  argument. The finite path comparisons must use the same fixed coefficient
  functionals and rooted estimates as the actual history. Replacing a
  terminal Wilson normalization must leave every fixed lower-scale
  construction unchanged eventually, or have an explicitly vanishing
  comparison error. A new selection of equally bounded coefficients at each
  occurrence would not suffice.

  For a finite set of activity labels, write both local integrals in common
  physical coordinates. Converging means and positive precisions, with their
  uniform inverse and derivative bounds, give convergence against a common
  Gaussian majorant. The protected change of variables keeps the physical
  fine event indicators fixed, including their reference thresholds, and
  retains its common Jacobian. The common majorant of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> concerns the actual source and
  event indicator; it cannot be replaced by a bound for an unrelated flat
  Gaussian. The projected scalar coefficient also needs convergence in its
  local derivative norm. Convergence of the gas alone does not identify it.

  The infinite dictionaries require a separate tail argument. For an input
  array <math|f>, let <math|b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>> be the supremum of the
  absolute actual activity, including its event indicator. On a fixed finite
  torus the activity bounds give <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>>.
  A Cauchy sequence has uniformly small value tails: compare with one late
  fixed member, truncate that member's summable tail, and use
  <math|b<rsub|f><around|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|f-g><around|(|i|)>+b<rsub|g><around|(|i|)>>.
  This does not assert density of finite arrays in every derivative norm.
  Output derivative tails require the stronger support reserve in the
  connected expansion. The local projection is finite and the fusion sum has
  a strict convergence margin, so these estimates, when proved on the common
  inputs, survive both operations.

  The auxiliary zero-array histories used in the proof require the same
  checks. Their incoming activity bounds vanish, but their path, stationary
  branch, cutoff geometry and differentiated Gaussian factors do not vanish.
  One must use the same block bridge, clipping data and stationary graph.
  Uniform implicit-function uniqueness identifies a auxiliary graph built
  from the same repeated block steps with the selected one; merely invoking
  the existence of some admissible stationary graph is insufficient.

  The schedule, finite-history tuning and logarithmic running follow from the
  finite-step theorem. The fixed-scale conclusion is
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch10:convergence>.
  Steps<nbsp><reference|ch10:parameter-supplier> and
  <reference|ch10:cutoff-supplier> in the proof of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch10:stability-assumption> establish the
  stronger local comparisons from the protected expansion and converging
  background coefficients. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch05:horizon-limit>
  supplies the fixed-scale quadratic limit for the actual nested history.
  This completes the fixed-scale construction, which remains distinct from
  subsequent convergence of all continuum correlations.

  <assign|chapter-nr|10>

  <chapter|Marked insertions and finite-scale
  comparison><label|ch11:insertions>

  A block transformation acts linearly on an inserted density even though its
  effective-action coordinates evolve nonlinearly. Keeping the density itself
  in view resolves two difficulties: several insertions generate joint terms,
  and an auxiliary sequence of block kernels can preserve all original
  correlation integrals without preserving their intermediate densities. This
  chapter establishes that finite-step calculus and the local comparison
  estimates it needs. Statements about a limiting quantum field require
  further uniform estimates; they are not consequences of the source algebra
  alone.

  Throughout, the polynomial label space and its local section are
  <math|V<rsub|d>> and <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> from
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch02:observables>. All block kernels are the
  normalized kernels of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>. A history
  means one fixed choice of these kernels, inverse couplings, adjacent path
  pairs, and local representations. The estimates of
  Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> and
  <reference|ch09:renormalization> are used only on that same history.

  The argument has four parts. First the squarefree source algebra and
  support estimates retain every joint insertion term. Next the
  single-insertion estimates and the common Gaussian comparison construct the
  restarted history and the normalized product-moment bounds. Backward
  comparison then relates local trials to the actual renormalized fields and
  microscopic equations. Finally a common localization onto the polynomial
  label space and a larger source weight give the bound from a finite
  separating family, from which uniform finite-difference bounds follow.

  <section|An algebra for finitely many insertions>

  Let <math|\<cal-I\>> be a finite set of insertion labels and define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|\<cal-I\>>=<CC><around|[|z<rsub|i>:i\<in\>\<cal-I\>|]>/<around|(|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>:i\<in\>\<cal-I\>|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|S>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>z<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  The variables commute; a monomial with a repeated label is zero. Every
  element has the unique form <math|<big|sum><rsub|S\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\>>a<rsub|S>*z<rsub|S>>.
  Its positive-degree ideal is nilpotent of order
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>+1>, so exponential and logarithm series on
  this ideal are finite. For positive weights <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>>, put

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<omega\>>=<big|sum><rsub|S\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\>><around|\||a<rsub|S>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>\<omega\><rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  This norm is submultiplicative by the disjoint-subset convolution formula.
  The weights rescale source variables; they are not probabilities.

  For an element <math|M> with <math|M<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1>,

  <\equation>
    <around|[|z<rsub|S>|]>*log M=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|S|)>><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1>*<around|(|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>-1|)>!<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>M<rsub|I>.<label|ch11:cumulants>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-P\><around|(|S|)>> denotes the set of partitions into
  nonempty blocks. This is the standard moment\Ucumulant identity; we use the
  classical cumulant calculus of Leonov and
  Shiryaev<nbsp><cite|LeonovShiryaev1959>, rather than develop a second
  connected-expansion formalism. Nilpotence makes the formula an algebraic
  identity even when there is no positive probability law behind the
  coefficients. On bounded coefficient sets, <math|log> is Lipschitz with a
  constant depending only on <math|<around|\||\<cal-I\>|\|>> and the
  coefficient bound: expand the finite sum and telescope each product.

  <subsection|The exact density and its integral>

  At cutoff <math|N>, let <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0> be the unmarked
  density with <math|Z<rsub|N>=<big|int>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|N>\<gtr\>0>,
  where <math|\<lambda\><rsub|N>> is product Haar probability measure. For
  physical insertions <math|F<rsub|i>>, assume every
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>F<rsub|i>> is integrable and
  set

  <\equation>
    q<rsub|N>*<around|(|z,U|)>=\<rho\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>><around|(|1+z<rsub|i>*F<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|m>=\<cal-B\><rsub|m>*q<rsub|m+1>.<label|ch11:source-density>
  </equation>

  The block map acts coefficientwise. Consequently
  <math|Z<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<big|int><around|[|z<rsub|S>|]>*q<rsub|m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|m>>
  is the original cutoff moment of the insertions indexed by <math|S>.

  If one instead uses the dimensionless one-insertion density of degree
  <math|d>, its recursion is <math|H<rsub|m>=L<rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<cal-B\><rsub|m>*H<rsub|m+1>>.
  The scalar rescaling belongs to this convention; it is absent from
  <eqref|ch11:source-density>, where the physical normalization is already in
  <math|F<rsub|i>>. Mixing these two conventions changes the unit insertion.
  In particular the reference-level coefficient of the physical unit in a
  degree-<math|d> convention is <math|a<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>,
  not the coefficient obtained from the individual scaling dimension of
  <math|e<rsub|0>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Changing the block history><label|ch11:mass>Replace any kernels
    below a fixed level <math|k> by other normalized positive block kernels.
    Push the complete polynomial <math|q<rsub|k>> through them. Every
    coefficient integral is unchanged. A field-independent polynomial
    <math|J\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<cal-I\>>> commutes with every push; if
    <math|J<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1>, the unmarked density and its partition
    integral are unchanged.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For any integrable coefficient <math|h>, normalization and Fubini give
    <math|<big|int>\<cal-B\>*h*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>=<big|int>h*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\>*h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
    Iteration proves the first assertion. Multiplication by <math|J> is a
    fixed linear map of the finite coefficient space, so it commutes with the
    integral. Its constant coefficient proves the last assertion.
  </proof>

  There is also a conditional-expectation interpretation. Draw the terminal
  field with law <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|N>/Z<rsub|N>>
  and then follow the selected block kernels. The conditional expectation of
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>F<rsub|i>> given the level-<math|m> field is
  <math|<around|[|z<rsub|S>|]>*q<rsub|m>/<around|[|z<rsub|\<varnothing\>>|]>*q<rsub|m>>
  almost everywhere where the denominator is positive. This follows by
  testing against a bounded function of the level-<math|m> field. Its value
  on the zero-density set is immaterial. Two histories generally have
  different conditional laws; the proposition asserts equality of their
  integrated moments.

  <section|Joint local terms and large-field components>

  At an intermediate level a local representation has the form

  <\equation>
    e<rsup|\<Phi\><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|S\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>z<rsub|S>*h<rsub|S><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-F\><math-up|eligible>><big|prod><rsub|Y\<in\>\<cal-F\>><around*|(|K<rsub|Y><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|S\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>z<rsub|S>*k<rsub|Y,S><around|(|U|)>|)>.<label|ch11:local-source>
  </equation>

  Each regular term carries its required small-field restriction; each
  large-field term retains its original eligibility and large-field
  conditions. An index <math|S> may contain several insertion labels. Such
  terms are generated by integration even if the terminal regular source has
  only terms linear in the sources. The full dependence region includes the
  insertion stencils, event indicators, paths, and Gaussian windows. It need
  not coincide with the exclusion support of a large-field activity.

  One must never divide a marked coefficient by <math|K<rsub|Y>>: that
  activity may vanish. For example, two complementary insertion factors on a
  selected large-field support contribute

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|Y>*<around|(|1+z<rsub|1>*b<rsub|1>|)>*<around|(|1+z<rsub|2>*b<rsub|2>|)>=K<rsub|Y>+z<rsub|1>*K<rsub|Y>*b<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>*K<rsub|Y>*b<rsub|2>+z<rsub|1>*z<rsub|2>*K<rsub|Y>*b<rsub|1>*b<rsub|2>.
  </equation*>

  The joint coefficient contains the original activity once.

  More generally expand the terminal product into its small-field parts and
  complementary parts. Join each complementary stencil to every selected
  large-field support it meets, and join intersecting complementary stencils.
  Exact large-field indicator coverage ensures that a complementary stencil
  containing a failed cutoff condition meets at least one selected support.
  Merge each resulting component, retaining the constituent indices, original
  eligibility criteria, and union of their prescribed sets of large-field
  sites. Its value is the product of those constituents, with each selected
  activity included once. Expanding the constituent indices recovers the
  original term uniquely; hence this is an exact coefficient identity. This
  algebraic construction gives all joint terminal large-field terms,
  including at zeros of the original activities.

  For a bound uniform in the number of inserted points, assume at most one
  insertion anchor per site and a fixed finite menu of stencil radii and
  degrees. Then a site belongs to only a bounded number of insertion
  stencils. To apply the component tree estimate of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch08:chapter> to the union of the stencils, use a
  larger support exponent in the bounds on the individual factors. At a
  Wilson terminal scale every new component contains an original large-field
  support, whose suppression is <math|exp
  <around|(|-c*\<beta\><rsub|N>*t<rsub|N><rsup|2>*n|)>>. Reserve half this
  exponent before summing attachments. A polynomial in
  <math|B<rsub|N>,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>> times the remaining suppression
  tends to zero faster than every inverse power. This proves small joint
  terminal bounds when the weighted insertion complements themselves have a
  summable site bound. That latter bound is a quantitative local-section
  input; it is not implied by the finite polynomial identity.

  <section|Support bounds for several marked components>

  Fix <math|n> insertion labels in each reflected replica cell. Suppose every
  elementary principal term has at least one marked point, contains its
  marked points in its full dependence region, and that region is within
  distance <math|\<rho\>> of each such point. Suppose marked points in
  different cells are separated by more than <math|2*\<rho\>>. A component
  formed by intersecting these full regions then lies in one cell. Indeed
  marks on adjacent terms have distance at most <math|2*\<rho\>>, so they
  belong to the same cell. Connect the distinct labels used by the component.
  Erasing cycles gives a path of at most <math|n-1> edges. All its marked
  points are therefore within <math|2*n*\<rho\>> of one another, and every
  point of its full region is within <math|<around|(|2*n+1|)>*\<rho\>> of
  each marked point. This bound is independent of the number of elementary
  terms in the component.

  A second estimate controls increasing the source weights. Let each
  component carry a nonempty label set <math|S>, full region <math|X>, and
  let <math|t<around|(|S|)>> be the number of replica cells represented in
  <math|S>. Suppose at most <math|n> labels occur per cell. Increase the
  common source weight from <math|\<omega\><rsub|->> to
  <math|\<omega\>=H<rsup|U>*\<omega\><rsub|->>. For components within one
  cell the cost is at most <math|H<rsup|U*n>>. For components meeting several
  cells, retain an additional exponent in the support weight
  <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>. If

  <\equation>
    \<sigma\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>U*n*<space|0.17em>t<around|(|S|)>*log
    H,<label|ch11:spare-support>
  </equation>

  then the increased label weight and decreased support weight give

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|U<around|\||S|\|>>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>1.
  </equation*>

  Consequently a marked-point incidence bound <math|p<rsub|1>> for
  single-cell terms at exponent <math|e>, and <math|p<rsub|2>> for other
  terms at exponent <math|e+\<sigma\>>, give the bound
  <math|H<rsup|U*n>*p<rsub|1>+p<rsub|2>> at exponent <math|e>.

  The geometric lower bound <eqref|ch11:spare-support> and the stronger
  incoming norm are both required. An estimate proved only at exponent
  <math|e> cannot be reused at <math|e+\<sigma\>>. Large-field components
  require their own joint attachment estimate because one such component may
  carry marks from many cells. The suppression reserved above, together with
  the stronger support norm, absorbs this additional weight. The support
  exponent is therefore chosen before the marked expansion and before the
  history thresholds are selected.

  <section|One marked step with the actual local
  factors><label|ch11:marked-step>

  We give quantitative bounds before applying the Gaussian comparison. This
  also specifies the support reserve needed when the number of inserted
  points grows with the volume. Fix an output exponent <math|e\<geqslant\>0>,
  put <math|v=e+5>, and let <math|B<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|c>> be the
  adjacent support radii. For the prescribed enlargement
  <math|X<rsup|\<uparrow\>>> of a fine support, the covering argument of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch06:primitive-bounds> gives

  <\equation*>
    L*c<rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|X<rsup|\<uparrow\>>|)>\<leqslant\>15<rsup|4>*<around|{|L+10*c<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently, if <math|10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e+7|)>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|f>>,

  <\equation>
    e<rsup|<around|(|v+2|)>*c<rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|X<rsup|\<uparrow\>>|)>>\<leqslant\>T<rsub|e>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|f>*c<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>,<space|2em>T<rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e+7|)>|)>.<label|ch11:cover-transfer>
  </equation>

  The numerical constant describes this choice of support enlargement; the
  essential feature is its fixed additive cost and <math|L<rsup|-1>> fine
  cover cost.

  In one sector and tag, distinguish three kinds of factors: unmarked local
  corrections, incoming large-field factors, and insertion factors with
  nonempty sets of labels. Their full dependence regions determine the
  intersection graph. The exact prescribed set of large-field sites condition
  says that every selected incoming prescribed large-field set is supplied by
  the unmarked local correction factors in its component. In particular a
  component with no unmarked correction has no incoming large-field factor.
  This is a condition on the exact cover decomposition, retained when marked
  factors are attached, rather than a consequence of spatial intersection
  alone.

  Let <math|\<delta\>> be the containing-site bound for unmarked correction
  amplitudes. Suppose the incoming large-field amplitudes, already weighted
  by <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|f>*c<rsub|B<rsub|f>>>>, have site bound
  <math|\<eta\>>. Let <math|a> bound the insertion incidence sum at a fixed
  mark after <eqref|ch11:cover-transfer>, at exponent <math|v+2>. If at most
  <math|g> marks project to a coarse site, set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-W\><around|(|B|)>=<around|(|33*B|)><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<geqslant\>0><around|(|j+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-j>,<space|2em>\<Sigma\>=\<delta\>+\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*L<rsup|4>*T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|c>|)>*g*a.
  </equation*>

  The insertion and background incidence bound at a specified mark is
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a>; no <math|L<rsup|4>> occurs there. The site
  bound is <math|\<Sigma\>> at exponent <math|v+1>. To see the latter
  conversion, root a support at one of its actual fine sites or marks. Its
  covering number is at least <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|d*<around|(|o,x|)>/<around|(|8*B<rsub|c>|)>|\<rfloor\>>>.
  The number of possible roots in shell <math|j> is at most
  <math|<around|(|33*B<rsub|c>|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|j+1|)><rsup|4>>, while
  losing one support exponent provides <math|e<rsup|-j>>. Background roots
  have <math|L<rsup|4>> possible fine residues; mark roots have at most
  <math|g> choices.

  Choose inflation factors <math|m,l\<geqslant\>1>. The common local integral
  bound of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> may be applied with the
  unmarked amplitudes inflated by <math|m> for factors with a nonempty
  prescribed set of large-field sites, and by <math|l> for other corrections.
  Smallness remains available by increasing the inverse coupling threshold at
  fixed <math|m,l>. A nonleading insertion factor is either pointwise at most
  its bounded amplitude divided by <math|l>, or supported on an event of
  actual conditional probability at most <math|l<rsup|-1>> and bounded by
  that amplitude. For a compatible connected collection <math|C>, therefore,

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t<rsub|C><around*|\||<big|int><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>C>F<rsub|j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>C>b<rsub|j>,>>|<row|<cell|t<rsub|C>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|m,>|<cell|<text|some
    factor prescribes large-field sites>,>>|<row|<cell|l,>|<cell|<text|no
    sites are prescribed and some factor is
    nonprincipal>,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|<text|purely
    principal>.>>>>><eq-number><label|ch11:integrated-component>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|\<nu\>> is the single actual Gaussian/Haar law of the sector. If
  an unmarked correction occurs, its inflated common product bound absorbs
  <math|t<rsub|C>>, while the other factors are bounded pointwise. If none
  occurs, the prescribed set of large-field sites condition removes incoming
  large-field factors; use either the pointwise or event bound for one
  nonleading insertion. No independence is used in either case.

  Assume <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|c>+1|)><rsup|4>*\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>1>.
  The rooted-tree bound gives connected component site sum at most
  <math|\<Sigma\>> at exponent <math|v>, and marked-point incidence at most
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a>. Indeed let <math|c<rsub|i>=c<rsub|B<rsub|c>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>,
  <math|a<rsub|i>=b<rsub|i>*e<rsup|v*c<rsub|i>>> and
  <math|<wide|b|^><rsub|i>=b<rsub|i>*e<rsup|<around|(|v+1|)>*c<rsub|i>>>. The
  sum of <math|<wide|b|^><rsub|j>> over regions meeting <math|X<rsub|i>> is
  at most <math|<around|\||X<rsub|i>|\|>*\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>.
  Iterating <math|R<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=a<rsub|i>> and
  <math|R<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|q+1|)>>=a<rsub|i>*exp
  <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|j:X<rsub|j>\<cap\>X<rsub|i>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>R<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|q|)>>|)>>
  gives <math|R<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|q|)>>\<leqslant\><wide|b|^><rsub|i>>.
  Every finite connected collection has a spanning tree and is included in
  this upper bound. This is the rooted-tree majorization underlying the
  polymer criterion of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch08:chapter>; it is applied
  here with the full marked regions, rather than proved anew for each subset
  of marks.

  The split of an incoming principal factor <math|W*z<rsub|S>> by a local
  cutoff is exact:

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|1+\<gamma\>*W*z<rsub|S>|)>*<around|(|1+<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)>*W*z<rsub|S>|)>=1+W*z<rsub|S>.
  </equation*>

  The second term is nonleading and the actual warning estimate gives its
  supporting event probability at most <math|x<rsup|-1>>. Thus
  <math|l\<leqslant\>x> absorbs that term. If principal and arbitrary
  remainder incidence bounds before enlargement are <math|h> and <math|z>,
  respectively, one may take

  <\equation>
    a=2*T<rsub|e>*h+l*T<rsub|e>*z.<label|ch11:marked-incidence>
  </equation>

  Both pieces retain the same incoming event indicator and dependence region.
  Purely principal connected components have the one-cell support bound
  proved earlier. Their actual integral is the joint moment in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:joint-gaussian>, since it contains neither
  an unmarked correction nor an incoming large-field factor.

  Finally normalize the unmarked local gas by the same exact connected
  expansion as in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch09:renormalization>. If its local
  normalization corrections have total site bound <math|k> at exponent
  <math|v> after the <math|l> inflation, choose an output bound
  <math|\<psi\>> with <math|e<rsup|\<Sigma\>>-1+k\<leqslant\>\<psi\>>. The
  regular nonleading amplitudes are then bounded by <math|l>, the selected
  components by <math|m>, and the marked-point incidence by
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a>. The empty-label corrections contribute zero to
  that incidence. In particular output incidence at most one follows from
  <math|T<rsub|e>*\<eta\>+a\<leqslant\>1>; it is not an extra independence or
  finite-arity assumption. The estimates hold for every finite label set with
  the stated occupancy <math|g>, uniformly in the number of replica cells.

  <subsection|The one-insertion graph estimates><label|ch11:one-graph>

  Fix a degree <math|d> with coarse derivative order at least <math|max
  <around|(|2,d+1|)>>, fine spatial weight at least <math|d>, fine kernel
  order at least <math|d+2>, and coarse kernel level at least <math|d+3> for
  the simultaneous calculation for normalized local insertions and the
  regular and large-field remainders. Assume the period is at least
  <math|100*<around|(|1+B<rsub|c>|)>>. The fixed normalized local insertion
  is tested with one additional derivative beyond each requested derivative
  used in a coefficient evaluation; this requirement does not impose an
  additional derivative on an arbitrary incoming activity.

  Write <math|\<kappa\><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>
  for the square root of the actual large-field bound at level <math|i>. The
  remainder norm used here is the sum of the anchored, support-weighted
  regular majorants and <math|\<kappa\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>> times the anchored,
  support-weighted large-field majorants, with their original
  support\Uprescribed set of large-field sites indices. Thus the fine and
  coarse remainder norms use <math|\<kappa\><rsub|f>> and
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|c>>, respectively, not a common value.

  For this degree write <math|w<rsub|i>> and <math|f<rsub|i>> for the scaling
  dimension and curvature-factor count of a homogeneous basis vector. The
  leading rescaling is <math|<around|(|S<rsub|L>*x|)><rsub|i>=L<rsup|d-w<rsub|i>>*x<rsub|i>>,
  so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|L><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Localization by Taylor expansion of the fine local section on the free
  interpolated coarse jet gives a triangular change of basis <math|T> with
  diagonal <math|<around|(|t<rsub|c>/t<rsub|f>|)><rsup|f<rsub|i>>>. Its
  entries and inverse are bounded at fixed degree and stage: the
  differentiated interpolation kernel has bound
  <math|C<rsub|d,r>*L<rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>> for
  <math|r\<leqslant\>d>, summable against the polynomial growth of a radial
  jet. Thus the leading map is <math|T*S<rsub|L>>.

  Let <math|G> be an incoming linear graph of regular vanishing-jet and
  large-field remainders, with norm at most <math|s<rsub|f>> per coefficient.
  The actual marked step gives a linear coefficient map <math|M<rsub|G>> and
  a raw remainder map <math|R<rsub|G>>. For a common error bound
  <math|\<epsilon\>> above the preselection bound
  <math|C<rsub|d>/L+o<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>, which may be chosen as
  <math|L<rsup|-2/5>> after the stage choices, it satisfies

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||M<rsub|G>*x-T*S<rsub|L>*x|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*<around|(|<around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|>+<around|\<\|\|\>|G*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|G>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>\|S<rsub|L>*x<rsup|\<circ\>>\|+\<epsilon\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|G*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>.<eq-number><label|ch11:graph-estimates>>>>>
  </align>

  The circle deletes the unit coordinate. Finite symmetry averaging only
  enlarges <math|C<rsub|d>,\<epsilon\>> by fixed degree-dependent constants.

  Here is the smallness argument for the remainder, including large supports.
  Root the connected expansion at its unique marked factor; the remaining
  factors have the common bound already established. For a regular remainder
  on support <math|X>, put <math|r=c<rsub|B<rsub|f>><around|(|X|)>>. If its
  coarse cover exceeds <math|B<rsub|c>>, one retained unit of exponential
  support decay gives <math|E<rsub|L><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>|)>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|c>/2>>,
  where <math|E<rsub|L><around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*x<rsup|a<rsub|L>>>
  includes the Gaussian-scale costs and fixed derivative polynomials. This
  still tends to zero because <math|B<rsub|c>\<asymp\><around|(|log
  x|)><rsup|2>>. Otherwise distinguish <math|r<rsup|3>\<less\>L> and
  <math|r<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>L>. In the first case the support fits in the
  fixed centered nonwrapping lift. Vanishing radial holonomy derivatives
  imply cancellation of screened polynomial link moments: if direction
  <math|v<rsub|j>> is polynomial of degree at most <math|a<rsub|j>-1> on the
  tested links and <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>d>, then
  <math|D<rsup|k>*<around|(|R\<circ\>exp|)><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|k>|]>=0>.
  To see this, write the local analytic coordinates as <math|log
  <around|(|e<rsup|q<around|(|x|)>>*H<around|(|J|)><rsub|e>*e<rsup|-q*<around|(|x+e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>>|)>>,
  with a site gauge polynomial <math|q> and radial coefficients <math|J>.
  Their differential maps weight-<math|a> parameters onto polynomial link
  fields of degree at most <math|a-1>: decompose a homogeneous polynomial
  one-form into its radial part and the gradient of
  <math|<around|(|x\<cdot\>A<around|(|x|)>|)>/<around|(|a|)>>, then correct
  the lower-degree link-average error. Higher derivatives preserve the summed
  filtration weight. Gauge invariance removes <math|q>; polarization of the
  vanishing holonomy derivatives and induction in the chain rule remove all
  partitions except the desired derivative. Locality permits screening to the
  tested links, always in the same centered lift. The discrete Taylor
  decomposition of the actual interpolating field therefore cancels its
  polynomial part through degree <math|d>; its remainder contributes
  <math|L<rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>>. In the second case use
  <math|1\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>*<around|(|1+r|)><rsup|3*<around|(|d+1|)>>>.
  The additional incoming exponential support decay absorbs this polynomial
  and all derivative costs. Multiplication by the insertion rescaling
  <math|L<rsup|d>> therefore leaves <math|C<rsub|d>/L> in both cases. For a
  large-field input of physical support-weighted mass <math|m>, the incoming
  normalized mass is <math|m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>>. Its selected slot has no
  purely principal component. The nonprincipal and selected-component
  estimates, followed by local subtraction, give

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|<math-up|orb>><around|\||x<rsub|<math-up|out>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|<math-up|out>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>,\<kappa\><rsub|c>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>,<space|2em>\<eta\>=L<rsup|-2/5>.
  </equation*>

  The underlying real/imaginary input decomposition costs <math|2*m>, not
  <math|2*m/\<kappa\><rsub|f>>. The factor <math|\<kappa\><rsub|f><rsup|-1>>
  enters only the input norm; the outgoing selected terms are weighted by
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|c><rsup|-1>> in the local subtraction. The common
  large-field energy bound absorbs that outgoing normalization before the
  displayed estimate is formed. Both adjacent bounds are retained in this
  comparison. A normalized local insertion contributes its extracted
  polynomial and a bounded Taylor remainder; its coefficient error has the
  same small factor. These are the three terms in
  <eqref|ch11:graph-estimates>. A wrapping small-field remainder is never
  declared large-field.

  Choose <math|L> and then the inverse coupling threshold so the coefficient
  perturbation is smaller than half the inverse bound of <math|T>. Absorption
  gives <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|f>*<around|\||M<rsub|G>*x|\|>>
  after fixing a suitable stage column constant <math|s<rsub|f>>. Thus
  <math|M<rsub|G>> is invertible. The outgoing graph
  <math|G<rprime|'>=R<rsub|G>*M<rsub|G><rsup|-1>> lies in the prescribed ball
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|c>> if
  <math|s<rsub|c>> is chosen above the finite constant from
  <eqref|ch11:graph-estimates>. This ball invariance is part of the choice,
  not a consequence of invertibility alone. The unit has zero remainders and
  is sent exactly to <math|L<rsup|d>> times itself.

  All arrays and maps taking local polynomial coefficients are fixed
  independently of <math|G>. For graphs <math|G,H> with
  <math|a=<around|\<\|\|\>|G-H|\<\|\|\>>/s<rsub|f>>, the distinguished factor
  in their difference is <math|<around|(|G-H|)>*x>; the normalized local
  insertion cancels. The same rooted sums therefore prove

  <\equation>
    <around|\||<around|(|M<rsub|G>-M<rsub|H>|)>*x|\|>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|R<rsub|G>-R<rsub|H>|)>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>*a<around|\||x|\|>,<space|2em>\<delta\>=C<rsub|d>*\<epsilon\>*s<rsub|f>.<label|ch11:graph-difference>
  </equation>

  The harmless <math|C<rsub|d>> covers the finite symmetrization. Increase
  the block ratio and threshold until <math|4*\<delta\>*s<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>1>,
  together with the preceding inverse and invariance inequalities. These are
  actual raw-map difference estimates; continuity alone would not supply
  them. They are the input for the rebasing contraction of the coefficient
  graphs.

  For fixed incoming data the maps are continuous in the actual inverse
  coupling and the canonical path coefficients in weighted
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> at a strict decay-width reserve, and in the incoming
  activity arrays in the same strong norm. All local integrands use the same
  protected physical-coordinate change and fixed dictionaries. The common
  integrable bounds in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> justify
  dominated convergence on each fixed finite truncation. The strict
  path-width reserve and the strong support reserve make the omitted
  countable tails uniformly small. Taking the finite truncation first and
  then its size to infinity proves continuity in these weighted norms. Finite
  localization by Taylor expansion and symmetry averaging preserve
  continuity. Inversion preserves it on the common inverse-norm ball. No
  extra derivative of an incoming activity is taken when comparing
  equal-regularity adjacent stages.

  <subsection|Propagating the already formed terms involving at least two
  insertions><label|ch11:higher-modes>

  Suppose every pair of distinct marked ancestors has periodic
  maximum-distance at least <math|B<rsup|2>>. If a full support contains
  <math|r\<geqslant\>2> such ancestors, then

  <\equation>
    B*r\<leqslant\>64*c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>.<label|ch11:separated-cover>
  </equation>

  A doubled spanning traversal of a minimal connected cover has length at
  most <math|6*B*<around|(|c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>-1|)>>. Assign each
  ancestor to a covering center. The assigned centers are distinct and
  separated by at least <math|B<rsup|2>-2*B>. Their cyclic traversal costs at
  least <math|r*<around|(|B<rsup|2>-2*B|)>>. For <math|B\<geqslant\>8> the
  stated constant follows. This argument uses periodic distances.

  Retain two cover units beyond those needed by the connected expansion. If
  the source weights grow by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>> per mark, then
  eventually

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>/2>,<space|2em><around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2,
  </equation*>

  because <math|64*log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|f>/B\<leqslant\>1/2>. The
  second unit absorbs site conversion and attachments. Existing terms
  involving at least two insertions thus gain at least <math|e<rsup|-B/64>>
  while retaining their final support weights. This uses the full incoming
  majorant for terms involving at least two insertions before changing
  weights; the single-insertion bound alone would not give it.

  Let <math|H<rsub|f>> be that stronger incoming incidence bound and
  <math|\<zeta\>> the complete weighted incidence sum for terms linear in the
  sources. With a strict reserve in the unmarked site criterion of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:marked-step>, root each component at an
  existing factor involving at least two insertions or at a factor linear in
  a source in a new join. The common integral and rooted-tree estimates give

  <\equation>
    H<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|e>*<around|{|e<rsup|-B/64>*H<rsub|f>+\<zeta\>|}>.<label|ch11:higher-induction>
  </equation>

  The constant includes fixed support conversion and the finite degree menu,
  but not the period or number of marks. A new component involving several
  insertions has at least two individual insertion labels; using the bound
  for one gives the deliberately weaker bound by <math|\<zeta\>>. Existing
  large-field coefficients involving several insertions are treated as whole
  incoming factors with their prescribed sets of large-field sites retained.
  Newly selected components use the energy reserve of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> to absorb the fixed inflations
  in <eqref|ch11:integrated-component>. Choose the stage threshold so
  <math|C<rsub|e>*e<rsup|-B/64>\<leqslant\>1/2> and
  <math|C<rsub|e>*\<zeta\>\<leqslant\>1/2>. Then
  <math|H<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>1> implies <math|H<rsub|c>\<leqslant\>1>. The
  joint terminal construction starts the induction.

  If the input is specified at exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|f>-4>, its
  support conversion must still leave the two coarse reserve units. For
  example,

  <\equation*>
    10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|e+9|)>\<leqslant\>L*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|f>-4|)>
  </equation*>

  suffices for the enlargement used here. The four removed fine units and the
  two retained coarse units are different costs. A declaration that exponents
  lie in <math|<around|[|98,100|]>> would not prove this inequality.

  <section|A common Gaussian comparison>

  The comparison of several local polynomials must preserve their cross
  covariances. Separate Gaussian approximations for individual factors are
  insufficient. The following finite-degree statement is the useful form,
  permitting a different cutoff for every subset of marks.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Joint polynomial comparison with
    cutoffs><label|ch11:joint-gaussian>Fix <math|n,D> and
    <math|A,B\<geqslant\>1>. For each nonempty
    <math|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>, let <math|p<rsub|I>> be a
    gauge-invariant polynomial of degree at most <math|D> and coefficient
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm at most <math|A>. For every member <math|t>
    of a finite probability mixture, let one probability space carry a
    centered joint Gaussian array <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>>, common to all
    subsets, with covariance <math|C<rsub|t>>. Suppose

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||a<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>B,<space|1em><around|\||<around|(|C<rsub|t>|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>,<around|\||<around|(|C<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>B,<space|1em><around|\||<around|(|C<rsub|t>-C<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    For each <math|I> let <math|0\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|t,I>\<leqslant\>1>
    satisfy <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|(|1-\<gamma\><rsub|t,I>|)>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>.
    On its positive set suppose the actual normalized local curvature
    coordinates, after an allowed gauge rotation, obey
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\><rsub|t,I,j>-a<rsub|j>-\<eta\><rsub|t,j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>
    on all used coordinates. The polynomial's invariance removes the
    rotation.

    With the same weights <math|\<lambda\><rsub|t>> for all subsets and the
    empty product, define

    <\equation*>
      M<rsub|S>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|S|)>><big|sum><rsub|t>\<lambda\><rsub|t>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>\<gamma\><rsub|t,I>*p<rsub|I><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|t,I>|)>,<space|2em>M<rsub|S><rsup|\<ast\>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around|(|S|)>>\<bbb-E\><rsub|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>p<rsub|I>*<around|(|a+\<eta\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and set <math|M<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=M<rsub|\<varnothing\>><rsup|\<ast\>>=1>.
    The symbol <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>> may denote the Gaussian
    pairing functional for a symmetric array, without assuming positivity.
    There is a polynomial <math|P<rsub|n,D>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|S\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>\|M<rsub|S>-M<rsub|S><rsup|\<ast\>>\|+<around|\<\|\|\>|log
      M-log M<rsup|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*P<rsub|n,D>*<around|(|A,B|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its constants are independent of the number of unused coordinates and of
    the number of mixture members.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix a partition with <math|\<ell\>> blocks and put
    <math|\<gamma\>=<big|prod><rsub|I\<in\>\<pi\>>\<gamma\><rsub|t,I>>. Then
    <math|1-\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|I><around|(|1-\<gamma\><rsub|t,I>|)>>,
    so <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|(|1-\<gamma\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>*\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>
    without independence. Telescope each product monomial to replace the
    physical coordinates by <math|a+\<eta\><rsub|t>>. Every difference
    contains one factor of size <math|\<varepsilon\>> and at most
    <math|\<ell\>*D-1> Gaussian factors. Fixed marginal moments and Hölder's
    inequality bound the result by <math|\<varepsilon\>*A<rsup|\<ell\>>*C<rsub|n,D>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|n*D>>.
    Only coordinates in that monomial occur; no ambient-dimension count is
    introduced.

    Cauchy\USchwarz removes <math|\<gamma\>> at cost
    <math|<sqrt|\<ell\>>*<space|0.17em>\<varepsilon\>> times the second
    moment of the polynomial. Finally the Gaussian pairing
    formula<nbsp><cite|Isserlis1918> expresses each reference moment as a
    finite sum of products of means and covariances. Telescope these products
    to replace <math|C<rsub|t>> by <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>. This costs another
    <math|\<varepsilon\>> times a fixed polynomial in <math|A,B>. Average
    with the common probability weights and sum over finitely many
    partitions. The finite polynomial formula <eqref|ch11:cumulants> gives
    the logarithm bound. The empty coefficient is exactly one because the
    empty product has no cutoff.
  </proof>

  The proposition applies to the purely principal components of a marked
  expansion, after the scalar normalization has been extracted once. It does
  not identify the whole interacting density with a Gaussian. Deleting an
  additive remainder for an estimate may leave the precision, affine mean and
  prescribed path data unchanged, but it changes the interacting density.
  Exact reconstruction always uses the genuine density. This distinction is
  indispensable when matching the Gaussian comparison to the block
  transformation.

  <subsection|The exact cross-covariance on the selected
  forest><label|ch11:tree-covariance>

  We identify the flat reference used in the preceding comparison. Use
  physical counting coordinates on the two adjacent tori. Let
  <math|D,D<rprime|'>> be their bond-to-plaquette curls and let

  <\equation*>
    K=D<rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>*D,<space|1em>K<rprime|'>=<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|c>*D<rprime|'>,<space|1em>A=K+b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*Q,
  </equation*>

  where <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|c>> are the actual positive curvature metrics
  and <math|Q> is the rooted gauge-covariant block average derivative. Let
  <math|I<rsub|F>> insert free forest coordinates and
  <math|A<rsub|F>=I<rsub|F><rsup|\<ast\>>*A*I<rsub|F>>. The tree gap gives
  <math|A<rsub|F>\<gtr\>0>. Set <math|X=I<rsub|F>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*I<rsub|F><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
  The rooted gauge projection <math|\<cal-P\>> onto the same forest satisfies
  <math|A*\<cal-P\>=A> and <math|D*\<cal-P\>=D>, since its gauge potential
  vanishes at each block root. Thus <math|X*A=\<cal-P\>> and <math|D*X*A=D>;
  no inverse of the gauge-degenerate full <math|K> is used.

  Choose matrices <math|Z,Z<rprime|'>> solving <math|K*Z=D<rsup|\<ast\>>> and
  <math|K<rprime|'>*Z<rprime|'>=<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>. They
  exist because <math|ker K=ker D> and the symmetric matrix <math|K> has
  range <math|<around|(|ker D|)><rsup|\<perp\>>>; similarly for
  <math|K<rprime|'>>. The matrices <math|P<rsub|f>=D*Z> and
  <math|P<rsub|c>=D<rprime|'>*Z<rprime|'>> are independent of the choices,
  symmetric and positive semidefinite.

  For the actual stationary forest response <math|W>, the adjacent Gaussian
  identities of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian> read

  <\equation*>
    K*W=Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*K<rprime|'>,<space|1em>A*W=b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|1em>D*W=J*D<rprime|'>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|J=H<rsub|0,f><rsup|-1>*T*H<rsub|0,c>> is the curvature
  response. Here <math|T> is the installed two-form transport and
  <math|H<rsub|0,f>,H<rsub|0,c>> are the unmodified diagonal operators; the
  actual repaired metrics <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|c>> still define
  <math|K,K<rprime|'>> and agree with them on curls. Thus <math|J> is not the
  raw transport <math|T>. Transpose the first identity and multiply by
  <math|Z>: <math|K<rprime|'>*Q*Z=<around|(|D<rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*J<rsup|\<ast\>>>.
  Multiplying by <math|<around|(|Z<rprime|'>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>> gives
  <math|D<rprime|'>*Q*Z=P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|\<ast\>>>. On the other hand
  <math|A*Z=D<rsup|\<ast\>>+b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>*Q*Z>. Multiply by <math|D*X>
  and use <math|D*X*A=D> and <math|D*X*b*Q<rsup|\<ast\>>=D*W> to obtain

  <\equation>
    <around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)>*A<rsub|F><rsup|-1>*<around|(|D*I<rsub|F>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=D*X*D<rsup|\<ast\>>=P<rsub|f>-J*P<rsub|c>*J<rsup|\<ast\>>.<label|ch11:conditional-curvature>
  </equation>

  This proves every cross-entry simultaneously, including different
  plaquettes and color coordinates. It retains both actual metrics, even when
  an adjacent pair straddles a change of stage. The right side is positive
  because the left is a covariance. Harmonic and gauge modes were handled
  through the range equations, not discarded.

  At zero background the actual row Gram is precisely <math|A<rsub|F>>: the
  absolutely summed path coefficients reproduce their curvature
  factorization, the gauge-covariant block average derivative is <math|Q>,
  and each signed orientation has the same quadratic form. Their probability
  average therefore adds no extra factor. The finite path cutoff and actual
  background are handled by the inverse-window comparison, not by changing
  this exact identity.

  <subsection|Actual local coordinate and cutoff
  estimates><label|ch11:actual-reader>

  Fix a clean neighborhood and one forest, tag, coarse gauge and Gaussian
  sample for all coordinates read by the polynomial family.
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch06:linear-comparison> gives actual-to-flat mean
  error <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>|)>> and covariance error
  <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f>|)>>, plus exponentially small window
  tails. After multiplication by the finitely many curl rows and summing the
  finite-range window entries, these estimates remain
  <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>. Combining with
  <eqref|ch11:conditional-curvature> supplies the full conditional curvature
  array, rather than only its diagonal. All comparisons use the same root
  frame; the gauge-invariance of each polynomial then permits its physical
  evaluation in a different local frame without changing its value.

  Here is an explicit scale choice that makes the nonlinear error small. Put
  <math|R=x<rsup|-3/8>> and <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>> on the actual
  inverse coupling band. The coarse logarithm on the required patch has size
  <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>=o<around|(|R|)>>. Restrict the finitely
  many Gaussian read coordinates so the physical fluctuation is at most a
  fixed fraction of <math|R>. Since <math|R/g=x<rsup|1/8+o<around|(|1|)>>>,
  the Gaussian tail bound makes failure of this restriction smaller than
  every inverse power of <math|x>, even after the polynomial-in-<math|B>
  count of the local reads. On this event, the product-logarithm and
  curvature Taylor remainders are <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*R<rsup|2>|)>>.
  Dividing by <math|g> gives <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*x<rsup|-1/4>|)>>. The
  actual mean error in Gaussian units is <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|a>*t<rsub|f><rsup|2>/g|)>=x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>.
  The omitted path tails are smaller than every power of <math|x>.

  For a smooth cutoff also requiring the fine small-field cutoff, use the
  remote event indicator conclusion of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch06:mean-cancellation>.
  The linear fine comparison row is <math|O<around|(|L<rsup|-2>*t<rsub|f>|)>>;
  choose the fixed block ratio before the inverse coupling threshold so it
  lies strictly within the fine plateau. The gauge-covariant block average
  comparison has its independently chosen strict plateau width. Restricting
  the local noise to the smaller fixed fraction of <math|t<rsub|f>/g>
  required by that proposition has failure smaller than every power of
  <math|x>, because <math|t<rsub|f>/g=M<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|cut>>> grows
  faster than every power of <math|log x>. Intersect these cutoff events with
  the radius-<math|R> event. Their failure bound is still summable over the
  finitely many local reads.

  Thus for every fixed family of polynomial reads with the prescribed
  clearance and support, the hypotheses of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:joint-gaussian> hold with, for example,
  <math|\<varepsilon\>=x<rsup|-1/32>> after increasing the threshold. The
  covariance error is smaller than <math|\<varepsilon\>> and cutoff loss
  smaller than <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>. A common Gaussian sample and
  coarse forecast are used for all subsets. The scalar factor is the one
  extracted from the unmarked integral before marks are chosen; all remaining
  principal products are integrated against its normalized Gaussian/Haar
  probability law. Incoming large-field and correction factors belong to the
  other components already bounded in <eqref|ch11:integrated-component>, and
  are not absorbed into a subset-dependent probability measure.

  <section|Restarting below a comparison level>

  A useful auxiliary history keeps the actual inverse coupling constant below
  a comparison level while retaining the paths, adjacent quadratic
  identities, and block geometry. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:mass>
  preserves all original normalized coefficient integrals along this history.
  The construction below provides its local representations and estimates on
  that same restarted history.

  Two changes of representation are involved. For a large reference inverse
  coupling parameter <math|s>, define

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|s>=e<rsup|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>h<rsub|s>=e<rsup|<around|(|log
    log s|)><rsup|3/2>>,<space|2em>t=s<rsup|-1/2>*H<rsub|s>,<space|1em>\<vartheta\>=s<rsup|-1/2>*h<rsub|s>.
  </equation*>

  The restarted large-field threshold is <math|\<vartheta\>>, not <math|t>;
  its support radius is the square of the original logarithmic-square radius.
  Eventually <math|2*\<vartheta\>\<leqslant\>t>. For the piecewise
  quadratic/linear clip <math|d<rsub|t>>, with linear coefficient
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16>,

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|t><around|(|r|)>-d<rsub|\<vartheta\>><around|(|r|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>d<rsub|t><around|(|r|)>-d<rsub|\<vartheta\>><around|(|r|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|r\<gtr\>\<vartheta\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|\<vartheta\>\<less\>r\<leqslant\>t> this follows from
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*r<rsup|2>/2-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<vartheta\>*r>;
  for <math|r\<gtr\>t> it is <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*<around|(|t-\<vartheta\>|)>*r>.
  The positive difference controls newly selected large-field sites. The next
  construction assigns their event indicators, eligibility criteria, joint
  insertion terms and path tails consistently.

  <subsection|Constructing the restarted representations><label|ch11:restart-construction>

  We now construct the auxiliary history just described. The physical
  insertions retain their original calibration; only the integration below
  the comparison level is restarted. All support norms in this subsection use
  connected covers and include full dependence regions. Their exponents are
  chosen with the reserves required by the marked expansion before choosing
  an inverse coupling threshold.

  Write <math|x<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> and, for <math|x> large,
  put

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|2>>,<space|1em>h<around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|log log
    x|)><rsup|3/2>>,<space|1em>b<around|(|x|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|-1/2>*h<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The new representation at level <math|i> has radius
  <math|B<rsub|i>=b<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>> and threshold
  <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>. Its clip has the original two
  coefficients, evaluated at this new threshold. Its path coefficients and
  block-kernel branches are the original ones. In particular, \Pretaining the
  clip\Q means retaining its coefficients and formula, not asserting equality
  of two different threshold functions.

  <paragraph|The recording scale.> There is a unique sufficiently large
  <math|r<around|(|x|)>> satisfying

  <\equation*>
    r<around|(|x|)><rsup|-1/2>*H<around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)>=\<vartheta\><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the function on the left is eventually strictly decreasing; its
  values at <math|x> and <math|x<rsup|2>> bracket the right side. Thus
  <math|x\<leqslant\>r<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>x<rsup|2>>, and taking
  logarithms gives <math|r<around|(|x|)>=x<rsup|1+o<around|(|1|)>>>. This is
  a recording parameter for the local calculation, not the actual inverse
  coupling. In particular

  <\equation*>
    b<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>4*b<around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>25*b<around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)>+30\<leqslant\>B<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<around|(|r<around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>
  </equation*>

  eventually. On each fixed adjacent stage pair,
  <math|x<rsub|i+1>=x<rsub|i>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L<rsub|i>> implies
  <math|B<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)>/\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>1>.
  The local chart, clipped source, Gaussian precision and tail events are
  those of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> with this recording
  scale and these actual thresholds. Its elementary scale inequalities
  continue to hold: the radii are logarithmic powers and the thresholds are
  <math|x<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>. The actual inverse coupling band
  remains expressed in <math|x<rsub|i>>, not in
  <math|r<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>. In particular one must not invoke a lower
  bound <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>r<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>/2>. For every fixed
  <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, changing the reference parameter and the actual
  band instead give, eventually,

  <\equation*>
    y<rsup|1-\<eta\>>\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*y,<space|2em>y=r<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a prescribed finite local estimate, fix the number of powers of
  <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1>> and <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>> that occur before
  choosing <math|\<eta\>>. Replacing a comparison power of <math|y> by the
  corresponding actual inverse coupling power then loses at most
  <math|y<rsup|C*\<eta\>>>, where <math|C> is fixed by that estimate. Choose
  <math|\<eta\>> small enough to retain its negative exponent. The
  dimensionless quadratic gap and path bounds are unchanged; physical
  covariance factors retain their actual <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1>>
  normalization. Likewise, the cutoff-to-noise ratio is computed exactly as
  <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>/g\<asymp\>h<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>,
  and the selected-component energy is <math|\<beta\>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>c*h<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>>.
  These comparisons, not a fictitious inverse coupling band in <math|y>, are
  used below.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Re-expression of the endpoint><label|ch11:restart-endpoint>At a
    sufficiently late comparison level <math|k>, the original density has an
    exact presentation with radius <math|b<around|(|x<rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>>,
    threshold <math|\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>>, the original path
    coefficients, clip coefficients, inverse coupling and scalar, regular
    bound no larger than its original bound, and large-field site bound

    <\equation*>
      G<around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>=exp <around|{|-a<rsub|\<ast\>>*h<around|(|x<rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> is chosen sufficiently small at each
    fixed stage before its threshold for the starting scale. The construction
    retains the old large-field labels and their eligibility criteria inside
    the new activities.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Abbreviate <math|s=x<rsub|k>>, <math|t=s<rsup|-1/2>*H<around|(|s|)>>,
    <math|\<vartheta\>=\<vartheta\><around|(|s|)>>, <math|M=b<around|(|s|)>>
    and <math|B=M<rsup|2>>. Write <math|A<rsub|t>,A<rsub|\<vartheta\>>> for
    the path actions with their respective event indicators and clips, and
    <math|E<rsub|t>,E<rsub|\<vartheta\>>> for the original regular
    coefficients with the corresponding event indicators. The old regular
    bound is at most <math|s<rsup|-11/10>>; the old large-field bound is at
    most <math|w<rsub|s>=e<rsup|-H<around|(|s|)><rsup|1/5>>>. These are the
    selected trajectory's endpoint bounds.

    Eventually <math|2*\<vartheta\>\<leqslant\>t>. The clip comparison proved
    above gives <math|d<rsub|t><around|(|r|)>-d<rsub|\<vartheta\>><around|(|r|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>>
    whenever <math|r\<gtr\>\<vartheta\>>, and a nonnegative difference
    otherwise. Distribute each plaquette's difference among its incident
    sites. Allowing five sites is a harmless upper bound. For the set
    <math|F<rsub|\<vartheta\>>> of new large-field sites this gives local
    energy contributions <math|d<rsub|z>> with

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z>d<rsub|z>=<big|sum><rsub|p><around|(|d<rsub|t><around|(|r<rsub|p>|)>-d<rsub|\<vartheta\>><around|(|r<rsub|p>|)>|)>,<space|2em>d<rsub|z>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>/5*<space|1em><around|(|z\<in\>F<rsub|\<vartheta\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The omitted quadratic path rows give another nonnegative energy
    contribution. Truncate each row at true path length <math|B> before
    squaring, and keep its half-square only where the old retention condition
    holds and the new one fails. The relaxed small-field condition reads a
    radius-<math|B> neighborhood: outside it
    <math|2\<leqslant\>\<vartheta\>*e<rsup|B/c>> makes the condition
    automatic. The truncated half-square therefore reads radius at most
    <math|2*B> and vanishes if its tested neighborhood is new-small-field.
    Distribute its value equally among the new large-field sites in that
    neighborhood, making no division when the set is empty. The resulting
    local energy contributions <math|b<rprime|'><rsub|z>\<geqslant\>0> depend
    only on a neighborhood of radius at most <math|4*B>, and their sum is
    exactly the sum of truncated half-squares. Perform this operation
    separately for each signed orientation, retaining its averaging weight.

    Choose <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>/20>, put
    <math|u=a<rsub|\<ast\>>*h<around|(|s|)><rsup|2>>, and for large-field
    sites <math|z> put <math|X<rsub|z>=exp
    <around|[|-\<beta\>*<around|(|d<rsub|z>+b<rprime|'><rsub|z>|)>|]>>. The
    actual band gives <math|\<beta\>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>h<around|(|s|)><rsup|2>/2>,
    hence <math|<around|\||X<rsub|z>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-2*u>>. The
    remaining exponent difference is

    <\equation*>
      D=-<around|(|A<rsub|t>-A<rsub|\<vartheta\>>|)>+\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|z><around|(|d<rsub|z>+b<rprime|'><rsub|z>|)>+<around|(|E<rsub|\<vartheta\>>-E<rsub|t>|)>.
    </equation*>

    It consists of the omitted quadratic row pairs, the cubic, quartic and
    loop event indicator differences, and the regular event indicator
    differences. Each term vanishes on a new-small-field full dependence
    region. At a fixed larger support exponent <math|a+2> its site bound is

    <\equation>
      \<epsilon\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>C*B<rsup|C>*<around|{|\<beta\>*C<rsub|<math-up|path>><rsup|2>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*B/4>+\<Gamma\><rsub|s>*t<rsup|2>+\<beta\>*C<rsub|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|t<rsup|3>+t<rsup|4>|)>+s<rsup|-11/10>|}>=s<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>.<label|ch11:restart-endpoint-error>
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0> is a retained true-length path rate and
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>2*H<around|(|s|)>> is the loop
    coefficient bound. For the first term expand the difference of squares
    into ordered path pairs, one of length exceeding <math|B>, and retain
    part of their exponential length weight. For the atoms restricted by
    event indicators use their old bounds <math|t<rsup|j>> and the cover
    bound <math|1+2*j+<math-up|length>/B>. For an old support <math|Y>, its
    fixed halo obeys <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsup|<around|[|8|]>>|)>\<leqslant\>C+C*<around|(|M/B|)>*c<rsub|M><around|(|Y|)>>.
    Eventually its old exponent absorbs this new exponent and the extra unit
    used to turn an anchored bound into a site bound. These are the support
    conversions of Section<nbsp><reference|ch06:primitive-bounds>; their
    losses are polynomial in <math|B>. The same argument bounds enlarged old
    large-field labels by <math|C*B<rsup|C>*w<rsub|s>>. No path multiplicity
    or winding path is removed.

    Coalesce the absolutely summable terms by full support into
    <math|D<rsub|Y>>. For large <math|s>, put
    <math|W<rsub|Y>=e<rsup|D<rsub|Y>>-1>, so
    <math|<around|\||W<rsub|Y>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||D<rsub|Y>|\|>> and
    <math|W<rsub|Y>> vanishes when its support is new-small-field. The
    identity

    <\equation>
      e<rsup|-A<rsub|t>-E<rsub|t>>=e<rsup|-A<rsub|\<vartheta\>>-E<rsub|\<vartheta\>>>*<big|prod><rsub|Y><around|(|1+W<rsub|Y>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|z\<in\>F<rsub|\<vartheta\>>>X<rsub|z><label|ch11:restart-endpoint-identity>
    </equation>

    is exact. Multiply by the old large-field gas and expand the optional
    <math|W<rsub|Y>> factors. Keep the old compatibility and eligibility
    criteria. Group using intersections of full dependence supports,
    including the fixed halo for old large-field conditions and the
    radius-<math|4*B> supports of <math|X<rsub|z>>. Every nonzero component
    contains a selected <math|X<rsub|z>>: an old activity contains an old
    large-field site, hence a new large-field site, and every nonzero
    <math|W<rsub|Y>> reads a new large-field indicator. The factor at that
    site is mandatory and intersects that component. Assign the component its
    exact new set of large-field sites and also retain the union of its old
    sets of large-field sites and all their conditions. Conversely, those
    constituents recover the original compatible collection and optional
    subset. Thus the regrouping is a bijection of terms, not a replacement of
    old eligibility by a new condition. Disjoint new exclusion supports are
    the unions of these full read supports. Their eligibility criteria
    require exact coverage of the new large-field sites there.

    Reserve <math|e<rsup|-u>> per selected site factor. The remaining site
    sum at exponent <math|a+1> is at most

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|s>=2*\<epsilon\><rsub|s>+C*B<rsup|C>*w<rsub|s>+<around|(|8*B+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|<around|(|a+1|)>*9<rsup|4>>*e<rsup|-u>=s<rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>.
    </equation*>

    A radius-<math|4*B> box has a connected <math|B>-cover of at most
    <math|9<rsup|4>> boxes; at most <math|<around|(|8*B+1|)><rsup|4>> such
    boxes contain a given site. The connected-set bound of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch08:chapter>, losing one support exponent,
    applies since <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<rho\><rsub|s>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Every component retains at least one reserved factor, so its total site
    bound is at most <math|e<rsup|-u>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<rho\><rsub|s>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|s|)>>.
    There is no extra sum over prescribed sets of large-field sites: selected
    site factors already specify them, and exact-coverage conditions only
    remove terms. Absolute convergence justifies countable labels and
    coalescing.

    The regular coefficients themselves are unchanged. Their domains and
    allowed directions shrink with the threshold, and increasing the cover
    radius decreases their support weights. Their old bounds therefore
    persist. The exact identity and the gas bijection prove the assertion,
    with unchanged scalar. All operations preserve gauge invariance, reality
    and signed symmetry, by reindexing the original orientation average.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The genuine density and its neutral reference.> At an
  intermediate level write the genuine presentation as
  <math|R=e<rsup|-E>*R<rsup|0>>, where <math|R<rsup|0>> is obtained by
  deleting only the regular additive family
  <math|E=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>E<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Its path, clip,
  large-field activities and all their conditions remain unchanged. If the
  anchored weighted value bound of that family is <math|q>, then
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||E<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>q<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>;
  this proves integrability at finite volume, but is not used as a
  volume-uniform estimate. Instead insert local factors

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|\<alpha\>>=e<rsup|-E<rsub|\<alpha\>>>-1,<space|2em><big|prod><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|1+h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>=e<rsup|-E>.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|<around|\||h<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||E<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>><around|\||E<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>>,
  their local site bound is the old anchored bound times a fixed polynomial
  in the radius. They are inserted into the same expansion as the neutral
  Gaussian integral. Thus the object integrated is always <math|R>, also for
  every coefficient of a marked density. Deleting <math|E> for the neutral
  estimates never deletes it from the density identity.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One restarted history on the comparison
    window><label|ch11:restart-family>Fix the finite degree and derivative
    requests and the comparison window with top level <math|k>, reference
    inverse coupling <math|s=x<rsub|k>>, and at most <math|<around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|4>> steps. Take its thresholds for the starting scales large
    enough that <math|3*s/4\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>s> and
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>-s|\|>\<leqslant\>s/8>. On the
    original adjacent path data there are presentations <math|P<rsub|i>>,
    <math|i\<leqslant\>k> in that window, with the bounds
    <math|<around|(|B<rsub|i>,\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>>, common
    actual inverse coupling <math|\<beta\>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, large-field
    bound <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>> and additive value cost at most

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|k-i>=s<rsup|-1/200>*A<rsub|s><rsup|4*<around|(|k-i|)>>,<space|2em>A<rsub|s>=e<rsup|<around|(|log
      log s|)><rsup|3>>.
    </equation*>

    Their exact densities satisfy

    <\equation>
      D<rsub|k>=\<rho\><rsub|N,p,k>,<space|2em>D<rsub|i>=\<cal-B\><rsub|i,\<beta\>>*D<rsub|i+1>=<math-up|density><around|(|P<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em><big|int>D<rsub|i>=Z<rsub|N,p>.<label|ch11:restart-density>
    </equation>

    Here the block operator has the original geometric branches, evaluated at
    the common inverse coupling. The same local factors, normalization and
    all deletion-of-support identities supply both weighted marked-step uses.
    The constants are uniform in cutoff, period and the compatible local
    fields, for the fixed requests.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Start with Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:restart-endpoint>. The
    reference-inverse-coupling band gives
    <math|x<rsub|i>/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*x<rsub|i>> on every
    step. Only the actual inverse coupling is frozen; the reference inverse
    couplings, original path pairs, metric floors, gauge-covariant block
    average derivatives and forest charts keep their original adjacent
    values. The recording-scale inequalities above verify the radii and
    threshold comparisons in the local integral. The fine path and clip in
    that integral are exactly the ones in the incoming presentation. A change
    of stage uses its own adjacent pair, not the previous stage's quadratic
    form.

    Suppose the genuine incoming value cost is <math|q<rsub|n>>,
    <math|n=k-i-1>. Its neutral version has zero such cost and the same
    large-field bound and loop coefficient bound. Thus it meets the small
    incoming regular bound in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>; we
    do not claim that the larger <math|q<rsub|n>> meets that derivative
    bound. The genuine additive factors are handled by the local multipliers
    just defined.

    We spell out the quantitative margins on this actual expansion. The
    primitive exponent decomposition in Section<nbsp><reference|ch06:primitive-bounds>,
    with recording parameter <math|y=r<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>, gives an
    unselected local exponent of site size <math|t<rsub|y>> with
    <math|2*t<rsub|y>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|-1/48>> after enlargement of its
    threshold. Indeed the corresponding exterior row, determinant and Haar
    bounds are <math|y<rsup|-1/4+o<around|(|1|)>>>, including radius
    polynomials and the actual inverse coupling factors just discussed; the
    linear residual is bounded by <math|y<rsup|-4>*y<rsup|2>> before these
    subpower losses. The two determinant-word series and inverse correction
    are exponentially small in <math|b<around|(|y|)>>, including the
    <math|y<rsup|4>> factor for two Gaussian coordinates and any fixed
    inverse powers of the actual inverse coupling. The inherited path terms
    have costs <math|O<around|(|\<Gamma\>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>|)>> and
    <math|O<around|(|\<beta\>*<around|(|\<vartheta\><rsup|3>+\<vartheta\><rsup|4>|)>|)>>,
    with true-length reserves. The inherited bounds before changing the
    reference parameter can be weakened to <math|y<rsup|-1/12>>, with fixed
    finite powers of the actual inverse coupling; choose the preceding
    <math|\<eta\>> so their loss is less than <math|1/48>. Thus all these are
    negative powers with a strict margin beyond <math|1/24>. One may weaken
    their sum successively to <math|y<rsup|-1/24>> and then
    <math|y<rsup|-1/48>> to include exponentiation, support conversion and
    fixed constants. The neutral additive part is zero. The local exponents
    are bounded extensions on the same chart event indicators; outside them
    the failed cutoff conditions are retained as tail-event factors, not
    evaluated as small exponents.

    Selected components have, after the extracted Gaussian and local
    normalization factors have been bounded, bound <math|exp
    <around|[|-K*h<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>*r|]>> for <math|r>
    selected cutoff conditions, by the actual component energy in
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>. Choose
    <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> also so small that
    <math|4*a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>K/2>. For both marked-step uses at
    once take

    <\equation*>
      m=max <around|{|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|-2>,G<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|-1>|}>,<space|2em>1\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>max
      <around|(|1,H<around|(|s|)><rsup|q-3>|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|q> is the fixed requested finite power. The ratio
    <math|h<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/h<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>>
    tends to one uniformly in the window: its logarithm is
    <math|O<around|(|<sqrt|log log s>/log s|)>>. Thus <math|log
    m\<leqslant\>4*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*h<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>>
    eventually, retaining at least half the component energy. Apply the
    factor <math|m> only to components with an actual selected prescribed set
    of large-field sites, and to a selected component within each such
    component. The original prescribed set of large-field sites coverage
    ensures that an incoming large-field factor cannot occur without such a
    component. Factors <math|l>, finite decorations and local counting costs
    have logarithms <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>+log
    B<rsub|i>|)>> per selected cutoff condition and fit inside the remaining
    margin.

    The simultaneous tail estimate of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>
    uses these very chart coordinates and event indicators under the same
    Gaussian law, including any bounded genuine multipliers. It therefore
    bounds all compatible factors from failed cutoff conditions jointly. Its
    cutoff exponent and the retained selected-component exponent dominate
    every multiple of <math|log s>. The unselected components after inflation
    cost at most <math|s<rsup|-1/48+o<around|(|1|)>>>. The projected old
    large-field site sum is bounded by <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B<rsub|i>|)>*L<rsub|i><rsup|4>*T<rsub|e>*G<around|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)>>,
    also smaller than every inverse power of <math|s>. It need not include
    <math|m> a second time: the selected component estimate already includes
    that factor.

    The genuine additive multipliers have uninflated site bound
    <math|C*<around|(|42*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|n>>. Every fixed
    product of radius factors, support conversion constants and <math|l> is
    eventually at most <math|A<rsub|s><rsup|1/4>>. Hence their inflated site
    bound is at most <math|q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|1/4>>. For
    <math|n\<leqslant\><around|(|log log s|)><rsup|4>> and every fixed
    <math|r>,

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/400>.
    </equation*>

    This follows by taking logarithms: the positive contribution is
    <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|7>|)>>, whereas the negative
    term is <math|-<around|(|log s|)>/200>. The connected expansion can
    therefore be rooted at an additive multiplier when one occurs; its site
    bound fits <math|\<omega\><rsub|n>=q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s>>. The other
    components fit <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>-\<omega\><rsub|n>>, where
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>=q<rsub|n>*A<rsub|s><rsup|3>>, since
    <math|s<rsup|-1/48+o<around|(|1|)>>=o<around|(|q<rsub|n>|)>>. This proves
    the inflated product bound and its site summability on the genuine, not a
    substituted, density. All finite stage factors in this paragraph are
    absorbed simultaneously by its choice of a sufficiently large starting
    scale; no uniform supremum over infinitely many stages is used.

    It remains to reconstruct the coarse presentation. The regular connected
    logarithm has anchored cost at most <math|16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>. Keep the
    original coarse path action <math|A<rsub|i>>. Its difference from the
    base action extracted in the Gaussian integral consists of the cubic,
    quartic and quadratic-loop terms restricted by their event indicators,
    with cost at most

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\>*e<rsup|7*e>*C<rsub|i>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|3>+\<beta\>*e<rsup|9*e>*C<rsub|i>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>+e<rsup|5*e>*\<Gamma\><rsub|i>*\<vartheta\><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsup|-1/200>.
    </equation*>

    The cover bound for a degree-<math|j> path atom is
    <math|1+2*j+<math-up|length>/B<rsub|i>>; the path decay absorbs the last
    term. Add this correction to the regular logarithm. Its total value cost
    is at most <math|s<rsup|-1/200>+16*\<delta\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|n+1>>.
    The sign is fixed by <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>>>*e<rsup|-\<ell\><rsub|i>>=e<rsup|-A<rsub|i>>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>+\<beta\>*B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>>-A<rsub|i>|)>>>;
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|ref>>> here is the base action, not the
    radius.

    At the sliced-integrand level the event indicators retain the finite
    tag-count factor; it cancels the denominator of the normalized tag
    average when the slices are summed. Signed orientations retain their
    original probability weights. Neither averaging introduces a second
    determinant or a second extracted scalar. The positive Gaussian prefactor
    determines a real scalar logarithm. No branch of a complex logarithm is
    chosen. The remaining large-field factors, including the regular
    logarithms with holes, are grouped at their full support and exact old
    prescribed sets of large-field sites as in <eqref|ch08:reassembly>. The
    raised hole correction is bounded by <math|16*l*\<delta\><rsub|n>*e<rsup|16*\<delta\><rsub|n>>>,
    using <math|<around|\||e<rsup|-c>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||c|\|>><around|\||c|\|>>.
    This and the other component costs are
    <math|s<rsup|-1/400+o<around|(|1|)>>>, within the available
    <math|H<around|(|s|)><rsup|-1/8>> reserve. The inflated connected bound
    gives outgoing large-field bound at most
    <math|m<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>, hence at
    most <math|G<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>. No residual path correction is left
    outside the regular family. Averaging the regular pieces over the finite
    signed motion group retains their anchored bound and invariant sum. The
    exact factorization and reassembly thus give
    <math|D<rsub|i>=\<cal-B\><rsub|i,\<beta\>>*D<rsub|i+1>> on every coarse
    configuration, including configurations with failed cutoff conditions.
    There is no division by a large-field activity, which can vanish.

    At each step choose this expansion before specifying the marked family
    and retain it for subsequent use. Smaller support exponents and smaller
    inflations are then estimates on the same coefficients. Only the newly
    constructed lower entry is installed; the endpoint and all already
    installed higher entries are unchanged. This proves finite descending
    induction, including adjacent stage changes. Finally positivity of
    <math|D<rsub|i>> follows from the positive endpoint and probability
    kernels, not from positivity of individual activities. Their mass-one
    identity and finite-volume integrability give
    <math|<big|int>D<rsub|i>=Z<rsub|N,p>> by Fubini. The same equalities hold
    coefficientwise for finite marked polynomials. This proves the asserted
    normalization and the common-history statement.
  </proof>

  Finally let <math|C<rsup|<math-up|ref>>> be a field-independent polynomial
  of prescribed connected reference correlations with zero constant
  coefficient. The corrected source is

  <\equation*>
    J*q<rsub|N>,<space|2em>J=exp <around|(|-C<rsup|<math-up|ref>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  and its exact push is <math|J*q<rsub|k>>. Its empty coefficient remains the
  original unmarked density. One must start this comparison from the complete
  <math|q<rsub|k>>, including all regular and large-field coefficients
  involving several insertions. Multiplying a freshly formed product of
  single insertions would discard those coefficients and would not compare
  the original correlations.

  <section|The normalized integral with several
  insertions><label|ch11:replicated>

  Suppose the stopping representation on <math|V> sites has <math|n> copies,
  weighted single-insertion bounds <math|x,y>, and higher regular and
  large-field incidence at most one each. Assign each marked term to one of
  its marks and each unmarked term to one site in its support. The
  coefficient norm is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|v<rsub|0>+V*<around|(|b<rsub|<math-up|act>>+\<kappa\>|)>+n*<around|(|x+y+2|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|v<rsub|0>> is the scalar, <math|b<rsub|<math-up|act>>> the
  absolute action cost per site and <math|\<kappa\>> the unmarked activity
  site sum. The estimate uses <math|1+t\<leqslant\>e<rsup|t>> for the
  eligible activity product and retains every coefficient involving several
  insertions. The two unit bounds on the terms involving at least two
  insertions come from <eqref|ch11:higher-induction>.

  Choose <math|0\<less\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>1> below the small-field
  threshold. A maximal packing in the ambient real matrix space of dimension
  <math|D<rsub|G>>, with Haar invariance, gives
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|G>*<around|{|\<chi\><around|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>r|}>\<geqslant\><around|(|r/4|)><rsup|D<rsub|G>>>.
  Restrict every forward link to normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance from
  the identity at most <math|\<rho\>/4>; the event has probability at least
  <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/16|)><rsup|4*D<rsub|G>*V>> and all plaquettes have
  normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance from the identity at most
  <math|\<rho\>>. If the exact action there is bounded by
  <math|V*<around|[|b<rsub|<math-up|act>>+\<beta\>*b<rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|\<rho\>|)>|]>>,
  positivity gives

  <\equation*>
    Z\<geqslant\>e<rsup|v<rsub|0>-V*<around|[|b<rsub|<math-up|act>>+\<beta\>*b<rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|\<rho\>|)>+4*D<rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|16/\<rho\>|)>|]>>.
  </equation*>

  Signed regular costs are included in <math|b<rsub|<math-up|act>>>. Division
  yields

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||Z<rsup|-1>*<big|int><around|[|z<rsub|\<cal-I\>>|]>*q*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><frac|e<rsup|V*q+n*<around|(|x+y+2|)>>|<big|prod><rsub|i>\<omega\><rsub|i>>,>>|<row|<cell|q>|<cell|=2*b<rsub|<math-up|act>>+\<kappa\>+\<beta\>*b<rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|\<rho\>|)>+4*D<rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|16/\<rho\>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch11:replicated-bound>>>>>
  </align>

  The scalar cancels. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:mass> identifies the
  coefficient integrals with the original ones. A reflected tiling at scale
  <math|a<rsub|m>>, stopped at <math|a<rsub|k>>, has
  <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>> sites per cell. Taking the
  number-of-cells root, with common weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|k>>, gives

  <\equation>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|m><around|(|F|)>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k>+x+y+2>.<label|ch11:root-bound>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|m>> denotes that chessboard seminorm. Multiplying
  an admissible residual weight by <math|c> divides the bound by <math|c>,
  uniformly in the period when the local bounds are uniform.

  <subsection|An independent positional modulus><label|ch11:positional>

  We use the usual reflection-positive transfer representation and chessboard
  inequality for the Wilson measure <cite|OsterwalderSeiler1978|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978>.
  These apply to the original positive Wilson law; no reflection positivity
  of an individual effective activity is asserted. The product inequality
  bounds insertions in distinct reflection cells by their chessboard
  seminorms. Expanding a copied product of <math|F+G> over subsets of cells
  proves the triangle inequality for these chessboard seminorms, including
  when one of them vanishes.

  For correlation evaluation positions separated in every coordinate, the
  required modulus follows independently of difference insertions. Represent
  a cyclic correlation as a transfer word
  <math|<tr><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|q>B<rsub|i>*T<rsup|H<rsub|i>>/Z>, where
  <math|T> is positive, <math|B<rsub|i>> is a local slab kernel, the slab
  width is <math|w>, and the total tour length is
  <math|M=q*w+<big|sum><rsub|i>H<rsub|i>=2*s*w>. The reflected-copy bound for
  a slab and its adjoint gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2*s>\<leqslant\>A*Z<rsup|1/<around|(|2*s|)>>>.
  The standard Schatten\UHölder inequality
  <cite-detail|TroppMatrixAnalysis2022|Theorem<nbsp>6.32> now bounds the word
  with complex gaps <math|h<rsub|i>> by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<tr><big|prod><rsub|i>B<rsub|i>*T<rsup|h<rsub|i>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2*s>*<space|0.17em>Z<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h<rsub|i>/M>\<leqslant\>A<rsup|q>*Z,
  </equation*>

  provided their real parts are nonnegative and their sum equals the original
  total free length. This uses the spectral identity
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|h>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|M/<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h>=Z<rsup|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>h/M>>.

  Moving one insertion by <math|l> lattice units changes two gaps to
  <math|H<rsub|0>-z,H<rsub|q-1>+z>. If both original gaps exceed <math|2*R>,
  the normalized word is analytic and bounded by <math|A<rsup|q>> for
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2*R>. Cauchy's estimate, integrated from
  <math|0> to <math|l\<leqslant\>R>, gives a difference at most
  <math|A<rsup|q>*l/R>. With <math|R=\<rho\>/a<rsub|N>>, this is
  <math|A<rsup|q>*l*a<rsub|N>/\<rho\>>, a physical Lipschitz bound
  independent of the mesh. The chessboard bound is chosen at one fixed
  interior cell scale below the coordinate separation <math|\<rho\>>.

  The transfer kernels act on a compact continuous slice space. The
  finite-matrix estimate passes to them by common tagged partitions: replace
  each continuous kernel <math|K*<around|(|x,y|)>> by the matrix
  <math|<sqrt|\<mu\><around|(|C<rsub|u>|)>*\<mu\><around|(|C<rsub|v>|)>>*K*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|u>,\<xi\><rsub|v>|)>>.
  Positivity of <math|T> is preserved. Every fixed cyclic trace converges to
  its cyclic kernel integral by uniform continuity, including the partition
  trace and the finitely many norms obtained from reflected copies of the
  slab. Since <math|Z\<gtr\>0>, the normalized endpoint inequalities pass to
  the limit. Thus the argument does not assume a finite-dimensional transfer
  space. It supplies the modulus at the separated correlation configurations
  needed in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:absorption>, without using the
  difference estimate that lemma is intended to prove.

  Recall that <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> transports a cutoff coefficient
  to its fixed reference level. A comparison coefficient is admissible when
  its inverse coordinates under <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> have a uniform
  subpower bound. The graph norm still combines the regular majorant with the
  large-field majorant multiplied by <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>>.

  <section|Backward comparison of a local trial><label|ch11:trial-descent>

  The inhomogeneous graph estimate controls local trials as well. Put
  <math|s<rsub|i>=L<rsub|i><rsup|1/8>>, <math|e<rsub|i>=s<rsub|i><rsup|-3>>
  and <math|A<rsub|i>=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>. Choose
  <math|100000*K<rsub|i><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>>,
  so the raw error is at most <math|e<rsub|i>>. Subtract one normalized local
  insertion density from the terminal trial, choosing its reference
  coefficient so the bottom residual coefficient is zero. Invertibility
  permits this choice; exact linearity propagates the same subtraction
  through every step. Let <math|x<rsub|i>> be the residual coefficient and
  <math|c<rsub|i>> its complete remainder norm bound, including the
  subtracted graph of normalized local insertions. With
  <math|y<rsub|i>=<around|\||S<rsub|L<rsub|i>>*x<rsub|i+1>|\|>>, the raw
  inequalities give

  <\equation*>
    y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*<around|{|<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>+e<rsub|i>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>+c<rsub|i+1>|)>|}>,<space|2em>c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>*y<rsub|i>+e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|i+1>|\|>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|A<rsub|i><around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i>>,
  substitution gives <math|y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>4*K<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i+1>>.
  The ratio choice ensures <math|4*K<rsub|i>*A<rsub|i+1>*e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|<around|(|4*K<rsub|i><rsup|2>+1|)>*s<rsub|i>*e<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Hence <math|A<rsub|i+1><around|\||x<rsub|i+1>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>
  and <math|s<rsub|i>*c<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|i+1>>. The induction starts
  at the bottom with zero coefficient. If the terminal trial has coefficient
  plus remainder cost at most <math|w>, the bound on the graph of normalized
  local insertions gives <math|c<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>w+C<rsub|N>*<around|(|w+<around|\||x<rsub|N>|\|>|)>>.
  Absorb <math|<around|\||x<rsub|N>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|N>/A<rsub|N>> to
  obtain <math|c<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*w>. Thus

  <\equation>
    c<rsub|i>+<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*w*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|N>-\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>/8>.<label|ch11:descent-error>
  </equation>

  For a fixed-stencil trial <math|f=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*F>, extract its
  degree-<math|e> coefficient <math|z> and subtract the corresponding
  physical section. Split the difference with zero polynomial coefficient
  into its small-field part on the union stencil <math|D> and its
  complementary part. Attach the latter to the original selected large-field
  factors; the terminal density is unchanged. If <math|c<rsub|N,p>> bounds
  local derivatives and <math|b<rsub|N,p>> the global value, sufficient costs
  are

  <\equation*>
    q=K<rsub|e>*c<rsub|N,p>,<space|1em>h=c<rsub|N,p>+C<rsub|N>*q,<space|1em>r=b<rsub|N,p>+2*A<rsub|e>*\<cal-N\><rsub|N>*q,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|N>=1+q+e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>*<around|(|h+2<around|\||D|\|>*r|)>,<space|2em>E<rsub|N,p>=4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>+e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|N>+162>*r*\<zeta\><rsub|e,N>*u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|A<rsub|e>> bounds the global section, <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|N>>
  is its finite sum of inverse threshold powers, <math|\<sigma\><rsub|N>> the
  support exponent, <math|u<rsub|N>\<gtr\>0> the terminal attachment reserve,
  and <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,N>> its source weight. Eventually the fixed
  stencil has cover at most81; two extra support units cost
  <math|e<rsup|162>>. The first term absorbs the coefficient and ordinary
  remainder; the second absorbs the complementary attachment with its
  original prescribed set of large-field sites. If
  <math|c<rsub|N,p>,b<rsub|N,p>,C<rsub|N>,e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>,\<cal-N\><rsub|N>,u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>
  are subpower in <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, then so is <math|E<rsub|N,p>>.

  Descent and the product-moment estimate now give, at every fixed stopping
  pair <math|<around|(|m,k|)>>, a reference label <math|v<rsub|N,p>> with
  inverse terminal coordinates bounded by <math|2*E<rsub|N,p>> and

  <\equation>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|m>*<around|{|F-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|e,v<rsub|N,p>|)>|}>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|e,m,k>*E<rsub|N,p>*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|N>/8>.<label|ch11:trial-error>
  </equation>

  Indeed the residual source weight is inversely proportional to its bound in
  <eqref|ch11:descent-error>. All constants are independent of period when
  the terminal norm bounds are. Thus the discrepancy for local trials tends
  to zero in the chessboard seminorm. The same estimate applies to finite
  differences formed from translates, using the derivative bounds on the
  translated stencils. It bounds inverse terminal coordinates, not the raw
  reference labels.

  <section|Finite-dimensional detection before taking
  limits><label|ch11:detectors>

  A useful part of the later difference argument is entirely finite
  dimensional. We record it now, keeping its analytic inputs visible. Let
  <math|V> be a fixed finite-dimensional label space,
  <math|E<rsub|i>\<subset\>V> the equation subspace at a finite cutoff, and
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>> a bounded projection with kernel <math|E<rsub|i>>.
  Such projections need not converge. Choose a fixed invariant Euclidean
  inner product and average an orthogonal projection with this kernel over
  the finite symmetry group, assuming <math|E<rsub|i>> is invariant. Each
  conjugate kills <math|E<rsub|i>> and differs from the identity by a map
  into <math|E<rsub|i>>. The average has these two properties, which imply
  idempotence: if <math|v-\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v\<in\>E<rsub|i>>, then
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|v-\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|)>=0>. Its norm remains
  bounded uniformly in <math|i>.

  Let <math|L<rsub|i>:V\<to\><CC><rsup|r>> be the finite family of
  correlation functionals used to detect the quotient. Suppose

  <\equation>
    <around|\||\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\|>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\||L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\|><label|ch11:detector-bound>
  </equation>

  with one constant <math|Z>, independent of cutoff and physical volume. This
  follows from a sufficiently accurate finite-cutoff comparison with an
  injective free matrix of correlation pairings: if
  <math|<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>K*<around|\||G*c|\|>> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q-G|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*K|)><rsup|-1>>,
  then <math|<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>K\|Q*c\|+<tfrac|1|2><around|\||c|\|>>,
  hence <math|<around|\||c|\|>\<leqslant\>2*K*<around|\||Q*c|\|>>. The
  separating family of free correlations comes from the finite Maxwell
  quotient; no continuum rank assertion is needed for this argument.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Absorption of a possibly large equation
    component><label|ch11:absorption>Let <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>V> be encoded
    coefficients of a normalized lattice difference and put
    <math|y<rsub|i>=\<Pi\><rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>>. Suppose
    <math|<around|\||L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C> and, for

    <\equation*>
      z<rsub|i>=<frac|x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>|1+<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>>,
    </equation*>

    the microscopic equation comparison gives
    <math|<around|\||L<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*Z|)><rsup|-1>>
    eventually, uniformly in the physical volume. Then
    <math|<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*Z*C+1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Since <math|L<rsub|i>*y<rsub|i>=L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>-<around|(|1+<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>|)>*L<rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>>,
    <eqref|ch11:detector-bound> gives <math|<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>Z*C+<tfrac|1|2><around|(|1+<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>|)>>.
    Move the last half-norm to the left.
  </proof>

  The normalized equation coefficient <math|z<rsub|i>> lies in <math|ker
  \<Pi\><rsub|i>>. Applying an equation estimate to it still requires the
  estimate's growth hypothesis. For instance, if <math|x<rsub|i>> has a
  subpower coefficient bound, then <math|z<rsub|i>> has one: its first term
  is a bounded scalar multiple of <math|x<rsub|i>>, while
  <math|y<rsub|i>/<around|(|1+<around|\||y<rsub|i>|\|>|)>> is bounded by one.
  Thus the argument does not presuppose a uniform bound for the raw
  difference coefficient <math|x<rsub|i>>.

  There is also an exact way to test finite differences using bounded
  separated observations. For a lattice spacing <math|a> and integer
  <math|h\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|h>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|h-1><frac|F*<around|(|x+<around|(|j+1|)>*a*e|)>-F*<around|(|x+j*a*e|)>|a>=<frac|F*<around|(|x+h*a*e|)>-F<around|(|x|)>|h*a>.
  </equation*>

  Taking <math|h=<around|\<lceil\>|b/a|\<rceil\>>> bounds the right side by
  <math|2*sup <around|\||F|\|>/b>. To replace averaged correlation evaluation
  positions by the desired positions, however, one needs a positional modulus
  established independently of the normalized difference estimate being
  proved. That modulus is an essential analytic input, not another
  consequence of this telescoping identity. Likewise the microscopic equation
  insertion must vanish against the actual distant spectator product at
  finite cutoff, uniformly over the physical volumes under consideration. An
  identity obtained only after an ordered limit would not justify the
  absorption lemma's uniform hypothesis.

  <section|Collected estimates and local calibration>

  The chapter now supplies the quantitative single-insertion raw-map
  estimates <eqref|ch11:graph-estimates>\U<eqref|ch11:graph-difference>,
  including ball invariance and fixed-input continuity; the complete
  recurrence for terms involving at least two insertions
  <eqref|ch11:higher-induction>; and the normalized product-moment estimate
  <eqref|ch11:replicated-bound>. The exact tree covariance
  <eqref|ch11:conditional-curvature> and the local estimates in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:actual-reader> identify the actual common
  Gaussian used for the principal components. The source weights and positive
  support reserves appearing in these statements must be included in the
  selected stage requirements.

  For a finite cutoff, composing the invertible single-insertion coefficient
  maps from <math|N> to the reference level defines
  <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>>. It is real and symmetry-equivariant, and
  the exact unit rule gives the reference unit coefficient
  <math|a<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>. The physical insertion is
  therefore

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|N,p><around|(|d,c;x|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|S<rsub|1/t<rsub|N>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  with the factor-degree scaling <math|S<rsub|t>> of the local section.
  Uniform column estimates follow by multiplying the displayed one-step
  inverse bounds; their subpower growth requires the corresponding selected
  schedule inequalities.

  There remains a distinct local calibration issue before claiming the
  all-period finite-difference theorem. A translated finite difference of
  degree <math|d> has a protected local representative at degree <math|d+1>:

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|N><rsup|d+1>*\<Delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>>*O<around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*O<around|(|x+a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*O<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  This cancels the explicit <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, but moving the
  derivative-weight origin costs <math|2<rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|N>*q<rsub|N>>>.
  The stage entrance must include <math|2<rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|N>*q<rsub|N>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>
  to keep that cost subpower. The same requirement applies to repeated fixed
  finite differences.

  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent> now supplies the trial encoding
  with subpower inverse terminal coordinates and a vanishing
  chessboard-seminorm error, under its explicit terminal and stage bounds.
  The microscopic equation section and its comparison with the physical
  section are supplied next. The argument with the separating family proves
  the finite-cutoff comparisons needed for uniform differences.

  <section|Calibrating the microscopic equations on the same
  history><label|ch11:equation-calibration>

  Fix a degree <math|d>. The section <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>> and
  automorphism <math|R<rsub|d>> are those of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch02:equation-adapted>; neither is chosen again
  when the cutoff or period changes. The physical section remains
  <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>. We compare the two on the history
  already used to define <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>>.

  We first specify an additional finite stage requirement. At stage <math|j>,
  let <math|a<rsub|d*j>> bound the derivatives of <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>
  requested by that stage, for every <math|d\<leqslant\>j>. These are finite
  constants by the powered-curvature-factor estimate in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch02:observables>. Let <math|q<rsub|d>> bound the
  fixed union stencil and put

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|d*j>=2+e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|j>>*<around|(|a<rsub|d*j>+C<rsub|j>+2*q<rsub|d>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>> is the support exponent and <math|C<rsub|j>>
  the column bound. These quantities are fixed before the block length. Let
  <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d>\<geqslant\>1> bound both the finite symmetry
  actions on coefficient <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm and their averaging
  coefficients, and put <math|\<delta\><rsub|d>=<around|(|2*\<cal-O\><rsub|d><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  Include the finite maximum

  <\equation>
    L<rsup|1/8>\<geqslant\>max<rsub|d\<leqslant\>j>
    2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j>|)>*Q<rsub|d*j>*\<delta\><rsub|d><rsup|-1><label|ch11:calibration-floor>
  </equation>

  in the stage-<math|j> block floor. Include
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>> in
  the adjacent stage conditions on the starting scale. These requests are
  compatible with the selection order: the section and its derivative
  constants are fixed first, then the length, then the inverse coupling
  threshold. Raising a later threshold does not change the section. Thus they
  can be imposed for all stages before selecting the common history, with no
  dependence on <math|p>.

  Define the normalized microscopic section at the origin by

  <\equation*>
    O<rsup|\<cal-V\>><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*<around|[|S<rsub|1/t<rsub|N>>*v|]><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The factor scaling <math|S<rsub|1/t<rsub|N>>> commutes with
  <math|R<rsub|d>> only when <math|R<rsub|d>> preserves factor count, which
  is not asserted here. Accordingly its exact equation labels at this
  normalization are

  <\equation>
    I<rsub|N,d><rsup|\<cal-V\>>=S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|d>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>.<label|ch11:scaled-equation-labels>
  </equation>

  Since <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> is homogeneous in factor count, one can
  equally write <math|I<rsub|N,d><rsup|\<cal-V\>>=<around|(|S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|d>*S<rsub|1/t<rsub|N>>|)>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>.
  This conjugation must be retained when using a cutoff-independent unscaled
  equation section.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|One comparison map for all equation
    labels><label|ch11:equation-comparison>Under
    <eqref|ch11:calibration-floor> and the terminal attachment bounds of
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent>, there is, for every
    sufficiently large <math|N> and every <math|p>, one real equivariant map
    <math|P<rsub|N,p,d>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|N,p,d>-I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1\<to\>1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1\<to\>1>\<leqslant\>2.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed stopping pair <math|<around|(|m,k|)>>,

    <\equation>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|m>*<space|-0.17em><around*|{|O<rsup|\<cal-V\>><rsub|N,p><around|(|v|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|d,<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>P<rsub|N,p,d>*v;0|)>|}>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,m,k><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*E<rsub|N>*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|N>/8>,<label|ch11:equation-root-error>
    </equation>

    where <math|E<rsub|N>> is subpower in <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>,
    uniformly in <math|p>. The same map works for every spectator; it is
    constructed before any spectator is specified.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For each basis vector, the exact terminal decomposition used in
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent> applies to the fixed section
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>>. Its part with zero polynomial coefficient is
    localized on the union stencil; the complementary part is attached to the
    original large-field selection, with no change of density. It gives
    coefficient <math|e<rsub|j>>, ordinary norm bound
    <math|Q<rsub|N>=Q<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>>, and complete source norm
    bound

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|N>=4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>+e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|N>+162>*Z<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|val>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>*u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|Z<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|val>>=<around|(|A<rsub|\<cal-V\>>+2*A<rsub|<math-up|loc>>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j>t<rsub|N><rsup|-s<rsub|j>>>,
    with <math|A<rsub|\<cal-V\>>,A<rsub|<math-up|loc>>> the global value
    norms of the two fixed sections. The ordinary terminal norm bound uses a
    sharper attachment estimate than the general trial bound. If
    <math|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>> is the terminal large-field bound
    and <math|D> the union stencil, its contribution to the remainder norm
    normalized by <math|<sqrt|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>> is

    <\equation*>
      2<around|\||D|\|><sqrt|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|N>*c<rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|D|)>>*Z<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|val>>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed attach the complementary local function to the original nonempty
    prescribed large-field set and sum its possible distinguished sites in
    <math|D>; the remaining large-field suppression bounds this normalized
    mass. The same exact attachment retains reserved mass
    <math|e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|N>+2|)>*c<rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|D|)>>*Z<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|val>>>.
    Impose the additional conditions on the starting scale

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D|\|>*w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>c<rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|D|)>\<leqslant\>81,<space|2em><sqrt|w<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>*Z<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|val>>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|<around|\||D|\|>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|d>> bounds the ordinary
    contribution by <math|2*q<rsub|d>*e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>>,
    explaining exactly the definition of <math|Q<rsub|N>>. These are
    post-length threshold conditions. At fixed adjacent stages the value norm
    is a fixed polynomial in the inverse small-field threshold, whereas the
    large-field bound decays faster than every such power. Their finite
    simultaneous threshold therefore exists. The unweighted reserved mass is
    not discarded. This last term is required: the ordinary derivative bound
    alone does not bound the complementary large-field contribution.

    Backward comparison gives a reference coefficient <math|v<rsub|j>> and
    the chessboard-seminorm error in <eqref|ch11:trial-error>. Put
    <math|u<rsub|j>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>v<rsub|j>>. There is a
    slightly stronger endpoint estimate than the displayed bulk descent
    bound. At the last step its cone inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||e<rsub|j>-u<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*K<rsub|N-1>*L<rsub|N-1><rsup|-3/8>*c<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|N-1><rsup|-1/8>*c<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|N>|)>*Q<rsub|N>*L<rsub|N-1><rsup|-1/8>.
    </equation*>

    The middle inequality follows from the previously imposed block floor.
    Requirement <eqref|ch11:calibration-floor> bounds this by
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|d>>.

    Let <math|U*e<rsub|j>=u<rsub|j>>. Average <math|U> by conjugation with
    the finite geometric symmetry group, and then with the real structure,
    obtaining <math|P>. For the conjugation term use
    <math|<wide|U*<wide|v|\<bar\>>|\<bar\>>>, so the result is still complex
    linear. The norm of <math|P-I> is at most
    <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|d><rsup|2>*\<delta\><rsub|d>=1/2>, and the Neumann
    series gives the stated inverse bound. Apply the same averaging to the
    field errors. Linearity and the triangle inequality for the chessboard
    seminorm give <eqref|ch11:equation-root-error>, enlarging its fixed
    constant to cover the finite orbit. This preserves one common history,
    all source monomials, and one common map.

    Finally <math|C<rsub|N>>, <math|e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|N>>>,
    <math|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,N>>, and
    <math|u<rsub|N><rsup|-1>> have the subpower bounds already required for
    trial descent. The extra condition on the starting scale bounds
    <math|a<rsub|d,J<around|(|N|)>>> by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>>,
    which is subpower. Finite sums and products preserve this property,
    proving the claim for <math|E<rsub|N>>.
  </proof>

  The varying equation subspace in reference coordinates is therefore

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|E><rsub|N,p,d>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>P<rsub|N,p,d>*S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|d>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>.<label|ch11:actual-equation-subspace>
  </equation>

  For <math|v<rsub|N>\<in\>I<rsub|N,d><rsup|\<cal-V\>>> of subpower norm, the
  first term in <eqref|ch11:equation-root-error> has exactly zero correlation
  against every bounded spectator outside its microscopic stencil, by
  <eqref|ch02:microscopic-null>. For fixed positive physical separation, this
  exclusion holds at every sufficiently fine mesh, uniformly in period.
  Holder's chessboard bound then shows that the physical insertion with
  coefficient <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>P<rsub|N,p,d>*v<rsub|N>> has
  vanishing separated correlations against any finite family of spectators
  with bounded chessboard seminorms. This proves the equation inclusion
  needed for the argument using a finite separating family; equality with the
  full null space still uses the free nondegeneracy and detection estimates.

  The scaling in <eqref|ch11:actual-equation-subspace> also has a useful
  check. Since <math|R<rsub|d>-I> never lowers factor count and
  <math|t<rsub|N>\<to\>0>, the conjugate <math|S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|d>*S<rsub|1/t<rsub|N>>>
  remains bounded. Its strictly factor-raising terms tend to zero. Terms at
  equal factor count can still raise scaling dimension and need not tend to
  zero under this scaling alone. Thus the raw triangular construction and the
  near-identity comparison <math|P> do not, by themselves, prove that this
  equation subspace approaches the unmodified Maxwell ideal. No such
  identification is required here: use bounded projections with kernel
  exactly <eqref|ch11:actual-equation-subspace> and establish the bound from
  the separating family <eqref|ch11:detector-bound> on their complements. The
  terminal map <math|<math-ss|Z>> already includes the scaling-dimension
  evolution; its physical convention has not been changed.

  <section|The moving equation space and uniform finite
  differences><label|ch11:uniform-differences>

  All eventual assertions in this section mean: there is <math|N<rsub|0>>
  such that the assertion holds for every <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and
  every period <math|p>. A bound after first fixing <math|p> is insufficient.
  Write <math|i=<around|(|N,p|)>>, <math|X<rsub|i>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,e>>,
  and

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|i>=X<rsub|i>*P<rsub|N,p,e>*S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|e>*\<cal-I\><rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  This is precisely the equation space <eqref|ch11:actual-equation-subspace>.
  Every factor is invertible, so <math|dim K<rsub|i>=dim \<cal-I\><rsub|e>>
  at each finite cutoff. Choose the bounded equivariant projections
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>> with kernel <math|K<rsub|i>> as in the construction
  of the separating family above, and fix
  <math|A\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Their rank is <math|r=dim <around|(|V<rsub|e>/\<cal-I\><rsub|e>|)>>,
  without any limiting rank assertion.

  Call a coefficient family <math|z<rsub|i>> admissible here if
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> is
  bounded by a sequence subpower in <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>, independent of
  <math|p>. The inverse-column bound makes every bounded coefficient family
  admissible. This class is closed under finite sums and multiplication by
  scalar families of modulus at most one.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Vanishing chessboard seminorms for the actual equation
    space><label|ch11:uniform-sweep>If <math|z<rsub|i>> is admissible and
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*z<rsub|i>=0>, its physical insertion has chessboard
    seminorm tending to zero, uniformly in <math|p>, at every sufficiently
    fine fixed cell scale. The assertion includes all its finitely many
    signed and conjugated orientations.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|v<rsub|i>=P<rsub|N,p,e><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|i>>.
    Its norm is subpower, and <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|e>*\<cal-I\><rsub|e>>.
    The error in <eqref|ch11:equation-root-error> is therefore at most
    <math|e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|N>/16>> eventually, uniformly in <math|p>,
    after increasing the fixed mesh threshold.

    The microscopic comparison insertion itself has chessboard seminorm
    exactly zero. Indeed, every other reflected copy lies outside its fixed
    microscopic equation neighborhood once the mesh is sufficiently fine
    relative to the fixed physical cell. Apply <eqref|ch02:microscopic-null>
    to one copy and the product of all other copies. That product is bounded
    and measurable at each finite cutoff, regardless of the size of its
    coefficients. There is no limiting argument in this identity. The
    smallest allowed physical period is positive, so one mesh threshold
    ensures the required nonwrapping and separation for every period. The
    triangle inequality for the chessboard seminorm proves the claim.
  </proof>

  In the following local comparison, <math|S<rsub|t>> multiplies each basis
  coordinate by <math|t> to its curvature-factor count, whereas
  <math|W<rsub|\<rho\>>> uses scaling dimension. Neither is the terminal map
  <math|<math-ss|Z>>. The equation correction <math|R<rsub|d>> and its ideal
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> are those of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch02:equation-adapted>;
  the separate map <math|P<rsub|N,p,d>> compares the two local sections.

  <subsection|A common localization map for the separating
  family><label|ch11:common-extraction>

  The map used to normalize free comparison columns cannot be chosen
  independently of the polynomial actually integrated. Here is the
  construction from the actual single-insertion representation. Fix a
  polynomial degree <math|D\<geqslant\>d>, to be increased below, before the
  stage derivative order; require <math|D+1> derivatives. At comparison depth
  <math|k> put

  <\equation*>
    s=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>,<space|1em>H=e<rsup|<around|(|log log
    s|)><rsup|2>>,<space|1em>M=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>B=M<rsup|2>,<space|1em>b=8*B,<space|1em>\<gamma\>=<frac|1|16*<around|(|D+2|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The original insertion norm uses radius <math|M>, whereas the restarted
  representation uses radius <math|B>. These are different radii.

  Let <math|A*u> be the actual local polynomial coefficient in physical
  normalization. The calibrated single-insertion bound controls the complete
  regular series by <math|C<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  Split it at original connected cover number <math|M>. The part with cover
  number at most <math|M> is contained in the radius-<math|b> patch: a
  connected chain of <math|M> original-radius cubes has diameter at most
  <math|4*M<rsup|2>+4*M\<leqslant\>8*M<rsup|2>>. The remaining part has norm
  at most <math|\<eta\>*C<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  with <math|\<eta\>=e<rsup|-e*M>>, by retaining one positive support
  exponent <math|e>. Keep its original event indicators and all original
  large-field terms. Only the near part is replaced below.

  Use logarithmic link coordinates <math|U<rsub|e>=exp
  <around|(|\<theta\>*x<rsub|e>|)>>, <math|\<theta\>=s<rsup|-1/2>*H>, in this
  patch. The Taylor polynomial through ordinary degree <math|D> of the near
  regular sum is well defined. Indeed the series of derivatives is absolutely
  bounded by the regular norm. Polarizing the directional derivatives bounds
  its coefficient sum by <math|C<rsub|D>*<around|(|1+n<rsub|b>|)><rsup|D>*C<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  where <math|n<rsub|b>=O<around|(|b<rsup|4>|)>> is the number of real link
  coordinates. The Taylor remainder is bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsup|D+1>>,
  up to a fixed constant. The finite degree-<math|d> localization by radial
  Taylor expansion of the near sum is its prescribed local polynomial
  coordinate: all regular remainders have zero extracted polynomial. Applying
  the extraction to a Taylor polynomial instead of the actual holonomy curve
  changes this by at most <math|C*<around|(|1+b|)><rsup|a>*\<theta\>> times
  its coefficient norm. This follows from the local holonomy equation: the
  normalized logarithm of a threshold holonomy and its linearized edge
  integral differ by <math|\<theta\>> times a fixed-degree polynomial in the
  link location. Only finitely many terms contribute through degree <math|d>.

  All constants just used are fixed before <math|s>. The stage delay can
  impose

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|D>*<around|(|1+n<rsub|b>|)><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>,<space|2em>C*<around|(|1+b|)><rsup|a>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>.
  </equation*>

  The second and third inequalities follow from <math|log b=O<around|(|log
  log s|)>=o<around|(|log H|)>>; the first is a delay for the already
  selected stage column bound. The discarded-support factor <math|\<eta\>>
  and the nonlinear ray error <math|\<theta\>> are smaller than every fixed
  inverse power of <math|H>. Consequently the ordinary linear radial Taylor
  localization of the resulting polynomial agrees with <math|A*u> up to
  relative error <math|H<rsup|-1>>, uniformly in <math|N,p,u>.

  Convert links to linear sums of based plaquette curvatures in the axial
  gauge on this same patch. Let <math|W<rsub|u>> be the resulting raw
  polynomial in curvature divided by <math|g=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|-1/2>>.
  Each axial row has coefficient sum at most <math|4*b*g/\<theta\>>. The
  inverse coupling band gives <math|g/\<theta\>\<leqslant\><sqrt|8/7>*<space|0.17em>H<rsup|-1>>,
  so this row sum is eventually at most one. Thus <math|deg
  W<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>D> and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|u>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>\<leqslant\>H*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  after the finite delays. The construction is linear in <math|u> because the
  Taylor polynomial of the actual function is unique; it does not require a
  linear choice of its separate activity representatives.

  The raw polynomial also approximates the same physical insertion on the
  restarted small-field region. Its threshold is
  <math|\<vartheta\>=s<rsup|-1/2>*e<rsup|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|3/2>>>.
  Put <math|a=4*b*\<vartheta\>>. The actual axial link logarithm divided by
  <math|\<theta\>> and the linear sum of plaquette curvatures divided by
  <math|\<theta\>> differ by at most <math|C<rsub|G>*a<rsup|2>/\<theta\>>,
  and their size is at most <math|2*a/\<theta\>=H<rsup|-1+o<around|(|1|)>>>.
  Taylor's remainder and replacement of one monomial factor at a time
  therefore give

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:raw-polynomial-error><around|\||F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>><around|(|U|)>-W<rsub|u>*<around|(|g<rsup|-1>*F<around|(|U|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-D>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation>

  For example the Taylor term is at most <math|4*H<rsup|\<gamma\>>*H<rsup|<around|(|-1+\<gamma\>|)>*<around|(|D+1|)>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=4*H<rsup|-D-15/16>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  The nonlinear coordinate error and discarded-support error are smaller than
  any required inverse power. For normalized target columns with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|c<rsub|d>>>, choose
  <math|D\<gtr\>c<rsub|d>+v> before the stage orders to obtain error
  <math|H<rsup|-v>>.

  Let <math|K<rsub|k>> be the original same-insertion covariance of the
  actual Gaussian comparison. Define the polynomial used in Wick calculations
  by

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|cal*|P>*u=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<Delta\><rsub|K<rsub|k>>|)>*W<rsub|u>.
  </equation*>

  This sign is deliberate: its evaluation with covariance whose diagonal
  block is decreased by <math|K<rsub|k>> equals the Gaussian evaluation of
  <math|W<rsub|u>> with the undecreased covariance. The identity follows by
  multiplying the commuting finite operators <math|exp
  <around|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<Delta\><rsub|K<rsub|k>>|)>> and <math|exp
  <around|(|-<tfrac|1|2>\<Delta\><rsub|K<rsub|k>>|)>>. It remains valid for
  indefinite symmetric covariances.

  Set <math|z=\<theta\>/g\<geqslant\><sqrt|7/8>*<space|0.17em>H>. For
  <math|p<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>=W<rsub|u>*<around|(|z*y|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|exp <around|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<Delta\><rsub|K<rsub|k>>|)>*W<rsub|u>|)>*<around|(|z*y|)>=exp
    <around|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<Delta\><rsub|z<rsup|-2>*K<rsub|k>>|)>*p<rsub|u><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  The rescaled polynomial <math|p<rsub|u>> is the Taylor polynomial with the
  factor <math|g/\<theta\>> in its axial substitution cancelled. Its
  remaining axial rows have norm at most <math|4*b>. Since
  <math|<around|(|4*b|)><rsup|D>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<gamma\>>> eventually,
  its coefficient bound is <math|4*H<rsup|3*\<gamma\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<alpha\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  where <math|\<alpha\>=4*\<gamma\>=1/<around|(|4*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>>. The
  finitely many radial Taylor coefficient functionals likewise have
  coefficient norm at most <math|H<rsup|\<alpha\>>> after the stage delay.
  These bounds are needed before applying the Wick estimate. The actual
  covariance has entry bound at most <math|H<rsup|1/4>> after a stage delay,
  so the rescaled covariance entries are at most <math|H<rsup|-1>>
  eventually. For polynomials of degree at most <math|D>, the coefficient
  norm of the difference between this Wick operator and the identity is at
  most

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|-1>*w<rsub|D>,<space|2em>w<rsub|D>=<tfrac|1|2>D<rsup|2>*e<rsup|D<rsup|2>/2>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed a contraction has coefficient norm at most <math|D<rsup|2>/2>; sum
  the finite exponential series, bounding its terms in absolute value. This
  bound is independent of the number of ambient coordinates because the
  polynomial coefficient norm already sums their coefficients.

  Let <math|\<cal-E\>> denote exposure in the fixed radial polynomial frame,
  and choose <math|c<rsub|d>> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|d>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-E\>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|coeff>>>.
  The low-weight coefficient extraction of <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P>*u>
  belongs to this frame: the original covariance is a real spatial matrix
  tensored with the colour identity, so its contractions commute with the
  adjoint colour action. Axial conversion and weighted truncation also
  commute with that action. Define <math|T*u> to be precisely these frame
  coefficients, retaining the inverse coupling scaling above. Using
  <math|\<alpha\>=1/<around|(|4*<around|(|D+2|)>|)>>, the preceding
  coefficient bounds, linear substitution, and the Wick estimate give

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|A*u-T*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|d>*<around|{|H<rsup|-1>+D*2<rsup|D>*H<rsup|-1+\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+2|)>>+w<rsub|D>*H<rsup|-1+\<alpha\>*<around|(|D+1|)>>|}>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>
  </equation*>

  eventually. Constants and conjugation are preserved at every operation,
  hence <math|T*1=1> and <math|T*<wide|u|\<bar\>>=<wide|T*u|\<bar\>>>. This
  constructs the same real linear map for every column; it is not an
  arbitrary replacement for <math|A>. The inverse bound and simultaneous lift
  equations stated below therefore apply to this actual localization onto the
  polynomial label space.

  Finally let <math|\<chi\>> be the restarted small-field indicator on the
  whole patch. Define <math|f<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near,mask>>> to be only
  the near sum with its original event indicators restored, including its
  scalar term. It equals the without its event indicator
  <math|F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>> wherever <math|\<chi\>=1>, because
  the restarted threshold is smaller than the original one. Then

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near,mask>>=\<chi\>*W<rsub|u>+\<chi\>*<around|(|F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>-W<rsub|u>|)>+<around|(|f<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near,mask>>-\<chi\>*F<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|near>>|)>
  </equation*>

  is exact. The second term has the bound <eqref|ch11:raw-polynomial-error>;
  the third vanishes wherever <math|\<chi\>=1> and is assigned to the
  original prescribed large-field set. The distant regular sum and original
  large-field terms were retained throughout. Substituting this identity into
  the finite squarefree exponential preserves every higher source variable
  coefficient. Thus the actual density, polynomial
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P>>, coefficient extraction <math|T>, and
  normalized lifts all refer to the same terminal insertion.

  The common extraction may also be chosen equivariant under the finite
  signed coordinate group. Average the raw polynomial construction on domain
  and range before defining <math|T>. The actual insertion, covariance and
  localization by radial Taylor expansion have the corresponding covariance
  identities, so this preserves both the exact density comparison and its
  low-weight coefficient extraction. All errors are enlarged by at most a
  fixed finite symmetry norm. Since their relative bounds tend to zero,
  choose the threshold to make the averaged error at most one half. Average
  with conjugation at the same time. This uses one polynomial construction
  for all transformed columns, rather than symmetrizing independently chosen
  lifted vectors.

  At a fixed comparison depth the inverse front-column bounds give
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  uniformly in <math|N,p>. The relative half-error therefore implies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|T*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  so <math|T> is injective and hence invertible. Let
  <math|v<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|r>> be real representatives of the free
  separating family. Write <math|W<rsub|\<rho\>>> for scaling-dimension
  dilation and <math|S<rsub|t>> for factor-count dilation. With
  <math|\<rho\>=H<rsup|b>> solve all the equations

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:simultaneous-detector-lifts>S<rsub|g/\<theta\>>*T*u<rsub|j,N,p>=W<rsub|\<rho\>>*v<rsub|j>.
  </equation>

  They have unique real solutions using the same <math|T>. The inverse
  coupling band gives <math|\<theta\>/g\<leqslant\>2*H>, and therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j,N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*D<rsub|k>*<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|2*H>*W<rsub|\<rho\>>*v<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Their sum is a finite bound <math|B<rsub|k>> independent of cutoff and
  period. No bound uniform in comparison depth is needed. Moreover
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|T*u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>
  is bounded by a fixed power <math|H<rsup|b*d+c<rsub|d>>>, where the
  additional exponent <math|c<rsub|d>> depends on factor counts and the
  finite target list, not on <math|b>. This is the amplification used when
  choosing the Taylor order and the geometric magnification.

  Here <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>> is the connected cover number at radius
  <math|B>. The support exponent multiplies <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>> in
  an exponential norm, whereas a source weight is raised to the number of
  distinct insertion labels. Changing one of these weights does not change
  the coefficient or its original prescribed large-field set.

  <subsection|Absorbing the larger source
  weight><label|ch11:weight-restoration>

  A bound by one at the original source weight is not enough for the
  comparison of separating correlations. The following argument absorbs the
  necessary change of weight uniformly in the number of reflected cells.
  Write <math|n\<leqslant\>2> for the number of marks in each cell, fix
  <math|U\<geqslant\>1/16>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<omega\><rsub|->=H<rsup|1/16-U>,<space|2em>\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/16>.
  </equation*>

  For a marked activity with support <math|X> and source variable set
  <math|I>, its weighted absolute bound contains
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|-><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>*e<rsup|e<rsub|\<ast\>>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>>.
  Choose <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+2> before constructing the marked expansion.
  The same expansion, estimated at <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>, is used below;
  there is no change of coefficients when its support exponent is lowered.

  Each prescribed support is contained in the fixed enlargement of a
  radius-<math|A*B> ball around its cell's finitely many insertion points,
  with <math|A> chosen before the inverse coupling. A connected grid of
  radius-<math|B> cubes covers it with at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+A|)><rsup|4>> cubes. Thus its support bound at exponent
  <math|f> is bounded by <math|R<rsub|f>=exp
  <around|{|f*C*<around|(|1+A|)><rsup|4>|}>>, independent of <math|B> and the
  period. At successive stages these are finite pre-threshold constants. The
  stage choices of sufficiently large starting scales impose a common bound
  <math|H<rsup|\<epsilon\><rsub|j>>> on <math|R<rsub|e+2>>,
  <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>>, <math|L<rsup|4>*T<rsub|e+2>>, and the fixed
  incidence constants, with <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|j>*\<downarrow\>*0>.
  Consequently every finite product of these constants is
  <math|H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> along the selected comparison levels. This
  uniform delay, rather than treating a changing stage constant as an
  absolute constant, justifies the exponent comparisons below. This
  bounded-cover assertion is used at restoration levels. It is not an
  assertion that the separated opening sites at distance <math|H<rsup|b>> fit
  in one radius-<math|A*B> ball. The supports of their terms involving at
  least two insertions are instead estimated by
  <eqref|ch11:opening-higher-weight>. At an earlier leading step a nonzero
  connected principal component has radius at most
  <math|<around|(|6*n+3|)>*B> around each of its own marked insertion points;
  a term involving two insertions with more widely separated insertion points
  is absent. Only these nonzero components enter the principal support bound.

  Let <math|Y<rsub|c>> contain all prescribed supports in cell <math|c>.
  Suppose these sets have mutual distance at least <math|D<rprime|'>>, and
  every activity support contains the sites of all its marks. Let
  <math|t<around|(|I|)>> count the cells represented in <math|I>, and let
  <math|t<around|(|X|)>> count the sets <math|Y<rsub|c>> met by <math|X>.
  Then <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>n*t<around|(|I|)>\<leqslant\>n*t<around|(|X|)>>.
  For <math|t<around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>2>, a connected <math|B>-cover gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:dispersal>c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\>*t<around|(|X|)>,<space|2em>\<lambda\>=<frac|D<rprime|'>|3*<around|(|2*B+1|)>>,
  </equation>

  once <math|D<rprime|'>/3\<gtr\>2*B+1>. To see this, choose one point of
  <math|X\<cap\>Y<rsub|c>> per represented cell, and follow a path in its
  connected cover until distance <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|D<rprime|'>/3|\<rfloor\>>>
  from that point. A cube covers at most <math|2*B+1> of the successive
  distance values. The resulting initial path segments belong to disjoint
  distance balls, and a cube cannot meet two of them. Summing proves the
  inequality. Here connected cover means the connected cover used in the
  activity norm; one cannot replace it by an arbitrary disconnected cover of
  the marked sites.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Regular weight restoration><label|ch11:restore-regular> Suppose
    the regular remainder has rooted activity norm at most
    <math|H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>> at <math|<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|->,e+2|)>>,
    with support containing its marked sites, and <math|\<lambda\>/log
    H\<to\>\<infty\>>. If <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+U*n+2>, its rooted
    activity norm at <math|<around|(|\<omega\>,e|)>> is at most
    <math|H<rsup|-1>> for all sufficiently large <math|H>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Separate activities meeting one cell from those meeting at least two. The
    first class gains at most <math|H<rsup|U*n>> on changing weight. For the
    second class retain one additional unit in the exponent of the support
    weight and use

    <\equation*>
      H<rsup|U<around|\||I|\|>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|U*n*t<around|(|I|)>>*e<rsup|-\<lambda\>*t<around|(|I|)>>\<leqslant\>1
    </equation*>

    eventually. Thus the new bound is at most
    <math|<around|(|H<rsup|U*n>+1|)>*H<rsup|\<kappa\>-q>\<leqslant\>2*H<rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>>.
    The estimate is independent of the total number of cells.
  </proof>

  The support containment is preserved by the actual algebra. Initially a
  marked insertion contains its site. A coarse activity contains the
  projected union of the fine supports, and its source variable set is their
  union. Prescribed support enlargement, event indicator splitting and the
  connected regrouping all retain those projected points. This induction
  applies to both regular and large-field activities. For a window whose cell
  side at its lower comparison scale is at least <math|<around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|20>> times the middle lattice spacing, whereas
  <math|B\<leqslant\>4<around|(|log s|)><rsup|4>>, the cell neighborhoods
  have <math|D<rprime|'>\<geqslant\><around|(|log s|)><rsup|20>/2> after
  their fixed-radius enlargements. With <math|H=exp <around|(|<around|(|log
  log s|)><rsup|2>|)>> this proves <math|\<lambda\>/log H\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  uniformly in period. The condition concerns the distance between replica
  cells, not between the two insertions within one cell.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Large-field weight restoration><label|ch11:restore-forced>Suppose
    the old large-field site bound is <math|\<eta\><rsub|->> at
    <math|<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|->,e+2|)>>, where <math|\<eta\><rsub|->>
    decays faster than every inverse power of <math|H>. Suppose each cell has
    at most <math|2<rsup|n>> additional failed-cutoff factors, supported in
    <math|Y<rsub|c>>, whose coefficient bounds before source variable
    weighting are at most <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> and
    whose support bounds at exponent <math|e+1> are at most
    <math|R<rsub|e+1>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. Every nonzero such factor
    must meet an old large-field activity. Then their exact connected
    regrouping has site bound at most

    <\equation*>
      4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->
    </equation*>

    at <math|<around|(|\<omega\>,e|)>>, for all sufficiently large <math|H>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A failed-cutoff factor of nonconstant source monomial <math|I> occurs in
    the exact identity

    <\equation*>
      exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<chi\>*W-R|)>=exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<chi\>*<around|(|W-R|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>exp<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|-<around|(|1-\<chi\>|)>*R|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its last exponent is square zero for each source monomial. Expand it as a
    product of factors <math|1-<around|(|1-\<chi\>|)>*R*z<rsub|I>>. Whenever
    such a factor is nonzero, its support contains a large-field indicator
    covered by an original prescribed large-field set. Thus every surviving
    connected component contains an old large-field activity. This is a
    pointwise identity of the complete marked density.

    Put <math|F=log H>. A new failed-cutoff factor has bound
    <math|p<rsub|f>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/8>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|e+1>> and
    assign it auxiliary bound <math|w<rsub|f>=e<rsup|-F>*p<rsub|f>>. Assign
    an old activity <math|o> the auxiliary bound

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|o>=e<rsup|g>*e<rsup|2<rsup|n>*F*t<around|(|X<rsub|o>|)>>*p<rsub|o>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|p<rsub|o>> is its bound at <math|<around|(|\<omega\>,e+1|)>>.
    The original bound <math|p<rsub|o><rsup|->> at
    <math|<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|->,e+2|)>> gives

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|o>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|g>*H<rsup|<around|(|U*n+2<rsup|n>|)>*t<around|(|X<rsub|o>|)>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|o>|)>>*p<rsub|o><rsup|->.
    </equation*>

    For activities meeting at least two cell neighborhoods,
    <eqref|ch11:dispersal> absorbs the power of <math|H>. For the remaining
    activities its cost is at most <math|H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>>. The old
    auxiliary site sum is therefore at most
    <math|e<rsup|g>*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->>.

    Set <math|\<delta\>=H<rsup|-1/2>> and choose
    <math|e<rsup|g>=\<delta\>/<around|(|2*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->|)>\<geqslant\>1>.
    The new factors have auxiliary site sum at most
    <math|2<rsup|n>*C<rsub|\<ast\>>*R<rsub|e+1>*H<rsup|-7/8>>, so the total
    auxiliary sum <math|\<Gamma\>> is at most <math|\<delta\>> eventually.
    Assign each failed-cutoff factor to an old activity it meets. At most
    <math|2<rsup|n>*t<around|(|X<rsub|o>|)>> factors are assigned to
    <math|o>. In each nonzero connected component, their discarded
    <math|e<rsup|F>> factors are thus bounded by the
    <math|e<rsup|2<rsup|n>*F*t<around|(|X<rsub|o>|)>>> factors, leaving at
    least one factor <math|e<rsup|-g>>. The connected-set estimate of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch08:chapter> gives the site sum at exponent
    <math|e> at most

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|-g>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<Gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<delta\>*e<rsup|-g>=4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->.
    </equation*>

    Its counting condition <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>
    follows from <math|B=O<around|(|<around|(|log s|)><rsup|4>|)>> and the
    definition of <math|H>. No independence of large-field indicators or
    activities is used.
  </proof>

  These lemmas explain the required stronger support estimate. For the same
  leading expansion let its regular remainder satisfy

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-q>,<space|2em>z<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>*z<rsub|j>,<space|2em><around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><rsup|k-\<ell\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>>
  </equation*>

  at exponent <math|e+2> and weight <math|\<omega\><rsub|->>. This implies
  the premise of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:restore-regular>. The constants
  at exponent <math|e+2> must be fixed before <math|\<alpha\>> and the block
  ratio. Applying an estimate proved only at exponent <math|e> a second time
  does not establish this premise.

  If the corrected leading coefficients at the middle level are at most
  <math|H<rsup|-31/32>>, their rooted contribution at the restored weight is
  at most <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|-27/32>>. Together with the two
  restoration lemmas this gives regular bound

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|-1>+2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|-27/32>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3/4>
  </equation*>

  and large-field bound at most <math|H<rsup|-q>>. The reference connected
  polynomial is subtracted once, on the same sample and with the same subset
  moments. Its failed-cutoff part is precisely the part handled above.

  On the lower interval suppose the actual marked step has the quantitative
  bound

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:lower-weighted-step>z<rsub|j+1>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*z<rsub|j>+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsub|j><rsup|2>
  </equation>

  at the restored weight, and its large-field part stays in the allowed
  interval. If <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|\<ell\>-w>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/4>>,
  induction gives

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|j>*H<rsup|-3/4>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Thus every step remains in the domain <math|H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1>
  used in the induction. At the output the source variable incidence and
  large-field site bounds are at most one. On a torus with <math|q<rsub|0>>
  sites and <math|n*q<rsub|0>> marks, the scalar majorant of the marked
  exponential gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:weighted-output-envelope>\<omega\><rsup|q<rsub|0><around|\||I|\|>>*<around|\||<around|[|z<rsub|I><rsup|<math-up|all>>|]>*\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|marked>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|<text|unmarked exponent><around|(|U|)>+q<rsub|0>*<around|(|1+n|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Every cell carries the source monomial <math|I> in the coefficient on the
  left. The unmarked integral is still the original partition function by
  <eqref|ch11:source-density>. This supplies the numerator needed in the
  finite separating family comparison, once the stronger leading recurrence
  and <eqref|ch11:lower-weighted-step> have been proved for the combined
  marked estimate. The ordinary bound for terms involving at least two
  insertions by one has neither the source weight nor the small initial
  remainder needed here.

  <paragraph|The two weighted step estimates.> The inflations required here
  depend on the inverse coupling, so the fixed-inflation statement alone is
  insufficient. Split the positive prescribed set of large-field sites decay
  of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields> into reserved fractions and
  choose the reported bound <math|G<around|(|x|)>> so that multiplication by
  <math|G<around|(|x|)><rsup|-2>> leaves a fixed positive fraction. This
  choice is made before the thresholds. The amplitudes without prescribed
  large-field sites have a negative inverse coupling power times subpower
  factors; multiplying by <math|H<rsup|c>> for fixed <math|c> retains that
  power, since <math|log H=o<around|(|log x|)>>. These reserves establish the
  common product bound <eqref|ch11:integrated-component> for the following
  inflations, including their product over distinct prescribed sets of
  large-field sites.

  Use <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>=e+2>, put <math|T=T<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>> and
  choose <math|\<alpha\>\<geqslant\>8*T>. For
  <math|H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>> and principal
  incidence at most <math|H<rsup|-4>>, take

  <\equation*>
    l=max <around*|(|1,<frac|H<rsup|-3>|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>>|)>,<space|2em>m=G<around|(|x|)><rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|l\<leqslant\>max <around|(|1,H<rsup|q-3>|)>> and
  <math|l/x\<leqslant\>1> eventually. Thus the actual cutoff-failure
  probability absorbs the complementary principal cutoff. The insertion
  incidence satisfies

  <\equation*>
    a=l*T*z+2*T*H<rsup|-4>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>H<rsup|-3>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|l\<gtr\>1>, use <math|l*T*z\<leqslant\><around|(|T/\<alpha\>|)>*H<rsup|-3>>;
  if <math|l=1>, use <math|z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-4>>. Since
  <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B|)>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> and
  <math|G<around|(|y|)>> decreases faster than every inverse power of
  <math|H>, choosing <math|\<delta\>=H<rsup|-1/8>/16> gives
  <math|\<Sigma\>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/8>/4> and
  <math|T*G<around|(|y|)>+a\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>>. The connected bound and
  division by <math|l,m> give

  <\equation*>
    z<rsub|<math-up|nonprincipal>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>/l\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)>*z,<space|2em>z<rsub|<math-up|principal>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3>,<space|2em>z<rsub|<math-up|forced>>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  When <math|l=1>, its definition implies
  <math|H<rsup|-3>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|-q>>, so the first estimate
  still holds. Reassigning a principal source term to its prescribed enlarged
  support adds at most <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>*y> if its
  coefficient discrepancy is <math|y>. The joint Gaussian estimate at the
  requested power gives <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>*y\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-q>>.
  Its complementary cutoff part retains the original large-field prescribed
  set of large-field sites. With <math|e<rsup|-g<rsub|i>>=G<around|(|x|)>>
  the bound

  <\equation*>
    e<rsup|g<rsub|i>>*G<around|(|x|)><rsup|2>+n*H<rsup|-2>*R<rsub|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1>
  </equation*>

  gives outgoing large-field bound <math|G<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|H<rsup|-1>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|x|)>>.
  This proves the leading recurrence <math|z<rsub|<math-up|new>>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>*z>
  at the stronger exponent.

  Below the middle level there are no principal factors. Use exponent
  <math|e>, <math|l=1>, <math|m=G<around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>> and incoming
  large-field bound at most <math|z*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|<math-up|low>><around|(|y|)>>,
  where <math|0\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|low>><around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>1>.
  The outgoing incidence is at most

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|e>*z*<around|(|1+a<rsub|<math-up|low>><around|(|y|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*T<rsub|e>*z\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|{|\<alpha\>*z+H<rsup|1/2>*z<rsup|2>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The site conditions hold for <math|H<rsup|-q>\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/2>>
  by the same estimates, and the large-field output is at most
  <math|G<around|(|y|)>>. This proves <eqref|ch11:lower-weighted-step>. For
  the polynomial attenuation used here, the reserved exponential large-field
  decay gives <math|G<around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-q>*a<rsub|<math-up|low>><around|(|y|)>>.
  Since <math|z\<geqslant\>H<rsup|-q>>, the outgoing large-field bound is at
  most <math|z*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|<math-up|low>><around|(|y|)>>, as
  required for the next step.

  This calculation requires the reserved decay and corrected body product
  estimate on the actual restarted representation. It does not transfer a
  bound from a different Gaussian law or change an incoming bound after the
  history is fixed. The same-data restart conditions remain in force.

  The parameter <math|H=e<rsup|<around|(|log log s|)><rsup|2>>> measures the
  slowly growing comparison scale, not a covariance or a Hilbert space. The
  actual Gaussian fluctuation scale is <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>. The
  source density <math|q<rsub|k>> remains the full pushed polynomial of
  <eqref|ch11:source-density>, including its higher coefficients; it is
  unrelated to the fixed tolerance exponent <math|q>.

  <subsection|The actual covariance on a comparison
  window><label|ch11:window-covariance>

  We record the covariance estimates used in the comparison of separating
  correlations, including their finite-period errors. Fix enough smoothing
  orders for the finite number of differences below. Let
  <math|H<around|(|p|)>> denote the original two-form metric symbol of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>, with <math|H<around|(|0|)>=I>, a
  positive lower bound, and uniform exponential coefficient bounds. Put
  <math|q<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|p|)>=e<rsup|i*p<rsub|\<mu\>>>-1>, let
  <math|X> be exterior multiplication by <math|q> from one-forms, and let
  <math|U> be exterior multiplication from scalars. The actual curvature
  covariance is

  <\equation*>
    P<around|(|p|)>=X<around|(|p|)>*<around|{|X<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*H<around|(|p|)>*X<around|(|p|)>+U<around|(|p|)>*U<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>|}><rsup|-1>*X<around|(|p|)><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>P<around|(|0|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Indeed the completed inverse differs from the original potential inverse
  only in gradient directions, killed by <math|X>. The exterior identity
  gives coercivity at least <math|c<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>>. Taylor
  expansion at zero, with differentiated bounds, yields

  <\equation*>
    D<rsup|j>*P<around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|-j>|)>,<space|2em>D<rsup|j>*<around|(|P<around|(|p|)>-P<rsub|0><around|(|p|)>|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|1-j>|)>,<space|1em>P<rsub|0>=X<rsub|0><around|\||p|\|><rsup|-2>*X<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|1em>X<rsub|0>=i*p\<wedge\>.
  </equation*>

  All constants are uniform in the kernel history within the already fixed
  quadratic bounds.

  Use a smooth compact frequency partition whose <math|2*\<pi\><ZZ><rsup|4>>
  translates sum to one, equal to one near zero. This leaves the finite
  Fourier sum unchanged, including its zero-frequency value. For difference
  orders <math|j,h>, subtract the Gaussian-regularized continuum multiplier.
  The remainder has order <math|j+h+1> at zero and rapidly decreasing
  derivatives away from zero. Splitting its Fourier integral at radius
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-1>> and integrating by parts <math|j+h+6>
  times outside that ball gives <math|C<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-j-h-5>>. The
  inner integral has the same bound. The finite Fourier sum differs from its
  integral by at most <math|C*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)>/N<rsub|<math-up|per>>>:
  on frequency cubes of side <math|N<rsub|<math-up|per>><rsup|-1>> use the
  derivative bound <math|C/<around|\||p|\|>>, which is integrable in four
  dimensions, and the phase difference. This also handles the cube containing
  the singular sampled point. Gaussian regularization of the continuum
  potential has kernel <math|<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1-e<rsup|-<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>/4>|)>>
  in radian convention. Differentiating it therefore changes the separated
  Maxwell covariance only by an exponentially decreasing term. Consequently,
  for <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:finite-covariance><around|\||\<Delta\><rsup|j>*\<Delta\><rsup|h>*\<kappa\><rsub|k><around|(|x|)>-\<partial\><rsup|j>*\<partial\><rsup|h>*C<rsub|<math-up|Max>><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C\|x\|<rsup|-j-h-5>+C*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)>/N<rsub|<math-up|per>>.
  </equation>

  The lattice-side lower bound for the smallest physical period makes the
  second term negligible on every fixed-power-<math|H> window, uniformly in
  all periods.

  For the actual installed curvature response <math|\<Psi\><rsub|i>>, one
  also needs to move differences through the response, not merely bound its
  differentiated row. The box-filter identities in the alias formula give

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<Psi\><rsub|i>=Q<rsub|i,w>*\<Delta\><rsub|w>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|y><around|\||Q<rsub|i,w>*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>R*L<rsub|i><rsup|-<around|\||w|\|>-2>.
  </equation*>

  On the principal alias divide by the coarse difference using
  <math|<around|(|e<rsup|i*p/L>-1|)>/<around|(|e<rsup|i*p>-1|)>=<around|[|L<space|0.17em><math-up|avg><rsub|L><around|(|p/L|)>|]><rsup|-1>>.
  On other aliases consume one box factor for each difference; their
  geometric-series identity has the same coarse factor. Sufficient smoothing
  order leaves the remaining powers nonnegative. The quotient is regular also
  on coordinate hyperplanes. Its exponential coefficient bound follows at a
  narrower width from <math|<around|(|e<rsup|i*n*p>-1|)>/<around|(|e<rsup|i*p>-1|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a=0><rsup|n-1>e<rsup|i*a*p>>
  for <math|n\<gtr\>0>, with the analogous negative sum for
  <math|n\<less\>0>. The extra factor <math|<around|\||n|\|>> is absorbed by
  that strict width reserve. This proves the row bound with <math|R> fixed
  before <math|L>. The response preserves closed cochains, including harmonic
  ones, by the exact exterior-transport identities of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>.

  Let <math|\<cal-L\>=\<Psi\><rsub|k-1>*\<cdots\>*\<Psi\><rsub|\<ell\>>>,
  <math|m=k-\<ell\>> and <math|S=a<rsub|\<ell\>>/a<rsub|k>>. A sign-valued
  test function on a specified row, without requiring that function to be
  closed, gives

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|j+2>*<big|sum><rsub|y><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|w>*\<cal-L\>*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|d>*R<rsup|m>*<space|1em><around|(|j=<around|\||w|\|>\<leqslant\>d-1|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|m=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|b+2|)>*log H/log L|\<rceil\>>>
  with <math|b\<geqslant\>6>, and choose <math|L> after <math|R> so that
  <math|4*<around|(|b+2|)>*log R\<leqslant\><around|(|\<lambda\>/4|)>*log L>.
  Then <math|S\<geqslant\>H<rsup|b+2>> and the right side is at most
  <math|P=H<rsup|\<lambda\>/4>> after a stage delay. The actual middle-scale
  field supplies such a closed background, rather than an independently
  selected one. In axial gauge on its radius-<math|B> patch take
  <math|g<rsup|-1>> times the Lie-algebra curvature coordinate of each link,
  extend this one-cochain by zero, and take its discrete exterior derivative
  <math|u>. Then <math|d*u=0> exactly, including across the artificial patch
  boundary, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>16*B*\<vartheta\>/g=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>\<leqslant\>P>.
  The truncation at the patch boundary is included in the comparison error.
  Retain only response paths lying in the protected patch and put the omitted
  paths in the regular remainder. The exponential response bound makes their
  absolute contribution at most <math|C*B<rsup|a>*e<rsup|-c*B>*g<rsup|-1>>,
  with the same bound for every fixed requested difference. The clipped field
  outside the patch is bounded before multiplication by <math|g<rsup|-1>>.
  Since <math|B> grows as <math|<around|(|log s|)><rsup|4>>, this error is
  smaller than every fixed inverse power of <math|H>, even after
  <math|g<rsup|-1>=O<around|(|<sqrt|s>|)>>. Thus zero extension is not
  asserted to preserve the full conditional mean exactly. On the interior
  stencil its difference from the actual normalized plaquette curvature is
  <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|2>*\<vartheta\><rsup|2>/g|)>>, smaller than every
  fixed inverse power of <math|H>. This follows by subtracting the linear sum
  from the four-link product in that same gauge. For this closed middle-scale
  background <math|u>, the mean <math|\<mu\>=\<cal-L\>*u> and the transported
  coarse covariance <math|B<rsub|0>=\<cal-L\>*\<kappa\><rsub|\<ell\>>*\<cal-L\><rsup|T>>
  satisfy

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|j+2>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsup|j>*\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsup|2>,<space|2em>S<rsup|j+h+4>*<around|\||\<Delta\><rsup|j>*\<Delta\><rsup|h>*B<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsup|3>.
  </equation*>

  The exact conditional covariance identity
  <eqref|ch11:conditional-curvature> telescopes through the window. After the
  same-insertion normal ordering used for <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P>>, its
  covariance is

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|z,w|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|i\<ne\>j>*\<kappa\><rsub|k>*<around|(|z<rsub|i>+z,z<rsub|j>+w|)>-B<rsub|0>*<around|(|z<rsub|i>+z,z<rsub|j>+w|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular its diagonal is <math|-B<rsub|0>>, not zero. It need not be
  positive; only the polynomial Wick evaluation uses this covariance.

  Take the measurement magnification <math|\<rho\>=H<rsup|b>>. The preceding
  bounds, <eqref|ch11:finite-covariance>, and
  <math|\<rho\>/S\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-2>> imply weighted mean and covariance
  errors at the retained jet coordinates at most <math|H<rsup|-3>>. The
  highest difference and remainder coordinates have bound <math|P>. Here the
  continuum approximation error contributes
  <math|O<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|-1>|)>=O<around|(|H<rsup|-b>|)>>, the
  transported mean contributes <math|H<rsup|-4>*P<rsup|2>>, and the
  transported covariance contributes <math|H<rsup|-4>*P<rsup|3>>. The
  finite-period term is smaller than these after the common mesh threshold.

  To turn these into one finite coordinate system, choose dual
  finite-difference functionals <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> on the
  polynomial closed curvature cochains <math|t<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, with
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|t<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>>.
  They exist by descending from the highest nonzero homogeneous curvature,
  exactly as in the radial-jet construction. Retain coordinates
  <math|\<rho\><rsup|g<rsub|\<alpha\>>>*\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>*f> and, at
  every stencil point <math|z>, the residual coordinate
  <math|\<rho\><rsup|d+1>*<around|(|f-<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|f|)>*t<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)><around|(|z|)>>.
  This gives an exact reconstruction of every stencil value. For a closed
  cochain, repeated finite-difference summation along coordinate paths bounds
  its residual by the top difference times a fixed power of the stencil
  radius. These costs are <math|H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> and are absorbed by
  <math|H<rsup|\<lambda\>/4>>. The common enlarged mean and covariance are
  therefore bounded by <math|H<rsup|\<lambda\>>>, and their physical jet
  block differs from the free one by at most <math|H<rsup|-2>>. The free
  comparison has zero residual coordinates. All subsets use this one enlarged
  system.

  <subsection|From the common polynomial localization to the corrected pair
  bound><label|ch11:corrected-pair-comparison>

  Solve the simultaneous lift equations using the actual <math|T> constructed
  in Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:common-extraction>. For the finitely many
  free polynomials, their coefficient sizes are fixed. The solved lift has a
  bound <math|H<rsup|b*d+c<rsub|d>>> in normalized local insertion
  coordinates. Choose <math|b> larger than <math|c<rsub|d>+3>, then choose
  <math|D> large enough for <eqref|ch11:raw-polynomial-error>; all these
  choices precede the derivative orders and final scale thresholds. After the
  common finite-coordinate substitution, the retained weighted polynomial is
  exactly the intended free polynomial. Every remaining monomial has weight
  at least <math|d+1>, so its coefficient sum is at most <math|H<rsup|-2>> by
  the factor <math|\<rho\><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>> and this choice of
  <math|b>.

  For completeness, if a polynomial has coefficient norm <math|A> and degree
  at most <math|D<rsub|0>>, Wick recursion bounds its expectation by
  <math|A*<around|[|2*B*<around|(|D<rsub|0>+1|)>|]><rsup|D<rsub|0>>> when all
  means and covariance entries are bounded by <math|B\<geqslant\>1>. Changing
  these entries by at most <math|\<epsilon\>> changes it by at most the same
  expression times <math|\<epsilon\>>. This is proved by selecting the first
  variable of a monomial and summing its mean term and at most
  <math|D<rsub|0>-1> pair terms; subtract products one factor at a time. It
  is independent of the number of coordinates.

  Set <math|\<lambda\>=1/<around|[|4*<around|(|2*D+D+1|)>|]>> and
  <math|B=H<rsup|\<lambda\>>>. The common coordinate estimate and the
  coefficient tail just proved give, for every subset of the two insertions,
  moment error at most <math|C*H<rsup|-2>*H<rsup|2*D*\<lambda\>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-3/2>>.
  The empty moment is exactly one. The actual nonlinear-to-polynomial error
  has the same bound: use <eqref|ch11:raw-polynomial-error>, the common with
  a smooth cutoff Gaussian estimate, and telescope the finitely many
  polynomial transport steps. If a step has coefficient norm at most
  <math|A<rsub|2*D>> and discrepancy at most <math|H<rsup|-v>>, the product
  error over <math|m> steps is at most <math|m*H<rsup|-v>*A<rsub|2*D><rsup|m>>.
  Choose the block length after <math|A<rsub|2*D>> and then <math|v> so this
  is at most <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>. The local-coordinate error is a negative
  inverse coupling power times subpower factors and hence is below every
  fixed <math|H<rsup|-v>> on the comparison band. The comparison uses the
  same mean, covariance and common cutoff factors at every subset;
  independently chosen Gaussian samples would not justify the product
  estimate.

  For two source variables the connected coefficients are simply the two
  means and the covariance of the polynomial values. Subtraction of these
  expressions gives a fixed polynomial bound in the subset moments times
  their errors. Their free values remain bounded on the selected separated
  configurations. Thus the corrected leading coefficients at the middle level
  are at most <math|H<rsup|-31/32>>, with room to spare. Here is the explicit
  estimate for the terms involving at least two insertions already present at
  the opening level. Write <math|C=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, <math|B=C<rsup|2>>, let
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> be their original support exponent, and
  let <math|r<rsub|0>=s<rsup|-a/2>> be their original source variable radius.
  Their normalized rooted bound is at most one by
  <eqref|ch11:higher-induction>. The new radius is
  <math|r=H<rsup|f>\<leqslant\>1>, where <math|f=1/16-U\<leqslant\>0>. For a
  support containing <math|m\<geqslant\>2> distinct marked sites, the chosen
  configurations have pairwise separation at least <math|C<rsup|2>>. The
  separated-site bound and cover enlargement give

  <\equation*>
    C*m\<leqslant\>64*c<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>,<space|2em><around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsup|+8>|)>\<leqslant\>100000*<around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<theta\><rsub|0>*c<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>.
  </equation*>

  For any prescribed <math|q>, set <math|\<Gamma\>=<around|(|q+10|)>*log H>
  and <math|u<rsub|0>=<around|(|a/2|)>*log s>. The stage thresholds can
  impose

  <\equation*>
    64*<around|(|u<rsub|0>+\<Gamma\>+100000*<around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>|)>\<leqslant\>C*\<theta\><rsub|0>/2,<space|2em>100000*C*<around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>\<leqslant\>B*\<theta\><rsub|0>/2.
  </equation*>

  This is possible because <math|C> grows like <math|<around|(|log
  s|)><rsup|2>>, whereas <math|u<rsub|0>=O<around|(|log s|)>> and <math|log
  H=o<around|(|log s|)>>. Consequently, term by term on the original marked
  alphabet,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch11:opening-higher-weight>r<rsup|m>*e<rsup|<around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>*c<rsub|B><around|(|X<rsup|+8>|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<Gamma\>>*r<rsub|0><rsup|m>*e<rsup|\<theta\><rsub|0>*c<rsub|C><around|(|X|)>>.
  </equation>

  This changes weights on the complete family of terms involving at least two
  insertions; it does not compare <math|r> directly with <math|r<rsub|0>>.
  Root-to-site conversion costs only <math|\<cal-W\><around|(|B|)>=H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>.
  At the opening all distinct sites, including those in one measurement cell,
  are separated: their distance is a fixed positive multiple of
  <math|H<rsup|b>>, and <math|C<rsup|2>=o<around|(|H<rsup|b>|)>>.

  Choose <math|U> so the normalized single-insertion bound at radius
  <math|H<rsup|f>> is at most <math|H<rsup|-8>>. Its near Taylor error is at
  most <math|H<rsup|f+b*d+c<rsub|d>-D>>, and its distant-support part is at
  most <math|H<rsup|f+b*d+c<rsub|d>>*e<rsup|-\<theta\><rsub|0>*C>>. Increase
  <math|D> to make both at most <math|H<rsup|-q-2>>. Together with
  <eqref|ch11:opening-higher-weight> these give the initial regular remainder
  <math|H<rsup|-q>> at exponent <math|e<rsub|\<ast\>>>. For the large-field
  part perform the exact endpoint re-expression jointly with these marked
  factors, before summing components. Every nonzero failed cutoff and every
  old large-field activity meets a new large-field site. Its selected site
  factor has size at most <math|e<rsup|-2*u>>, with
  <math|u=a<rsub|\<ast\>><space|0.17em><math-up|hot><around|(|s|)><rsup|2>>.
  Reserve <math|e<rsup|-u>=G<around|(|s|)>> once per component. The remaining
  rooted sum is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    H<rsup|-q-8>+\<cal-W\><around|(|B|)>*<around|{|O<around|(|H<rsup|-8>|)>+O<around|(|H<rsup|-q-8>|)>|}>+H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>*<sqrt|e<rsup|-H<rsup|1/5>>>+<around|(|8*B+1|)><rsup|4>*e<rsup|-u+O<around|(|e<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>,
  </equation*>

  which is at most <math|H<rsup|-1>> and satisfies the connected-set
  smallness condition after the stage delays. Thus its regrouped site sum is
  at most <math|G<around|(|s|)>*<around|(|e<rsup|H<rsup|-1>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>G<around|(|s|)>>.
  All original and new prescribed sets of large-field sites and compatibility
  conditions are retained, and component decomposition is reversible in every
  squarefree coefficient. This proves the complete opening bound; no higher
  coefficient of the actual pushed density is discarded.

  The leading interval has <math|m=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
  H/log L|\<rceil\>>> steps. Before choosing <math|L\<geqslant\>3>, fix
  <math|\<kappa\>=1+2*<around|(|b+2|)>*log <around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)>/log 3>.
  Then <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+2|)><rsup|m>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<kappa\>>>
  eventually for every allowed <math|L>. Choose
  <math|q\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>+U*n+2> before the Taylor order <math|D> and
  the later block floor involving its polynomial transport norm. This
  prevents a circular choice between <math|q,D> and <math|L>. The lower
  interval only increases the cell side by a factor <math|C<rsup|10>>, so it
  uses at most <math|<around|\<lceil\>|10*log C/log L|\<rceil\>>> steps.
  Since <math|log C=O<around|(|log log s|)>=o<around|(|log H|)>>, its
  amplification <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+1|)><rsup|\<ell\>-w>> is at most
  <math|H<rsup|1/4>> eventually. The selected plateau must include these two
  finite windows; their total length is <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log log
  s|)><rsup|2>|)>> at a fixed stage and lies inside the short interval
  admitted by the restart construction.

  Sections<nbsp><reference|ch11:weight-restoration> and
  <reference|ch11:common-extraction>, together with the actual restarted
  representation, now give the weighted output envelope
  <eqref|ch11:weighted-output-envelope>. To normalize it one must retain the
  Gaussian power in the partition function. Tree gauge fixing leaves
  <math|3*V+1> free links on a four-dimensional torus of <math|V> sites.
  Restrict them to a ball for the normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance of
  radius <math|g/4> to obtain

  <\equation*>
    Z\<geqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\>-V*c<rsub|1>>*<around|(|c<rsub|->*g<rsup|dim
    <gLie>>|)><rsup|3*V+1>.
  </equation*>

  For the numerator, successive axial plaquette elimination integrates
  <math|3*V-3> distinct links, leaving the four wrapping links. An explicit
  elimination order verifies that count. Represent sites by
  <math|0,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|<math-up|per>>-1> and let <math|p<around|(|x|)>>
  be the largest nonzero coordinate. The tree uses
  <math|<around|(|x-e<rsub|p<around|(|x|)>>,p<around|(|x|)>|)>> for
  <math|x\<ne\>0>. Retain the four wrapping links
  <math|<around|(|-e<rsub|i>,i|)>>. For any other free link
  <math|<around|(|x,i|)>> order it by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|t,\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|i=t|}>>,<with|math-display|false|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>t=max
    <around|(|i,p<around|(|x|)>|)>,>|\<nobracket\>>
  </equation*>

  lexicographically. If <math|i\<less\>t>, choose its plaquette in directions
  <math|<around|(|i,t|)>> based at <math|x-e<rsub|t>>. If <math|i=t>,
  freeness implies <math|x<rsub|i>=N<rsub|<math-up|per>>-1>; put
  <math|w=x+e<rsub|i>>, take <math|j=p<around|(|w|)>\<less\>i>, and choose
  the <math|<around|(|j,i|)>> plaquette based at <math|x-e<rsub|j>>. Every
  other free link in the chosen plaquette has smaller priority. The chosen
  plaquettes are distinct, since equality would require each of two links to
  have smaller priority than the other. Integrate in decreasing order. The
  selected link occurs once, as <math|a*U*b> or <math|a*U<rsup|-1>*b>, so
  Haar invariance evaluates its factor. All other plaquette factors are at
  most one and may be discarded. This proves the stated <math|3*V-3>
  integrations, including the wrapping links except for the four explicitly
  retained ones. The Haar estimate <math|<big|int><rsub|G>e<rsup|-\<chi\><around|(|u|)>/<around|(|g/c|)>>*d*u\<leqslant\>c<rsub|+>*<around|(|g/c|)><rsup|dim
  <gLie>>> then gives the corresponding upper bound. It follows that the
  normalized coefficient with weight <math|z> obeys

  <\equation*>
    z*<around|\||\<bbb-E\>*F|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
    <around|{|V*C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>-4*dim <around|(|<gLie>|)>*log g|}>.
  </equation*>

  The scalar <math|\<sigma\>> cancels. A genuine additive value error
  <math|\<delta\>> contributes <math|2*V*\<delta\>>, one copy in numerator
  and one in denominator, and is included in <math|C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>>.
  These estimates use the same nonnegative output density.

  At the output <math|V=q<rsub|0>>. Its smallest-period site count grows
  exponentially in the output logarithmic scale, so <math|2*dim
  <around|(|<gLie>|)>*log \<beta\>/q<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>1> eventually. Choose
  the later stage delay with <math|C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>+1\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>=log
  H/32>. Applying the preceding estimate to
  <eqref|ch11:weighted-output-envelope> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<text|corrected coefficient
    >I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|I<rsub|k>>*\<omega\><rsup|-<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>,<space|2em>\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,2|}>.
  </equation*>

  This is the desired corrected pair estimate on the original partition
  denominator. At each fixed deep comparison level its simultaneously
  normalized columns are uniformly bounded in <math|N,p>. Undoing the finite
  connected subtractions and then taking a sufficiently deep comparison level
  establishes Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:finite-detector-input>, using
  the actual small value and joint product bounds of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:restart-family>. The estimates are before
  extraction and their thresholds are independent of the period.

  <subsection|Finite-arity comparison on a common finite set of
  scales><label|ch11:finite-arity-comparison>

  The pair argument extends to any fixed arity, with the same partition
  normalization and one polynomial localization map for each degree. We state
  the result in the form needed for smooth free-field comparison. All
  constants may depend on the fixed finite arity, degree list and compact
  configuration set.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Common finite-arity Gaussian comparison><label|ch11:n-mark-comparison>Fix
    <math|n\<geqslant\>1>, finitely many real polynomial labels of bounded
    degree, and a compact set of point configurations with positive pairwise
    distance. Fix finitely many magnification exponents
    <math|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|t>> large enough for the preceding local
    estimates. The selected stage requirements can be imposed for this finite
    request so that one actual localization onto the polynomial label space
    <math|T<rsub|k,N,p,d>> per degree and one restarted history serve all
    these scales. At scale <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>=H<rsup|b<rsub|j>>> use the
    exact simultaneous lifts

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|g/\<theta\>>*T<rsub|k,N,p,d>*u=W<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|j>>*v.
    </equation*>

    They are real and equivariant, and bounded in <math|N,p> for each fixed
    comparison depth. Set <math|G<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1> and let
    <math|G<rsub|I>> be the free moment of the chosen labels at the rescaled
    configurations. Define the corrected coefficients by

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1,<space|2em>R<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>F<rsub|i>-<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>J\<subseteq\>I>G<rsub|J>*R<rsub|I\<setminus\>J>.
    </equation*>

    Then, for every nonempty <math|I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>,
    the chessboard bound on the corresponding comparison cell is

    <\equation>
      <label|ch11:n-mark-root><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|root>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>
    </equation>

    at all sufficiently large cutoffs, uniformly in period and in the stated
    compact configuration window. The cell side can be chosen at most
    <math|a<rsub|k>*H<rsup|b<rsub|j>+5>>. The estimate requires positive
    point separation, but no positive lower bound on individual coordinate
    differences.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Make all finite requests before selecting the stage orders, ratios and
    delays. Construct <math|T> at the top level <math|k> from the actual
    insertion and its original same-insertion covariance. Neither its
    near-support Taylor construction nor that covariance depends on the
    choice of middle or output level. Choose the Taylor order and derivative
    reserves for the largest of the finitely many requests. Restart once on
    the longest required window. Restriction of that history supplies the
    shorter windows, with unchanged top map and endpoint. There is no new
    normalization for individual spectator subsets.

    Work throughout in the full <math|n>-source squarefree algebra. The
    polynomial <math|<big|sum><rsub|I>G<rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>> has constant
    coefficient one, so its inverse exists there. Multiplying the actual
    source by this inverse gives exactly the displayed recursion.
    Equivalently subtract its finite connected logarithm. This operation
    commutes with every block integration and leaves the empty density, hence
    its partition function, unchanged.

    Use

    <\equation*>
      \<omega\><rsub|->=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*n|)>-U>,<space|2em>\<omega\>=H<rsup|1/<around|(|8*n|)>>,<space|2em>I<rsub|k>=<frac|log
      H|16*n>.
    </equation*>

    All support and regrouping arguments above already use <math|n> only
    through <math|n>, <math|2<rsup|n>>, and the fixed polynomial degree.
    Choose <math|U\<geqslant\>1/<around|(|8*n|)>> to control the finite list
    of single insertions, then <math|\<kappa\>,q,D>, and finally the ratios
    and delays in the order already specified. The initial estimate for terms
    involving at least two insertions <eqref|ch11:opening-higher-weight>
    applies to all cardinalities; the complete incoming polynomial is
    retained.

    For the common Gaussian calculation use degree bound <math|n*D> and, for
    example, <math|\<lambda\>\<leqslant\>1/<around|[|32*n<rsup|2>*<around|(|D+1|)>|]>>.
    The coefficient tails and physical covariance errors are
    <math|H<rsup|-2>>, so the polynomial Wick estimate makes all subset
    moment errors at most <math|H<rsup|-3/2>>. The finite logarithm is a
    polynomial of degree at most <math|n> in these subset moments. Its
    derivative is bounded on a fixed neighborhood of their free values, since
    every free subset moment is bounded on the compact separated
    configuration set. Hence the connected coefficient errors are also
    <math|O<around|(|H<rsup|-3/2>|)>> and in particular at most
    <math|H<rsup|-31/32>> eventually. This supplies the same corrected
    leading bound as before.

    Each source monomial gains at most <math|\<omega\><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|1/8>>
    at the restored weight. Regular weight restoration therefore gives
    <math|H<rsup|-1>>; the corrected leading contribution is at most
    <math|2<rsup|n>*R<rsub|e>*H<rsup|-27/32>>; and the regrouping of
    activities constrained to cover prescribed sites has bound
    <math|4*H<rsup|U*n+2<rsup|n>>*\<eta\><rsub|->>. The upper and lower
    recurrences then give incidence and site bounds at most one at the
    output. Their scalar envelope has expense at most <math|1+n> per site.
    The actual partition estimate absorbs this finite expense by imposing
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|stage>>+1\<leqslant\>I<rsub|k>>. Thus the corrected
    source coefficient has chessboard seminorm at most
    <math|e<rsup|I<rsub|k>>*\<omega\><rsup|-<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>>,
    proving <eqref|ch11:n-mark-root>.

    For the geometric bound, the leading scale ratio is at most
    <math|L*H<rsup|b<rsub|j>+2>> and the lower ratio at most
    <math|L*C<rsup|10>>. The stage delays give
    <math|L<rsup|2>*C<rsup|10>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|3>>, so the total physical
    cell side is at most <math|a<rsub|k>*H<rsup|b<rsub|j>+5>>. All covariance
    estimates depend only on the full displacement between distinct points.
    On the compact window its minimum length is positive, also after rounding
    at sufficiently large <math|H>. The opening-support estimate uses the
    same positive maximum-coordinate distance. Neither argument divides by a
    single coordinate difference. This proves the asserted uniformity,
    including configurations arbitrarily close to coordinate coincidences.
  </proof>

  Different scales in this proposition use their own normalized lifts, but
  the coefficient map is identical. Consequently, if
  <math|b<rsub|0>\<gtr\>b<rsub|j>>, the lift normalized at <math|b<rsub|0>>
  is exactly the linear combination of the degree-<math|d> lifts at
  <math|b<rsub|j>> obtained by applying <math|W<rsub|H<rsup|b<rsub|0>-b<rsub|j>>>>
  to its free label. On a fixed finite-dimensional degree space that costs at
  most <math|C*H<rsup|<around|(|b<rsub|0>-b<rsub|j>|)>*d>> in coefficient
  norm. The chessboard estimate for a single insertion and its free
  expectation therefore give the same factor for its chessboard seminorm.
  This is the comparison needed for the size and displacement bounds at three
  scales; it does not assert that one fixed unnormalized label has a small
  corrected error at all three.

  We can now state the finite-cutoff comparison supplied by these estimates.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Comparison of the separating correlations at finite
    cutoff><label|ch11:finite-detector-input>On the selected trajectory with
    the stage orders, block floors and choices of sufficiently large starting
    scales specified above, for each <math|e> there is an injective free
    measurement matrix <math|G:<CC><rsup|r>\<to\><CC><rsup|s>> and the
    following finite-cutoff approximations. For arbitrarily small
    <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> one can choose a fixed comparison depth <math|k>,
    bounded real local polynomial coefficient families
    <math|u<rsub|1,i>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|r,i>> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j,i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|k>>,
    and finitely many bounded families of normalized local spectator
    insertions, all on the selected history, such that their
    point-correlation matrix <math|Q<rsub|i><rsup|0>> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|i><rsup|0>-G|\<\|\|\>><rsub|max>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>
    </equation*>

    eventually for every period. The correlation configurations and small
    neighborhoods of them have a positive common coordinate separation and
    lie inside the smallest physical period. All these bounded normalized
    local insertion families have uniform chessboard bounds at every
    sufficiently fine fixed cell depth.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose the free basis and separating configurations from
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch03:maxwell>. Construct their actual common
    polynomial localization and simultaneous lifts by
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:common-extraction>, and use the restarted
    family of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:restart-family>. The opening
    estimate, stronger support bounds, restored source weight and output
    normalization in Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:corrected-pair-comparison>
    give corrected subset chessboard-seminorm error
    <math|e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>>, uniformly in cutoff and period. Undoing the
    connected subtractions gives point-matrix error at most
    <math|C<rsub|k>*e<rsup|-I<rsub|k>>>, where <math|C<rsub|k>> is a
    polynomial in the finitely many free subset moments. These moments remain
    bounded as the rounded, rescaled configurations approach their fixed
    separated targets. Thus choose one sufficiently deep <math|k> to obtain
    the requested <math|\<eta\>>. The exact lift equations give a fixed bound
    <math|B<rsub|k>> for all cutoffs and periods at that depth. The
    product-moment estimate supplies the chessboard bounds for normalized
    local insertions. No convergence or choice of a limiting equation
    subspace is used.
  </proof>

  This is a comparison before any cutoff limit. Its common coefficient
  extraction, auxiliary density and normalized lifts are the ones constructed
  above.

  Recall the exact moving equation kernel
  <math|K<rsub|i>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,e>P<rsub|N,p,e>*S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|e>*\<cal-I\><rsub|e>>.
  Its bounded projection <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>> is distinct from the
  near-identity section comparison <math|P<rsub|N,p,e>>. The latter is
  invertible; the former has kernel <math|K<rsub|i>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|A separating correlation family on the entire moving
    complement><label|ch11:uniform-detector>Using
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:finite-detector-input>, for every fixed
    finite difference word <math|l> there are averaged separating correlation
    functionals <math|L<rsub|i>> and a constant <math|Z>, independent of
    <math|N,p>, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>Z*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|1em><around|(|v\<in\>V<rsub|e>|)>
    </equation*>

    eventually for every period. Their tested correlation of a finite
    difference <math|D<rsub|l>*F> of a normalized local insertion with
    bounded chessboard seminorm is uniformly bounded. Their tested
    correlation of any trial whose chessboard seminorm tends to zero tends to
    zero uniformly in period.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose <math|K> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>K*<around|\<\|\|\>|G*c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    and put <math|\<delta\>=<around|(|2*K*r|)><rsup|-1>>. The case <math|r=0>
    is immediate. Use the assumed comparison with entrywise error at most
    <math|\<delta\>/4>. The positional modulus for normalized local
    insertions from Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:positional> holds on the
    separated neighborhoods with one constant and threshold for this finite
    list.

    Choose <math|b\<gtr\>0> so small that moving the distinguished point by
    at most <math|2<around|\||l|\|>*b> stays in those neighborhoods and
    changes every normalized local insertion measurement by at most
    <math|\<delta\>/4>. Put <math|h<rsub|N>=<around|\<lceil\>|b/a<rsub|N>|\<rceil\>>>.
    Average over the shifts <math|0,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|N>-1> once in each
    coordinate listed in <math|l>, always in the distinguished slot. These
    normalized averages define <math|L<rsub|i>>. For the empty word take no
    averages.

    The rows <math|<around|(|I-\<Pi\><rsub|i>|)>*u<rsub|j,i>> are bounded,
    hence admissible. Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:uniform-sweep> and the
    chessboard inequality with one distinguished insertion show that their
    averaged measurements tend to zero. Thus the matrix

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|i>=<around|(|L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|j,i>|)><rsub|j=1><rsup|r>
    </equation*>

    is within <math|\<delta\>> of <math|G> eventually. For any
    <math|c\<in\><CC><rsup|r>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>K\<\|\|\>Q<rsub|i>*c\<\|\|\><rsub|\<infty\>>+K*r*\<delta\><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2*K*<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|i>*c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
    </equation*>

    The <math|r> vectors <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|j,i>> are therefore
    independent. They span <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|i>>,
    since its dimension is exactly <math|r>. Writing
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v=<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|j,i>>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>A*r*B<rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2*A*r*B<rsub|k>*K*<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*\<Pi\><rsub|i>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|Z\<geqslant\>max <around|(|1,2*A*r*B<rsub|k>*K|)>>.

    For the tested correlation assertions, inserting a distinguished trial of
    chessboard seminorm <math|u<rsub|i>> with the bounded spectator fields
    gives a bound <math|C*u<rsub|i>> uniformly throughout the neighborhoods.
    Averaging preserves it. For a difference word, differences commute with
    these averages, and each coordinate telescopes exactly:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|h<rsub|N>>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|h<rsub|N>-1><frac|H*<around|(|x+<around|(|j+1|)>*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-H*<around|(|x+j*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>|a<rsub|N>>=<frac|H*<around|(|x+h<rsub|N>*e<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>-H<around|(|x|)>|h<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>>.
    </equation*>

    Iteration bounds the averaged difference by
    <math|<around|(|2/b|)><rsup|<around|\||l|\|>>> times the undifferentiated
    correlation bound. All endpoints stay in the chosen neighborhoods. This
    proves the tested correlation bound without assuming any pointwise bound
    on the differentiated field.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Uniform bounds for finite differences><label|ch11:all-period-differences>For
    the selected trajectory with the trial estimates of
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent> and the stage requirements
    already specified for their uniformity, every fixed finite list of
    fields, every finite difference word applied separately to its slots, and
    every <math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, their finite-volume correlation is
    bounded by one constant for all sufficiently large <math|N>, every period
    <math|p>, and every tuple with pairwise periodic maximum-coordinate
    distance at least <math|r<rsub|0>> in physical units.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Start with a bounded local polynomial coefficient and a difference word
    <math|l>. The exact physical difference identity and the translated local
    section bound make this a protected trial at degree
    <math|e=d+<around|\||l|\|>>. The factor
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|<around|\||l|\|>>> in its normalization index
    cancels all inverse mesh powers. Its stencil uses at most
    <math|2<rsup|<around|\||l|\|>>> fixed translates; its derivative cost
    grows by at most <math|<around|(|1+2<rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|N>*q<rsub|N>>|)><rsup|<around|\||l|\|>>>,
    a subpower quantity under the stage requirement on the starting scale.
    Trial descent gives an admissible coefficient <math|x<rsub|i>> and an
    error <math|E<rsub|i>=O<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>-D<rsub|l>*F> with
    chessboard seminorm tending to zero.

    The correlation pairings just proved give
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:absorption> with
    <math|y<rsub|i>=\<Pi\><rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>> and
    <math|z<rsub|i>=<around|(|x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>>.
    The latter family is admissible: its first term is a bounded scalar
    multiple of <math|x<rsub|i>>, and its second is norm bounded. It lies in
    the exact kernel of <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i>>, so its tested correlation
    tends to zero by Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:uniform-sweep>. Consequently
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*Z*C+1>.
    The chessboard bound for normalized local insertions applies to
    <math|O<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>>, and the unnormalized admissible kernel
    family <math|x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>> has vanishing chessboard seminorm. The
    identity

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|l>*F=O<around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>+O<around|(|x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>|)>-E<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    gives a uniform chessboard bound for the difference trial. Repeat for the
    finite list of orientations and slots.

    For clarity, two cell scales are needed to plant a separated tuple.
    Choose a fixed coarse cell side <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>r<rsub|0>>.
    Choose a finer fixed depth <math|k\<geqslant\>\<ell\>>, beyond all the
    finitely many scale thresholds for the chessboard bounds, with
    <math|6*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|\<ell\>>>. At mesh <math|N> the
    corresponding integer spans <math|t,T> satisfy
    <math|a<rsub|N>*t=a<rsub|k>> and <math|a<rsub|N>*T=a<rsub|\<ell\>>>. In
    each coordinate one point forbids at most <math|6*t> translations of the
    coarse tiling if a margin <math|3*t> is required. The <math|n> points
    therefore leave a permissible translation, since <math|6*n*t\<less\>T>.
    Distinct points occupy distinct coarse cells: sharing a cell would make
    their periodic maximum-coordinate distance at most
    <math|a<rsub|\<ell\>>\<less\>r<rsub|0>>. The chessboard-seminorm
    comparison between aligned fine and coarse tilings transfers the
    depth-<math|k> bounds to these interior cells; it follows from the
    repeated chessboard inequality (maximizing the position of a repeated
    copy in its containing cell). Translation invariance alone is not this
    comparison. Planting the finitely many trials now bounds the correlation
    by the product of their fixed chessboard bounds. All spans, margins and
    mesh thresholds were chosen independently of <math|p>. The periodic
    difference identity also holds at wrapped sites, completing the proof.
  </proof>

  <assign|chapter-nr|11>

  <chapter|The ultraviolet limit at fixed volume><label|ch12:ultraviolet>

  We keep the physical period <math|R<rsub|p>> fixed and let the ultraviolet
  cutoff <math|N> tend to infinity. The level <math|m> has spacing
  <math|a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>; blocking proceeds from <math|m+1> to
  <math|m>. The purpose of this chapter is to compare different cutoffs on
  one fixed configuration space. Compactness by itself would give only a
  subsequence. The additional ingredient is an estimate for stopping the
  marked evolution at an intermediate level, with a constant independent of
  that level.

  The common trajectory of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch10:trajectory> and the
  marked estimates of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> supply the
  stability and finite-difference estimates used here. We state their precise
  forms to distinguish a change of cutoff from comparison of two insertions
  under one fixed transformation.

  <section|Fixed-level convergence data><label|ch12:inputs>

  Fix finitely many labels <math|v<rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rsub|i>>> and
  distinct physical origins. At a fixed level <math|m> above their reference
  levels, use one dictionary of local supports and large-field inventories
  for all cutoffs <math|N\<gtr\>m>. All small-field event indicators and
  eligibility criteria in this dictionary are fixed at level <math|m>. A
  large-field profile is the sum of activities having one particular support
  and prescribed set of large-field sites; the original activity list can be
  infinite even though the number of such profiles on a fixed finite torus is
  finite.

  The required background convergence consists of Cauchy convergence of
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>>, of the path coefficients in their
  exponentially weighted sums, and of the regular and large-field activity
  arrays in the norms of Section<nbsp><reference|ch04:activities>. The scalar
  multiple of the density need not converge. For the marked transformation we
  need fixed-input continuity across cutoffs: at each fixed level, applying
  the cutoff-dependent marked map to any fixed admissible remainder graph
  gives Cauchy output graphs and coefficient maps. This condition is stronger
  than contraction for two graphs acted on by the same map. For the common
  construction, Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch10:convergence> supplies the
  background limits. The marked estimates and fixed-input comparison in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:one-graph> supply the corresponding
  single-insertion maps. We keep the needed forms explicit to show how the
  two comparisons enter this argument.

  Here is a useful way to obtain the required path convergence. For canonical
  path indices <math|a> of length <math|\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>, suppose the
  coefficients <math|c<rsub|N,a>> converge individually and obey a common
  bound at width <math|w\<gtr\>v>. Then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||c<rsub|N,a>-c<rsub|a>|\|>*e<rsup|v*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>\<leqslant\>2*C*e<rsup|-<around|(|w-v|)>*\<ell\><around|(|a|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The canonical indices enumerate displacement vectors and finitely many
  plaquette directions, rather than all walks of a given length. For a row
  their length is <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>+4>, and

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|x\<in\><ZZ><rsup|4>>e<rsup|-\<delta\>*<around|(|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>+4|)>>=e<rsup|-4*\<delta\>><around*|(|<frac|1+e<rsup|-\<delta\>>|1-e<rsup|-\<delta\>>>|)><rsup|4>\<less\>\<infty\>.
  </equation*>

  For an atom of degree <math|q> the analogous sum has <math|q-1>
  displacement vectors and exponent <math|4*<around|(|q-1|)>>. Dominated
  convergence therefore gives convergence in the smaller width <math|v>. A
  strict width reserve makes coordinatewise coefficient convergence
  sufficient for the actual path-action norm; convergence of an unrelated
  finite-volume matrix would not do so.

  <section|Contraction of the complete marked remainder><label|ch12:graphs>

  For a fixed degree, let <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|m>> denote the space of
  remainder arrays at level <math|m>. Its seminorm is the infimum of the sum
  of the regular derivative majorants and the large-field value majorants
  multiplied by <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>>, where
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>
  is the square root of the actual level-<math|m> large-field bound, as in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:one-graph>. We keep the arrays themselves,
  without identifying different arrays merely because their evaluations
  agree. Evaluation of the regular sum and of each large-field profile is a
  bounded linear map in this seminorm. This follows first for any admissible
  majorant and then by taking its infimum.

  An admissible graph <math|G:V<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-R\><rsub|m>> obeys
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-R\><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
  where <math|s<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> is fixed by the finite-degree
  construction. Set

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|m>*<around|(|G,H|)>=sup<rsub|x\<ne\>0>
    <frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|G-H|)>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-R\><rsub|m>>|s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
  </equation*>

  Thus any two admissible graphs have distance at most two. Write
  <math|I<rsub|G>> for the coefficient map of one marked step and
  <math|J<rsub|G>> for its unreparametrized remainder map. Its output graph
  is <math|\<Phi\>*G=J<rsub|G>*I<rsub|G><rsup|-1>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Graph contraction><label|ch12:contraction>Suppose the marked step
    maps admissible graphs into admissible graphs, and the common-support
    marked difference estimates give, for
    <math|a=d<rsub|m+1>*<around|(|G,H|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|G>-I<rsub|H>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*a,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|J<rsub|G>-J<rsub|H>|)>*x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-R\><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m>*a<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|G><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|m+1>.
    </equation*>

    If <math|4*\<delta\><rsub|m>*s<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>1>, then
    <math|d<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<Phi\>*G,\<Phi\>*H|)>\<leqslant\>a/2>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|y=I<rsub|G><rsup|-1>*x>, use
    <math|J<rsub|H>=<around|(|\<Phi\>*H|)>*I<rsub|H>> to write

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<Phi\>*G-\<Phi\>*H|)>*x=<around|(|J<rsub|G>-J<rsub|H>|)>*y+<around|(|\<Phi\>*H|)>*<around|(|I<rsub|H>-I<rsub|G>|)>*y.
    </equation*>

    Its norm is at most <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|m>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|m>*a*s<rsub|m+1><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>,
    which is at most <math|a*s<rsub|m><around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>/2>.
  </proof>

  The difference estimates are for the complete regular and large-field
  remainder on common supports. Linearity of a single insertion allows its
  local polynomial part to cancel. A contraction only for the regular part
  would not prove this lemma for the actual inserted density. For the actual
  transformation, <eqref|ch11:graph-estimates> gives ball invariance and the
  inverse bound, while <eqref|ch11:graph-difference> gives these two
  differences with <math|\<delta\><rsub|m>=C<rsub|d>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m>*s<rsub|m+1>>.
  The selected ratio and thresholds for the starting scales impose
  <math|4*\<delta\><rsub|m>*s<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>1>. The fine and coarse
  remainder norms retain their distinct factors
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>> and <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>>.
  Weighted path continuity at a strict width reserve and activity-norm
  continuity in Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:one-graph>, together with
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch10:convergence>, give the fixed-input cutoff
  continuity as well.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Forgetting the terminal graph><label|ch12:forgetting>For the
    selected trajectory, the graph contraction and fixed-input cutoff
    continuity just verified imply that the selected graphs and coefficients
    normalized at one reference level are Cauchy at every fixed level.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|m> and a positive integer <math|h>. Replace the selected graph
    at <math|m+h> by one fixed admissible graph and propagate down <math|h>
    steps. The resulting graphs are Cauchy as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>:
    fixed-input continuity and contraction give this by finite induction.
    Their distance from the actual graphs is at most <math|2<rsup|1-h>>.
    First let <math|h> be large and then let both cutoffs tend to infinity.
    This proves the Cauchy assertion without an infinite product of inverse
    maps.

    The coefficient at its reference level is the prescribed label. For each
    subsequent fixed level solve the finite-dimensional relation
    <math|x<rsub|m,N>=I<rsub|m,N>*x<rsub|m+1,N>>. The maps are Cauchy and
    their inverses uniformly bounded at this fixed level. The identity

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|m,N><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|m,N>-I<rsub|m,M><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|m,M>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m><around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|m,N>-x<rsub|m,M>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|I<rsub|m,N>-I<rsub|m,M>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>|\<\|\|\>>*I<rsub|m,M><rsup|-1>*x<rsub|m,M>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>|)>
    </equation*>

    proves the assertion by finite induction. Evaluation of
    <math|G<rsub|m,N>*x<rsub|m,N>> is Cauchy by the graph bound and its
    bounded evaluation maps.
  </proof>

  Consequently the complete functions multiplying a single source
  <math|f<rsub|i,N,m><around|(|U|)>> and large-field profiles linear in the
  sources <math|k<rsub|i,N,m,Q><around|(|U|)>> converge uniformly in
  <math|U>. Here <math|Q> is a support together with its exact prescribed set
  of large-field sites. Multiplication by a fixed Borel event indicator
  preserves uniform convergence; no continuity of the event indicator as a
  function of <math|U> is required.

  <section|The scalar normalization cancels><label|ch12:normalization>

  Write <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>> for the actual positive density at the fixed
  period, <math|s<rsub|N,m>> for its extensive scalar term, and

  <\equation*>
    <wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N,m>=e<rsup|-s<rsub|N,m>>*\<rho\><rsub|N,m>,<space|2em>Z<rsup|0><rsub|N,m>=<big|int><wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N,m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m,p>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<mu\><rsub|m,p>> is product probability Haar measure.
  Background convergence makes <math|<wide|\<rho\>|~><rsub|N,m>> uniformly
  Cauchy. Indeed, curvatures are bounded on the compact group, so weighted
  path convergence implies uniform action convergence. Summing a rooted
  activity norm over the finitely many sites gives uniform convergence of the
  regular sum and every large-field profile. The exact-cover gas is a finite
  polynomial in the latter profiles and fixed eligibility indicators. Its
  product with the exponential of the regular action therefore converges
  uniformly.

  The limit of <math|Z<rsup|0><rsub|N,m>> is strictly positive. Choose a
  fixed link ball <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|m>> about the identity small enough
  that every inspected plaquette satisfies the level-<math|m> cutoff. On this
  set there are no large-field sites, so the exact-cover sum is one. The
  action and the regular sum are bounded above by a constant
  <math|A<rsub|m,p>> uniformly on the cutoff tail. Since Haar measure assigns
  positive mass to every nonempty open link ball,

  <\equation>
    Z<rsup|0><rsub|N,m>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-A<rsub|m,p>>*\<mu\><rsub|m,p><around|(|\<cal-U\><rsub|m>|)>\<gtr\>0.<label|ch12:positive-partition>
  </equation>

  Nonnegativity of the full density justifies restricting the integral to
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|m>>. Positivity of each partition function alone would
  not imply a positive limit.

  Let <math|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|n>> be commuting variables with
  <math|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>=0>, and write <math|z<rsub|S>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>S>z<rsub|i>>.
  The exact marked density at level <math|m> has regular terms linear in the
  sources <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*f<rsub|i>>, higher regular entries
  <math|H<rsub|X>>, and large-field factors

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|Q>+<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*k<rsub|i,Q>+K<rsub|Q><rsup|\<geqslant\>2>.
  </equation*>

  Every term in <math|H<rsub|X>> and <math|K<rsub|Q><rsup|\<geqslant\>2>>
  contains at least two distinct source variables. All insertion origins
  belong to its support. Keep the complete terms linear in the sources and
  set only these higher entries to zero. Call the resulting density
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|X><rsup|0><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|z,U|)>>. The
  expression

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>=<frac|<big|int><around|[|z<rsub|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>|]>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|N,m>>*<with|math-font|cal*|X><rsup|0><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|z,U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|m,p>|Z<rsup|0><rsub|N,m>><label|ch12:stopped>
  </equation>

  is Cauchy in <math|N> for fixed <math|m>. Its numerator is a finite
  polynomial in uniformly convergent grouped profiles and functions
  multiplying a single source, multiplied by a uniformly convergent
  exponential. Its denominator has the positive limit in
  <eqref|ch12:positive-partition>. The same scalar has been removed from
  both. In particular, deleting terms involving at least two insertions does
  not mean deleting the large-field profiles linear in the sources.

  <section|A stopping estimate uniform in the stopping
  level><label|ch12:stopping>

  Recall that <math|B<rsub|m>> is the support radius from
  <eqref|ch04:thresholds> and <math|<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>
  counts a connected cover of <math|X> by radius-<math|B<rsub|m>> boxes.
  Suppose the physical origins belong to one common lattice and are distinct
  modulo <math|R<rsub|p>>. Their separation at level <math|m> is at least
  <math|\<eta\>/a<rsub|m>> for some <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>. Since
  <math|a<rsub|m>*B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<to\>0>, choose a fixed base level
  <math|r> so that separation is at least <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>> for every
  <math|m\<geqslant\>r>. Enlarge <math|r> to include all reference levels.

  We use the following marked norm conclusion. Terms involving at least two
  insertions have rooted majorant sums at most one at support exponent
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>>. The transfer accepts exponent
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m+1>-4> and restores exponent
  <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>>. It retains all inventories and insertion origins.
  Moreover, after transfer to the fixed base level, normalized coefficient
  extraction is bounded by

  <\equation>
    C<rsub|r>=<frac|exp <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|r,p>|\|>*q<rsub|r>+n*<around|(|x<rsub|r>+y<rsub|r>+2|)>|}>|<big|prod><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>>.<label|ch12:root-bound>
  </equation>

  Here <math|q<rsub|r>> is the vacuum normalization bound;
  <math|w<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> are fixed source weights; <math|x<rsub|r>> bounds
  the weighted regular sizes of the terms linear in the sources; and
  <math|y<rsub|r>> bounds their weighted large-field profile sums. Such
  weights can be chosen uniformly on the cutoff tail: the fixed-level
  coefficients linear in the sources are bounded, so scale each weight down
  to its positive allowed size. Crucially all four quantities in
  <eqref|ch12:root-bound> are evaluated at <math|r>, not at the later
  stopping level <math|m>. These statements concern the complete actual
  marked transfer. They follow from the estimate for terms involving at least
  two insertions <eqref|ch11:higher-induction> and the normalized chessboard
  bound <eqref|ch11:root-bound>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Removal of terms involving at least two
    insertions><label|ch12:omission>Under these marked norm and transfer
    bounds, for each <math|m\<gtr\>r> and all sufficiently large <math|N>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|r>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>.
    </equation*>

    The constant <math|C<rsub|r>> is independent of <math|m,N>; the cutoff
    threshold can depend on <math|m>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A support containing two origins separated by <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>>
    has cover number at least <math|B<rsub|m>/64>. Multiply every term of
    source degree at least two, and only those entries, by a complex scalar
    <math|u>. For <math|<around|\||u|\|>\<less\>S<rsub|m>=e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>>
    its majorant still fits exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|m>-2>, since

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||u|\|>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|m>-2|)><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|m><math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Transport the entire modified marked density to <math|r>. The support
    reserve allows its first and subsequent steps, and the constant and
    coefficients linear in the sources are unchanged. If a source monomial
    contains <math|q> origins, the stronger geometric bound
    <math|B<rsub|m>*q\<leqslant\>64<math-up|cov><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>
    also absorbs a change of each source weight by at most
    <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/128>>. This is the weight reserve needed when
    using the marked transfer.

    Let <math|F<rsub|N,m><around|(|u|)>> be the normalized integral of its
    full-source coefficient. Exact probability-kernel integration preserves
    that integral and its vacuum denominator. The fixed-base estimate gives
    <math|<around|\||F<rsub|N,m><around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r>>
    throughout the disk. The source algebra is nilpotent; after grouping the
    finitely many support and prescribed-site profiles, <math|F<rsub|N,m>> is
    a polynomial in <math|u>, hence holomorphic. For
    <math|S<rsub|m>\<gtr\>2>, Cauchy's derivative estimate on circles of
    radius <math|S<rsub|m>/2> about points of <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> gives
    <math|<around|\||F<rsub|N,m><around|(|1|)>-F<rsub|N,m><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|r>/S<rsub|m>>.
    These two values are the actual cutoff moment and <eqref|ch12:stopped>,
    respectively.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Cauchy convergence on common lattice
    points><label|ch12:dense-cauchy>For the selected trajectory, the
    correlations converge along the whole cutoff sequence at every
    configuration of distinct points belonging to one common lattice.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Given an error tolerance, first choose <math|m> so that the two stopping
    errors in Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch12:omission> are small. Keep <math|m>
    fixed. The stopped expression <eqref|ch12:stopped> is Cauchy as both
    cutoffs tend to infinity. The triangle inequality proves the assertion.
    No new subsequence is chosen.
  </proof>

  <section|Smoothness and all lattice differences><label|ch12:smooth>

  The remaining passage is a standard discrete version of the Arzelà\UAscoli
  theorem and convergence of derivatives; compare
  <cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorems<nbsp>7.17 and<nbsp>7.25>. We record the
  precise application, since the rounded lattice functions are not
  continuous. Let <math|U\<subset\><RR><rsup|D>> be open,
  <math|a<rsub|N>*\<downarrow\>*0>, and let <math|F<rsub|N>> be functions on
  <math|a<rsub|N><ZZ><rsup|D>>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*F<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<around|(|F<rsub|N>*<around|(|x+a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|j>|)>-F<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Assume every fixed iterated difference is bounded on each compact subset of
  <math|U>, eventually in <math|N>, and the rounded values are Cauchy on a
  dense subset. Then there is <math|F\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|U|)>>
  such that all rounded iterated differences converge locally uniformly to
  the corresponding derivatives of <math|F>.

  Only the discrete-to-continuous adaptation needs explanation. For nearby
  lattice points, telescoping along coordinate edges gives
  <math|<around|\||G<rsub|N><around|(|y|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\||y-x|\|><rsub|1>>
  whenever the first differences of <math|G<rsub|N>> are bounded on a
  slightly larger compact neighborhood. Rounding adds at most
  <math|2*D*C*a<rsub|N>> to this modulus. A finite net from the dense Cauchy
  set therefore proves locally uniform convergence of <math|F<rsub|N>>.
  Bounded second differences give the discrete Taylor estimate

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||G<rsub|N><around|(|y|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|y<rsub|j>-x<rsub|j>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\||y-x|\|><rsub|1><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed small <math|h\<gtr\>0>, compare
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N,j>*G<rsub|N><around|(|x<rsub|N>|)>> to the secant
  with interval <math|h<rsub|N>=a<rsub|N>*<around|\<lceil\>|h/a<rsub|N>|\<rceil\>>>.
  The error is at most <math|C*h<rsub|N>>. The secants converge first as
  <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>; then <math|h\<to\>0> proves that the first
  differences are Cauchy. Their own first-difference bounds give locally
  uniform convergence. Passing to the displayed Taylor inequality identifies
  their limits as the derivatives of the limit of <math|G<rsub|N>>. Iteration
  proves the assertion for every finite order. All paths and shifted stencils
  lie in the enlarged compact neighborhood.

  Apply this to periodic lifts on

  <\equation*>
    U<rsub|p,n>=<around|{|<around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\><around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>\<nin\>R<rsub|p><ZZ><rsup|4><text|
    for >i\<ne\>j|}>.
  </equation*>

  The union of the nested physical lattices is dense here. Its points supply
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch12:dense-cauchy>; the all-order separated
  finite-difference bounds supply the other hypothesis. Points sharing one or
  several coordinate projections are allowed. Only coincidences of the full
  four-dimensional points modulo the period are excluded. The limiting
  function, denoted <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>>, is smooth and
  periodic on this domain. The difference hypothesis is supplied by
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch11:all-period-differences>: on a compact subset
  of <math|U<rsub|p,n>> the periodic separation has a positive lower bound,
  and any fixed shifted difference stencil stays in a slightly larger
  separated compact set on the cutoff tail.

  <section|The fixed-volume conclusion><label|ch12:conclusion>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Full ultraviolet limit><label|ch12:uv-theorem>For the common
    selected trajectory with the strengthened stage requirements of
    Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch10:trajectory> and
    <reference|ch11:insertions>, the correlations have the smooth
    full-sequence limit <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>> at every fixed
    physical period. For every <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|U<rsub|p,n>|)>>
    supported in an admissible period cell and every finite coordinate list
    <math|\<alpha\>>,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>> a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*y|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|N,\<alpha\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
    </equation*>

    Here differences in the summand are divided by their physical step
    lengths. Their shifted sites must lie in the slightly enlarged separated
    set, which holds on a cutoff tail.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The preceding section gives the full smooth limit on separated
    configurations. The last assertion is a Riemann-sum consequence of
    locally uniform convergence. Fixed forward, backward or centered stencils
    give the same limit: telescope each stencil as a finite average of
    shifted unit forward differences. The averaging weights sum to one and
    the shifted points approach the same point.
  </proof>

  The convergence threshold in this theorem may depend on <math|p>. Bounds on
  compact separated sets that were uniform in <math|p> remain uniform for the
  limiting kernels, by passage to the limit. These two quantifier statements
  serve different purposes: the former removes the ultraviolet cutoff; the
  latter permits the subsequent infinite-volume extraction. Finite reflection
  inequalities also pass to the limit whenever every correlation in their
  finite quadratic form is supported away from collisions. Neither this
  argument nor reflection positivity asserts an unsubtracted limit at
  coincident points. Distributional extensions and reconstruction require
  their own arguments.

  <assign|chapter-nr|12>

  <chapter|Continuum correlations and local fields><label|ch13:continuum>

  The ultraviolet limit leaves a theory on each fixed physical torus. We now
  let its period grow. The construction may select a subsequence of volumes,
  but it does not select a new subsequence of ultraviolet cutoffs for the
  correlations. Local fields are then defined by their responses in all
  separated correlations. Polynomial labels that give the same responses
  represent the same field.

  We use the full fixed-volume limit of Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch12:uv-theorem>,
  the volume-uniform finite-difference bounds of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch11:all-period-differences>, and the equation
  comparisons and the comparisons using separating families in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>. Compactness gives the volume
  extraction; the separate equation inclusion and rank bounds identify its
  local fields.

  <section|One subsequence of volumes><label|ch13:extraction>

  Recall that <math|<Conf><rsub|n>> is the open set of pairwise distinct
  points in <math|<around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>, without any restriction
  on their coordinate projections. Let <math|p<around|(|k|)>> be any strictly
  increasing sequence of volume indices. On every compact subset of
  <math|<Conf><rsub|n>>, the periodic kernels
  <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p<around|(|k|)>,v>> are eventually defined
  with separation bounded below independently of <math|k>. Each of their
  derivatives is uniformly bounded there. The cutoff threshold used to prove
  the bound may depend on the fixed volume; after taking that ultraviolet
  limit it disappears from the bound.

  Arzelà\UAscoli and diagonal extraction <cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem<nbsp>7.25>
  give one refinement <math|P<around|(|k|)>=p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>
  and a multilinear family <math|S<rsub|n>> such that

  <\equation>
    M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|P<around|(|k|)>,v>\<longrightarrow\>S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|in
    >C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|<Conf><rsub|n>|)>.<label|ch13:volume-limit>
  </equation>

  The diagonal family is countable: arities, degree lists, basis-label lists,
  derivative lists, and compact exhaustions of the configuration spaces.
  Multilinearity extends its conclusions to all labels. Integration by parts
  identifies the extracted derivative limits with the derivatives of
  <math|S<rsub|n>>. No diagonalization over all smooth test functions is
  needed. For each fixed separated test function <math|f> the order of limits
  is

  <\equation>
    lim<rsub|k\<to\>\<infty\>> lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>>
    a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*y|)>*M<rsub|N,P<around|(|k|)>>*<around|(|v;y|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.<label|ch13:ordered-limit>
  </equation>

  The inner limit is the whole cutoff sequence. Every further volume
  refinement preserves <eqref|ch13:volume-limit> and
  <eqref|ch13:ordered-limit>.

  Permutation symmetry, conjugation, the distinguished unit and hypercubic
  covariance pass from the finite correlations by these limits. Translation
  by an arbitrary real vector is obtained by approximating it on the same
  lattice; the uniform first-difference bound controls the rounding error.
  Full orthogonal covariance will require a separate argument.

  <section|Separated cells and errors in local observables><label|ch13:cells>

  The chessboard seminorm of Section<nbsp><reference|ch01:reflection> bounds
  an observable by its repeated reflected copies in a tiling. For local
  observables <math|F<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,F<rsub|n>> interior to distinct cells
  of the same translated tiling <math|\<cal-T\>>, the chessboard inequality
  gives

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|i>F<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>>.<label|ch13:chess>
  </equation>

  It is the lattice inequality already cited from
  <cite|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978>, with the constant observable in every
  unused cell. The estimate does not grow with the distances between the
  cells.

  Here is the required geometric placement. For a tiling of physical side
  <math|a>, the translations in one coordinate bringing one of <math|n>
  anchors within distance <math|w> of a boundary have total length at most
  <math|2*n*w> modulo <math|a>. If <math|2*n*w\<less\>a>, a translation
  avoids all these strips; a lattice translation does so for sufficiently
  fine cutoff. Choose one in each coordinate. Anchors with pairwise
  maximum-coordinate distance greater than <math|a> cannot share a cell.
  Hence if the centered local bounds apply at scale <math|k> with margin
  <math|3*a<rsub|k>>, and <math|6*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>>, anchors
  separated by more than <math|a<rsub|m>> admit distinct
  side-<math|a<rsub|m>> cells with the required margins.

  We shall use an error family <math|E<rsub|N,p>> with the following
  property: for any fixed arity and sufficiently small fixed cell side, its
  chessboard seminorm is at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|N,p>\<to\>0> in the
  ordered limits, uniformly over placements satisfying a specified interior
  margin. The cell side can be chosen after the arity and a requested lower
  scale, before the cutoff. The spectator insertions have uniform centered
  seminorm bounds on sufficiently fine fixed scales. Place the error and all
  spectators in distinct cells, choosing a still finer scale for the
  spectators' margins. Equation <eqref|ch13:chess> yields

  <\equation>
    <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h\<ne\>i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,y<rsub|h>|)>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|v,\<delta\>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|N,p>,<label|ch13:vanishing-error>
  </equation>

  for configurations separated by <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. The weighted
  number of lattice points in the fixed compact support of a test function is
  bounded. The same error therefore vanishes after separated smearing. This
  conclusion uses the stated order of choices for the error norm; pointwise
  smallness at one configuration would not suffice.

  <section|Fields as equivalence classes of labels><label|ch13:quotient>

  A label includes its degree space: the same formal polynomial in
  <math|V<rsub|d>> and <math|V<rsub|e>> can have different cutoff
  normalizations. For <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, a fixed spectator list
  <math|v>, and a separated smooth test function <math|f>, define the
  one-slot response

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|v,f><around|(|c|)>=<big|int>f*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x|)>*S<rsub|n+1>*<around|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation*>

  Let

  <\equation>
    N<rsub|d><around|(|S|)>=<big|cap><rsub|v,f>ker
    L<rsub|v,f>,<space|2em>F<rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/N<rsub|d><around|(|S|)>.<label|ch13:null>
  </equation>

  The intersection permits every finite spectator list, of arbitrary degrees.
  Smoothness makes this equivalent to vanishing in every pointwise separated
  correlation. Permutation symmetry ensures that nullity has the same meaning
  in every slot. The quotient <math|F<rsub|d>> is finite dimensional, but its
  dimension has not yet been identified.

  We use the bounded equivariant idempotents <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:uniform-differences>, with kernel
  <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>P<rsub|N,p,d>*S<rsub|t<rsub|N>>*R<rsub|d>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>.
  Choose a common ordered limit <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>> after a coefficient
  refinement. More precisely, diagonal extraction over the countably many
  degrees and volumes gives one increasing sequence <math|N=n<around|(|j|)>>
  along which the bounded coefficient arrays converge at each fixed volume. A
  further volume refinement makes those limits converge as well. All
  coefficient limits below use these refined ordered tails. This auxiliary
  selection does not change the already existing full cutoff limits of the
  correlations. Write <math|r<rsub|d>=dim
  \<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> for the dimension of the free
  Maxwell quotient in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch03:maxwell>. The construction
  gives:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>the ranks of <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>> are at most
    <math|r<rsub|d>>;

    <item>every bounded family in <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>> has vanishing
    separated responses in the ordered limits;

    <item>there are finite matrices of limiting separated responses,
    evaluated on comparison labels, that approach an invertible
    <math|r<rsub|d>\<times\>r<rsub|d>> free matrix of correlation pairings.
  </enumerate>

  The first statement is exact rank equality, since every map defining the
  finite equation space is invertible. For the second,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:uniform-sweep> makes its chessboard seminorms
  tend to zero; bounded rows have uniformly subpower inverse terminal
  coordinates, and <eqref|ch13:vanishing-error> supplies every spectator
  response. For the third, Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:finite-detector-input>
  gives the actual finite-cutoff matrices with error tending to zero as their
  comparison depth increases. Include their finitely many bounded label
  columns at each of the countably many comparison depths in the same
  coefficient refinement. At each fixed depth, their ordered limits preserve
  the matrix estimate by multilinearity and the uniform correlation bounds.
  An injective rectangular matrix of correlation pairings has an invertible
  square minor; retain those rows.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Identification by two rank bounds><label|ch13:rank>For this
    selected continuum family, <math|N<rsub|d><around|(|S|)>=ker
    \<Pi\><rsub|d>> and <math|dim F<rsub|d>=r<rsub|d>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For fixed <math|c>, the bounded family
    <math|<around|(|1-\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>|)>*c> has vanishing responses.
    Taking limits gives <math|L<rsub|v,f>*<around|(|<around|(|1-\<Pi\><rsub|d>|)>*c|)>=0>
    for all <math|v,f>, so <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|d>\<subset\>N<rsub|d><around|(|S|)>>.
    Converging idempotents have eventually constant ranks: if their norm
    difference is less than one, each is injective on the other's range. Thus
    <math|dim F<rsub|d>\<leqslant\><math-up|rank><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|d>>.
    Each comparison matrix factors through <math|F<rsub|d>>, since its rows
    are separated responses. Matrices sufficiently close to the invertible
    free matrix of correlation pairings have rank <math|r<rsub|d>>. Hence
    <math|dim F<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>r<rsub|d>>. Equality of dimensions and the
    kernel inclusion prove the result.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Representatives chosen before the spectators>

  Approximate inclusion and derivative labels need not stay bounded. The
  following finite-dimensional observation explains the normalized null
  estimate required to replace them by fixed vectors. It also avoids choosing
  a separate representative for every test function.

  Let <math|V> be finite dimensional, <math|\<Pi\><rsub|u>\<to\>\<Pi\>>
  idempotents, and <math|L<rsub|i,u>\<to\>L<rsub|i>> linear functionals,
  along a proper filter of indices. Assume <math|ker
  \<Pi\>=<big|cap><rsub|i>ker L<rsub|i>>. If vectors <math|x<rsub|u>> satisfy

  <\equation>
    L<rsub|i,u><around|(|x<rsub|u>|)>\<to\>t<rsub|i>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i,u><around*|(|<frac|x<rsub|u>-\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|\<\|\|\>>>|)>\<to\>0<space|1em><text|for
    every >i,<label|ch13:normalized-null>
  </equation>

  there is one <math|z\<in\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\>> with
  <math|L<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=t<rsub|i>> for all <math|i>. To see this, put
  <math|b<rsub|u>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
  and <math|z<rsub|u>=b<rsub|u>*\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>>. A common cluster
  point <math|<around|(|b,z|)>> satisfies
  <math|b+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>, <math|\<Pi\>*z=z> and
  <math|L<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=b*t<rsub|i>>. If <math|b=0>, then
  <math|z\<in\>ker \<Pi\>\<cap\><math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\>>, a
  contradiction. The vector <math|z/b> is the desired representative.
  Compactness is used only in one fixed finite-dimensional space; all
  response identities hold at the same cluster point because they hold along
  the filter.

  In the application, the filter is generated by ordered tails:
  <math|p=P<around|(|k|)>> with <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>, and
  <math|N=n<around|(|j|)>> with <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>>.
  The normalized null condition is available for every admissible coefficient
  family in the sense of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:uniform-differences>.
  Indeed, if <math|x<rsub|N,p>> has subpower inverse terminal coordinates,
  then <math|x/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*x|\<\|\|\>>|)>> has the
  same property, whereas <math|\<Pi\>*x/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*x|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
  is bounded and therefore admissible. Their difference lies in the exact
  finite equation kernel. Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch11:uniform-sweep>, followed
  by <eqref|ch13:vanishing-error>, proves <eqref|ch13:normalized-null> for
  all spectators on the same tail. This argument applies even when the
  original <math|x<rsub|N,p>> are unbounded.

  <section|Inclusions and derivatives><label|ch13:calculus>

  For each basis label <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, the protected trial
  construction of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent> gives
  approximate representatives in <math|V<rsub|e>> for <math|e\<geqslant\>d>
  and in <math|V<rsub|d+1>> for a physical lattice difference. To check the
  inclusion trial, multiply the original cutoff observable by
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e>>; its local representative is
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e-d>> times the degree-<math|d> section, so its
  derivative and value bounds remain subpower. For a difference, the extra
  factor <math|a<rsub|N>> cancels the inverse step, and the fixed
  translated-stencil cost is bounded by the coupling-threshold requirements
  used in Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch11:all-period-differences>. Trial descent
  yields admissible coefficients and vanishing chessboard seminorm errors,
  uniformly in period. The preceding paragraph gives their normalized null
  estimates. All representatives and comparison errors are chosen before the
  spectators and test functions. Applying the preceding finite-dimensional
  observation gives fixed linear maps on labels,
  <math|<wide|\<iota\>|^><rsub|d,e>> and <math|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|d,\<mu\>>>,
  such that

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|<wide|\<iota\>|^><rsub|d,e>*c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>>|<cell|=S*<around|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>,<eq-number><label|ch13:inclusion-response>>>|<row|<cell|S*<around|(|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|d,\<mu\>>*c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>>|<cell|=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|\<mu\>>>*S*<around|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch13:derivative-response>>>>>
  </align>

  For the derivative target, exact torus summation by parts is

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*y|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|N,i,\<mu\>>*M<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y><frac|f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*y-a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|i,\<mu\>>|)>-f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*y|)>|a<rsub|N>>*M<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>.
  </equation*>

  The quotient on the right tends uniformly to
  <math|-\<partial\><rsub|i,\<mu\>>*f>; the zero-order separated bound
  controls its error. This identifies the distributional derivative target.
  Smoothness gives <eqref|ch13:derivative-response> pointwise.

  These maps descend to unique maps <math|\<iota\><rsub|d,e>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|e>>
  and <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>> with the
  displayed responses. They preserve null classes by their definition. The
  inclusions are injective and satisfy

  <\equation*>
    \<iota\><rsub|d,d>=1,<space|2em>\<iota\><rsub|e,f>*\<iota\><rsub|d,e>=\<iota\><rsub|d,f>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>=\<partial\><rsub|\<nu\>>*\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>.
  </equation*>

  Each identity follows by testing against every spectator and using
  <eqref|ch13:null>; the derivative maps also commute with inclusions.
  Conjugation and hypercubic covariance descend in the same way. The union of
  the <math|F<rsub|d>> is therefore a filtered space of local fields, with
  derivatives raising the filtration by one. No multiplication of two fields
  at the same point has been defined by this construction.

  <section|Reflection positivity and separated power
  bounds><label|ch13:positivity>

  Let a word mean a finite list of labels and positions, with correlation
  denoted <math|S<around|(|w|)>>. Write <math|w<rsup|\<ast\>>> for
  conjugation of its labels and reversal of its order. Time is coordinate
  four. Let <math|\<theta\>*w> reflect that coordinate and act on its labels
  by the corresponding signed transformation, as well as applying
  <math|\<ast\>>. For words with strictly positive times, lattice bond
  reflection positivity passes to

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*S*<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  It also passes for joint smooth test functions supported on positive-time
  separated configurations. Indeed their lattice supports stay away from both
  reflection planes on the ordered tail. The lattice plane
  <math|x<rsup|4>=a<rsub|N>/2> tends to zero, and the uniform separated bound
  controls the moving test functions. Taking the two limits preserves
  positivity of each finite matrix. Pointwise positivity follows by
  concentrating smooth bumps. This uses the finite lattice result of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch01:reflection>; it asserts no positivity for
  unspecified extensions at coincident points.

  For later use take the centered chessboard estimate
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O|\<\|\|\>><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>C*a<rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>>,
  uniform on a bounded set of labels of degrees at most <math|d> and every
  sufficiently fine fixed level <math|k>. It follows by choosing
  <math|\<epsilon\>=1> in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch13:sharp-centered>,
  whose proof below uses only the bound for reflected tilings of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> and the selected schedule. Slow
  growth of the block ratios gives a constant <math|c\<gtr\>0> with

  <\equation>
    a<rsub|k+1>\<geqslant\>c*a<rsub|k><rsup|2>.<label|ch13:scale-thickness>
  </equation>

  The first scale below a length <math|r\<leqslant\>1> is consequently at
  least a constant times <math|r<rsup|2>>. Choose a cell side below
  <math|r/2>, then a centered scale below that side divided by
  <math|6*<around|(|n+1|)>>. The latter is at least
  <math|c<rsub|n>*r<rsup|4>>. The separated-cell estimate yields

  <\equation>
    <around|\||S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n,v>*<space|0.17em>r<rsup|-4*<around|(|d+1|)>*n>,<space|2em>min<rsub|i\<ne\>j><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>r.<label|ch13:power>
  </equation>

  The constant is independent of the diameter of the configuration. Applying
  the same argument to the fixed degree-raised labels in
  <eqref|ch13:derivative-response> gives a polynomial separated bound for
  every derivative.

  <section|Repeated reflected words><label|ch13:repeated>

  The preceding bound cannot be used with arity tending to infinity without
  controlling its constants. We need a more specific estimate. Let <math|w>
  have <math|n\<geqslant\>1> insertions, degrees at most <math|d> and
  coefficient norms at most <math|R>, pairwise separation at least <math|r>,
  and times in <math|<around|[|r,h-r|]>>, where
  <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>. We call <math|r> its buffer in the slab of
  width <math|h>. For a positive-time exterior word <math|v>, set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>>*v,<space|2em>w<rsup|\<dagger\>>=\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>>*\<theta\>*w,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|OS>><rsup|2>=S*<around|(|\<theta\>*v*<space|0.17em>v|)>.
  </equation*>

  At this point <math|A<rsub|w>> acts only on words; boundedness on a Hilbert
  space is not assumed.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Uniform bound for repetitions><label|ch13:word-bound>Under the
    centered chessboard bounds and their interior-cell comparison used above,
    choose <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>h> so that <math|w> still has buffer
    <math|r/2>. There are <math|C=C*<around|(|d,R|)>> and
    <math|\<kappa\>=4*<around|(|d+1|)>> such that, for every exterior word
    <math|v>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|OS>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|v>*<space|0.17em><around|[|C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|\<kappa\>*n>|]><rsup|4*j>,<space|2em>j\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|s,C,\<kappa\>> are chosen before <math|v>, and
    <math|B<rsub|v>\<less\>\<infty\>> is independent of <math|j>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose a cell scale <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/32> as the first permitted
    such scale, and <math|s=a<rsub|m>*<around|\<lfloor\>|h/a<rsub|m>|\<rfloor\>>>.
    Equation <eqref|ch13:scale-thickness> gives
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|1>*r<rsup|2>>. Choose a centered scale
    <math|a<rsub|k>\<less\>\<alpha\>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|2>>, with
    <math|20*\<alpha\>\<less\>c<rsub|1>>. Again taking the first such scale
    gives <math|20*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>> and
    <math|a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|2>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|4>>.

    The expanded reflection form contains <math|4*j*n> main insertions and
    two exterior copies. Since <math|s> is a multiple of <math|a<rsub|m>>,
    the main points have at most <math|2*n> residue patterns modulo the
    side-<math|a<rsub|m>> tiling, independently of <math|j>. The strip count
    therefore places all main points in distinct cells with margins
    <math|5*a<rsub|k>>, using constants independent of the number of
    repetitions.

    Choose finer aligned cell and centered scales for the finitely many
    exterior points, with exterior cell side at most <math|a<rsub|k>>. Their
    two copies have times <math|\<pm\><around|(|2*j*s+t|)>>, so a choice
    depending on their minimum positive time and internal separation works
    for every <math|j>. A further translation smaller than the exterior cell
    side adjusts their margins while leaving the main margins at least
    <math|3*a<rsub|k>>. The odd integer ratios preserve the fine cell grid
    under coarse reflections. Apply the chessboard inequality first to the
    coarse cells, regarding all exterior factors in one coarse cell as a
    single observable, then to the fine cells for those factors. If <math|X>
    bounds the exterior centered seminorms, the result is

    <\equation*>
      X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>*<around|(|C<rsub|0>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>|)><rsup|4*j*n>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|B<rsub|v>=max <around|(|1,X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>|)>> and
    use the lower bound on <math|a<rsub|k>>. Every use of the lattice
    inequality is at fixed <math|j> before taking the ordered limits. A
    cutoff threshold uniform in <math|j> is unnecessary.
  </proof>

  This estimate is the input for bounded slab insertions in the reflection
  Hilbert space. Its derivation precedes that operator construction and does
  not use orthogonal covariance or a phase decomposition. The equation
  comparison, admissible trial encoding, estimates for finite separating
  families and centered estimates all refer to the same selected family. Full
  orthogonal covariance and products at coincident points remain separate
  constructions.

  <section|Sharp fixed-arity short-distance bounds><label|ch13:sharp-section>

  For a fixed number of insertions the loss in <eqref|ch13:power> can be
  reduced to an arbitrarily small excess over the sum of their degrees. This
  uses the normalized integral over the reflected tiling, not the stronger
  Gaussian comparison used for rank detection. We keep all incoming joint
  marked components; replacing them by a product of terms linear in the
  sources would invalidate the estimate.

  Write <math|\<tau\><rsub|m>=log <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>>, so
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>>.
  Let <math|C<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> include the inverse-column,
  finite-section and source constants at stage <math|j>. The stage ratio and
  coupling threshold may be chosen to satisfy

  <\equation>
    <frac|log C<rsub|j>|log <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>,<space|2em><frac|<around|(|L<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|site>>|\<tau\><rsub|i+1>>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|ch13:sharp-schedule>
  </equation>

  Here <math|B<rsub|i>> is the support-block radius, and
  <math|q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|site>>\<geqslant\>1> is the per-site
  normalization cost of <eqref|ch11:replicated-bound>; it is not a marked
  density or the scalar coupling correction. For the admissible path-action
  representation one can use <math|q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|site>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>*<around|(|1+log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)>>. Indeed the scalar-free exponent has upper
  bound <math|C<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>*V>, while on the event that every link
  <math|U> has normalized Hilbert\USchmidt distance
  <math|\<chi\><around|(|U|)>> at most <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>/4>
  it has lower bound <math|-C<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>*V> and all large-field
  indicators vanish. The leading positive path quadratic is bounded there by
  <math|C<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsup|2>*V>
  with <math|\<rho\>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>>; exponential path
  summability bounds the path lengths in this estimate. Signed regular costs
  and the absolute activity site sum have their admissible bounds. The
  Haar-ball argument of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:replicated> therefore
  costs only <math|C<rsub|J<around|(|i|)>>*V+2*D<rsub|G>*V*log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, where <math|D<rsub|G>> is an ambient real
  matrix dimension for the gauge group.

  These requirements are compatible with sufficiently large coupling
  thresholds. At each fixed stage <math|B<rsub|i>=O<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|log
  <around|(|2+\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
  <math|q<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|site>>=O<rsub|j><around|(|1+log
  <around|(|2+\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>|)>>, so the last quotient in
  <eqref|ch13:sharp-schedule> tends to zero at its fixed ratio. Choose the
  ratio after <math|C<rsub|j>>, then increase the coupling threshold until
  that quotient is at most <math|1/j> for both adjacent-stage choices. The
  same threshold choice gives the displayed bound on consecutive
  <math|B<rsub|i>>, <math|log L<rsub|i>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>>, and
  <math|log C<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>> at
  transitions. Choose each ratio against both adjacent-stage column
  constants, which are fixed beforehand. These are conditions on the single
  selected schedule; no new schedule is chosen after fixing a correlation.

  <subsection|A normalized local insertion>

  For degree <math|d> and coefficients in a fixed bounded ball, set

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|k>=C<rsub|d,R><rsup|<math-up|sec>>*C<rsub|J<around|(|k|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>C<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|k>=a<rsub|k><rsup|d>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>*E<rsub|k><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The fixed section constant <math|C<rsub|d,R><rsup|<math-up|sec>>> includes
  the coefficient radius and all its signed and conjugated images. Choose the
  fixed integer <math|w<rsub|d>> large enough to absorb the finite powers of
  the inverse small-field threshold in the degree-<math|d> section and its
  counterpart constrained to cover the prescribed large-field sites, as well
  as the source attachment bound. Multiplication of the single-insertion
  inverse bounds gives the factor <math|E<rsub|k>>; the scaling normalization
  is exactly <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>>. Thus multiplication of the
  <em|complete> source by <math|\<omega\><rsub|k>> makes the exponential
  bound for its regular term linear in the sources less than three and the
  bound for its large-field term linear in the sources less than one. Its
  remaining normalized size can be placed below the attachment bound by
  increasing <math|w<rsub|d>>.

  The bounds in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> for terms involving
  at least two insertions retain every incoming source term and every
  original eligibility event. Decreasing all mark weights only decreases
  their absolute mode sums. The source attachment inequalities at the
  intervening levels propagate this chosen small weight; they require the
  same inverse-column and threshold powers already included in
  <math|E<rsub|k>,w<rsub|d>>. Consequently <eqref|ch11:root-bound>, with
  <math|x=3,y=1>, gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|cell><around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*exp
    <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>+6|}>.<label|ch13:sharp-root>
  </equation>

  It holds on a full cutoff tail uniformly in period and in the coefficient
  ball, whenever the reflected anchors at the stopping level have spacing at
  least <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>>. Their spacing stays adequate at finer
  levels because <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3> whereas
  <math|B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>/B<rsub|i><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2>.

  The logarithm of <math|E<rsub|k>> is <math|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>>:
  split its sum into a finite initial segment and a tail where <math|log
  C<rsub|J*<around|(|i+1|)>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*log L<rsub|i>>, then let
  <math|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>. The separate terminal column is
  absorbed by the same ratio bounds and <math|log
  L<rsub|k-1>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>>. Also <math|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>>. Therefore

  <\equation>
    6-log \<omega\><rsub|k>=d*\<tau\><rsub|k>+o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>.<label|ch13:weight-size>
  </equation>

  This is the reason the prefactor from the reflected tiling does not
  introduce a fixed extra scaling degree.

  <subsection|The stopping scale and centered estimate>

  Choose <math|k=k<around|(|m|)>> as the first level <math|k\<geqslant\>m>
  with <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>. It exists
  because <math|B<rsub|k>> grows subexponentially in <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>>,
  whereas <math|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|\<tau\><rsub|k>-\<tau\><rsub|m>>>.
  By minimality,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>\<leqslant\><around|(|L<rsub|k-1>*B<rsub|k-1><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation*>

  If the first level already qualifies the same conclusion follows from the
  fixed-stage cost bound. Since <math|q<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>\<geqslant\>1>,

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rsub|k>-\<tau\><rsub|m>=log
    <around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|site>>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation*>

  It follows that <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>/\<tau\><rsub|m>\<to\>1>. Combining
  this with <eqref|ch13:sharp-root> and <eqref|ch13:weight-size> proves the
  following statement.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Sharp centered bound><label|ch13:sharp-centered>For every degree
    <math|d>, coefficient radius <math|R> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    there is <math|C<rsub|d,R,\<epsilon\>>>, fixed before the cell scale
    <math|m>, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|cell><around|(|m,y|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,R,\<epsilon\>>*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<epsilon\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed <math|m> this holds for all sufficiently large cutoffs,
    uniformly in <math|p,y> and in signed and conjugated images. The
    insertion is interior in that cell on the same tail.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For all sufficiently large <math|m>, the logarithm of the right side of
    <eqref|ch13:sharp-root> is at most <math|<around|(|d+\<epsilon\>|)>*\<tau\><rsub|m>>
    by the preceding limits. For the finite remaining set of <math|m>, use
    its selected fixed stopping level and take the maximum of the finite
    constants. The fixed lattice stencil becomes interior at every fixed
    physical cell scale as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. Translation covariance
    makes the threshold independent of <math|y>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Arbitrary separated points>

  The slow ratio condition gives a sharper first-below-scale estimate than
  <eqref|ch13:scale-thickness>. For any fixed <math|c\<gtr\>0>, let
  <math|m<around|(|r|)>> be the first level with <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>c*r>.
  Then

  <\equation>
    <frac|log <around|(|1/a<rsub|m<around|(|r|)>>|)>|log
    <around|(|1/r|)>>\<longrightarrow\>1*<space|1em><around|(|r*\<downarrow\>*0|)>.<label|ch13:first-below>
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|a<rsub|m-1>\<geqslant\>c*r\<gtr\>a<rsub|m>=a<rsub|m-1>/L<rsub|m-1>>,
  and the logarithm of the overshoot is at most <math|log
  L<rsub|m-1>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m-1>|)>>. Any fixed finite iteration of
  first-below choices has the same property.

  For a fixed arity <math|n> and mutually separated anchors, first choose a
  cell side below a fixed small multiple of their minimum separation
  <math|r>. Translate its boundaries away from all anchors. Then choose a
  centered scale smaller than this side divided by <math|6*<around|(|n+1|)>>.
  The interior-cell comparison and the chessboard inequality apply. Both
  chosen scales have logarithm <math|<around|(|1+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*log
  <around|(|1/r|)>> by <eqref|ch13:first-below>. Apply
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch13:sharp-centered> with a sufficiently small
  excess to each degree separately. We obtain, for every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <around|\||S<rsub|n>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|n>;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<b-A\>,n,\<epsilon\>>*r<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>-\<epsilon\>>,<space|2em>0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>A<rsub|i>\<in\>F<rsub|d<rsub|i>>,<space|1em>min<rsub|i\<ne\>j><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>r.<label|ch13:sharp-separated>
  </equation>

  The same bound is uniform over bounded sets of fixed raw representatives.
  Its constant is independent of the diameter of the configuration. For fixed
  derivatives, replace the differentiated field by its degree-raised label;
  their total order is added to <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>>.

  <section|Bounded real approximations for separated
  words><label|ch13:bounded-approximations>

  The later phase argument needs real approximants bounded uniformly in the
  cutoff. Compactness of the link space at each cutoff is insufficient: the
  physical normalization of a local field grows as the mesh shrinks. We
  instead stop at one fixed coarse level and pull back a bounded coarse
  function through the probability block kernels.

  We record the precise marked estimate used in this construction. It is the
  local-trial comparison for reflected tilings, with every term involving at
  least two insertions retained, not only its one-column coefficient
  estimate. At a coarse level <math|h>, write <math|C<rsub|h>> for its
  prescribed coefficient-column bound, <math|Q<rsub|h>> for the ordinary norm
  bound of a normalized local trial, and <math|E<rsub|h>> for its complete
  bound, including the complementary large-field part. For the selected
  trajectory these bounds and all fixed coefficient products are subpower in
  <math|a<rsub|h><rsup|-1>>. The last-step endpoint estimate and chessboard
  seminorm estimate for the residual required here are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|h>-I|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1\<to\>1>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|h>|)>*Q<rsub|h>*L<rsub|h-1><rsup|-1/8>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4>,<eq-number><label|ch13:coarse-matrix>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<text|trial
    residual>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|k>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k,m,d>*E<rsub|h>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h>-\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>/8>.<eq-number><label|ch13:coarse-residual>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|k,m> are fixed before <math|h>; <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|k>> is the
  centered cell tiling at level <math|k> from
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch13:cells>, and <math|J<rsub|h>> maps trial basis
  coordinates to their selected level-<math|h> polynomial coordinates. Both
  estimates must concern the history restricted from the actual cutoff
  <math|N>, uniformly in <math|N\<geqslant\>h> and the physical period.

  The first inequality is the endpoint comparison used in
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:equation-comparison>. Its final <math|1/4>
  is an additional finite stage requirement: require
  <math|L<rsub|h-1><rsup|1/8>\<geqslant\>8*<around|(|1+C<rsub|h>|)>*Q<rsub|h>>.
  For the normalized small-field polynomial the local smooth cutoff is
  identically one on the ordinary chart, so its ordinary derivative constant
  is fixed before this block choice. Its global value is controlled by the
  square-root large-field bound, by a larger coupling threshold as in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:equation-calibration>. This does not replace
  <math|E<rsub|h>> by <math|Q<rsub|h>> in the second estimate.

  The second inequality requires more than a small coefficient linear in the
  sources. We prove its complete marked version next. The source weight is
  increased across successive scales; it is not increased by its entire final
  factor on an already formed coarse component.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|The coarse-trial residual on reflected
    tilings><label|ch13:coarse-residual-supplier>On the selected histories,
    after the finite coupling-threshold requirements specified below,
    <eqref|ch13:coarse-residual> holds with all incoming regular and
    large-field terms involving at least two insertions retained. Its
    constant is independent of the outer cutoff, the period, and the number
    of replica cells.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix the degree ceiling before the schedule. At level <math|i>, choose a
    positive number <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>> so that a term linear in the
    sources with complete normalized size at most <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>>
    fits every local attachment and adjacent comparison bound of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>. Include the finite signed
    symmetry orbit in these bounds. More explicitly, if <math|D<rsub|i>> is
    the complete adjacent comparison constant and <math|u<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>
    the terminal attachment bound, require

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>2*D<rsub|i>*\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|i>+162>*Z<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|val>>*\<zeta\><rsub|i>/u<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|Z<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|val>>> bounds the global section and
    its complementary cutoff part; this symbol denotes a bound, not a
    partition function. Also decrease <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>> to put the
    incidence sum for terms linear in the sources below the small constant in
    <eqref|ch11:higher-induction>. These choices concern normalized
    coefficients, before the physical factor <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>>.

    These positive upper bounds can be chosen with <math|<around|\||log
    \<zeta\><rsub|i>|\|>=O<rsub|j><around|(|1+log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)>> on every fixed stage. Indeed the section
    derivatives, inverse small-field thresholds, and adjacent comparison
    constants have finite powers of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, with
    stage-dependent constants. The terminal attachment bound may be taken as
    <math|u<rsub|i>=<tfrac|1|4><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>>; the
    original large-field suppression is reserved separately in the attachment
    estimate and need not be inverted to define <math|u<rsub|i>>. The factor
    <math|e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|i>+162>> is constant on each stage. Taking
    the minimum of this finite list of reciprocal bounds gives such a choice.
    At transitions between adjacent stages use the minimum of both lists. All
    requirements below therefore follow by increasing the coupling
    thresholds, since <math|B<rsub|i>> grows as a positive constant times
    <math|<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>> on a fixed
    stage.

    Choose <math|0\<less\>\<phi\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1> such that

    <\equation>
      \<phi\><rsub|i>*L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1>*max
      <around|(|1,C<rsub|i+1>|)>*\<zeta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i+1>,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>.<label|ch13:residual-height>
    </equation>

    For example take the minimum of one and the ratio in the first
    inequality. The logarithm of its inverse is bounded by a finite stage
    constant times <math|1+log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>, plus
    <math|<around|(|d+1|)>*log L<rsub|i>>, and hence is eventually below
    <math|B<rsub|i>/256>. Any finite local incidence bound
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> can simultaneously be included so that
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>*\<phi\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|B<rsub|i>/128>\<geqslant\>1>:
    its inverse has at most another fixed power of
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>. This verifies the comparison and
    attachment bounds as well as the displayed, weaker upper bound on
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>>. These are countably many finite degree
    requests to the single schedule of coupling thresholds.

    Put <math|G*<around|(|h,i|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h>-\<tau\><rsub|i>|)>/8>>
    and choose

    <\equation*>
      w=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*G*<around|(|h,m|)>|2*E<rsub|h>>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|res>>=w*<big|prod><rsub|j=m><rsup|i-1>\<phi\><rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    The residual coefficients from <eqref|ch11:descent-error>, with the fixed
    harmless column factors included in <math|E<rsub|h>>, satisfy
    <math|c<rsub|i>+<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|h>/G*<around|(|h,i|)>>.
    Since the ratio of <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>/G*<around|(|h,i|)>> at
    successive levels is <math|L<rsub|i><rsup|d+1/8>>,
    <eqref|ch13:residual-height> gives

    <\equation>
      \<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|res>>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*<around|{|c<rsub|i>+<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>|}>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|res>>*a<rsub|i><rsup|-d>*E<rsub|h>/G*<around|(|h,i|)>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|i>/2*<space|1em><around|(|m\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>h|)>.<label|ch13:residual-height-bound>
    </equation>

    In particular the negative normalized local insertion used in the
    subtraction has normalized cost at most <math|\<zeta\><rsub|h>> at
    <math|h>. Above <math|h>, its inverse coefficient bounds grow by at most
    <math|C<rsub|i+1>> per step; the same bound recurrence controls these
    factors and the physical factor <math|L<rsub|i><rsup|d>>. Thus its
    terminal source satisfies the joint attachment bound at <math|N>, and its
    complete push to <math|h> is available at the weights
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|res>>>.

    Here is the support calculation for its terms involving at least two
    insertions, including those formed above <math|h>. Open the centered
    cells at <math|m> with spacing at least <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>>. This
    spacing persists because <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3> and the adjacent
    support radii satisfy the schedule of <eqref|ch13:sharp-schedule>. Apply
    reweighting on the incoming fine support <math|X>. If it contains
    <math|r\<geqslant\>2> distinct marked ancestors, then
    <math|B<rsub|i+1>*r\<leqslant\>64*c<rsub|B<rsub|i+1>><around|(|X|)>> by
    <eqref|ch11:separated-cover>. Since <math|B<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|i+1>>
    and <math|\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|res>>/\<omega\><rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|res>>=\<phi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>>,
    one factor from the incoming exponential support weight gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|res>>/\<omega\><rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|res>>|)><rsup|r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|B<rsub|i+1>><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|B<rsub|i>*r/128-c<rsub|B<rsub|i+1>><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-c<rsub|B<rsub|i+1>><around|(|X|)>/2>.
    </equation*>

    To make this argument iterable, let <math|A<rsub|i>\<gtr\>4> be the full
    exponent maintained in the norm for terms involving at least two
    insertions at level <math|i>, and impose

    <\equation*>
      10\<cdot\>15<rsup|4>*<around|(|A<rsub|i>+9|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|i>*<around|(|A<rsub|i+1>-4|)>.
    </equation*>

    Reserve four units of the incoming <math|A<rsub|i+1>> norm for this
    weight increase, site conversion, and attachments. The support
    contraction of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> converts the
    remaining exponent <math|A<rsub|i+1>-4> into the full outgoing exponent
    <math|A<rsub|i>>, including its local reserves. Thus the estimate does
    not lose two exponent units at each successive step. Choosing the bounds
    for terms linear in the sources small enough makes
    <eqref|ch11:higher-induction> preserve the bound one in these full norms
    for both arrays of terms involving at least two insertions. This
    calculation applies to every cardinality and period. A large-field array
    is treated as one incoming factor with its complete original prescribed
    set of large-field sites, not as a product of its coefficients linear in
    the sources. The terminal joint construction reserves the original
    large-field decay for the complementary factors. Hence there is no
    uncontrolled reweighting by <math|w<rsup|r>> at the intermediate level
    <math|h>.

    At that level insert the positive local trial in every cell. If
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|h>> is the complete pushed negative
    source, the new polynomial is

    <\equation*>
      <with|math-font|cal*|A><rsub|h>*exp<rsub|<math-up|nil>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|v>z<rsub|v>*F<rsub|v>|)>.
    </equation*>

    In its local representation this adds <math|F<rsub|v>> only to the
    regular entry linear in the sources. All regular terms involving at least
    two insertions and all original large-field terms remain unchanged. The
    scalar part is exactly the coefficient of the identity in the global
    exponential. The nonconstant part multiplied by the smooth cutoff has
    zero complementary profile because its small-field restriction is
    included in its definition. Linearity makes the new term linear in the
    sources precisely the residual whose coefficients satisfy
    <eqref|ch13:residual-height-bound>; its adjacent regular and large-field
    comparisons have bounds at most one by the choice of
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>>. Descend this whole polynomial to <math|m>. Its
    regular terms linear in the sources have bound at most two, and the
    large-field terms linear in the sources have bound at most one, and its
    two incidence bounds for terms involving at least two insertions are at
    most one.

    For completeness, if <math|\<cal-I\>> is the cell census and
    <math|U<rsub|v>> the negative terminal normalized local insertion without
    its minus sign, its full coefficient at <math|h> is

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|A\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\>><math-ss|P><rsub|h,N><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<nin\>A><around|(|-U<rsub|v>|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<in\>A>F<rsub|v>.
    </equation*>

    Duality and the disjoint-cone identity <eqref|ch13:cone-factorization>
    identify its integral with the original partition function times the
    expectation of <math|<big|prod><rsub|v><around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F<rsub|v>-U<rsub|v>|)>>.
    No product of separately pushed densities for single insertions has
    replaced it. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch11:root-bound> now yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F-U|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>w<rsup|-1>*exp
      <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|m>+5|}>=<frac|2*e<rsup|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|m>+5>|\<zeta\><rsub|m>*a<rsub|m><rsup|d>>*E<rsub|h>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h>-\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>/8>.
    </equation*>

    The prefactor depends only on the fixed stopping data. This proves
    <eqref|ch13:coarse-residual>, once the cones have the room verified
    below. All identities and estimates use the original selected history.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Coarse bounded approximation><label|ch13:bounded-singleton>Use the
    selected histories with the stage requirements above. Fix a degree, a
    real reference coefficient <math|c>, a sufficiently fine centered tiling
    <math|k>, and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. There are real observables
    <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>> with a finite bound
    independent of <math|N,p,y>, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|d,c;y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|k><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>
    </equation*>

    on the ordered cutoff and volume tails. Both observables are interior to
    the same cell. The construction includes their signed and conjugated
    images with the same bound.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Fix <math|m\<geqslant\>k> with <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|m>>.
    At a finer level <math|h>, use the coarse trial

    <\equation*>
      V<rsub|h>*<around|(|v;U|)>=a<rsub|h><rsup|-d>*<around*|(|v<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|<text|small-field
      stencil>>*P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>*<around|[|S<rsub|1/t<rsub|h>>*v<rsup|\<circ\>>|]><around|(|U|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The scalar part is retained exactly. The full inspected stencil includes
    the links needed by the relaxed small-field condition. For fixed <math|h>
    this is a bounded measurable function, with bound uniform in the outer
    cutoff and period: its coefficient range and allowed couplings are
    compact, and its section is a fixed polynomial in compact group
    variables.

    Let <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|N,h><around|(|U,d*V|)>> be the forward
    probability kernel from fine links to level <math|h>, and set
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F<around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|V|)>*\<cal-K\><rsub|N,h><around|(|U,d*V|)>>.
    It preserves real values and contracts the supremum norm. Locality here
    must include every random variable used to choose a gauge-covariant block
    average or gauge frame. Conditional on the preceding link field, use
    independent primitive random variables for those choices and for each
    coarse-link sample. The full ancestor cone of a stencil contains its
    fine-link reads and all these random variables at every intermediate
    level. For disjoint cones, conditioning and integrating the independent
    variables gives

    <\equation>
      <big|prod><rsub|C>\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F<rsub|C>=\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|C>F<rsub|C>|)>.<label|ch13:cone-factorization>
    </equation>

    This does not assert that coarse links remain conditionally independent
    after a shared frame choice has been averaged out. The full cones,
    including that choice, must be disjoint. Here is an explicit uniform
    radius bound. Let <math|j<rsub|i>> be the smoothing order in the step
    <math|i+1\<to\>i>. In Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch04:blocking>, each
    endpoint frame reads a box of fine-lattice radius
    <math|<around|(|j<rsub|i>+3|)>*L<rsub|i>>; the other endpoint is at
    distance <math|L<rsub|i>>, and the connector paths stay in these boxes
    and the intervening strip. Thus every input link and every shared frame
    seed for an output link lies within physical distance
    <math|<around|(|j<rsub|i>+7|)>*a<rsub|i>> of its initial endpoint, in the
    maximum norm. This bound also includes eligibility conditions and one
    boundary-link margin. If the coarse inspected stencil has radius
    <math|r<rsub|d>> in level-<math|h> units, its full ancestor cone
    therefore has radius at most

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|d>*a<rsub|h>+<big|sum><rsub|i=h><rsup|N-1><around|(|j<rsub|i>+7|)>*a<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The growing smoothing orders require a schedule condition, not merely
    finiteness at each step. Impose <math|<around|(|j<rsub|i>+7|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i><rsup|-1/2>>
    at all sufficiently late levels. This is a permitted finite
    coupling-threshold requirement: if <math|\<jmath\><rsub|q>> is the
    largest smoothing order used at stage <math|q> or its permitted adjacent
    pairs, require <math|b<rsub|q>*log 3\<geqslant\>2*log
    <around|(|\<jmath\><rsub|q>+7|)>> among the coupling-threshold conditions
    of Section<nbsp><reference|ch10:schedule>. When
    <math|J*<around|(|i+1|)>=q\<gtr\>1>, that schedule has
    <math|i\<geqslant\>b<rsub|q>> and <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>3<rsup|-i>>,
    giving the desired inequality. The finitely many initial levels are
    irrelevant after increasing <math|h>. Since
    <math|a<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>/3>, the radius is bounded,
    independently of <math|N>, by

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|d>*a<rsub|h>+<frac|a<rsub|h><rsup|1/2>|1-3<rsup|-1/2>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|h> so that this is smaller than the fixed interior margin of
    each reflected level-<math|k> cell. The cones, including their shared
    seeds, then lie strictly inside disjoint cells; sufficiently large
    physical periods prevent wrapping identifications.

    The bound <eqref|ch13:coarse-matrix> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|h><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>.
    Thus the reference insertion with coefficient <math|c> is a linear
    combination of the trial columns, with sum of absolute coefficients
    bounded by <math|2<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>*exp
    <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>h>log C<rsub|i+1>|)>>. Replace these
    columns by <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*V<rsub|h><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>> and
    call the resulting observable <math|\<cal-B\><rprime|'>>. Its supremum is
    finite for fixed <math|h> and independent of <math|N,p,y>.

    Replicate the discrepancies before applying the kernel. At level <math|h>
    the coarse factors multiply the complete source density, including its
    terms involving at least two insertions.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch13:cone-factorization> identifies the resulting
    residual with the product of the pullback discrepancies.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch13:coarse-residual-supplier> and recombination
    give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rprime|'>-O<around|(|c|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k,m,d,c>*exp
      <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>h>log
      C<rsub|i+1>|)>*E<rsub|h>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h>-\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>/8>.
    </equation*>

    The prefactor is <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h>|)>>>, so choose
    <math|h> to make this at most <math|\<delta\>>, before choosing the
    cutoff. For real <math|c> take <math|\<cal-B\>=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-B\><rprime|'>>;
    conjugation invariance and the triangle inequality for the chessboard
    seminorm preserve the bound. Translations and the finite symmetry orbit
    use the same stencils and constants. Outside the ordered tails set the
    approximants to zero if a bound for every index is desired.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Real word differences><label|ch13:bounded-word>On these selected
    histories, the bounded-real approximation used for phase reflection in
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch15:phase-symmetry> holds on a cofinal set of
    generic grids. Its supremum bound is independent of copy number and copy
    position; approximation thresholds may depend on each fixed pair.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Choose the grid level after the degree bound and any prescribed lower
    level, then fix a generic real word <math|w> of length <math|n>. Include
    all its signed and conjugated labels in a finite family. Choose centered
    cells with margins fitting between its distinct coordinate projections,
    and let <math|C> bound the single-insertion seminorms there. Apply
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch13:bounded-singleton> with error
    <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>. The approximants have seminorm at most
    <math|C+\<delta\>>, regardless of their possibly much larger supremum
    norms.

    For a product of <math|q> such separated factors, telescoping and the
    chessboard inequality give

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|q>\<cal-B\><rsub|a>-<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|q>O<rsub|a>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>q*\<delta\>*<around|(|C+\<delta\>|)><rsup|q-1>.
    </equation*>

    Form the difference <math|g<rsub|j>> of the bounded products for a
    translated word and its linear time reflection, using the same
    single-insertion approximation at every translated copy. If their
    supremum bounds for single insertions are <math|M<rsub|i>>, then
    <math|<around|\||g<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<big|prod><rsub|i>M<rsub|i>=:R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
    independent of all copy positions and indices. For two sufficiently
    widely separated copies, expand their product of differences into four
    products of <math|2*n> factors. Choose <math|\<delta\>> with
    <math|8*n*\<delta\>*<around|(|C+1|)><rsup|2*n-1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    The preceding inequality gives the desired pair error. Only finitely many
    configurations are involved at each fixed pair, so ordered tails can be
    intersected. The empty word difference is zero. Increasing the initial
    lower-level request gives the required cofinal grids.
  </proof>

  <section|Scaling dimension and the physical
  quotient><label|ch18:graded-quotient-section>

  An estimate on a polynomial coefficient is useful for local product
  replacement only if it retains the degree of the field that coefficient
  represents. We derive the required quotient estimate from the triangular
  marked transfer. This step uses the corrected physical inclusions and the
  normalized-null statement of Section<nbsp><reference|ch13:quotient>; those
  statements were established using the exact microscopic equation space.

  Fix an output degree <math|t>. Let <math|x<rsub|l><around|(|v|)>> be the
  coefficient of a reference label <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|t>> after transport to
  level <math|l>, and use the sum norm <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>>. The
  leading polynomial map at one step has the form
  <math|M<rsub|l><math-ss|d><rsub|l>>, where

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=L<rsub|l><rsup|t-<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>*x<rsub|\<alpha\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> is the scaling dimension of the basis
  polynomial, not its factor count. The leading map fixes the constant and is
  triangular: <math|<around|(|M<rsub|l>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>=0> off
  the diagonal whenever <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<beta\>|\|>>.
  Both <math|M<rsub|l>> and its inverse have norm at most the prescribed
  stage constant <math|K<rsub|l>>. The full map differs from it by the marked
  remainder bound of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:one-graph>. Enlarge the
  prescribed column bounds <math|C<rsub|l>> and block floors so that

  <\equation>
    64*K<rsub|l><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>,C<rsub|l+1>,<space|2em>C<rsub|l+1>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|l>,<space|2em>2*K<rsub|l>*L<rsub|l><rsup|-3/8>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>1.<label|ch18:graded-room>
  </equation>

  These are finite-stage inequalities, with constants fixed before the block
  ratio, and the schedule gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|l\<less\>m>log
  C<rsub|l+1>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Graded quotient estimate><label|ch18:graded-estimate>For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t> there are constants
    <math|C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>> with <math|log
    C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>> such that the
    following holds. Suppose <math|v<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|t>> converges on
    an ordered cutoff and volume refinement to <math|v>, and
    <math|<around|\||x<rsub|m><around|(|v<rsub|N,p>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>>
    on that tail. Then

    <\equation>
      <math-up|dist><rsub|F<rsub|t>><around|(|<around|[|v|]>,\<iota\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>*a<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|t-i|)>>*\<epsilon\>,<label|ch18:graded-distance>
    </equation>

    where <math|F<rsub|-1>=0>. Constants are independent of the refinement
    and the convergent label family. The norm on <math|F<rsub|t>> is fixed
    once.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For <math|i\<gtr\>0> let <math|P> project onto scaling dimensions below
    <math|i>. Triangularity makes <math|ker P> invariant under
    <math|M<rsub|l>> and its inverse. The degree scaling expands low
    coordinates by at least <math|L<rsub|l><rsup|t-i+1>>, and high
    coordinates by at most <math|L<rsub|l><rsup|t-i>>. The one-step marked
    bound and <eqref|ch18:graded-room> imply

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*<around|\||P*x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em><around|\||x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>*<around|\||P*x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|1em>L<rsub|l><rsup|t-i+1>*<around|\||P*x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*<around|\||P*x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>,<eq-number><label|ch18:low-cone>>>|<row|<cell|P*x<rsub|l+1>=0>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em><around|\||x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*L<rsub|l><rsup|t-i><around|\||x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>.<eq-number><label|ch18:high-step>>>>>
    </align>

    To check the first implication, put <math|Y=<around|\||P<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    The premise and <math|C<rsub|l+1>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|l>> give
    <math|<around|\||<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*Y>
    and <math|<around|\||x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>Y>. Applying the
    inverse leading map to the low part and absorbing the marked error gives
    <math|Y\<leqslant\>2*K<rsub|l>*<around|\||P*x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>>. The
    full output is at most <math|3*K<rsub|l>*Y>; <eqref|ch18:graded-room>
    gives both assertions. When the low part is zero, the high-degree scaling
    and <math|K<rsub|l>+1\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>> give
    <eqref|ch18:high-step>.

    At the microscopic level take the low plane <math|P*V<rsub|t>> and
    transport it. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch18:low-cone> shows that its
    projection onto the low coordinates at every later level is injective,
    and hence onto that fixed-dimensional coordinate space. At level <math|m>
    subtract its vector with the same low coordinates as
    <math|x<rsub|m><around|(|v<rsub|N,p>|)>>. The remaining coefficient has
    zero low part and norm at most <math|<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*\<epsilon\>>.
    Repeat the subtraction after each step towards the fixed reference level,
    applying <eqref|ch18:high-step> between subtractions. The sum of all
    subtracted vectors still lies in the transported microscopic low plane.
    Calling its reference label <math|y<rsub|N,p>>, we obtain

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||v<rsub|N,p>-y<rsub|N,p>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>G<rsub|m>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|r<around|(|t|)>>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|t-i>*\<epsilon\>,<space|2em>G<rsub|m>=<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>m><around|(|1+C<rsub|l>|)>*C<rsub|l+1>.
    </equation*>

    Finite initial transfer and norm-equivalence constants are absorbed in
    <math|G<rsub|m>>. Its logarithm is <math|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>>
    by the prescribed column bounds.

    It remains to identify this microscopic low plane with physical lower
    degrees; it is not the naive inclusion of unnormalized polynomials. The
    labels <math|y<rsub|N,p>> are bounded by the displayed inequality. The
    expansion in <eqref|ch18:low-cone>, iterated from the microscopic level,
    bounds their microscopic low coefficients by
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|t-i+1>*e<rsup|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|N>|)>>>.
    Therefore their degree-<math|t> physical representatives can be written
    with the normalization <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-<around|(|i-1|)>>> and
    coefficient/derivative growth <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|N>|)>>>,
    on the same fixed stencil. Their global value bounds also have this
    growth, since the threshold inverses have subpower growth.

    The local-trial comparison of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>
    matches these representatives to degree-<math|<around|(|i-1|)>> reference
    labels with vanishing separated residuals. The matched coefficients need
    not be bounded. Apply the normalized-null representative argument of
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch13:quotient>: division by one plus the norm of
    their corrected projection makes the equation remainder uniformly
    controlled, and its separated responses vanish. Since <math|y<rsub|N,p>>
    has a bounded reference limit, that argument gives a physical
    degree-<math|<around|(|i-1|)>> field with exactly its limiting responses.
    The all-spectator definition and corrected inclusion thus put every such
    limit <math|<around|[|y|]>> in <math|\<iota\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>>.
    Passing to a convergent subsequence of the bounded <math|y<rsub|N,p>>
    proves <eqref|ch18:graded-distance>. Extending a tail-defined bounded
    family by zero outside its tail changes none of these limits.

    For <math|i=0> no low plane is removed. The reverse coefficient estimate
    directly bounds <math|<around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>> by
    <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|m>|)>>*<around|(|a<rsub|r<around|(|t|)>>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|t>*\<epsilon\>>.
    Passing to the quotient proves the same conclusion. All fixed reference
    spacings are absorbed in <math|C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>>.
  </proof>

  The power <math|t-i> in <eqref|ch18:graded-distance> is what recovers a
  coefficient of degree <math|i> from an auxiliary extraction at a much
  higher degree <math|t>. An ungraded bound on <math|<around|[|v|]>> would
  lose precisely that power. The proof also explains why identifying the
  lower field requires all spectator responses, even though its eventual
  coefficient is measured by a fixed finite family of probes.

  <section|Local replacement of a cluster on the
  lattice><label|ch18:lattice-products>

  We prove a local replacement estimate before using any phase decomposition.
  It will also apply to mixed correlations with a fixed exterior word.
  Observable source variables and local polynomial extraction are standard
  renormalization-group tools; see, for example,
  <cite|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2015>. The quantitative estimates below
  concern the actual block kernels, activity norms and quotient fields
  constructed here; that reference does not supply these particular
  estimates.

  For <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>, write <math|f\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|c*T>> if,
  for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, eventually
  <math|<around|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|c+\<varepsilon\>|)>*T>>
  on an ordered cutoff and volume tail, uniformly on the specified bounded
  label sets and geometries. A factor <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|T|)>>> is
  called subpower in this paragraph. Choose levels <math|l=l<around|(|T|)>>
  with <math|\<tau\><rsub|l>/T\<to\>\<theta\>>, where
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|l>=log <around|(|1/a<rsub|l>|)>>. The schedule has
  <math|log L<rsub|l>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|l>|)>>, so first-crossing
  levels supply any positive <math|\<theta\>>. For <math|\<theta\>=0> one may
  first cross <math|<sqrt|T>>.

  <subsection|Signed local transport>

  A local degree-<math|t> marked density consists of a column coefficient
  <math|x<rsub|l>> and a remainder with vanishing extracted coefficient of
  norm <math|r<rsub|l>>. Its dimensionless normalization obeys
  <math|H<rsub|l>=L<rsub|l><rsup|t>*\<cal-B\><rsub|l>*H<rsub|l+1>>; the
  physical source density has no additional factor. These conventions are
  those of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:one-graph>. Keep the full prescribed
  large-field set and its normalization by the square root of the large-field
  bound in this remainder norm.

  The local-trial argument of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent>
  applies on any sufficiently late interval
  <math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>, not only from the original
  microscopic level. In detail a remainder with vanishing extracted
  coefficient of size <math|q> has output coefficient and remainder at most
  <math|L<rsub|l><rsup|-2/5>*q>. The regular term uses its zero radial Taylor
  polynomial through <math|t>; the large-field term retains half the
  suppression of its original prescribed set of large-field sites.
  Subtracting its output column restores the vanishing extracted coefficient
  and costs at most <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|l>|)>*L<rsub|l><rsup|-2/5>*q>.
  The block floor makes this at most <math|L<rsub|l><rsup|-3/8>*q>. This
  recentering is an equality of marked densities, including the prescribed
  set of large-field sites terms, and uses linearity rather than division by
  an activity.

  Add a free column parameter at the fine end and choose it so that the
  transported residual coefficient vanishes at <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  Invertibility of the column map permits this on the same history. The cone
  calculation in Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:trial-descent> then gives

  <\equation>
    r<rsub|l>+<around|\||x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>.<label|ch18:signed-transport>
  </equation>

  Here <math|q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>> is the combined original coefficient and
  remainder cost, before the affine column subtraction. To recall the
  numerical point, with <math|Y=<around|\||<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|\|><rsub|1>>,
  <math|U=<around|\||x<rsub|l>|\|><rsub|1>> and
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|l>=L<rsub|l><rsup|-3/8>> the two inequalities are
  <math|Y\<leqslant\>K<rsub|l>*<around|[|U+\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*<around|(|Y+r<rsub|l+1>|)>|]>>
  and <math|r<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|l>*Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*r<rsub|l+1>>.
  The cone <math|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l>|)><rsup|2>*U\<leqslant\>r<rsub|l>>
  gives <math|Y\<leqslant\>4*K<rsub|l>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*r<rsub|l+1>> and
  <math|L<rsub|l><rsup|1/8>*r<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|l+1>>. It starts with
  <math|x<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=0>. At the fine end absorb the subtracted
  column cost to obtain the second inequality, and iterate for the first. The
  sign of the local difference has been retained throughout.

  <subsection|Grouping marks and retaining their supports>

  Use the square-zero source algebra of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>.
  The total number of copies may be arbitrary, but a reflected cell contains
  a fixed number <math|n> of insertion marks. A group is a set of these marks
  with a common representative. Grouping is bookkeeping for supports and
  source weights: original variables and every joint mode remain in the
  expansion. In particular partial modes of a group are not discarded, and
  grouping is not asserted to be an algebra homomorphism replacing their
  product by a new variable.

  Recall that <math|c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>> counts the radius-<math|B> balls
  in a smallest connected cover of <math|X>, and <math|\<sigma\>> is its
  exponential support weight. If a marked support <math|X> has to contain new
  representatives joined by at most <math|h> cover balls per mark, then

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|B>*<around|(|X\<cup\><around|{|y<rsub|i>:i\<in\>I|}>|)>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|B><around|(|X|)>+h<around|\||I|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Shrinking each affected weight by <math|\<eta\>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>>
  absorbs this support enlargement. The incidence norm has a second cost: a
  new incidence can be assigned to an old mark on its joining path. If at
  most <math|\<frak-N\>> representatives occupy a cover ball, there are at
  most <math|<around|(|1+\<frak-N\>|)>*n> assignments per representative.
  Take <math|\<eta\>\<leqslant\><around|[|<around|(|1+\<frak-N\>|)>*n|]><rsup|-1>>.
  Unmarked supports are unchanged. This is a termwise estimate on the
  complete marked series, hence is uniform in its number of replica cells.

  At each level retain a maximal <math|B<rsub|l><rsup|2>>-separated set of
  representatives, keeping the representative of the center, and join each
  discarded representative to a retained neighbor. A merger decreases the
  number of groups; there are at most <math|n-1> mergers within a cell. A
  deliberately generous joining bound is

  <\equation*>
    h<rsub|l>=16*<around|[|1+L<rsub|l>*<around|(|B<rsub|l>+B<rsub|l+1>+1|)><rsup|2>|]><rsup|4>.
  </equation*>

  Impose <math|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|l+1>+1|)>*h<rsub|l>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|l>|)>>
  by choosing the stage coupling thresholds. At fixed stage <math|h<rsub|l>>
  is a polynomial in <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l>> with fixed ratio,
  so this is a finite admissible request. The occupancy factors have the same
  property. The minimum <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1> of all
  weight-reduction factors for support enlargement on
  <math|m\<leqslant\>l\<less\>j> therefore has <math|-log
  \<alpha\><rsub|j>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|j>|)>>: split the maximum cost
  into a fixed initial segment and a tail bounded by
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<tau\><rsub|j>>, and then let
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>.

  Between mergers a single live group costs at most <math|6*L<rsub|l>> in its
  weight. Here the incoming regular contribution is carried <em|without>
  Taylor subtraction. Its full support conversion cost is
  <math|T<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|l>>, as in <eqref|ch11:cover-transfer>;
  it must not be assigned the contraction factor for a vanishing extracted
  coefficient. If its incoming mass is <math|\<frak-m\>>, the other local
  terms with vanishing extracted coefficient have total mass <math|p<rsub|l>>
  with <math|2*p<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<frak-m\>> after increasing the coupling
  threshold, and the large-field terms have normalized mass
  <math|f<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<frak-m\>>. These are the termwise local bounds
  used above, retaining the original events. Resolving real and imaginary
  parts costs at most two, so

  <\equation*>
    2*<around|(|p<rsub|l>+T<rsub|l>*\<frak-m\>|)>+f<rsub|l>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*L<rsub|l>+2|)>*\<frak-m\>\<leqslant\>6*L<rsub|l>*\<frak-m\>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|l><rsup|4>*\<frak-m\>.
  </equation*>

  The last inequality holds for <math|L<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>3>. It applies in
  the submultiplicative marked norm to a whole incoming joint factor; it does
  not multiply separate bounds for correlated marks. With <math|g> groups
  left, their available product weight is consequently at least

  <\equation>
    <around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|4*n>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8|)><rsup|n*<around|(|n-g|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>.<label|ch18:group-weight>
  </equation>

  Each step loses at most <math|L<rsub|l><rsup|-4*n>>, and a merger uses at
  most <math|n> of the weight-reduction factors for support enlargement. This
  proves the formula by induction, including all support and prescribed set
  of large-field sites data.

  <subsection|Extraction with a freely chosen residual weight>

  Put

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|l><rsup|<math-up|mark>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1><rsup|-2>,<space|1em>\<phi\><rsub|l>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>\<Xi\><rsub|l>=e<rsup|<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1>>>,<space|1em>y<rsub|t,l>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|t>>.
  </equation*>

  Choose the fixed integer <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> to absorb the finite
  polynomial and source-attachment losses at degree <math|t>. The stage
  coupling thresholds ensure <math|y<rsub|t,l>*C<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|\<phi\><rsub|l>*L<rsub|l><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,l>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|t,l+1>>.
  These are finite threshold requests after the ratio is fixed. All their
  logarithms, as well as <math|log \<Xi\><rsub|l>>, are
  <math|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|l>|)>>.

  For a fixed exterior reflection cell at level <math|k>, let

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|\<ast\>>=min <around|{|l\<geqslant\>k:B<rsub|l><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|l>|}>,<space|2em>N<rsub|k>=exp
    <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|<math-up|site>>+5|}>.
  </equation*>

  The sparse-stopping argument of <eqref|ch13:sharp-schedule> gives
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>/\<tau\><rsub|k>\<to\>1> and <math|log
  N<rsub|k>=o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>. Indeed if
  <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>=l+1\<gtr\>k>, minimality gives
  <math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|l>*B<rsub|l><rsup|2>>;
  the last condition of that equation bounds both the normalization cost and
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>-\<tau\><rsub|k>> by
  <math|o<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>. When
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|T|)>> the same proof gives <math|log
  N<rsub|k>=o<around|(|T|)>>.

  Suppose the cluster has become one group at level <math|m+1>, with
  available weight <math|\<omega\>>. Set

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|ext>>=4*\<Xi\><rsub|m>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|t>/\<omega\>,<space|2em>E<rsub|m>=8*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|ext>>.
  </equation*>

  Taylor extraction through radial degree <math|t> gives coefficient and
  complement each bounded by <math|q<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|ext>>>. To verify
  this, divide by the available weight, apply the integral Taylor formula in
  the local derivative norm, and sum the finite coefficient conversions and
  support moments with their positive exponent reserve. Their costs are fixed
  stage constants and powers of logarithmic radii and inverse thresholds,
  hence eventually at most <math|\<Xi\><rsub|m>>. The factor
  <math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|t>> converts the physical source to the normalized
  degree-<math|t> density. Large-field terms keep their original normalized
  majorants. Extraction of the coefficient of all marks in a group is a
  linear operation on the exact density; products of partial modes are
  retained before taking it. Each term is assigned the union of its supports
  and original prescribed set of large-field sites, so this operation never
  divides by the unmarked gas.

  Choose a residual weight <math|\<varpi\>\<gtr\>0> at <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  and thereafter <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l+1>=\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<varpi\><rsub|l>>.
  The two sufficient inequalities are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,m>/y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|mark>>*\<omega\>/<around|(|16*\<Xi\><rsub|m>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*\<varpi\>*a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|-t>*E<rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|1/8>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>/2.<eq-number><label|ch18:residual-weights>>>>>
  </align>

  The first absorbs the local polynomial coefficient bound and the second the
  bound for its signed transport. Indeed the recursion for these bounds gives
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,l>/y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
  Combining this with <eqref|ch18:signed-transport> and the physical factor
  <math|a<rsub|l><rsup|-t>>, the ratio of required to allowed weight at level
  <math|l> is at most its value at <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> times
  <math|<around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|7/8>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Thus no intermediate level has been left unchecked.

  The extracted matched field has coefficient norm at level <math|m> at most
  <math|2*E<rsub|m>>. The interior residual has chessboard seminorm at most
  <math|2*N<rsub|k>/\<varpi\>>. To see the latter on arbitrarily many copies,
  put one residual in every reflected cell before integrating. Taking the
  coefficient of the required source monomial in the marked sum costs
  <math|\<varpi\><rsup|-1>> per cell; the normalized integral bound
  <eqref|ch11:root-bound> costs <math|N<rsub|k>> per cell. Apply this
  separately to the original local difference and its compensating column,
  and use the chessboard triangle inequality. The rooted component estimates
  and bounds used in this calculation depend on marks per cell, not on the
  number of cells. All the terms involving at least two insertions generated
  during transport are included in these sums. The estimate is therefore
  stronger than a bound on one tested mean.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Lattice cluster replacement><label|ch18:lattice-replacement>Let a
    cluster contain <math|n> fields of total degree <math|W>. Choose
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>
    and levels <math|k,m,j> with <math|\<tau\><rsub|k>/T\<to\>\<theta\><rsub|->>,
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|m>/T\<to\>\<theta\>>, and
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|j>/T\<to\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>. The cluster lies inside
    the centered level-<math|<around|(|m+1|)>> block, its points lie on the
    level-<math|j> grid with separation at least
    <math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>*a<rsub|j>>, and its full dependence supports fit
    inside the level-<math|k> cell. Put <math|Z=W*\<theta\><rsub|+>+4*n*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>>.
    For each fixed auxiliary degree <math|t> there are output labels
    <math|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>\<in\>V<rsub|t>>, on the actual
    histories, satisfying

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||x<rsub|m><around|(|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>|)>|\|><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|Z-t*\<theta\>|)>*T>,>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|t,v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>;0|)>|\|><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsub|k>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|[|Z-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>|]>*T>.<eq-number><label|ch18:lattice-estimate>>>>>
    </align>

    The difference is interior. Constants and thresholds are uniform in
    cutoff and period on the ordered tails and in the number of copies of
    this fixed local cluster.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The weights of normalized local insertions in <eqref|ch13:weight-size>
    give <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>=e<rsup|-W*\<theta\><rsub|+>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>.
    Keep all incoming modes above <math|j>; separation permits their marked
    bounds but does not assert statistical independence. The grouping
    estimate gives <math|\<omega\>=e<rsup|-Z*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> at
    <math|m+1>, and therefore <math|E<rsub|m>=e<rsup|<around|(|Z-t*\<theta\>|)>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>.
    Set

    <\equation*>
      \<varpi\>=exp <around|{|<around|[|<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>-Z|]>*T|}>.
    </equation*>

    In the first inequality of <eqref|ch18:residual-weights>, the left
    exponent is <math|-Z-15*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>/16>,
    strictly smaller than the right exponent <math|-Z>. In the second, the
    full left exponent is <math|-<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>/16\<less\>0>
    while the right side has exponent zero. Both strict margins absorb every
    subpower factor. The extraction estimate and <math|log
    N<rsub|k>=o<around|(|T|)>> give the claim. All support shifts take place
    within the specified cell; reflections and conjugations retain the same
    bounds.
  </proof>

  <subsection|A mixed-correlation consequence without
  phases><label|ch18:mixed-replacement>

  Fix finite correlation reconstruction maps onto the filtered spaces
  <math|F<rsub|D>>. More explicitly, choose a basis in each successive
  quotient and extend it to <math|F<rsub|D>>, with dual coordinates realized
  by finite linear combinations of separated probe correlations. Arrange that
  a degree-<math|i> coordinate vanishes on <math|F<rsub|i-1>>, and that
  reconstruction fixes every field in <math|F<rsub|D>>. Keep each old
  coordinate functional as its original correlation formula on every larger
  field space. When lifting a new quotient basis, subtract its values under
  all the old coordinates, so those coordinates annihilate the new basis
  vectors. This is possible by finite-dimensional separation by all spectator
  correlations and successive subtraction of lower degrees. Consequently the
  degree-<math|D> reconstruction of a field in <math|F<rsub|t>>,
  <math|t\<gtr\>D>, is exactly its low-degree component in this fixed
  biorthogonal system. Denote the resulting reconstruction of a word by
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|D>*w>. It depends on the cluster, not on its later
  spectator. No rotation average or phase measure is used here.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Mixed local replacement><label|ch18:mixed-bound>Let
    <math|x<rsub|i>=o+r*z<rsub|i>>, with the <math|z<rsub|i>> in a fixed
    compact separated configuration set, and let <math|W> be the total
    cluster degree. For any fixed separated exterior word <math|v>, any
    <math|D> and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||b<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|w;o|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>-W-\<varepsilon\>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||S*<around|(|w*v|)>-S*<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|D>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>|)><around|(|o|)>*v|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|D+1-W-\<varepsilon\>>.<eq-number><label|ch18:mixed-product-rate>>>>>
    </align>

    Here <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>D>,
    are the fixed probe coordinates. The same field
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|D>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>> works for every
    exterior word. The estimates are uniform on fixed bounded label sets and
    separated geometries, and hold with any fixed finite positional
    derivatives, with their increased scaling dimensions.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First use generic level-<math|j> grid points. Put <math|T=log
    <around|(|1/r|)>> and choose <math|0\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>\<less\>1\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>.
    The cluster then fits the block and separation conditions for large
    <math|T>. For each fixed geometry, the output labels in
    <eqref|ch18:lattice-estimate> are bounded at fixed <math|m> and hence at
    the reference level by the inverse-column bound. Take a
    coefficient-convergent refinement and call its field <math|A<rsub|t>>.
    The chessboard inequality bounds its residual tested against any fixed
    exterior word by exponent <math|Z-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>>.
    The same refinement works for all spectators, since the coefficient limit
    was taken before testing.

    The graded quotient estimate <eqref|ch18:graded-distance> shows that a
    degree-<math|i> coordinate of <math|A<rsub|t>> has exponent
    <math|Z-i*\<theta\>>. Its coordinate functional vanishes on
    <math|F<rsub|i-1>>; this is where an ungraded coefficient estimate would
    lose the desired power. Choose <math|t> so large that
    <math|<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|D+1|)>*\<theta\>>.
    This also bounds every raw coordinate being evaluated, whose degree is at
    most <math|D>. We do not require the residual to bound all coordinates
    through the auxiliary degree <math|t>. Testing the residual against the
    fixed probes bounds the coordinate errors. Reconstruct <math|A<rsub|t>>
    and omit degrees <math|i\<gtr\>D>. Each omitted coordinate has exponent
    at most <math|Z-<around|(|D+1|)>*\<theta\>>, and its fixed exterior
    correlations are bounded. This gives that exponent for the raw
    replacement error, and <math|Z-i*\<theta\>> for each raw coordinate.

    For completeness the grid restriction can be removed without assuming
    differentiability of the selected coefficients of <math|A<rsub|t>>. Use
    the fixed correlation reconstruction instead. Its coefficients and its
    tested residual are smooth functions of the cluster positions. Their
    derivatives replace an input by a field of degree one higher. The sharp
    separated bounds, using fixed cells for exterior probes and shrinking
    cells only for cluster factors, give a crude exponent <math|W+1>: choose
    total excess one. Thus one can take <math|c<rsub|0>=1>. The grid exponent
    is <math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*W+c<rsub|1>>, where
    <math|c<rsub|1>=4*n*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>-<around|(|D+1|)>*\<theta\>>
    for the residual, and <math|c<rsub|1>=4*n*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>-i*\<theta\>>
    for a degree-<math|i> coordinate. These numbers are independent of
    <math|W> with <math|n,D,i> fixed. Multiplicative constants may depend on
    each of the finitely many higher derivative orders used in the following
    induction. For all sufficiently large total degrees the crude estimate
    implies the grid target, because <math|\<theta\><rsub|+>\<gtr\>1>.
    Descend in total degree. Moving to a generic grid configuration costs
    <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|j>|)>=e<rsup|-\<theta\><rsub|+>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>;
    the derivative bound at degree <math|W+1> gives precisely the target
    exponent at <math|W>. The connecting paths remain separated. Only
    finitely many higher derivatives are needed for each starting degree, so
    their constants and thresholds can be chosen together. Finally let
    <math|\<theta\>*\<uparrow\>*1> and <math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*\<downarrow\>*1>
    and absorb the remaining loss into the prescribed <math|\<varepsilon\>>.
    This proves the mixed estimate using neither phase symmetry nor an
    operator-product theorem.
  </proof>

  <assign|chapter-nr|13>

  <chapter|Reflection spaces, Ward identities and Euclidean
  symmetry><label|ch14:ward>

  We first use reflection positivity in each coordinate direction separately.
  Continuous rotations are not assumed. The resulting Hilbert spaces and
  bounded insertions provide the positivity estimates needed to construct
  rotational Ward identities. Relativistic reconstruction requires additional
  arguments and is not a consequence of the Hilbert-space construction alone.

  Throughout, <math|S> is the mixed correlation hierarchy of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum>. Its finite-dimensional field
  spaces are <math|F<rsub|d>>, with the compatible inclusions and derivative
  maps constructed there. Statements using this calculus retain the actual
  marked estimates required in that chapter. All correlations in the present
  chapter are evaluated at distinct points or against test functions
  supported away from collisions.

  <section|Reflection spaces for the coordinate axes><label|ch14:reflection>

  Fix an axis <math|\<mu\>>. A positive word
  <math|w=<around|(|<around|(|x<rsub|i>,A<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>>
  has <math|x<rsub|i><rsup|\<mu\>>\<gtr\>0> and distinct points; the empty
  word is allowed. Let <math|\<theta\><rsub|\<mu\>>*w> reflect the points
  across <math|x<rsup|\<mu\>>=0>, apply the signed coordinate reflection to
  the labels, conjugate them, and reverse their order. On formal finite
  linear combinations set

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|v,w|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<mu\>>=S*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|\<mu\>>*v*<space|0.17em>w|)>.
  </equation*>

  The form is positive semidefinite by reflection positivity. Its null space
  is orthogonal to every vector by Cauchy\USchwarz. The quotient and
  completion give <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, with
  dense word vectors <math|<around|[|w|]><rsub|\<mu\>>> and unit vacuum
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|[|\<varnothing\>|]><rsub|\<mu\>>>.
  This is the standard Osterwalder\USchrader quotient
  <cite-detail|OsterwalderSchrader1973|Section3>. Only the quotient
  construction is used here, not the reconstruction theorem.

  Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|t>> mean translation by <math|t*e<rsub|\<mu\>>>. On
  formal word sums put <math|T<rsub|t>*c=<around|[|\<tau\><rsub|t>*c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>>,
  <math|t\<geqslant\>0>. Translation invariance implies

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:heat-pairing><around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|s>*c,T<rsub|t>*d|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|\<langle\>|T<rsub|0>*c,T<rsub|s+t>*d|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<mu\>>.
  </equation>

  Every fixed orbit <math|T<rsub|t>*c> is bounded: the two reflected word
  halves have a fixed positive separation and the separated-correlation bound
  of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum> is uniform as they move apart.
  If <math|M=sup<rsub|t\<geqslant\>0><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|t>*c|\<\|\|\>>>,
  Cauchy\USchwarz in <eqref|ch14:heat-pairing> gives
  <math|M<rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|0>*c|\<\|\|\>>*M>. Thus
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|t>*c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|0>*c|\<\|\|\>>>,
  including when <math|c> is null. The operators descend and extend to
  self-adjoint contractions <math|E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>>. Their
  semigroup law holds on the dense word span and hence everywhere.
  Correlation continuity gives strong continuity. The spectral theorem yields

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:heat>E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>=e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|\<mu\>>>,<space|2em>H<rsub|\<mu\>>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em>lim<rsub|t\<to\>\<infty\>>
    E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>=P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|0><space|1em><text|strongly>,
  </equation>

  where <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|0>> projects onto <math|ker
  H<rsub|\<mu\>>>.

  Transverse translations, by <math|y> with <math|y<rsup|\<mu\>>=0>, preserve
  the reflected form and have inverse translations. They therefore give
  strongly continuous unitaries <math|U<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|y|)>>,
  commuting with <math|E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>>. Both leave
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>> fixed. At this stage we do not claim that
  <math|ker H<rsub|\<mu\>>> is one-dimensional, or that its vectors are fixed
  by transverse translations.

  The commuting spectral measures of <math|H<rsub|\<mu\>>> and the three
  transverse momenta give a joint projection-valued measure
  <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>> on <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>\<times\><RR><rsup|3>>:

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:coordinate-spectrum>E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>*U<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|y|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-t*E+i*p\<cdot\>y>*<space|0.17em>d*P<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>.
  </equation>

  This is a coordinate half-space spectrum. No cone bound
  <math|E\<geqslant\><around|\||p|\|>> has been proved or used. For
  <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>0>, the same spectral integral
  defines the holomorphic contraction <math|e<rsup|-z*H<rsub|\<mu\>>>>; its
  derivatives are bounded on every smaller right half-plane. This elementary
  heat analyticity will be used before any rotation action is constructed.

  <section|Bounded insertions in a slab><label|ch14:slabs>

  A word <math|w> lies in a slab of width <math|h> if
  <math|0\<less\>x<rsub|i><rsup|\<mu\>>\<less\>h> for each of its points. Its
  reflected slab word is <math|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>=\<tau\><rsub|h>*\<theta\><rsub|\<mu\>>*w>.
  Formally define <math|I<rsub|w>*v=w*\<tau\><rsub|h>*v>. Reflection and
  translation give

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:formal-adjoint><around|\<langle\>|<around|[|I<rsub|w>*c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>,<around|[|d|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>,<around|[|I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*d|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  This identity is on formal word sums; neither boundedness nor preservation
  of null vectors is assumed yet.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Slab norm bound><label|ch14:slab-bound> For labels of bounded
    degree and norm, a word of length <math|n\<geqslant\>1>, with pairwise
    separation and slab margins at least <math|r>,
    <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>, defines a bounded operator
    <math|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>> on <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>.
    There are constants depending only on the label bounds such that

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)><around|[|v|]><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|[|w*\<tau\><rsub|h>*v|]><rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|\<kappa\>*n>.
    </equation*>

    The constants are independent of the width and the exterior word.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch13:word-bound> first gives the estimate at
    a width <math|s\<leqslant\>h>, with a slightly smaller buffer. Put
    <math|T=I<rsub|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>>*I<rsub|w>> at that width and
    <math|D=C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|\<kappa\>*n>>. For every formal
    finite sum <math|c> its repeated-word estimate gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|T<rsup|j>*c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|c>*D<rsup|2*j>>.
    By <eqref|ch14:formal-adjoint>, <math|T> is symmetric for the possibly
    degenerate reflected form. Set <math|b<rsub|j>=D<rsup|-2*j><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|T<rsup|j>*c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>>
    and <math|M<rsub|c>=sup<rsub|j\<geqslant\>0> b<rsub|j>>. Then
    <math|b<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>*b<rsub|2*j>>,
    so <math|M<rsub|c>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>>.
    In particular

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|I<rsub|w>*c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>,<around|[|T*c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|c|]><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    The operator preserves null vectors and extends boundedly. Increase the
    width to <math|h> by right multiplication by
    <math|E<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|h-s|)>>.
  </proof>

  The empty word gives <math|B<rsub|h><around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|h|)>>.
  Dense word matrix elements also prove the exact identities

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>|<cell|=B<rsub|h><around|(|w<rsup|\<sharp\>>|)>,<eq-number><label|ch14:slab-laws>>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|h+k>*<around|(|w*\<tau\><rsub|h>*v|)>>|<cell|=B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>*B<rsub|k><around|(|v|)>,>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|h+t><around|(|w|)>>|<cell|=B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>*E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>,<space|2em>B<rsub|t+h>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|t>*w|)>=E<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|t|)>*B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>.>>>>
  </align>

  Transverse covariance reads <math|U<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|y|)>*B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>*U<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|y|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=B<rsub|h>*<around|(|w+y|)>>.
  These assertions concern bounded slab insertions, not unsmeared point
  fields acting on an arbitrary Hilbert vector.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Norm-smooth insertion positions><label|ch14:slab-smooth>On the
    open configuration space inside a fixed slab,
    <math|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>> is smooth in operator norm. A position
    derivative replaces the corresponding field label by its derivative in
    <math|F<rsub|d+1>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For a fixed configuration choose a smaller common buffer. The norm bound
    holds locally for the original tuple and each differentiated tuple. On
    dense word matrix elements the derivative identity is exactly the scalar
    derivative identity of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum>. If a weak
    derivative <math|D*A<around|(|x|)><around|[|v|]>=C<around|(|x|)><around|[|v|]>>
    on a dense domain is locally bounded in operator norm, the scalar
    mean-value inequality gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<around|(|y|)>-A<around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|<around|[|x,y|]>><around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\||y-x|\|>>.
    Apply this first to every differentiated tuple, proving continuity of its
    operator. The same inequality after subtracting
    <math|C<around|(|x|)>*<around|[|y-x|]>> proves norm differentiability of
    <math|A>. Iteration proves all orders. This argument explains why weak
    differentiability alone would have been insufficient.
  </proof>

  <section|Comparing the mixed fields with free
  fields><label|ch14:comparison>

  The comparison is a statement about the actual marked construction,
  stronger than equality of dimensions of field spaces. We record its
  quantitative form and then derive it from the finite-cutoff comparison. For
  fixed arity and degree bounds, let <math|M<rsub|k>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  <math|r<rsub|k>\<to\>0> and <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>. Suppose the
  same coefficient lifts in every slot give rescaled kernels
  <math|S<rsub|k>>, with, on a compact separated window,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:grid-input><around|\||S<rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|k><rsup|n>,<space|1em><around|\||\<nabla\>*S<rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*V<rsub|k><rsup|n>/\<delta\><rsub|k>,<space|1em><around|\||S<rsub|k>-S<rsup|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|k><space|1em><text|on
    its grid>.
  </equation>

  The gradient bound is required outside coordinate-equality strips of width
  <math|4*\<delta\><rsub|k>>; the grid has spacing <math|s<rsub|k>>, and
  <math|S<rsup|0>> is the free correlation. The necessary small errors are

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:grid-cost>A<rsub|k><rsup|n>*\<delta\><rsub|k>\<to\>0,<space|2em>V<rsub|k><rsup|n>*s<rsub|k>/\<delta\><rsub|k>\<to\>0,<space|2em>s<rsub|k>=o<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>.
  </equation>

  For example the source construction uses
  <math|r<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>>,
  <math|s<rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>>,
  <math|A<rsub|k>=C*M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D>>,
  <math|V<rsub|k>=C*M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D>>
  and <math|\<delta\><rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|c>>, with strictly negative
  exponents in <eqref|ch14:grid-cost>. The marked coefficient inverse,
  Gaussian comparison and displacement estimates must all concern these same
  lifts. The following construction verifies this compatibility.

  <subsection|Obtaining the three comparison bounds from one lattice
  family><label|ch14:lattice-comparison>

  Here are the scale choices behind <eqref|ch14:grid-input>. Fix the arity
  bound <math|n>, maximum degree <math|D> and a compact configuration window
  <math|K> with positive point separation. Enlarge the finite label list to
  include its signed coordinate images and conjugates. All constants and all
  three exponents below are chosen for this one finite request. Choose
  positive numbers <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|1>,\<alpha\><rsub|2>>
  and <math|c\<less\>0> with

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<alpha\><rsub|1>+6>|<cell|\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>,>|<cell|\<alpha\><rsub|2>+5>|<cell|\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>+c,>>|<row|<cell|n*D*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>+c>|<cell|\<less\>0,>|<cell|n*D*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c>|<cell|\<less\>0.<eq-number><label|ch14:comparison-exponents>>>>>
  </align>

  For example first choose <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>=7>, next
  <math|-c\<gtr\>7*n*D>, then <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<gtr\>5-c>,
  and finally increase all three <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>> together until the
  last inequality holds and all are positive. Increasing their common part
  does not alter the preceding inequalities.

  At an admissible comparison depth <math|k> put <math|H<rsub|k>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
  set

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*H<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>,<space|2em>s<rsub|k>=H<rsub|k><rsup|-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|k>=H<rsub|k><rsup|c>.
  </equation*>

  Thus <math|r<rsub|k>*s<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>>: grid rounding occurs on the
  actual construction lattice. Use one common polynomial extraction <math|T>
  from Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:common-extraction>. If <math|v> is a
  fixed real polynomial representative of a free quotient field, its lift
  <math|u<rsub|k,N,p>> solves

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|g/\<theta\>>*T*u<rsub|k,N,p>=W<rsub|H<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>>*v.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|S<rsub|t>> multiplies a monomial with <math|f> curvature factors
  by <math|t<rsup|f>>, whereas <math|W<rsub|\<rho\>>> multiplies one of
  scaling dimension <math|j> by <math|\<rho\><rsup|j>>;
  <math|g=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k><rsup|-1/2>> and <math|\<theta\>> is the local
  normalization used for <math|T>. These two dilations have different
  purposes. The inverse bound in <eqref|ch11:simultaneous-detector-lifts>
  gives bounded lifts at each fixed <math|k>, uniformly in the cutoffs and
  periods. Their real, unit and signed symmetry identities are exact. No
  separate choice is made for different spectator words.

  For clarity, the finite-arity use of the corrected-source estimate is as
  follows. For a subset <math|J> of the slots write <math|G<rsub|J>> for its
  free moment at the rescaled positions, and define

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1,<space|2em>R<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>I<rsub|i>-<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>J\<subseteq\>I>G<rsub|J>*R<rsub|I\<setminus\>J>.
  </equation*>

  These are precisely the coefficients of the actual squarefree source
  polynomial divided by the free moment polynomial. The latter has constant
  coefficient one and hence an exact inverse in the finite squarefree
  algebra. For the limiting Gaussian variables every nonempty coefficient has
  expectation zero. The extraction of coefficients of source monomials,
  covariance comparison and marker-weight restoration of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:n-mark-comparison> apply to this
  <em|entire> finite algebra, including its joint incoming terms. With
  <math|q=log H<rsub|k>/<around|(|16*n|)>> and marker amplitude
  <math|e<rsup|2*q>>, the output normalization is at most <math|e<rsup|q>>
  per reflected cell, and extraction gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|cell>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|q-2*q<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|)>.<label|ch14:subset-root>
  </equation>

  The stage coupling threshold is chosen after this fixed arity and its
  source constants. The estimate is on the original positive partition
  function and includes every reflected copy. Uniformity on <math|K> uses
  positive point separation in the covariance estimate; it does not require a
  positive lower bound on each coordinate difference. Unwinding the finite
  recursion and applying the chessboard inequality therefore gives the grid
  error <math|C<rsub|K>*H<rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>> at generic grid
  points. Distinct coordinates there differ by at least <math|a<rsub|k>> in
  physical units, so the assignments to lattice sites require no rounding.

  Apply the same coefficient map and the same finite comparison request also
  at exponents <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>> and <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>>. At its
  own normalization a single insertion has chessboard seminorm at most
  <math|1+<around|\||G<rsub|<around|{|i|}>>|\|>>. Changing its scaling
  dilation to <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>> costs at most
  <math|C*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|j>|)>*D>>.
  Set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|k>=C*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D>,<space|2em>V<rsub|k>=C*H<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D>.
  </equation*>

  The comparison cells have sides at most
  <math|a<rsub|k>*H<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|j>+5>>. Consequently the
  first cells are <math|o<around|(|r<rsub|k>|)>>, and the second are
  <math|o<around|(|r<rsub|k>*\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>>, by
  <eqref|ch14:comparison-exponents>. The chessboard inequality gives
  <math|<around|\||S<rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|k><rsup|n>> on <math|K>.
  The positional modulus of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:positional>, applied
  with physical margin <math|r<rsub|k>*\<delta\><rsub|k>>, gives
  <math|<around|\||\<nabla\>*S<rsub|k>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*n*V<rsub|k><rsup|n>/\<delta\><rsub|k>>
  outside the coordinate strips. Include the finitely many coordinate hybrids
  used in rounding in the excluded strips; their total volume is still
  <math|O<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>>. The physical windows lie inside the
  smallest period for large <math|k>, so the periodic and ordinary
  separations agree.

  Take an ordered limit point of the lifts at each fixed <math|k>. The
  uniform bounds pass to the already chosen continuum hierarchy. Their
  exponents give exactly <eqref|ch14:grid-cost>, since

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|k><rsup|n>*\<delta\><rsub|k>=C*H<rsub|k><rsup|n*D*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>+c>,<space|2em>V<rsub|k><rsup|n>*s<rsub|k>/\<delta\><rsub|k>=C*H<rsub|k><rsup|n*D*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c>.
  </equation*>

  Both tend to zero, and <math|s<rsub|k>/\<delta\><rsub|k>\<to\>0> follows
  from the second inequality. A diagonal sequence over the finite requests
  gives a single comparison sequence. The bounded coefficient limits do not
  alter the chosen correlation hierarchy or its order of limits.

  The coordinate-equality strips have volume
  <math|O<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>> on a fixed window. Their
  contribution to any test function of supremum norm at most one is
  <math|O<around|(|<around|(|A<rsub|k><rsup|n>+1|)>*\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>>.
  Outside them, rounding to the grid and <eqref|ch14:grid-input> cost
  <math|O<around|(|V<rsub|k><rsup|n>*s<rsub|k>/\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>> plus the
  free modulus of continuity. It follows that

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:uniform-tests>sup<rsub|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>1,<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>K><around*|\||<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|S<rsub|k><around|(|x|)>-S<rsup|0><around|(|x|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  This is local <math|L<rsup|1>> convergence; it includes coordinate ties
  which are not collisions. Testing against each fixed <math|f> separately
  would be weaker than the displayed assertion.

  The lifts can be made exactly compatible with inclusions. Write
  <math|i<rsub|d>:F<rsub|d><rsup|0>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1><rsup|0>> for the free
  inclusion, <math|j<rsub|d>> for the physical inclusion and <math|L<rsub|d>>
  for a real, hypercubic-equivariant left inverse of <math|i<rsub|d>>. From
  provisional maps <math|U<rsub|k,d>> define recursively

  <\equation*>
    <wide|U|~><rsub|k,d+1>=j<rsub|d>*<wide|U|~><rsub|k,d>*L<rsub|d>+U<rsub|k,d+1>*<around|(|I-i<rsub|d>*L<rsub|d>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Exact inclusion identities of correlations preserve
  <eqref|ch14:uniform-tests> in every slot. The unit is fixed at degree zero.
  Free finite detecting families from Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch03:maxwell>
  make these maps eventually injective; equality of dimensions in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum> makes them isomorphisms. For each
  finite request the tail is chosen once, before the test function. A single
  sequence for all requests is obtained only by explicitly diagonalizing
  arity, degree, compact windows and errors; the numerical comparison level
  must be chosen after those finite requests.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Derivative transport and smooth
    comparison><label|ch14:smooth-comparison>For the comparison maps just
    described,

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|k,d,a>\<assign\>r<rsub|k>*U<rsub|k,d+1><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*U<rsub|k,d>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|0>
    </equation*>

    in finite-dimensional operator norm. Rescaled correlations converge in
    <math|C<rsup|j>> on every compact separated window, for every fixed
    <math|j>, also for convergent moving labels.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Choose free tested correlations giving an injective map
    <math|L:F<rsub|d+1><rsup|0>\<to\><CC><rsup|q>>. Their corresponding maps
    <math|L<rsub|k>> converge to <math|L> by <eqref|ch14:uniform-tests>;
    hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|L<rsub|k>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<geqslant\>c<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>
    eventually. Integration by parts in the insertion position gives
    <math|L<rsub|k>*D<rsub|k,d,a>*v\<to\>L*\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|0>*v>. No
    bound on <math|D<rsub|k,d,a>> was assumed: subtracting the limiting
    derivative and using the lower bound supplies it and its convergence.
    Moving labels are handled by expansion in fixed finite bases. Every fixed
    derivative of the correlation error consequently tends to zero locally in
    <math|L<rsup|1>>. Multiply by a smooth cutoff supported away from
    collisions. Integration by parts gives a Fourier bound by
    <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|-M>> times the sum of its
    <math|L<rsup|1>> derivative norms through order <math|M>. Choose
    <math|M\<gtr\>4*n+j> and apply Fourier inversion.
  </proof>

  <section|The conserved stress line><label|ch14:stress-line>

  Let <math|\<cal-K\><rsup|0>> and <math|\<cal-K\>> be the real spaces of
  centered, real, symmetric, hypercubic-covariant degree-four tensor arrays
  in the free and mixed field spaces. Centered means that each component has
  zero expectation. Let <math|\<cal-V\><rsup|0>,\<cal-V\>> be the degree-five
  covariant vector spaces. The free divergence calculation of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch03:maxwell> has rank two. To check that its
  target is the full covariant vector space, note that degree five permits
  only colour-contracted <math|F*\<partial\>*F>. Sign parity and permutations
  leave the two expressions

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|c>:F<rsub|b*c>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*c>:,<space|2em>V<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|2|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|c\<less\>d,c,d\<ne\>b>:F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|c*d>:,
  </equation*>

  with colour summation understood. The apparent further contractions
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|b*d>*\<partial\><rsub|c>*F<rsub|c*d>> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|c>*F<rsub|b*d>>
  reduce to these: use the Maxwell equation in the first and the Bianchi
  identity in the second. The two independent rows of correlation pairings in
  <eqref|ch03:divergence-matrix> show that these vectors are independent and
  in the divergence image. Thus free divergence is onto, with kernel spanned
  by <math|T<rsup|0>>.

  Center the componentwise map <math|U<rsub|k,4>> after transport. It gives
  an isomorphism <math|C<rsub|k>:\<cal-K\><rsup|0>\<to\>\<cal-K\>>: its
  inverse transports back and centers there, and preservation of the unit
  makes the two scalar corrections cancel. Let <math|V<rsub|k>> be the
  corresponding vector isomorphism. The derivative comparison gives

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><math-up|div><space|0.17em>C<rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\><math-up|div><rsup|0>.
  </equation*>

  Compose with a fixed right inverse of free divergence. The result tends to
  the identity and is invertible for large <math|k>, so physical divergence
  is onto. Rank\Unullity therefore gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:conserved-line>dim ker <around|(|<math-up|div>\|<rsub|\<cal-K\>>|)>=1.
  </equation>

  This step uses the full low-degree rank computation, not just the existence
  of a free conserved tensor.

  Choose a nonzero <math|J> on this line. Normalize its pulled-back arrays
  <math|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*J> to have the norm of
  <math|T<rsup|0>> and take a convergent subsequence on that
  finite-dimensional sphere. Smooth comparison at arity one ensures that its
  limit is centered. Derivative transport ensures conservation. It must
  therefore be <math|T<rsup|0>> or <math|-T<rsup|0>>. Changing the sign of
  <math|J> once gives positive numbers <math|c<rsub|k>> with

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:balanced-stress>U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*<around|(|c<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J|)>\<longrightarrow\>T<rsup|0>.
  </equation>

  This fixes an orientation of the physical line. It does not yet fix its
  Ward normalization, and no positive lower bound for <math|c<rsub|k>> has
  been asserted.

  <section|Shrinking shells and local charge fields><label|ch14:shells>

  Choose <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)>>
  equal to one near an insertion <math|x<rsub|0>> and zero near all
  spectators. For a Euclidean Killing field <math|v<around|(|y|)>>, either
  constant or <math|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>> with
  <math|K<rsup|<math-ss|T>>=-K>, set

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:flux>\<cal-Q\><rsup|J><rsub|v>*<around|(|A;\<b-B\>|)>=-<big|int>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*v<rsub|\<nu\>><around|(|y|)>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>><around|(|y|)>,A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y.
  </equation>

  Repeated indices are summed. Conservation and symmetry of <math|J> show
  that the value is independent of the cutoff as long as it separates the
  same distinguished insertion from the spectators. Indeed the difference is
  the integral of a total divergence in a collision-free region, and
  <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*v<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>*J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>=0>.

  The actual two-mark estimate needed here has the following form. If
  <math|A\<in\>F<rsub|d>>, let <math|\<varkappa\>=0> for constant <math|v>
  and <math|\<varkappa\>=1> for linear <math|v>. Put
  <math|D=d+1-\<varkappa\>>. For a finite family of separated spectator
  correlation functionals <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>> on <math|F<rsub|D>> and
  coefficients <math|g<rsub|i>> annihilating that space, there must be
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>*\<downarrow\>*0> such that

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:annular-input>sup<rsub|1/4\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|3+\<varkappa\>>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>*<around|(|x<rsub|0>+\<rho\><rsub|m>*z|)>,A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation>

  Here the same correction coefficient at the distinguished insertion must
  work for every spectator in the finite family. The power
  <math|3+\<varkappa\>> is the four-dimensional volume minus one cutoff
  derivative plus the degree of the Killing field.

  The mixed local replacement estimate of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:mixed-bound> supplies this assertion
  directly, without using phase decomposition or rotational Ward identities.
  Apply it to the two-field cluster <math|<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>,A|)>>,
  of total degree <math|d+4>, and truncate at <math|D=d+1-\<varkappa\>>. Its
  replacement field <math|C<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<rho\>,z|)>\<in\>F<rsub|D>> is
  chosen by fixed probes before the exterior spectator. For every spectator
  in the finite family the residual is bounded uniformly on
  <math|1/4\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2> by
  <math|C*\<rho\><rsup|D+1-<around|(|d+4|)>-\<epsilon\>>>. The same
  coefficients therefore cancel under <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i>=0>.
  After multiplying by the shell factor,

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsup|3+\<varkappa\>><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>*<around|(|x<rsub|0>+\<rho\>*z|)>,A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsup|1-\<epsilon\>>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>1.
  </equation*>

  This proves <eqref|ch14:annular-input> for every sufficiently small radius,
  and hence along any sequence <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>*\<downarrow\>*0>. The
  full compact annulus is allowed because the local replacement proof already
  removed its lattice-grid restriction by derivative bounds. Only point
  collisions are excluded; coordinate ties cause no additional restriction.
  This use of local replacement precedes its phase-uniform operator-product
  formulation.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Representation of the local flux><label|ch14:charges>There are
    unique linear maps <math|p<rsub|b>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>> and
    <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> such that the
    constant and linear fluxes <eqref|ch14:flux> equal the correlations with
    <math|p<rsub|b>*A> and <math|Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*A> at
    <math|x<rsub|0>>, respectively.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Every finite linear dependence among the spectator functionals on
    <math|F<rsub|D>> annihilates the flux: shrink the shell, rescale its
    integration variable and use <eqref|ch14:annular-input>. Choose a finite
    separating subfamily of spectator functionals. Its injective map from
    <math|F<rsub|D>> has finite-dimensional range; the preceding annihilator
    property says that the corresponding flux vector lies in that range. It
    therefore specifies a unique field, and adjoining any other spectator
    correlation functional proves the identity for it as well. Continuity
    extends the identity from generic grid configurations to every separated
    configuration. Linearity follows from uniqueness.
  </proof>

  Differentiating the shell identity with the distinguished insertion, using
  conservation to move the cutoff and symmetry to remove the Killing
  derivative, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:charge-commute>Y<rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*A=\<partial\><rsub|b>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*A+<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*p<rsub|c>*A,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|b>*p<rsub|c>*A=p<rsub|c>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*A.
  </equation>

  Equivalently one can differentiate after moving the whole local shell with
  <math|x<rsub|0>>; the derivative of <math|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>
  supplies the displayed constant flux term. Faithfulness of separated
  spectator correlation functionals turns these response identities into
  field identities. The same argument shows compatibility with inclusions,
  reality and signed coordinate symmetries; both charges annihilate the unit.

  <section|Coordinate spectral measures and the angular
  boundary><label|ch14:spectral>

  We recall that <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>> in <eqref|ch14:coordinate-spectrum> is
  the joint measure of nonnegative heat energy and transverse momentum. For a
  point field, translate its insertion by <math|u*e<rsub|\<mu\>>>,
  <math|u\<gtr\>0>, into the positive half-space and define the undamped
  measure

  <\equation*>
    d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|A>*<around|(|E,p|)>=e<rsup|2*u*E>*d*<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|A*<around|(|u*e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|]>,P<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>*<around|[|A*<around|(|u*e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|]>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  The semigroup law makes this independent of <math|u>. It is finite on
  <math|E\<leqslant\>M>; it need not have finite total mass. Word measures
  are defined after translating the entire word beyond its extreme point.
  Polarization defines mixed measures. For integrable nonnegative
  <math|g<rsub|1>,g<rsub|2>> and <math|<around|\||f|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|1>*g<rsub|2>>,
  spectral Cauchy\USchwarz gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:spectral-CS><around*|\||<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|A*B>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><big|int>g<rsub|1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|A>*<big|int>g<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|B>.
  </equation>

  Positive damping and the separated derivative bounds imply all polynomial
  transverse moments. One direct justification takes centered differences of
  the reflected two-point function: their spectral multiplier is
  <math|<around|(|2*sin <around|(|t*p<rsub|j>/2|)>/t|)><rsup|2*r>>. Fatou's
  lemma and the integral remainder for finite differences give the moment
  bound as <math|t\<to\>0>. Further heat damping gives energy moments. For
  point fields, integration over small damping times against a sufficiently
  high power of time also gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:polynomial-moments><big|int><around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|A>\<less\>\<infty\>
  </equation>

  for some <math|D> depending on <math|A,r>.

  <subsection|What opposite reflection signs control>

  If a field or word combination <math|A> has reflection signs
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=\<pm\>1> in axes in a set <math|I>, its
  two-point kernel has, in time direction <math|i>, the spectral sign
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>. For an even frequency profile <math|f> use

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\>*f<around|(|E|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-E<around|\||t|\|>><around*|(|<big|int>e<rsup|i*p*t>*f<around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p|)>*d*t=2*E*<big|int><frac|f<around|(|p|)>|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*p.
  </equation*>

  At <math|E=0> the limiting value is <math|2*\<pi\>*f<around|(|0|)>>. To
  compare representations in different axes without an arbitrary collision
  extension, subtract the low moments of the profiles. Choose even
  <math|\<sigma\>\<geqslant\>0> of integral one supported in
  <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>2>, and the Lagrange
  polynomials <math|L<rsub|j>> for the nodes <math|<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>m>. Define the annular correction by

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><around|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<rho\>>*f|)><around|(|q|)>*g<around|(|q|)>*<space|0.17em>d*q=<big|int>\<sigma\><around|(|v|)>*f<around|(|p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>L<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|p/<around|(|\<rho\>*v|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>*g*<around|(|\<rho\>*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p*<space|0.17em>d*v.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|f-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<rho\>>*f> has its first <math|2*m> moments
  zero. Interpolation of <math|<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
  gives its exact response

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:positive-residual>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<rho\>>*f<around|(|E|)>=2*E*<big|int><frac|f<around|(|p|)>|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<big|int>\<sigma\><around|(|v|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|<around|(|\<rho\>*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>-p<rsup|2>|<around|(|\<rho\>*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*v*<space|0.17em>d*p.
  </equation>

  For <math|f\<geqslant\>0> supported in <math|<around|[|-\<rho\>,\<rho\>|]>>
  this is nonnegative. If <math|f<rsub|w><around|(|p|)>=w<rsup|-1>*f*<around|(|p/w|)>>
  with <math|f=1> near zero and <math|0\<less\>w\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>\<rho\>>,
  it is bounded below by <math|c<rsub|m>/<around|(|E+w|)>> on
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>. For large <math|E> its decay can be
  made arbitrarily high by increasing <math|m>. The upper bound, unlike the
  low-energy lower bound, holds also for growing <math|w>: it requires only
  <math|\<rho\>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f<rsub|w>\<subset\><around|[|-\<rho\>,\<rho\>|]>>.
  For <math|E\<leqslant\>1> use <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|w>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C/w>;
  for <math|E\<geqslant\>1> the product in <eqref|ch14:positive-residual> is
  bounded by <math|C<rsub|m>*min <around|(|1,<around|(|\<rho\>/E|)><rsup|2*m>|)>>.
  Thus <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<rho\>>*f<rsub|w><around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|w<rsup|-1>+\<rho\><rsup|D>|)>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D>>
  for any fixed <math|D\<leqslant\>2*m>. Together with
  <eqref|ch14:polynomial-moments> this gives, for fixed <math|r>,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<rho\>>*f<rsub|w><around|(|E|)><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|w<rsup|-1>+\<rho\><rsup|D>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Choose <math|D> from the damped moment first, and then <math|m>
  sufficiently large; <math|D> does not grow with the later subtraction
  order.

  We now give the precise comparison. For a word combination <math|A>, assume
  its offsets have zero coordinates in <math|I> and that reflection in axis
  <math|i> acts by <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>> for <math|i\<in\>I>. These
  geometric restrictions permit arbitrarily small positive placements in any
  of the chosen time axes, so the integrated moment bound
  <eqref|ch14:polynomial-moments> holds in each. They will be relaxed for the
  line axis below. For even nonnegative fixed profiles
  <math|f<rsub|j>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR>|)>> write
  <math|f<rsub|j,v><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|j>*<around|(|p/v|)>>; in
  particular the normalization preserves its integral, while its supremum
  costs <math|v<rsup|-1>>. For a product of profiles
  <math|g=<around|(|g<rsub|k>|)><rsub|k\<in\>I>> define

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=<big|int>\<cal-L\>*g<rsub|i><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<in\>I\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>g<rsub|k><around|(|p<rsub|k>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>*<around|(|E,p|)>.
  </equation*>

  The same even product test function of the unreflected pair kernel has
  spectral values <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>>
  in every axis <math|i>. First require sufficiently many vanishing moments
  in two axes, so the test function vanishes to the required order at the
  collision. This makes the comparison an equality of convergent integrals of
  the original kernel. If <math|I> omits axes, insert in each omitted
  frequency coordinate a profile <math|h*<around|(|p/T|)>> with <math|h=1>
  near zero, and let <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>; the damped moment bounds
  justify this limit. Thus opposite signs give
  <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=-<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g|)>>.

  Let <math|a<rsub|i>=\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<rho\><rsub|i>>*g<rsub|i>> and
  <math|a<rsub|j>=\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<rho\><rsub|j>>*g<rsub|j>>, where each
  radius contains the support of its profile and <math|m> exceeds the
  necessary collision orders. Apply the signed equality to the product with
  residual profiles in both <math|i> and <math|j>. Expanding it gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>;g<rsub|\<ne\>i>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>;g<rsub|j>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|j>|)>+<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>;g<rsub|i>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|i>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch14:sign-transfer>>>>>
  </align>

  Here the semicolon leaves every unmentioned profile unchanged. The omitted
  term is <math|-<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>;g<rsub|\<ne\>j>|)>\<leqslant\>0>,
  because its temporal residual response and all its transverse profiles are
  nonnegative. The corrections <math|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>> themselves can have
  signs. They occur only as transverse profiles on the right, so replacing
  them by nonnegative majorants gives an upper bound. This explains precisely
  where positivity is used.

  We claim that when both signs occur in <math|I>, for
  <math|\<lambda\>\<in\>e<rsup|<NN>>>, <math|q\<less\>0>,
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0>, and every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:mixed-sign><big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>I\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>f<rsub|j,\<lambda\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|j>>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|E+\<lambda\><rsup|q>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|A>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>.
  </equation>

  The following induction proves the claim while keeping the order of
  parameter choices explicit. Choose a walk
  <math|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|N>> through <math|I> with opposite signs
  at consecutive entries, starting at <math|i<rsub|0>=i>, with every axis
  visited before its final entry. Put <math|V<rsub|s>=<around|{|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|s-1>|}>>.
  Fix <math|K\<geqslant\>0> bounding the magnitudes of all negative width
  exponents. Induct backwards on <math|s> to find <math|a<rsub|s>\<gtr\>0>
  such that

  <\equation>
    <big|int>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>>*f<rsub|i<rsub|s>,\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|s>>>><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i<rsub|s>>f<rsub|j,\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i<rsub|s>><rsup|A>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>.<label|ch14:sign-induction>
  </equation>

  Here <math|0\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|s>> on
  <math|V<rsub|s>>, <math|-K\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|s>>
  elsewhere, <math|0\<leqslant\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|s>> and
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|s>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>. The fixed
  <math|R<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1> contains the support at the current radius;
  <math|m> may be any sufficiently large fixed subtraction order. Products
  here and below range over <math|I>.

  At <math|s=N> all widths are at least one, hence the transverse suprema are
  bounded. The residual moment estimate with moment order zero bounds the
  left side by <math|C*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|N>>>+\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>*D>|)>>.
  Choose <math|a<rsub|N>*D\<less\>\<delta\>>. For the step from <math|s+1> to
  <math|s> write <math|i=i<rsub|s>>, <math|j=i<rsub|s+1>> and
  <math|p<rsub|\<ast\>>=K*<around|\||I\<setminus\><around|{|i,j|}>|\|>>.
  Choose, in this order,

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\>=a<rsub|s+1>,<space|2em>2*r*\<alpha\>\<gtr\>p<rsub|\<ast\>>+max
    <around|(|K,\<delta\>|)>+1,<space|2em>D=D*<around|(|A,r|)>,<space|2em>0\<less\>a<rsub|s>\<less\>min
    <around|{|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>/<around|(|D+1|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Only after these choices enlarge <math|m> to handle the moment exponent
  <math|D> and the collision orders. Correct <math|g<rsub|i>> at radius
  <math|R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>> and <math|g<rsub|j>> at a fixed
  multiple of <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<alpha\>>>. The correction in <math|i>
  has a fixed nonnegative annular majorant of width
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>>, since its coefficients involve only
  factors <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2*r<rprime|'>*<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|i>-\<eta\>|)>>\<leqslant\>1>.
  The second term of <eqref|ch14:sign-transfer> is therefore covered by the
  next induction step, with the previously current axis now having
  nonnegative width exponent. The correction in <math|j> is bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|r>*\<lambda\><rsup|-2*r*\<alpha\>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>>
  on its annulus; its coefficients are bounded because
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>\<less\>\<alpha\>>. The remaining narrow suprema
  cost at most <math|\<lambda\><rsup|p<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. The first term is
  consequently at most

  <\equation*>
    C*\<lambda\><rsup|p<rsub|\<ast\>>-2*r*\<alpha\>+max
    <around|(|-\<omega\><rsub|i>,\<eta\>*D|)>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|p<rsub|\<ast\>>-2*r*\<alpha\>+max
    <around|(|K,a<rsub|s>*D|)>>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  This proves <eqref|ch14:sign-induction>. Finally take <math|s=0>,
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>=q>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=\<beta\><rsub|j>>, and
  <math|\<eta\>=0>. The lower bound <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|m,R<rsub|0>>*f<rsub|i,\<lambda\><rsup|q>><around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|m>/<around|(|E+\<lambda\><rsup|q>|)>>
  on <math|E\<leqslant\>1>, with a bump <math|f<rsub|i>=1> near zero, gives
  <eqref|ch14:mixed-sign>. No continuous rotation was used.

  <subsection|The separate diagonal-stress estimate>

  For <math|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>> with <math|\<mu\>\<ne\>b>, the two odd tensor
  indices give opposite signs and <eqref|ch14:mixed-sign> applies. A diagonal
  stress component has only positive signs, so it requires conservation. We
  describe the contact subtraction needed just for this argument; it is not a
  positive extension of the correlation hierarchy.

  Let <math|K<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>,J<rsub|c*d><around|(|0|)>|)>>
  for <math|x\<ne\>0>, and choose an integer <math|D> above the collision
  orders of these kernels and their first derivatives. For one-dimensional
  Schwartz test functions let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|D>> retain Taylor orders less
  than <math|D>, multiplied by a fixed even cutoff. On product test functions
  define

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|h|)>=<big|int>K<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|x|)>*<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>h<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|i>\<Pi\><rsub|D>*h<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>*d*x.
  </equation*>

  Each term of the telescoping product difference has a factor
  <math|O<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|D>|)>>, ensuring
  integrability. Using the same cutoff in every axis preserves signed tensor
  symmetries and exchange of the index pairs. Conservation makes its
  divergence a distribution supported at zero with finitely many derivatives.
  Inversion removes its even orders. Here is an explicit subtraction of the
  possible linear orders. Put <math|\<chi\><rsub|0><around|(|x|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j>\<chi\><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>>,
  using the even Taylor cutoff above, and set

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|a*b,c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|i>R<rsub|i*b,c*d>*<around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|0>|)>|)>,<space|2em>R<rprime|'><rsub|a*b,c*d>=R<rsub|a*b,c*d>-q<rsub|a*b,c*d>*\<delta\><rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|i*b,c*d>>
  evaluated on <math|x<rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|0>> equals
  <math|-q<rsub|a*b,c*d>>, whereas the divergence of
  <math|q<rsub|i*b,c*d>*\<delta\><rsub|0>> has the same value. Thus their
  difference has no linear derivative of <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>>. To check
  the tensor symmetries, reflection parity makes <math|q<rsub|a*b,c*d>>
  vanish unless each coordinate index occurs an even number of times. The
  remaining index patterns are <math|a*a,a*a>, <math|a*a,b*b> and
  <math|a*b,a*b> (including their within-pair reversals); coordinate
  permutations, within-pair symmetry of <math|R>, and exchange of its two
  pairs give the same symmetries for <math|q>. The subtraction is therefore
  compatible with all the tensor identities. We henceforth rename
  <math|R<rprime|'>> as <math|R>. Its Fourier divergence polynomial starts in
  degree three.

  Write <math|<wide|R|^>> for this corrected functional and
  <math|F<rsub|b,c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>*<wide|R|^><rsub|i*b,c*d>>.
  For a chosen time axis <math|\<mu\>>, set

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|0>>|<cell|=<wide|R|^><rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>,\<mu\>*\<mu\>>,>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|1>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j,k\<ne\>\<mu\>>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*<wide|R|^><rsub|k*\<mu\>,j*\<mu\>>,>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|2>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j,k,r,s\<ne\>\<mu\>>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*p<rsub|r>*p<rsub|s>*<wide|R|^><rsub|s*k,r*j>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Conservation on the two index pairs gives
  <math|Q<rsub|1>=p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>*Q<rsub|0>+D<rsub|1>> and
  <math|Q<rsub|2>=p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>*Q<rsub|1>+D<rsub|2>>, where the
  polynomials <math|D<rsub|1>,D<rsub|2>> start in degrees four and six.
  Sufficiently many temporal moment subtractions annihilate them exactly. On
  a fixed temporal annulus the transverse responses of
  <math|Q<rsub|0>,Q<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|2>> are bounded by
  <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>> times
  respectively <math|1,W<rsup|2>,W<rsup|4>>, if the transverse widths are at
  most <math|W>. For a diagonal entry, divide its annular temporal profile by
  <math|p<rsub|\<mu\>>> and put that derivative on the label; the resulting
  vector has mixed signs. Odd profiles are corrected by
  <math|p<rsup|-1>*\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<rho\>>*<around|(|p*f|)>>, and their
  residual Laplace absolute value is bounded by
  <math|C*<around|(|E+w|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>>. Thus
  <eqref|ch14:mixed-sign> and spectral Cauchy\USchwarz apply. Removing one
  transverse moment restriction at a time terminates after three axes. The
  annular coefficients remain bounded because all shrinking width exponents
  are nonpositive.

  For a fixed even annular <math|\<sigma\>>, evaluate <math|Q<rsub|r>>
  against <math|g<rsub|v,r><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*<around|(|p/v|)><rsup|-2*r>*\<sigma\>*<around|(|p/v|)>>,
  so <math|p<rsup|2>*g<rsub|v,r+1>=v<rsup|2>*g<rsub|v,r>>. Compare the widths
  <math|W> and <math|2*W> and subtract their fixed annular corrections. Write
  <math|c<rsub|r><around|(|t|)>=W<rsup|-2*r><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>Q<rsub|r><around|(|g<rsub|t*W,r>|)>>,
  with the transverse profiles fixed, and let <math|d<rsub|r>> be the same
  normalized real response to <math|<around|(|I-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,8>|)>*<around|(|g<rsub|W,r>-g<rsub|2*W,r>|)>>.
  Conservation gives <math|c<rsub|r+1><around|(|t|)>=t<rsup|2>*c<rsub|r><around|(|t|)>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
  for <math|t=1,2>. The correction has zero constant moment and higher even
  moments <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|2>|)>>, so
  <math|d<rsub|r>=c<rsub|r><around|(|1|)>-c<rsub|r><around|(|2|)>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>.
  Eliminating <math|c<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>,c<rsub|0><around|(|2|)>> from
  these three relations gives

  <\equation*>
    4*d<rsub|0>=5*d<rsub|1>-d<rsub|2>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The corrected dyadic profile is signed, so its positivity needs one further
  observation. For fixed correction nodes
  <math|A<rsub|j>=<around|(|8*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>>, the function

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|E><around|(|z|)>=<frac|1|z+E<rsup|2>>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|A<rsub|j>-z|A<rsub|j>+E<rsup|2>>
  </equation*>

  is positive and decreasing for <math|0\<leqslant\>z\<leqslant\>16>, since
  <math|<around|\||v|\|>\<geqslant\>1> and <math|A<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>256>.
  The dyadic response is the integral of <math|2*E*<around|[|R<rsub|E>*<around|(|W<rsup|2>*p<rsup|2>|)>-R<rsub|E>*<around|(|4*W<rsup|2>*p<rsup|2>|)>|]>>
  against the nonnegative annular profile
  <math|p<rsup|-2*r>*\<sigma\><around|(|p|)>> and the correction profile. For
  <math|W\<leqslant\>1> it is therefore nonnegative. On <math|E\<leqslant\>1>
  the product factors have a fixed positive lower bound; retaining the
  difference of the two resolvents gives the lower bound
  <math|c*E*W<rsup|2>/<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>> for <math|r=0>. Conservation
  with the sufficiently many zero moments now gives
  <math|d<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>, <math|d<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>0>,
  <math|d<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0>: each temporal multiplier
  <math|p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>> becomes <math|-E<rsup|2>> in the Laplace
  response, with the contact terms killed exactly. Therefore, for a
  nonnegative transverse product profile <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:diagonal-mass><big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|E*W<rsup|4>|<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|4>|)>.
  </equation>

  The integrand vanishes at <math|E=0>. The zero-energy part must be kept
  separate in the subsequent Cauchy\USchwarz estimate.

  <subsection|Vanishing angular flux at infinity><label|ch14:collinear>

  For <math|K=E<rsub|b*a>-E<rsub|a*b>> and spectator positions on a
  coordinate line <math|<RR>e<rsub|l>>, choose anisotropic exponents
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|a>=\<omega\><rsub|b>=-100>,
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|l>=-150> when <math|l\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>, and
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=-300> on remaining axes. Let
  <math|w<rsub|j>=\<lambda\><rsup|1+\<omega\><rsub|j>>>,
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>\<mu\>>w<rsub|j><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/w<rsub|j>|)>>,
  with fixed even nonnegative <math|\<psi\>=1> near zero, and put

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|A;q|)>=<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>|E+\<lambda\><rsup|q>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|A>.
  </equation*>

  Project the spectator word to odd parity in <math|a,b> and even parity in
  the other axes, calling the result <math|B>. For a reflected line axis this
  is a finite combination of words on the same line. The mixed-sign estimate
  gives

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|B;-3000|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>>|)>,<space|1em>I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>;\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<ne\>b|)>,<space|1em>\<gamma\><rsub|l>=1,<space|1em>\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>\<leqslant\>150.
  </equation*>

  For the line time axis this requires a genuine temporal gap. Choose a
  nonnegative real even smooth profile <math|\<sigma\><rsub|0>>, let
  <math|c=\<sigma\><rsub|0>\<ast\>\<sigma\><rsub|0>>, and normalize
  <math|\<Phi\>=<wide|c|^>=<around|(|<wide|\<sigma\>|^><rsub|0>|)><rsup|2>>
  so <math|\<Phi\><around|(|0|)>=1>. Thus <math|\<Phi\>\<geqslant\>0>. If
  translating the word by <math|u> puts it in the positive half-space, choose
  an even cutoff <math|\<zeta\>=1> on <math|<around|[|-2*u-1,2*u+1|]>>,
  supported in <math|<around|[|-2*u-2,2*u+2|]>>. Remove from <math|c<rsub|w>>
  the profile with Fourier transform <math|\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|w*t|)>>.
  Its inverse-Fourier supremum is bounded uniformly in <math|w>, whereas the
  residual has positive response

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>e<rsup|-E<around|\||t|\|>>*<around|(|1-\<zeta\><around|(|t|)>|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|w*t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t\<geqslant\>c<rsub|u>/<around|(|E+w|)>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  For the lower bound integrate on <math|R\<less\>t\<less\>R+\<gamma\>/<around|(|E+w|)>>,
  with <math|R=2*u+2>, <math|w*R\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>> and
  <math|\<Phi\>\<geqslant\>1/2> on <math|<around|[|-3*\<gamma\>,3*\<gamma\>|]>>.
  Apply the sign-transfer inequality using this residual and an opposite-sign
  transverse axis. Its correction term is bounded by the three-axis
  mixed-sign estimate and the uniform supremum above; its other term is
  bounded using a sufficiently high transverse moment, now with fixed damping
  <math|e<rsup|-2*u*E>>. The parity pattern has both signs among the
  transverse axes and a sign opposite to the line axis, so this proves any
  positive exponent, in particular <math|\<gamma\><rsub|l>=1>. For a
  different time axis use the three transverse-to-line axes and bound the
  omitted line profile by its supremum. With width
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|1+\<omega\><rsub|l>>> this costs exponent
  <math|-<around|(|1+\<omega\><rsub|l>|)>+1=-\<omega\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>150>.
  These arguments keep the word measures fixed and include their zero-energy
  mass; an arbitrary frequency bump would not have the positive temporal-gap
  response used here. The diagonal estimate is <eqref|ch14:diagonal-mass>
  with <math|W=\<lambda\><rsup|-99>>. The crude bound
  <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>>
  follows from finite mass on <math|E\<leqslant\>1> and the profile suprema.
  Outside <math|E\<leqslant\>1> and <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j>>,
  positive damping and Schwartz decay make the relevant test functions
  <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-M>|)>> for every fixed <math|M>.

  Take even <math|\<eta\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR>|)>>
  equal to one near zero, put <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)>=t*\<eta\><around|(|t|)>>,
  and define

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|100>*\<ell\>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-100>*y<rsub|a>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>a>\<ell\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>>*y<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|b>=f<rsub|a*b>,<space|1em>V<rsub|a>=-f<rsub|b*a>.
  </equation*>

  The other components of <math|V> vanish. Near all insertions
  <math|V<around|(|y|)>=K*y>. Conservation and the local shell identities
  express the total raw angular response as
  <math|-<big|int>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*V<rsub|c>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*c>,\<b-A\>|)>>.
  The terms <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a*b>*J<rsub|a*b>> and
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*a>*J<rsub|b*a>> cancel exactly. In the
  remaining terms, reflection projects the spectators to <math|B>. Fourier
  scaling supplies <math|\<lambda\><rsup|3>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>> and
  the temporal derivative supplies <math|e<rsup|-E/\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>>.
  Apply <eqref|ch14:spectral-CS>. For the diagonal term split at
  <math|E=\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>, using <eqref|ch14:diagonal-mass> above
  this value and the crude bound below it. The worst resulting exponents,
  including the prefactor <math|\<lambda\><rsup|100>>, are

  <\equation*>
    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\>=l\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>|<cell|100+3-150+<around|(|1+1|)>/2=-46>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\>\<nin\><around|{|a,b,l|}>>|<cell|100+3-300+<around|(|150+1|)>/2=-121.5>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\>=b,E\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>|<cell|100+3+<around|(|150+1-396|)>/2=-19.5>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\>=b,E\<less\>\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>|<cell|100+3-1500+<around|(|150+900|)>/2=-872.>>>>>
  </equation*>

  The exchanged column has the same bounds. Every term tends to zero, whereas
  the total shell response is independent of <math|\<lambda\>>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:line-ward><big|sum><rsub|i><around*|{|S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,Y<around|(|K|)>*A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>|)>+<around|(|K*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|b>*S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,p<rsub|b>*A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>|)>|}>=0
  </equation>

  for every separated configuration on a coordinate line. This argument used
  coordinate positivity and conservation, not a cone spectrum or a
  pre-existing rotation action.

  <section|Normalization and propagation of the Ward
  identities><label|ch14:propagation>

  Translate a collinear configuration in its line direction in
  <eqref|ch14:line-ward> and subtract the original identity. With a plane
  generator mixing that line and a transverse direction one obtains the
  translation Ward sum for <math|p<rsub|b>>. The commutation law
  <eqref|ch14:charge-commute> gives its positional derivatives as well. The
  usual positional derivatives <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>> obey the same
  identities.

  The finite vector correlation functionals of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch03:vector-tests> have a one-dimensional common
  kernel in the free vector-map space, spanned by <math|\<partial\><rsup|0>>.
  Adjoin a linear functional nonzero on that vector; the matrix of
  correlation pairings becomes injective. Smooth comparison transports this
  injectivity to the mixed finite-dimensional vector-map space. Thus its Ward
  kernel has dimension at most one. It contains both <math|p> and
  <math|\<partial\>>, so <math|p=c<rsub|d>*\<partial\>> at every sufficiently
  large degree. Inclusions and nonzero free differentiation identify these
  constants across degrees and extend the identity to the lower ones. Call
  the common constant <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Changing variables in the shell
  integrals and using <eqref|ch14:balanced-stress> gives

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:charge-limits><frac|r<rsub|k>|c<rsub|k>>*U<rsub|k,d+1><rsup|-1>*p<rsub|b>*U<rsub|k,d>\<to\>\<partial\><rsub|b><rsup|0>,<space|2em><frac|1|c<rsub|k>>*U<rsub|k,d><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*U<rsub|k,d>\<to\>L<rsub|d><rsup|0><around|(|K|)>.
  </equation>

  A finite separating family of correlation pairings justify these operator
  limits, even if the operator norms were not known in advance. Since
  <math|r<rsub|k>*U<rsub|k,d+1><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*U<rsub|k,d>\<to\>\<partial\><rsub|b><rsup|0>\<ne\>0>
  in some degree, the first limit gives <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>\<ne\>0> and
  <math|c<rsub|k>\<to\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>>. In particular
  <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>, since <math|c<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>. Put
  <math|L<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>=c<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>>.
  Then

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:derivative-commutator>L<rsub|d+1><around|(|K|)>*\<partial\><rsub|b>=\<partial\><rsub|b>*L<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>+<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*\<partial\><rsub|c>.
  </equation>

  Every positional derivative of the normalized angular Ward sum is therefore
  another such sum with a differentiated label.

  <subsection|Analyticity supplied by a positive Gram kernel>

  The missing step from a line to an open set is analyticity, not smoothness.
  We establish exactly the analyticity needed. For <math|x<rsup|a>\<gtr\>0>
  let <math|\<Psi\><rsub|A><around|(|x|)>=<around|[|A<around|(|x|)>|]><rsub|a>>
  in the coordinate reflection space. The curve
  <math|t\<mapsto\>\<Psi\><rsub|A>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>> is smooth for
  <math|b\<ne\>a>: its weak derivative against every word vector is
  <math|\<Psi\><rsub|\<partial\><rsub|b>*A>*<around|(|x+t*e<rsub|b>|)>>,
  which is continuous by translation continuity. Integrating this continuous
  vector curve and using the dense word span proves strong differentiability.
  Iterate for higher orders. In the normal direction the curve is analytic
  away from the reflection plane, since it is a positive-time heat orbit.

  Let <math|F<rsub|A,B><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|A<around|(|x|)>,B<around|(|0|)>|)>>.
  Its restriction to <math|u*e<rsub|a>>, <math|u\<gtr\>0>, is analytic by
  that heat argument. In the plane <math|<around|(|a,b|)>> with
  <math|K*e<rsub|a>=e<rsub|b>>, <math|K*e<rsub|b>=-e<rsub|a>>, define

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|A,B>*<around|(|u,z|)>=F<rsub|e<rsup|-\<theta\>*L<around|(|K|)>>*A,e<rsup|-\<theta\>*L<around|(|K|)>>*B>*<around|(|\<rho\>*e<rsub|a>|)>,<space|1em>\<rho\>=<around|(|u<rsup|2>+z<rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,<space|1em>\<theta\>=arctan
    <around|(|z/u|)>.
  </equation*>

  This is analytic for <math|u\<gtr\>0>: the label spaces are
  finite-dimensional and the radial functions are analytic. It solves the
  angular identity because <math|<around|(|u*\<partial\><rsub|z>-z*\<partial\><rsub|u>|)>*\<theta\>=1>
  and the same operator kills <math|\<rho\>>. It agrees with the actual
  <math|F> at <math|z=0>. All transverse jets of the actual angular
  expression vanish there, by <eqref|ch14:line-ward> and
  <eqref|ch14:derivative-commutator>. For <math|H=F-Q>, differentiating the
  angular expression <math|n> times in <math|z> gives at <math|z=0>

  <\equation*>
    u*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n+1>*H<rsub|A,B>=-\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n>*<around|(|H<rsub|L<around|(|K|)>*A,B>+H<rsub|A,L<around|(|K|)>*B>|)>+n*\<partial\><rsub|u>*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|n-1>*H<rsub|A,B>.
  </equation*>

  The last term is omitted at <math|n=0>. Induction proves equality of all
  transverse jets of <math|F> and <math|Q>, simultaneously for all labels.

  Equality of jets has not yet proved equality of functions. The positive
  Gram identity supplies the decisive bound. If a smooth Hilbert-valued curve
  <math|v<around|(|t|)>> satisfies <math|<around|\<langle\>|v<around|(|s|)>,v<around|(|t|)>|\<rangle\>>=A*<around|(|t-s|)>>
  and the derivatives of <math|A> at zero agree with an analytic function,
  then

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsup|<around|(|n|)>><around|(|t|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||A<rsup|<around|(|2*n|)>><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|-2*n>*<around|(|2*n|)>!\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|2/R|)><rsup|2*n><around|(|n!|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  Hilbert-space Taylor remainders tend to zero for
  <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<less\>R/2>, uniformly in <math|t>. Apply this to
  the single-insertion curve above: its Gram kernel is
  <math|F<rsub|A,A<rsup|\<dagger\><rsub|a>>>*<around|(|2*x<rsup|a>*e<rsub|a>+<around|(|t-s|)>*e<rsub|b>|)>>,
  whose jets are those of <math|Q>. Thus single-insertion vectors are
  analytic along every transverse coordinate as well.

  If one insertion is a strict maximum in some coordinate, separate it from
  the spectator word by a coordinate hyperplane. Its moment is a matrix
  element of this analytic single-insertion curve against a fixed word
  vector. Strict minima are handled by the existing signed reflection. Hence
  the Ward sum is analytic along any coordinate segment on which the moving
  point stays a strict extremum in some coordinate. This uses no integration
  of the spin operators into a rotation group.

  <subsection|A finite path through separated configurations>

  Configurations with distinct first coordinates are dense. Starting from
  one, choose the point with smallest first coordinate among those not yet
  moved. Move it in the second coordinate until it exceeds all other second
  coordinates; it remains strictly first in the first coordinate. Next move
  it in the first coordinate beyond all other points, remaining extreme in
  the second. Finally set its other coordinates to zero, remaining extreme in
  the first. Repeat with the remaining points. Every segment is separated and
  of the analytic kind just proved. The result lies on the first coordinate
  axis. There all Ward sums and all their derivatives vanish. Reverse the
  path: the one-variable identity theorem on each segment propagates the
  identity and its derivative identities. Continuity then gives, on every
  separated configuration,

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:global-ward><big|sum><rsub|i><around*|{|S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,L<around|(|K|)>*A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>+<around|(|K*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|b>>*S*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>|}>=0.
  </equation>

  This is why replacing analyticity by smoothness in the propagation argument
  would leave a gap.

  <section|The rotation action and the normalized
  tensor><label|ch14:rotations-section>

  Commuting two identities <eqref|ch14:global-ward> shows that the internal
  defect <math|<around|[|L<around|(|K|)>,L<around|(|M|)>|]>-L<around|(|<around|[|K,M|]>|)>>
  has zero correlation sums. It is real, alternating, filtration preserving,
  kills the unit, and has the signed covariance of a pair of rotation
  generators. The finite internal-defect correlation functionals of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch03:internal-tests> are injective on this defect
  space. Their injectivity transfers under the smooth finite-dimensional
  comparison, exactly as for the vector correlation functionals above. The
  defect is therefore zero. Injective inclusions extend this conclusion from
  any sufficiently large degree to the lower ones.

  One must still exclude a representation only of the universal cover of
  <math|S*O<around|(|4|)>>. Here the free comparison gives an exact
  intertwiner. Choose the six plane generators and, at one degree, write
  their free matrices as <math|C<rsub|i>> and their transported mixed
  matrices as <math|B<rsub|k,i>>. By <eqref|ch14:charge-limits> these
  converge to <math|C<rsub|i>>. On the matrix space put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|k,i><around|(|X|)>=B<rsub|k,i>*X-X*C<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<cal-C\><rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The bracket identities make <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|k>> commute with every
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>>. At the limit an invariant inner product makes
  <math|\<cal-C\>> self-adjoint and negative semidefinite, with kernel the
  commutant of the free action. Its zero eigenvalue is isolated.
  Finite-dimensional spectral projections around that eigenvalue give real
  projections <math|P<rsub|k>\<to\>P> commuting with all
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>>. They also commute with the signed symmetries on
  matrix space. The restrictions of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>> to
  <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P<rsub|k>> tend to zero. Every plane
  generator is a commutator of two others, so their maximal norm satisfies
  <math|m<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*m<rsub|k><rsup|2>>. Eventually
  <math|m<rsub|k>=0>. Consequently <math|f<rsub|k>=P<rsub|k>*I\<to\>I> is
  invertible and <math|B<rsub|k,i>*f<rsub|k>=f<rsub|k>*C<rsub|i>>. The
  intertwiner <math|U<rsub|k,d>*f<rsub|k>> is real and preserves the signed
  action. Conjugating the free <math|O<around|(|4|)>> action by it gives the
  required genuine orthogonal-group representation. This finite-dimensional
  argument also fixes the periodicity of plane rotations, rather than
  assuming it from Lie brackets.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Euclidean rotations><label|ch14:rotations> Under the actual marked
    comparison and annular estimates used above, there are continuous
    complex-linear representations <math|R<rsub|d>> of <math|O<around|(|4|)>>
    on <math|F<rsub|d>>, extending the signed coordinate symmetries,
    preserving reality and the unit, and compatible with inclusions. Their
    infinitesimal generators are <math|L<rsub|d>>, their derivatives
    transform as vectors, and

    <\equation*>
      S*<around|(|R<rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|g|)>*A<rsub|i>;g*x|)>=S*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>,<space|2em>g\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>.
    </equation*>

    They are isomorphic in each degree to the free label representations.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The preceding construction supplies the representations. The function

    <\equation*>
      t\<longmapsto\>S*<around|(|R<around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>*\<b-A\>;e<rsup|t*K>*x|)>
    </equation*>

    has zero derivative by <eqref|ch14:global-ward>. Plane rotations and
    signed reflections generate <math|O<around|(|4|)>>. Covariance, the fact
    that inserting the identity leaves correlations unchanged, and the exact
    derivative and inclusion identities show that the corresponding label
    identities have zero responses against every spectator. Faithfulness of
    the field quotient proves their equality. Thus the independently chosen
    finite-degree intertwiners need not themselves commute with inclusions;
    the resulting actions do.
  </proof>

  Let <math|J<rsub|0>=c<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>*J>. With probability Haar
  measure on <math|O<around|(|4|)>> set

  <\equation>
    <label|ch14:averaged-stress>T<rsub|a*b>=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>><big|sum><rsub|i,j>g<rsub|a*i>*g<rsub|b*j>*R<rsub|4><around|(|g|)><around|(|J<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i*j>*<space|0.17em>d*g.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Normalized stress tensor><label|ch14:normalized-stress>The tensor
    <math|T> is real, centered, symmetric, conserved and nonzero. It
    transforms as a tensor under <math|R<rsub|4>>. Its constant and linear
    local fluxes are respectively the positional derivative and
    <math|L<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>>. Along the same comparison sequence,
    <math|U<rsub|k,4><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*T|)>\<to\>T<rsup|0>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Haar invariance gives the tensor law. All other linear properties pass
    through the integral, conservation using vector covariance of
    derivatives. In a transformed shell change variables by <math|g> and use
    correlation covariance; its actions are <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>> and
    <math|L<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>>. Averaging preserves these normalized
    actions. If <math|T=0>, all tested positional derivatives would vanish,
    contradicting smooth comparison with a free differentiated quadratic
    field. Finally <math|T> belongs to the same centered conserved line as
    <math|J>. Its translation action forces <math|J=c<rsub|\<ast\>>*T>, and
    <eqref|ch14:balanced-stress> with <math|c<rsub|k>\<to\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>>
    proves the comparison limit.
  </proof>

  The complexified finite-dimensional rotation representations also specify
  the label representations for the later continuation to Lorentz
  transformations. No Lorentz action on a physical Hilbert space, uniqueness
  of the vacuum, or positive extension of collision kernels has been
  constructed in this chapter.

  <assign|chapter-nr|14>

  <chapter|Decomposition into phases><label|ch15:phases>

  A translation-invariant correlation family can be a mixture of states with
  different long-distance behavior. We separate this mixture using operators
  on the zero-energy subspace of its reflection Hilbert space. A phase will
  be a character of a commutative operator algebra, not a set of gauge
  configurations. Its correlations are obtained by evaluating compressed
  whole words.

  The inputs are the common field spaces <math|F<rsub|d>> and separated
  correlation family of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum>, bounded slab
  operators with their adjoint and concatenation laws, and orthogonal
  covariance of that same family. The latter is stronger than the original
  lattice hypercubic covariance. We also require the bounded real lattice
  approximations and corrected moment estimates for separating families
  specified below. The assertions retain these analytic hypotheses; a
  decomposition theorem for an abstract state alone does not establish them.

  <section|The zero-energy subspace><label|ch15:ground>

  Use time coordinate four. Write <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> for the
  mixed reflection Hilbert space, <math|\<Omega\>> for its unit vacuum,
  <math|E<around|(|t|)>=e<rsup|-t*H>> for the heat semigroup, and
  <math|U<around|(|y|)>> for spatial translations. Let <math|P<rsub|0>> be
  the projection onto <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>=ker H>. The spectral theorem
  gives <math|E<around|(|t|)>\<to\>P<rsub|0>> strongly as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  Every spatial translation acts trivially on <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>. Here
  the orthogonal covariance input is essential. For two separated clusters
  put <math|F<rsub|v,w><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|v*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|x>*w|)>>.
  At sufficiently large time separation this is a matrix element of
  <math|U<around|(|<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>*E*<around|(|x<rsup|4>-c|)>> between
  fixed vectors. Hence a fixed temporal displacement changes it by a quantity
  tending to zero, uniformly in arbitrary spatial displacements. If
  <math|b<rsup|4>\<gtr\>0>, choose an orthogonal map <math|K> with
  <math|K*b=<around|\||b|\|>*e<rsub|4>>. Then
  <math|<around|(|K*e<rsub|4>|)><rsup|4>=b<rsup|4>/<around|\||b|\|>\<gtr\>0>.
  Rotate the clusters and apply the temporal statement along
  <math|t*K*e<rsub|4>>. This proves <math|F<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|4>+b|)>-F<rsub|v,w>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|4>|)>\<to\>0>.
  For spatial <math|y> use <math|b=y+e<rsub|4>> and remove the extra unit
  time shift. Taking strong heat limits and using the density of word vectors
  gives

  <\equation>
    U<around|(|y|)>*P<rsub|0>=P<rsub|0>.<label|ch15:spatial-ground>
  </equation>

  This conclusion concerns the ground space of the mixture; it does not say
  that this space is one dimensional.

  <section|Compression and separation of clusters><label|ch15:compression>

  For a word <math|w> placed inside a slab of width <math|h>, let
  <math|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>> be its bounded insertion operator and define

  <\equation*>
    M<around|(|w|)>=P<rsub|0>*B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>.
  </equation*>

  Increasing the width appends a heat operator, and shifting the word in time
  inserts one on the other side. Both disappear after compression. Spatial
  placement disappears by <eqref|ch15:spatial-ground>. Thus
  <math|M<around|(|w|)>> is defined for any distinct-point word independently
  of its slab placement, with

  <\equation*>
    M<around|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=1,<space|2em>M<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>,<space|2em>S<around|(|w|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  For positive-time words also put <math|Y<rsub|w>=B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>>,
  which maps into the full reflection Hilbert space. The slab adjoint and
  concatenation laws give

  <\equation>
    M*<around|(|\<theta\>*v*<space|0.17em>w|)>=Y<rsub|v><rsup|\<ast\>>*Y<rsub|w>,<space|2em>M*<around|(|\<theta\>*v|)>=M<around|(|v|)><rsup|\<ast\>>.<label|ch15:whole-gram>
  </equation>

  If successive time gaps between translated words tend to infinity, their
  correlation tends to the corresponding ordered product of compressed
  operators:

  <\equation>
    lim<rsub|j\<to\>\<infty\>> S*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|1><around|(|j|)>>*w<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|k><around|(|j|)>>*w<rsub|k>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<around|(|w<rsub|k>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>,<space|1em><around|(|z<rsub|i+1><around|(|j|)>-z<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>|)><rsup|4>\<to\>\<infty\>.<label|ch15:cluster>
  </equation>

  The spatial parts of the displacements can be arbitrary. Indeed, if
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<psi\>> and <math|t<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  then <math|U<around|(|y<rsub|j>|)>*E<around|(|t<rsub|j>|)>*\<psi\><rsub|j>\<to\>P<rsub|0>*\<psi\>>
  by contraction, strong heat convergence and <eqref|ch15:spatial-ground>.
  Iterating this fact through the bounded slab operators proves
  <eqref|ch15:cluster>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The compression algebra><label|ch15:algebra>The norm-closed unital
    star algebra <math|\<frak-A\>> generated by all <math|M<around|(|w|)>> on
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>> is commutative and separable. The vector
    <math|\<Omega\>> is cyclic and separating for it.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We give the particular geometric commutativity argument. Place four words
    at

    <\equation*>
      0,<space|1em>j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+2*e<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>j*<around|(|2*e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>,<space|1em>3*j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Exchanging coordinates one and four interchanges the time order of the
    middle clusters. Covariance and <eqref|ch15:cluster> therefore exchange
    the two middle operators, initially with that orthogonal map applied to
    all four words. Apply the same cluster limit to four words placed on the
    ray <math|j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>>, which the exchange fixes.
    This removes the common transformation of the labels and internal
    positions. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)><around|[|M<around|(|w<rsub|2>|)>,M<around|(|w<rsub|3>|)>|]>*M<around|(|w<rsub|4>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0.
    </equation*>

    The vectors <math|M<around|(|v|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|v|]>> span
    a dense subspace of <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|0>>; reflected words give the
    adjoints in the left slot. The bounded commutator is therefore zero.
    Density proves cyclicity. If <math|A*\<Omega\>=0> for
    <math|A\<in\>\<frak-A\>>, commutativity gives <math|A*B*\<Omega\>=0> for
    all <math|B\<in\>\<frak-A\>>, hence <math|A=0>. Norm continuity of slab
    operators, finite-dimensional label spaces, and countable dense position
    sets give a countable generating family, hence separability.
  </proof>

  Compression is not multiplication of finite-separation words. In fact

  <\equation>
    M*<around|(|\<theta\>*v*<space|0.17em>w|)>-M<around|(|v|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<around|(|w|)>=Y<rsub|v><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|1-P<rsub|0>|)>*Y<rsub|w>.<label|ch15:compression-defect>
  </equation>

  The right side usually survives at finite separation. It is the whole-word
  identity <eqref|ch15:whole-gram>, rather than a product of scalar phase
  expectations, that will give phase reflection positivity.

  <section|Characters and the representing measure><label|ch15:characters>

  Let <math|\<Xi\>> be the space of unital star characters of
  <math|\<frak-A\>>. The commutative Gelfand representation identifies
  <math|\<frak-A\>\<simeq\>C<around|(|\<Xi\>|)>> isometrically; see the
  original <cite-detail|GelfandNaimark1943|Section<nbsp>2, Lemma<nbsp>1>. The
  compact space <math|\<Xi\>> is metrizable because <math|\<frak-A\>> is
  separable. The positive normalized vacuum functional has a unique
  representing probability measure <math|\<nu\>> by the Riesz theorem; for
  this application see <cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.4.1
  and<nbsp>2.8.2>. It has full support. Otherwise a nonzero continuous
  function supported in a nonempty open null set would correspond to an
  <math|A\<ne\>0> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|A|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>=0>,
  contradicting separation.

  Define

  <\equation>
    S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<xi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)>,<space|2em>S<around|(|w|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>>S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>.<label|ch15:mixture>
  </equation>

  Every fixed separated value is continuous in <math|\<xi\>>. This is a
  representation of the mixed correlations by characters of the ground
  algebra. Vacuum uniqueness in these phases will be proved separately.

  We shall repeatedly use the following consequence of full support. If
  <math|A\<in\>\<frak-A\>> and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*A|)><rsup|j>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|2*j>>
  for all <math|j>, then

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>=sup<rsub|\<xi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C.<label|ch15:moment-norm>
  </equation>

  This is the usual limit of <math|L<rsup|2*j>> norms on a probability space,
  combined with full support for a continuous function. In applications, the
  moment bound comes by taking widely separated copies of the same lattice
  expression, applying the chessboard bound at fixed copy number, and then
  taking the ordered limits and the cluster limit. The order of these
  operations matters.

  <section|Symmetries in each phase><label|ch15:phase-symmetry>

  Symmetry of a mixture need not imply symmetry of every component. Here an
  additional lattice variance estimate gives the required operator identity.
  Write <math|\<vartheta\>> for linear time reflection on positions and
  labels, without conjugation. For a generic real grid word <math|w>
  (distinct projections in each coordinate), set
  <math|Z=M<around|(|w|)>-M*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*w|)>>. The adjoint law
  gives <math|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>=-Z>, so

  <\equation*>
    a\<assign\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,Z<rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=-<around|\<\|\|\>|Z*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  Corollary<nbsp><reference|ch13:bounded-word>, using the complete residual
  estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch13:coarse-residual-supplier>, gives the
  following bounded real approximation. For widely translated lattice copies
  <math|f<rsub|i>> of <math|w-\<vartheta\>*w>, and every
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there are real <math|g<rsub|i>> with
  <math|<around|\||g<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
  uniformly in cutoff, volume and copy position, such that

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|g<rsub|i>*g<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>-<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<less\>j|)>
  </equation*>

  on the ordered cutoff tails at fixed translation and fixed pair. There is
  no corresponding assumed bound on <math|f<rsub|i><rsup|2>>. Variance
  positivity for <math|q+1> copies gives

  <\equation*>
    0\<leqslant\><around*|\<langle\>|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|q>g<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>+2*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j><around|(|<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>+\<varepsilon\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  First take the ordered cutoff limits at fixed <math|q> and translations,
  then send their separation to infinity. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch15:cluster>
  gives <math|0\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*<around|[|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>+q*<around|(|a+\<varepsilon\>|)>|]>>.
  Send <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>> and then <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>.
  Thus <math|a=0>, and separation of <math|\<Omega\>> gives <math|Z=0>. The
  cofinal grids are chosen after the degree and lower-level request. Generic
  configurations are dense in the full separated configuration space. Norm
  continuity and multilinearity extend the identity to every word.

  Orthogonal covariance of the mixture induces simultaneous invariance of
  compressed product moments whenever a cluster-separating ray has positive
  time before and after rotation. Such a ray exists unless time is reversed;
  compose with <math|\<vartheta\>> in that case. On the dense vectors
  <math|M<around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>> this gives a unitary orthogonal action,
  under which time reflection now acts as the identity. Its conjugates, all
  hyperplane reflections, therefore act trivially as well. Since these
  generate <math|O<around|(|4|)>>,

  <\equation>
    M*<around|(|K*w|)>=M<around|(|w|)>*<space|2em><around|(|K\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>|)>.<label|ch15:phase-invariant>
  </equation>

  Character evaluation proves symmetry in every phase. The proved lattice
  approximation estimate is indispensable to this conclusion.

  <section|Positivity, field identities and null spaces><label|ch15:fields>

  For positive words <math|w<rsub|i>>, <eqref|ch15:whole-gram> gives the
  positive operator

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c<rsub|i>|\<bar\>>c<rsub|j>*M*<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|w<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*Y<rsub|w<rsub|j>>|)>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation*>

  It belongs to <math|\<frak-A\>>. Positivity agrees with ambient operator
  positivity in a unital C<rsup|<math|\<ast\>>>-subalgebra, and characters
  are positive; see <cite-detail|Landsman1998|Section<nbsp>2.6>. Hence each
  phase is reflection positive, with a Gram form that need not have rank one.
  Norm-smooth slab dependence gives smooth phase correlations and their
  uniform separated bounds. Field inclusion, derivative and stress identities
  pass to phases when written as operator identities, tested against all
  exterior words before character evaluation. For a stress shell the weights
  avoid collisions, so the norm-continuous operator integral is defined. The
  expectation identity alone would not suffice.

  A mixed null field has zero slab operator, by testing against the dense
  word vectors. It is therefore null in every phase:
  <math|N<rsub|d><around|(|S|)>\<subset\>N<rsub|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>>.
  The reverse inclusion uses the quantitative separating families from the
  same lattice family. Apply Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:n-mark-comparison>
  with two marks per comparison cell. At a fixed sufficiently deep comparison
  level <math|k>, its corrected coefficients for one and two insertions have
  chessboard seminorm at most <math|B<rsub|k>=H<rsub|k><rsup|-1/32>>,
  uniformly in cutoff and period. Choose a simultaneous coefficient limit for
  the finitely many bounded lifts of the separating family at this fixed
  <math|k>, on a refinement of the already selected correlation limits. For
  every fixed integer <math|j>, put <math|2*j> copies of a corrected word and
  its adjoint at widely separated translations. The chessboard bound is
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2*j>>: the two-mark estimate is used with arbitrary
  replica census, not rerun with arity <math|2*j>. Its constant is chosen
  before <math|j>; only the cutoff and translation thresholds depend on that
  fixed number. The repeated-word identity and the cluster limit
  <eqref|ch15:cluster> identify the limiting product with
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|\<Delta\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Delta\>|)><rsup|j>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>.
  Taking successive deeper levels <math|k=k<rsub|\<ell\>>> therefore supplies
  compressed errors for one and two insertions <math|\<Delta\>> with

  <\equation>
    <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|\<Delta\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Delta\>|)><rsup|j>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>><rsup|2*j>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<geqslant\>1|)>,<space|2em>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<longrightarrow\>0.<label|ch15:detector-moments>
  </equation>

  Here one may take <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>=B<rsub|k<rsub|\<ell\>>>>,
  enlarging it by a fixed finite row constant if necessary. The free row
  moments are bounded, so <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<to\>0>. These are
  estimates uniform in the number of reflected copies, stronger than a bound
  on the mixed mean. The finite reference rows use fixed free labels and
  convergent noncollision configurations; their one-point means are uniformly
  bounded. To make the pair correction explicit, if the reference one-point
  means are <math|s,s<rprime|'>> and the reference connected pair is
  <math|c>, use

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|1>=M<around|(|u|)>-s*1,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rprime|'><rsub|1>=M<around|(|v|)>-s<rprime|'>*1,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|2>=M*<around|(|u*v|)>-s*M<around|(|v|)>-s<rprime|'>*M<around|(|u|)>+<around|(|s*s<rprime|'>-c|)>*1.
  </equation*>

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch15:moment-norm> bounds each error by
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>> in every character. Therefore the phase pair
  differs from <math|s*s<rprime|'>+c> by at most
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||s|\|>+<around|\||s<rprime|'>|\|>|)>>.
  The phase matrices of correlation pairings converge uniformly in
  <math|\<xi\>> to the injective free matrix of correlation pairings. Since
  they factor through <math|V<rsub|d>/N<rsub|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>>,
  its dimension is at least <math|dim \<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>.
  The inclusion of null spaces gives the opposite bound through
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch13:rank>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    N<rsub|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>=N<rsub|d><around|(|S|)><space|1em><text|for
    every >\<xi\>.<label|ch15:same-fields>
  </equation>

  All phases have the same field filtration. Neither abstract character
  decomposition nor positivity alone proves this equality.

  <section|Selecting a phase with a unique ground
  vector><label|ch15:selection>

  Construct the reflection Hilbert space and heat semigroup of each phase
  using its separated hierarchy. Let <math|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>> denote its
  ground projection. For a positive word <math|w>, the nonnegative heat
  matrix elements decrease to <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><around|[|w|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  and are dominated by the continuous zero-time Gram value. Dominated
  convergence and <eqref|ch15:mixture> yield

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><around|[|w|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><around|\||S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>.
  </equation*>

  Their difference is <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><around|[|w|]><rsub|\<xi\>>-S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
  It vanishes almost everywhere. Rational positive configurations with basis
  labels form one countable family dense in every phase reflection space, by
  Gram continuity. Intersect its full-measure sets. Outside the resulting
  null set,

  <\equation>
    ker H<rsub|\<xi\>>=<CC>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>.<label|ch15:ae-ground>
  </equation>

  Choose and retain one <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> in this full-measure set.
  At this stage we have not eliminated exceptional phases or shown that
  <math|\<nu\>> is a point mass.

  The slab bounds are uniform in all phases, including exceptional ones. For
  the formal iterates <math|v<rsub|j>=<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v>,
  the mixed maps satisfy <math|Y<rsub|v<rsub|j>>=<around|(|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|)><rsup|j>*Y<rsub|v>>.
  Thus

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|v<rsub|j>|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<xi\>*<around|(|Y<rsub|v<rsub|j>><rsup|\<ast\>>*Y<rsub|v<rsub|j>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|v>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|4*j>.
  </equation*>

  The repeated-power argument gives bounded phase slab operators with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
  and their identities and derivatives follow on the dense word span. These
  uniform bounds are the input for relativistic reconstruction and for the
  later removal of exceptional phases. No spectral gap has been obtained
  here.

  <section|Sharp separated bounds in every
  phase><label|ch15:sharp-phase-section>

  Recall that <eqref|ch13:sharp-separated> bounds the mixed fixed-arity
  correlation by the sum of degrees plus any positive excess. The estimate is
  also uniform in the phase <math|\<xi\>>. To see this without bounding phase
  evaluations by the mixed expectation of the same word, apply the preceding
  chessboard argument to arbitrarily many separated copies of that <em|whole>
  word and their adjoints. The cell scales are chosen from the residue
  patterns and separations of one fixed word, not from the total number of
  copies. Copies placed far apart occupy distinct cells and have the same
  one-word factor on the right of the chessboard inequality. Passing to the
  separated-cluster limit yields the same bound to the corresponding power
  for the moments of its compressed operator
  <math|M<around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<around|(|w|)>>. The norm formula of
  <eqref|ch15:moment-norm> and the character bound then give

  <\equation>
    sup<rsub|\<xi\>\<in\>\<Xi\>><around|\||S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<b-A\>,n,\<epsilon\>>*r<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>-\<epsilon\>>.<label|ch13:sharp-phase>
  </equation>

  Thus the fixed-arity scaling cost is the sum of degrees plus an arbitrarily
  small excess. No uniform constant as arity tends to infinity is asserted;
  the repeated-word estimate remains the appropriate statement for that
  different purpose.

  <assign|chapter-nr|15>

  <chapter|Relativistic reconstruction in every
  phase><label|ch16:reconstruction>

  We now pass from separated Euclidean correlations to relativistic fields.
  The order of the argument matters. Bounded slab insertions give analytic
  functions of complex time and their joint tempered boundary values. Lorentz
  covariance and spectral support then supply a full spacetime tube, in which
  Euclidean permutation symmetry becomes locality. Only after these steps do
  we form the Hilbert space of field words.

  Throughout this chapter we use the conclusions of
  Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch14:reflection> and<nbsp><reference|ch15:phases>:
  the coordinate reflection spaces, exact Euclidean symmetries and common
  phase field calculus. Write <math|\<Xi\>> for the compact phase space,
  <math|\<nu\>> for its full-support probability measure, and
  <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> for its correlations. The finite-dimensional spaces
  <math|F<rsub|d>> have compatible inclusions; their algebraic direct limit
  is denoted by <math|F>. All estimates below concern a fixed finite list of
  labels. Uniformity means uniformity in <math|\<xi\>>, not in the number or
  degree of those labels.

  The underlying reconstruction principles are those of Wightman and
  Osterwalder\USchrader <cite|Wightman1956|OsterwalderSchrader1973|OsterwalderSchrader1975>.
  Here the available Euclidean data are smooth functions off the diagonals,
  with bounded slab operators. We therefore verify the boundary construction
  directly; an unproved extension of these functions across Euclidean
  collisions is not an input.

  <section|Complex time and a uniform boundary
  distribution><label|ch16:boundary-section>

  Use coordinate four as time. In the phase reflection space let
  <math|E<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|s|)>=e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
  and <math|U<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|y|)>> be the heat and spatial translation
  operators. The spectral theorem extends <math|E<rsub|\<xi\>>> to
  <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>s\<gtr\>0> as a holomorphic contraction.
  Let <math|B<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|A|)>> be the slab
  operator of width <math|2*\<varepsilon\>> with the single label <math|A>
  placed at its center. The phase version of the slab bound gives

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|A|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A>*\<varepsilon\><rsup|-q<rsub|A>>,<space|2em>B<rsub|\<varepsilon\>+u><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|A|)>=E<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|u|)>*B<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|A|)>*E<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|u|)>,<space|1em>0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>1,<label|ch16:slabs>
  </equation>

  where <math|u\<geqslant\>0> in the padding identity. Linearity and
  compatibility under <math|F<rsub|d>\<hookrightarrow\>F<rsub|d+1>> follow by
  testing against all exterior words.

  For <math|z<rsub|j>=t<rsub|j>+i*y<rsub|j>> with
  <math|y<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>y<rsub|n>>, set

  <\equation*>
    G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|z,x|)>=E<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|-i*z-2*\<varepsilon\>|)>*U<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|4*\<varepsilon\>\<less\>min<rsub|j><around|(|y<rsub|j+1>-y<rsub|j>|)>>
  define

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<b-A\>;z,\<b-x\>|)>=<around*|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>,B<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|A<rsub|1>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j=2><rsup|n><around*|[|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|j-1>,x<rsub|j>-x<rsub|j-1>|)>*B<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|A<rsub|j>|)>|]>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<rangle\>>.<label|ch16:heat-function>
  </equation>

  The product is ordered from left to right with increasing <math|j>. Padding
  and the semigroup law show that this expression is independent of
  <math|\<varepsilon\>>: replacing it by <math|\<varepsilon\>+u> moves two
  factors <math|E<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|u|)>> onto each intervening gap,
  while the exterior factors fix the vacuum. At purely imaginary times it is
  <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> at the corresponding Euclidean points. It is
  holomorphic in the time variables and continuous in the real spatial
  variables. No norm continuity of the spatial unitaries is required.

  Contraction and <eqref|ch16:slabs>, with <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<delta\>/8>,
  give

  <\equation>
    <around|\||<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|t+i*y,x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsup|-m>,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>y<rsub|j+1>-y<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>,<label|ch16:gap-bound>
  </equation>

  uniformly in all real positions and in <math|\<xi\>>. This is the
  quantitative regularity needed for reconstruction.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Joint tempered boundary><label|ch16:boundary>For every label tuple
    there is a tempered distribution <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<b-A\>|)>>
    in all <math|4*n> real coordinates, obtained as the boundary of
    <eqref|ch16:heat-function> along arbitrary strictly ordered heights
    tending to zero. The distributions obey one common Schwartz seminorm
    bound, depend continuously on <math|\<xi\>> when tested, and have the
    mixed correlation distribution as their <math|\<nu\>>-average. The same
    assertions hold at fixed weakly ordered imaginary heights, including tied
    heights.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For a joint Schwartz test function <math|f> put
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|f|)>=<big|int><math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|t+i*y,x|)>*f*<around|(|t,x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t*<space|0.17em>d*x>.
    Let <math|r=<around|(|0,1,\<ldots\>,n-1|)>> and
    <math|D=-i*<big|sum><rsub|j>r<rsub|j>*\<partial\><rsub|t<rsub|j>>> acting
    on test functions. Cauchy's formula, <eqref|ch16:gap-bound>, and
    integration by parts give <math|<frac|d|d*s>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|f|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|D*f|)>>.
    Taylor's formula, evaluated backwards from <math|s=1>, is the exact
    identity

    <\equation>
      \<psi\><rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|f|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|m><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>|k!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+r><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|D<rsup|k>*f|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><frac|<around|(|-s|)><rsup|m>|m!>*\<psi\><rsub|y+s*r><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|D<rsup|m+1>*f|)>*<space|0.17em>d*s.<label|ch16:taylor-boundary>
    </equation>

    The integrand is bounded by <math|C*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|m+1>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>
    for every weakly ordered <math|y>: the factor <math|s<rsup|m>> cancels
    the worst gap singularity. The right-hand side therefore defines the
    extension to all such <math|y>, continuously by dominated convergence.
    Its bound is a fixed finite sum of <math|L<rsup|1>> norms of derivatives
    of <math|f>, hence a Schwartz seminorm. In particular there is no
    restriction on ratios of the heights approaching zero.

    For strictly ordered heights, phase continuity follows from the normal
    family theorem in the time variables: every convergent phase sequence has
    locally convergent analytic subsequences, and agreement at imaginary
    times identifies each limit with the limiting phase by the identity
    theorem. Local boundedness is uniform by <eqref|ch16:gap-bound>.
    Dominated convergence after testing gives continuity of
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>>>. The same uniqueness argument
    continues the barycenter identity from imaginary times.
    Formula<nbsp><eqref|ch16:taylor-boundary> transfers both claims to weakly
    ordered heights and to the boundary. Arity zero and one require no gap
    argument and are covered by their constant bounds.
  </proof>

  <section|Lorentz covariance and the spectral
  cone><label|ch16:covariance-section>

  Let <math|s<rsub|4>> be reflection of Euclidean coordinate four. The field
  adjoint on <math|F> is the antilinear involution

  <\equation*>
    A<rsup|\<ast\>>=R<around|(|s<rsub|4>|)>*<wide|A|\<bar\>>.
  </equation*>

  If <math|L<rsub|a*b>> is the infinitesimal Euclidean rotation action on
  labels, the spatial rotation generators are <math|L<rsub|i*j>> and the
  boost generators are <math|-i*L<rsub|4*i>>. Their commutation relations
  give the proper orthochronous Lorentz action <math|\<rho\>>. It descends
  from the covering group because a full spatial turn already acts trivially
  under <math|R>. The above involution commutes with <math|\<rho\>>: the sign
  from conjugating <math|i> cancels the reflection sign on
  <math|L<rsub|4*i>>. We use spacetime coordinates
  <math|<around|(|x<rsup|1>,x<rsup|2>,x<rsup|3>,t|)>>, metric
  <math|\<eta\>=<math-up|diag><around|(|-1,-1,-1,1|)>>, and

  <\equation*>
    <wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>=<around|{|<around|(|p,E|)>:E\<geqslant\><around|\||p|\|>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Fourier duality uses the ordinary coordinate pairing <math|p\<cdot\>x+E*t>.
  The dual Lorentz action is the inverse transpose, which also preserves
  <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>.

  Differentiate Euclidean rotational covariance at imaginary times and
  continue the identity using the Cauchy\URiemann equations. The result is
  the infinitesimal Lorentz covariance identity for
  <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>. Testing and taking its joint
  boundary proves the distributional identity: terms containing a height tend
  to zero because the tested boundary families have the common seminorm bound
  of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch16:boundary>. Integrating the
  finite-dimensional label action and the corresponding flow on Schwartz test
  functions proves Lorentz covariance of <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>.
  Translations and spatial rotations continue directly.

  Use the Fourier convention

  <\equation*>
    <wide|f|^><around|(|X|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-i*X\<cdot\>p>*f<around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p,<space|2em><wide|W|^><around|(|f|)>=W<around|(|<wide|f|^>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For a compactly supported test function <math|b> in momentum space,
  differentiating simultaneously the imaginary height and the factor
  multiplying <math|b> gives

  <\equation>
    <wide|W|^><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>=\<psi\><rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<wide|e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|j>y<rsub|j>*p<rsub|j><rsup|4>>*b|^>|)>.<label|ch16:laplace>
  </equation>

  Indeed the two differentiated terms cancel, and their common value at
  height zero is the left side. Compact frequency support makes every
  intermediate test function a Schwartz function.

  Translation invariance restricts support to
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>=0>. Put
  <math|q<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|k=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|k>>. If the support of
  <math|b> lies in <math|q<rsub|j><rsup|4>\<less\>0>, let the corresponding
  imaginary gap tend to infinity in <eqref|ch16:laplace>, keeping the other
  gaps positive. Exponential decay of the weighted test function and the
  uniform bound at gaps at least one show that its pairing vanishes. A
  partition of unity and Lorentz covariance now give

  <\equation>
    <math-up|supp><wide|W|^><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>\<subset\>K<rsub|n>,<space|2em>K<rsub|n>=<around*|{|p:<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>=0,<space|1em>q<rsub|j>\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>*<around|(|2\<leqslant\>j\<leqslant\>n|)>|}>.<label|ch16:cone-support>
  </equation>

  To justify the last step, a vector outside <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>
  can be made to have negative time component by a proper orthochronous
  Lorentz transformation. Compact subsets of the complement admit finitely
  many such neighborhoods.

  <section|The spacetime tube and locality><label|ch16:locality-section>

  The boundary distribution just constructed is denoted by
  <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>; <math|K<rsub|n>> in
  <eqref|ch16:cone-support> is its momentum support cone. Define

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|n>=<around|{|Y:Y<rsub|j+1>-Y<rsub|j>\<in\><wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>|}>,<space|2em>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>=<around|{|X+i*Y:Y<rsub|j+1>-Y<rsub|j>\<in\>V<rsub|+>|}>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|p\<in\>K<rsub|n>>, telescoping gives
  <math|p\<cdot\>Y=<big|sum><rsub|j=2><rsup|n>q<rsub|j>\<cdot\><around|(|Y<rsub|j>-Y<rsub|j-1>|)>\<geqslant\>0>.
  For strictly interior <math|Y> this is bounded below by
  <math|c<rsub|Y><around|\||p|\|>>, locally uniformly in <math|Y>. One way to
  see the strict margin is to choose a ball of radius <math|2*c<rsub|Y>>
  around <math|Y> inside the strict-gap cone and apply positivity to
  <math|Y-c<rsub|Y>*p/<around|\||p|\|>>.

  Choose a smooth cutoff <math|\<chi\>> equal to one on the distance-one
  neighborhood of <math|K<rsub|n>>, supported in its distance-three
  neighborhood, with all derivatives bounded. Convolution of the distance-two
  neighborhood's indicator with a bump of radius <math|1/2> is one such
  choice. Then

  <\equation>
    \<cal-W\><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;Z|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4*n>*<wide|W|^><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|\<chi\><around|(|p|)>*e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>Z>|)>,<space|2em>Z\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>,<label|ch16:full-tube>
  </equation>

  is well defined: the tested function and all its derivatives decay
  exponentially on the support of <math|\<chi\>>. It is holomorphic into
  Schwartz space before the distribution is applied. It agrees with
  <eqref|ch16:heat-function> at strictly ordered scalar heights, by
  <eqref|ch16:laplace> and compact-frequency approximation.

  For <math|Y\<in\>C<rsub|n>>, the multiplier
  <math|\<chi\>*e<rsup|-p\<cdot\>Y>> and its derivatives are uniformly
  bounded on compact sets of <math|Y>. In fact distance at most three from
  <math|K<rsub|n>> gives <math|-p\<cdot\>Y\<leqslant\>3<around|\||Y|\|>>.
  Multiplication by this function is therefore continuous on Schwartz space,
  including as <math|Y> approaches a tied gap. This proves the partial
  boundary formula

  <\equation>
    W<rsub|n,Y><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|f|)>=<wide|W|^><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|\<chi\>*e<rsup|-p\<cdot\>Y>*<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4*n>*<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>X>*f<around|(|X|)>*<space|0.17em>d*X|)>.<label|ch16:partial-boundary>
  </equation>

  It has boundary <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>> at <math|Y=0>. The cutoff is
  essential here: the exponential alone need not be a Schwartz multiplier off
  the spectral cone. Tube Lorentz covariance follows from boundary
  covariance; changed cutoffs agree near <math|K<rsub|n>> and hence give the
  same pairing.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Adjacent spacelike exchange><label|ch16:locality>Let
    <math|\<sigma\>> exchange slots <math|j> and <math|j+1>. On the open set
    where <math|X<rsub|j+1>-X<rsub|j>> is spacelike,

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;X|)>=W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<sigma\>*\<b-A\>;\<sigma\>*X|)>
    </equation*>

    as distributions. This statement permits arbitrary exterior insertions.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Keep positive scalar imaginary gaps except for one tied pair
    <math|y<rsub|j>=y<rsub|j+1>>. Write its spacelike real difference as
    <math|<around|(|v,t|)>>, and set <math|e=v/<around|\||v|\|>>. The
    imaginary part of its image under a boost of parameter <math|i*\<alpha\>>
    in direction <math|e> is <math|sin \<alpha\>*<space|0.17em><around|(|t*e,<around|\||v|\|>|)>>,
    a strictly forward vector for small positive <math|\<alpha\>>. All other
    gaps stay strictly forward by continuity. Thus a small positive imaginary
    boost enters the original tube, and a negative one followed by exchange
    enters the exchanged tube.

    Fix one sufficiently small nonzero boost parameter first. The transformed
    tied-gap point is strictly inside the relevant tube, so a whole complex
    neighborhood of the original point is mapped into that tube. Pulling back
    the holomorphic function, with its entire finite-dimensional label
    action, therefore defines a genuine germ on that neighborhood. Use the
    opposite sign and the exchanged tube for the second germ. Real Lorentz
    covariance and the identity theorem show that each germ extends its
    original tube function; the whole short imaginary-boost path stays in
    that tube at neighboring interior points.

    At vanishing real times these germs coincide. Their restrictions to the
    Euclidean slice <math|<around|(|i*t<rsub|k>,x<rsub|k>|)>> equal the same
    smooth, permutation-symmetric function on its two ordered sides. At the
    tied pair the spatial points are distinct, so this Euclidean function is
    smooth there. Consequently all derivatives of the two restrictions agree
    at the tie. The slice is totally real and its tangent space spans the
    ambient complex vector space. The holomorphic Taylor coefficients
    therefore agree, proving identity of the germs.

    For general real times multiply every real time by
    <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>. The distinguished difference remains
    spacelike along this path and the other imaginary gaps remain strict. A
    finite chain of overlapping germ neighborhoods propagates the equality
    from <math|s=0> to <math|s=1> by uniqueness. Take the continuous partial
    boundaries <eqref|ch16:partial-boundary>, and then remove the other
    imaginary gaps. This proves the identity against test functions compactly
    supported in the spacelike open set. Smooth compact cutoffs converging in
    Schwartz space give the stated distributional version. No permutation of
    coincident Lorentzian boundary values has been assumed.
  </proof>

  <section|Field words and the reflection space><label|ch16:fields-section>

  A letter is a pair <math|<around|(|A,f|)>> with <math|A\<in\>F> and
  <math|f\<in\><with|math-font|cal*|S><around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)>>. The star of
  a word reverses its order and replaces every letter by
  <math|<around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>>. Let <math|\<cal-P\>>
  be their algebraic span, including the empty word <math|1>. The
  distributions <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>> define a functional
  <math|w<rsub|\<xi\>>> on <math|\<cal-P\>> by evaluation on product test
  functions.

  The essential positivity statement follows inside the phase reflection
  space. Give a word strictly increasing positive imaginary heights, smear
  its real variables, and use the slab product to obtain a vector
  <math|h<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>>. Its Gram pairing with another
  such vector is a tested continued correlation, with the reflected first
  word on the left. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch16:boundary> permits the
  two height lists to tend to zero independently, so

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|h<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>,h<rsub|z><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|c|)>|\<rangle\>>\<longrightarrow\>w<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>.
  </equation*>

  Expanding <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>-h<rsub|z><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  proves that these vectors are Cauchy. Their limits
  <math|v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>> have Gram form
  <math|w<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>>, which is therefore
  positive. The same common seminorm bounds extend positivity to finite
  sequences of joint Schwartz test functions by tensor-product density.

  The standard word reconstruction <cite|Wightman1956> now gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-N\><rsub|\<xi\>>=<around|{|b:w<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*b|)>=0|}>,<space|1em>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>=\<cal-P\>/\<cal-N\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<space|1em>\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>=<wide|\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<bar\>>,<space|1em>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=<around|[|1|]>,<space|2em>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A,f|)><around|[|b|]>=<around|[|<around|(|A,f|)>*b|]>.
  </equation*>

  For clarity, its null-space check is worth recording. If <math|b> is null,
  Cauchy\USchwarz says it pairs to zero with every word. The concatenation
  identity moves a letter to the other side of the pairing, so its product
  with <math|b> also pairs to zero with every word, including itself. Thus
  all fields are well defined on the same invariant dense domain. The same
  identity gives

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A<rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>\<subset\>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A,f|)><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  They are closable, and the joint distribution bound gives continuity of
  <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A,f|)>*v> in Hilbert norm
  for every <math|v\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. We do not assert that these
  generally unbounded fields are bounded or that their self-adjoint closures
  exist without further input.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Fields and unitary equivalence with the reflection-positive
    space><label|ch16:fields>In every phase the fields above are local,
    covariant under a strongly continuous unitary representation of the
    proper orthochronous Poincaré group, and have joint translation spectrum
    in <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>. The map

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>><around|[|b|]>=v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>
    </equation*>

    extends to a unitary map from <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>> onto the
    phase reflection space. It sends vacuum to vacuum, intertwines spatial
    translations and heat semigroups, and the field labeling is faithful.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Covariance preserves the word Gram form, hence defines unitaries on its
    quotient. Schwartz continuity proves strong continuity first on words and
    then on their completion. Locality follows by applying
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch16:locality> inside arbitrary word matrix
    elements. The joint spectral theorem for the commuting translation
    unitaries, together with <eqref|ch16:cone-support> applied to such matrix
    elements, places their spectrum in <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>.

    The Gram identity makes <math|J<rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> an
    isometry. Its range is closed, but density must still be checked. For a
    smooth compactly supported joint frequency test function <math|b>,
    multiplication by <math|e<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|j>y<rsub|j>*p<rsub|j><rsup|4>>>
    is harmless. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch16:laplace> identifies the norms and
    mixed pairing of the image of the resulting boundary word with the
    smeared positive-height vector at heights <math|y>. They are the same
    vector. Test functions with compact Fourier support are dense in Schwartz
    space; therefore the closed range contains every smeared positive-height
    vector. Approximate identities, followed by a perturbation of tied times
    and permutation of separated Euclidean points, give all Euclidean word
    vectors. These span the reflection space. The same positive-height
    identities prove heat and spatial intertwining.

    Finally a label acting as zero has every boundary correlation zero. The
    Laplace identity, Euclidean symmetry and continuity imply that all its
    separated Euclidean correlations vanish. The common null-space identity
    of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch15:phases>, and the injectivity of the
    matrix of correlation pairings used there, force this label to be zero in
    <math|F>.
  </proof>

  <section|The low-energy estimate and uniqueness of the vacuum in each
  phase><label|ch16:vacuum-section>

  The phase measure has full support, and the zero-energy space is a line for
  almost every phase by <reference|ch15:ae-ground>. To remove the exceptional
  set requires a bound uniform in <math|\<xi\>>. We prove it from locality
  and the spectral cone. This argument controls energies near zero, not a
  positive mass gap.

  For a finite sum <math|A> of field words with compactly supported smooth
  test functions, let <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>>> be the joint spectral
  measure of <math|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, and let
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>> be that of
  <math|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. Their total masses have a
  common bound <math|M> by Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch16:boundary>.
  Enclose all supports in a fixed spacetime box. Locality gives some
  <math|R\<less\>\<infty\>>, independent of phase, such that

  <\equation>
    a<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y,t|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|[|A<rsup|\<ast\>>,\<alpha\><rsub|<around|(|y,t|)>><around|(|A|)>|]>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=0*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>R,<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)>,<label|ch16:local-commutator>
  </equation>

  where <math|\<alpha\><rsub|z><around|(|A|)>=U<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)>*A*U<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  on the word domain. The spectral expression is the difference of the
  Fourier transforms of <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>>> and the reflected measure
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

  Take the odd time average

  <\equation*>
    <wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|y|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><frac|a<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y,t|)>-a<rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y,-t|)>|2*i>*<space|0.17em>d*t.
  </equation*>

  It is bounded by <math|M> and supported in <math|<wide|B|\<bar\>><rsub|R>>.
  Its spatial Fourier measure is the <em|sum> of the pushforwards of
  <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|p,E|)>>
  and <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|p,E|)>>
  under <math|p> and <math|-p>, respectively, where

  <\equation*>
    \<varpi\><around|(|E|)>=<frac|1-cos E|E>,<space|2em>\<varpi\><around|(|0|)>=0.
  </equation*>

  Both measures are positive; on <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>,
  <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>E/\<pi\>>. Choose a smooth
  momentum bump <math|b<rsub|u>>, equal to one on <math|B<rsub|u>>, supported
  in <math|B<rsub|2*u>>, and between zero and one. Its inverse Fourier
  transform is bounded by <math|C*u<rsup|3>>. On the shell
  <math|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u>, cone support implies
  <math|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>u>, so positivity and Fourier inversion
  yield

  <\equation*>
    c*u*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|{|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>\<leqslant\><around*|\||<big|int><wide|a|\<bar\>><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|y|)>*<wide|b|^><rsub|u><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y|\|>\<leqslant\>C*M*R<rsup|3>*u<rsup|3>.
  </equation*>

  Dyadic summation proves the first assertion below.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Uniform low-energy bound><label|ch16:vacuum>For every such
    <math|A> there is <math|C<rsub|A>>, independent of phase, such that

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)>,<eq-number><label|ch16:low-energy>>>|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-P<rsub|\<Omega\>,\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|A><rprime|'>*s<rsup|-2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|s\<geqslant\>1|)>,<eq-number><label|ch16:heat-decay>>>>>
    </align>

    where <math|P<rsub|\<Omega\>,\<xi\>>> is the projection onto
    <math|<CC>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. In every phase, <math|ker
    H<rsub|\<xi\>>=<CC>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>; the same holds in its
    reflection space.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Initially use the full zero-energy projection <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>>,
    without assuming that its range is a line. Increase the constant in
    <eqref|ch16:low-energy> so the estimate holds for all <math|u\<gtr\>0> by
    the total mass bound. Tonelli's theorem gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-Q<rsub|\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|E\<gtr\>0>e<rsup|-s*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<xi\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A><rprime|'>*s*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-s*u>*u<rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*u=2*C<rsub|A><rprime|'>*s<rsup|-2>.
    </equation*>

    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch16:fields> transfers almost-everywhere vacuum
    uniqueness from the reflection spaces, so
    <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=P<rsub|\<Omega\>,\<xi\>>> almost everywhere. For
    fixed <math|s\<gtr\>0> the vacuum-subtracted heat pairing is continuous
    in <math|\<xi\>>: it is the joint distribution at a fixed weakly ordered
    height with gap <math|s> between its two word halves. This follows either
    by the independent height limits or by <eqref|ch16:partial-boundary>.
    Thus its bound by <math|2*C<rsub|A><rprime|'>*s<rsup|-2>> holds
    everywhere, since any nonempty open violation set would have positive
    <math|\<nu\>>-measure.

    Let <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>. Strong heat convergence gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    Since <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>> fixes the unit vacuum, its value on each such
    word vector lies in the vacuum line. Compactly smeared words are dense:
    multiply Schwartz test functions by expanding smooth cutoffs and use
    joint Gram continuity. It follows that
    <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=P<rsub|\<Omega\>,\<xi\>>> on the full space. The
    onto heat intertwiner gives the last assertion.
  </proof>

  <section|The weak commutant and local stress
  charges><label|ch16:charges-section>

  Irreducibility should be stated in a form appropriate to unbounded fields.
  Their common domain is <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, the span of field
  words applied to the vacuum. A bounded operator <math|B> belongs to their
  weak commutant if

  <\equation>
    <around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u,B*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B*\<Phi\>*v|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>u,v\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<label|ch16:weak-commutant>
  </equation>

  for every smeared field <math|\<Phi\>>. The adjoint inclusion proved above
  ensures that these <math|u> are in its adjoint domain; no preservation of
  <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>> by <math|B> is assumed.

  <\proposition>
    <label|ch16:irreducible>The weak commutant in every phase consists of
    scalar operators.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Iterate <eqref|ch16:weak-commutant> to move words through matrix
    elements. For a word <math|A> put <math|u=A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>,
    <math|v=A<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. The functions

    <\equation*>
      p<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|u,e<rsup|i*z*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>q<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|B<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,e<rsup|i*z*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*v|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    are bounded and holomorphic in the upper half-plane, continuous to its
    boundary, and satisfy <math|p<around|(|t|)>=q*<around|(|-t|)>>. Pasting
    across the real line and using Morera's theorem gives a bounded entire
    function, hence a constant. The heat limit and
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|ch16:vacuum> imply
    <math|B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=b*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. Moving words
    again gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|A<rprime|'>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,B*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=b*<around|\<langle\>|A<rprime|'>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Density proves <math|B=b*I>. Scalars plainly satisfy the condition.
  </proof>

  Let <math|T<rsub|a*b>> be the normalized Euclidean stress tensor of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch14:normalized-stress>. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<zeta\><rsub|i>=1*<around|(|i\<leqslant\>3|)>,<space|2em>\<zeta\><rsub|4>=-i,<space|2em>T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>=-\<zeta\><rsub|a>*\<zeta\><rsub|b>*T<rsub|a*b>.
  </equation*>

  We use the same notation for its smeared relativistic field. Thus
  <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|44>=T<rsub|44>>,
  <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|4*i>=i*T<rsub|4*i>>, and
  <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|i*j>=-T<rsub|i*j>>. The adjoint involution,
  covariance and the continued derivative identity give a symmetric Hermitian
  Lorentz tensor with

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|a>\<eta\><rsub|a*a>*T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>=0<space|1em><text|on
    >\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed continuation of a Euclidean derivative label is
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A,f|)>=-\<zeta\><rsub|a><rsup|-1>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A,\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>>.

  For an affine Killing vector <math|K<around|(|x|)>=k+V*x>, where
  <math|V<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*\<eta\>+\<eta\>*V=0>, let
  <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>> be its infinitesimal Lorentz label action. Choose
  real <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR>|)>> of integral
  one and <math|c\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR><rsup|3>|)>>,
  and define the local charge

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|K>*<around|(|f,c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b>T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|4*b>*<around*|(|K<rsup|b>*<around|(|c\<otimes\>f|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose <math|g\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<RR><rsup|4>|)>>
  and <math|c=1> on a ball such that every point <math|<around|(|y,t|)>>
  outside that ball with <math|t\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f> is
  spacelike to <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>g>. Then

  <\equation>
    i*<around|[|Q<rsub|K>*<around|(|f,c|)>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A,g|)>|]>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>*A,g|)>-<big|sum><rsub|a>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A,\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|K<rsup|a>*g|)>|)><space|1em><text|on
    >\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>.<label|ch16:charge-law>
  </equation>

  This is a local charge identity, not a claim that the unsmeared spatial
  integral of stress exists as an operator.

  To justify its continuation, first integrate the Euclidean shell identity
  with a spatial cutoff <math|c> on the two sides of the distinguished
  insertion. Conservation moves the derivative to <math|c>; that term stays
  separated from the insertion. The two current integrals have one-sided
  limits whose jump is <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>*A+K\<cdot\>\<partial\>*A>.
  Retain strictly ordered positive heights for every spectator and put their
  complex times on a path <math|z<rsub|j><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>=i*y<rsub|j>+\<lambda\>*t<rsub|j>>.
  Place the current immediately above or below the distinguished time, at
  distance <math|\<varepsilon\>>.

  On <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*c> the spacelike holomorphic
  germs from Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch16:locality> bound the current
  integrals uniformly as <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>, locally along
  this path. Conservation bounds the difference between two such integrals by
  <math|C*<around|\||\<varepsilon\>-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|\|>>. Their
  limits are therefore holomorphic. At small imaginary <math|\<lambda\>> the
  jump is the Euclidean one; analytic continuation gives the same jump at
  <math|\<lambda\>=1>. Smearing with <math|f> and <math|g> and taking the
  joint partial boundaries proves <eqref|ch16:charge-law>; integration by
  parts produces <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|K<rsup|a>*g|)>>. General
  word spectators follow from the common Schwartz bounds. Locality removes
  any change in <math|c> outside the stated ball. This retains precisely the
  normalization established by the Euclidean shell flux.

  <assign|chapter-nr|16>

  <chapter|The trace law and its consequences><label|ch17:trace>

  The leading short-distance limit of the stress tensor is the traceless
  Maxwell tensor. It therefore says nothing by itself about the leading
  nonzero term in the trace. That term is smaller by one inverse running
  coupling, and its two-point function by two. The perturbative origin of
  this phenomenon is the trace anomaly of Collins, Duncan and Joglekar
  <cite|CollinsDuncanJoglekar1977>. The all-radius assertion below requires
  additional estimates for the selected lattice construction; it does not
  follow by substituting a perturbative beta function into a formal identity.

  Retain the Ward-normalized tensor <math|T> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch14:normalized-stress>, the phase
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> chosen in Section<nbsp><reference|ch15:selection>,
  and its relativistic reconstruction. The latter supplies a positive-cone
  translation spectrum and tempered boundary correlations. We prove the
  stronger quantitative current transfer needed for the trace asymptotic,
  including its low-moment cancellations. The construction and reconstruction
  in the preceding chapters do not depend on this refinement.

  <section|Centering and the positive spectral measure><label|ch17:spectrum>

  Put <math|\<tau\>=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>T<rsub|a*a>>,
  <math|e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>=S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|\<tau\>|)>>
  and

  <\equation*>
    \<Theta\>=\<tau\>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>*1,<space|2em>G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=S<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;r*e<rsub|4>,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  The real number <math|e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>> is the expectation in the
  selected phase, which need not equal the mixed expectation. The unit field
  is retained through the common filtration. The fact that inserting the
  identity leaves correlations unchanged extends to the boundary
  correlations, so, on the common invariant word domain,

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<tau\>><around|(|f|)>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><around*|(|<big|int>f|)>*I,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0.
  </equation*>

  For completeness, the Euclidean correlation with a unit insertion is
  constant in that insertion position. Holomorphic uniqueness on ordered time
  regions identifies its continuation with the correlation after deletion.
  Boundary values and smearing give the displayed operator identity on dense
  word matrix elements. This establishes centering on the domain, as well as
  for separated expectations.

  Write <math|p=<around|(|\<b-p\>,E|)>>, <math|p<rsup|2>=E<rsup|2>-<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|><rsup|2>>,
  and <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>=<around|{|E\<geqslant\><around|\||\<b-p\>|\|>|}>>.
  Use <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|p|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>
  in the translation spectral convention, and
  <math|\<cal-W\>*\<psi\><around|(|p|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\><around|(|y-x|)>>*\<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x*<space|0.17em>d*y>.
  Then <math|\<cal-W\>*<around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>=<wide|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\<bar\>><wide|h|^><rsub|+>>.
  Positivity, translation covariance and temperedness give a unique positive
  measure <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<Theta\>>> of polynomial growth, supported in
  <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|+>>, with

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|2>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;\<psi\>|)>>|<cell|=<big|int>\<cal-W\>*\<psi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\><rsub|\<Theta\>>,<eq-number><label|ch17:spectral-pairing>>>|<row|<cell|G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=<big|int>e<rsup|-r*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<rho\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|p|)>.<eq-number><label|ch17:laplace>>>>>
  </align>

  Here is the direct spectral identification needed for these formulas. For
  <math|v<rsub|f>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>,
  covariance gives <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>*v<rsub|f>=<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>*v<rsub|h>>
  by convolution. On a compact momentum set choose <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>>
  nonzero and define <math|d*\<rho\><rsub|\<Theta\>>=<around|\||<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\|><rsup|-2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|v<rsub|f>>>
  there. The convolution identity makes the definitions agree on overlaps.
  Polarization gives <eqref|ch17:spectral-pairing> first on product test
  functions and then on joint Schwartz test functions. Tempered continuity
  bounds compact masses polynomially. The positive cone permits exponential
  damping, and uniqueness of the holomorphic boundary value identifies its
  Laplace transform with the Euclidean function. The scalar transformation
  law of <math|\<Theta\>> makes <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<Theta\>>> Lorentz
  invariant. In particular <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> is real, nonnegative and
  nonincreasing. The expression <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>>
  need not be monotone.

  <section|Comparison at prescribed radii><label|ch17:radii>

  Let <math|U<rsub|i,d>:\<cal-M\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>
  be real comparison isomorphisms, preserving the unit, inclusions and signed
  coordinate symmetries. They are evaluated at a radius
  <math|r<rsub|i>*\<downarrow\>*0>: <math|U<rsub|i,d>*A> is inserted at
  <math|r<rsub|i>*x>. The actual inverse coupling at a finer insertion level
  <math|k<rsub|i>> is denoted by <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>>, and
  <math|b<rsub|i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k<rsub|i>>> is its reference value.
  The radius level <math|n<rsub|i>> precedes <math|k<rsub|i>>.

  The strengthened comparison, proved below, is as follows. Every prescribed
  positive null sequence has a subsequence with these data such that

  <\equation>
    \<beta\><rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>\<to\>1,<space|2em><frac|\<beta\><rsub|i>|log
    <around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)>>\<to\>\<kappa\><rsub|G>,<label|ch17:running-radius>
  </equation>

  and all fixed separated correlations and their fixed derivatives, with
  convergent moving free labels, converge to their Maxwell values. For pairs
  without derivatives this comparison is uniform over the phases, including
  at nearest lattice-rounded points. Shell integrals are included. It must
  use the same field quotient and lattice family as before. A comparison
  along one freely chosen scale sequence is weaker.

  We explain the radius coverage and the relevant quantitative demands. Write
  <math|M<rsub|n>=M<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|cut>>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log
  log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and allow

  <\equation*>
    r*<around|(|n,u|)>=a<rsub|n>*M<rsub|n><rsup|u>,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1,<space|2em>k<around|(|n|)>=min
    <around|{|m\<gtr\>n:log <around|(|a<rsub|n>/a<rsub|m>|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>|}>.
  </equation*>

  At fixed degree bound <math|D> and arity bound <math|N<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  choose comparison exponents with

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c\<less\>0,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>\<gtr\>6,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<gtr\>5-c,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*N<rsub|\<ast\>>+c\<less\>0,<space|2em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*N<rsub|\<ast\>>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c\<less\>0.>>>>
  </gather*>

  The common size of the <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>> can be increased after
  their differences and <math|c> have been fixed. The marked comparison
  required at every sufficiently late level has, uniformly in <math|u>,
  single-insertion seminorm bounds <math|C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|j>|)>*D>>
  and grid errors tending to zero. With strip width <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|c>>
  and scaled mesh at most <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>>, the two
  integration errors are bounded by

  <\equation*>
    C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*N<rsub|\<ast\>>+c>,<space|2em>C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*N<rsub|\<ast\>>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c>.
  </equation*>

  They tend to zero. Thus the strip removal and derivative-transport argument
  of Section<nbsp><reference|ch14:comparison> applies to these windows.
  Bounds uniform in the number of copies give phase uniformity by
  <eqref|ch15:moment-norm>. The next subsection verifies these window
  estimates, including their uniformity across changes of stage, from marked
  propagation.

  The coupling thresholds can be chosen to ensure
  <math|L<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>>. Since
  <math|M<rsub|n>> increases and <math|L<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|n+1>>
  eventually, <math|r*<around|(|n,\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>r*<around|(|n+1,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|)>>.
  The intervals therefore cover every sufficiently small radius. The first
  crossing places a prescribed radius in one of them. Diagonalize finite
  degree, arity, window and derivative requests, requiring
  <math|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|)>*log
  M<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>>. The overshoot in
  <math|k<around|(|n|)>> is negligible compared with
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>>, as is the logarithm of the radius
  multiplier. The running law in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch10:trajectory>
  then gives <eqref|ch17:running-radius>. This proves radius coverage. It
  remains to verify the uniform comparison estimates used in these windows.

  <subsection|Comparison windows crossing changes of
  stage><label|ch17:variable-windows>

  The finite comparison in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:n-mark-comparison>
  extends to the radius windows just described. We give the estimates because
  requiring every window to lie inside a plateau would leave gaps in radius
  coverage.

  Fix a finite degree, arity and derivative request. In this paragraph
  <math|C<rsub|j>> includes the linear marked-column constants, the
  curvature-response row constants and the finite polynomial-source
  propagation constants for this request, all fixed <em|before> the block
  ratio of stage <math|j>. Write <math|<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>> for
  the ratio chosen at stage <math|j>, so <math|L<rsub|i>=<with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>
  with <math|J<rsub|i>=J*<around|(|i+1|)>> in the scale convention of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch10:trajectory>. The ratio choices may require

  <\equation*>
    \<varepsilon\><rsub|j>\<assign\><frac|log <around|(|2+C<rsub|j>|)>|log
    <with|math-font|cal*|L><rsub|j>>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  For example one may impose <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>j<rsup|-2>>.
  Constants depending on the already selected ratio, such as nonlinear
  stationary-jet bounds, are not included here. They enter the local small
  errors and endpoint constants, and are handled by sufficiently large
  coupling thresholds. These local errors are summed after propagation; their
  post-ratio constants are never multiplied once per step. Only a bounded
  number of endpoint factors is multiplied, giving
  <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. This distinction avoids a circular
  ratio choice.

  Put <math|H<rsub|n>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. For <math|b> in a fixed compact
  interval of sufficiently large positive numbers, stop backwards from
  <math|n> at the first crossing of <math|<around|(|b+2|)>*log H<rsub|n>>,
  and then at the first crossing of another <math|10*log B<rsub|n>>. Here
  <math|B<rsub|n>> is the radius of the restarted representation; <math|log
  B<rsub|n>=O<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)>>. The schedule
  bound <math|L<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>> makes each
  overshoot <math|O<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)>=o<around|(|log
  H<rsub|n>|)>>. The entire logarithmic window has length
  <math|O<around|(|log H<rsub|n>|)>>, and the number of steps is
  <math|O<around|(|log H<rsub|n>|)>> since every
  <math|L<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3>. It is therefore shorter than
  <math|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|4>>, as required
  by Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:restart-family>. All its levels tend to
  infinity. The actual increment estimate is a constant times the logarithmic
  block length plus the coupling-threshold-controlled local drift error.
  Summing it over this window gives

  <\equation*>
    max<rsub|i><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|i>/\<beta\><rsub|n>-1|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>*<around|(|1+log
    H<rsub|n>|)>|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>>.
  </equation*>

  In particular coupling changes are smaller than every fixed inverse power
  of <math|H<rsub|n>>, not just qualitatively <math|o<around|(|1|)>>. A
  window may cross any number of stages; no bound by two stages is needed.

  For the linear propagation constants the exact bound is

  <\equation>
    <label|ch17:window-product>log <big|prod><rsub|i<text| in
    window>><around|(|2+C<rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|i<text| in
    window>> \<varepsilon\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>*<big|sum><rsub|i<text| in
    window>>log L<rsub|i>=o<around|(|log H<rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation>

  Thus their total cost is <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. In
  particular the differentiated response estimate in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> becomes

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|r+2>*<big|sum><rsub|y><around|\||\<Delta\><rsup|r>*<around|(|\<Psi\><rsub|n-1>*\<cdots\>*\<Psi\><rsub|\<ell\>>|)><around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|d>*<big|prod><rsub|i=\<ell\>><rsup|n-1>R<rsub|J<rsub|i>>=H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em>S=a<rsub|\<ell\>>/a<rsub|n>.
  </equation*>

  The actual covariance decomposition telescopes for these unequal responses
  just as it does for equal ratios: each conditional covariance is
  transported by the product of the subsequent actual responses. It uses no
  replacement by a common stage's quadratic form. The finite-cutoff
  covariance estimate <eqref|ch11:finite-covariance> is uniform in the actual
  free history. Its stage-dependent constants, protected-path errors,
  fixed-ratio jet errors and finite incidence costs are made smaller than the
  requested powers of <math|H<rsub|n>> by choosing sufficiently large
  coupling thresholds. Sums over <math|O<around|(|log H<rsub|n>|)>> steps
  lose only <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. At each step nonlinear
  propagation is the stated pre-ratio linear map plus an
  coupling-threshold-controlled small error. Thus the post-ratio constants in
  the nonlinear comparison occur additively in those local errors, not in the
  repeated linear amplification. The regular and large-field source
  recurrences therefore retain their strict negative powers after the
  amplification <eqref|ch17:window-product>.

  One can now repeat the proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch11:n-mark-comparison>
  with unequal ratios. The initial Taylor order and support reserves are
  chosen for the largest <math|b> and the fixed finite request. The entire
  incoming squarefree polynomial is retained. The actual quadratic covariance
  and corrected free-subset polynomial are common to all subsets; the empty
  coefficient, and hence the denominator, is unchanged. Weight restoration
  still uses <math|\<omega\>=H<rsub|n><rsup|1/<around|(|8*N<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>>
  and <math|I=log H<rsub|n>/<around|(|16*N<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>. Every
  additional propagation loss is <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>; the
  margins in the <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|-3/2>> moment error,
  <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|-31/32>> connected error and <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|-1>>
  regular-restoration bounds absorb it. The root estimate is therefore at
  most <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|-c<rsub|\<ast\>>>> for a fixed
  <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> depending only on the finite arity, for
  example <math|c<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/<around|(|32*N<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>> after
  increasing the coupling threshold. Its cell side is at most
  <math|a<rsub|n>*H<rsub|n><rsup|b+5>>. The positive partition estimate is
  imposed at the actual bottom stage, with its constant at most <math|I>; it
  is not multiplied once per step.

  The top polynomial extraction <math|T> depends only on the actual insertion
  and its original self covariance. It is independent of the middle and
  output stopping levels. Restart on the longest window and restrict that
  history for the shorter ones. This supplies the same <math|T> for all
  <math|b> in the compact interval. The finite number of coefficient bounds
  above is uniform in <math|b>: changing <math|b> only changes stopping
  integers and the scaling dilation <math|W<rsub|H<rsub|n><rsup|b>>>, whose
  exponents lie in a fixed compact interval. All upper bounds used the
  longest window and the largest dilation cost. No compactness over arbitrary
  incoming activity arrays is invoked.

  Apply this construction to <math|b=\<alpha\><rsub|j>+u-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>,
  <math|j=0,1,2>, and <math|u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>>.
  At its own normalization a single insertion has bounded chessboard
  seminorm. Changing from <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>> to
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>> costs at most <math|C*H<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|j>|)>*D>>
  by the exact scaling dilation on the common top extraction. These are
  precisely the size bounds used in the two strip and mesh estimates above.
  Their exponents are strictly negative, so the harmless
  <math|H<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>> losses do not change the conclusion.
  The full-copy estimates give the same pair bounds in every phase. The
  existing argument using separated correlation pairings then gives fixed
  derivatives and shell fluxes with these same frames. Thus the
  prescribed-radius comparison used in this chapter follows from the actual
  marked construction, including windows that cross changes of stage.

  <section|Tensor coordinates and the regulated current><label|ch17:current>

  Use the free scalar and tensor

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-O\>=<tfrac|1|4><big|sum><rsub|a,b,\<alpha\>>:<around|(|F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>|)><rsup|2>:,<space|1em>L=T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>,<space|1em>D<rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*\<cal-O\>,<space|1em>X<rsub|a*b>=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|c,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|b*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>:.
  </equation*>

  The coordinate sum in <math|\<cal-O\>> is over ordered pairs. The symbol
  <math|X> here denotes the off-diagonal tensor, not a polymer support. These
  three tensors span the centered degree-four symmetric hypercubic tensors.
  Before imposing the Maxwell equation, their vector divergences have
  coordinates in <math|E=<RR><rsup|3>>, represented by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|c>F<rsub|b*c>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*c>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a,c>F<rsub|a*c>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|a*c>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a,c>F<rsub|b*c>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*c>,
  </equation*>

  with colour contraction and Wick ordering understood. The free quotient
  <math|s:E\<to\>V> has kernel spanned by <math|v=<around|(|0,0,1|)>>, and
  <math|V> is the two-dimensional vector space of
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch14:stress-line>. For a tensor with diagonal
  entries <math|z<rsub|0>*<big|sum><rsub|c>F<rsub|a*c><rsup|2>+z<rsub|1>*<big|sum><rsub|b,c>F<rsub|b*c><rsup|2>>
  and off-diagonal entries <math|z<rsub|2>*<big|sum><rsub|c>F<rsub|a*c>*F<rsub|b*c>>,
  Bianchi gives divergence coordinates <math|<around|(|2*<around|(|z<rsub|0>-z<rsub|2>|)>,2*z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>/2,z<rsub|2>|)>>.
  Hence

  <\equation>
    \<rho\>*<around|(|x,y,z|)>=-2*x-8*y,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>B|)>*\<cal-O\>=-<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>B.<label|ch17:rho>
  </equation>

  In particular <math|\<rho\><around|(|v|)>=0> and the values for
  <math|L,X,D> are <math|0,0,-4>.

  At a lattice site set <math|f<rsub|a*b>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<gLie>><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\><around|(|a,b|)>>-U<rsub|\<square\><around|(|a,b|)>><rsup|-1>|)>/2>
  and start with

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|a*b>=<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|c*d>,f<rsub|c*d>|\<rangle\>>-<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|a*c>,f<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><around|(|Y<rsub|b><rsup|x>|)><rsub|e>=\<b-1\><rsub|e<rsup|->=x>*f<rsub|b,e<rsup|<math-up|dir>>><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|Y> is a left link displacement. If <math|<math-ss|S>> is the
  leading path action without its coupling, define its current defect by
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>>,
  using

  <\equation*>
    \<nabla\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsub|j>*<around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|j>-<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|-j>|)>,<space|2em><around|(|c<rsub|1>,c<rsub|2>,c<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>|)>=<around|(|4/5,-1/5,4/105,-1/280|)>.
  </equation*>

  The operator <math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a>> shifts by one site in direction
  <math|a>. Initially the trace and antisymmetric part of <math|J> vanish.
  During transfer they must remain exact forward differences; smallness of
  their values alone would not suffice for the trace rate below.

  We fix the coefficient functionals in that calculation. For a local scalar
  <math|F>, its Hessian Fourier kernel is

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>>*<around|(|p,q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x,y>e<rsup|i*<around|(|p\<cdot\>x+q\<cdot\>y|)>>*D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|x,c,\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|y,c,\<alpha\>>|]>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|e<rsub|x,c,\<alpha\>>> is the link-coordinate basis vector. Fix
  one colour <math|\<alpha\>>. For a vector <math|F<rsub|b>> and a scalar
  action <math|F> put

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|l><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c,b>i*<around|[|z<rsup|2>*t|]><wide|F<rsub|b>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>+t*e<rsub|b>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|q><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]>*<wide|F|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>>*<around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|h><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<math-ss|l><around|(|F|)>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]><wide|F<rsub|c>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Brackets denote Taylor coefficients including reciprocal factorials. The
  volume normalization in <math|<math-ss|q>> is for the translation-invariant
  action; <math|<math-ss|l>> evaluates the anchored vector response. Define
  <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=<math-ss|h><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y|)>>.
  The second term in <math|<math-ss|h>> corrects the lattice derivative. The
  regulated current calculation uses

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<tfrac|1|2>log det
    <around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>-log det
    <around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>,<space|1em>R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>=<math-ss|q><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-D\><around|(|\<mu\>|)>=-\<mu\>*R<rprime|'><around|(|\<mu\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|H> is the left Hessian of <math|<math-ss|S>>, <math|d> its
  infinitesimal gauge insertion, and <math|<math-ss|q>> is the coefficient
  functional just defined. Its normalization counts twelve ordered transverse
  planes. The actual Ward calculation requires its Haar coefficient
  <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>> to obey

  <\equation>
    \<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=\<cal-D\><around|(|\<mu\>|)>+O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>+o<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>=12*\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    \<mu\>+O<around|(|1|)>.<label|ch17:regulator-input>
  </equation>

  The <math|o<around|(|1|)>> is uniform on each fixed interval
  <math|<around|[|\<delta\>,1|]>> as the current defect tends to zero and the
  period tends to infinity; the constant in <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>> is
  independent of <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. This identity concerns the
  actual gauge-fixed Hessian and transferred current. The perturbative
  anomaly citation does not supply it.

  The next section derives this regulated identity from explicit weighted
  current bounds and convergence of the matched current jets. It keeps the
  gauge-coordinate corrections and the lattice coefficient correction. The
  following current-contraction section then verifies these hypotheses for
  repeated stationary transfer.

  <section|Exact current transport and the scalar
  regulator><label|ch17:current-suppliers>

  Several ingredients of the trace transfer can be established before its
  quantitative current estimates. We give them separately, since they use the
  actual difference operator and the actual path action. They do not identify
  a lattice coefficient by comparison with a separately chosen continuum
  action.

  <subsection|Difference-preserving block averages>

  Let <math|L> be a block ratio and <math|s<rsub|a>> a unit fine translation.
  Write <math|\<delta\><rsub|a>=s<rsub|a>-1>,
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>=s<rsub|a><rsup|L>-1>, and let
  <math|\<nabla\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsub|j>*<around|(|s<rsub|a><rsup|j>-s<rsub|a><rsup|-j>|)>>
  be the eighth-order centered difference used above. Its coefficients
  satisfy <math|2*<big|sum><rsub|j>j*c<rsub|j>=1>. Define finite Laurent
  polynomials

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsub|a>>|<cell|=L<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|L-1>s<rsub|a><rsup|v>,>|<cell|G<rsub|a>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsub|j>*<big|sum><rsub|v=-j><rsup|j-1>s<rsub|a><rsup|v>,>>|<row|<cell|N<rsub|a>>|<cell|=G<rsub|a><around|(|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>|)>*M<rsub|a>,>|<cell|K<rsub|0>>|<cell|=<big|prod><rsub|a>M<rsub|a>*N<rsub|a>,>>|<row|<cell|K<rsub|a>>|<cell|=G<rsub|a>*M<rsub|a>*<big|prod><rsub|c\<ne\>a>M<rsub|c>*N<rsub|c>,>|<cell|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|e>>>|<cell|=N<rsub|a>*<big|prod><rsub|c\<ne\>a>M<rsub|c>*N<rsub|c>.>>>>
  </align*>

  All these operators act on the fine anchor of a local function before
  stationary substitution. If <math|\<cal-I\>*F=F<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>|)>>,
  coarse translation of <math|\<cal-I\>*F> is
  <math|\<cal-I\>*<around|(|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>*F|)>>; fine unit shifts are not
  silently identified with coarse shifts.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exact block identities><label|ch17:block-identities>There is a
    finite Laurent polynomial <math|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>> such that

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|K<rsub|0>*\<nabla\><rsub|a>>|<cell|=L<rsup|-1>*\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*K<rsub|a>,>|<cell|K<rsub|0>*\<delta\><rsub|a>>|<cell|=L<rsup|-1>*\<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|e>>,>|<cell|K<rsub|a>-K<rsub|0>>|<cell|=\<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>.<eq-number><label|ch17:block-algebra>>>>>
    </align>

    The kernels have support of radius <math|C*L> and absolute coefficient
    sums bounded by a numerical constant independent of <math|L>; their
    masses are one except that no mass is prescribed for
    <math|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The identities <math|G<rsub|a>*\<delta\><rsub|a>=\<nabla\><rsub|a>> and
    <math|M<rsub|a>*\<delta\><rsub|a>=L<rsup|-1>*\<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>>
    prove the first two assertions by commutation of Laurent polynomials. For
    the third, work in the quotient ring with <math|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>=1>.
    There <math|M<rsub|a><rsup|2>=M<rsub|a>>,
    <math|M<rsub|a>*G<rsub|a>=G<rsub|a><around|(|1|)>*M<rsub|a>=M<rsub|a>>
    and <math|G<rsub|a><around|(|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>|)>=G<rsub|a><around|(|1|)>=1>.
    Thus <math|M<rsub|a>*<around|(|G<rsub|a>-G<rsub|a><around|(|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>|)>*M<rsub|a>|)>>
    is zero in that quotient, which is precisely divisibility of
    <math|K<rsub|a>-K<rsub|0>> by <math|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>-1>. The original
    kernels have finite products of a fixed number of bounded coefficient-sum
    factors, each of range <math|C*L>. For the quotient, group coefficients
    in each residue class modulo <math|L>. Its numerator has zero sum in each
    class, and only a bounded number of nonzero entries in that class.
    Division by <math|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>-1> is a partial sum along this finite
    sequence, so increases its absolute sum by only a bounded factor. This
    also proves the support bound for <math|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>>.
  </proof>

  For a fine current with <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>,
  set

  <\equation*>
    J<rprime|'><rsub|a*b>=L<rsup|4>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>,<space|2em>q<rprime|'><rsub|a>=L<rsup|3>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|e>>*q<rsub|a>|)>+L<rsup|4>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>*J<rsub|a*a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch17:block-algebra> proves exactly
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J<rprime|'>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*q<rprime|'><rsub|a>>.
  Indeed split <math|K<rsub|a>=K<rsub|0>+\<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>>
  and then apply the second identity to <math|K<rsub|0>*\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>.
  Similarly, if <math|J<rsub|a*b>-J<rsub|b*a>=<big|sum><rsub|c>\<delta\><rsub|c>*t<rsub|a*b*c>>,
  the transferred antisymmetric part is the sum of the transferred old
  differences and

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*L<rsup|4>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>-\<delta\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|c>>*L<rsup|4>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|t>>*J<rsub|b*a>|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular symmetry may fail pointwise after blocking, but its failure
  and the trace remain exact differences. This conclusion is independent of
  estimates on the current's Taylor coefficients.

  <subsection|What scalar mass regularization changes>

  Consider the same admissible path action used in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>, with its left Hessian <math|H> and
  infinitesimal gauge insertion <math|d>. At the zero field the completed
  vector operator is <math|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>=<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*I+O<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|3>|)>>,
  and the ghost operator is <math|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d=<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*I+O<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|4>|)>>,
  with the global lattice ellipticity stated there. Define
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>> with the scalar mass <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*I>
  as above. The normalized quadratic response of
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>> has the same logarithmic coefficient as the
  regulated response in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. This fact
  concerns its coefficient integral, before any relation to the Haar
  divergence of a current.

  To see this directly, retain the actual vector and ghost first and second
  background insertions <math|b,c> and replace only their denominators by the
  scalar-mass denominators. In each channel the logarithmic Hessian is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|tr><around|{|a<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)><rsup|-1>*c*<around|(|-p,p;k|)>-a<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)><rsup|-1>*b*<around|(|-p;k+p|)>*a<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|k+p|)><rsup|-1>*b*<around|(|p;k|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The scalar mass has no background insertion. The actual local Taylor
  expansion and vertex estimates of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>
  therefore apply without the regulator-vertex terms. With
  <math|T=<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>>, the inverse and its <math|r> momentum
  derivatives cost <math|C*T<rsup|-2-r>>, for every fixed required <math|r>.
  Differentiate <math|R*a=I> repeatedly to obtain this bound; the denominator
  floor is now simply <math|a<rsub|\<mu\>>\<geqslant\>c*T<rsup|2>*I>. Two
  external derivatives of the displayed expression cost at most
  <math|C*T<rsup|-4>>. For <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1>,
  comparison with the massless continuum vertices by the resolvent identity
  costs

  <\equation*>
    C<around*|(|<around|\||k*<around|\||<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|\|>*k|\|><rsup|-6>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The first term is the actual lattice Taylor remainder, and the second is
  the scalar-mass denominator difference. Both have bounded integrals in four
  dimensions, uniformly in <math|\<mu\>>. The region
  <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<less\>\<mu\>> also has bounded integral, since its
  volume is <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|4>|)>> and its integrand is
  <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|-4>|)>>. Away from zero the integral is
  uniformly bounded. Thus the continuum homogeneous term alone determines the
  logarithm. With the twelve-plane coefficient convention of this chapter
  this gives

  <\equation>
    R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>=12*\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    \<mu\>+O<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1.<label|ch17:scalar-log-supplier>
  </equation>

  This is the same one-loop normalization already calculated using the actual
  action in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. At fixed
  <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> the integrand is periodic analytic on a common
  smaller strip, so its torus sums converge to this integral; no uniform rate
  as <math|\<mu\>*\<downarrow\>*0> is required. This proof does not
  differentiate the bounded remainder.

  <subsection|Exact integration by parts and the remaining current estimate>

  For a smooth vector field <math|Y> on a finite product of compact link
  groups, Haar integration gives the exact identity

  <\equation>
    <big|int><around*|[|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y-D*\<cal-A\><around|[|Y|]>|]>*e<rsup|-\<cal-A\>>*<space|0.17em>d*U=0.<label|ch17:finite-haar>
  </equation>

  It follows by integrating the divergence of <math|e<rsup|-\<cal-A\>>*Y>.
  With a spectator <math|F> the right side becomes
  <math|-<big|int>D*F<around|[|Y|]>*e<rsup|-\<cal-A\>>*<space|0.17em>d*U>. In
  particular it is zero when <math|Y> acts only on links disjoint from those
  of <math|F>. Smooth truncations can be inserted before this identity is
  used; their differentiated terms must be retained as marked remainders.

  There is also an exact cancellation available for the inherited quadratic
  loop term. Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching> represents its Hessian as
  a sum with two lattice differences on each leg. Consequently it annihilates
  constant and affine polynomial link fields. The degree-three external
  momentum coefficient in its variation by the leading current pairs a
  constant leg with a quadratic leg, or two affine legs; both vanish. This
  proves the zero trace-functional coefficient of that quadratic loop
  contribution. It does not bound the nonlinear current remainder.

  The missing quantitative step is now quite specific. One needs the actual
  stationary pullback of the current defect, with the gauge-covariant block
  average frame correction, in the exponentially weighted order-two, -three
  and -four coefficient norms. In particular the order-two pullback must
  annihilate its affine\Uaffine and affine\Uquadratic polynomial moment
  conditions <em|before> estimating the remainder. The usual smooth transport
  bound <math|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|a>=O<around|(|L<rsup|-1-r>|)>>
  alone does not assert these cancellations. After gauge removal the relevant
  affine field costs <math|L<rsup|-2>> and its quadratic term
  <math|L<rsup|-3>>; multiplication by the current rescaling <math|L<rsup|5>>
  would leave the unacceptable factors <math|L> and <math|1> from their two
  products. Only removal of these exact terms leaves the <math|L<rsup|-1>>
  remainder used in the proposed contraction. Fourth-order action matching
  must therefore be combined with an explicit identity for the current's
  matched jets, not merely with a bound on the action's matching error.

  The group-coordinate identity needed for this comparison is proved next.
  Its proof is independent of the contraction of the actual current sequence,
  established in Section<nbsp><reference|ch17:quadratic-current>.

  <subsection|The group-coordinate contact
  identity><label|ch17:contact-section>

  Fix a finite lattice and a background <math|U> in the product of link
  groups. Use the invariant inner product on its link Lie algebra and the
  chart <math|z\<mapsto\>e<rsup|z>*U> (edge by edge). Let <math|S> be a
  smooth gauge-invariant action, <math|H=D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>\|<rsub|z=0>>
  its symmetric left-coordinate Hessian, and <math|d> the infinitesimal gauge
  map at <math|U>. The displacement <math|Y> is assumed gauge covariant, so
  its local flow <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|s*Y<around|(|U|)>>*U>
  commutes with gauge transformations. It need not be the exact time-<math|s>
  flow; all following identities use only its first derivative at zero.

  Define the chart velocity and its linear part by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|z|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|\<partial\><rsub|s>|\|><rsub|0>*log
    <around*|(|\<Psi\><rsub|s>*<around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>*\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>V=D<rsub|z>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)>=0>. This definition
  includes the change of the left frame as the base point moves. Replacing
  <math|V> by an ordinary derivative of the coefficient function <math|Y>
  would omit that change. Let

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|U>*<around|(|v,z|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|\<partial\><rsub|s>*\<partial\><rsub|t>|\|><rsub|0>*log
    <around*|(|<math-up|gauge><rsub|e<rsup|s*v>><around|(|e<rsup|t*z>*U|)>*U<rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>N<rsub|i*\<alpha\>>=D*S<around|(|U|)>*<around|[|J<rsub|U>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|i>|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  Indices <math|i> run through link coordinates and <math|\<alpha\>> through
  gauge coordinates. Differentiating gauge invariance first in <math|v>, then
  in <math|z>, gives <math|H*d=-N>.

  For <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> at a background where the following inverses
  exist, write

  <\equation*>
    R=<around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>G=<around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\>=<tfrac|1|2>log det <around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>-log
    det <around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>.
  </equation*>

  Near the zero background the positive denominators of the preceding
  subsection make these ordinary real analytic functions. Define a link
  vector <math|Z> by its action against an arbitrary covector <math|\<ell\>>:

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<ell\><around|(|Z|)>=>|<cell|<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|i,j>R<rsub|i*j>*<space|0.17em>\<ell\>*<around*|(|D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|i>,e<rsub|j>|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|-<big|sum><rsub|i,\<alpha\>><around|[|G*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|V+V<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*R|]><rsub|\<alpha\>*i>*<space|0.17em>\<ell\>*<around|(|J<rsub|U>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|i>|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|ch17:contact-Z>>>>>
  </align>

  All matrices and derivatives here belong to the same background and
  coordinate chart.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Exact contact identity><label|ch17:contact-identity>With these
    definitions,

    <\equation>
      <tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around*|(|R*D<rsup|2>*<around|(|D*S<around|[|Y|]>|)>|)>-<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y=D*\<Gamma\><around|[|Y|]>-\<mu\><rsup|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*V|)>+D*S<around|[|Z|]>.<label|ch17:contact>
    </equation>

    No stationarity of <math|U> is assumed. In particular the
    <math|D*S<around|[|Z|]>> term cannot be discarded before applying the
    relevant coefficient functional.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let a dot denote differentiation along
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)>>, using the left chart anew at that
    base point. The chain rule for <math|S*<around|(|\<Psi\><rsub|s>*<around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>|)>>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      D<rsup|2>*<around|(|D*S<around|[|Y|]>|)>=<wide|H|\<dot\>>+V<rsup|\<ast\>>*H+H*V+C,<space|2em>C<rsub|i*j>=D*S*<around|[|D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|i>,e<rsub|j>|]>|]>.
    </equation*>

    This is an exact chart calculation, not a replacement of the group by its
    tangent space. The differential of <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s>> in these moving
    charts is <math|I+s*V+o<around|(|s|)>>. Exponential charts have Haar
    density one at their origins, so its Jacobian at the base point has
    derivative <math|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y=<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>V>.

    Gauge equivariance of <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s>> gives
    <math|<wide|d|\<dot\>>=V*d>: differentiate
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|<math-up|gauge><rsub|e<rsup|v>><space|0.17em>U|)>=<math-up|gauge><rsub|e<rsup|v>><space|0.17em>\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)>>
    first in <math|v> and then in <math|s>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      D*\<Gamma\><around|[|Y|]>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*<wide|H|\<dot\>>|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*R*V|)>-<math-up|tr><around*|(|d*G*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|V+V<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    On the other hand the Hessian chain rule and
    <math|R*H=I-R*d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>-\<mu\><rsup|2>*R> yield

    <\equation*>
      <tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*D<rsup|2>*<around|(|D*S<around|[|Y|]>|)>|)>-<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>V=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*<wide|H|\<dot\>>|)>-<math-up|tr><around|(|R*d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*V|)>-\<mu\><rsup|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*V|)>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*C|)>.
    </equation*>

    Finally <math|H*d=-N> implies the exact resolvent identity

    <\equation*>
      R*d=d*G+R*N*G.
    </equation*>

    Subtract the two preceding displays. The leftover gauge terms are

    <\equation*>
      -<math-up|tr><around*|(|<around|(|R*d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*R-2*d*G*d<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*V|)>=-<math-up|tr><around*|(|G*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|V+V<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*R*N|)>.
    </equation*>

    Inserting the definition of <math|N> and the term
    <math|<frac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|R*C|)>> identifies their sum with
    <math|D*S<around|[|Z|]>> from <eqref|ch17:contact-Z>. This proves
    <eqref|ch17:contact>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Cancellation of the action-gradient terms>

  The functional <math|<math-ss|l>> above extracts the coefficient of
  <math|z<rsup|2>*t> from the diagonal polarization pair at momenta
  <math|p=z*e<rsub|a>>, <math|q=-z*e<rsub|a>+t*e<rsub|b>>, and sums over
  <math|a\<ne\>c> and <math|b>. We check why it kills the action variations
  in <eqref|ch17:contact>. Work at fixed positive <math|\<mu\>> so all
  kernels are analytic in momentum near zero. A gauge-invariant, translation-
  and hypercubic-invariant scalar action <math|F> has quadratic kernel
  <math|c<rsub|F>*<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I-p\<otimes\>p|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|3>|)>>
  by the Ward calculation in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. This
  applies to both <math|S> and <math|\<Gamma\>>. Let <math|Z<rsub|b>> be any
  anchored gauge-covariant displacement, including <math|Y<rsub|b>> or the
  displacement <eqref|ch17:contact-Z>. Simplicity of the gauge algebra
  implies <math|Z<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>=0>. The linearized covariance
  identity says <math|D*Z<rsub|b><around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|0>=0>.

  Use <math|i> for its output link direction and <math|c> for its input
  polarization. Its joint momentum symbol, with output dual variable <math|p>
  and input variable <math|q>, therefore has expansion

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|D*Z<rsub|b>|)><rsub|i*c><around|(|p,q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j>q<rsub|j>*A<rsub|b,i*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>j>+O<around|(|<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>+<around|\||q|\|>|)><rsup|2>|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|b,i*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>j>=-A<rsub|b,i*j><rsup|<space|0.17em>c>.
  </equation*>

  The factors <math|i> from Fourier differentiation may be absorbed into
  <math|A>. There is no term depending only on <math|p>: the identity
  obtained by multiplying by the gradient symbol in <math|q> forces it to
  vanish. For <math|a\<ne\>c>, the coefficient of <math|z<rsup|2>*t> in the
  quadratic variation <math|D*F<around|[|Z<rsub|b>|]>> is, apart from the
  common factor <math|c<rsub|F>>,

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|b,c*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>b>-2*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*A<rsub|b,c*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>a>+\<delta\><rsub|b*c>*A<rsub|b,a*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>a>.
  </equation*>

  This follows by inserting the two kinetic kernels on its two legs. The
  first sum over <math|a\<ne\>c> gives <math|3*<big|sum><rsub|b,c>A<rsub|b,c*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>b>>.
  The second gives minus twice that sum, since
  <math|A<rsub|c,c*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>c>=0>. The last gives minus the same
  sum after using <math|A<rsub|c,a*c><rsup|<space|0.17em>a>=-A<rsub|c,a*a><rsup|<space|0.17em>c>>
  and exchanging <math|a,c>. Thus

  <\equation>
    <math-ss|l><around|(|D*F<around|[|Z|]>|)>=0.<label|ch17:gradient-cancellation>
  </equation>

  Higher momentum orders cannot contribute to the coefficient of degree
  three. Gauge covariance of <eqref|ch17:contact-Z> follows by transforming
  its chart tensors, inverse operators and contracted indices together. The
  mass <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*I> is unchanged by these orthogonal gauge frame
  changes. The calculation therefore applies to both action-gradient terms of
  the exact contact identity. It is a finite-momentum cancellation, not a
  statement that the corresponding functions vanish pointwise.

  <subsection|Power counting the regulator remainder>

  The remaining estimate has a transparent sufficient numerator condition.
  Let <math|W<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,W<rsup|<around|(|0|)>>> be the vector and
  ghost remainder operators obtained by subtracting the leading curvature
  displacement from <math|V>; in the notation of the current calculation
  these are

  <\equation*>
    W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>=<math-up|sym><around|(|V<rsub|b>-d*M<rsub|b>-D<rsub|b>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em>W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=<math-up|sym><around|(|M<rsub|b>*d|)>-<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<chi\>> is the indicator of the anchored lattice site,
  <math|<around|(|M<rsub|b>*h|)><around|(|x|)>=\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>>,
  <math|<around|(|D<rsub|b>*h|)><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>>,
  and <math|<around|(|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|r|)>>*h|)><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<around|(|x|)>>
  on <math|r>-forms. The full lattice shifts and chart tensors must be
  retained when extracting their coefficient; their continuum symbols only
  identify the leading terms that cancel.

  For that leading calculation the gauge derivative is <math|d=-\<partial\>>
  and the curvature displacement is <math|<around|(|V<rsub|b>*h|)><rsub|a>=\<chi\>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b>*h<rsub|a>-\<partial\><rsub|a>*h<rsub|b>|)>>.
  Then <math|d*M<rsub|b>+D<rsub|b>=-\<chi\>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*h<rsub|b>>
  and <math|<math-up|sym><around|(|\<chi\>*\<partial\><rsub|b>|)>=-<frac|1|2>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<chi\>>.
  Thus both <math|W<rsup|<around|(|r|)>>> have zero first-order principal
  symbol. Covariantizing these expressions gives the same cancellation at
  constant background to first field order: the skew-adjoint multiplication
  by <math|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>A<rsub|b>> has zero symmetric part, and
  the remaining <math|<math-up|ad><space|0.17em>A<rsub|a>*h<rsub|b>> term
  cancels the corresponding term of <math|d*M<rsub|b>>. Accordingly their
  zero-, one- and two-field symbols have orders <math|2,1,0>, respectively. A
  lattice/current sequence having the stated matched free jets inherits
  precisely these vanishings in its limit. Weighted coefficient bounds
  control the Taylor remainders.

  Here is the resulting estimate without suppressed differentiation orders.
  Set <math|z=<around|\||q|\|>+\<mu\>>. For <math|n\<leqslant\>2> background
  derivatives and <math|r\<leqslant\>3> external momentum derivatives the
  inverse estimates are <math|C*z<rsup|-2-n-r>>. The remainder symbols have
  bounds <math|C*z<rsup|2-n-r>>. The quadratic-field coefficient of
  <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|R*W<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|G*W<rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>>,
  after the three momentum derivatives defining <math|<math-ss|l>>, is
  therefore bounded by a sum of terms

  <\equation*>
    C*z<rsup|-2-n-r>*<space|0.17em>z<rsup|2-<around|(|2-n|)>-<around|(|3-r|)>>=C*z<rsup|-5>.
  </equation*>

  In four dimensions <math|<big|int><rsub|<around|\||q|\|>\<leqslant\>1><around|(|<around|\||q|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-5>*<space|0.17em>d*q\<leqslant\>C/\<mu\>>.
  The region away from zero is bounded. Hence the regulator factor
  <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> gives a remainder at most <math|C*\<mu\>>. For
  approximate matched jets, first fix <math|\<mu\>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>;
  their errors then tend to zero uniformly on that regulator interval. Only
  afterwards send <math|\<delta\>> to zero. This is the order required by the
  logarithmic mean-value argument.

  <subsection|The corrected coefficient and order of
  limits><label|ch17:regulator-completion>

  We can retain the lattice correction in <math|<math-ss|h>> throughout,
  avoiding an unsupported claim that it vanishes separately. Write
  <math|<math-ss|c><around|(|F|)>=<frac|1|2><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]><wide|F<rsub|c>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)>>,
  so <math|<math-ss|h>=<math-ss|l>-<math-ss|c>>. The quadratic variation
  <math|D*F<around|[|Z|]>> in the preceding calculation starts at momentum
  degree three. Thus <math|<math-ss|c><around|(|D*F<around|[|Z|]>|)>=0>, in
  addition to <eqref|ch17:gradient-cancellation>. Both functionals therefore
  annihilate the action-gradient terms of <eqref|ch17:contact>.

  Suppose now that the actual current sequence has the following properties
  at the fixed output level: uniform exponentially weighted order-two through
  order-four coefficient bounds; convergence of the needed low polynomial
  jets to the curvature current; the exact trace and antisymmetry difference
  identities; and defects tending to zero through order four in those
  weighted norms in

  <\equation*>
    D*S<around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>\<equiv\><rsub|4><big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>.
  </equation*>

  The notation <math|\<equiv\><rsub|4>> refers to equality of field
  derivatives through order four at the zero background. It suffices because
  the contact expression uses two internal and two external field
  derivatives. At fixed <math|\<mu\>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
  contracted defect errors tend to zero. Translation covariance makes
  contraction by the inverse <math|R> commute with translating the anchor.
  The <math|<math-ss|l>> coefficient of the contracted current divergence
  selects <math|a=b> at its sole linear total-momentum derivative. The sum is
  the contracted trace of <math|J>, which has a further total-momentum zero
  because it is a forward difference. Its coefficient is zero. For
  <math|<math-ss|c>>, the total momentum is zero from the outset and the
  divergence vanishes directly. The exact contact identity consequently
  yields

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2><math-ss|h><around*|(|<math-up|tr><around|(|R*V<rsub|b>|)>|)>+o<around|(|1|)>,
  </equation*>

  uniformly on every fixed positive regulator interval. All operators here
  are functions of the background before the two field derivatives are
  extracted.

  The definitions of <math|W<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,W<rsup|<around|(|0|)>>>
  give an exact trace decomposition. Use <math|R*d=d*G+R*N*G> to obtain

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|tr><around|(|R*V<rsub|b>|)>=>|<cell|<math-up|tr><around*|(|R*<around|(|D<rsub|b>-<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|G*T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>+D*S<around|[|Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>|]>+<math-up|Rem><rsub|b>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|Rem><rsub|b>=>|<cell|<math-up|tr><around|(|R*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|G*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\><around|(|Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>|)>=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i,\<alpha\>><around|(|G*M<rsub|b>*R|)><rsub|\<alpha\>*i>*\<ell\>*<around|(|J<rsub|U>*<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|i>|)>|)>.>>>>
  </align*>

  For example, the part containing <math|d*M<rsub|b>> becomes
  <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|G*M<rsub|b>*d|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|R*N*G*M<rsub|b>|)>>;
  its symmetric ghost part is precisely <math|<frac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|G*T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|G*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>>,
  and its remaining term is <math|D*S<around|[|Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>|]>>. This
  also fixes the sign of the gauge correction.

  For the three displayed principal terms the Fourier transform of
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\>> supplies
  <math|e<rsup|-i*t>-1=-i*t+O<around|(|t<rsup|2>|)>> at total momentum
  <math|t*e<rsub|b>>. With the factor <math|i> in <math|<math-ss|l>>, the
  <math|D<rsub|b>> terms sum to <math|<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R|)>>:
  the selected derivative has <math|a=b> and the component trace then sums
  its diagonal entries. Each of the four <math|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>>
  terms gives <math|<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R|)>>, and
  each <math|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>> term gives
  <math|<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>G|)>>. The volume
  normalization is exactly the translation average of the single anchor.
  Their total is therefore

  <\equation*>
    -<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>R|)>+2<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>G|)>.
  </equation*>

  Their <math|<math-ss|c>> coefficients are zero, since
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\>> has zero symbol at total momentum zero.
  Multiplication by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> gives
  <math|-\<mu\>*\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>><math-ss|q><around|(|\<Gamma\>|)>> by
  differentiation of the two determinants. Thus, with the lattice correction
  fully retained,

  <\equation>
    \<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2><math-ss|h><around|(|<math-up|Rem>|)>+o<around|(|1|)>.<label|ch17:corrected-regulated-ward>
  </equation>

  The action-gradient term <math|D*S<around|[|Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>|]>> is
  annihilated by both parts of <math|<math-ss|h>> as proved above.

  The <math|<math-ss|l>> remainder was bounded by <math|C*\<mu\>> after
  multiplication by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>. The correction <math|<math-ss|c>>
  uses only two external momentum derivatives, so the same product rule costs
  <math|C*z<rsup|-4>>. Its integral is at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+<around|\||log \<mu\>|\|>|)>> in four dimensions, and
  <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|1+<around|\||log
  \<mu\>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\>> for <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>.
  Consequently <eqref|ch17:corrected-regulated-ward> proves the regulator
  estimate, including the anchored correction, from the stated current
  hypotheses.

  Finally combine it with <eqref|ch17:scalar-log-supplier>. On a fixed
  interval <math|<around|[|u-A,u|]>> in the variable <math|log \<mu\>>, the
  bounded error in <math|R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>-12*\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
  \<mu\>> implies that some regulator has
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>+12*\<kappa\><rsub|G>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*C+o<around|(|1|)>|)>/A>
  after the period is large. The remainder is at most
  <math|C*e<rsup|u>+o<around|(|1|)>> on that interval. First take the current
  and period limits there, next <math|u\<to\>-\<infty\>>, and finally
  <math|A\<to\>\<infty\>>. Since <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>> is
  independent of <math|\<mu\>>, this proves

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>\<longrightarrow\>-12*\<kappa\><rsub|G>.
  </equation*>

  The group-coordinate contact identity, coefficient cancellation and
  regulator estimate are now established. Their application still requires
  the actual transferred current sequence to satisfy the weighted bounds and
  matched-jet convergence stated at the start of this subsection; those
  conditions are not consequences of the contact algebra.

  <section|Current conservation and uniform transfer
  bounds><label|ch17:quadratic-current>

  The potentially dangerous quadratic defect has an exact cancellation that
  should be imposed before estimating its norm. We prove preservation of that
  cancellation for the actual stationary tangent. This removes an additive
  interpretation of the order-two matching error. We then control the
  nonlinear stationary jets and the current itself.

  Fix a stage and an admissible action. Let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>> be a
  gauge-invariant anchored current defect, with zero constant and linear
  field coefficients. Its Hessian at zero has an exponentially local joint
  Fourier symbol <math|E<rsub|b>*<around|(|p,q|)>>, with two independent
  field momenta. Gauge invariance gives a Ward identity on each leg. We say
  that its quadratic defect has order at least four when

  <\equation>
    E<rsub|b>*<around|(|p,q|)>=O<around|(|<around|(|<around|\||p|\|>+<around|\||q|\|>|)><rsup|4>|)><space|1em><text|near
    ><around|(|0,0|)>.<label|ch17:defect-four>
  </equation>

  This refers to spatial momentum degree, not the field degree of the
  Hessian. It includes the affine\Uaffine and affine\Uquadratic polynomial
  cancellations needed in the current estimate.

  For the initial Wilson current, <eqref|ch17:defect-four> follows from the
  leading continuum identity. Its linearized curvature is <math|d*A>; the
  leading quadratic action is the curvature square; and the centered
  difference <math|\<nabla\>> has linear symbol <math|i*p>. The quadratic
  Taylor coefficient of <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-D*S<around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>>
  therefore has leading spatial momentum degree three. At that degree it is
  exactly

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|a>*<around*|(|<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|c*d><rsup|2>-<big|sum><rsub|c>F<rsub|a*c>*F<rsub|b*c>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|c>F<rsub|b*c>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*c>=0.
  </equation*>

  The identity uses the linear Bianchi equation; colour inner products are
  implicit. Lower spatial degrees vanish because each curvature contains a
  difference and the action variation contains one more. All remaining
  lattice Taylor terms have degree at least four. The same reasoning applies
  to any initial path action with the normalized weight-four Taylor
  polynomial already established in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>.

  <subsection|Preservation of the low moments>

  Let <math|W> be the actual stationary tangent from coarse link variables to
  fine link variables, with block ratio <math|L>. The first derivative of the
  nonlinear stationary substitution is this <math|W>; its higher derivatives
  cannot enter the quadratic pullback because the defect has zero first field
  derivative. Fine gauge changes of <math|W> have no effect on this quadratic
  calculation by the Ward identities of <math|E<rsub|b>>.

  The exact stationary identity of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>
  implies, for the alias <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*l|)>/L>,

  <\equation>
    K<rsub|f><around|(|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>|)>*N<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>=A<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>*K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,<label|ch17:current-alias>
  </equation>

  where <math|N<rsub|l>> is the alias amplitude of <math|W>, <math|A<rsub|l>>
  is the corresponding amplitude of the adjoint of the gauge-covariant block
  average, and <math|K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.
  At a nonprincipal alias <math|l\<ne\>0>, project <math|N<rsub|l>> off the
  fine gradient direction. The completed fine kinetic matrix is invertible
  near <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|0|)>\<ne\>0>; hence that projected amplitude
  is analytic and <math|O<around|(|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>|)>>. The removed
  gradient is killed by the defect Ward identity. The principal amplitude is
  analytic by the exponential locality of <math|W>. No bound uniform in
  <math|L> on an individual nonprincipal inverse is needed for this exact
  Taylor-order statement.

  Consider the actual anchor average <math|K<rsub|0>> from
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch17:block-identities>, performed before stationary
  substitution. Its symbol has a double zero at every nonprincipal alias: at
  least one coordinate of <math|l> is nonzero, and the factor
  <math|M<rsub|a><rsup|2>*G<rsub|a><around|(|s<rsub|a><rsup|L>|)>> has two
  factors of the block average <math|M<rsub|a>> there. In a two-leg alias
  term the averaging multiplier is evaluated at
  <math|k<rsub|l><around|(|p|)>+k<rsub|l<rprime|'>><around|(|q|)>>. The
  quadratic pullback therefore has the following three kinds of terms.

  If both aliases are principal, <eqref|ch17:defect-four> and analytic
  amplitudes retain order four. If exactly one is nonprincipal, its
  transverse amplitude has order two and the anchor average has another
  order-two zero, giving order at least four. If both are nonprincipal, the
  two projected amplitudes already give order four, including when their
  alias indices cancel in the anchor multiplier. Every term thus satisfies
  <eqref|ch17:defect-four> on the coarse lattice. This is a finite alias
  identity and holds on the same actual history.

  In particular the quadratic jet is not merely close to having the required
  affine cancellations: it has them exactly, provided the current update uses
  the exact pulled-back defect <math|\<cal-E\><rprime|'><rsub|b>=L<rsup|5>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|0>*\<cal-E\><rsub|b>|)>>
  through the relevant field order. If an implementation redefines that
  defect by action matching, equality of these quadratic jets must be
  retained; a bounded additive matching error would not preserve this
  argument.

  <subsection|A homogeneous bound for the quadratic channel>

  The analytic division argument of Section<nbsp><reference|ch07:division>
  can be applied to each of the two independent momentum legs of
  <math|E<rsub|b>>. The Ward identities produce one curvature difference on
  each leg. Their homotopy divisions lower Taylor order by one each, so
  <eqref|ch17:defect-four> leaves a coefficient of total order at least two.
  Coordinatewise Taylor division in the remaining eight variables factors two
  additional differences, distributed between the legs. On a fixed smaller
  coefficient width these operations are bounded by constants depending only
  on the stage and the width loss. Thus <math|E<rsub|b>> is a sum of terms
  with either one and three differences, or two and two differences, on its
  two field legs. The coefficient sum is bounded by
  <math|C<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|b>|\<\|\|\>>> in the original
  weighted norm.

  The actual tangent may be replaced by the smooth representative
  <math|W<rsub|a>> because the quadratic Ward identities kill its gradient
  correction. Its fine-difference bounds are

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|a>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*L<rsup|-1-r>,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>3.
  </equation*>

  Consequently either allocation of four differences costs at most
  <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-6>> for the two legs. The anchor average
  <math|K<rsub|0>> has bounded absolute coefficient sum, not a factor
  <math|L<rsup|4>>; it is an average rather than an unnormalized sum.
  Translated coefficient weights are absorbed by a fixed narrower coarse
  width, exactly as in the inherited quadratic action estimate of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. Multiplication by the current
  normalization <math|L<rsup|5>> proves

  <\equation>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*\<cal-E\><rprime|'><rsub|b><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|out>>\<leqslant\><frac|C<rsub|j>|L>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|b><around|(|0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|in>>.<label|ch17:quadratic-current-contraction>
  </equation>

  This is homogeneous in the actual defect. No constant source term has been
  introduced. Dividing the coefficients by <math|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^>|)><rsup|2>> only adds the adjacent logarithmic ratio,
  which is bounded and tends to one on a fixed-stage tail. The selected block
  ratio makes this quadratic channel a strict contraction.

  <subsection|The nonlinear terms need fixed-ratio bounds>

  The nonlinear stationary jets enter only below the diagonal in field order.
  Their constants may depend on <math|L>, because the coupling threshold is
  chosen after the ratio. What must remain uniform before <math|L> is the
  coarse coefficient width on which these constants are measured. We obtain
  that width from the completed inverse of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>.

  Write <math|z<around|(|v|)>> for the stationary internal coordinates, and
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|(|v,z|)>> for the same joint
  fine-plus-frame action as there. Let <math|T*u=<around|(|u,D*z<around|(|0|)>*u|)>>,
  and let <math|<with|math-font|cal*|H>> be its internal Hessian. On the
  forest slice implicit differentiation gives

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|H>*z<rsub|2>*<around|[|u,v|]>>|<cell|=-D<rsup|3>*<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|[|T*u,T*v,<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|<with|math-font|cal*|H>*z<rsub|3>*<around|[|u,v,w|]>>|<cell|=-D<rsup|4>*<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|[|T*u,T*v,T*w,<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|]>-<big|sum><rsub|<around|{|i*j|}><around|{|k|}>>D<rsup|3>*<with|math-font|cal*|S>*<around|[|<around|(|0,z<rsub|2>*<around|[|u<rsub|i>,u<rsub|j>|]>|)>,T*u<rsub|k>,<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|]>.<eq-number><label|ch17:stationary-higher-jets>>>>>
  </align>

  There are three terms in the sum. The right sides are internal covectors,
  with all mixed gauge-covariant block average and frame terms included.

  These formulas have period-independent bounds <math|C<rsub|j,L>> on one
  fixed smaller coarse Fourier strip. To check the width statement, let
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|F>> be projection onto the forest slice along the joint
  gauge directions. At zero it is computed by finite sums along the block
  tree and the root frame; its coefficients and those of
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|F><rsup|\<ast\>>> have norm bounded by a finite
  <math|C<rsub|j,L>>, but bounded range in coarse blocks. The dual
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|F><rsup|\<ast\>>> extends a slice covector by zero on
  gauge directions. For such a covector the completed inverse
  <math|<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>> of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching> solves the Hessian equation modulo
  gauge. Thus the slice inverse is

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\><rsub|F>*<with|math-font|cal*|C><rsup|-1>*\<Pi\><rsub|F><rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  The completed inverse has a common positive coarse width chosen before
  <math|L>; multiplication by the finite-range projections does not destroy
  it. The path vertices and derivatives of the gauge-covariant block average
  also have a common coarse width, with finite <math|C<rsub|j,L>> norms.
  Restrict the external strips by a fixed factor so all intermediate momentum
  sums lie in this inverse strip. Formula<nbsp><eqref|ch17:stationary-higher-jets>
  then proves the asserted bounds for <math|z<rsub|2>,z<rsub|3>>. It does not
  assert bounds uniform in <math|L> for their norms.

  For every anchored gauge-invariant function, evaluate this stationary
  branch in an anchored radial gauge. At first order, the gauge-corrected
  link at fine displacement <math|x> is a sum of the tangent curvature over
  coordinate rectangles of total area at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)>>. The smooth curvature bound for
  <math|W<rsub|a>> gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||W<rsup|<math-up|rad>>*<around|(|x,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-2>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)>*e<rsup|C<around|\||x|\|>/L>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*d*<around|(|0,y|)>>.
  </equation*>

  The polynomial factor and fine exponential are absorbed by the weighted
  local coefficient norm. A common strip reduction handles the coarse weight.
  Higher derivatives of this gauge change are finite products along the same
  paths; the fixed-order factors are polynomial in path length. Their
  period-independent norms are therefore still finite <math|C<rsub|j,L>> on
  the same smaller coarse width.

  The raw higher coefficients <math|D<rsup|3>*\<cal-E\>,D<rsup|4>*\<cal-E\>>
  need not separately satisfy linear Ward identities. We use gauge invariance
  of the complete function to make this radial substitution first; its
  nonlinear derivatives multiply only lower field derivatives of the defect.

  Now differentiate the exact pullback <math|\<cal-E\><rprime|'>=L<rsup|5>*\<cal-I\>*<around|(|K<rsub|0>*\<cal-E\>|)>>.
  In field order <math|r> the term involving <math|D<rsup|r>*\<cal-E\>> has
  only <math|r> first stationary tangents. For <math|r=3,4>, radial gauge
  bounds it by <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|5-2*r>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*\<cal-E\>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  The quadratic diagonal has the stronger estimate
  <eqref|ch17:quadratic-current-contraction>. Every other chain-rule term
  involves <math|D<rsup|s>*\<cal-E\>> with <math|s\<less\>r> and higher
  stationary or gauge jets. Hence, for <math|r=2,3,4>,

  <\equation>
    e<rprime|'><rsub|r>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j,r><around|(|L|)>*e<rsub|r>+<big|sum><rsub|2\<leqslant\>s\<less\>r>C<rsub|j,L,r,s>*e<rsub|s>,<space|2em>a<rsub|j,2><around|(|L|)>,a<rsub|j,3><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>/L,<space|1em>a<rsub|j,4><around|(|L|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>/L<rsup|3>.<label|ch17:unnormalized-defect>
  </equation>

  Here <math|e<rsub|r>> is the weighted norm of the field derivative of order
  <math|r>. The estimates are homogeneous in the defect, because every term
  in the composition contains one of its derivatives. There is no
  action-independent source on the right.

  Put <math|<wide|e|\<bar\>><rsub|m,r>=e<rsub|m,r>/<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|r>>. For a fine-to-coarse step from
  <math|m+1> to <math|m>, the coefficient below the diagonal becomes

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|j,L,r,s>*<frac|<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|s>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|r>>,<space|2em>s\<less\>r.
  </equation*>

  At fixed <math|j,L> this tends to zero as the coupling threshold increases.
  The diagonal logarithmic ratio tends to one. Choose <math|L> first so the
  three diagonal coefficients are at most <math|1/8>, then choose the
  coupling threshold so each row sum, including the below-diagonal terms, is
  at most <math|1/2>. The finitely many adjacent-stage cases are included
  before each choice. This proves a homogeneous contraction of the normalized
  defect vector. Starting with the Wilson defect, whose normalized norm is
  bounded, its value after <math|n> transfers is at most <math|C*2<rsup|-n>>.
  At a fixed output level the logarithmic normalization is fixed, so the
  actual unnormalized defect derivatives tend to zero as well.

  <subsection|Why action matching introduces no source>

  We specify the displacement map, including its sign. At the stationary fine
  field and coarse frame, let <math|<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|U,h|)>> be the
  actual gauge-covariant block average with its chosen frame. Its
  left-trivialized differential is

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|R<rsub|f>*\<eta\>|)><rsub|e>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*s>|\|><rsub|0>*log
    <around*|(|<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|e<rsup|s*\<eta\>>*U,h|)><rsub|e>*<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|U,h|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|b<rsub|y,f>> be the fixed frame-route coefficients defined by
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|f>b<rsub|y,f>*\<eta\><rsub|f>=\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|0;\<eta\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<tau\><rsub|y>*<around|(|r;\<eta\>|)>>,
  where <math|\<tau\>> is the signed path average in
  <eqref|ch05:frame-linearization>. With the fixed route
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|y,f>> from coarse root <math|L*y> to the tail of the
  fine link <math|f>, set

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|R<rsub|0>*\<eta\>|)><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|f>b<rsub|y,f><math-up|Ad><around|(|h<around|(|y|)>*U<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|y,f>|)>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|f>,<space|2em><around|(|d<rsub|V>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|e>=\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|->|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|V<rsub|e>|)>*\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|+>|)>.
  </equation*>

  At coarse anchor zero the displaced coarse current is

  <\equation>
    Y<rprime|'><rsub|b>=L<rsup|5>*<big|sum><rsub|x>K<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|R<rsub|f>+d<rsub|V>*R<rsub|0>|)>*Y<rsub|b><rsup|x><around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>|)>.<label|ch17:actual-current-map>
  </equation>

  Translations give the other anchors. The sum is over fine anchors; the
  output is a vector on coarse links. At the identity <math|R<rsub|0>=R> of
  <eqref|ch05:actual-Q>, <math|R<rsub|f>=Q=M+d<rsub|0,c>*R>, and
  <math|d<rsub|V>=-d<rsub|0,c>>. In this convention the plus sign in
  <eqref|ch17:actual-current-map> cancels the root correction exactly:

  <\equation>
    <around|(|R<rsub|f>+d<rsub|V>*R<rsub|0>|)>\|<rsub|0>=M.<label|ch17:flat-current-map>
  </equation>

  The smooth straight average, rather than a rough root-path row, therefore
  controls its equal-order Taylor terms. All derivatives of
  <math|R<rsub|f>+d<rsub|V>*R<rsub|0>> have finite <math|C<rsub|j,L>> bounds
  on the common coarse strip. Their fixed paths have length
  <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|L|)>> and remain within bounded coarse distance;
  differentiation adds only finite path-length factors.

  For the exact stationary action the current identity is preserved before
  any estimates. The envelope theorem differentiates the stationary value
  only through the explicit coarse field. In the block penalty, simultaneous
  left multiplication of the gauge-covariant block average and the coarse
  link leaves the penalty unchanged. Thus a fine displacement <math|Y>
  induces the coarse displacement <math|R<rsub|f>*Y>; its root correction
  <math|d<rsub|V>*R<rsub|0>*Y> has zero action variation by coarse gauge
  invariance. Together with <eqref|ch17:block-algebra> this gives

  <\equation*>
    D*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|[|Y<rprime|'><rsub|b>|]>=L<rsup|5>*\<cal-I\>*K<rsub|0>*D*S<around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|c>>*J<rprime|'><rsub|a*b>=L<rsup|5>*\<cal-I\>*K<rsub|0>*<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>.
  </equation*>

  Joint fine and frame stationarity is used here, as in the completed
  quotient calculation of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>. Matching
  replaces <math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>> by an action with identical Taylor
  coefficients through field order four. Since
  <math|Y<rprime|'><around|(|0|)>=0>, its action variation still agrees
  through order four. Therefore the displayed defect pullback is exact at
  every field order used in <eqref|ch17:unnormalized-defect>. A separate
  additive <math|C/<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^>|)><rsup|2>> matching error
  is neither needed nor permitted in this argument.

  <subsection|Uniform current and difference-primitive bounds>

  Recall that <math|J<rsub|a*b>> is the lattice stress current and
  <math|Y<rsub|b>> its associated gauge-covariant displacement. The functions
  <math|q<rsub|a>> and <math|t<rsub|a*b*c>> are the forward-difference
  primitives of its trace and antisymmetric part, respectively, as defined in
  Section<nbsp><reference|ch17:current-suppliers>. They are distinct from the
  small-field threshold <math|t> used in marked norms.

  For completeness we describe the accompanying bounds needed in the
  regulator criterion. Subtract fixed local reference stress and curvature
  displacements, whose leading Taylor coefficients by scaling dimension are
  those used initially. The reference functions have finite stencils and
  fixed coefficient bounds. The lowest jets of the transported functions
  agree with them exactly. For the stress this follows from the principal
  tangent amplitude <math|N<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=L<rsup|-1>*I>, the mass-one
  anchor average, and the fact that nonprincipal transverse amplitudes have
  order at least two. If exactly one alias is nonprincipal, the linear Ward
  identity on the other leg supplies one further principal momentum, so this
  contribution has order at least three. Two nonprincipal aliases have order
  at least four. Consequently only the principal pair contributes to the
  quadratic weight-four coefficient, which is unchanged.

  For the displacement one additionally uses the column sum of the
  gauge-covariant block average. By <eqref|ch17:flat-current-map>, the
  corrected differential is exactly <math|M> at zero. The block-average alias
  formula for <math|M> kills every nonprincipal alias at zero coarse
  momentum, so its fine column sum in a fixed direction is constant. Its row
  mass is <math|L>; there are <math|L<rsup|4>> fine residues. The column sum
  is therefore <math|L<rsup|-3>>. This is an identity for the corrected
  differential, with no residual root-gradient term. Since the leading fine
  curvature of <math|W<rsub|a>> is <math|L<rsup|-2>> times the coarse
  curvature, the factors in the current update multiply to
  <math|L<rsup|5>*L<rsup|-3>*L<rsup|-2>=1>. This preserves the linear
  curvature jet of <math|Y>. Gauge covariance then fixes its accompanying
  quadratic commutator term: its leading covariant weight-two polynomial is
  that same curvature.

  The stress remainder's quadratic symbol now has order at least three.
  Two-leg curl division leaves one extra difference, so its diagonal transfer
  is <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|4>*L<rsup|-5>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>/L>. Higher
  stress orders have radial-tangent bounds <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|4-2*r>>.
  For <math|Y>, its zero-order corrected differential <math|M> has row sum at
  most <math|C<rsub|j>*L>, while <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-4>>.
  To see the resulting local gain, write a translated current coefficient as
  <math|y*<around|(|f-x;z<rsub|1>-x,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|r>-x|)>>, with output
  link <math|f> and anchor <math|x>. For each fixed coarse output edge
  <math|e> its absolute sum is bounded by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\||M<rsub|e*f>|\|>*<big|sum><rsub|x>\|y(f-x;<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|)||\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|-3><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>>.
  </equation*>

  Only a bounded number of coarse output edges are within the gauge-covariant
  block average and averaging patches; exponential coefficient tails control
  the rest. This proves the same gain for the full local output-link norm.
  The linear displacement remainder has a curvature difference and one
  additional difference. If the extra difference acts on its field leg, it
  costs <math|L<rsup|-1>> through <math|W<rsub|a>>. If it acts on the fine
  output index, summation by parts moves it onto <math|M<rsub|e*f>>; the
  box-filter identity gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|f><around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f>*M<rsub|e*f>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>>,
  again gaining <math|L<rsup|-1>> relative to its row mass
  <math|C<rsub|j>*L>. Thus its diagonal factor is
  <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|5>*L<rsup|-3>*L<rsup|-3>=C<rsub|j>/L>. For field
  order <math|r\<geqslant\>2> its diagonal factor is
  <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|2-2*r>>. Derivatives of the gauge-covariant block
  average or its frame correction multiply lower field orders of <math|Y>, so
  their fixed-<math|L> constants are absorbed by the same logarithmic
  normalization as above. Evaluating in a local radial gauge is legitimate
  for this covariant displacement: transform its output frame and the
  differential of the gauge-covariant block average together. The additional
  frame derivatives again occur only below the field-order diagonal.

  The trace and antisymmetry primitives start at field order two. Their old
  terms carry <math|L<rsup|3>> and hence gain
  <math|C<rsub|j>*L<rsup|3-2*r>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>/L>. Their new terms are
  the explicit <math|L<rsup|4>*K<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|t>>*J> terms proved in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|ch17:block-identities>; the equal-order forcing from
  <math|J> is bounded independently of <math|L>. Choose a fixed smaller
  weight for these primitive norms so this one-way forcing has norm at most
  <math|1/8>. The other diagonal terms are then made small by <math|L>, and
  all lower-order terms by coupling threshold. Reference functions contribute
  finite <math|C<rsub|j,L>> forcing, divided by positive powers of <math|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^>>; a sufficiently large coupling threshold can make that
  forcing at most <math|1/4>.

  Thus a weighted sum of the normalized current and primitive norms obeys
  <math|X<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>*X<rsub|m+1>+<frac|1|4>>. The initial
  Wilson current and zero primitives have bounded normalized norms. Uniform
  bounds follow throughout the history, and in particular at each fixed
  output level. At that level these are the unnormalized finite constants
  required by the regulator criterion, since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>
  is fixed. The needed free scaling-dimension jets are unchanged, and the
  defect tends to zero by the homogeneous contraction above. All estimates
  use the same gauge-covariant block average, stationary branch, action
  matching and periodized kernels as the marked trajectory.

  <section|Quantitative stability of the trace
  functional><label|ch17:stability>

  The finite-dimensional argument is elementary, but its rate is essential.
  Suppose <math|s:E\<to\>V> has right inverse <math|t> and one-dimensional
  kernel <math|<RR>v>. Let <math|s<rsub|i>:E\<to\>V>,
  <math|t<rsub|i>:V\<to\>E> satisfy <math|s<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i>=I>,
  <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>t>, and let <math|v<rsub|i>\<to\>v>. If
  <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> and

  <\equation*>
    e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0,<space|2em>e<rsub|i><around|\||\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<to\>0,
  </equation*>

  then, for <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=\<rho\>*t<rsub|i>>,

  <\equation>
    s<rsub|i>\<to\>s,<space|1em>sup<rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<infty\>,<space|1em>e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0.<label|ch17:stable-functional>
  </equation>

  Indeed <math|k<rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>-t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>\<to\>v> lies
  in <math|ker s<rsub|i>>. The maps <math|<around|(|h,c|)>\<mapsto\>t<rsub|i>*h+c*k<rsub|i>>
  converge to the invertible map <math|<around|(|h,c|)>\<mapsto\>t*h+c*v>, so
  their inverses are uniformly bounded. Writing
  <math|y=t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*y+c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*k<rsub|i>> gives
  <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>>>
  and <math|<around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|)>*y=-c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*\<rho\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>,
  which proves the claim.

  Here are the complete rate estimates needed for the trace conclusion; the
  next section proves them for the common family. Write

  <\equation*>
    e<rsub|i>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
    b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/5|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|i>=exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/6|)>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|q<rsub|i>/e<rsub|i>\<to\>0> and both are smaller than every
  positive power of <math|b<rsub|i>>. The letter <math|q<rsub|i>> in this
  section is a scalar bound, unrelated to the inserted densities of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>. Let
  <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|L>,B<rsub|i><rsup|X>,B<rsub|i><rsup|D>> be the
  centered transferred tensors and set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>K<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>*U<rsub|i,5><rsup|-1><math-up|div>.
  </equation*>

  The required same-family current transfer estimates are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*q<rsub|i>,<space|1em>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>,<eq-number><label|ch17:transfer>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|Z>\<to\><math-up|div><rsup|<math-up|Max>><space|0.17em>Z,<space|1em>e<rsub|i>*<around|[|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|Z>|)>-\<rho\><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>Z|)>|]>\<to\>0*<space|1em><around|(|Z=X,D|)>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|A<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|Z>\<to\>Z*<space|1em><around|(|Z=L,X,D|)>,<space|1em>b<rsub|i><rsup|p><math-up|tr><around|(|A<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|Z>|)>\<to\>0*<space|1em><around|(|Z=L,X,p\<in\><NN>|)>.>>>>
  </align>

  These bounds are stronger than smooth free comparison. The next section
  proves them using the kernel identities and a separate low-moment estimate
  for spatial remainders.

  <section|From the current identity to marked coefficient
  estimates><label|ch17:marked-rates>

  The integration by parts needed here is performed before the large-field
  expansion. This order matters: a large-field activity is only measurable,
  so its derivative cannot be inserted into the expansion as an ordinary
  bounded function.

  <subsection|A differentiated block kernel>

  Use the exact block kernel of <eqref|ch04:kernel>. Conditional on its fine
  field and frame variables, its dependence on a coarse link is
  <math|q<rsub|b*\<beta\>>*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>, where
  <math|q<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|u|)>=Z<rsub|\<gamma\>><rsup|-1>*exp
  <around|[|-\<gamma\>*w<around|(|u|)>|]>>. For every fixed order <math|r>,
  compactness and smoothness of <math|w> give

  <\equation>
    <label|ch17:kernel-score><around|\||D<rsup|r>*q<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r>*<around|(|1+\<gamma\>|)><rsup|r>*q<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|u|)>.
  </equation>

  Indeed successive left derivatives give a polynomial in derivatives of
  <math|w>, with at most <math|r> factors of <math|\<gamma\>>. The
  normalization is independent of <math|u>. For a product of link kernels the
  corresponding bound has an additional polynomial in the number of
  differentiated links. No lower bound on <math|q<rsub|\<gamma\>>> is used.

  First truncate the exponentially local current and its stationary kernels
  on a finite neighborhood. For the displacement use a finite covariant
  completion of its controlled jets through field order four, not the
  uncontrolled higher derivatives of a deep transferred current. Use the
  coefficientwise covariant path completion: replace each fixed-order
  curvature or covariant-difference monomial by its plaquette and
  transported-path representative, and correct its lower jets triangularly
  through order four. Each fixed number of link derivatives adds only a fixed
  power of the path length. The completion therefore has a bounded weighted
  norm at a strictly narrower exponential width; it is not an arbitrary jet
  section on a space of growing dimension. Complete in that weighted norm
  first and then truncate. All additional fixed-order derivative bounds
  depend only on the controlled jets and the fixed stage. Extend this local
  small-field formula smoothly to the compact product group. One can use a
  smooth cutoff supported strictly inside the logarithmic chart, equal to one
  on the smaller chart used for Taylor extraction, and then average over the
  compact local gauge group. Equivalently extend its finite covariant
  holonomy jets. This preserves the required jets and covariance. The finite
  derivative costs are inverse powers of the chart width and polynomials in
  the support size. They are allowed in the bounds below. Only after this
  extension do we integrate by parts. For an exponentially local current do
  this first for finite support truncations. Fixed powers of the support
  length are absorbed by a strict reserve in the exponential coefficient
  width. The derivative bounds and tail errors are consequently summable, so
  the finite-period identity passes to the weighted tail limit.

  Let <math|k*<around|(|U,V|)>> be that exact kernel, let
  <math|f<around|(|U|)>> be the entire incoming density, and put
  <math|F<around|(|V|)>=<big|int>k*<around|(|U,V|)>*f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U>.
  All original fine large-field indicators and compatibility conditions are
  part of <math|f> or of the frame integral; none is differentiated. For a
  smooth coarse displacement <math|Y>, define

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-D\><rsub|Y>*k=<around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y|)>*k+D<rsub|V>*k<around|[|Y|]>.
  </equation*>

  Dominated differentiation, using <eqref|ch17:kernel-score>, gives the
  <em|density identity>

  <\equation>
    <label|ch17:kernel-divergence><math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><around|(|F*Y|)>=<big|int>\<cal-D\><rsub|Y>*k*<around|(|U,V|)>*f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U.
  </equation>

  Consequently its integral against every spectator independent of the varied
  coarse links is zero. This is an identity for the full source, including
  its nonpolynomial remainder, not an assertion that a finite Taylor
  polynomial alone has zero exterior moments.

  The right hand side is an admissible marked conditional integral. Its extra
  factor is a finite sum of smooth local kernel scores times <math|Y> or its
  derivative. On a protected dependence region the incoming measurable
  product remains fixed under the change of variables in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch06:large-fields>. Additional coarse derivatives
  act on the score and the common density ratio only. The bounded
  pointwise-jet estimate <eqref|ch06:actual-jet-integral> therefore applies
  with a fixed polynomial loss in <math|\<beta\>>, support size and current
  derivative bounds. Rooting the connected expansion at this extra factor
  gives the same marked expansion as in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions>.
  In particular large-field terms retain their original prescribed sets of
  sites. This argument does not differentiate the event indicators introduced
  when that marked integral is subsequently decomposed.

  The ordinary contribution needs a sharper identity than the absolute score
  bound. Fix the full dependence region of a rooted connected component and
  use one additional coarse derivative beyond those requested for the
  extracted coefficient. On its smaller small-field chart, make exactly the
  conditional Gaussian change of variables used to construct the ordinary
  expansion. Write its normalized conditional integrand as
  <math|p*<around|(|V,z|)>> and its integral as
  <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|ord>><around|(|V|)>>. For finite ordinary selections,
  differentiation under this integral gives

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|V>*Z<rsub|<math-up|ord>><around|[|Y|]>=<big|int>D<rsub|V>*p<around|[|Y|]>*<space|0.17em>d*z,<space|2em><big|int>D<rsub|V>*p<around|[|Y|]>*<space|0.17em>d*z=Z<rsub|<math-up|ord>>*D<rsub|V>*log
    Z<rsub|<math-up|ord>><around|[|Y|]>.
  </equation*>

  Every Jacobian, relative determinant and conditional normalization is
  included in <math|p>; thus this is not the derivative of an unnormalized
  Gaussian moment. The absolutely convergent ordinary logarithm, with one
  derivative reserve, passes this identity to the countable expansion. By its
  definition in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch09:renormalization>,

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|<math-up|ord>>=\<cal-C\>*exp <around|[|-\<beta\>*S-\<cal-L\>-E<rsub|<math-up|reg>>|]>,<space|2em>D<rsub|V>*log
    \<cal-C\>=0.
  </equation*>

  If an intermediate local normalization depends on <math|V>, it is included
  in <math|\<cal-L\>+E<rsub|<math-up|reg>>>, not in <math|\<cal-C\>>.
  Consequently the ordinary score is <em|exactly>
  <math|-\<beta\>*D*S<around|[|Y|]>-D*\<cal-L\><around|[|Y|]>-D*E<rsub|<math-up|reg>><around|[|Y|]>>.
  The regular term is estimated using its already proved differentiated
  regular norm. We do not estimate it by the polynomial-in-<math|\<beta\>>
  absolute score bound.

  This argument uses the full rooted component, not just the score stencil.
  In the rooted connected expansion, ordinary factors in components disjoint
  from the marked component cancel between the differentiated numerator and
  its normalization. Every term with a failure anywhere in the marked
  connected component, including a distant failure joined to the score, is
  kept in the complementary marked sum. The smooth cutoffs used above are
  constant on the smaller small-field chart; their derivatives are supported
  where at least one enlarged chart condition fails. Attach this condition to
  its actual dependence support. The same Gaussian or energy estimate for the
  nested cutoffs bounds this new failure, while all incoming prescribed
  large-field sets are retained. The absolute score bound is used only for
  these complementary terms, where the exponential failure probability
  absorbs its polynomial <math|\<beta\>> cost. No derivative of a hard
  indicator is taken. This proves the ordinary cancellation and the
  complementary large-field estimate separately, which is essential to
  preserve the regular power gain.

  <subsection|The finite degree-five coefficient>

  At the comparison level <math|k> write

  <\equation*>
    h=<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em>H=e<rsup|h>,<space|2em>t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>*H.
  </equation*>

  All constants in this paragraph are for a fixed stage and the fixed finite
  derivative and degree request; they are fixed before its coupling
  threshold. Let <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|5>> denote radial Taylor extraction
  through degree five and let <math|f> be curvature-factor count on a
  homogeneous coefficient. Set <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|5,t>=t<rsup|f-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|5>>,
  followed by real signed-permutation averaging. There are no invariant
  linear color terms. Thus the nonzero vector coefficients under
  consideration have <math|f\<geqslant\>2>, and this normalization does not
  introduce a negative power of <math|t> beyond the displayed
  <math|t<rsup|-2>>.

  Apply the exact marked step to <eqref|ch17:kernel-divergence>, retaining
  its entire remainder, and use the current identity through order four. Let
  <math|j<rsub|k>> be the radial coefficient of the current divergence, in
  the same threshold coordinates. The classical term
  <math|-\<beta\>*D*S<around|[|Y|]>> is <math|-\<beta\>*j<rsub|k>> up to the
  contracting current defect. Thus the coefficient of the full
  zero-exterior-moment source is <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>-\<beta\>*j<rsub|k>>;
  <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>> alone is not asserted to have zero exterior
  moments. Its remaining degree-five scalar-vector coefficient can be written

  <\equation>
    <label|ch17:finite-sigma>\<Sigma\><rsub|k>=\<cal-P\><rsub|5,t>*<around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y-D*\<cal-L\><around|[|Y|]>|)>+E<rsub|<math-up|stat>>+E<rsub|<math-up|tail>>+E<rsub|<math-up|reg>>+E<rsub|<math-up|mark>>.
  </equation>

  Here stationary and loop kernels are truncated at
  <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>>. Their
  common exponential decay makes <math|E<rsub|<math-up|stat>>> and
  <math|E<rsub|<math-up|tail>>> at most <math|H<rsup|-1>> after finite
  projection losses. The regular error satisfies

  <\equation*>
    t<rsup|-2>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/10>*H<rsup|-2>.
  </equation*>

  A directional derivative along <math|Y> has the same bound up to the fixed
  current norm: the derivative costs <math|t<rsup|-1>> while
  <math|Y=O<around|(|t|)>> on the small-field chart. The previously proved
  logarithmic current bounds and fixed localization losses are absorbed by
  increasing the coupling threshold.

  For the marked remainder one must first cancel the whole classical current
  divergence, not merely use the absolute kernel-score bound. The current
  identity agrees through field order four. The finite covariant completion
  just chosen has controlled higher derivatives, so the remaining classical
  error starts at order five and costs

  <\equation*>
    t<rsup|-2>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>=O<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/2>*H<rsup|3>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Fixed current and localization factors preserve a bound of the form
  <math|C<rsub|j>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/10>*H<rsup|C<rsub|j>>>
  after increasing the coupling threshold. The ordinary loop and Jacobian
  Taylor remainders have no leading factor <math|\<beta\>> and gain an
  additional small-field factor. This controls the high-field-order
  remainder. It does not control a low-field-order term with many spatial
  differences: vanishing Taylor coefficients through the specified scaling
  dimension alone give no additional small-field factor. The remaining
  low-field-order spatial terms are controlled by exact polynomial
  preservation, rather than by an extra power of the field.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Low-moment estimate for spatial
    remainders><label|ch17:spatial-moments>For the actual response and the
    regular local sources just constructed, a remainder whose radial Taylor
    coefficients through scaling dimension five vanish has extracted
    degree-five coefficient <math|O<around|(|H<rsup|-1/2>|)>>. The estimate
    is uniform in period and in the finite current truncations, after the
    stage orders and thresholds have been chosen with one extra derivative
    reserve. It applies to both the nonclassical coefficient and the
    normalized current divergence.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First identify the polynomial response. Recall the notation of
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>: <math|D<rsub|l>,D> are the
    rescaled fine and coarse covariance symbols, <math|x<rsub|l>,x> their
    gradient columns, and <math|\<alpha\>=<big|sum><rsub|l><around|\||m<rsub|l>|\|><rsup|2><tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>>.
    Write <math|s=\<lambda\><rsub|M<rsub|c>><around|(|p|)>> and
    <math|D=s<rsup|-1><math-up|diag><around|(|B<rsub|a>|)>>, where the
    <math|B<rsub|a>> are the regularized diagonal covariance factors. The
    scalar correction

    <\equation*>
      \<chi\>=<frac|x<rsup|\<ast\>><math-up|diag><around|(|B<rsub|a><rsup|-1>|)>*x|s>
    </equation*>

    is analytic and nonzero at zero, as in the pole cancellation for the
    transport formulas. Put <math|\<sigma\>=\<chi\><rsup|-1>*x<rsup|\<ast\>><math-up|diag><around|(|B<rsub|a><rsup|-1>|)>>,
    so that <math|\<pi\>=s<rsup|-1>*\<sigma\>> in those formulas. For a
    nonprincipal alias define

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|l,a>=<wide|m<rsub|l>|\<bar\>><around*|{|<frac|<tr><space|0.17em>D<rsub|l>|s*\<alpha\>>-<frac|<around|(|D<rsub|l>|)><rsub|a*a><around|\||r<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l,a>|)>|\|><rsup|2>|B<rsub|a>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Substitution into the straight transport gives the regular formula

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|l>=\<cal-B\><rsub|l>+<math-up|diag><around|(|g<rsub|l,a>|)>*x<rsub|l>*\<sigma\>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\><rsub|l>=D<rsub|l>*F<rsub|1,l><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|D<rsub|l>> is regular at a nonprincipal alias, and
    <math|s*\<alpha\>> and <math|B<rsub|a>> are analytic and nonzero at zero.
    Thus no uncancelled factor <math|s<rsup|-1>> remains. Both terms contain
    <math|<wide|m<rsub|l>|\<bar\>>>, with
    <math|m<rsub|l>=w<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l>|)><rsup|j>> and
    <math|w<rsub|L><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|l><around|(|0|)>|)>=0>. Every
    derivative of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> of order less than <math|j>
    therefore vanishes at zero. The principal column has value <math|I>. This
    is stronger than the order-two transverse estimate sufficient for the
    current contraction. Stars and conjugates in these symbols mean analytic
    transpose at the opposite momentum, as in
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch05:gaussian>.

    Let <math|A<rsub|x><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>> be the physical row from
    coarse direction <math|b> at <math|<around|\<lfloor\>|x/L|\<rfloor\>>+z>
    to fine direction <math|a> at <math|x>. Differentiating the Fourier
    reconstruction through order less than <math|j> therefore gives, for
    every vector polynomial <math|p> of degree less than <math|j>,

    <\equation>
      <label|ch17:polynomial-response><around|(|W<rsub|s>*p|)><around|(|x|)><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b,z>A<rsub|0><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>*p<rsub|b>*<around|(|x/L+z|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|z>A<rsub|0><rsup|a*b><around|(|z|)>=L<rsup|-1>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>.
    </equation>

    The exponential row bound justifies the derivatives and the sum.
    Polarization gives mixed polynomial moments. In particular the output is
    a global polynomial of no larger degree, not a polynomial separately on
    each block. Its homogeneous degree-<math|r> leading term is
    <math|L<rsup|-r-1>*p<around|(|x|)>>; lower coefficients obey the same
    pre-ratio row bounds.

    Choose <math|j> above the fixed probe order. In a polarized coefficient
    evaluation take only tuples of potential polynomials whose summed scaling
    dimension is at most five. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch17:polynomial-response>
    preserves or lowers each polynomial degree, so every resulting tuple
    remains within that same finite jet menu. The polynomial-moment
    cancellation of Section<nbsp><reference|ch11:one-graph> makes its
    deterministic coefficient evaluation exactly zero. This is not a claim of
    vanishing on arbitrary polynomial inputs of degree less than <math|j>.
    Cropping the coarse field at radius <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>> changes this coefficient evaluation
    only by the exponential row tail, times fixed polynomial directional and
    support costs. That tail is smaller than every prescribed power of
    <math|H<rsup|-1>>.

    The actual tangent is <math|W<rsub|a>=W<rsub|s>-d<rsub|f>*E*R*W<rsub|s>>.
    Its difference from <math|W<rsub|s>> is a fine gradient. The coefficient
    source is a color scalar and a spacetime vector, invariant under local
    fine gauge transformations. Indeed gauge equivariance of <math|Y> and
    invariance of Haar volume imply invariance of
    <math|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y>; the action and
    loop variations are invariant for the same reason. Apply the
    corresponding fine gauge transformation before evaluating its local jet.
    The Baker\UCampbell\UHausdorff formula with the same local gauge choice
    compares the two exponential representatives with an error
    <math|C<rsub|j>*P<around|(|B|)>*t<rsup|2>> in the link logarithms, where
    <math|P> is a fixed polynomial. Dividing by the fine threshold gives
    <math|C<rsub|j>*P<around|(|B|)>*t>. This argument uses the exact
    correction to the gauge-covariant block average, not an unmatched
    straight average.

    It remains to compare the actual conditional insertion to this polynomial
    coefficient evaluation. Normalize the input link logarithms by <math|t>.
    The stationary nonlinear response, the same local gauge coordinate
    change, and the deterministic crop contribute
    <math|C<rsub|j>*P<around|(|B|)>*t> and the exponential tail just
    described. Gaussian fluctuations contribute
    <math|C<rsub|j>*P<around|(|B|)>*g/t>, where <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>,
    by the fundamental theorem of calculus and the one additional derivative
    reserve. Explicitly, if <math|p<around|(|z|)>\<geqslant\>1> is a
    polynomial envelope for the finite Gaussian coordinates, the difference
    between actual and deterministic evaluation in each requested derivative
    are bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*N<rsub|k>*P<around|(|B|)>*<around|{|t+g/t+e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*B>|}>*<space|0.17em>p<around|(|z|)><rsup|C<rsub|j>>,
    </equation*>

    with an appropriate enlargement of the crop radius in the last term. Here
    <math|N<rsub|k>> is the norm of the already <math|t<rsup|-2>>-normalized
    regular source and its derivatives in normalized link logarithms,
    including its current and loop coefficients. The factor-count extraction
    has already been performed; no further <math|t<rsup|-2>> is applied to
    this bound. For this bound subtract the deterministic coefficient
    evaluation inside the same actual normalized conditional integral. Its
    derivative through the prescribed scaling dimension is zero. Use the
    joint polynomial moments and differentiated density scores of that one
    conditional law to estimate the evaluation difference; no small
    total-variation distance between the nonlinear law and a Gaussian is
    asserted. The pointwise jet and moment estimates are those of
    <eqref|ch06:actual-jet-integral>; no differentiation of incoming
    large-field factors is involved. The complement is estimated by the
    already retained large-field bound. Finite coefficient recovery has a
    fixed stage cost.

    There are no invariant constant spacetime-vector or linear color terms.
    Hence these regular sources start at field order two. Their values and
    normalized-link derivatives on the threshold ball contain a factor
    <math|t<rsup|2>>; the explicit <math|t<rsup|-2>> removes exactly this
    factor. The classical part has already been cancelled and its order-five
    remainder treated separately above. Thus for the present sources
    <math|N<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>H<rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>+o<around|(|1|)>>>,
    with <math|\<theta\><rsub|j>\<to\>0>: current norms are logarithmic and
    the retained loop norm has this bound. Since <math|B> and all crop radii
    are fixed powers of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>, their
    polynomial factors are <math|H<rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. The actual
    coupling band gives <math|g/t\<leqslant\>C*H<rsup|-1>>, whereas <math|t>
    and the crop tail decay faster than every fixed inverse power of
    <math|H>. The resulting coefficient error is
    <math|H<rsup|-1+\<theta\><rsub|j>+o<around|(|1|)>>>, hence at most
    <math|C*H<rsup|-1/2>> after increasing the coupling threshold. Large
    supports are first truncated using the spare exponential support weight;
    its tail obeys the same bound. This proves the proposition for the full
    local sum.
  </proof>

  The differentiated-kernel bound <eqref|ch17:kernel-score> applies to the
  complementary part. The common large-field estimate retains the square-root
  bound <math|exp <around|(|-H<rsup|1/5>/2|)>>. Combining
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch17:spatial-moments> with the high-field
  remainder, the nonwrapping contributions are bounded by

  <\equation>
    <label|ch17:sigma-error>C*H<rsup|-1/2>+C<rsub|j>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-1/10>*H<rsup|C<rsub|j>>+C<rsub|j>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>*H<rsup|C<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-H<rsup|1/5>/2>.
  </equation>

  The fixed-order score has changed the polynomial constants, not the
  large-field event or its exponential gain. If <math|N<rsub|<math-up|per>>>
  is the period in level-<math|k> units, a wrapping term has covering number
  at least <math|N<rsub|<math-up|per>>/<around|[|10*<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>|]>-1>.
  Retaining two units of the exponential support exponent gives the
  additional error

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|j>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>*H<rsup|C<rsub|j>>*exp
    <around|{|-N<rsub|<math-up|per>>/<around|[|5*<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>|]>+2|}>.
  </equation*>

  It vanishes in the ordered period limit at fixed <math|k>.

  The two terms other than <math|C*H<rsup|-1/2>> in <eqref|ch17:sigma-error>
  are <math|o<around|(|H<rsup|-1>|)>>. To check the order of limits
  explicitly, put <math|x=log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>. The logarithm
  of the first bound multiplied by <math|H> is at most
  <math|C<rsub|j>+<around|(|C<rsub|j>+1|)>*x<rsup|2>-e<rsup|x>/10>; that of
  the second is at most <math|C<rsub|j>+C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|x>+<around|(|C<rsub|j>+1|)>*x<rsup|2>-e<rsup|x<rsup|2>/5>/2>.
  Both tend to minus infinity at each fixed stage. Increasing its coupling
  threshold makes the bounds uniform on that stage.

  The loop coefficient has zero <math|\<rho\>> by its two moment
  cancellations: its quadratic kernel kills both constant and affine links,
  whereas the cubic momentum coefficient functional pairs a constant with a
  quadratic link or two affine links. This uses the actual loop kernel of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch07:matching>, not only gauge invariance. The
  regulator calculation identifies the remaining coefficient as
  <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>/12>. One can check the factor directly in
  the three raw vector coordinates of <eqref|ch17:rho>: the cubic momentum
  functional takes values <math|-24,-96,0>, while <math|\<rho\>> takes values
  <math|-2,-8,0>. The lattice correction is the matching lower-order Taylor
  correction in <math|\<frak-h\>>.

  Choose the current depth and then the period at each fixed <math|k> so that
  the regulator error and the finite momentum approximation are smaller than
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-30>>. The retained loop bound is
  <math|2*H<rsup|\<theta\><rsub|j>>>, with <math|\<theta\><rsub|j>\<to\>0>,
  and current derivative norms are logarithmic. Sufficiently large coupling
  thresholds therefore give joint ordered limit coefficients satisfying

  <\equation>
    <label|ch17:sigma-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*H<rsup|1/6>,<space|2em>\<rho\><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>.
  </equation>

  All coefficient maps and the underlying correlations are retained in the
  same joint limit. Compactness is used only after the uniform finite-cutoff
  estimates have been established.

  <subsection|The equation direction and the necessary rates>

  Let <math|j<rsub|k>> be the degree-five coefficient of the transported
  current's divergence, in the same threshold coordinates. Its local Taylor
  coefficient has zero <math|\<rho\>>: the trace is a forward difference of a
  degree-three primitive, whose invariant projection is a unit. The same
  localization and marked error bounds compare the transported coefficient
  with this Taylor coefficient within <math|C*H<rsup|-1/4>>. Consequently, in
  the notation of the stability argument,

  <\equation*>
    j<rsub|i>\<to\>-v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>,<space|1em><around|\||\<rho\><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*H<rsub|i><rsup|-1/4>,<space|2em>v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>=\<beta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-j<rsub|i>\<to\>v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>.
  </equation*>

  With <math|e<rsub|i>=H<rsub|i><rsup|1/5>> this gives
  <math|e<rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>\<to\>0>.

  There is also a stronger, independent bound on the image of this vector.
  The source is carried as a density-valued kernel insertion, without
  dividing it by <math|F> or claiming that the resulting quotient is local.
  More directly, for every incoming marked coefficient
  <math|f<rsub|A><around|(|U|)>>, including all the original fine spectator
  factors,

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<cal-D\><rsub|Y>*k*<around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=0<space|1em><text|for
    each >U,<space|2em><big|int>\<cal-D\><rsub|Y>*k*<around|(|U,V|)>*f<rsub|A><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U*<space|0.17em>d*V=0.
  </equation*>

  The same score source is used for every <math|A>, so the joint rooted
  expansion retains all pushed spectator cross-modes and this exact
  zero-moment statement. There is no smoothness requirement on
  <math|f<rsub|A>>. For a smooth local coarse spectator
  <math|A<around|(|V|)>> one also has

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>A<around|(|V|)>*<space|-0.17em><big|int>\<cal-D\><rsub|Y>*k*<around|(|U,V|)>*f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U*<space|0.17em>d*V=-<big|int>D<rsub|V>*A<around|[|Y|]>*<space|-0.17em><big|int>k*<around|(|U,V|)>*f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U*<space|0.17em>d*V.
  </equation*>

  It is zero when the spectator does not read the varied links. For an actual
  exponentially local spectator, first truncate its dependence region and use
  this identity on disjoint terms. Retain the remaining terms in the same
  joint marked expansion. A connected term reaching from the source to a
  spectator at distance <math|R> has covering number at least
  <math|R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>-1>. Retaining two units of the
  exponential support exponent bounds these terms by <math|C*exp
  <around|[|-2*R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>|]>>, with their normalized
  large-field majorants as well. For spectator configurations separated by a
  fixed positive multiple of <math|a<rsub|n>>, their distance in
  level-<math|k<around|(|n|)>> units is at least <math|c*exp
  <around|(|T<rsub|n>|)>>. This tail is therefore smaller than
  <math|e<rsup|-T<rsub|n>/100>> after all fixed losses. Every spectator
  subset uses the same empty density. Match its full coefficient and
  remainder backwards from <math|k<around|(|n|)>> to <math|n>, where

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|n>=<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>,<space|2em>\<tau\><rsub|k<around|(|n|)>>-\<tau\><rsub|n>\<geqslant\>T<rsub|n>
  </equation*>

  is the first crossing. Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch11:descent-error> gives
  <math|e<rsup|-T<rsub|n>/8>> before multiplication by the terminal norm
  bounds. Fixed projection and threshold costs have logarithm
  <math|O<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)>>; accumulated pre-ratio
  column costs are <math|o<around|(|T<rsub|n>|)>>. Here it is only the latter
  constants that are multiplied along the window. Fixed-ratio nonlinear jet
  constants have already been absorbed into the errors controlled by the
  stage coupling thresholds.

  Replicate at the first backward crossing of <math|B<rsub|n><rsup|2>>, so
  <math|B<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|n><rsup|2>>.
  The actual positive-density estimate in <eqref|ch11:replicated-bound> costs
  at most

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|4>*B<rsub|n><rsup|8>*q<rsub|n>+6=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|22>|)>=o<around|(|T<rsub|n>|)>,
  </equation*>

  using <math|L<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>>,
  <math|B<rsub|n>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and the vacuum bound
  <math|q<rsub|n>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. Inverse source-weight bounds have
  logarithm <math|O<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n>|)>>. The physical
  mark normalization cancels the factor <math|a<rsub|n><rsup|-5>>. Thus the
  normalized exterior discrepancy is at most <math|e<rsup|-T<rsub|n>/100>>.
  This argument uses the full zero-moment source; its Taylor remainder is
  carried by the descending graph, not discarded.

  In the diagonal radius selection require
  <math|r<rsub|i>/a<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|<around|(|log
  log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>|)>>. Finite separating
  matrices of free two-point correlation pairings have uniformly bounded left
  inverses. For every fixed <math|p> the image therefore satisfies

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|i><rsup|p><around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*b<rsub|i><rsup|p>*exp
    <around|{|5<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>-T<rsub|n<rsub|i>>/100|}>\<longrightarrow\>0.
  </equation*>

  This proves the quantitative kernel hypothesis in the stability lemma. Its
  bounded right inverses give bounded <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>, with
  <math|e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>.

  The same source identity gives <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>-s<rsub|i>*\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>0>.
  For <math|Z=X,D>, ordinary trial descent gives
  <math|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|Z>-s<rsub|i><math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>Z=o<around|(|b<rsub|i><rsup|-p>|)>>
  for every fixed <math|p>. Apply the bounded functional and use
  <math|H<rsub|i><rsup|1/6>/e<rsub|i>\<to\>0> in <eqref|ch17:sigma-bound>.
  This proves the first two lines of <eqref|ch17:transfer>.

  Finally the trace of <math|X> is identically zero, while the transferred
  trace of <math|L> is an exact forward difference. Match its degree-three
  primitive at a fixed late stopping pair. Trial descent gives endpoint error
  <math|C*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|k>-\<tau\><rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>>,
  uniformly near the configurations used in the finite separating correlation
  pairings. Subexponential coefficient conversions and support truncation
  make this <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\>>|)>> for any fixed
  <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<less\>1/8>. Average over
  <math|m=<around|\<lfloor\>|a<rsub|k><rsup|-1+\<eta\>/2>|\<rfloor\>>>
  consecutive differences. The unit endpoints cancel, leaving at most
  <math|2*C*a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\>>/<around|(|m*a<rsub|k>|)>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\>/2>|)>>.
  The physical averaging window <math|m*a<rsub|k>> tends to zero, so the
  finite matrix of correlation pairings converges to its original invertible
  matrix. This proves the last rate in <eqref|ch17:transfer>, including
  multiplication by every fixed power of <math|b<rsub|i>>. Ordinary Taylor
  matching supplies its remaining convergence
  <math|A<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|Z>\<to\>Z>.

  <section|The coefficient and the all-radius limit><label|ch17:limit>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The trace coefficient><label|ch17:coefficient>For the
    prescribed-radius comparison maps constructed above,

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>\<longrightarrow\>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<cal-O\>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The free divergences of <math|X,D> form a basis of <math|V>. Thus the
    maps <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>=c<rsub|0>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|X>+c<rsub|1>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>
    have bounded inverses eventually. Put
    <math|c<rsub|i>=-f<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>>,
    so <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*q<rsub|i>> and
    <math|Z<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>+c<rsub|i,0>*B<rsub|i><rsup|X>+c<rsub|i,1>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>
    is conserved. Applying <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> to its zero divergence
    gives

    <\equation*>
      4*c<rsub|i,1>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>+c<rsub|i,0>*\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|X>|)>+c<rsub|i,1>*<around|[|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>|)>+4|]>.
    </equation*>

    The last two terms are <math|O<around|(|q<rsub|i>|)>*o<around|(|e<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>=o<around|(|1|)>>,
    whence <math|c<rsub|i,1>\<to\>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>/4>. Since
    <math|q<rsub|i>/\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>0>,
    <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*Z<rsub|i>\<to\>L>. The
    faster trace bounds give <math|\<beta\><rsub|i><math-up|tr><around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i,4><rsup|-1>*Z<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<cal-O\>>.

    The conserved line gives <math|Z<rsub|i>=\<nu\><rsub|i>*T>. Choose one
    free translation flux pairing with nonzero value. Its flux on the
    rescaled <math|Z<rsub|i>> tends to that value. The Ward identity for
    <math|T> and derivative comparison make its corresponding flux tend to
    the same nonzero value. Their ratio is <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>>, so
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>\<to\>1>. Finally centering subtracts
    <math|e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>*\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*1\<to\>0>
    by <eqref|ch17:running-radius>.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|The all-radius trace law><label|ch17:all-radii>The centered trace
    in the selected phase satisfies

    <\equation>
      lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=\<cal-A\><rsub|G>\<assign\><frac|3*dim<rsub|<RR>><gLie>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>\<gtr\>0.<label|ch17:trace-law>
    </equation>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The free normalization is <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<cal-O\>,\<cal-O\>;x,0|)>=\<cal-A\><rsub|G><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-8>>.
    One can check its factor without a tensor convention ambiguity: the
    curvature covariance is <math|<around|(|H<rsub|a*i>*H<rsub|b*j>-H<rsub|a*j>*H<rsub|b*i>|)>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|4>|)>>
    with <math|H=I-2*x*x<rsup|<math-ss|T>>/<around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>
    orthogonal. The sum of its squared numerators over ordered pairs is
    <math|24>. The two Wick pairings and the two factors <math|1/4> give
    <math|2\<cdot\>24/16=3> per colour.

    Start with any prescribed null sequence of radii, and take its comparison
    subsequence. Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch17:coefficient> gives
    convergent moving labels, while <eqref|ch17:running-radius> cancels the
    factor <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G><rsup|2>> in their pair correlation. For a
    generic pair at distance <math|t>, lattice rounding therefore yields

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)>*G<rsub|\<Theta\>>*<around|(|r<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>\<cal-A\><rsub|G>*t<rsup|-8>,<space|2em>t<rsub|i>\<to\>t.
    </equation*>

    Choose the pair <math|0,<around|(|t/2,t/2,t/2,t/2|)>> for
    <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>. For <math|t\<less\>1> the monotonicity of
    <eqref|ch17:laplace> bounds the upper limit at <math|r<rsub|i>> by
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|G>*t<rsup|-8>>; <math|t\<gtr\>1> bounds the lower
    limit. Send <math|t> to one from each side. Every prescribed null
    sequence has a subsequence with limit <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|G>>, which
    proves <eqref|ch17:trace-law> by the subsequence criterion.
  </proof>

  <section|What the trace law excludes><label|ch17:exclusion>

  Specify the comparison class before using this asymptotic. Consider finite
  linear combinations of finite products of the mass-shell Laplace kernels

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|m,k,h><around|(|r|)>=<big|int><rsub|<RR><rsup|3>><frac|\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-p\>|)><rsup|k>*h<around|(|\<b-p\>|)>|2*\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-p\>|)>>*e<rsup|-r*\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-p\>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|3>*\<b-p\>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|m>=<around|(|m<rsup|2>+<around|\||\<b-p\>|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|m\<geqslant\>0>, <math|k> is a nonnegative integer and <math|h>
  is a polynomial; empty products are constants. The measure has no implicit
  Fourier normalization. All these integrals are locally integrable at zero
  and converge for <math|r\<gtr\>0>.

  Each such profile has, near zero, a finite sum of terms
  <math|r<rsup|a><around|(|log r|)><rsup|b>>, with integer <math|a> and
  nonnegative integer <math|b>, plus a bounded remainder. This is the
  standard Laplace asymptotic expansion <cite|Wong2001>, applied as follows:
  decompose <math|h> into homogeneous terms, use polar coordinates, and
  expand <math|<around|(|\<omega\><rsup|2>-m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|(|j+1|)>/2>>
  for <math|\<omega\>\<geqslant\>2*m>. Choose enough terms that the remainder
  has as many energy moments as needed for the other factors in a product.
  The retained integer powers integrate to powers and logarithms; the bounded
  energy interval contributes an analytic function. Thus only nonnegative
  powers of the logarithm occur.

  After multiplication by <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>>, a
  nonzero leading term with <math|a\<leqslant\>-8> diverges in magnitude,
  while every term with <math|a\<gtr\>-8> and the bounded remainder tend to
  zero. Therefore a finite limit, if it exists, is zero. The positive limit
  in <eqref|ch17:trace-law> proves that <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> cannot agree
  with any such profile on any interval <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>.

  This excludes that specified finite mass-shell polynomial comparison. It
  does not exclude a generalized free field chosen to have the same positive
  measure <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<Theta\>>>, nor an infinite Wick expansion. No
  mass gap follows from a short-distance trace law.

  <assign|chapter-nr|17>

  <chapter|Operator products and extensions across
  diagonals><label|ch18:products>

  There are two distinct questions about colliding insertions. An operator
  product expansion replaces a small cluster by finitely many local fields
  when tested against separated spectators. A distributional extension
  assigns values to test functions whose supports meet collision diagonals.
  The first requires quantitative information about the marked
  renormalization group. The second already follows, with a conservative
  finite order, from the polynomial separated bounds. Neither construction
  defines a product of point fields by simply setting their positions equal.

  We use the common filtration <math|F<rsub|d>>, its inclusions
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|d,e>>, and the mixed correlations <math|S> from
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum>. The orthogonal actions on these
  spaces and the phase correlations <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> are those of
  Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch14:ward> and <reference|ch15:phases>. Write
  <math|\<frak-A\>> for the commutative compression algebra of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch15:phases> and <math|M<around|(|w|)>> for the
  compression of a whole separated word <math|w>. We first convert the
  lattice replacement estimate into the norm bound needed for the operator
  product expansion. The contact extension constructed later does not use it.

  The OPE originates with Wilson<nbsp><cite|Wilson1969>. Fröb and Holland
  establish the Yang\UMills OPE to every fixed order of perturbation
  theory<nbsp><cite|FrobHolland2016>. That result explains the relevant
  structure, but does not supply the nonperturbative estimate required below.
  The standard distribution-extension argument is the normal Taylor
  subtraction of Brunetti and Fredenhagen
  <cite-detail|BrunettiFredenhagen2000|Section<nbsp>5.2,
  Theorems<nbsp>5.2--5.3>. We use that procedure with an explicit choice
  adapted to the union of all pair diagonals; no reconstruction theorem
  requires this particular extension.

  <section|A fixed prescription for all finite
  degrees><label|ch18:prescription>

  A probe is a fixed word <math|v> at distinct nonzero positions. It defines
  <math|\<lambda\><around|(|A|)>=S*<around|(|A<around|(|0|)>*v|)>> on each
  field space on which <math|A> is defined. These functionals separate
  <math|F<rsub|d>>, by the definition of correlation-null fields. Since
  <math|F<rsub|d>> is finite dimensional, finitely many probes span its dual.
  A probe chosen at one degree continues to act at every higher degree by the
  same correlation, using the physical inclusions.

  One can choose fields <math|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>> and finite linear
  combinations <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>> of probes, with a degree
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> attached to each, such that

  <\equation>
    b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|u<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>,<space|2em>A=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>d>b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|A|)>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<space|1em><around|(|A\<in\>F<rsub|d>|)>,<label|ch18:dual>
  </equation>

  where fields in lower degrees are included in <math|F<rsub|d>> when
  necessary. All these identities hold simultaneously at every finite degree.
  Here is the useful linear-algebra construction. Suppose the choices through
  <math|d> have been made and let <math|R:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>> be
  inclusion. The old probes give a map <math|B:F<rsub|d+1>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>
  with <math|B*R=1>. For any section <math|S<rsub|0>> of the quotient map
  <math|q:F<rsub|d+1>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>/R*F<rsub|d>>, replace it by
  <math|S<rsub|0>-R*B*S<rsub|0>>. The corrected section is killed by every
  old probe. Lift a quotient basis through it, and represent its dual
  quotient coordinates by finite probe combinations. This extends
  <eqref|ch18:dual> without changing an earlier field or probe. In particular
  <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>>> vanishes on all degrees below
  <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>. This is only finite-dimensional linear
  algebra; no infinite expansion of a field is being asserted.

  For a cluster word <math|w>, define the unsymmetrized field reconstruction

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|d><around|(|w|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>d>b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|w|)>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|b<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|w|)>> means replacing the
  distinguished field in its probe correlations by the entire cluster. The
  mixed table is always used for this measurement. Thus the coefficients have
  no dependence on a phase or on spectators later placed outside the cluster.
  At a center <math|o>, apply this definition to <math|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w>.
  For each finite collection of probes there is a positive clearance: a
  cluster sufficiently close to <math|o> cannot meet a probe point, even
  after rotating the cluster. All subsequent formulas are used on this clear
  region.

  Write <math|R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>> for the orthogonal action on
  <math|F<rsub|d>>, with normalized Haar measure on <math|O<around|(|4|)>>.
  Symmetrize the reconstruction by

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|d>*w=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>>R<rsub|d><around|(|K|)>*H<rsub|d>*<around|(|K<rsup|-1>*w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*K,<space|2em>B<rsub|d>*w=<tfrac|1|2><around|{|A<rsub|d>*w+<wide|A<rsub|d>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>|\<bar\>>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Both maps fix every single field of degree at most <math|d> inserted at
  zero. They are smooth on the clear configuration region, since finitely
  many smooth correlation functions are integrated over a compact group. They
  preserve reality and intertwine rotations. Translation covariance is
  implemented by translating the center as above.

  Averaging must be followed by a compatible removal of lower degrees. Let
  <math|I<rsub|q>*A=A<around|(|0|)>>, put
  <math|L<rsub|i,q>=B<rsub|i>*I<rsub|q>>, and define

  <\equation>
    Q<rsub|0>=1,<space|2em>Q<rsub|j>=1-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j>\<iota\><rsub|i,j>*Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>,<space|2em>E<rsub|j>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<less\>j>ker
    <around|(|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>|)>.<label|ch18:graded>
  </equation>

  The inclusion identities give, by induction,

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,q>=0*<space|1em><around|(|q\<less\>j|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<leqslant\>j>\<iota\><rsub|i,j>*Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,q>=\<iota\><rsub|q,j>*<space|1em><around|(|q\<leqslant\>j|)>.
  </equation*>

  Consequently <math|Q<rsub|j>> is the projection onto <math|E<rsub|j>> along
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|j-1,j>*F<rsub|j-1>>. These spaces are invariant under
  rotations and conjugation. Fix real bases
  <math|<around|(|O<rsub|j,a>|)><rsub|a>> of <math|E<rsub|j>>, taking the
  unit itself in degree zero, and denote their coordinate functionals by
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,a>>. The coefficients used in this chapter are

  <\equation>
    c<rsub|j,a>*<around|(|w;o|)>=\<omega\><rsub|j,a>*Q<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>.<label|ch18:coefficients>
  </equation>

  The fields, probes and projections are fixed once, before any truncation,
  phase, spectator list or small-distance limit.

  <section|The marked replacement estimate><label|ch18:input>

  The expansion of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> keeps every joint
  nilpotent marker coefficient. To replace a cluster by a single field, one
  needs more than the one-insertion graph contraction. Markers may merge as
  their separation becomes smaller than a block. Their source weights
  multiply; an incoming term involving at least two insertions is kept as one
  factor, with its support, prescribed set of large-field sites and
  eligibility condition. Its stronger support norm absorbs the loss when
  several marks are assigned to the same coarse region. After local Taylor
  subtraction, the signed residual must be transported on the same history
  and compared in the physical quotient. Taking absolute values before that
  subtraction loses the cancellation.

  Here is the quantitative replacement estimate. It is formulated so that its
  two uses, determination of local polynomial coefficients and phase-uniform
  error control, are visible. Put <math|w<rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>>
  for a fixed cluster degree list, and let

  <\equation*>
    x<rsub|i>=o+r*z<rsub|i>,<space|2em><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>M,<space|2em><around|\||z<rsub|i>-z<rsub|j>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>\<gtr\>0.
  </equation*>

  Cluster labels range over fixed bounded subsets of their finite-dimensional
  spaces. Spectator labels have bounded degrees and coefficients; their
  positions stay at distance at least <math|c\<gtr\>0> from <math|o> and from
  one another. The constants below may depend on these finite lists,
  <math|M,\<eta\>,c> and a requested error exponent, but not on positions,
  phases or <math|r>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Same-history marked replacement><label|ch18:replacement-input>For
    every finite degree <math|D> and every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, on the
    geometry just specified and for all sufficiently small <math|r>, the
    unsymmetrized reconstruction coefficients obey

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||b<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<around|(|w;o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>-w<rsub|0>-\<epsilon\>>.
    </equation*>

    For each spectator word <math|v>, set

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|D>=M*<around|(|w*v|)>-M*<around|(|<around|(|H<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>|)><around|(|o|)>*<space|0.17em>v|)>.
    </equation*>

    The actual lattice marked replacement, with its signed residual and all
    original terms involving at least two insertions retained, gives the
    bound uniform in the number of reflected copies

    <\equation>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|Z<rsub|D><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<rsub|D>|)><rsup|k>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|C*r<rsup|D+1-w<rsub|0>-\<epsilon\>>|)><rsup|2*k>,<space|2em>k\<geqslant\>1.<label|ch18:replacement-moments>
    </equation>

    The constant and the small-<math|r> threshold precede <math|k>; cutoff
    thresholds may depend on each fixed <math|k>. The same estimates hold for
    every fixed finite list of differentiated insertions, with the
    corresponding scaling dimensions, and for the finitely many probe
    families above, uniformly under their rotations and conjugations.
  </proposition>

  This is an analytic estimate on the selected family, not an assertion that
  any bounded marked expansion has an OPE. Its construction requires the
  signed local transport, the stronger anchored marked support bound, and the
  graded quotient comparison. In particular, if a moving field has
  coefficient size <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|m>> at scale <math|m>, the
  needed quotient estimate is of the form

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|dist><rsub|F<rsub|t>><around|(|A,\<iota\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|t-i|)>>*e<rsup|o<around|(|log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>|)>>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|m>.
  </equation*>

  The lower field must be identified by all spectator responses, using the
  normalized-null lemma of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum>. A bound
  on a chosen coordinate representative alone does not supply this statement.
  The estimate uniform in the number of reflected copies also cannot be
  replaced by a bound on the mixed mean of <math|Z<rsub|D>>. These are
  supplied by the lattice estimate of Section<nbsp><reference|ch18:lattice-products>
  and its passage to the compression algebra below.

  <\render-proof|Proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:replacement-input>>
    <label|ch18:raw-replacement>The lattice estimate in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:lattice-replacement> was proved before
    phase decomposition. We use its uniformity in replica number to obtain
    the algebra-valued statement for the reconstructions <math|H<rsub|D>>
    fixed above.

    Let <math|F> be an interior lattice difference with chessboard seminorm
    at most <math|B>, and let its exterior factors have interior seminorms at
    most <math|B<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|q>>. Put copies of the complete
    difference and exterior factors in separated cells before taking limits.
    The repeated-word identity and chessboard inequality give, for its
    compressed limit <math|Z> and each integer <math|n\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|\<Xi\>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|Z|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|B*<big|prod><rsub|l=1><rsup|q>B<rsub|l>|)><rsup|2*n>.
    </equation*>

    The constants on the right precede <math|n>; only the cutoff and large
    translation thresholds depend on each fixed number of copies. This uses
    the complete signed density, with every term involving at least two
    insertions, from the proof of <eqref|ch18:lattice-estimate>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|ch15:moment-norm> then gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l>>.
    Positivity here belongs to the limiting commutative algebra and the
    original Wilson law, not to an individual signed effective activity.

    Apply this observation to the auxiliary field <math|A<rsub|t>> in the
    proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:mixed-bound>. On grid
    configurations its replacement error has norm with exponent
    <math|Z<rsub|0>-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>>,
    where <math|Z<rsub|0>=W*\<theta\><rsub|+>+4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>>.
    Fixed exterior factors cost a bounded factor. The graded quotient bound
    gives exponent <math|Z<rsub|0>-i*\<theta\>> for a degree-<math|i>
    component of <math|A<rsub|t>>; all the finitely many fields used in its
    reconstruction have uniformly bounded compressed exterior words. Testing
    the same error against the fixed probes gives the probe coordinate
    errors. With <math|t> chosen as in that proof, the omitted degrees and
    the low-coordinate errors therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|M*<around|(|w*v|)>-M*<around|(|<around|(|H<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>|)><around|(|o|)>*v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|[|Z<rsub|0>-<around|(|D+1|)>*\<theta\>|]>*T>.
    </equation*>

    There is no spectator-dependent choice of <math|H<rsub|D>>: the only
    geometry-dependent auxiliary field has disappeared from this inequality.

    The derivative descent of Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:mixed-bound>
    works in this norm. Equivalently, apply it to every character, using the
    same sharp chessboard seminorm bounds, and take their supremum. The crude
    and grid constants are uniform in the character. A positional derivative
    changes its input degree by one; a finite descending induction removes
    the grid restriction, also for any fixed list of differentiated fields.
    Letting the two resolution heights tend to one yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|M*<around|(|w*v|)>-M*<around|(|<around|(|H<rsub|D>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>|)><around|(|o|)>*v|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|D+1-W-\<varepsilon\>>.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient assertion is exactly the corresponding fixed-probe
    estimate in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:mixed-bound>. Compact label
    sets, conjugation, and the finite probe families do not change its
    constants. For their rotation orbits the same uniform estimates apply on
    the compact set of rotated labels and positions; choose <math|r> so all
    these probes remain separated from the cluster.

    Finally apply the vacuum state to <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|D><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<rsub|D>|)><rsup|n>>.
    Its norm is at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|D>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2*n>>,
    giving <eqref|ch18:replacement-moments> with the same <math|C> and the
    same small-<math|r> threshold for every <math|n>. Thus the order of
    quantifiers is obtained from the norm estimate, rather than from an
    uncontrolled sequence of separate moment constants.
  </render-proof>

  <section|Finite-order operator product expansion><label|ch18:ope>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Phase-uniform finite-order expansion><label|ch18:ope-theorem>The
    fixed coefficients <eqref|ch18:coefficients> satisfy, for every finite
    <math|D> and <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||c<rsub|j,a>*<around|(|w;o|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|j-w<rsub|0>-\<epsilon\>>,<space|2em>j\<leqslant\>D,>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||M*<around|(|w*v|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D,a>c<rsub|j,a>*<around|(|w;o|)>*M*<around|(|O<rsub|j,a><around|(|o|)>*v|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|D+1-w<rsub|0>-\<epsilon\>>.<eq-number><label|ch18:ope-bound>>>>>
    </align>

    The constants and threshold are uniform on the bounded label sets and
    separated geometries of Section<nbsp><reference|ch18:input>. Evaluating a
    character, or the mixed vacuum functional, gives the same bound for its
    correlations.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Full support of the phase measure converts
    <eqref|ch18:replacement-moments> into the operator-norm estimate by
    <eqref|ch15:moment-norm>. To compare the unsymmetrized and symmetric
    reconstructions, write <math|h=H<rsub|H>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>>
    at an auxiliary finite degree <math|H>. Every component of
    <math|B<rsub|j>*w-L<rsub|j,H>*h> is an average of finitely many
    replacement errors tested against rotated probe words. Their clearance is
    uniform by compactness of <math|O<around|(|4|)>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||B<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>-L<rsub|j,H>*h|\|>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|H+1-w<rsub|0>-\<epsilon\>>.
    </equation*>

    This identity uses covariance of the mixed correlations to rotate the
    probes, as well as the cluster; rotating labels alone would not suffice.

    Apply <math|Q<rsub|j>>. In the expansion of <math|h>, every field of
    degree below <math|j> is killed. The remaining finitely many terms have
    powers at least <math|r<rsup|j-w<rsub|0>-\<epsilon\>>>, by the raw
    coefficient estimate. Taking <math|H\<geqslant\>j> proves the first
    bound. For the second, take <math|H=D> and sum the reconstruction
    comparison through <math|j=D>. The reconstruction identity following
    <eqref|ch18:graded> returns <math|h> exactly. Multiplying the coefficient
    errors by <math|M*<around|(|O<rsub|j,a><around|(|o|)>*v|)>> costs a
    uniform constant on the separated spectator geometry. Add the raw
    operator error. Finitely many sums and averages are controlled by first
    using a smaller error exponent.
  </proof>

  For two fields of degrees <math|d<rsub|A>,d<rsub|B>>, the degree-<math|j>
  coefficient thus has size at most <math|C*r<rsup|j-d<rsub|A>-d<rsub|B>-\<epsilon\>>>.
  The remainder may still grow when <math|D+1\<less\>d<rsub|A>+d<rsub|B>>;
  its order nevertheless improves with <math|D>. No assertion concerns the
  convergence of the infinite sum in <math|D>.

  The derivative version uses the differentiated marked replacement estimate.
  One first fixes the finite derivative order and the degree margin, then
  carries out the same finite probe functionals. The derivative identities of
  the field filtration identify the resulting derivatives. Uniform
  differentiated bounds permit passage from a dense grid to all separated
  positions by the mean-value theorem. Mere pointwise grid estimates without
  those bounds would not justify that passage.

  For two nested clusters at radii <math|\<rho\>\<less\>R>, replacements can
  be composed by telescoping the signed difference. The first error is tested
  against the unchanged second cluster; the next is multiplied by the already
  produced coefficients. Accordingly a uniform nested estimate requires the
  replacement bound when exterior separation also tends to zero, with its
  dependence on <math|R> retained. Fixed-positive-clearance
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch18:replacement-input> by itself does not give
  that stronger result. For any prescribed finite tree, the required
  additional statement is a signed bound at every vertex, with
  degree-<math|q> coefficient cost <math|\<rho\><rsup|q-d>> and residual gain
  <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|D+1>>, up to arbitrary small power
  losses, including the coefficients and the operators that its descendants
  apply to the test function. With those bounds, finite telescoping proves
  the corresponding finite-tree expansion. This stronger multi-scale
  assertion is not needed for the distributional extension below.

  <section|An explicit finite-order extension across every
  diagonal><label|ch18:extension>

  We now use only a uniform polynomial separated bound. Fix an arity
  <math|n\<geqslant\>2> and a field list <math|\<b-A\>>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<less\>j><around|{|x<rsub|i>=x<rsub|j>|}>,<space|2em>\<delta\><around|(|x|)>=min<rsub|i\<less\>j><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  Suppose, as supplied by the separated bounds and the uniform phase slab
  bounds, that for some finite <math|P\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <around|\||S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K>*min
    <around|(|1,\<delta\><around|(|x|)>|)><rsup|-P>*<space|1em><around|(|x\<in\>K\<setminus\>\<Delta\>|)>,<label|ch18:polynomial>
  </equation>

  uniformly in <math|\<xi\>> on every compact <math|K>. The mixed family has
  the same bound. No optimal value of <math|P> is required. Choose once an
  integer <math|k\<geqslant\>0> with <math|k+1\<gtr\>P>, and a length
  <math|s\<gtr\>0>.

  We specify which normal jet is subtracted near simultaneous collisions. Let
  <math|m=n*<around|(|n-1|)>/2> be the number of pairs and put, on
  <math|<Conf><rsub|n>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><around|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|-2>|)><rsup|-1/2>.
  </equation*>

  Then <math|\<delta\>/<sqrt|m>\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>.
  Choose a fixed nonnegative smooth function <math|\<varphi\>> on
  <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>, equal to one on
  <math|<around|[|0,2*<sqrt|m>|]>> and zero on
  <math|<around|[|4*<sqrt|m>,\<infty\>|)>>. For a pair
  <math|h=<around|{|i,j|}>> define

  <\equation>
    a<rsub|h><around|(|x|)>=<frac|\<varphi\>*<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|>/\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>|<big|sum><rsub|u\<less\>v>\<varphi\>*<around|(|<around|\||x<rsub|u>-x<rsub|v>|\|>/\<rho\><around|(|x|)>|)>>.<label|ch18:pair-weights>
  </equation>

  The denominator is at least one, because a closest pair has ratio at most
  <math|<sqrt|m>>. Thus <math|a<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>0> and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>=1>. Only pairs whose separation is at most
  <math|4*<sqrt|m>*\<rho\>> are selected. These weights are smooth off
  <math|\<Delta\>>, invariant under simultaneous translations and rotations,
  and permuted with the slots.

  Let <math|\<pi\><rsub|h>*x> replace <math|x<rsub|i>,x<rsub|j>> by their
  centroid, leaving the other positions unchanged, and put
  <math|z<rsub|h>=x-\<pi\><rsub|h>*x>. This is the orthogonal normal
  displacement from the pair diagonal; its norm is
  <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|>/<sqrt|2>>. Define the normal
  Taylor polynomial of a test function by

  <\equation>
    T<rsub|h,k>*f<around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<ell\>=0><rsup|k><frac|1|\<ell\>!>*D<rsup|\<ell\>>*f*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|h>*x|)><around|[|z<rsub|h>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|h>|]>.<label|ch18:normal-Taylor>
  </equation>

  Only normal directions occur; the centroid and all external positions
  remain parameters. Finally fix <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>|)>>
  with <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\>\<leqslant\>1>, <math|\<chi\>=1> on
  <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and <math|\<chi\>=0> on
  <math|<around|[|2,\<infty\>|)>>, and set

  <\equation>
    <around|(|\<cal-W\>*f|)><around|(|x|)>=f<around|(|x|)>-\<chi\>*<around|(|\<rho\><around|(|x|)>/s|)>*<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h><around|(|x|)>*T<rsub|h,k>*f<around|(|x|)>.<label|ch18:subtraction>
  </equation>

  This formula, including <math|k,s,\<varphi\>,\<chi\>>, is the subtraction
  convention. It is the same in every phase and is fixed before the test
  function. It deliberately subtracts more jets than an optimal
  scaling-degree prescription may require.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|A common contact extension><label|ch18:extension-theorem>Under
    <eqref|ch18:polynomial>, the formula

    <\equation>
      \<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|f|)>=<big|int><rsub|<Conf><rsub|n>>S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<around|(|\<cal-W\>*f|)><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<label|ch18:extension-formula>
    </equation>

    defines a distribution of local order at most <math|k+1>. It extends the
    separated correlation, is uniform in the phase on compact supports of
    test functions, and is continuous in <math|\<xi\>> on each test function.
    Averaging over phases gives the same formula for the mixed correlations.
    Translations, orthogonal covariance and simultaneous permutation symmetry
    are preserved by this prescription.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This is the normal Taylor extension argument of
    <cite-detail|BrunettiFredenhagen2000|Theorem<nbsp>5.3>; the following
    verification explains why one subtraction treats every partial diagonal
    here. When <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>s>, the weights sum to one, so

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\>*f=<big|sum><rsub|h>a<rsub|h>*<around|(|f-T<rsub|h,k>*f|)>.
    </equation*>

    For each selected pair, <math|<around|\||z<rsub|h>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<sqrt|2*m>*\<rho\>>.
    Taylor's integral remainder therefore bounds this expression by
    <math|C*\<rho\><rsup|k+1><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|k+1>>>.
    Since <math|\<delta\>\<geqslant\>\<rho\>>, <eqref|ch18:polynomial> bounds
    its product with the kernel by <math|C*\<rho\><rsup|k+1-P><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|k+1>>>,
    which is bounded and hence locally integrable. This remains true when
    arbitrarily many other separations vanish at independent rates: the
    selected pair always has displacement comparable to the smallest
    separation. No fixed collision pattern or single radial approach is
    assumed.

    On the transition region <math|s\<leqslant\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>2*s>, all
    separations are bounded below by <math|s>, and the finite Taylor
    polynomials are bounded by the same seminorm of the test function.
    Outside this region the integrand uses <math|f> itself. If <math|f> is
    supported in a compact <math|K>, a nonzero subtracted jet requires
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|h>*x\<in\>K> and <math|<around|\||z<rsub|h>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|n>*s>.
    Thus the integrand is supported in one fixed compact enlargement
    <math|K<rprime|'>> of <math|K>. We obtain

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|f|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K<rprime|'>>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>k+1><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.
    </equation*>

    This proves continuity and the local order assertion. If <math|f> is
    supported away from <math|\<Delta\>>, all its jets at every
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|h>*x\<in\>\<Delta\>> vanish, so
    <eqref|ch18:extension-formula> is the original separated integral.
    Continuity in the phase and the mixture formula follow by dominated
    convergence with the same bound. The normal projections, pair weights and
    radial cutoff commute with the stated geometric symmetries.
  </proof>

  For <math|n=1> use the original smooth one-point function, and for
  <math|n=0> use the normalized unit. A consistent multilinear family on the
  whole filtration is obtained using the fixed invariant summands
  <math|E<rsub|j>> in <eqref|ch18:graded>. For each finite tuple
  <math|<around|(|j<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,j<rsub|n>|)>> choose <math|P> uniformly
  on the finite basis lists of <math|E<rsub|j<rsub|1>>\<times\>\<cdots\>\<times\>E<rsub|j<rsub|n>>>,
  and choose the same <math|P,k> for every permutation of that tuple. Use
  this one subtraction on the tuple and extend multilinearly there. Every
  field has a unique finite sum of components in the <math|E<rsub|j>>, so
  extension by finite multilinearity gives a prescription on all of <math|F>.
  It is compatible with the inclusions and preserves rotations because each
  <math|E<rsub|j>> is invariant. Choosing an order independently for each
  vector, or redefining it on each larger degree space, would not have these
  properties.

  <section|Sharper subtraction and dependence on the
  prescription><label|ch18:scope>

  The explicit extension above is conservative. A degree-sensitive
  alternative assigns to every collision subset <math|h> the normal dimension
  <math|q<rsub|h>=4*<around|(|<around|\||h|\|>-1|)>>, the total input degree
  <math|d<rsub|h>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>h>d<rsub|i>>, and

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|h>=max <around|(|0,d<rsub|h>+1-q<rsub|h>|)>.
  </equation*>

  If a contact projection extracts a degree-<math|q> field from that cluster,
  one subtracts the normal Taylor jet of the test function through order
  <math|L<rsub|h>-1-q> when <math|q\<less\>L<rsub|h>>. A single shell then
  has the combined power

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsup|q-d<rsub|h>>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|L<rsub|h>-q>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|q<rsub|h>>=\<rho\><rsup|q<rsub|h>+L<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>>,
  </equation*>

  with exponent at least one. This explains the source prescription and its
  proposed sharper local-order bound. For several collisions the shell
  estimate must be established for the complete signed expression, including
  nested subtractions and all child operators acting on test functions. The
  parent Taylor operator acts first on the test function; the child operators
  act after it. They cannot be commuted for nested subsets. A partition of
  unity cannot be moved through these normal Taylor operators without its
  resulting diagonal jets. A one-shell estimate alone does not verify that
  stronger forest prescription. We have therefore used the fully specified
  pair-weighted subtraction <eqref|ch18:subtraction>, for which absolute
  convergence at all collision patterns was proved directly.

  Two extensions agreeing on <math|<Conf><rsub|n>> differ by a distribution
  supported on <math|\<Delta\>>. Along a smooth stratum, a finite-order
  difference is a sum of normal derivatives of delta distributions with
  distributional coefficients. This is the usual local renormalization
  freedom <cite-detail|BrunettiFredenhagen2000|Section<nbsp>5.2>. Changing
  the cutoff or subtraction order can change contact terms while preserving
  separated correlations and the OPE estimates. Contact reflection
  positivity, contact Ward identities, uniqueness, unit removal and
  identification with unsubtracted coincident-point lattice limits require
  additional normalization arguments.

  <assign|chapter-nr|18>

  <chapter|Assembly and the scope of the construction><label|ch19:assembly>

  The preceding chapters distinguish finite algebraic identities, analytic
  estimates for the lattice transformation, and consequences for the
  continuum theory. This chapter states how they fit together. The
  construction and finite-order operator products use the estimates proved in
  the preceding chapters. The sharper trace asymptotic is separated below
  because it requires the additional current estimates of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch17:trace>.

  <section|One family and the order of its limits><label|ch19:family>

  Choose the compact matrix group <math|G> with simple nonabelian real Lie
  algebra, its trace inner product as in Section<nbsp><reference|ch01:gauge>,
  and a positive soft-block penalty <math|b>. Fix a sufficiently large coarse
  inverse coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. The common schedule of
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch10:trajectory> selects all block lengths,
  derivative orders, support weights, coupling thresholds and admissible
  physical periods <math|R<rsub|p>>. At cutoff <math|N> and period index
  <math|p>, shooting from the Wilson measure gives its bare coupling
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,N>> with prescribed coarse endpoint
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>.

  The same finite histories must define the coefficient normalizations of
  every local insertion. One cannot separately choose convenient histories
  for convergence, for free separating families and for the trace law. The
  microscopic sections are fixed before <math|N,p>, and their comparison maps
  act simultaneously on all labels and spectator correlation pairings. Any
  additional finite stage requirements, such as the equation calibration
  floor, belong to the selection of this one schedule.

  For each fixed physical volume the first limit is the full limit
  <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. Only afterwards is a subsequence of physical
  volumes extracted. Given any increasing sequence of period indices, a
  single refinement supplies all arities, degree bounds, label basis elements
  and derivatives of the separated kernels. Auxiliary coefficient limit
  points may be selected on ordered tails; they do not change this full inner
  limit of the correlations. Scaling comparison sequences and the choice of a
  phase are later operations on the same continuum family.

  <section|Assembly of the construction><label|ch19:theorem-section>

  The hypotheses used along the construction have now been supplied for one
  common family. Chapters<nbsp><reference|ch09:renormalization> and
  <reference|ch10:trajectory> give the actual block estimates and fixed-input
  convergence. Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch11:insertions> supplies the marked
  comparisons, including restarted histories and arbitrary fixed numbers of
  insertions. Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum> constructs the bounded
  approximations and local product estimates. The free comparison and annular
  estimates in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch14:ward>, and the repeated estimate
  for a separating family in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch15:phases>, use these
  same objects. Thus they are not additional assumptions in the following
  assembly statement.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Assembly of the common Wilson family><label|ch19:conditional>The
    chosen Wilson family has the following properties. For every prescribed
    increasing volume sequence there is one refinement with a hierarchy
    <math|S> of smooth separated Euclidean correlations and compatible
    finite-dimensional field spaces <math|F<rsub|d>>. Their dimensions and
    orthogonal representations agree with those of the invariant free Maxwell
    comparison spaces. The fields have units, injective inclusions and
    commuting positional derivatives.

    There is a compact metrizable phase space <math|\<Xi\>>, a full-support
    probability measure <math|\<nu\>>, and phase hierarchies
    <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> with <math|S=<big|int>S<rsup|\<xi\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>>.
    All phases have the same field-null spaces, Euclidean covariance and
    reflection positivity. Each phase reconstructs a local relativistic
    quantum field theory with tempered Wightman correlations, a faithful
    common field filtration, a positive-cone translation spectrum and a
    unique invariant vacuum. Its weak field commutant is scalar. These
    statements are uniform over the phases in the finite-list senses
    specified in the preceding chapters.

    The common real stress tensor is symmetric and conserved, with its
    normalization fixed by local translation and rotation Ward identities.
    Its relativistic continuation gives the local charge law
    <eqref|ch16:charge-law>. The fixed OPE prescription of
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch18:prescription> gives the phase-uniform
    finite-order coefficient and remainder bounds <eqref|ch18:ope-bound>.
    Independently, the fixed subtraction prescription of
    Section<nbsp><reference|ch18:extension> extends all separated
    correlations to finite-order distributions across collision diagonals,
    compatibly with the field inclusions, translations, rotations and
    permutations.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The dependency chain uses the estimates established above.
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch12:ultraviolet> takes the full cutoff limit at
    each fixed period. The common diagonal extraction and two rank bounds in
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch13:continuum> give the fields and their
    differential calculus. That chapter also proves the repeated-word bound,
    so bounded slab insertions in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch14:ward> do not
    depend on later reconstruction or operator products.

    Coordinate reflection positivity, the free comparison and the annular
    estimates give the conserved tensor, its local charges, Euclidean
    rotations and Ward normalization in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch14:ward>.
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch15:phases> then compresses whole words to the
    mixed ground space. The real approximation and moments for separating
    families prove symmetries and the equality of field spaces in every
    character, beyond what abstract commutative algebra alone gives.

    The slab bounds supply joint boundary distributions in
    Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch16:reconstruction>. Covariance gives the
    spectral cone, analytic continuation gives locality, and word
    reconstruction gives the relativistic fields. The unitary equivalence of
    the reconstructed and reflection-positive Hilbert spaces and the
    phase-uniform low-energy estimate remove exceptional vacua. The same
    chapter proves weak irreducibility and continues the stress charge law.

    The finite OPE and contact extension follow from their separate arguments
    in Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch18:products>. Each step preserves the
    originally chosen family and the already selected correlation limits.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|The trace law><label|ch19:conditional-trace>In the retained phase
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|<RR>><gLie>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently the centered trace cannot agree near zero with any finite
    linear combination of products of the mass-shell Laplace kernels
    specified in Section<nbsp><reference|ch17:exclusion>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Apply the rate-sensitive tensor cancellation and prescribed-radius
    squeeze of Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch17:trace>. The positive spectral
    measure required there already follows from reconstruction.
  </proof>

  <section|Conclusions that are not supplied><label|ch19:scope>

  The logarithmic ultraviolet law and the low-energy estimate describe
  different regimes. The latter bounds spectral mass near zero by a power; it
  does not show that the spectrum avoids an interval above the vacuum. A
  positive mass gap is not proved.

  Vacuum uniqueness within each phase does not imply uniqueness of the phase,
  a point-mass phase measure, or uniqueness of the infinite-volume limit. The
  construction uses a refinement of a prescribed volume sequence. It does not
  prove a simultaneous continuum and infinite-volume limit, independence of
  the block penalty or all normalization choices, or a separate lattice
  realization of each character phase.

  The trace comparison excludes the finite class explicitly defined in
  Chapter<nbsp><reference|ch17:trace>. It does not rule out every generalized
  free field or every infinite Wick expansion on the basis of one two-point
  function. The OPE is an expansion to each fixed finite degree; no infinite
  series convergence has been asserted. The contact extension has a specified
  subtraction convention and may have larger local order than an optimized
  prescription. Positivity at contacts, contact Ward identities, the fact
  that inserting the identity leaves correlations unchanged, and uniqueness
  of contact terms require additional normalization conditions.

  The trace argument uses more than convergence to the free tensor. Its
  low-moment estimate distinguishes field degree from scaling dimension: a
  quadratic expression with extra spatial differences is still quadratic in
  the field. Exact polynomial preservation by the linear response, together
  with the normalized Gaussian fluctuation estimate, supplies the required
  decay in Proposition<nbsp><reference|ch17:spatial-moments>. This is why
  that additional calculation is needed after reconstruction.

  <chapter*|Notation in the current draft>

  This index lists the main symbols used in the written chapters. Auxiliary
  symbols are defined where used. The chapter and equation references give
  the precise conventions; in particular, lattice distances and physical
  distances must not be interchanged.

